4677,adml-class,"# Description

## Build and improve a series of consumer propensity models (who is likely to make the repeat purchase next week) using the dataset in the lecture</p>
### *You can use the following data or the combination to train and validate the model*
> (T)    X20210731\_Y20210807\_0827\_mp\_clean.csv
> (T-1) X20210724\_Y20210731\_0827\_mp\_clean.csv
> (T-2) X20210717\_Y20210724\_0827\_mp\_clean.csv
> Use (T+1) X20210807\_Y20210814\_0827\_mp\_clean\_wolabel.csv as the final prediction data, and submit the prediction 


### Based on your learning in question 1 to question 3, propose and build the best model based on T, T-1 and T-2 data, make the prediction for T+1 data, and submit the prediction!</p>
----------------------
# Evaluation

# Evaluation Metric
## You should use Top500 precision as the main factor to evaluate your model performance. *However, because the Kaggle doesn't support TopK precision as its metrics, we will use the simple categorical precision as our leaderboard metrics.*
<p>You could see definition of relevant metrics regarding categorical accuracy from here <a href=""https://towardsdatascience.com/keras-accuracy-metrics-8572eb479ec7""> Categorical Accuracy</a>. 


# Submission Format
### In this case, you should transform your result to fit into the sample submission in this Kaggle competition: set your Top500 customer's predict\_prob to be ""1"", while all other customer's predict\_prob to be ""0"".

###  Example of how you transform your data (you could operate with your result in many ways, and here is just a reference for you).
#### Suppose you trained your model and your predicted result are stored in DataFrame called:
> df\_result\_test\_output 

Then you could get the Top500 customers by sorted the probability:

> df\_result\_test\_output\_sorted = df\_result\_test\_output.sort\_values(by=['predict\_prob'],ascending=False) 

In addition, use this sorted result to form the first 500 rows where all the repurchase_flag = ""1"":

    df_result_id = pd.DataFrame(df_result_test_output_sorted['unify_id'][:500].values)
    df_result_id.columns = ['unify_id']
    df_result_flag = pd.DataFrame([1 for i in range(500)])
    df_result_flag.columns = ['repurchase_flag']
    df_result = pd.concat([df_result_id, df_result_flag], axis=1)

Finally, generate all the remaining rows, where all the repurchase_flag = ""0"", it's very easy, just do it in your own way.","# Dataset Description

### Please refer to homework1 to see the raw data description.
**You could also find a sample for your submission in this section. It shows how your csv should be look like, but it is just a simple, which means it is `not complete`, you should have 500 rows in your submission with repurchase_flag to be ""1"", while all others are ""0"" with their correlated unify_id. In this sample we cut down all the following data after 1000 rows, but in your data, it should contains whole 98555 rows as the training data.**",True,True
