4728,crowd-bias-challenge,"# Description

## CrowdBias Challenge

Participate in the CrowdBias challenge organised with [CSCW 2021 Workshop - Investigating and Mitigating Biases in Crowdsourced Data](https://sites.google.com/view/biases-in-crowdsourced-data). Register [here](https://forms.gle/m2esEZegkDYyQ8jH7) to take part in teams of up to 4 members.

The broad task goal is to collect correct, unbiased labels for an annotation task in which each item to annotate has a true answer, known to the organizers. Teams are free to pursue whatever research designs they see fit, such as interface design, annotation workflows, assignment methods, aggregation approaches, etc. Teams will be evaluated based on a combination of accuracy and fairness metrics. Successful teams will also be invited to present their approach during the workshop.

Participants may use any crowdsourcing platform of their choice to collect human annotations. 
For any teams interested in using [Amazon¡¯s Sagemaker Ground Truth](https://aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/groundtruth/) (GT) or [Augmented Artificial Intelligence](https://aws.amazon.com/augmented-ai/) (A2I) service to collect human annotations, Amazon will provide $250 in AWS credits to the first 15 teams who commit to participating in the challenge and to using GT as part of their participation. AWS credits cannot be used with Amazon Mechanical Turk. 

Challenge participants are also invited to attend our workshop at CSCW. Please note that you will have to [register](https://cscw.acm.org/2021/registration/) for both the CSCW conference and the workshop in order to attend (per the registration link above, one can see that prices are fairly inexpensive as a virtual conference, and further discounted with early registration through October 1, 2021). 

**Step 1** - Follow this link to register for the CrowdBias challenge and receive AWS credits
https://forms.gle/m2esEZegkDYyQ8jH7

**Step 2** - **Optional** - Register for CSCW Conference and Workshop (use the code ¡°MIBI09¡±)
https://cscw.acm.org/2021/registration/

Please contact workshop organizers for any questions.
bcd21@easychair.org

----------------------
# Details

In this challenge, you need to collect apparent age labels for a given set of face images.

## Challenge Input

324 face images with the file name <image_id.jpg>. You can download the dataset through `Data` section. Please note that the image size can vary.


## Expected Output

You need to submit an abstract (maximum length of 200 words) describing your method and a CSV file that includes the final consolidated age labels. 

The CSV file should contain two columns (`image_id` and `age`) and be submitted through Kaggle. The file should only have a single entry for each image. You are allowed to make multiple submissions.

```
image_id,age
7105,40
```
The abstract and the final CSV file should be submitted through the [workshop submission website](https://easychair.org/conferences/?conf=bcd21) by **20th October 2021**. You are also encouraged to document the process. We will invite successful teams to present during the workshop. 


## Evaluation

The final ranking will be determined based on two metrics.
1. MAE calculated using the real age and the submitted apparent age
2. MAE difference between groups (determined based on the demographics and other attributes of people appearing in the images).

> Please note that the Kaggle leaderboard is only based on metric 1 and the final ranking will be different to the Kaggle rankings.

----------------------
# How To Use AWS Sagemaker Ground Truth

## How to use AWS Sagemaker GroundTruth (GT)

Participants may use any crowdsourcing platform of their choice to collect human annotations. 
For any teams interested in using free AWS credits with [Amazon¡¯s Sagemaker Ground Truth](https://aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/groundtruth/) (GT), we provide some helpful information below for getting started.

You can create a task in GT using the AWS management console or the AWS SDK ([Create a Labeling Job (API) - Amazon SageMaker](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/latest/dg/sms-create-labeling-job-api.html)). Check the following document for a step by step guide on setting up the challenge task in AWS Sagemaker Ground Truth.

[AWS Sagemaker Ground Truth - How to Guide](https://docs.google.com/document/d/10ic2TSZ8vwPc1u6u-2gU0gbG__vKeWfQn4Ut6SZTNLU/edit?usp=sharing)

### Additional Resources
- [A Complete Tutorial on Annotating Datasets with Amazon SageMaker Ground Truth](https://aws.amazon.com/getting-started/hands-on/build-training-datasets-amazon-sagemaker-ground-truth/)
- [Creating Custom Labeling Workflows - Amazon SageMaker](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/latest/dg/sms-custom-templates.html)
- [Github - Example task UIs for Amazon SageMaker Ground Truth](https://github.com/aws-samples/amazon-sagemaker-ground-truth-task-uis)","# Dataset Description

## Challenge Input

324 face images with the file name `<image_id.jpg>`. You can download the dataset below. Please note that the image size can vary.

## Acknowledgement

Original data from [ChaLearn LAP datasets](https://chalearnlap.cvc.uab.cat/) licensed under [CC BY-NC 4.0](https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/4.0/)",True,True
4729,ai-inductions-21-nlp,"# Description

Welcome to the AI Club inductions for the year 2021. It¡¯s been a year since the establishment of the club and now it¡¯s time to open our doors to new talent. This year we¡¯ve decided to set up two competions as a part of the induction process, a Natural Language Processing (NLP) task and a Computer Vision (CV) task. Participants can choose to take part in either competitions. We look forward to see your innovative submission. Best of luck and get hacking.

About this competition
This competition aims to build a simple question answering system. You will have to train an NLP model that takes a question and context as input and outputs the corresponding answer. The leaderboard will show you how your model fares over other participants and this will serve as a major criterion for this induction process.

Submission
To make a submission you will have to add your predictions to the test CSV file(sample csv file is there in ""Data"" section). You will be receiving a mail from AI Club NITC where you will be shared a folder. This csv file has to be uploaded onto the google drive shared folder. Within a span 30 minutes to 1 hour, your position will be updated on the leaderboard, the link for the leaderboard has been given below.

Leaderboard link -https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1pGt3ROFH0UnV2OK7gMfJLPACwGmtVcYu?usp=sharing

To save csv file:
df.to_csv(""submission.csv"", index = False)
df = DataFrame of the prediction csv file

Guidelines
1. Participants may participate in only one competition (NLP or CV).
2. The competition is open to all B19 and B20 students.
3. Participants are restricted to use only the data that has been provided on the respective competition pages. However, the use of pertained models is allowed.
4. All submissions must be made via the google drive link, any submission made via Kaggle will not be considered.
5. All the submission file must be of csv format.
6.Be careful while uploading the file. Ensure that you upload the correct file into the folder. You will have a maximum of 5 submission chances daily.
7. Any submission after 11:59 am on 15/10/2021 will not be considered.



----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated on a Jaccard-based FBeta score between predicted texts and ground truth texts, with Beta = 0.5 (a micro F0.5 score).

The following is Python code for calculating the Jaccard score for a single prediction string against a single ground truth string. Note that the overall score for a sample uses Jaccard to compare multiple ground truth and prediction strings that are pipe-delimited - this code does not handle that process or the final micro F-beta calculation.

def jaccard(str1, str2): 
    a = set(str1.lower().split()) 
    b = set(str2.lower().split())
    c = a.intersection(b)
    return float(len(c)) / (len(a) + len(b) - len(c))

Any matched predictions where the Jaccard score meets or exceeds the threshold of 0.5 are counted as true positives (TP), the remainder as false positives (FP).
Any unmatched predictions are counted as false positives (FP).
Any ground truths with no nearest predictions are counted as false negatives (FN).
All TP, FP and FN across all samples are used to calculate a final micro F0.5 score. (Note that a micro F score does precisely this, creating one pool of TP, FP and FN that is used to calculate a score for the entire set of predictions.)","# Dataset Description

About Dataset
The training data is a CSV file containing the question, context and the corresponding answer. We have also provided the start and end indexes of the context from which the answer was extracted. Your predictions will have to be made on the test CSV which contains the questions and the context. Your predictions have to be inserted into the PredictionString column in the submission CSV and the submission has to be made via the google drive link.

File Descriptions
1. train.csv - A CSV file containing the training data
2. test.csv - A CSV file containing the test data to make predictions on
3. submission.csv - A CSV file containing id to which prediction string should be added to make submission.",True,True
