4756,hw3p2test,"# Description


The feature data of this homework are the same as homework 1 part 2. There are many variable length utterances comprised of 40 dimensional timesteps. You can use recurrent layers and their variants. You may also use convolutional layers if you so choose.

Your task will be to take the sequence of features and map them to a sequence of phonemes. Keep in mind the number of phonemes will not equal the number of timesteps. Refer to the write-up for more information.
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric will be the Levenshtein distance (character level minimum edit distance) from the ground truth. The labels will have the sequence of phonemes, but not the alignment. To handle this CTC Loss will be used during training and a search should be used on the output distribution for prediction. More information is provided in the writeup.",# Dataset Description,True,True
4757,MLDS-2021-22,"# Description

This is the official Kaggle competition for the 2021-22 MLDS Long Competition at UT Austin. You can find all necessary information in the ""Data"" tab above. This competition is comprised of 20,000 different synthetic images, with 10,000 as a training and testing set and the other 10,000 as the final validation and scoring set. You will be graded on how well you can identify 38 unique objects in each scene by giving a confidence that it exists for each image. The final deadline for this project is currently set to midnight on April 17th, 2022. A PowerPoint with further explanation can be found in the ""Data"" tab under `Custom Image Object Detection.pptx`

[Join our Discord](https://discord.gg/zQUrvdKJbb) to ask any questions or join the club!
----------------------
# Evaluation

The scoring portion of the contest will be over the second half of the data provided, namely those with an ID between 10,001 and 20,000. A submission should be a `.csv` containing two columns: `Id` and `Predicted`. 

- `Id` is a concatenation of the the scene number, between 10,001 and 20,000 with the class number 1-43. This should be formatted as {scene_id}_{class_id}, ex. ""`00001_38`""
- `Predicted` should be your probabilistic belief that the class_id specified is in the scene_id specified (from the previous example, how likely it is that you think class 38 is in scene 00001). Your input for this confidence must be a valid probability, given as a float from 0-1. 

An example file is shown in the `sample_submission.csv` file provided.","# Dataset Description

data should contain images, overview.",True,True
