4798,logical-rhythm-2k21-cars24,"# Description

In an alternate reality, sid organized Lobster Games(a variant of squid games ). For the first challenge, 'Price is Right', participants have to guess the price of the car in front of them. If they fail to do so, they will be eliminated on spot i.e. ( shot in the head ). Deepanshu got his hands on this dataset and wants to develop a model to predict the price for him. Help Deepanshu to achieve the least RMSLE (Root Mean Squared Logarithmic Error).

This is a problem under Logical Rhythm 2k21, where you need to predict the price of cars using the 8 attributes provided to you. The competition evaluation metric is RMSLE. A detailed description of the evaluation metric can be found under the evaulation  tab.

All The best!!
----------------------
# Evaluation

# Metric

Submissions are evaluated on Root-Mean-Squared-Error (RMSE) between the logarithm of the predicted value and the logarithm of the observed sales price. Taking logs means that errors in predicting expensive houses and cheap houses will affect the result equally.

<p>The Root Mean Squared Log Error (RMSLE) can be defined using a slight modification </p> on sklearn's mean_squared_log_error function, which itself a modification on the familiar Mean Squared Error [MSE](https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/generated/sklearn.metrics.mean_squared_error.html) metric.

The formula for RMSLE is represented as follows:
\[ RMSLE = \sqrt{\frac{1}{n} \sum\_{i=1}^n (\log(p\_i + 1) - \log(a\_i+1))^2 }\]","# Dataset Description

The dataset contains 35 different car models from 7 different manufacturers

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

## Columns

*   **Car ID**: ID of the car.
*   **model_year**: The year in which the model was released
*   **maker**:  The name of the company manufacturing the car 
*   **model_name**: The name of the model.
*   **city**: Name of the city in which the listing was posted 
*   **distance_covered(km)**: Distance covered by the vehicle.
*   **fuel_type**: The type of fuel used by the car.
*   **transmission_type**: Whether the car is Automatic or Manual
*   **pre_owner**: Wether the owner is the first owner or not.
*   **price**: The listed price of the car. The target variable that need to be predicted.",True,True
4799,cisc873-dm-f21-a2,"# Description

### Speed Dating Match Prediction

<img src=""https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F4409738%2Fbfec644abd12b3ec43c8b06aa3d27653%2F5f0dd150ec40c.jpeg?generation=1602296689548875&alt=media"" width=300/>


In this assignment, we are going to predict the outcome of a specific speed dating session based on the profile of two people, so we can implement a recommendation system to better match people in speed dating events. We did have another online dating dataset (Tinder-like) but that one is super dirty which requires extensive cleaning, so we do this one instead. This is a binary classification task. Given a data sample (information about the dating session), we are going to predict the probability (0-1, float) that the dating session will lead to a successful match. 

The dataset is clean, but has a lot of missing values. The strategy for missing value replacement has to be tuned. Also, as you can guess, this dataset is highly unbalanced (mostly unmatched). The idea of this assignment is to treat the complete workflow from data preprocessing to model training as a single pipeline, and search for the hyperparameters for this pipeline. 
Enjoy! 


### Tasks:

?? Meme competition:
Include/find a MEME that you liked related to data science/data mining/machine learning

?? Understand the template:
Put detailed comment on each line of the code to show your understanding of the template (you can find it at the Data tab). Then describe: What is the experimental protocol used and how was it carried out? How did we tune hyper-parameters in the template? What is the search space and what is the criteria to determine good/bad hyper-parameters? 

?? Problem Formulation:
Define the problem. What is the input? What is the output? What data mining function is required? What could be the challenges?  What is the impact? What is an ideal solution?

?? Model Tuning and Documentation:
Now based on the template, try to improve the model's performance on the public leaderboard by following the data science life-cycle for tuning. You can try different features, different hyperparameters/configurations of the model, and even a different model. For each trial, document the reason why you want to make the certain change and the expected outcome, before running the code. Record the observed performance and your thought on it. Recall that we have a DS life-cycle and we should know what to do when underfit/overfit. The final result is not important, but the process is. Documentation of your thought process is very important, since most people forgot why they test certain model/hyperparameter after they got the result (it takes time). It also helps a lot when you got stuck. You can organize the notebook by listing 

- thoughts and observations for trial 0, plan for trial 1
- code for trial 1
- thoughts and observations for trial 1, plan for trial 2
- code for trial 2
...

You have to tune at least 10 times. All the tried solutions should be different (e.g. different feature sets/different preprocessing). The tried solutions should cover at least:
- one grid search trial
- one random search trial
- one bayesian search trial

?? Answer the questions below (**briefly**):

?? Why a simple linear regression model (without any activation function) is not good for classification task, compared to Perceptron/Logistic regression?
??What's a decision tree and how it is different to a logistic regression model?
??We discussed three variants of decision tree (single-tree) in our lecture, what are their differences?
??What is the difference between the random forest model, and a bagging ensemble of CART models?
??How is gradient boosting different to boosting (adaboost)?

### Deliverable:

?? A python notebook containing the documentation of how you reach the final design as well as the answers to the questions. 


### Marking:

?? 2 for technical soundness of the final script (whether you did things right e.g. not using ID as a feature, tuning ranges reasonable e.g. not a giant search space for grid search)
?? 4 for clarity, organization and soundness of the documentation (how & why you adjust your design to improve the performance) (example deduction: decision tree with observed high bias -> trim more, linear model with observed high bias in testing set -> more regularizations)
?? 2 for the clarity of your own code (all the code should have comments)
?? 2 your answers to the questions (deductions based on errors)

Note: Emoji allowed and encouraged so it will be more fun for us to grade it.

----------------------
# Evaluation

In this assignment, we will use AUROC as a metric on the predicted probability:

https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Receiver_operating_characteristic",# Dataset Description,True,True
4800,midsw251birds2021,"# Description

<h2>
    Welcome to MIDS W251 team! You¡¯re in the right place.
</h2>
<p>
    This is the legendary Bird Watch competition ¨C for you to dive into ML
    competitions and familiarize yourself improving deep learning models.
</p>
<p>
    The competition is simple: create a model that predicts which class of
    species a bird is.
</p>
<p>
    Once you¡¯re ready to start competing, click on the Join the Competition
button in the data page to create an account and gain access to the competition data. Then
    check out the <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/darraghdog/beat-the-bird-benchmark"">Beat the Benchmark</a> script in the code page for an examples
    how to make your first submission!
</p>
<h2>
    Overview of How Kaggle¡¯s Competitions Work
</h2>
<p>
    1. Join the Competition
</p>
<p>
    Read about the challenge description, accept the Competition Rules and gain
    access to the competition dataset.
</p>
<p>
    2. Get to Work
</p>
<p>
    Download the data, build models on it locally or on Kaggle Kernels (our
    no-setup, customizable Jupyter Notebooks environment with free GPUs) and
    generate a prediction file. See the Code page of the competition.
</p>
<p>
    3. Make a Submission
</p>
<p>
    Upload your prediction as a submission on Kaggle and receive an accuracy
    score. Alternatively you can create a kaggle script in the code section of the compeition and submit the output of that script to the competition. To do this, you must write your predictions out to a file, as seen in the <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/darraghdog/beat-the-bird-benchmark"">Beat the Benchmark</a> script, and save the script. This will run in the background, you will need to wait some time. Once finished, go to the output section of the script, select the output file, and press Submit. 
</p>
<p>
    4. Check the Leaderboard
</p>
<p>
    See how your model ranks against other Kagglers on our leaderboard.
</p>
<p>
    5. Improve Your Score
</p>
<h2>
    What Data Will I Use in This Competition?
</h2>
<p>
    In this competition, you¡¯ll gain access to two similar datasets which
    include image file. One dataset is titled `train.csv` and the other is
    titled `test.csv`.
</p>
<p>
    Train.csv will contain the filenames and will reveal the label of the bird
    species, also known as the ¡°ground truth¡±. The file metadata.csv can be
    used to see the real species name of each of the 270 labels.
</p>
<p>
    The `test.csv` dataset contains similar information but does not disclose
    the ¡°ground truth¡± for each passenger. It¡¯s your job to predict these
    outcomes.
</p>
<p>
    Using the patterns you find in the train.csv image data, predict whether
    the class of the test image files.
</p>
<p>
    Check out the Data tab to explore the datasets even further. Once you feel
    you¡¯ve created a competitive model, submit it to Kaggle to see where your
    model stands on our leaderboard against other Kagglers.
</p>
<h2>
    How to Submit your Prediction to Kaggle
</h2>
<p>
    Once you¡¯re ready to make a submission and get on the leaderboard:
</p>
<p>
    1. Click on the ¡°Submit Predictions¡± button
</p>
<p>
    2. Upload a CSV file in the submission file format. You¡¯re able to submit 5
    submissions a day.
</p>
<p>
    Submission File Format:
</p>
<p>
    For every image in the test dataset, submission files should contain two
    columns: `filename` and `label`. Filename should be a string and label
    should be an integer between 0 and 269.
</p>
<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>filename,label<br>test/6fbed82c07cd.jpg,0<br>test/d8399f767c45.jpg,0<br>...<br></pre>
<h2>
    A Last Word on Kaggle Notebooks
</h2>
<p>
    As we mentioned before, Kaggle Notebooks is our no-setup, customizable,
    Jupyter Notebooks environment with free GPUs and a huge repository of
    community published data &amp;amp; code.
</p>
<p>
    In every competition, you¡¯ll find many Kernels publically shared with
    incredible insights. It¡¯s an invaluable resource worth becoming familiar
with. Check out this competition¡¯s Kernel¡¯s    <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/darraghdog/beat-the-benchmark"">here</a>.
</p>
<p>
    To create a notebook, go to the Code page and create a new notebook. Make
    sure to set GPU as your accelerator once created. Each person has
    approximately 30 hours of GPU compute time each week, I believe it resets
    on Fridays.
</p>
----------------------
# Evaluation

<h2>Goal</h2>
It is your job to predict the `label`, or species, of each image in the test dataset.
For each image in the test set, you must predict a 0 to 269 value for the label, where 270 is the number of bird species. The bird species can be seen in the `metadata.csv` file on the data tab.<p></p>
<p>The evaluation metric will be multi class accuracy, where discrete class labels must be predicted.</p>


<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p><strong>For every image in the test dataset</strong>, submission files should contain two columns: `filename` and `label`. Filename should be a string and label should be an integer between 0 and 269.</p>
<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>filename,label<br>test/6fbed82c07cd.jpg,0<br>test/d8399f767c45.jpg,0<br>...<br></pre>","# Dataset Description

<p>You can use the train.csv and all images in the dataset to score as high as you can on the leaderboard. Below are descriptions of the data provided. </p>

<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>train.csv</strong> - the training set, including labels</li>
<li><strong>test.csv</strong> - the test set without labels</li>
<li><strong>sample_submission.csv</strong> - a sample submission file in the correct format</li>
<li><strong>metadata.csv</strong> - the actual classes of each label</li>
</ul>",True,True
4801,decision-trees-2021,"# Description

In this competition, you will predict whether or not someone has a cardiovascular disease given info about their health. You can use either a decision tree or a random forest.

Don't use any libraries like scikit learn. You should code the decision tree algorithm yourself. You can use numpy and pandas for simple computation and data IO if you wish. However, it is entirely possible to do this competition without any libraries.

You can find shell code at https://tjmachinelearning.com/schedule/2021

For some more decision trees resources, check out a learning resources guide we compiled for the National Machine Learning Open competition we hosted last summer (https://docs.google.com/document/d/1VoxJVC6Vv13XN2XuJFd5ZMcaCBCCJnjYHvQaxpjfuRE/edit?usp=sharing)

As always, if you are still confused on how to get started there are lots of great decision tree tutorials out there (for example https://machinelearningmastery.com/implement-decision-tree-algorithm-scratch-python/), and you can always reach out to any of the officers or email tjmachinelearning@gmail.com for help
----------------------
# Evaluation

We will be using categorization accuracy to score submissions (percentage of correct classifications)","# Dataset Description

<h1>Data</h1>
_________________________
<strong>The features you will receive are:</strong>

age (in number of days)

gender (1 for women, 2 for men)

height (in cm)

weight (in kg)

aphi (systolic blood pressure)

aplo (diastolic blood pressure)

cholesterol (1 for normal, 2 for above normal, 3 for well above normal)

gluc (glucose levels, 1 for normal, 2 for above normal, 3 for well above normal)

smoke (whether or not the individual smokes)

alco (whether or not the person consumes alcohol, 0 or 1)

active (whether person engages in some form of physical activity)

_________________________________

<strong>The target (what you will be trying to predict):</strong>

cardio (whether or not the individual has a cardiovascular disease)

_____________________________
<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>train.csv</strong> - the training set</li>
<li><strong>test.csv</strong> - the test set</li>
<li><strong>sample.csv</strong> - a sample submission file in the correct format</li>
</ul>",True,True
4802,redesunicen2021,"# Description

Bienvenidos a la competencia amistosa de clasificaci¨®n de m¨²sica para la cursada de Introducci¨®n a las Redes Neuronales y Aprendizaje Profundo del 2021. En esta competencia el objetivo es clasificar distintos segmentos de audio en una de 8 clases balanceadas. El conjunto de datos es una versi¨®n preprocesada de [FMA: A Dataset For Music Analysis]( https://arxiv.org/abs/1612.01840) en su versi¨®n small. 
Los datos fueron procesados para generar Mel-frequency cepstral coefficients (MFCC). El ceptral puede ser visto como un espectrograma del espectrograma. Captura las diferentes frecuencias y la potencia en distintos momentos de tiempo. Es espectrograma fue generado de la siguiente forma:

```
y, sr = librosa.load(""musica.mp3"")
spect = librosa.feature.melspectrogram(y=y, sr=sr,n_fft=2048, hop_length=512)
spect = librosa.power_to_db(spect, ref=np.max)
```

La m¨¦trica objetivo es categorical accuracy. El conjunto de datos de test est¨¢ dividido en dos 20% para calcular la m¨¦trica objetivo mientras dure el evento y 80% para calcular la m¨¦trica al finalizar. En el leaderboard hay dos benchmarks: el random y el obtenido con el modelo desarrollado en la notebook de sample. Random es simplemente asignar aleatoriamente una clase a cada ejemplo. Cada equipo puede enviar 2 predicciones por d¨ªa. 

Al finalizar la competencia cada equipo deber¨¢ entregar el c¨®digo y el modelo que utilizaron para generar las predicciones. Adem¨¢s, se debe entregar un breve informe describiendo que arquitecturas se evaluaron y cu¨¢les son las conclusiones del trabajo.","# Dataset Description

# Archivos
* train.csv: contiene los track_id y labels para el conjunto de entrenamiento.
* test.csv: contiene los track_id para el test en la plataforma.
* music.npz: contienen los MFCC de todas las canci¨®nes. Tiene por clave el track_id.
* processed.npz: MFCC ordenados como en el notebook de ejemplo. Sus claves son:
    + x_train: un arreglo numpy de 3 dimensiones con los MFCC de las canciones de entrenamiento ordenadas como est¨¢n ordenadas en el train.csv.
    + y_train: los labels para las canciones.
    + x_test: un arreglo numpy de 3 dimensiones con los MFCC de las canciones de testing ordenadas como est¨¢n ordenadas en el test.csv.
* extra_features.csv: caracter¨ªsticas adicionales para las canciones (opcional).
* sample.csv: Ejemplo de como se debe formatear el archivo a enviar (benchmark random).",True,True
4803,caltech-256-image-classification,"# Description

## Objective: 

To classify images in the Caltech-256 dataset, which is an improvement over Caltech-101 dataset using a Convolutional Neural Network.

## Problem Statement

To build and implement a Convolutional Neural Network model to classify images in the Caltech-256 dataset.

At the end of this competition, you will be able to:

1. Load and extract features of images available in the Caltech-256 dataset using ImageDataGenerator
2. Build convolutional neural networks using either Keras or PyTorch deep learning libraries
3. Use the pre-trained models using either Keras or PyTorch deep learning libraries

## Description: 

Caltech-256 is an object recognition dataset containing approximately 30,000 real-world images, of different sizes, spanning 256 classes (256 object classes and an additional clutter class). Each class is represented by at least 80 images. The dataset is a superset of the Caltech-101 dataset.

Here is a handy link to Kaggle's competition documentation (https://www.kaggle.com/docs/competitions), which includes, among other things, instructions on submitting predictions (https://www.kaggle.com/docs/competitions#making-a-submission).
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation pages describes how submissions will be scored and how students should format their submissions.

The evaluation metric for this competition is Categorization Accuracy.

The file should contain a header and have the following format:

img_path, label
test/1.jpg, Faces-Easy-101","# Dataset Description

## Context
The Caltech 256 is considered an improvement to its predecessor, the Caltech 101 dataset, with new features such as larger category sizes, new and larger clutter categories, and overall increased difficulty. This is a great dataset to train models for visual recognition: How can we recognize toasters, cell phones, skyscrapers, sail boats, speed boats and many other categories in cluttered pictures? How can we classify images in these categories?

## Content
There are 30,607 images in this dataset spanning 256 object categories. Object categories are extremely diverse, ranging from vehicles to computer monitor to tuning fork to watch to skyscraper to guitar. 

## Acknowledgements
Original data source and banner image: http://www.vision.caltech.edu/Image_Datasets/Caltech256/

When using this dataset, please remember to cite:

Griffin, G. Holub, AD. Perona, P.
The Caltech 256.
Caltech Technical Report.

## Inspiration
Can you build a model that classifies images in the Caltech-256 dataset into their respective categories?
For example, by taking input as an image, can your deep learning model (a Convolutional Neural Network ) classify whether the object is a ostrich, fried egg, leopard, skyscraper, penguin, or one of the other 251 object categories?

## Files

*   **Caltech 256_Test** - the test set
*   **Caltech 256_Train** - the training set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format


## Columns of the sample submission file

*   `img_path` - path of the image
*   `label`?- category/class of the object/image",True,True
