4822,cs470-mvtec,"# Description

# Kaggle AD Challenge: Unsupervised Image Anomaly Detection Competition
## Project Goals:
- Students compete at Kaggle, a predictive model and analysis contest platform, and have hands-on experience and acquire deep learning knowledge through solving real-world image anomaly detection problem. Competition will be done by performing anomaly segmentation task for one category of MVTec AD image dataset. Evaluate project by measuring segmentation pixelwise AUROC(area under a receiver operating characteristic curve) performance.

## Dataset and Experimental Environment:
- Dataset: MVTec AD is a dataset for benchmarking anomaly detection methods with a focus on industrial inspection. It contains over 5000 high-resolution images divided into fifteen different object and texture categories. Each category comprises a set of defect-free training images and a test set of images with various kinds of defects as well as images without defects. In this competition, we compete using only one category (capsule) of the MVtec AD dataset. There is no anomaly in the train dataset.
- Experimental Environment:  Kaggle InClass competition, IPython notebook. Pytorch. Code for data loader and evaluation function will be provided.

----------------------
# Evaluation

Evaluate project by measuring Segmentation pixelwise AUROC(area under a receiver operating characteristic curve) performance. The Area Under the Curve (AUC) is the measure of the ability of a classifier to distinguish between classes and is used as a summary of the ROC curve. The higher the AUC, the better the performance of the model at distinguishing between the positive and negative classes.

Read more here:

[AUC-ROC Curve in Machine Learning Clearly Explained](https://www.analyticsvidhya.com/blog/2020/06/auc-roc-curve-machine-learning/)

[ML Crash course](https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course/classification/roc-and-auc)

[Understanding AUCROC](https://towardsdatascience.com/understanding-auc-roc-curve-68b2303cc9c5)

[Lets learn about AUCROC article](https://medium.com/greyatom/lets-learn-about-auc-roc-curve-4a94b4d88152)
----------------------
# Prizes

- Pass - If your score is better than TA's
- Fail - Otherwise","# Dataset Description

# Context
Automatic detection of the defect with no detectives samples often requires detecting them in Industrial anomaly detection
This dataset is for educational purposes

# Acknowledgements
Based on the public dataset by the MVTec company
Paul Bergmann, Michael Fauser, David Sattlegger, Carsten Steger. MVTec AD - A Comprehensive Real-World Dataset for Unsupervised Anomaly Detection; in: IEEE Conference on Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (CVPR), June 2019

# Inspiration
The main goal of this dataset is to explore self-supervised learning on capsule images in order to solve anomaly detection problems.",True,True
4823,multilingualabusivecomment,"# Description

## Overview
Multilingual Abusive Comment Detection Challenge is an innovative new challenge towards combating abusive comments on Moj, one of India's largest short-video apps in multiple regional languages. We invite submissions to this challenge where the goal is to develop AI solutions for predicting abusive comments posted on the Moj app in 10+ languages given natural language data along with contextual user data. Key novelties around this dataset include:

- Massive & Multilungual: 10+ low-resource Indic languages

- Human annotated

- Metadata for each comment (Eg. #likes, #reports, etc.)

**Note: All the teams are requested to fill this [form](https://docs.google.com/forms/d/1NHdJKa5AEsuFGtuJV2PaeEimjU0nz_TaYWKz2nkDZEw/edit?ts=616ec046) to be eligible for prizes** (ignore, if already filled).


We encourage participants to carry out discussions [here](https://www.kaggle.com/c/multilingualabusivecomment/discussion) for this challenge.

## Acknowledgements

We thank Moj and ShareChat for providing this dataset.
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is [Mean F1-Score](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/F-score). The F1 score, commonly used in information retrieval, measures accuracy using the statistics precision \( \text{p} \) and recall \( \text{r} \).

Precision is the ratio of true positives (\text{tp}) to all predicted positives \( \text{tp} + \text{fp} \). Recall is the ratio of true positives \( \text{tp} \) to all actual positives (\text{tp} + \text{fn}). The F1 score is given by:

\[ \text{F1} = 2\frac{\text{p} \cdot \text{r}}{\text{p}+\text{r}}\ \ \mathrm{where}\ \ \text{p} = \frac{\text{tp}}{\text{tp}+\text{fp}},\ \ \text{r} = \frac{\text{tp}}{\text{tp}+\text{fn}} \]

The F1 metric weights recall and precision equally, and a good retrieval algorithm will maximize both precision and recall simultaneously. Thus, moderately good performance on both will be favored over extremely good performance on one and poor performance on the other.

## Submission Format

For every comment in the test set, submission files should be in CSV format. It should contain two columns: `CommentId` and `Label`. The `CommentId` is just line number of the corresponding test instance which will be released when you register. `Label` 1 indicates *Abusive Comment*, and 0 indicates *Non-abusive comment*. 


The file should contain a header and have the following format:
```
CommentId,Label
0,0
1,0
2,1
...
```","# Dataset Description

## Dataset Statistics:

### Training Data Size: 1500898 (`ShareChat-IndoML-Datathon-NSFW-CommentChallenge_Train.csv`)

- No. of Non-abusive Comments: 1148019
 
- No. of Abusive Comments: 352879

### Features/Columns:

1. `commentId`: Comment Identifier

2. `commentText`: comment text (as a string)

3. `language`: Language of the post on which this comment was made. (*You might use this as a weak indicator of the language.*)

4. `user_index`: Unique user identifier

5. `post_index`: Unique post identifier having this comment

6. `report_count_comment`: Number of times this comment has been reported

7. `report_count_post`: Total number of reports on all comments of this post

8. `like_count_comment`: Number of likes on the comment

9. `like_count_post`: Total number of likes on all comments of this post

10. `label`: Label to predict. 0-Non-abusive; 1-Abusive



### Sample Test/Validation Data Size: 641037 (+1 header) (`ShareChat-IndoML-Datathon-NSFW-CommentChallenge_Test_NoLabel.csv`)",True,True
