# Evaluation

The evaluation pages describes how submissions will be scored and how students should format their submissions. You don't need a sub-title at the top; the page title appears above. For an example of a high quality evaluation page, see the 2013 KDD Cup page, which we have included below:

---

The evaluation metric for this competition is [Mean F1-Score](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/F-score). The F1 score, commonly used in information retrieval, measures accuracy using the statistics precision \( \text{p} \) and recall \( \text{r} \).
Precision is the ratio of true positives \(\text{tp}\) to all predicted positives \(\text{tp} + \text{fp}\). Recall is the ratio of true positives \(\text{tp}\) to all actual positives \(\text{tp} + \text{fn}\). The F1 score is given by:

\[ \text{F1} = 2\frac{\text{p} \cdot \text{r}}{\text{p}+\text{r}}\ \ \mathrm{where}\ \ \text{p} = \frac{\text{tp}}{\text{tp}+\text{fp}},\ \ \text{r} = \frac{\text{tp}}{\text{tp}+\text{fn}} \]

The F1 metric weights recall and precision equally, and a good retrieval algorithm will maximize both precision and recall simultaneously. Thus, moderately good performance on both will be favored over extremely good performance on one and poor performance on the other.

## Submission Format

**For every author in the dataset**, submission files should contain two columns: `AuthorId` and `DuplicateAuthorIds`. `DuplicateAuthorIds` should be a space-delimited list. Every `AuthorId` counts as its own duplicate, and every duplicate should be listed under each of its respective ids. For example, if you suspect author A, B, and C are the same, you should list `A,A B C`, `B,B A C`, `C,C A B`.


The file should contain a header and have the following format:
```
AuthorId,DuplicateAuthorIds
1,1
```",# Dataset Description,True,True
4825,utkonos-baskets,"# Description

![](https://intranet.utkonos.ru/images/intranet/2021/11/17/141203.png)

## Utkonos Baskets Competition

Utkonos is a well-known company in the Russian e-grocery market. To help our clients navigate a wide range of products, we develop various recommendation systems that we embed into our interfaces.

In this competition, we propose to develop a recommendation model, the purpose of which is to help collecting a grocery basket. We have historical data on already collected baskets for a certain period. Each basket is a set of products, represented by their ids.

Let's assume that a customer has already added a certain number of products to the basket. Your goal is to offer them some  relevant products that can be added to the current basket.


----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is **Precision@10**. 

The test set was created by removing a random product from a full basket. For each such basket, you should propose 10 products, which may complement this basket. If one of the proposed products coincides with the removed product, you get 0.1, if not - you get 0. The final score is the mean across all the test baskets.","# Dataset Description

## Files

**train.csv** contains one column `basket` , which is a string of comma separated product ids.

**test.csv** contains two columns: `id` with basket ids for evaluation, and `basket` with a string of comma separated product ids.

We also provide submission.csv containing following columns:

* `id` - basket id corresponding to **test.csv** file
*  `product` - id of the proposed product
*  `rank` - the rank of the product (not considered in evaluation)
*  `iteration`, `similarity`, `runid` - technical columns needed for the evaluation system.
 
Basically, you only need to replace the values in column `product` with your suggested products and leave other columns as is.",True,True
4826,stat4706-fall21-group-project,"# Description

## Group size

2 to 5 students (unless otherwise specified by instructor)

## Important Date

A first draft may be turned in by **November 11** through Canvas. This will be returned (ungraded) with comments to help you improve your project submission. 

Final projects (which will be graded) need to be turned in on **Tuesday December 7 by 11:59 pm**. 

## Analysis

You will need to conduct different analyses using R or other softwares to answers the following questions:

1. Data visualization to gain three conclusions from the data. Please elaborate your conclusion with descriptive statistics, a graphical measure, and hypothesis testing. (e.g. Is there a significant difference in price for the same model with different transmission?)

2. Construct a prediction linear regression model for the car sale price. The prediction accuracy could be verified by submitting your solution to Kaggle. (Refer to evaluation page for further instructions.)

3. Based on your analysis, what suggestions will you give for dealers and consumers? For dealers, they are interested in developing a smart pricing strategy to make more profits. For customers, they want a bargain. 

## Evaluation Metric

The evaluation metric for this competition is R-Square (Coefficient of Determinant). The higher the prediction accuracy, the higher the R-square value.

## Submission Format

The submission data should be a csv file with 2 columns and 1001 rows. The first column is **Id**, the second column is **Price**. ID column maps the predicted price to the *test_data.csv*.

## Further help

If you need additional inspiration, check out these existing high-quality tasks:

1. Predict the Spreading of Coronavirus. ([Link](https://www.kaggle.com/sudalairajkumar/novel-corona-virus-2019-dataset/tasks?taskId=508))

2. Top 50 Spotify Songs 2019. ([Link](https://www.kaggle.com/leonardopena/top50spotify2019/tasks?taskId=198))

## Acknowledgment

We would like to thank Aditya for the 100,000 scraped used car listing data. More details check this [link](https://www.kaggle.com/adityadesai13/used-car-dataset-ford-and-mercedes/metadata).","# Dataset Description

## What files do I need to fit the model?

train_data.csv contains the listing price to fit the prediction linear regression model.

## What should I expect the data format to be?

Data for training, testing and submission should be in csv format.

## What am I predicting?

Listing Price for used cars. 

## How to deal with overfitting & underfitting?

test_data.csv removes the price column, so you could test your model prediction accuracy on the test set. 

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

## Columns

*   `model` - BMW car model
*   `year`?- registration year
*   `price` - price in EUR
*   `transmission` - type of gearbox
*   `mileage` - distance used
*   `fuelType` - engine fuel
*   `tax` - road tax
*   `mpg` - miles per gallon
*   `engineSize` - size in liters",True,True
