4852,stat6031-fall-2021-class-competition,"# Description

This is the home page of the in-class competition for STAT 6031 Applied Regression Analysis in Fall 2021 at University of Cincinnati. Students can work on this project in teams, but each team should consist of NO MORE THAN 3 members. Note that students are strongly encouraged to form teams on this project. If a student would like to work on this project on his/her own, please contact the instructor for approval. 

This dataset describes the listing activity in Hawaii, USA in September 2021.

## Dependent Variable
The dependent variable is:
```
price
```

That is, you will always predicting the ""price"", i.e., price  per night, though there are problems the dataset can help answer.

## Contents:
This dataset includes a lot of information about host, reviews, and the properties.  The variable names in the dataset are self-explanatory. You are encouraged to try the following combinations of predictors and compare the performance of the resulting models:

1. Numeric predictors
2. Qualitative predictors
3. All predictors
4. Any predictors: You can also construct new predictors using those in the data, and use all or some of these predictors. The sky is the limit!

There are several **required** components in this project:

- Model selection: Please carefully document the models you have tried and how you decide between them.
- Prediction competition: You are given both a training and testing dataset. You can submit your predictions on the test dataset to Kaggle and compete with others in terms of root mean squared error (RMSE).
- Model interpretation: Interpret the final model you choose, and produce visualizations for diagnostics.
- Linear regression: You are required to build a regression model that give the RMSE at the test dataset as good as you can.

_You can later build and apply other type of models such as LASSO, CNN, XGBOOST, but the project score will be based on the regression model you submitted and presented. With this said, if you are able to try another two additional models, you can get up to 20 extra points for your project._

## Deliverables

All the following components are required. 

- **Kaggle Submission**: Each team should submit their predictions at least once.
- **Code**: You should submit your code and make sure all your results are **reproducible**! I will setup OneDrive folder for you to submit your code. Without reproducible code submitted, 0 point will be assigned to the team. 
- **Presentation**: Each team will present its results in the last week of the fall semester. The presentation is expected to be 15min with 3min for Q&A. You can (but not required) use Google Slides to prepare slides for easy collaboration. The slides should be sent to the Instructor before the presentation date.
- **Report**: Each team must submit to gradescope a 5-10 page report on this project. This report should be self-contained. You should explain your team's attempts at building models and model selection. You can (but not required) use Google Docs (or Overleaf if you use Latex) for easy collaboration. The report should be sent to the Instructor before deadline. For teamwork, the team should also include a cover page with description on each member's contributions and with all members' signatures. Without the cover page, the report will be assigned 0 point.

_After the final exam, the code, presentations, and reports will later be posted on Canvas for a couple of weeks for the class to share their code/findings._


----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated on root mean-squared prediction error for the total income.


## Submission Format

The submission file only contains 2 columns. The first is the index (id), and the remaining columns need to be filled in with your predictions (price).


The file should contain a header and have the following format:
```
id, price
873182,36
```","# Dataset Description

## File descriptions
- **airbnb_train.csv** - the training set; with _price_
- **airbnb_test.csv** - the test set; without _price_
- **airbnb_sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format


## Data fields
The dataset contains information about over 3,000 Airbnb listings in Washington DC, USA in September 2022.  The CSV file contains self- explanatory columns, and all values in the dataset are also self-explanatory. 

The column of _price_ will play a role of your dependent variable. You can use it directly as your dependent variable or choose to perform some transformation. However, when you upload your submission, you need to provide predictions of _price_ **without** transformation.",True,True
