4869,gt-cse6250-fall-2021-hw5,"# Description

<p>A gatech.edu email address is required to participate.</p>
<p>Follow the sign-up direction using your GT email address, or if you already have an account, change the email on your existing account via your profile settings so that you can participate. Make sure your display name (not necessarily your username) matches your GT account username so that your Kaggle submission can be linked to the grading system.

In the homework5.pdf, we ask you to improve the performance of your RNN model with variable-length sequences on mortality prediction. In this challenge, you will submit your my_predictions.csv to compete with your classmates.

You will be required to submit the predicted labels for the test patients. After you submit, your accuracy on 50% of the test dataset will be visible to the class on the public leaderboard. You can use this accuracy as a sanity check to make sure your classifier is performing as intended and improve your model.</p>
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:<br>
<br>
SUBJECT_ID, MORTALITY<br>
1, 0.5432<br>
8, 0.9123<br>
9, 0.1251<br>
10, 0.3253<br>
etc.</p>","# Dataset Description

<p>Use the data files provided in our Homework 5. Below gives a sample submission file.</p>
<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>kaggle_sample_solution.csv</strong> - a sample submission file in the correct format</li>
</ul>
<h2>Data fields</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>SUBJECT_ID</strong> - an anonymous id unique to a given patient</li>
<li><strong>MORTALITY</strong> - a predicted probability of mortality</li>
</ul>",True,True
4870,emory,"# Description

IMPORTANT:
You must also sign up through the Google form to be considered a participant for this challenge.
link: https://forms.gle/kMinDQ8wrGtQP6Ci7

Professor Rajiv Garg, Goizueta Data Analytics Club, and Information Systems and Operations Management Area proudly present you¡ªAnalysts@Emory! Analysts@Emory data analytics challenge is created to get people excited about coding and analytics, and gaining an opportunity to participate in a friendly data analytics challenge at Emory University!

The goal of the challenge is to utilize the provided datasets to complete the following tasks:
- Build a prediction model using python (70% of score based on performance matrix)
- Create visualizations and insights report from the data (20% of score) to explain prediction model (max 4 double spaced pages)
- Present the finding in a 10-minute in-person presentation during first week of December (10%)

To sum up, your goal is to build a model that predicts the price change of Bitcoin in the next hour. You will create a binary variable (your target prediction) that is either 0 or 1. Where ""0"" means price drops by 0.01% or more, while 1 means price grows by 0.01% or more.

Datasets: cryptocurrency (BTC, ETH, USDT, DOGE, SHIBU) price and volume information from coinmarketcap.com, which can be accessed through data page. For predictive model, you are allowed to use only the data provided. For the insights report, you can bring in additional data to explain the changes in prices - please cite the data sources and plan to share a link to the data.


For questions please contact any of the owners: 
1. Pandora Shou - ( pandora.shou@emory.edu )
2. Shivam Bajaj - ( shivam.bajaj@emory.edu )
3. Shuang Wu - ( shuang.wu@emory.edu )
4. Yuyao Wang - ( yuyao.wang@emory.edu )
----------------------
# Evaluation

Your delivery will be graded based on the following criteria:
1. model performance based on F-measure (70%)
2. data visualization &amp; insights (20%)
3. presentation (10%)

---
**Model performance based on F-measure**

The evaluation metric for this competition is [Mean F1-Score](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/F-score). The F1 score, commonly used in information retrieval, measures accuracy using the statistics precision and recall

Precision is the ratio of true positives to all predicted positives. Recall is the ratio of true positives to all actual positives. The F1 score is given by:

![](https://www.gstatic.com/education/formulas2/397133473/en/f1_score.svg)

The F1 metric weights recall and precision equally, and a good retrieval algorithm will maximize both precision and recall simultaneously. Thus, moderately good performance on both will be favored over extremely good performance on one and poor performance on the other.


**Data Visualization**

The grading over data visualization is based on the data ink used (the most information in the least visual elements) and overall aesthetic. 

**Presentation**
The grading over presentation is based on effective organization of slides and clarity of communication. 

----------------------
# Timeline

November 2, 2021 -- start time
Dec 5, 2021 -- Model & PPT Submission Deadline
Dec 6, 2021  -  Dec 10, 2021  -- presentation week, available time spots will be released soon


All deadlines are at 11:59 PM ET on the corresponding day unless otherwise noted. The competition organizers reserve the right to update the contest timeline if they deem it necessary.

----------------------
# Prizes

Special thanks for sponsorship from Professor Garg! 
For the contest this year, we are giving out trophies to every member inside the team. Also, the winners will get a secret surprise gift ?? 
The awards will be presented to the following winning teams:

- Highest scoring (winner) undergraduate team
- Second-highest scoring (runners up) undergraduate team
- Highest scoring (winner) graduate team
- Second-highest scoring (runners up) graduate team

----------------------
# Requirements

Team Members:
1. everyone inside your team should be Emory students
2. your team has 1-3 members. While single players are allowed, we strongly recommend you to build team of 2-3 students. There will be a lot of work, and it will be beneficial for you to divide the task with a friend or just diversify the ideas.
3. if your team has a graduate student, your team will be automatically categorized as the graduate student team

Coding Language: python
----------------------
# Submission

You can make as many submissions as you like, but we will only consider your latest submission before the deadline of December 5th.

If you are submitting as a team, you do NOT need to merge within the Kaggle platform (it's actually disabled), but all team members must be listed as collaborators on the submitted Notebook, and all team members must accept the competition rules before the submission deadline.

A valid submission will include a Notebook Analysis. All notebooks submitted must be made public on or before the submission deadline to be eligible. If submitting as a team, all team members must be listed as collaborators on all notebooks submitted.

If you encounter trouble submitting the files on Kaggle, please send the files to Shuang.wu@emory.edu or shivam.bajaj@emory.edu","# Dataset Description

Coding language: Python
Note: You don't have to use Python for visualization

Datasets: cryptocurrency (BTC, ETH, USDT, DOGE, SHIBA INU) price and volume information from coinmarketcap.com, the prices of other cryptocurrencies can give you insight on market changes.
Goal: predict the price of BTC in the next hour where 0=price drops by 0.02% or 1=price goes up by 0.02%

REMEMBER that no one can predict bitcoin change at 100% accuracy. The goal is to make the accuracy as high as possible.

Participants should be able to answer these types of questions after reading the data description:
- What am I predicting?  
- What variables are available for prediction?
- What level of granularity is there in the data?

The dataset has tracked and produced files for hourly time series pricing data for the crypto market. Each file is in CSV format and can be imported automatically by Python scripts or other automated processes. 

In each file, you will find the following columns. This OHLC (Open/High/Low/Close) pricing data is taken from the daily exchanges.

Unix Timestamp - Also known as ""Epoch Time"". You can convert it to your local time.
Date - This timestamp is in UTC(Universal Time Coordinated) Time Zone
Symbol - The symbol for which the time series data refers
Open - The opening price of the time period
High - The highest price of the time period
Low The lowest price of the time period
Close - The closing price of the time period
Volume (Crypto) - The volume in the transacted Ccy. Ie. For BTC/USDT, this is in BTC amount
Volume Base Ccy - The volume in the base/converted ccy. Ie. For BTC/USDT, this is in USDT amount
Trade Count - The unique number of trades for the given time period",True,True
