4873,prml-data-contest-jul-2021-rb-section,"# Description

                                        
![](https://blog.cimbali.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2016/07/o-HUMAN-GENE-SUPREME-COURT-facebook.jpg)

<br> 
## **Machine Learning on Medical Data** <br>

Hello everyone! Welcome to the Data Contest module of your PRML course. <br>

Your task is to build supervised models to predict each of the six clinical descriptors using gene expression data of patients with neurodegenerative diseases. The dataset is taken from two separate studies, and the model development should be done separately for each dataset. All six descriptors are binary and related to the disease status or treatment outcomes.
<br>

Dataset I consists of 130 samples and dataset II consists of 340 samples for training the classifiers. Dataset I includes 2 clinical descriptors and 4 descriptors in dataset II. As the dataset comes from a clinical setting, take the data characteristics into account while modeling. <br>

Use the data in creative ways to build six classifiers for predicting each of the six clinical descriptors. You are free to choose any feature-selection/ensemble/non-deep learning models for classification, but make sure to give a brief description of them in the write-up.

----------------------
# Evaluation


## Evaluation Metric

The evaluation metric for this competition is the Matthews correlation coefficient (MCC).

![](https://miro.medium.com/max/3000/1*8E2rPn_ccOqGuPYj1gBTAg.png)


## Submission Format

Your submission must concatenate the predictions for each endpoint in the order CO1, CO2, CO3, CO4, CO5, CO6. Each line in your submission must be a comma-separated list of two values, id (starting from 0) and prediction. Below are the id numbers to be followed for each prediction.

Endpoint: Id                
CO1:        0 - 99                
CO2: 100 - 199           
CO3: 200 - 413          
CO4: 414 - 627          
CO5: 628 - 841          
CO6: 842 - 1055        


The order of the predictions should follow the order of the samples in the test.csv files.


Check the dummy_submission.csv file for a template.","# Dataset Description

There are four files available for this contest. 


---
## Dataset_1_Training.csv 

Dataset_1_Training.csv has rows corresponding to the gene expression of a gene for each sample and the last two rows correspond to the endpoints CO1 and CO2. 

- CO1 and CO2 takes binary values

---
## Dataset_1_Testing.csv 

same as Dataset_1_Training.csv except leaving the last two rows

- Use the Dataset_1_Training.csv file for training the model (could use a different model for each endpoint) and then use the trained model to predict the endpoints for the samples in Dataset_1_Testing.csv 

---

## Dataset_2_Training.csv 

Dataset_2_Training.csv has rows corresponding to the gene expression of a gene for each sample and the last four rows correspond to the endpoints CO3, CO4, CO5, and CO6. 

- CO3, CO4, CO5, and CO6 takes binary values

---
## Dataset_2_Testing.csv 

same as Dataset_2_Training.csv except leaving the last four rows

- Use the Dataset_2_Training.csv file for training the model (could use a different model for each endpoint) and then use the trained model to predict the endpoints for the samples in Dataset_2_Testing.csv 

---
dummy_submission.csv

This is an example of the expected Output file for submission on Kaggle.

Each line in your submission must be a comma-separated list of two values, a line number (starting from 0), and a prediction for the endpoints concatenated in the order CO1, CO2, CO3, CO4, CO5, and CO6. 

Check the dummy_submission.csv file for a template.",True,True
4874,jaw,"# Description

Given various Information related to a customer¡¯s behavior in online shopping websites,
we want to create a model that can predict whether that customer will generate revenue for the website's company or not.
It is therefore a binary classifcation problem.
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation pages describes how submissions will be scored and how students should format their submissions. You don't need a sub-title at the top; the page title appears above. For an example of a high quality evaluation page, see the 2013 KDD Cup page, which we have included below:

---

The evaluation metric for this competition is [Mean F1-Score](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/F-score). The F1 score, commonly used in information retrieval, measures accuracy using the statistics precision \( \text{p} \) and recall \( \text{r} \).

Precision is the ratio of true positives \(\text{tp}\) to all predicted positives \(\text{tp} + \text{fp}\). Recall is the ratio of true positives \(\text{tp}\) to all actual positives \(\text{tp} + \text{fn}\). The F1 score is given by:

\[ \text{F1} = 2\frac{\text{p} \cdot \text{r}}{\text{p}+\text{r}}\ \ \mathrm{where}\ \ \text{p} = \frac{\text{tp}}{\text{tp}+\text{fp}},\ \ \text{r} = \frac{\text{tp}}{\text{tp}+\text{fn}} \]

The F1 metric weights recall and precision equally, and a good retrieval algorithm will maximize both precision and recall simultaneously. Thus, moderately good performance on both will be favored over extremely good performance on one and poor performance on the other.

## Submission Format
check sample.Csv in the data tab","# Dataset Description

**Information about the dataset:**
The dataset consists of 12,330 entries, each one representing a session (belonging to a user)
in a 1-year period to avoid any tendency to a specific campaign, special day, user profile, or
period. The dataset and the information about it are taken from Kaggle. However, changes
may have been applied.

**Information about the attributes:**
The dataset consists of 10 numerical and 8 categorical attributes.  

- **Revenue**: the class label.
- **Administrative:** the number of the administrative pages visited by the user in
that session.
- **Administrative Duration:** the total time spent by the user in administrative
pages
- **Informational:** the number of the informational pages visited by the user in that
session.
- **Informational Duration:** the total time spent by the user in informational
pages.
- **Product Related:** the number of the product related pages visited by the user
in that session.
- **Product Related Duration:** the total time spent by the user in product related
pages.
- **Bounce Rate (of a web page):** the percentage of users that entered and left
(bounced) the website through this same webpage
- **Exit Rate (of a web page):** the percentage of pageviews for this web page that
were the last in the session
- **Page Value (of a web page):** the average value for a web page that a user visited
before completing an e-commerce transaction
- **Special Day:** the closeness of the visiting date to a special day (like Mother¡¯s
Day, Valentine's Day). The values are between 0 and 1, 0 being far from any
special day and 1 being closest to a special day.
- **operating system:**
- **browser:** the operating system used
- **region:** the region of the user
- **traffic type:** the traffic type
-** visitor type:** the visitor type (new visitor or returning visitor
- **weekend:** whether the date of the visit is weekend or not
- **month of the year:** the month of the year of the visit",True,True
4875,upv-sie-21,"# Description

La competici¨®n

El hundimiento del Titanic fue uno de los naufragios m¨¢s famosos de la historia: el 15 de abril de 1912 durante su viaje inaugural, el Titanic se hundi¨® despu¨¦s de chocar con un iceberg, muriendo 1.502 de 2.224 pasajeros y tripulantes. Esta tragedia conmocion¨® a la comunidad internacional y condujo a llevar a cabo mejores normas de seguridad para los buques.

Una de las razones por las que el naufragio llev¨® a la p¨¦rdida de la vida era que no hab¨ªa suficientes botes salvavidas para los pasajeros y la tripulaci¨®n. Sin embargo, existi¨® un elemento de ¡°suerte¡± en sobrevivir al hundimiento: algunos grupos de personas ten¨ªan m¨¢s probabilidades de sobrevivir que otras, como las mujeres, los ni?os y la clase alta.

El problema que se presenta en esta competici¨®n se centra en analizar qu¨¦ tipo de personas eran m¨¢s propensas a sobrevivir utilizando los datos de los pasajeros (es decir, nombre, edad, sexo, clase socio-econ¨®mica, etc.).
----------------------
# Evaluation

Meta
El objetivo es predecir si un pasajero sobrevivi¨® o no al hundimiento del Titanic.
Para cada una de los pasajeros del conjunto de test debe predecirse un valor 0 o 1 para la variable de clase (""Survived"").

M¨¦trica
La puntuaci¨®n obtenida es el porcentaje de pasajeros predichos correctamente. Esto se conoce como exactitud o accuracy.

Formato de archivo de env¨ªo

Debes enviar un archivo csv con exactamente 309 entradas m¨¢s una fila de encabezado. El env¨ªo mostrar¨¢ un error si tiene columnas o filas adicionales (m¨¢s all¨¢ de ""PassengerId"" y ""Survived"").

El archivo debe tener exactamente 2 columnas:

""PassengerId"" (identificador de los pasajeros del conjunto de test)
""Survived"" (contiene Las predicciones binarias: 1 para supervivientes, 0 para fallecidos)","# Dataset Description

<h2 id=""ficheros"">Ficheros</h2>
<p>Los datos se han dividido en dos grupos:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>trainTitanic.csv - el conjunto de datos para entrenamiento</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>testTitanic.csv - el conjunto de datos para evaluaci&oacute;n</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>gender_submission.csv - ejemplo de fichero de env&iacute;o</p>
</li>
</ul>
<h2 id=""datos"">Datos</h2>
<p>El set de entrenamiento debe ser usado para construir los modelos de aprendizaje autom&aacute;tico. En el conjunto de entrenamiento se proporciona la clase (""survived"", sobrevive o no) para cada pasajero. Los modelos de aprendizaje entrenados se basar&aacute;n en las caracter&iacute;sticas descriptivas de los pasajeros (como el sexo, la clase, etc.). Tambi&eacute;n se puede generar nuevas atributos derivados para crear nuevas caracter&iacute;sticas.</p>
<p>El conjunto de datos de prueba o evaluaci&oacute;n deben usarse para ver qu&eacute; tan bien funciona los modelos entrenados con el conjunto de entrenamiento con datos no vistos. Para el conjunto de datos de prueba no se proporciona la clase para cada pasajero y es tu trabajo predecir si los pasajeros del conjunto de pruebas sobreviven o no.</p>
<p>Tambi&eacute;n incluimos gender_submission.csv, un conjunto de predicciones que asumen que s&oacute;lo las pasajeras sobreviven, como ejemplo de c&oacute;mo debe ser un archivo de env&iacute;o.</p>
<h2 id=""atributos"">Atributos</h2>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Variable</th>
<th>Definici&oacute;n</th>
<th>Valores</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>survival</td>
<td>Survival</td>
<td>0 = No, 1 = Yes</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>pclass</td>
<td>Ticket (clase)</td>
<td>1 = 1st, 2 = 2nd, 3 = 3rd</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>sex</td>
<td>Sexo</td>
<td>&nbsp;</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Age</td>
<td>Edad en a&ntilde;os</td>
<td>&nbsp;</td>
</tr>
<tr>
