4925,applications-of-deep-learning-wustlfall-2021,"# Description

![Virtual City](https://data.heatonresearch.com/images/wustl/kaggle/city-size/orbit-107.jpg)
There are many different ways that engineers measure cities.  What is the population?  What is the total land area occupied by the city.  In this exercise, we will see if you can create a model to predict the square feet contained in various virtual cities.

You are provided with images looking towards the city center.  Each image is 512x512, compressed with JPEG.  The following sample image shows a typical view of a virtual city.  You can see towers of a variety of sizes and colors. 

![Typical City View](https://data.heatonresearch.com/images/wustl/kaggle/city-size/2.jpg)

Not all views of the city will be the same. The following view looks at a city much closer up; however, results in some cropping of important detail.

![Up Close](https://data.heatonresearch.com/images/wustl/kaggle/city-size/10.jpg)

Some of the images will show you the city from somewhat above.  Here you can see the city roads more clearly.
![Above the City](https://data.heatonresearch.com/images/wustl/kaggle/city-size/321.jpg)

Some cities are very small.

![Small City](https://data.heatonresearch.com/images/wustl/kaggle/city-size/350.jpg)

Other cities are larger.

![Large City](https://data.heatonresearch.com/images/wustl/kaggle/city-size/947.jpg)


----------------------
# Evaluation

Kaggle will evaluate your submission with [RMSE](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Root-mean-square_deviation).

## Benchmark Scores

There are two benchmark scores I created in the Leaderboard.

* **Average of Training Set** - This is the score you can get if you simply take the average all training data `sqft` and repeat that value for every test set row.
* **Transfer Learning Starter** - This is the score that you can get by submitting the transfer learning example I placed in the code section.


## Submission Format

The file should contain a header and have the following format:
```
id,sqft
1,3240
```","# Dataset Description

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set

## Columns

*   `id` - The `id` of this city, for example, `id` 1 corresponds to the image in `1.jpg`.
*   `sqft`?- square feet of the city in millions of square feet.

## Other notes

* Remember your target is an area, not a volume.  The square feet, in millions, is equal to the sum of each building's `width * height * floors`.
* The height of each floor is regulated by the nation to an undisclosed (yet uniform) number of feet.",True,True
4926,coms4771-fall2021-regression-competition,"# Description

Welcome to COMS 4771 Fall 2021 large-scale regression task. Your goal for this assignment is to develop a good quality regressor for the given dataset.

The given dataset comes from musical songs domain. It consists of about 1/2 Million songs from 20th and 21st century. The task is to predict the year the song was released given the musical content of the song.

To ease the preprocessing burden, each song (observation) has already been vectorized into 90 high quality timbre based features.

You can use any resource/code/package/toolbox that is publicly available to develop your regressor. Your primary goal for this assignment is to develop a good quality song-year predictor. (This of course will be only possible if you employ good Machine Learning practices).
----------------------
# Evaluation

##Evaluation Criteria
You must get a Mean Absolute Error (MAE) score of at most 6.500 on the private holdout test-dataset to get full credit. If your MAE score is over the simple baseline (see leaderboard), then you will get zero credit. If your MAE score is between the simple baseline and the full credit threshold, then you will be awarded points linearly proportional to your deviation from the full credit threshold. For example, if your MAE is halfway between the simple baseline and full credit threshold, then you will get half credit. Extra points will be awarded to the highest performing students, using the following scoring mechanism:

$$\text{Extra Credit Points}= 5(1?\frac{\text{Your Rank}?1}
{\text{Number of Participants with MAE < 6 }}
)?1[\text{Your MAE}&lt; 6]$$
----------------------
# Submission

## Submission Format
There are 24000 test examples. The submission file will be a CSV file with two columns: `dataid` and `prediction`. `dataid` is a unique identifier (between 1 and 2400) for each ride, and `prediction` is the estimated duration that ride.

For example, if you predict trips 1 through 5 as lasting 600 seconds and the rest of the songs 6 through 51630 as lasting 30000 seconds the submission file must look as follows
```
dataid,prediction
1,600
2,600
3,600
4,600
5,600
6,30000
7,30000
8,30000
9,30000
10,30000
11,30000
...
2399,30000
2400,30000
```","# Dataset Description

The data file contains three variables: trainx, trainy, testx.

- trainx is a 463715¡Á90 matrix of training data, where each row is a 90-dimensional feature representation of a song.
- trainy is a 463715¡Á1 vector of labels associated with each training data. Each row is the release year of the corresponding song in trainx.
- testx is a 51630¡Á90 matrix of test data.

Special note for those who are not using Matlab: Python users can use scipy to read in the mat file, R users can use R.matlab package to read in the mat file.",True,True
4927,cost-prediction-for-logistic-company-ml,"# Description

A logistics company is interested to optimize its delivery network. It has provided you a dateset of the network wide deliveries and associated trip costs. It has tasked you with building a model that predicts the cost of deliveries.

Datasets
Training data ¨CTraining dataset contains 38999 observations. Actual responses (labels) are in the variable called ¡®Cost¡¯ Testing data ¨C contains 802 observations with 11 variables and no labels

Evaluation Metric : RMSE Score

<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>trip,cost<br>t333282728025, 23.3433
<br></pre>","# Dataset Description

<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>train.csv</strong> - the training set</li>
<li><strong>test.csv</strong> - the test set</li>
</ul>
<h2>Data fields</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>trip</strong> - Trip id</li>
<li><strong>date</strong> - Date, when trip was made</li>
<li><strong>dayPart</strong> - Day or Night</li>
<li><strong>exWeatherTag</strong> - Heat / Snow</li>
<li><strong>originLocation</strong> - Source location</li>
<li><strong>destinationLocation</strong> - Destination location</li>
<li><strong>distance</strong> - distance traveled</li>
<li><strong>type</strong> - Expedited or Not</li>
<li><strong>weight</strong> - weight carried by carriers</li>
<li><strong>packageType</strong> - Type of packages</li>
<li><strong>carrier</strong> -  Name of the carriers</li>
</ul>",True,True
