4931,nnfl-2021-assignment-2,"# Description

<h1>NNFL Assignment 2</h1>
<p>You have been given a set of videos labeled either as ""fire"" or ""not_fire"". Your task to process this data as you see fit and create a model that will classify the unlabeled data present in the test folder.</p>
<p>You are allowed to make 10 submissions to the public leaderboard as a team everyday. The test data has futher been split in half and you can only few the result of 50% of the test data on the public leaderboard. Please ensure you follow the submission format.</p>
<h2>Acknowledgements</h2>
<p>We thank Professor Tanmay for guiding us in creating this contest and providing access to the dataset used.</p>","# Dataset Description

<p>The data has been divided into train and test. The meta data for for both provide a list of file names within their respective folders. The train csv provides the true labels while the test cvs obviously does not. The data has been extracted frame by frame from a collection of videos.</p>",True,True
4932,cap-4611-2021-fall-extra-practice-01,"# Description

Hello everyone, and welcome to first of what is hopefully many extra practice opportunities. 

These practice competitions are made with a few kinds of people in mind:
- Those who are developing a passion for the topic
- Those who want to satisfy an exploratory itch (how does this compare *in detail* to this other thing?)
- And those who want to make use of an extra credit opportunity in a class that doesn't otherwise offer it.

Since this is for your sake, and not exclusively mine, it will also be driven largely by your feedback, effort, and ability to communicate with those around you effectively. Expect rules and guidelines to change over time as the practice of concept gets ironed out. 

What you put into it, will reflect not only in this course, but throughout your life. So give it your all. Be fair, be honest, and give it a good shot.

In the future, as the datasets get more and more complex working in teams may be worthwhile considering, and even perhaps necessary. If that is experience you would like to have sooner rather than later, you're welcome to let me know. 


All feedback, questions, etc. are more than welcome to be directed to SaltBlock#4394 on discord, also under the alias @crisp_k in the UCF server. Please do not bother Dr. H with questions regarding these, as part of the agreement was that the heavy lifting would be done by us. This *isn't* an official assignment, and is relatively off the books.

Further regarding questions, before asking, have you: 
- Performed an search of your question online first?
- Consulted the limitless non-living resources available?
- *Read the docs?*
- Read the docs again?

Chances are, you can find the answer to your question. If you really can't, then consult someone. As in this class, consider the ability to perform independent research an important goal.
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric used is accuracy, nice and simple.","# Dataset Description

This is the famous IRIS dataset, but subsetted into training and testing data for a competition format.
It is broken down as follows: 70% for training, 30% for testing and submission.

Both the training and testing data do **not** contain an equal distribution of each categorical type. This is done in hopes of creating a more ""realistic"" practice environment. However, there is no missing data, and there ought not be any outliers within the data.


You'll need each of the files. By now you oughta know how to proceed from here, given the data.
## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

## Columns

*   `Id`
*   `SepalLengthCm`
*   `SepalWidthCm`
*   `PetalLengthCm`
*   `PetalWidthCm`
*   `Species`

This is a famous dataset, should you need more information, you can look it up on your own. Independent research is important. 

From: https://archive.ics.uci.edu/ml/datasets/Iris",True,True
4933,ucl-ai-society-card-fraud-detection,"# Description

---

## Dataset Description
### 1. train.csv
- `Time ` - Number of seconds elapsed between this transaction and the first transaction in the dataset
- `V1  - V28` - result of a PCA Dimensionality reduction to protect user identities and sensitive features
- `Amount ` - Transaction amount
- `Class ` - 1 for fraudulent transactions, 0 otherwise

### 2. test.csv
Same columns, but you do not have the `Class ` column, which means that you have to predict the outcome of the match ??  

---

## Learning outcomes
- Apply the knowledge you've gained in previous weeks: Regressions, SVM, etc.
- Immediately try out the Neural Networks that you have learnt from the Week 4 contents.

## Acknowledgements

https://www.kaggle.com/mlg-ulb/creditcardfraud
----------------------
# Evaluation

## Evaluation Metric

The evaluation metric for this competition is **Classification Accuracy**.  
It is defined by the number of correct predictions divided by the number of total predictions.

---

## Submission Format

**For every author in the dataset**, submission files should contain two columns: `id` and `Class`.

The file should contain a header and have the following format:
```
id, Class
750, 1
751, 0
752, 1
753, 0
```","# Dataset Description

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set. You should predict the `Class` based on this file
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

For more descriptions of columns, read https://www.kaggle.com/mlg-ulb/creditcardfraud",True,True
4934,cx-kaggle-final-project-fall-2021,"# Description

<p>Welcome fellow data enthusiasts! I am sure you have learned so much from the past CX lectures and are eager to get your hands dirty. This competition is aimed to help solidify the concepts you have learned this semester and hope you enjoy this competition! </p>

<h2>Happy Kaggling :)</h2>

----------------------
# Evaluation

## Metric
Submissions are evaluated on Mean Absolute Error.

## Submission File Format
See the submission file (sample_submission.csv) on the Data page.","# Dataset Description

