4954,mais202fall2021,"# Description

Each image is 28 pixels in height and 28 pixels in width, for a total of 784 pixels in total. Each pixel has a single pixel-value associated with it, indicating the lightness or darkness of that pixel, with higher numbers meaning darker. This pixel-value is an integer between 0 and 255.

The file `train_images.npy` contains all 50 000 training images.
The file `train_labels.csv` contains all 50 000 training labels.
The file `label_int_to_str_mapping` contains the mapping between integer labels and plain text labels.
The file `test_images.npy` contains all 20 000 testing images. Please use this file to generate your solutions.

To load your npy files use the following code:
```
import numpy as
train_images = np.load(""train_images.npy"")
```
To visualize an image you can use the following code block:
```
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt

def show_image(arr):
    two_d = (np.reshape(arr, (28, 28)) * 255).astype(np.uint8)
    plt.imshow(two_d, interpolation='nearest')
    plt.show()

show_image(train_images[0]) # 0 is the index of the training image you want to display
```

Labels
Each training and test example is assigned to one of the following labels:

0 T-shirt/top
1 Trouser
2 Pullover
3 Dress
4 Coat
5 Sandal
6 Shirt
7 Sneaker
8 Bag
9 Ankle boot

Submission
You can use the following code to submit your predictions on the test set (adapted to your pipeline as necessary):
```
x_test = np.load('test_images.npy')
x_test = x_test.reshape(len(x_test),1,28,28)
y_test = model.predict(x_test)

df_test = pd.read_csv('sample_submission.csv')
df_test['label'] = y_test
df_test.to_csv('submission.csv', index=False)
```
Resources
Feel free to use any online resources you find, this assignment is used to mimic some real life challenges you may run into and also allow you to work in groups with your fellow bootcampees. It is important not to worry about your ranking, take this opportunity to learn



----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is class label accuracy.

## Submission Format

Submission files should contain two columns: ID and labels. Each ID in the submission should be unique and be in increasing order. The IDs must be ordered from lowest to highest. Please see sample_submission.csv as an example.
The file should contain a header and have the following format:
```
ID,label
0, 1
1, 8
¡­, ¡­
```","# Dataset Description

## File descriptions
- train_labels.csv - contains the ground truth labels for all training images, indices correspond to the indices in train_images.npy
- train_images.npy - contains the training images, use np.load to load this file
- test_images.npy - contains all the unlabelled test images, use np.load to load this file
- label_int_to_str_mapping.csv - contains the mapping between integer labels to string labels
## Data fields
- ID - the index of the image in the train or test npy file
- label - the label of the image in the train npy file",True,True
4955,vision-challenge-sistemas-embebidos-2021-20,"# Description

**Descripci¨®n del reto**
En el siguiente enunciado se presenta el segundo reto del curso de Sistemas embebidos de la universidad de los Andes, enfocado en Visi¨®n artificial o Computer Vision. Este reto consiste en crear un modelo de ceros utilizando alg¨²n framework de inteligencia artificial (Tensorflow, PyTorch, SkLearn, etc) que sea capaz de clasificar de la mejor manera 43 tipos de se?ales de transito a partir de car¨¢cteristicas extra¨ªdas de cada una de las im¨¢genes. Sin embargo, el modelo que dise?en debe contar con las siguientes restricciones: 

- Debe ser un modelo que tenga como entradas las car¨¢cteristicas de las im¨¢genes extra¨ªdas con visi¨®n con computador: (Ejemplo - N¨²mero de bordes, Puntos clave, Transformaciones, etc.)
- El modelo debe ser un modelo con una arquitectura totalmente conectada. Es decir, no puede incluir capas o redes convolucionales. Esto con el objetivo de asegurar que la entrada de cada modelo sean las car¨¢cteristicas extra¨ªdas previamente. 
- El modelo debe tener contar con una arquitectura limitada: 
       - N¨²mero de capas ocultas m¨¢ximo: 50
       - N¨²mero de neuronas m¨¢ximo por capa: 5000

- Se puede utilizar diferentes funciones de preprocesamiento para filtrar las car¨¢cteristicas y as¨ª obtener las m¨¢s importantes o relevantes en la clasificaci¨®n como lo es PCA, entre otros. 

Cada equipo debe subir las etiquetas de cierto conjunto de im¨¢genes de evaluaci¨®n que se encuentran adjuntos en el dataset. Al subir su archivo con etiquetas se asignar¨¢ de forma automatica el puntaje de precisi¨®n que tiene el modelo de cada uno de los equipos. La calificaci¨®n del modelo del challenge ser¨¢ proporcional al puntaje que cada equipo sea capaz de obtener. Es decir, las calificaciones del reto ser¨¢n distribuidas de la siguiente manera seg¨²n el puesto que obtengan al finalizar la competencia el d¨ªa 12 de noviembre de 2021:

5.0
4.5
4.0
3.5

Una vez realicen y suban las etiquetas de las se?ales de transito del dataset de evaluaci¨®n, es necesario que realicen una presentaci¨®n en clase explicando la arquitectura que desarrollaron, las car¨¢cteristicas que extrayeron de cada imagen y realizando una comparaci¨®n con el modelo convolucional del anterior reto. Es decir, deben re-entrenar el modelo convolucional que previamente desarrollaron y comparar su desempe?o con el modelo de extracci¨®n de car¨¢cteristicas. Adem¨¢s deben subir el enlace de un v¨ªdeo demostrativo en donde pongan a prueba el desempe?o del modelo que desarrollaron utilizando una raspberry y una c¨¢mara web. El objetivo es hacer un v¨ªdeo comparativo del desempe?o de ambos modelos que ustedes han desarrollado y concluyan cu¨¢l es mejor y por qu¨¦.

Calificaci¨®n
La calificaci¨®n del challenge para cada uno de losequipos en total ser¨¢ distribuida de la siguiente manera:

Modelo del challenge (Nota de Kaggle): 60%
V¨ªdeo demostrativos desde la raspberry: 20%
Presentaci¨®n de clase: 20%

**Aclaraci¨®n**

El grupo de profesores har¨¢ un modelo que tenga una presici¨®n de referencia. Esta precisi¨®n de referencia debe ser superada por los estudiantes para que su calificaci¨®n sea asignada de forma correcta.

Para la detecci¨®n de se?ales de transito es totalmente valido llegar a utilizar otro tipo de datsets que encuentren en l¨ªnea o que realicen ustedes.

**Sobre el Dataset**

El dataset cuenta con dos carpetas: entrenamiento y test. En la carpeta de entrenamiento se tiene un total de 42 subcarpetas (Cada carpeta representa una clase) y dentro de ellas un archivo CSV en el que se especifica el **ancho**, **largo**, las coordenadas **x** y **y** del recuadro que encierra la se?al de transito y la clase a la que pertenece cada imagen. En la carpeta de test se tiene un total de 12,631 im¨¢genes junto con un archivo CSV en el que se especifica el **ancho**, **largo** y las coordenadas **x** y **y** del recuadro que encierra la se?al de transito. 

@inproceedings{Stallkamp-IJCNN-2011,
    author = {Johannes Stallkamp and Marc Schlipsing and Jan Salmen and Christian Igel},
    booktitle = {IEEE International Joint Conference on Neural Networks},
    title = {The {G}erman {T}raffic {S}ign {R}ecognition {B}enchmark: A multi-class classification competition},
    year = {2011},
    pages = {1453--1460}
}
----------------------
# Evaluation

La metrica de evaluaci¨®n para esta competencia es la Precisi¨®n por categoria. Esta estadistica comunmente usada en el ¨¢rea de Machine Learning nos determina que tan bueno o malo es nuestro modelo clasificando las diferentes etiquetas que se tienen. 

En general la precis¨®n es la raz¨®n entre los verdaderos positivos (Clasificados correctamente en una clase) y el n¨²mero de elementos totales que corresponden a dicha clase. 

## Formato de entrega
Para cada una de las im¨¢ganes de prueba en el dataset, las entregas deben tener dos columnas. 'Filename' y 'ClassId' refiriendose a la clase a la que permanece cada una de las im¨¢genes. La extensi¨®n del archivo debe ser un archivo .csv 

El archivo debe contener el siguiente formato de encabezado:
```
Filename,ClassId
0000.ppm,1
0001.ppm,12
0002.ppm,23
```","# Dataset Description

El dataset cuenta con dos carpetas: 
- Entrenamiento 
- Test",True,True
4956,cisc873-dm-f21-a4,"# Description

### Airbnb price category prediction

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F4409738%2F229f8a04bb84f4474b43b63a2a1da38d%2Fred-airbnb%20(1).png?generation=1606092019652707&alt=media)
(meme source: People Are Having a Lot of Fun With the New Airbnb Logo)

One of the biggest problems when people prepare to post a new listing on airbnb is, how much should one ask for? Of course the most intuitive way is to check how other similar postings price their apartment/house. So in this assignment, we are going to predict the listing price based on the listing characteristics ????, in this way to optimize user experience and lower the bar to be a new host?? ! 

Predicting the actual price could be simple (well you can just go search online ??), so we cut the pricing into three different bins for classification ??. For each listing, we recommend a pricing range to the new host rather than a fix price (how nice is that!). So we define three categories: `beginner, plus, premium` based on the created listing. Respectively we use `0, 1, 2` to denote these three categories. 

The dataset contains listings of different areas in Montreal during 2019. It comes with rich information for each listing, including a link to the thumbnails etc. As discussed in the last lecture, we will follow a multi-objective (multi-task) multi-modality solution.

Enjoy! 



### Tasks:

?? Meme competition:
Include/find a MEME that you liked related to data science/data mining/machine learning

?? Understand the template:
Put detailed comment on each line of the code to show your understanding of the template (you can find it at the Data tab). Then describe: What is the experimental protocol used and how was it carried out? How did we tune hyper-parameters in the template? What is the search space and what is the criteria to determine good/bad hyper-parameters? 

?? Problem Formulation:
Define the problem. What is the input? What is the output? What data mining function is required? What could be the challenges?  What is the impact? What is an ideal solution?

?? Model Tuning and Documentation:
Now based on the template, try to improve the model's performance on the public leaderboard by following the data science life-cycle for tuning. You can try different features, different hyperparameters/configurations of the model, and even a different model. For each trial, document the reason why you want to make the certain change and the expected outcome, before running the code. Record the observed performance and your thought on it. Recall that we have a DS life-cycle and we should know what to do when underfit/overfit. The final result is not important, but the process is. Documentation of your thought process is very important, since most people forgot why they test certain model/hyperparameter after they got the result (it takes time). It also helps a lot when you got stuck. You can organize the notebook by listing 

- thoughts and observations for trial 0, plan for trial 1
- code for trial 1
- thoughts and observations for trial 1, plan for trial 2
- code for trial 2
...

You have to tune at least 10 times. All the tried solutions should be different (e.g. different feature sets/different preprocessing). The tried solutions should cover at least:
- Text layers. Swap the original reduce_mean layer with a GRU/LSTM layer.
- Text layers. Try [BiDirectional](https://www.tensorflow.org/api_docs/python/tf/keras/layers/Bidirectional) Recurrent layer.
- Text layers. Try Attention to aggregate the time dimension.
- Image layers. The original layers for image contains one convolution layer. Try to customize those layers and add[ drop out layer](https://www.tensorflow.org/api_docs/python/tf/keras/layers/Dropout) for regularization. 


?? Answer the questions below (**briefly**):

??What is the default model for text data in the template? Is it a good one for sequential data? Why?
??What is gradient vanishing and gradient explosion, and how GRU/LSTM tries to mitigate this problem?
??What is multi-objective/multi-task learning? What is multi-modality learning? How this assignment fits into those paradigm? 
??How attention mechanism can help with the learning process? 
??In the template, we use early stopping. What is the purpose? 


### Deliverable:

?? A python notebook containing the documentation of how you reach the final design as well as the answers to the questions. 


### Marking:

?? 1 for technical soundness of the final script (whether you did things right e.g. not using ID as a feature, tuning ranges reasonable e.g. not a giant search space for grid search)
?? 2 for clarity, organization and soundness of the documentation (how & why you adjust your design to improve the performance) (example deduction: decision tree with observed high bias -> trim more, linear model with observed high bias in testing set -> more regularizations)
?? 1 for the clarity of your own code (all the template code and **new** code should have comments)
?? 4 for meeting the requirements (what to cover) for all the trials.
?? 2 your answers to the questions (deductions based on errors)

1 bonus point if you do transfer learning and up-training  
Note: Emoji allowed and encouraged so it will be more fun for us to grade it.

----------------------
# Evaluation

We will use accuracy as the evaluation criteria.","# Dataset Description

Data is also available at: 

https://github.com/CISC-873/Information-2021/releases/download/data/a4.zip",True,True
