4969,gis-3-machine-learning-lab-2021,"# Description

Car sharing service allows car renting and returning via a network of stations located throughout the city. The data generated by these systems is explicitly recorded and could be used for analysis. Car sharing systems, therefore, function as a sensor network, which can be used for studying mobility in a city. Now, we have collected the historical usage patterns and decided to use it for forecasting car rental demand for building a new station in the city.
<hr>

###Acknowledgements
The dataset used in this lab was provided by Gad Benram using the data from AutoTel car sharing (https://www.kaggle.com/gidutz/autotel-shared-car-locations).

----------------------
# Evaluation

<h2>Evaluation</h2>
Submissions are evaluated one the Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE). You could find your submission scores in **public leaderboard** (calculated with 40% of the test data) and **private leaderboard** (calculated with 60% of the test data after the deadline of this competition).

<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p>Your submission file must have a header and should be structured in the following format:</p>
<pre> Index,daily_car_demand<br> 0,0<br> 1,0<br> 2,0<br> ... <br> ... </pre>","# Dataset Description

<h3>Data Sources </h3>
The location of the parked cars was extracted from the AutoTel website, every two minutes for several weeks. The raw data was saved in CSV format. In this lab, there are 6 csv files provided: 
</br></br>
- <b>car_location_training.csv</b> : Contains the training data that you need. These are the raw data collected from the AutoTel website. In this lab, we will process these raw data step by step and use it for forecasting the daily car rental demand.
</br></br>
-  <b>car_location_testing_daily_withoutLable.csv </b> : Contains the data that you need to predict. It has more column then the training set as we have aggrated t the data as daily records and provided the number of parking spots in the new car station. The prediction results need to be submitted for the in-class competition.  
</br></br>
- <b>tel_aviv_neighborhood.csv</b> : Contains the data that you need for visualization.
</br></br>
-  <b>sample_submission.csv </b>: A sample of what you should upload for the competition. The sample submission file contains the results from a dummy model. 
</br></br>
-  <b>intermediate_results_df_test.csv </b>: The intermediate results of df_test we generated to save your time in Step 2. You will need to import this if you can't finish Part 2 before the break.
</br></br>
-  <b>intermediate_results_df_train_daily.csv </b>: The intermediate results of df_train_daily we generated to save your time in Step 2. You will need to import this if you can't finish Part 2 before the break.",True,True
4970,rob535-fall2021-task2,"# Description

<p>This is the second task for <b>ROB 535 - Self-driving Cars: Perception and Control</b>. In this task, you are asked to localize a vehicle in a snapshot.</p>

<h2>Method</h2>

<p>You can use any algorithm to complete this task, but with one restriction - if you are using an existing algorithm, like a neural network publically available on github, you <i>must</i> modify some part of it to get points. Using an existing algorithm without any modification is not allowed. Note that changing hyperparameters like learning rate and batch size does not count as a modification.</p>

<p>For example, you can change the number of layers, the type of activation function, etc. Combining multiple algorithms is also valid.</p>

<h2>GPU Resource</h2>

<p>Please register on <a href=""https://aws.amazon.com/education/awseducate/"">AWS Educate</a> to get a $100 credit promo code for your AWS account. After redeeming the promo code, you will be able to launch instances with Elastic GPUs.</p>

<h2>Grading</h2>

<p>Teams who beat the baseline will get additional 5 points on top of the points from Task 1.</p>
----------------------
# Evaluation

Your ""Team Name"" should be unique and contain the team number shown on Canvas. Each team will need to submit a text file in the following format:

    guid/image/axis,value
    0410e7f5-4bfd-4d35-b623-9f8d6b87c179/0000/x,0.29561827
    0410e7f5-4bfd-4d35-b623-9f8d6b87c179/0000/y,-1.6155027
    0410e7f5-4bfd-4d35-b623-9f8d6b87c179/0000/z,13.797426
    0410e7f5-4bfd-4d35-b623-9f8d6b87c179/0001/x,0.19018781
    0410e7f5-4bfd-4d35-b623-9f8d6b87c179/0001/y,-1.6319742
    0410e7f5-4bfd-4d35-b623-9f8d6b87c179/0001/z,13.804735
    ...

where the first row must be `guid/image/axis,value` with `guid` being the folder names under `test`, and `value` being the coordinate of the centroid of the vehicle along `axis`.

The evaluation metric used in this task is **RMSE**.
----------------------
# Report

Please see the ""Report"" section in Task 1.","# Dataset Description

See the **Data** section of Task 1.",True,True
4971,uci-cs178-f21,"# Description

For our course project this year, we will predict customer behavior in a classification task.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Scoring of predictions is done using AUC, the area under the ROC (receiver-operator characteristic) curve. This gives an average of your learner¡¯s performance at various levels of sensitivity to positive data. This means that you will likely do better if, instead of simply predicting the target class, you also include your confidence level of that class value, so that the ROC curve can be evaluated at different levels of specificity. To do so, you can report your confidence that each test data point is from class ""+1"" as a real number. Your predictions will then be sorted in order of confidence, and the ROC curve evaluated.

The file should contain a header and have the following format:
```
ID,Predicted
1,0.03780
...
```","# Dataset Description

These data represent customer analytics data, in which we wish to predict customer ""events"" (changing or ending levels of service) based on features of the customer.

**File descriptions**

- X_train.txt - the training set, features

- Y_train.txt - the training set, targets (0/1)

- X_test.txt - the test set, features

Kaggle keeps the test set's target values hidden; you will upload your predictions, and Kaggle will give feedback about your performance.

**Data fields**

- X1...X41 - Numeric (real-valued) features

- X42...X107 - Discrete (categorical or binary) features
  - Note, some features have very large numbers of categories, many with few data points; you may wish to compress these in some way.",True,True
4972,sys-4021-6021-glm,"# Description

[MindTrails](https://mindtrails.virginia.edu) is an evidence-based program designed to help people think differently about situations that make them feel anxious. The program is free as part of a study funded by the National Institute of Mental Health and has been completed by people of all racial and ethnic identities from many countries around the world. The program focuses on anxiety that people may experience regarding social situations, physical concerns, work, and COVID-19 (including populations with generalized anxiety, social anxiety, and panic disorder, and high anxiety that isn't part of a disorder). This program welcomes all eligible adults to participate in MindTrails. Also note that this program has not been studied as a tool to help people cope with trauma, including racial injustice and discrimination, or violence to yourself or others. The program encourage individuals coping with trauma to seek out other resources, such as the APA's PTSD guidelines for patients and families and the National Alliance on Mental Illness's resources for coping with racial injustice and discrimination.

## Calm Thinking
In this project, we focus on one of the sub-studies of MindTrails, called _Calm Thinking_. I created this dataset to explore different factors affecting participants' engagement in a digital mental health intervention. 


## Acknowledgements

We thank Jeremy Elbemer, Henry Behan, Dan Funk, Anna Baglione, and Prof. Teachman for providing this dataset.
----------------------
# Evaluation

In this competition you'll work with a dataset containing user characteristics (numerical and categorical) and their psychometrics score (numerical) reviews . Your goal is to predict the patient dropout in a give session of training.

## Grading
Notice that once you submit your predictions to the Kaggle platform, the resulting `f1` score will be calculated using only half of the test set. So, make sure that you are paying attention to whether or not your predictive model is stable (i.e. presents low variance) when applied to new, unseen datasets.

## Important notes:

When using this dataset, you agree that you 1) only use the data for research purposes 2) don't use the data for any commercial purposes 3) don't distribute the data to anyone else 4) cite the authors.

## Attribute Information:

`psychometrics (numerical)`: the value for each psychometrics during the study

`label (binary)`: whether a patient dropped out from MindTrails or not (1 = dropout)


## Training and Testing data:

The training and testing data you'll use for this competition can be found in the ""Data"" page above

---

The evaluation metric for this competition is [Mean F1-Score](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/F-score). The F1 score, commonly used in information retrieval, measures accuracy using the statistics precision \( \text{p} \) and recall \( \text{r} \).

Precision is the ratio of true positives \(\text{tp}\) to all predicted positives \(\text{tp} + \text{fp}\). Recall is the ratio of true positives \(\text{tp}\) to all actual positives \(\text{tp} + \text{fn}\). The F1 score is given by:

\[ \text{F1} = 2\frac{\text{p} \cdot \text{r}}{\text{p}+\text{r}}\ \ \mathrm{where}\ \ \text{p} = \frac{\text{tp}}{\text{tp}+\text{fp}},\ \ \text{r} = \frac{\text{tp}}{\text{tp}+\text{fn}} \]

The F1 metric weights recall and precision equally, and a good retrieval algorithm will maximize both precision and recall simultaneously. Thus, moderately good performance on both will be favored over extremely good performance on one and poor performance on the other.","# Dataset Description

## Process

1. load `training_data` and `test_data`

`test_data = read.csv(""test_data.csv"")`

`train_data = read.csv(""training_data.csv"")`

2. create a good GLM model

`my_glm_model = glm(label~1, data = training_data, family = binomial)` _this is just an example_

3. you can calculate `roc` score of your model by

`install.packages(""mltools"")`

`library(mltools)`

`auc_roc(predicted_values, test_data[,177])`


3. submit your predicted values `sample_submission.csv` on your test data

`predicted_values  = predict(my_glm_model, type = ""response"", newdata = test_data)`

`predicted_values_df = as.data.frame(predicted_values)`

`predicted_values_df['Id'] = seq(1,dim(predicted_values_df)[1], by = 1)`

`names(predicted_values_df) = c(""Expected"", ""Id"")`

`write.csv(predicted_values_df[,c('Id', ""Expected"")], ""sample_submission.csv"", row.names = FALSE)`

4. your model would be tested on unseen dataset
5.  the `roc` score would be calculated on the unseen data



## Files

*   **training_data.csv** - the training set
*   **test_data.csv** - the test set
*   the **label** column (i.e., column _177_) is your binary response variables `0/1`",True,True
4973,datalab-2021-cup2-object-detection,"# Description

Object Detection

In this competition, you have to train a model that recognizes objects in an image. Your goal is to output bounding boxes for objects.
Given an image(shape = [undefined, undefined, 3]), you need to output a list of bounding boxes (xmin, ymin, xmax, ymax, classlabel, confidencescore) for objects showed in image and its class.
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is comparing your mean Average Precision on the different packs of testing data with the ground truth results(mean squared error). The ranking on the leaderboard is 50% of the testing packs. Your final score will be decided by the rest of 50% after the end of the competition.

There are two baseline results, Benchmark-60 and Benchmark-80. You have to outperform Benchmark-60 to get 60 points, and Benchmark-80 to get 80. Meanwhile, the lower point(error) you achieve, the higher the final score you will get.","# Dataset Description

# The dataset is the PASCAL VOC 2007 for object detection.
File descriptions

* pascal_voc_training_data.txt - the training records
* pascal_voc_testing_data.txt - the testing image records
* VOCdevkit_train.zip - including all training images and annotations
* VOCdevkit_test.zip - including all testing images
* evaluate.zip - including evaluate.py, calculating mAP and output .csv file for uploading
* test_predion_example.txt - example file of an input file for evaluate.py
* sample_output_file.csv - example CSV file(output by evaluate.py) for you to submit on this competition",True,True
4974,iml2021,"# Description

### Introduction
This competition is organized in the context of the [Introduction to Machine Learning] (https://people.montefiore.uliege.be/lwh/AIA/) course (ELEN0062) at the University of Li¨¨ge, Belgium. The goal of the competition is to let you apply the methods or principles exposed in the theoretical course in order to address a real problem: develop a system to identify the nature of the physical activity performed by a subject, from measurements obtained from different sensors.

### Task
The mission of your group is to use supervised learning techniques to design an algorithm that can identify automatically the activity performed by a subject.

To achieve this, we provide you with two sets of data, one for learning and one for testing. Each dataset contains 3500 multivariate time-series (1 per subject) corresponding to the measurements obtained from 31 sensors, during 5s periods. Each time-series from the training set is labeled by one of the 14 different activities. Your goal is to provide a similar labelling for the time series in the test set.
----------------------
# Evaluation

### Evaluation metric
The submission file should contain two columns:
1. Time-series sample: the time-series identifier.
2. Prediction: the id of the predicted activity.

## Submission Format

A sample submission file is provided. The file should be the concatenation
of all subjects to predict. Several people's records will be used to track
your results on the public and private leaderboards.


The file should contain a header and have the following format:
```
Id,Prediction
1,4
2,7
3,10
4,10
5,8
```","# Dataset Description

### Files description
* **LS.zip** : the training set
* **TS.zip** : the test set
* **example_submission.csv** : a sample submission file, in the correct format
* **toy_script.py** : a small script loading data, fitting a model and creating a submission file

Both LS and TS archives contain text files denoted by ***SetName*_sensor_*Number*.txt**, where ***Number*** denotes the sensor identifier. Each such file contains 3500 time-series (i.e. rows) of length 512 (i.e. columns) for the considered sensor. Missing values have been set to -999999.99. The list of sensors is as follows:

 - 2: heart beat rate (*bpm*)
 - 3: hand temperature (*¡ãC*)
 - 4,5,6: hand acceleration (*3D, ms-2*)
 - 7,8,9: hand gyroscope (*3D, rad/s*)
 - 10,11,12: hand magnetometer (*3D, ¦ÌT*)
 - 13: chest temperature (*¡ãC*)
 - 14,15,16: chest acceleration (*3D, ms-2*)
 - 17,18,19: chest gyroscope (*3D, rad/s*)
 - 20,21,22: chest magnetometer (*3D, ¦ÌT*)
 - 23: foot temperature (*¡ãC*)
 - 24,25,26: foot acceleration (*3D, ms-2*)
 - 27,28,29: foot gyroscope (*3D, rad/s*)
 - 30,31,32: foot magnetometer (*3D, ¦ÌT*)

The time-series in the LS files have been obtained from 5 subjects while the time-series in the TS file have been collected from 3 new subjects not represented in the LS sample. You can find in both archives a file denoted **subject_Id.txt** containing the identifier of the subject corresponding to each time-series (3500 x 1 elements).

Finally, in the LS archive only, a file denoted **activity_Id.txt** gives the activity identifier corresponding to each time-series in learning set (3500 x 1 elements). The list of activies is as follows:

1. lying
2. sitting
3. standing
4. walking very slow
5. normal walking
6. nordic walking
7. running
8. ascending stairs
9. descending stairs
10. cycling
11. ironing
12. vacuum cleaning
13. rope jumping
14. playing soccer

Both sets were obtained from the PAMAP (http://www.pamap.org/) dataset.",True,True
4975,bas-479-option-1-will-they-donate,"# Description

See Canvas.

You are going to develop a model to predict if an alumnus will donate to UT in 2019 using information known about them through 2018.  The hope is that the patterns the model discovers will generalize into the future.  In other words, we can input what we know about the alumnus up through 2021 to come up with a good guess as to their probability of donating in 2022.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Your evaluation will be based on the AUC (area under the ROC curve).  This allows the model to be tuned toward identifying the set of most likely individuals to donate (even if those probabilities aren't that high).","# Dataset Description

See Canvas for the .RData file you'll need to use to make predictions.  The data attached here is simply a set of random numbers guessing the probability of donating.",True,True
4976,bas-479-option-2-how-much-will-alumni-donate,"# Description

See Canvas.

You are going to develop a model to predict how much an alumnus has donated to UT as of 2019. The hope is that the patterns the model discovers will generalize into the future. In other words, we can input what we know about the alumnus up through 2021 to come up with a good guess as to what they have donated to UT so far.  This could help gauge and predict the lifetime value of graduates.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Entries will be gauged based on the RMSE.  Since predictions are for the log10 of the total amount of cash given, the RMSE (at least 10^RMSE) tells us the factor by which the predicted values are off from the true values.","# Dataset Description

See Canvas for the RData file used to build models and make predictions.  This is just a dataset full of random guesses for the gift amounts.",True,True
