4985,geostatistics-athens-week-2021,"# Description

The Jura data set comprises seven heavy metals measured in the top soil of the swiss Jura, along with consistently coded land use and rock type factors, as well as geographic coordinates.

The competition consist in predicting the Cobalt top soil concentration at the validation locations.
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is the mean squared error (MSE). The MSE is defined as:</p>
<p>\[ MSE = \sum_{i=1}^n (Z_i-Z^{\star}_i)^2 \]</p>
<p> where Z_i is the actual value, unknown and hosted here, Z_i* is the predicted value and i is the index.</p> 
<p>The MSE measures the accuracy of the prediction. The lower the better!</p>
<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p> submission files should contain two columns: the index of the predicted site with name V1 and the predicted cobalt concentration named V2 see </p>
[the sample submission](https://cloud.mines-paristech.fr/index.php/s/n1Vak3TiRvseX0o)","# Dataset Description

<p> The Jura data set comprises seven heavy metals measured in the top soil of the swiss Jura, along with consistently coded land use and rock type factors, as well as geographic coordinates. 
</p>
<h2>File descriptions</h2>
Two of the provided files will be useful
<ul>
<li><strong>jura_pred.csv</strong> - the training set</li>
<li><strong>jura_val_loc.csv</strong> - validation locations and covariables</li>
</ul>
<h2>Data fields</h2>

<ul>
<li><strong> Xloc </strong> X coordinate, local grid km </li>
<li><strong> Yloc </strong>Y coordinate, local grid km </li>
<li><strong>Landuse</strong> Land use: 1: Forest, 2: Pasture (Weide(land), Wiese, Grasland), 3: Meadow (Wiese, Flur, Matte, Anger), 4: Tillage (Ackerland, bestelltes Land) </li>
<li><strong>Rock</strong> Rock Types: 1: Argovian, 2: Kimmeridgian, 3: Sequanian, 4: Portlandian, 5: Quaternary. </li>
<li><strong>Cd</strong> mg cadmium kg^-1 topsoil </li>
<li><strong>Co</strong> mg cobalt kg^-1 topsoil </li>
<li><strong>Cr</strong> mg chromium kg^-1 topsoil </li>
<li><strong>Cu</strong> mg copper kg^-1 topsoil </li>
<li><strong>Ni</strong> mg nickel kg^-1 topsoil </li>
<li><strong>Pb</strong> mg lead kg^-1 topsoil </li>
<li><strong>Zn</strong> mg zinc kg^-1 topsoil</li>
</ul>",True,True
4986,cs4487-course-project,"# Description

## Background
- Techniques for convincing manipulations of digital facial images
exist for decades via the use of visual effects 
- Recent technologies in deep learning, especially ¡°DeepFakes,¡±
have led to a dramatic increase in the realism of fake content
- While there are entertaining applications of DeepFakes, potential
weaponization of such techniques has raised great concerns
- DeepFake detection has become a pressing issue and a hot spot of
research

## Task: 
A binary classification problem
- Input: RGB facial images of size 299 x 299 x 3
- Output: A binary label to indicate whether an image is fake or not

## Training set: 
12,000 images, within which 8,000 images contain
fake faces, and the rest images are real
- Download link: https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1n18AqgUF_KqKbcxuLXrTcLq-pyfhSJD9?usp=sharing

## Test set: 
3,000 images that will not be released to the students
- Students have THREE chances to get access to the test set
- The best result among the three will be used for ranking

- Tip: It is a good practice to divide the available set further into
training, validation, and test set

## Group project: 
A group of at most two students is allowed

## What to hand in: 
a single ipython notebook (.ipynb) as the project report with source code files included
- Source files must contain the training code for reproduction
- An extra PDF file as project report is not recommended

## When to hand in: 
Dec. 6, 2021, 11:59 pm

## Where to hand in: 
- Submit the clean and runable test code to the TAs
- Submit the .ipynb file via Canvas

## GPUs: 
Some GPU hours are available on Microsoft Azure

## Grading (Totally 30 points)
- 30.0% - Technical correctness (whether the
methodologies/algorithms are correctly used)
- 30.0% - Experiment and analysis
- 20.0% - Quality of the written report (organized, complete
descriptions, etc.)
- 10.0% - Quality of project presentation (tentatively held in Wed,
Nov. 24)
- 10.0% - Reserved for Top-5 teams based on the test set
performance
----------------------
# Evaluation

## Evaluation Metric
The evaluation metric for this competition is Classification Accuracy, i.e. the percentage of your correct predictions over all your predictions on test set.


## Submission Format

Your submission files should be in .csv format. The data frame should contain two columns: `Id` and `Category`.  Each element of  column `Id` is the name of an image in test set, which is a string (e.g. 0.png). Each element of column `Category` should be an string from {0, 1}  where 0 for fake and 1 for real.  


The .csv file should contain a header and have the following format:
```
Id,Category
0.png,1
1.png,1
2.png,0
...
3999.png,0
```
