5019,bdma2021-updrive,"# Description

Your second project at Double Zero Data, the prestigious analytics consulting firm you work for, is to help UpDrive, a car dealer firm specialized in trading used cars in Europe. UpDrive focuses on acquiring and selling used cars in Europe, taking advantage of asymmetry of information, moral hazard and adverse selection. In some cases, UpDrive adds value by making simple and cheap repairs and/or upgrades in the cars they buy to sell later with a higher price and make more profit. In other cases, UpDrive just identifies market opportunities when a car is made available at a price much lower than expected, allowing it to make a quick sell at a profit.

As a first step towards a complete solution, you decide to assess how easy it is to predict used car price as a function of some of their publicly available characteristics. This task will provide you with some information on the likelihood that you can develop a good solution for UpDrive. You decide to scrape data from a used car online platform aiming to gather data about all used cars in Europe. After some data cleansing, you have created a proper dataset with several attributes (columns) and thousands of observations (rows). This dataset has variables that might or might not be useful to predict the listing price of a used car.

You should run the models you believe are adequate and that you believe will provide the best predictions. You can use the knowledge acquired in the Individual Assignment as starting point for this task. This task is different from the task in the Individual Assignment in at least one important aspect. There are more than two classes. Your goal is to predict to which price range (out of 6 ranges) does a used car belong to. 

----------------------
# Evaluation

For the context of this competition the best model is the model that predicts outcomes with highest accuracy, so you should use the variable **price_category** as your target variable.","# Dataset Description

The original data were scraped from a used car website using the
=webscraper.io= Chrome extension mentioned in class.

You will be using cleaned-up data based on the original scraped
dataset with some (but not all) of the information available from the
website.",True,True
5020,mrs21tutorial,"# Description

Welcome to the AL Competition!

##Challenge I - Ended
Your job is to apply regression algorithms for modeling the composition - property relationship of a given material dataset -> the smaller the RMSE (root mean square error), the merrier!

## Challenge II  - Open to Submissions till Midnight Wednesday 1st Dec 2021
Your job is to use active learning to identify the composition with the highest property value within the same material space -> the few iterations (sampling) it takes, the merrier!


-----------------------------------------------------------
## Instructions
*  [Competition Description Slides](https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1S1N3E-Z2xny2eAtJFs0IfZUF3MpUs4MWmlp7iHj5kfA/edit?usp=sharing)
----------------------
# Evaluation

---
The evaluation metric for Challenge I is [root mean square error] - on Kaggle.
The evaluation metric for Challenge II is [f(deviation X) + f(deviation Y) + f(iterations)] - on G-sheet

## Submission Format

**For Challenge I**, Kaggle submission files should contain two columns: `Composition (X)` and Property (Y)`. 
**For Challenge II**, participants enter results in the [G-sheet](https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1tRmtMVFGeouXwfFNl8-QQzVu4qilAEaxp5BRfShkEZY/edit?usp=sharing), winners will be invited to demonstrate their code notebook at the end of the competition.","# Dataset Description

## Challenge I
* Challenge I__Training. csv contains 50 compositions (X) randomly sampled within a target material space, with the material property value (Y) labeled for each composition.  
* Challenge I__X_Test. csv contains test compositions (X) within the same material space.
* Please submit a csv file on Kaggle that contains your predictions of property (Y) for each composition (X) in X_Test.csv.

## Challenge II
* Challenge II__Training.csv (available to participants when Challenge I ends) contains 1000 compositions (X) sampled within a target material space, with the material property value (Y) labeled for each composition.
* Please enter your predicted optimum property (Y) in this entire material space, the corresponding optimum composition (X), and the number of iterations you need to find this composition here: [G-sheet](https://docs.google.com/spreadsheets/d/1tRmtMVFGeouXwfFNl8-QQzVu4qilAEaxp5BRfShkEZY/edit?usp=sharing)


## Files

*   **Challenge I__Training.csv** - the training set for Challenge I
*   **Challenge I__X_Test.csv** - the test set for Challenge I
*   **Challenge I__Sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format for Challenge I
*   **Challenge II__Training.csv** - training set for Challenge II (available after Challenge I ends)




--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------",True,True
