5049,stat440-21-project3a,"# Description

According to United News of Bangladesh, the three top three sources of pollution in Bangladesh are cars, brick kilns, and construction. The Bangladeshi government has issued orders to move brick kilns away from schools or populated areas, and replace traditional brick kilns with more modern methods that do not pollute so much. For example, the United Nations Development Programme UNDP/GEF Project: Improving Kiln Efficiency in Brick Making Industry (GEF PIMS 1901) notes that ""... the traditional brick making business in Bangladesh is an informal industry which uses old kiln design and coal as the main fuel ... unrestricted concentration of brick kilns in certain regions in the country has given rise to a number of environmental issues."" Identification of the locations of brick kilns in Bangladesh is required to reduce pollution in Bangladesh and follow government policy. In this project, you will determine if chimney kilns are present in satellite imagery (differentiating between images without kilns, or with modernized versions of kilns such as zig-zag kilns and not chimney kilns). The data included in this competition are dedicated to the public domain.
----------------------
# Evaluation

You will work alone submit predictions about which images in the test set are kilns. Your submission will be judged based on AUC. You should submit a ""score"" for each testing item: a number between 0 and 1 (a continuous real number in [0,1] representing the probability: 0 indicates that you think there's a 0% chance that the image is of a chimney kiln, 1 = 100% that the image is of a chimney kiln). In the train ing a testing sets, a label of 0 indicates that it's not a chimney kiln. A 1 indicates that either there is no kiln, or it's a modernized version of a kiln such as a zig-zag kiln (and not a chimney kiln).

For project 3, you must submit to the leader board for either project 3A or project 3B or both. You must also provide a 2 page summary of your work (including code after the 2 pages) to crowdmark for either project 3A or project 3B. You can submit the summary and code for either project 3A or project 3B (but not both). You must not submit the summary and code for both. But you can submit to both leaderboards. If you submit to both leaderboards, your place on the leaderboard for the project that you don't submit the summary and code for on crowdmark will be treated as a bonus mark (up to 5% of total mark for course).

Between project 3A and project 3B, you must submit one of them to crowdmark. The one that you submit to crowdmark, you must include the following:

- A cover page (your name, student id, a title, your kaggle username, and an indication of if you're doing project 3A or project 3B for your summary).
- A two page summary of the project (the methods you used, the results you got).
- Additional pages of all of the code you used for your submissions.

This all must be submitted as a pdf to crowdmark.","# Dataset Description

- *images/*.png* - Satellite imagery. Each image has an *id* given by the filename (before the *.png* extension).
- *Ytr.txt* - comma separated variables label indicating if a kiln is in the corresponding image. The first column is the *id*, the second column is the label: *0 =* no chimney kiln, *1 =* chimney kiln. In *Ytr.txt*, there are only rows for ids that are in the training set.
- *pred.txt* - a submission ready version of the test set. The first column is the id (must match the file names of the testing items of *images/*.png*). In this file, the prediction is *0* for everything, but you must replace this with a score between *0* and *1* indicating your belief about the probability that the testing item is a chimney kiln or not (*0.0 = 0%* chance that it's a chimney kiln, *1.0 = 100%* chance that it's a chimney kiln).",True,True
