5059,ucu-ds-homework-3,"# Description

Build a regression model to predict ZNO results! Depend variable is ```BALL100```, others are predictors.

You have two new files: ```zno2020_test_dataset.rds``` and ```zno2020_test_submission.csv``` that you should use to make a prediction.

You should use old ZNO files from Homework 1 and Homework 2 to build a model. 

I expect, as a result, two files ```.R``` or ```.Rmd``` a file with code of your model (also data preparation and feature engineering) and ```.csv``` a file with price predictions test data. Also I need your estimated error that gives your model (only value of MAE).

Your goal is to predict better ZNO results than your colleagues using less features and better estimating correctness of your model. You can use only R and only regression methods ```(lm, glm)```.

But you have three losses that you should be careful about:

- **accuracy loss** (mae - mean absolute error);
- **expectation loss** (how correctly you estimate expected error);
- **complexity loss** (how many predictors you use to build a regression model).

## How many points you can get

- **accuracy loss**: (max. - 25 pt.)

It will be calculated relatively to the results of your colleagues: 

- median result - 15 pt.
- 2 sd lower than the median result - 5 pt.
- 2 sd upper than the median result or the best result - 25 pt.
- 3 sd lover than the median result - 1 pt.

Suppose median error == 33 and sd == 2

37 == 5 pt. => 34 == 12.5 pt., 31 == 20 pt. (if the best result is 2 sd upper than the median) etc.

Suppose the best result == 30.5 (is *not* 2 sd upper than the median) => 31 == 23 pt.

- **expectation loss**: accuracy_loss*(0.5/sqrt(abs(train_mae-test_mae)))

If the expectation loss > 25 pt. it will be truncated to 25 pt.

- **complexity loss**: (accuracy_loss*(sqrt(1/predictors_count)/0.2981424) - accuracy_loss)

If the complexity loss > 25 pt. it will be truncated to 25 pt.

If the sum of points > 50 pt. it will be truncated to 50 pt.


```{r setup, include=FALSE}
knitr::opts_chunk$set(echo = TRUE)
```

## Data

```{r cars}
suppressPackageStartupMessages(require(dplyr))

zno <- readr::read_rds(""~/UCU-R/data/zno.rds"") %>% 
  select(!ends_with(""12"")) %>% 
  mutate(FORBALL100 = pmax(ENGBALL100, FRABALL100, DEUBALL100, SPABALL100, na.rm = TRUE)) %>%
  select(-c(ENGBALL100, FRABALL100, DEUBALL100, SPABALL100))

zno <- zno %>% select(-ends_with(""100"")) %>% 
  left_join(
  zno %>% select(OUTID, ends_with(""100"")) %>%
    tidyr::pivot_longer(
      -OUTID,
      names_to = c(""course"", "".value""),
      names_pattern = ""(.*)(BALL\\d*)"",
      values_drop_na = TRUE
    ), by = ""OUTID""
) %>% filter(!is.na(BALL100))

glimpse(zno)
```

## Objectives

1) build a model to predict BALL100 variable;
2) you should optimize mean absolute error: ```mean(abs(real_ball-predicted_ball))``` and estimate it on training data (I need this value to estimate correctness of your validation);
3) you should make prediction of price for test data and write it to ```.csv``` file in the following format:
```OUTID|course|BALL100```. (I need it to estimate accuracy of your model).

## Hints

- Use ```link``` parameter in family functions to specify target-variable transformation. But remember that some transformation (log) needs inverse-transformation in prediction (exp).
- Use quasipoisson model to handle overdispersion problem.
- Use the Null Deviance and the Residual Deviance to calculate $R^2$ in ```glm```, specifically: ```1 - (Residual Deviance/Null Deviance)```
- Models with predictor with high p-value may be overfitted!!

## Initial example

```{r}
fit <- lm(BALL100 ~ I(BIRTH - year) + Kyiv_bi + ukr_class_lang_bi + EOTYPE_bi + 
            course + rural_bi, data=zno)

summary(fit)

cat(paste(""Expected MAE"", mean(abs(zno$BALL100 - fit$fitted.values))))
```

```{r}
test_submission <- readr::read_csv(""~/UCU-R/data/zno2020_test_submission.csv"")
test_dataset <- readr::read_rds(""~/UCU-R/data/zno2020_test_dataset.rds"")

prediction_df <- tibble(
  OUTID = test_submission$OUTID,
  course = test_submission$course, 
  BALL100 = predict(fit, test_submission %>% left_join(
    test_dataset, on = ""OUTID""
  ))
)

head(prediction_df)

readr::write_csv(prediction_df, ""~/UCU-R/data/initial_predict.csv"")
prediction_df %>% mutate(OUTID = paste0(OUTID, ""_"", course)) %>%
  select(-course) %>%
  readr::write_csv(""~/UCU-R/data/initial_predict_for_kaggle.csv"")
```

## How many poits can get this work?

Suppose in the test set MAE for this model == 32 and it is median result. Also this model has 4 predictors. And you have expected MAE result 31.6

- **accuracy loss**: 15 pt.
- **expectation loss**: 15 * (0.5 / sqrt(abs(31.6 - 32))) = 11.85854 pt.
- **complexity loss**: (15 * (sqrt(1 / 4) / 0.2981424) - 15) = 10.15576 pt. 

**Total result**: 15 + 11.85854 + 10.15576 = 37.0143 pt.

## Submission parts

- ```.R``` or ```.Rmd``` with model code;
- ```predict.csv``` with prediction;
- Expected MAE estimation (your validation result).

## Kaggle check

I have created Kaggle competition that you can use to check your model performance on the part of the test data. See here: https://www.kaggle.com/c/ucu-ds-homework-3

Needed submission format for Kaggle: OUUTID_course|BALL100 (1 and 2 column concatenated)

