5062,medml-mic,"# Description

# Participants are required to classify the given images into pneumonic or normal and submit the CSV.

## Machine Learning
It is the study of computer algorithms that can improve automatically through experience and by the use of data. It is seen as a part of artificial intelligence. 

## Image Processing
It is the analysis and manipulation of a digitized image, especially in order to improve its quality.

## AI in Medical Imaging 
It dramatically improves diagnostic performance by automatically extracting features of complex and precise medical images, and comparing their differences. Image analysis by AI algorithm is superior to the traditional image analysis method.


----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is Classification Accuracy.

## Submission Format


| name |  pneumonic |
| --- | --- |
| file\_name\_1\.jpeg | 0 |
| file\_name\_2\.jpeg | 1 |
| ... | ... |
| file\_name\_n\.jpeg | Prediction for file n |","# Dataset Description

You will be provided with 5 folders : 
<br>
1) train/normal : Contains x-rays of normal pairs of lungs for training(1341 nos.)
<br>
2) train/pneumonic : Contains x-rays of pneumonic pairs of lungs for training(3875 nos.)
<br>
3) val/normal : Contains x-rays of normal pairs of lungs for validation(8 nos.)
<br>
4) val/pneumonic : Contains x-rays of pneumonic pairs of lungs for validation(8 nos.)
<br>
5) Test : Contains anonymized x-rays pairs of lungs for classification. (624 nos.)",True,True
5063,moscow-housing-2,"# Description

<h1>Description</h1>
In this challenge you will predict the prices of apartments in Moscow.

Real estate is a popular investment vehicle and can be pretty lucurative. Whether you're an individual looking for a place to call home or an investor looking for a profitable opportunity, being able to appraise assets accurately - or at least better than the next guy - can be of huge value.

We have procured a dataset consisting of information about over 33,000 apartments in Moscow. It contains around 30 variables that encode data such as location, facilities, and building information. Your task is to do analysis, feature engineering, and ultimately create models that can reliably predict the listed price of both low- and high-end apartments.

To get started, please make yourself familiar with the Kaggle competition page. The data can be found under the [Data](data) tab. A more hands-on tutorial of the data can be found in the Introduction Notebook accessible from the [Code](code) tab. Once you have created a submission yourself, the score will show up in the [Leaderboard](leaderboard) tab. Click the [Evaluation](overview/evaluation) section in the menu to the left to see how the scores are calculated. This is the home page of the competition. You don't need a subtitle here. The competition sub-title will appear above. This is where you introduce the problem. You can upload images using the ""Insert Photo"" icon on the bar that displays above this textbox when in edit mode.

----------------------
# Evaluation


<h2>Evaluation Metric</h2>
<p>
Submissions for this challenge will be evaluated according to the `Root Mean Squared Log Error` ((\text{RMSLE})) of your apartment price predictions.
</p>
<p>
\[ 
\text{RMSLE}(y, \hat{y}) = \sqrt{\frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^{n} (\log(1 + \hat{y}\_i) - \log(1 + y\_i))^2 }
\]
</p>
<p>
In the equation above, (y_i) corresponds to the ground truth for datapoint (i), (\hat{y}_i) corresponds to the predicted value for datapoint (i), and (n) denotes the total number of datapoints.
</p>

**Note:** Remember that what you see on the leaderboard is only the public test set score which is RMSLE calculated over 50% of the full test set. In the end, you will be evaluated based on your predictions on the private test set which consists of the remaining 50%.

## Sample Submission

You should submit your test set predictions in the CSV format. The file should contain a header row and then one row for each test set apartment. This means that the total number of lines in the file should be equal to `1 + len(test_set) = 1 + 9937 = 9938`. Each row should have two columns; one with the apartment ID `(id)` and one with your prediction `(price_prediction)`. An example of the first 5 rows of a submission file is given below:

```
id,price_prediction
23285,82000000.0
23286,8800000.0
23287,6799644.869565218
23288,10100000.0
```","# Dataset Description


<h2>File descriptions</h2>

<p>
When downloading and extracting the dataset, you will find the following files:
</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>apartments_train.csv</strong> - training set apartments</li>
<li><strong>apartments_test.csv</strong> - testing set apartments</li>

<li><strong>buildings_train.csv</strong> - extra building information for training set apartments</li>
<li><strong>buildings_test.csv</strong> - extra building information for testing set apartments</li>

<li><strong>apartments_meta.json</strong> - Metadata about columns in apartments (train/test)</li>
<li><strong>buildings_meta.json</strong> - Metadata about columns in buildings (train/test)</li>

<li><strong>moscow.png</strong> - A streetmap of Moscow used in the introduction notebook</li>
</ul>

Note that the building data is given in a separate file because several apartments may map to the same building.

<h2>Apartment Data Fields</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>id</strong> - Unique ID for apartment </li>
<li><strong>seller</strong> - The type of apartment seller </li>
<li><strong>price</strong> - The listed price of the apartment (TARGET: only available in train)</li>
<li><strong>area_total</strong> - Total area of the apartment </li>
<li><strong>area_kitchen</strong> - Total kitchen area in the apartment </li>
<li><strong>area_living</strong> - Total living space area in the apartment </li>
<li><strong>floor</strong> - Primary building floor of the apartment </li>
<li><strong>rooms</strong> - Number of rooms in the apartment </li>
<li><strong>layout</strong> - Overal apartment layout </li>
<li><strong>ceiling</strong> - Ceiling height in the apartment </li>
<li><strong>bathrooms_shared</strong> - Number of shared bathrooms </li>
<li><strong>bathrooms_private</strong> - Number of private bathrooms </li>
<li><strong>windows_court</strong> - Whether the apartment has windows facing a courtyard </li>
<li><strong>windows_street</strong> - Whether the apartment has windows facing a street </li>
<li><strong>balconies</strong> - Number of balconies in the apartment </li>
<li><strong>loggias</strong> - Number of loggias in the apartment (balcony-like, google it) </li>
<li><strong>condition</strong> - Current condition of the apartment </li>
<li><strong>phones</strong> - Number of phone numbers associated with the advert </li>
<li><strong>building_id</strong> - ID used to map apartments to buildings </li>
</ul>

<h2>Building Data Fields</h2>

<ul>
<li><strong>id</strong> - Unique ID of building for joining with apartments </li>
<li><strong>new</strong> - Whether it is an old or new building </li>
<li><strong>latitude</strong> - Latitude coordinate of building </li>
<li><strong>longitude</strong> - Longitude coordinate of building </li>
<li><strong>district</strong> - Administriative district within Moscow </li>
<li><strong>street</strong> - Bulding street name </li>
<li><strong>address</strong> - Building address (within street) </li>
<li><strong>constructed</strong> - Year when the building was constructed </li>
<li><strong>material</strong> - Primary building material used in building </li>
<li><strong>stories</strong> - Total number of floors in the building </li>
<li><strong>elevator_without</strong> - Whether the building has apartments without elevator access </li>
<li><strong>elevator_passenger</strong> - Whether the building has apartments with passenger elevator access </li>
<li><strong>elevator_service</strong> - Whether the building has apartments with service elevator access </li>
<li><strong>parking</strong> - Parking options for building </li>
<li><strong>garbage_chute</strong> - Whether the building has a garbage chute system </li>
<li><strong>heating</strong> - Primary heating system used in the building </li>
</ul>",True,True
