5073,ucla-stats-101c-2021-lec4-new,"# Description

The final project dataset contains n observations of p variables. Your job is to develop a method for predicting the variable Y based on all other variables except variable 'Id'. You may use any of the techniques covered in class.
----------------------
# Evaluation

To evaluate this problem, we use the classic root mean squared error (RMSE) metric to measure the distance between your predicted value and the true value.

Mathematically, given the response values g and your predictions \hat{g}, the RMSE is computed as follows:

\[ RMSE = \sqrt{\frac{1}{n} \sum\_{i=1}^{n} (g\_i - \hat{g}\_i)^2 } \],

where n is the number of observations in the test data. Your goal is to predict Y close to the truth response so that the RMSE is minimized.
----------------------
# Introduction

## Getting Started
You can download the training and test data by clicking on the Data tab. 

This is individual work and should be completed independently.

## Submit Predictions
Once you are confident about the predicted responses for all observations in the test set, please submit a comma-separated values (CSV) file with two columns. The first column is called ""Id"" and it should be the same as the ""Id"" column in the test.csv file. The second column is called ""pred"" (the predicted values of Y). An example of a correct submission file is sample.csv located under the Data tab.

## Report
In addition to your score in this competition, you will submit a report. At the end of the report. Your report must be a pdf file. Below, I provide guidelines on the components your report should at least have:

1. Introduction. A brief description of the problem. My advice is that you do not write a lengthy introduction, but just give the necessary components, like the goal of the problem.

2. Methodology. A description of modelling methods you use. In this competition, you can use any data mining methods or statistical models. Please refer to the Rules of this competition for more information on how to use methods not seen in class. If you use a method not seen in class, you are expected to give an explanation of the main features of the method as well as some references. If you use methods seen in class, you do not need to provide this information.

3. Results. Describe your final model accompanied by a good motivation of why you used this model. An example of a good motivation is to demonstrate that the model you chose outperformed all other alternative models you tried. 
 
4. Appendix. An Appendix showing your code. The code should be such that I can run it and get the exact same output as you. Ideally, the code is well-documented and organized. 

5. Optional: References. Please make a correct use of the references and the citations within the main text. For instance, you can follow an APA format.


Once again, the components mentioned above are the least a report must have. In your report, every decision and method you use should be well motivated and clearly explained. The challenge of the report is to strike a balance between an ¡°executive summary¡± that has only the main features, and a good level of detail so that the grader can follow your entire reasoning.

The length of the report should be no more than 3 one-sided pages (not counting the Appendix nor the References). It should include informative graphs which are legible and of a good size. The correct way to include graphs is to label them (e.g. Figure 1), and discuss them within the text. For instance, ¡°Figure 1 shows that our model outperforms the other models we tried in terms of the RMSE.¡± That is the proper way to include figures in a report. If you are concerned about the page limit, you can take advantage of the Appendix. 

From your report, the grader should be able to follow your entire statistical reasoning smoothly and without too much trouble. Please also take into account that your grader will be tired at some point in time, which means that if your report is evaluated at the end, the grader will probably not have the same energy as when he started grading. So, it is very important that your report is well-written and comprehensive.

The report will be graded on a scale of 0 to 1. The three main evaluation rubrics are: completeness, comprehensiveness, well-motivated, and making a correct use of statistical techniques. Below, I provide a rough guide of the scoring of the report.

```
1.0 if the report is excellent: entirely comprehensive, well-motivated and complete. Additionally, it demonstrates a correct use of statistical techniques.

0.9 if the report is complete and demonstrates a correct use of statistical techniques, but it fails to be either entirely clear or well-motivated.

0.8 if the report is complete with a correct use of statistical techniques, but with several decisions or statements that are not entirely clear or not well-motivated.

0.7 if the report is incomplete, unclear and not well-motivated, in several of its sections. However, it demonstrates a good use of the statistical techniques.

0.6 if the report is incomplete and has sensible mistakes in terms of statistical reasoning.

0.0 if the report is not submitted.
```
The name of your report should be ¡°lastname\_firstname.pdf¡±, eg: ¡°shi\_chenlu.pdf¡±. You must submit the report to Section ¡°Final Project¡± of CCLE before December 11 at 11.59 PM (PST).




## Kaggle score
The evaluation metric for this competition is the Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE). Your Kaggle score will be calculated based on whether or not your model provides a smaller RMSE value on the test data than four benchmark models. The four models range from weak to strong in terms of the RMSE for the test data. Model 1 is the weakest model while Model 4 is the strongest.

Your Kaggle score will be assigned according to the following framework:

```
1.0 if your model has a smaller RMSE value on the test data than Models 1, 2, 3 and 4.

0.75 if your model has a smaller RMSE value on the test data than Models 1, 2 and 3.

0.50 if your model has a smaller RMSE value on the test data than Models 1 and 2.

0.25 if your model has a smaller RMSE value on the test data than Model 1.

0.0 if your model has a larger RMSE value on the test data than all benchmark models.
```

`Remark` The actual RMSE value used in the above calculation is not that displayed on the Public Leaderboard under the Leaderboard tab. By default, Kaggle also includes a Private Leaderboard, which is only available for the hosts. The results in Private Leaderboard are calculated similarly as for Public Leaderboard. However, the private results use a private test dataset. This also applies to the four benchmark models. Therefore, your model must not only perform well on the public test dataset, but must also generalize well to the private test dataset. Nevertheless, your Kaggle score will be computed using Private Leaderboard only, since this provides an unbiased assessment of the quality of the models.

## Grading
The final grade of each student is calculated as 40 Ks + 60 Rs, where Ks is the Kaggle score (calculated using the private test dataset), and Rs is the score of the report.
----------------------
# Deadlines

23:59 PST - December 7, 2021. End of the competition.

23:59 PST - December 11, 2021. Deadline for submitting the report.
----------------------
# Updates

Please use your full name as the team name and the display name, eg. Chenlu Shi.","# Dataset Description

The training data is in the file training.csv. The dataset includes 1000 observations of the response variable Y and 15 predictors.

The test data is in file test.csv. The dataset includes 2750 observations of 15 predictors. The test data does not include the Y column. The challenge is to closely predict the true value of Y in this dataset. The 'Id' column in this dataset identifies each observation in the test data. To avoid confusions, the values in the 'Id' column in the test.csv file are different from those in the training.csv file. We use more data points to evaluate the model here, which, but, is not the case for real applications. 

An example of a correct submission file is in sample.csv. The file must contain two columns labeled 'Id' and 'pred'. The values in the 'Id' column in the submission file must match those of the corresponding column in the test.csv file. In the submission file, the 'pred' column must contain the predictions of Y using predictors in the test data. The values in this 'pred' column must be numeric. The submission file must not include the predictor columns.",True,True
