5086,airplane-direction,"# Description

Petka and Vasily Ivanovich are flying in an airplane, and suddenly Vasily Ivanovich, sitting at the pilot's wheel, cries out: ¡°Petka, instruments! The answer follows: ""Thirty-eight."" ""What is thirty-eight?"" - ""And what about the instruments?""

On the plane, all the signatures to the instruments have disappeared, and our heroes cannot determine whether they are flying up or down in the clouds. The heroes have an accumulated array of data, while the horizon was visible, the data array is the values ??of the dashboards and the target variable is binary (1 or 0) - the plane is flying up or down. You need to predict the probability that the value of the target variable is 1 (a number between 0 and 1) for a new array of values.
----------------------
# Evaluation

## Evaluation metric

Accuracy is one metric for evaluating classification models. Informally, accuracy is the fraction of predictions our model got right. Formally, accuracy has the following definition:

`Accuracy = Number of correct predictions/ Total number of predictions`","# Dataset Description

Petka and Vasily Ivanovich are flying in an airplane, and suddenly Vasily Ivanovich, sitting at the pilot's wheel, cries out: ¡°Petka, instruments! The answer follows: ""Thirty-eight."" ""What is thirty-eight?"" - ""And what about the instruments?""

On the plane, all the signatures to the instruments have disappeared, and our heroes cannot determine whether they are flying up or down in the clouds. The heroes have an accumulated array of data, while the horizon was visible, the data array is the values ??of the dashboards and the target variable is binary (1 or 0) - the plane is flying up or down. You need to predict the probability that the value of the target variable is 1 (a number between 0 and 1) for a new array of values.

## Files

*   **train.csv** - array of aircraft instrument readings and target
*   **test.csv** - array of aircraft instrument readings",True,True
5087,a-tour-of-machine-learning-2021,"# Description

Histone modifications play an important role in affecting gene regulation. Specific histone modifications at specific locations in or near the gene can alter the expression of genes. Predicting gene expression from histone modification signals is a widely studied research topic.

In this competition you will predict gene expression levels (low=0, high=1) based on the presence of histone modifications at specific locations in the gene. You will try to find the model that learns the true underlying model best.

For each gene a region of 10.000bp around the transcription start site of the gene is extracted (5000bp upstream and 5000bp downstream). This region is binned in 100 bins of 100bp. For each bin five core histone modification marks are counted [1].

The dataset is compiled from the ""E047"" (Primary T CD8+ naive cells from peripheral blood) celltype from Roadmap Epigenomics Mapping Consortium (REMC) database.

[1] Kundaje, A. et al. Integrative analysis of 111 reference human epige-
nomes. Nature, 518, 317¨C330, 2015.
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is simple accuracy. 

</p><h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p><strong>For every gene in the test set</strong>, submission files should contain two columns: GeneId and Label. GeneId should match the GeneId in the test set and Label is the corresponding predicted probability for class 1 (high).</p>

<p>You can download the sample_submission.csv for an example.</p>","# Dataset Description

<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>data_train.csv</strong> - the train set</li>
<li><strong>data_test.csv</strong> - the test set</li>
<li><strong>sample_submission.csv</strong> - a sample submission file in the correct format</li>
</ul>",True,True
