5092,learning-phase-competition,"# Description

This is part of the learning phase for the Image Inpainting Project (IEEE). The deadline for making entries into this competition is 7 December 2021.


----------------------
# Evaluation

The file should contain a header and have the following format:
```
coarse_label, Id
1,1
```","# Dataset Description

# Similar Datasets:
CIFAR-10 Python (in CSV): LINK

# Context
The CIFAR-100 dataset consists of 60000 32x32 colour images in 100 classes, with 600 images per class. The 100 classes in the CIFAR-100 are grouped into 20 superclasses. Each image comes with a ""fine"" label (the class to which it belongs) and a ""coarse"" label (the superclass to which it belongs). There are 50000 training images and 10000 test images.
The meta file contains the label names of each class and superclass.

# Content
Here is the list of the 100 classes in the CIFAR-100:

# Classes:
1-5) beaver, dolphin, otter, seal, whale

6-10) aquarium fish, flatfish, ray, shark, trout

11-15) orchids, poppies, roses, sunflowers, tulips

16-20) bottles, bowls, cans, cups, plates

21-25) apples, mushrooms, oranges, pears, sweet peppers

26-30) clock, computer keyboard, lamp, telephone, television

31-35) bed, chair, couch, table, wardrobe

36-40) bee, beetle, butterfly, caterpillar, cockroach

41-45) bear, leopard, lion, tiger, wolf

46-50) bridge, castle, house, road, skyscraper

51-55) cloud, forest, mountain, plain, sea

56-60) camel, cattle, chimpanzee, elephant, kangaroo

61-65) fox, porcupine, possum, raccoon, skunk

66-70) crab, lobster, snail, spider, worm

71-75) baby, boy, girl, man, woman

76-80) crocodile, dinosaur, lizard, snake, turtle

81-85) hamster, mouse, rabbit, shrew, squirrel

86-90) maple, oak, palm, pine, willow

91-95) bicycle, bus, motorcycle, pickup truck, train

96-100) lawn-mower, rocket, streetcar, tank, tractor

and the list of the 20 superclasses:

1) aquatic mammals (classes 1-5)

2) fish (classes 6-10)

3) flowers (classes 11-15)

4) food containers (classes 16-20)

5) fruit and vegetables (classes 21-25)

6) household electrical devices (classes 26-30)

7) household furniture (classes 31-35)

8) insects (classes 36-40)

9) large carnivores (classes 41-45)

10) large man-made outdoor things (classes 46-50)

11) large natural outdoor scenes (classes 51-55)

12) large omnivores and herbivores (classes 56-60)

13) medium-sized mammals (classes 61-65)

14) non-insect invertebrates (classes 66-70)

15) people (classes 71-75)

16) reptiles (classes 76-80)

17) small mammals (classes 81-85)

18) trees (classes 86-90)

19) vehicles 1 (classes 91-95)

20) vehicles 2 (classes 96-100)

# Acknowledgements
Learning Multiple Layers of Features from Tiny Images, Alex Krizhevsky, 2009. Link

# Load data
train and test - pickle files

```python
dt = pd.read_pickle('../input/cifar-100/train')
```

```python
df = pd.DataFrame.from_dict(dt, orient='index')
```

```python
df = df.T
```",True,True
5093,cars-price,"# Description

You will predict car prices by using information about the car, such as the brand, model, type of transmission and fuel, engine volume and mileage, and the year of manufacture. 

You can submit the solution 20 times.
Good luck!
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is [MAE](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mean_absolute_error).

## Submission Format

Submission file should contain two columns: `id` and `price`. 

The file should contain a header and have the following format:
```
id,price
0,2529
1,2529
2,2529
3,2529
...
```","# Dataset Description

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

## Columns

*   `id` - id
*   `brand`?- car brand
*   `model`?- car model
*   `year`?- year of car manufacture
*   `engine`?- engine volume
*   `fuel`?- fuel type
*   `transmission`?- transmission  type
*   `km`?- car mileage
*   `price` - car price",True,True
5094,ucl-ai-society-clickbait-detection,"# Description

---

## Dataset Description
### 1. train.csv
- `headline ` - headline of an article
- `clickbait ` - 1 for clickbait headline, 0 otherwise

### 2. test.csv
Same columns, but you do not have the `clickbait ` column, which means that you have to predict if the headline is clickbait or not ??  

---

## Learning outcomes
- Apply the knowledge you've gained in previous weeks: General ML techniques
- Immediately try out the NLP technologies!

## Acknowledgements

https://www.kaggle.com/amananandrai/clickbait-dataset
----------------------
# Evaluation

## Evaluation Metric

The evaluation metric for this competition is **Classification Accuracy**.  
It is defined by the number of correct predictions divided by the number of total predictions.

---

## Submission Format

**For every author in the dataset**, submission files should contain two columns: `id` and `Class`.

The file should contain a header and have the following format:
```
id, clickbait
0, 1
1, 0
2, 1
3, 0
```","# Dataset Description

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set. You should predict the `clickbait` based on this file
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

For more descriptions of columns, read https://www.kaggle.com/amananandrai/clickbait-dataset",True,True
5095,temple-sinkhole-classification-2021,"# Description

<p>For this assignment, you will address a real-world binary classification problem where the goal is to identify sinkholes from LIDAR data.</p>

<h2>Acknowledgements</h2>
<p>We thank Professors Zhu and Jacobs for providing this dataset.</p>
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is [Average Precision][1]. This metric is commonly used information retrieval and is designed for problems with imbalanced data (usually fewer positive examples). 
</p><h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p>Submission files should contain two columns: ID and isSinkhole. isSinkhole should be a numeric value, where larger values correspond to the most confident sinkhole predictions. These values can be probabilities or arbitrary scores; the values are unimportant, just the relative ordering. 


  [1]: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Evaluation_measures_(information_retrieval)#Average_precision</p>","# Dataset Description

<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>train.csv</strong> - the training set</li>
<li><strong>test.csv</strong> - the test set</li>
<li><strong>sample.csv</strong> - a sample submission file in the correct format</li>
<li><strong>DataDescription.docx</strong> - supplemental information about the data</li>
</ul>",True,True
5096,2021-nycu-ml-hw5,"# Description

- Artificial Neural Networks - 30%
    - for ANN model, you could use any Neural Network base model you want and implement it by yourself.
    - Every framework (such as TensorFlow or PyTorch) is allowed.
    - explain the reasoning of your model choice, data augmentation, and training process.
----------------------
# Evaluation

For simplicity, the evaluation metric for this competition is overall [Accuracy](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Accuracy_and_precision#In_binary_classification):
$$ Accuracy=\frac{tp+tn}{tp+tn+fp+fn} $$

### Submission Format
You must submit a CSV file with exactly 10000 entries plus a header row.
The file should have exactly 2 columns:
- Id (sorted in any order)
- Category (must be string from all possible cuisine)
```
Id,Category
15717,mexican
25265,moroccan
6935,indian
46557,korean
8678,greek
```
Etc.
Sample submission file (sample_submission.csv) can be downloaded in [Data secsion](https://www.kaggle.com/c/2021-nycu-ml-hw5/data).","# Dataset Description

In the dataset, we include the recipe id, the type of cuisine, and the list of ingredients of each recipe (of variable length). The data is stored in JSON format.

An example of a recipe node in `train.json`:

```
 {  
 ""id"": 24717,  
 ""cuisine"": ""indian"",  
 ""ingredients"": [  
     ""tumeric"",  
     ""vegetable stock"",  
     ""tomatoes"",  
     ""garam masala"",  
     ""naan"",  
     ""red lentils"",  
     ""red chili peppers"",  
     ""onions"",  
     ""spinach"",  
     ""sweet potatoes""  
 ]  
 }
```

In the test file `test.json`, the format of a recipe is the same as `train.json`, only the cuisine type is removed, as it is the target variable you are going to predict.",True,True
5097,gse-524-f2021,"# Description

## DATA SOURCE

The board game company Cards Against Humanity (CAH) is known for putting a lot of effort into silly publicity stunts.  In 2017, they launched Cards Against Humanity Saves America, a series of politically-related activities.  One such activity was a monthly poll, asking some silly questions and some serious questions about the world. 

To conduct their polls in a scientifically rigorous manner, they partnered with Survey Sampling International ¡ª a professional research firm ¡ª to contact a nationally representative sample of the American public.

The analyses of the data conducted by CAH can be found here: https://thepulseofthenation.com/#poll-12.  You are welcome to reference their analyses to help inform your own.  Note that some questions from the poll have been omitted for purposes of this exam, and that others have been cleaned or edited so that not all possible responses are included.  

Please also note that, while the data itself is collected by a rigorous neutral party, the discussion on the CAH website is highly opinionated.  I share it for you information, not as an endorsement of their messages.

---

## GOAL

Your mission today is to use the questions asked in the CAH poll to predict the **political affiliation (Democrat, Republican, or Independent)** of the individual.

---

## Only your first two submissions will be graded!
----------------------
# Evaluation

## METRIC

The evaluation metric for this competition is the overall **accuracy** of your predictions. 

## SUBMISSION FORMAT

Submission files should contain two columns: `id_num` and `political_affiliation`.  The ID Numbers should be the same as those in the `test.csv` file, and the `political_affiliation_predicted` values should be your predicted affiliation. 

You can generate a properly formatted prediction file as follows, assuming `test_data` is your test dataset and `final_model_fit` is your fitted final chosen model:
```
final_predictions &lt;- data.frame(
    id_num  = test_data$id_num,
    political_affiliation_predicted = predict(final_model_fit, test_data)$.pred_class
)
```

```
final_predictions %&gt;% write_csv(""final_predictions.csv"")
```

*(Note: This will save the file `final_predictions.csv` to your working directory.  If you aren't sure how to find that, I am happy to help.)*
----------------------
# Grading

Your grade on this portion of the Final will be determined by the accuracy score achieved by your submitted predictions.

---

## GRADE TIERS

**35% or below:** F
**35%-40%:** D
**40%-45%:** C-
**45%-50%:** C
**50%-52%:** C+
**52%-54%:** B-
**54%-56%:** B
**56%-58%:** B+
**58%-60%:** A-
**60%-62%:** A
**62% and above%:** A+

## Challenge Points

The **top 5 scorers** will receive Challenge Points:

1st place: 25
2nd place: 20
3rd place: 15
4th place: 10
5th place: 5","# Dataset Description

## THE DATA

The survey that supplied this data was conducted in March 2018 by Survey Sampling International, at the request of Cards Against Humanity. 

Questions from the survey that are included in this dataset are:

*(Note: The bolded question is the target variable.)*

- What gender do you identify with?

- Age

- **In politics today, do you consider yourself a Democrat, a Republican, or Independent?**

- Would you say you are liberal, conservative, or moderate?

- What is your highest level of education?

- What is your race?

- Do you believe that prostitution should be against the law?

- Do you believe that it should be against the law to smoke marijuana for recreation?

- Do you believe the sale of human organs for transplants should be against the law?

- Would you consider yourself a religious person?

- Would you consider yourself pro-life or pro-choice?

- Do you generally disapprove of people who pursue casual sex, hookups, or one-night-stands?

- Based on your general impressions, would you say that people who smoke marijuana tend to have more casual sex than people who abstain from smoking marijuana?

- If abortion became strictly banned, do you think women in general would become more willing to have casual sex, less willing to have casual sex, or behave no differently?

- Do you agree or disagree with the following statement: A woman should have the right to do what she wants with her own body.

- Do you agree or disagree with this next statement: Abortion is morally wrong.

- And do you agree or disagree with this next statement: Sex without love is okay.

- Do you believe that an elected official who has committed sexual misconduct in their personal life can still behave ethically and fulfill their duties while in office?",True,True
5098,gse-524-fall-2021-ames,"# Description

## Data

This dataset consists of information about properties sold in Ames, Iowa.
You may have seen this data before, and you may find examples of it being used elsewhere online.  The version in this competition has many of the variables removed, for uniqueness and simplicity.

## Data fields

Here's a brief description of the variables:

* SalePrice - the property's sale price in dollars. This is the target variable that you're trying to predict.
* PID - An identifying number of the property
* LotFrontage: Linear feet of street connected to property
* LotArea: Lot size in square feet
* Street: Type of road access
* Neighborhood: Physical locations within Ames city limits
* BldgType: Type of dwelling
* HouseStyle: Style of dwelling
* OverallQual: Overall material and finish quality
* OverallCond: Overall condition rating
* YearBuilt: Original construction date
* RoofStyle: Type of roof
* Heating: Type of heating
* CentralAir: Central air conditioning
* Electrical: Electrical system
* GrLivArea: Above grade (ground) living area square feet
* FullBath: Full bathrooms above grade
* HalfBath: Half baths above grade
* Bedroom AbvGr: Number of bedrooms above basement level
* TotRmsAbvGrd: Total rooms above grade (does not include bathrooms)
* Functional: Home functionality rating
* ScreenPorch: Screen porch area in square feet
* PoolArea: Pool area in square feet
* YrSold: Year Sold
* SaleType: Type of sale

## GOAL
Your mission today is to use the available variables to predict the sales price of homes.

## Only your first two submissions will be graded!

## Acknowledgments
The Ames Housing dataset was compiled by Dean De Cock for use in data science education. It's an incredible alternative for data scientists looking for a modernized and expanded version of the often cited Boston Housing dataset. 
----------------------
# Evaluation

## Goal

It is your job to predict the sales price for each house. For each `PID` in the test set, you must predict the value of the `SalePrice` variable. 

## Metric

Submissions are evaluated on **Root-Mean-Squared-Error (RMSE)** between the *logarithm* of the predicted value and the *logarithm* of the observed sales price. (Taking logs means that errors in predicting expensive houses and cheap houses will affect the result equally.)

## Submission File Format

The file should contain a header and have the following format:

| PID | SalePrice |
| 907135180 | 169000.1 |
| 903206120  |   187724.1233 |

etc.

You can download an example submission file (`sample_submission.csv`) on the Data page.
----------------------
# Grading

Your grade on this portion of the Final will be determined by the accuracy score achieved by your submitted predictions.

---

## GRADE TIERS

*Note: these may be subject to change depending on outcomes.  If they are changed, they will only change in the direction of your advantage; i.e., your grade will be higher than expected.*

**0.21 or above:** F
**0.21 - 0.17:** D range
**0.17 - 0.16:** C range
**0.16 - 0.15:** B range
**0.15 - 0.14:** A range
**0.14 and below:** A+

## Challenge Points

The **top 5 scorers** will receive Challenge Points:

1st place: 25
2nd place: 20
3rd place: 15
4th place: 10
5th place: 5","# Dataset Description

## Files

*   **train_new.csv** - The training set.  There may be missing values.
*   **test_new.csv** - The test set, which you will make predictions on.  There are no missing values.
*   **sample_submission_new.csv** - A sample submission file in the correct format

## Columns for Submission

*   `PID` - The property ID
*   `SalePrice`?- The predicted price of the property


## IMPORTANT

Remember that your predictions are being assessed based on the **log** RMSE, but that your submissions should have the sale price predictions on the **original** scale.",True,True
