5110,egris-aprendizaje-automtico,"# Description

Dada la base ¡°tratamiento termico.csv¡± ya trabajada en clase, notar que la ¨²ltima columna llamada ¡°FN¡± est¨¢ asociada a tubos que est¨¢n Fuera de norma. Esto significa que alguna de las propiedades mec¨¢nicas medidas luego del proceso no cumplen con la banda o tolerancia exigida por la norma petrolera est¨¢ndar API.

a) Construir un modelo de clasificaci¨®n para definir si cierto tubo estar¨¢ en norma (FN=0) o fuera de norma (FN=1). La m¨¦trica que interesa para este proceso en particular es la siguiente:
Utilidad media = ( 200*TP + 0*TN + (- 400)*FN +(¨C 100)*FP ) / (TP+TN+FP+FN) Es decir, se pondera de forma diferente cada evento de la matriz de confusi¨®n.

b) El modelo elegido deber¨¢ ser aplicado sobre un conjunto ¡°test.csv¡±, el cual tiene ¨²nicamente las variables de proceso (es decir que no tiene medidas la Fluencia, Rotura ni Dureza). La predicci¨®n deber¨¢ ser subida al sitio. Deber¨¢n generar una cuenta con usuario.

El sitio Kaggle devolver¨¢ el valor de la m¨¦trica lograda y publicar¨¢ su valor en un ranking junto con otros participantes. El valor obtenido deber¨¢ superar cierto umbral m¨ªnimo establecido. Si queda por debajo, deber¨¢ mejorar su modelo.
c) Una vez logrado superar el umbral, podr¨¢ elegir otro modelo que le interese para mejorar su m¨¦trica frente a otros. El orden de m¨¦rito que surja ser¨¢ una de las notas asociadas a la materia.
----------------------
# Evaluation

La evaluaci¨®n ser¨¢ a partir de la utilidad media de la matriz de confusi¨®n ponderada de la siguiente forma:
Matriz de costos = [0 -400; -100 200]
Es decir:
beneficio de TN --> a11 = 0
costo de FN --> a12 =-400
costo de FP --> a21=-100
beneficio de TP --> a22=200

La m¨¦trica ""utilidad media"" queda definida como:

utilidad media = ( a11TN + a12FN + a21FP + a22TP ) / (TN+FN+FP+TP)","# Dataset Description

train: archivo para entrenar
test: archivo para aplicar el modelo
modelo_random.csv : ejemplo de archivo para subir al sitio (puede tener cualquier nombre)",True,True
5111,rutgers-data101-fall2021-assignment-13,"# Description

This is the submission site for the Data 101 course competition for predictions on the Nov19EastWestCoastFlights.testB.csv dataset.

Prof. Amelie Marian - November 2021


----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is RMSE

Submission Format
For every flight in the dataset, submission files should contain two columns: Id and Predicted Delay.

The file should be submitted in csv format:

You can export your prediction from R to csv using the following command
```
write.csv(flightdelay.predictions,file=""flightdelay.predictions.csv"")

Replacing ""flightdelay.predictions"" with your predictions (result from predict()) and ""flightdelay.predictions.csv"" with the file name you want to save in.
```","# Dataset Description

Detailed information can be found in the course Canvas for Assignments 13.

## **File descriptions**

- Nov18EastWestCoastFlights.train.csv - the training set
- Nov19EastWestCoastFlights.testB.csv - the test set for the Kaggle competition
- FlightB.simplerpart.csv - sample submission files in the correct format derived from a simple rpart prediction model rpart(ARR_DELAY_NEW~MKT_CARRIER+ORIGIN+DEST,data=Nov18EastWestCoastFlights.train,cp=0.0001).",True,True
5112,6th-idss-ai-for-agriculture-competition-track1,"# Description

**Track 1: the problem of parameters prediction **

We propose the CLIMATE database (EV0_competition_IDSS.csv) in the folder
with the climate parameters including Ev0: evaporation from Gabes STATION AVFA ¨C AgriDATA

	T : Air temperature [¡ãC]
	P : Precipitation [mm]
	HR : Relative humidity [%]
	VV: Wind speed [m/s]
	RS : Solar radiation [W/m2]
	Ev0 : Evapo-transpiration 

We propose a system with the **5 parameters as inputs **and **EV0** will be the predicted parameter as **output**.

**Step 1 Data pre-processing** Including the interpolation of the missing values of dataset, and the normalization. The last is used to eliminate the dimension influence between climate factors. Data normalization can speed up the gradient descend algorithm to find the optimal solution and possibly improve the prediction accuracy. 

**Step 2 **Input the preprocessing and normalized data into the model network with parameters randomly initialized. And then set the prediction step length, train errors by batching network. After that, compare the predicted value with the real value to calculate whether the iteration condition is satisfied. Thus, the error should descend to a certain degree, or the number of iterations is reached. 

**Step 3** Use the training sample to run the model through step 3, then input the test sample into the well-trained 

**Step 4** Plot the Variation curves of the input variables 

**Step 5** Calculate the metrics values Root-mean-square deviation (RMSE) of the parameters Ev0 which is predicted
Step 6 Evaluate the trained classifier based on the test set. Plot the variation curves of the loss function.
The final evaluation will rely on the following metric	**Root-mean-square error (RMSE)**
**rmse = math.sqrt(mean_squared_error(y_test, pred))**
RMSE is a frequently used measure of the differences between values (sample or population values) predicted by a model or an estimator and the values observed. The RMSE represents the square root of the second sample moment of the differences between predicted values and observed values or the quadratic mean of these differences.
 The RMSE of predicted values  (y hat) for times t of a regression's dependent variable  y  with variables observed over T times, is computed for T different predictions as the square root of the mean of the squares of the deviations: 
  
All documents will be sent to **cis.tn@ieee.org**

 
 

----------------------
# Evaluation

Evaluation Metric
The evaluation metric for this competition is root-mean-square error (RMSE). The RMSE score, is a frequently used measure of the differences between values (sample or population values) predicted by a model or an estimator and the values observed. The RMSE score is given by:

[https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Root-mean-square_deviation]()

Submission Format
For every unique combination in the dataset, submission files should contain two columns: Id and EVo[mm].

The file should have the following format:
Id (the same column index in the test set file) column and EVo[mm] column.","# Dataset Description

