5165,find-person-in-the-dark,"# Description

## Introduction
Pedestrian detection technology has been developed very mature, but it is still a very big challenge to detect pedestrians in low light conditions. Although infrared light images can assist pedestrian detection, however, in most scenes, only visible light images are available. Therefore, we organized this competition to promote the research of pedestrian detection in low light conditions.

## Dataset
The dataset used in this competition contains 15030 visible light images(11782 images for training and 3248 for testing), it is a subset of our previous work: [LLVIP: A Visible-infrared Paired Dataset for Low-light Vision](https://arxiv.org/abs/2108.10831). In low light conditions, even the human eye can hardly distinguish pedestrians. Therefore, our pedestrian marking work is completed with the assistance of infrared light images.
## License
Our dataset is made freely available to academic and non-academic entities for non-commercial purposes such as academic research, teaching, scientific publications, or personal experimentation. Permission is granted to use the data given that you agree to our [license terms](https://github.com/bupt-ai-cz/LLVIP/blob/main/Term%20of%20Use%20and%20License.md).
If you use this data for your research, please cite our paper £º
```
@inproceedings{jia2021llvip,
  title={LLVIP: A Visible-infrared Paired Dataset for Low-light Vision},
  author={Jia, Xinyu and Zhu, Chuang and Li, Minzhen and Tang, Wenqi and Zhou, Wenli},
  booktitle={Proceedings of the IEEE/CVF International Conference on Computer Vision},
  pages={3496--3504},
  year={2021}
}
```
## Contact
If you encounter any problems please post them in the [Discussion](https://www.kaggle.com/c/find-person-in-the-dark/discussion) column without hesitation, or contact us directly by the following email:
- shengjie.Liu@bupt.edu.cn
- czhu@bupt.edu.cn
## Related
If you like our 'Find Person in the Dark' Dataset, you will probably be interested in our other researches, please visit [here](https://github.com/bupt-ai-cz) for more details.
----------------------
# Evaluation

## Evaluation Metric
The evaluation metric for this competition is **Average Precision** at IoU thresholds of 0.5(AP@0.5) and 0.75(AP@0.75). We provide an authoritative [tool](https://github.com/rafaelpadilla/Object-Detection-Metrics) for AP calculation. You can download the file ``evaluate.zip`` in the 'data' column to get the tool.
### How to use the evaluation tool?
- Step1: Download and unzip the ``evaluate.zip`` file. 
- Step2: For each test image, output a list of bounding boxes ``classlabel confidencescore xmin ymin width height`` for objects, and save them as a ``.txt`` file. For example:

``190001.txt``:
```
person .98 1000 300 120 260
person .89 1200 300 80 220
```
``190002.txt``:
```
person .95 260 570 120 300
```
- Step3: Put your predicted ``.txt`` files in the folder ``evaluate/detections``.
- Step4: Run the python script ``pascalvoc.py`` and you will get the AP value of your predicted results.
```
python pascalvoc.py --threshold 0.5
```
```
python pascalvoc.py --threshold 0.75
```

## Submission Format
Your score will be obtained by calculating the Mean Absolute Error between your AP values and 1.
The submission file should contain a header and have the following format:
```
ID,value
AP@0.5,0.871
AP@0.75,0.455

```","# Dataset Description

## File descriptions
- ``train.zip``: including all training images and annotations.
- ``test.zip``: including all testing images.
- ``train_data.txt``: the training records, the format of bounding boxes is ``classlabel xmin ymin width height``
- ``test_data.txt``: the testing records.
- ``evaluate.zip``: including scripts for evaluation.
- ``submission_example.csv``: an example of submission file.",True,True
5166,iitm-mba,"# Description

# Case Study
 
PayBuddy is an online payments platform based out of the U.S.

Its an Online Payments Platform that was split from PayBay in 2015 and became an independent company. The current CEO of the company is Michael. 
Michael hosts a diverse portfolio of credit products like credit card, pay later etc.
PayBuddy facilitates money transfer service for free of cost between any two PayBuddy users. 

To ensure competitive advantage over competing brands, the company needs to build a propensity model to predict the customer propensity to apply for the new line of credit card called Evolution Card.
This would enable the following things
- Increase net TPV and adoption of Evolution
- Provide optimal incentives and rewards
- Optimal customer targeting
- Minimize credit risks

PayBuddy has decided to conduct a hackathon to invite submissions and ideas to help build this model. The focus would be on training a model that is able to give a benchmarking performance. 

Since the Evolution card is very new and was launched only at the beginning of 2021, we have limited data for about 6-month time. The benchmarking metric would be the AUC score on PayBuddy¡¯s internal test data. 

Since Evolution is a cobrand card, there could be customerss who are not already existing customers of PayBuddy. Hence, it is advised to model PayBuddy and non- PayBuddy customers separately because PayBuddy customers might have an additional set of features derived from internal PayBuddy data that can improve model performance further. These features will not be present for customers who are not existing customers of PayBuddy. 
----------------------
# Evaluation

For evaluation of the contest, participants need submit a csv file with the following format:

| cust_id | target |
| --- | --- |
| 1 | 0 |
| 2 | 0.6 |
| 3 | 0.8 |
| ... | ... |

The evaluation of the contest is the AUC of the predictions.

Each submission will be calculated on 70% of the test data and will be displayed on the public leaderboard. On the Final Leaderboard 100% of the test data. It will be hidden and will be revealed at a later stage.","# Dataset Description

#Metadata - Propensity Data

Email_features --> Email based features (domain, etc.)

Cashout features --> Withdrawal based features

new_acct_memo_rcv --> received transaction based features

new_acct_fi --> financial instrument based features

new_acct_memo_sent --> emoji based features

new_acct_receiver --> Sender engagement features

new_acct_sender --> Receiver engagement features

payment_cashout_fail --> Failed withdrawal features

train_df --> Training Dataset

test_cust_id --> Testing Dataset

All Participants are meant to train their respective models and run it on the test_cust_id.csv file. Participants should follow the submission format as specified in the Evaluation section.",True,True
