5201,up-data-practicum-ii-202122-football,"# Description

Your goal is to predict the outcome of football matches: 1 for a home team victory, 0 for a draw and -1 for the away team victory. You are provided with a train set of past matches and a test set of matches for which the outcomes are unknown for you. The dataset is from a real national league but the team IDs have been obfuscated to limit reverse engineering.

Each row in the train set is a single match played between the home and away team. The columns have the following meaning:
- id: id of the match (unique)
- season: season in which the match has been played
- stage: stage of the match (1st week ... last week of the season)
- date
- home\_team\_api\_id: id of the home team
- away\_team\_api\_id: id of the away team
- home\_team\_goal: goals scored by the home team
- away\_team\_goal: goals scored by the away team
- B365H ... BSA: bookmakers coefficients for the home/away/match
- outcome: outcome of the match, which needs to be predicted (1 for a home team victory, 0 for a draw and -1 for the away team victory). This can be calculated from the home\_team\_goal and away\_team\_goal.

The test set has a similar structure as the train except for the columns ""home\_team\_goal"", ""away\_team\_goal"",  and ""outcome"". Your task is to create a submission CSV file that contains the id of the match from the test set and your prediction who the outcome (-1, 0, or 1)

```
id		outcome
4509        0
4510       -1
4511       -1
4512       -1
4513       -1
```

## What is (not) allowed

You may introduce new features from existing ones. An example of a new feature would be winning streak, i.e. the percentage of victories in the last X matches.

You are allowed to augment the dataset with external data. An example would be adding data from other leagues.

The only data that you can't use are the actual results of the matches you are predicting (in case you manage to reverse engineer the dataset).

At the end of the course you will have to present the code (Jupyter Notebook) that generated your best score.
----------------------
# Evaluation

## Metric

The evaluation metric for this competition is **categorization accuracy**. Since you have to predict a multi-class variable (1 for a home team victory, 0 for a draw and -1 for the away team victory), the confusion matrix, from which the accuracy is calculated is a 3x3 matrix. The accuracy is calculated for each class separately using the formula

$$
		A = {TP+TN \over TP+TN+FP+FN}
$$

and then averaged. The TP is the number of datapoints correctly classified as the current class. The TN is the number of datapoints that are not the current class and were classified as one of the two remaining classes. FPs are datapoints that are not the current class but have been classified as such. The FNs are the datapoints that are the current class but have been classified as one of the remaining classes.


Please, refer to these pointers for further info:
- <a href=""https://towardsdatascience.com/confusion-matrix-for-your-multi-class-machine-learning-model-ff9aa3bf7826"">Confusion Matrix for Your Multi-Class Machine Learning Model</a>
- <a href=""https://arxiv.org/pdf/2008.05756.pdf"">Metrics for Multi-Class Classification: an Overview</a>





## Submission Format

For every match in the test set, the submission file should contain two columns: Id and Win. The submission file should be a CSV file containing a header row and using commas as delimiters. Example of the first couple of rows of a submission file:


```
id		outcome
4509        0
4510       -1
4511       -1
4512       -1
4513       -1
```","# Dataset Description

Your goal is to predict the outcome of football matches: 1 for a home team victory, 0 for a draw and -1 for the away team victory. You need to train a machine learning model using the train set and predict the outcome in the test set. You are provided with a train set of past matches and a test set of matches for which the outcomes are unknown for you. The dataset is from a real national league but the team IDs have been obfuscated to limit reverse engineering.

## Train Set

Each row in the train set is a single match played between the home and away team. The columns have the following meaning: - id: id of the match (unique) - season: season in which the match has been played - stage: stage of the match (1st week ... last week of the season) - date - home\_team\_api\_id: id of the home team - away\_team\_api\_id: id of the away team - home\_team\_goal: goals scored by the home team - away\_team\_goal: goals scored by the away team - B365H ... BSA: bookmakers coefficients for the home/away/match - outcome: outcome of the match, which needs to be predicted (1 for a home team victory, 0 for a draw and -1 for the away team victory). This can be calculated from the home\_team\_goal and away\_team\_goal.


## Test Set

The test set has a similar structure as the train except for the columns ""home\_team\_goal"", ""away\_team\_goal"", and ""outcome"". Your task is to create a submission CSV file that contains the id of the match from the test set and your prediction who the outcome (-1, 0, or 1)

```
id      outcome
4509        0
4510       -1
4511       -1
4512       1
4513       -1
```


## Files

- train.csv - the training set
- test.csv - the test set
- sample\_submission.csv - a sample submission file in the correct format",True,True
5202,itmo-csr-identification-2021-2-flat-price,"# Description

Simple contest for ITMO students
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is RMSE:
$$RMSE = \left\( \frac{1}{N} \Sigma_{i=1}^{N} \(  price\( i \) - price _{e} \(i\) \)^2 \right\)^\frac{1}{2}. $$

# Submission Format

For *every* record in the dataset, submission files should contain two columns: zero-based id and predicted price.

The file should contain a header and have the following format:
```
index,price
0,1
1,11
2,8
3,2
4,15
...
```","# Dataset Description

