5204,predio-de-insuficincia-cardaca,"# Description

# Contexto:
As doen?as cardiovasculares (DCVs) s?o a causa n¨²mero 1 de morte em todo o mundo, levando cerca de 17,9 milh?es de vidas a cada ano, o que representa 31% de todas as mortes em todo o mundo. Quatro em cada 5 mortes por DCV s?o devido a ataques card¨ªacos e derrames, e um ter?o dessas mortes ocorrem prematuramente em pessoas com menos de 70 anos de idade. A insufici¨ºncia card¨ªaca ¨¦ um evento comum causado por DCVs e este conjunto de dados cont¨¦m 11 recursos que podem ser usados ??para prever uma poss¨ªvel doen?a card¨ªaca.

Pessoas com doen?as cardiovasculares ou que apresentam alto risco cardiovascular (devido ¨¤ presen?a de um ou mais fatores de risco, como hipertens?o, diabetes, hiperlipidemia ou doen?a j¨¢ estabelecida) precisam de detec??o e gerenciamento precoces, em que um modelo de aprendizado de m¨¢quina pode ser de grande ajuda.


# Cita??es:
Criadores:

* Hungarian Institute of Cardiology. Budapest: Andras Janosi, M.D.
* University Hospital, Zurich, Switzerland: William Steinbrunn, M.D.
* University Hospital, Basel, Switzerland: Matthias Pfisterer, M.D.
* V.A. Medical Center, Long Beach and Cleveland Clinic Foundation: Robert Detrano, M.D., Ph.D.
----------------------
# Evaluation

# M¨¦trica:

Para esta competi??o utilizaremos o F1-Score como meio de avalia??o de submiss?es.

---

A m¨¦trica F1 pondera rechamada e precis?o igualmente, e um bom algoritmo de recupera??o ir¨¢ maximizar a precis?o e rechamada simultaneamente. Assim, o desempenho moderadamente bom em ambos ser¨¢ favorecido em vez do desempenho extremamente bom em um e no desempenho ruim do outro.

## F¨®rmula F1-Score

![alt text](https://i1.wp.com/inside-machinelearning.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/09/F1-Score.png?w=746&amp;ssl=1)

## Submission Format

**Para cada paciente no dataset**,  o arquivo de submiss?o deve conter as duas colunas: `PacientID` e `HeartDisease`. 


The file should contain a header and have the following format:
```
PacientID, HeartDisease
1,1
```","# Dataset Description

# Contexto:
As doen?as cardiovasculares (DCVs) s?o a causa n¨²mero 1 de morte em todo o mundo, levando cerca de 17,9 milh?es de vidas a cada ano, o que representa 31% de todas as mortes em todo o mundo. Quatro em cada 5 mortes por DCV s?o devido a ataques card¨ªacos e derrames, e um ter?o dessas mortes ocorrem prematuramente em pessoas com menos de 70 anos de idade. A insufici¨ºncia card¨ªaca ¨¦ um evento comum causado por DCVs e este conjunto de dados cont¨¦m 11 recursos que podem ser usados ??para prever uma poss¨ªvel doen?a card¨ªaca.

Pessoas com doen?as cardiovasculares ou que apresentam alto risco cardiovascular (devido ¨¤ presen?a de um ou mais fatores de risco, como hipertens?o, diabetes, hiperlipidemia ou doen?a j¨¢ estabelecida) precisam de detec??o e gerenciamento precoces, em que um modelo de aprendizado de m¨¢quina pode ser de grande ajuda.

# Informa??es:
1.  Age: Idade do Paciente [anos]
2. Sex: sexo do paciente [M: Masculino, F: Feminino]
3. ChestPainType: Tipo de dor no peito [TA: Angina T¨ªpica, ATA: Angina At¨ªpica, NAP: Dor n?o anginal, ASY: Assintom¨¢tico]
4. RestingBP: Press?o Sangu¨ªnea em Repouso [mm Hg]
5. Cholesterol: Colestorol S¨¦rico [mm/dl]
6. FastingBS: A?¨²car no Sangue em Jejum [1: if FastingBS > 120 mg/dl, 0: otherwise]
7. RestingECG: resultados de eletrocardiograma em respouso [Normal: Normal, ST: tendo anormalidade da onda ST-T (invers?es da onda T e / ou eleva??o ou depress?o ST> 0,05 mV), LVH: mostrando prov¨¢vel ou definitiva hipertrofia ventricular esquerda pelos crit¨¦rios de Estes]
8. MaxHR: frequ¨ºncia card¨ªaca m¨¢xima alcan?ada [valor num¨¦rico entre 60 e 202]
9. ExerciseAngina: angina induzida por exerc¨ªcio [Y: Yes, N: No]
10. Oldpeak: oldpeak = ST [Valor num¨¦rico medido na depress?o]
11. ST_Slope: a inclina??o do segmento ST de pico do exerc¨ªcio [para cima: inclina??o para cima, plana: plana, para baixo: inclina??o para baixo]
12. HeartDisease: classe de sa¨ªda [1: doen?a card¨ªaca, 0: normal]

# Fonte
Este conjunto de dados foi criado combinando diferentes conjuntos de dados j¨¢ dispon¨ªveis de forma independente, mas n?o combinados antes. Neste conjunto de dados, 5 conjuntos de dados card¨ªacos s?o combinados em 11 caracter¨ªsticas comuns, o que o torna o maior conjunto de dados de doen?as card¨ªacas dispon¨ªvel at¨¦ agora para fins de pesquisa. Os cinco conjuntos de dados usados ??para sua curadoria s?o:

Cleveland: 303 observa??es
H¨²ngria: 294 observa??es
Su¨ª?a: 123 observa??es
Long Beach VA: 200 observa??es
Conjunto de dados Stalog (cora??o): 270 observa??es
Total: 1190 observa??es
Duplicado: 272 observa??es

Conjunto de dados final: 918 observa??es",True,True
5205,so-economic-forecasts,"# Description

A guidance survey was completed by students from different socioeconomic backgrounds and school sections: general high school, vocational high school, and middle school.

# **The goal**
Can you determine the socioeconomic level of the parents from the responses provided by the students?



The socio-economic level of the parents is a score between 0 and 6, based on a single question:  
  `What is the profession of your father/mother ?`
----------------------
# Evaluation

# **Evaluation**

The evaluation metric chosen for this competition is the [RMSE](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Root-mean-square_deviation) : 
$${RMSE = \sqrt{ \frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N} ({y-\hat{y}})^2}}$$


The score is  the sum of the RMSE for the 2 parents

# Submission Format

**For every Student in the dataset**, submission files should contain tree columns: `Id_student` and `Father's socio-professional category`, `Mother's socio-professional category`.


The file should contain a header and have the following format:
```
Id_student,Father's socio-professional category,Mother's socio-professional category
1,4,6
2,1,2
.
.
.
53,0,0
```","# Dataset Description

This dataset comes from a study within the framework of a master in sociology to confirm correlations between : 
- Sense of competence
- Sense of academic achievement
- Sense of social fulfillment
- Academic Help
- Intrinsic Motivation
- Extrinsic motivation
- Amotivation



## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

## Columns

*   `Id_student` - to identify the Student
*   `Father's socio-professional category`?and `Mother's socio-professional category` are the columns to predict
*   `Qx` are explained in the Overview : [Here](https://www.kaggle.com/c/so-economic-forecasts/overview/questions-asked-to-students)",True,True
5206,idl-winter-hw2p2s1,"# Description

This is part 1 Face Classification for HW2P2. In addition to this, you should also finish part 2 Face verification

Given an image of a person¡¯s face, the task of classifying the ID of the face is known as face classification. The input to your system will be a face image and you will have to predict the ID of the face. The ground truth will be present in the training data and the network will be doing an
Multi-class Classification to get the prediction. You are provided with a validation set for fine-tuning your model.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Please refer to the writeup.","# Dataset Description

Please refer to the writeup.",True,True
5207,idl-winter-hw2p2s2,"# Description

This is part 2 Face verification for HW2P2. In addition to this, you should also finish part 1 Face classification.

In part 2 Face verification, you will use Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs) to design an end-to-end system for face verification. Your system will be given two images as input and will output a score that quantifies the similarity between the faces in these images. This helps us decide whether the faces from the two images are of the same person or not.

You can build on your face classification model, or use other methods like Siamese Networks. Read the writeup for more hints.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Please refer to the writeup.","# Dataset Description

Please refer to the writeup.",True,True
5208,idl-winter-hw3p2,"# Description

The feature data of this homework are the same as homework 1 part 2. There are many variable length utterances comprised of 40 dimensional timesteps. You can use recurrent layers and their variants. You may also use convolutional layers if you so choose.

Your task will be to take the sequence of features and map them to a sequence of phonemes. Keep in mind the number of phonemes will not equal the number of timesteps. Refer to the write-up for more information.
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric will be the Levenshtein distance (character level minimum edit distance) from the ground truth. The labels will have the sequence of phonemes, but not the alignment. To handle this CTC Loss will be used during training and a search should be used on the output distribution for prediction. More information is provided in the writeup.","# Dataset Description

File Descriptions - phoneme_list.py: contains mapping of phonemes to indices - train.npy: training features - train_labels.npy: training labels which are phoneme sequences - dev.npy: validation features - dev_labels.npy: validation phoneme sequences - test.npy: test data features - sample_submission.csv: sample submission

Please refer to the write-up.",True,True
5209,robi-ace,"# Description

Play with the datset. 

Train.csv -- training data
test.csv -- for testing the solution
----------------------
# Evaluation


The evaluation metric for this competition is [Mean F1-Score](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/F-score). The F1 score, commonly used in information retrieval, measures accuracy using the statistics precision \( \text{p} \) and recall \( \text{r} \).

Precision is the ratio of true positives \(\text{tp}\) to all predicted positives \(\text{tp} + \text{fp}\). Recall is the ratio of true positives \(\text{tp}\) to all actual positives \(\text{tp} + \text{fn}\). The F1 score is given by:

\[ \text{F1} = 2\frac{\text{p} \cdot \text{r}}{\text{p}+\text{r}}\ \ \mathrm{where}\ \ \text{p} = \frac{\text{tp}}{\text{tp}+\text{fp}},\ \ \text{r} = \frac{\text{tp}}{\text{tp}+\text{fn}} \]

The F1 metric weights recall and precision equally, and a good retrieval algorithm will maximize both precision and recall simultaneously. Thus, moderately good performance on both will be favored over extremely good performance on one and poor performance on the other.

## Submission Format

**For every author in the dataset**, submission files should contain two columns: `AuthorId` and `DuplicateAuthorIds`. `DuplicateAuthorIds` should be a space-delimited list. Every `AuthorId` counts as its own duplicate, and every duplicate should be listed under each of its respective ids. For example, if you suspect author A, B, and C are the same, you should list `A,A B C`, `B,B A C`, `C,C A B`.


The file should contain a header and have the following format:
```
AuthorId,DuplicateAuthorIds
1,1
```","# Dataset Description



*   train.csv - the training set
*   test.csv - the test set
*   sample_submission.csv - a sample submission file",True,True
