5217,multitask-affective-music-lab-2022,"# Description

# Description

In this lab we are going to use Deep Learning models for music classification. We will classify spectrograms extracted from music clips across genres and across affective dimensions.

After the lab you will be familiar with the following concepts

- Basic processing of music using deep learning models
- LSTMs, CNNs
- Transfer learning and / or Multitask learning concepts


The lab is split in 2 parts:

- Music genre recognition: Classification of songs across 20 genres using spectrogram information
- Affective music classification: Regression in 3 dimensions (valence, energy and danceability) using spectrogram information

# Data
You can find extracted spectrograms and annotations in the data tab. Data are accompanied by detailed dataset description.

# Code
In the notebooks tab you can find some ipython notebooks with helper code.

Kaggle provides some free kernels to run your experiments.

Kernels:

- have the data downloaded automatically for you
- are version controlled
- Can be public or private (use private)
- Have 16GB RAM
- Can have a GPU attached to them

Another free resource you can use to run your experiments is Google colab. We will provide you with some starting notebooks for colaboratory that handle the integration with kaggle.
You can read [this article](https://towardsdatascience.com/training-machine-learning-models-online-for-free-gpu-tpu-enabled-5def6a5c1ce3) for more free online GPU resources.","# Dataset Description

<h1> Data format </h1>

- Data hosted at: https://www.kaggle.com/geoparslp/patreco3-multitask-affective-music
- Data Loading tutorial: https://www.kaggle.com/geoparslp/lab3-data-loading-tutorial

For all datasets, spectrograms and beat-sync'ed spectrograms are given in zipped numpy array format (`.npy.gz`).
To load a spectrogram run:


`

    import numpy as np
    import gzip

    spectrogram_file = '/path/to/id.npy.gz'

    with gzip.GzipFile(spectrogram_file, 'r') as f:
        spectrograms = np.load(f)


    # spectrograms contains a fused mel spectrogram and chromagram
    # Decompose as follows
    mel_spectrogram = spectrograms[:128]
    chromagram = spectrograms[128:]
`    

<h1> Datasets </h1>

We provide 2 datasets (and their beat-sync'ed) version

<h2> [FMA-Genre] Small FMA genre classification spectrograms  dataset </h2>

Inside `data/fma_genre_spectrograms` folder.  
Contains spectroctrams for songs in FMA small dataset.  
In total there are 3834 songs distributed among 20 genres (almost) uniformly.  
The  data are split into a training and a proposed test set, organized in the following structure:

    ©À©¤©¤ test [765 entries]
    ©À©¤©¤ test_labels.txt
    ©À©¤©¤ train [3069 entries]
    ©¸©¤©¤ train_labels.txt

You are provided ground truth labels for both the training and the test set in `training_labels.txt` and `test_labels.txt` in the following format:

    Id	Genre
    1042.fused.full.npy.gz	Blues
    1043.fused.full.npy.gz	Blues
    1044.fused.full.npy.gz	Blues
    1045.fused.full.npy.gz	Blues

Where the spectrogram filename is used as ID in the first column and the genre as label in the second column. 

<h2> [FMA-Genre-Beat] Small FMA genre classification spectrograms  dataset (Beat synchronized) </h2>

Inside `data/fma_genre_spectrograms_beat` folder.

Same as [FMA-Genre], but spectrograms are beat-synchronized. This leads to smaller number of 
timesteps. For more information you can check [the librosa tutorial][1]

<h2> [Multi-Music] Multitask Dataset </h2>

Inside `data/multitask_dataset` folder.

Contains spectrograms for 1497 songs. We provide labels for valence, energy (arousal) and danceability.

    ©À©¤©¤ test [375 entries]
    ©À©¤©¤ train [1122 entries]
    ©¸©¤©¤ train_labels.txt

We provide ground truth labels for the training set in `train_labels.txt`. 
For test set evaluation create a submission file and submit to Kaggle.
Label format:

    Id.fused.full.npy.gz,valence,energy,danceability
    5823.fused.full.npy.gz,0.578,0.973,0.873
    6276.fused.full.npy.gz,0.839,0.782,0.655
    9505.fused.full.npy.gz,0.587,0.956,0.204
    5042.fused.full.npy.gz,0.222,0.226,0.189

<h2> [Multi-Music-Beat] Multitask Dataset (Beat syncronized) </h2>

Inside `data/multitask_dataset_beat` folder.

Same as [Multi-Music], but spectrograms are beat-synchronized. This leads to smaller number of 
timesteps.


  [1]: https://librosa.org/doc/main/tutorial.html#quickstart",True,True
