5260,exml-prelims-task-1,"# Description

#Exun 2021-2022
The event is a part of our annual tech symposium [Exun 2021-22](https://reg.exun.co/invite). If you're interested in participating in other technical events, please check out our [invite](https://reg.exun.co/invite/events)!


##Prelims Task-1
You are tasked to make a CNN to detect which side of the hand is being shown in the image (dorsal or frontal) using our dataset.

<br>
**Make sure to register at [http://exun.co/independentreg](http://exun.co/independentreg) and fill out this [form](https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSeL7Gg2IVddkt5be2-YhM0FqfiIwcq49ooRGQ0K1rznRBYvhA/viewform) after finalizing your Kaggle submissions.**
----------------------
# Evaluation

###Goal
The goal in this competition is to take an image of a hand, and determine what side of the hand ```(dorsal or palmar)``` is there in the picture.
For every image in the test set, you should predict the correct label. 
```
Labels :- (to be put in AspectofHand column of your submission csv)

Dorsal - 0
Palmar - 1
```

###Metric
This competition is evaluated on the categorization accuracy of your predictions (the percentage of images you get correct).

###Submission File Format
The file should contain a header and have the following format:
```
id,AspectofHand
LUqGsceRk_5611,1
VzoDqLVfQf_9620259,0
WKGonejNyr_523725,0
vmgtXBWpuR_24288,0
etc.
```

----------------------
# Timeline

###11 Jan 12:00pm IST to 13 Jan 12:00pm IST","# Dataset Description

The training data contains 2500 images per class ```(Dorsal and Palmer)```. The images are of size ```(1600px x 1200px)``` and in png format.


```
Dorsal - 0
```
<br>
```
Palmar - 1
```

##Note : If you can't download the dataset, try downloading the files individually.

#A note on hand labeling <br>
Per the rules and spirit of this contest, please do not manually label your submissions.
<br>
<br>
However, given that there is always one willing to cheat to rise to glory, **we reserve the right to wield the following anti-cheating measures:**

- Ask to spot-check your code at any point in the competition. Failure to provide proof of algorithm within a reasonable time frame may result in disqualification.

- Release a new test set towards the end of the competition and require predictions for this new set.

- After the competition, require access to your model to verify that the solution does not utilize human intervention.

**In short, if you cheat by hand labeling your predictions, expect to do it all over again, possibly many times.**  Thanks in advance for the fair competition and have fun.",True,True
5261,exml-prelims-task-2,"# Description

#Exun 2021-2022
The event is a part of our annual tech symposium [Exun 2021-22](https://reg.exun.co/invite). If you're interested in participating in other technical events, please check out our [invite](https://reg.exun.co/invite/events)!


##Prelims Task-2
In this task, we will be giving you a dataset for classification of some world-known company logos. Your model has to learn to classify them. 

<br>
**Make sure to register at [http://exun.co/independentreg](http://exun.co/independentreg) and fill out this [form](https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSfQRjG7Uh-uJDxFr5-LYWX1R5zc_ezKJhjJv33doj6L7DkjdQ/viewform) after finalizing your Kaggle submissions.**
----------------------
# Evaluation

###Goal
The goal in this competition is to take an image of a logo and determine the company to which it belongs.
For every image in the test set, you should predict the correct label. 
```
Labels :- (to be put in company column of your submission csv)

Amazon - 0
Apple - 1
Disney - 2
Facebook - 3
Google - 4
IBM - 5
Intel - 6
Netflix - 7
```


###Metric
This competition is evaluated on the categorization accuracy of your predictions (the percentage of images you get correct).

###Submission File Format
The file should contain a header and have the following format:
```
id,company
vwitaAIDKb_390965,0
xnJibWYcHP_87987,0
vOzNfUKoeU_621007,0
etc.
```

----------------------
# Timeline

###11 Jan 12:00pm IST to 13 Jan 12:00pm IST","# Dataset Description

The training data contains 24 images per class. Most images are of size ```(100px x 100px)```.
<br><br><br>

**Note: Augmentation Techniques are allowed**

##Note : If you can't download the dataset, try downloading the files individually.

#A note on hand labeling <br>
Per the rules and spirit of this contest, please do not manually label your submissions.
<br>
<br>
However, given that there is always one willing to cheat to rise to glory, **we reserve the right to wield the following anti-cheating measures:**

- Ask to spot-check your code at any point in the competition. Failure to provide proof of algorithm within a reasonable time frame may result in disqualification.

- Release a new test set towards the end of the competition and require predictions for this new set.

- After the competition, require access to your model to verify that the solution does not utilize human intervention.

**In short, if you cheat by hand labeling your predictions, expect to do it all over again, possibly many times.**  Thanks in advance for the fair competition and have fun.",True,True
5262,gnss-urban-positioning,"# Description

## Motivation
Global navigation satellite system (GNSS) positioning accuracy is degraded in urban canyons due to signal blockages and reflections. This competition aims to foster the machine learning algorithms in GNSS multipath/NLOS problem. Totally there are three tasks for this problem, which is LOS/NLOS classification, Pseudorange Error Prediction, Positioning Prediction.

This is the page for GNSS position prediction. You can upload your prediction result to evaluate the performance of your model in a different scenario.

## Acknowledgements

We thanks the fellows who contribute to the project:
Prof. Hsu, Mr. Huang, Mr. Zhang, Mr. Ivan, Mr. Zhong, Mr. Max, Mr. Xu
----------------------
# Evaluation

## Evaluation Metric
The evaluation metric for this competition is [RMSE](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Root-mean-square_deviation). The RMSE is commonly used to measure the differences between values. The RMSE for this task is calculated for both E and N errors.


## Benchmark
The benchmark result is from the conventional least-squares method. Because the ground truth of the testing set will not be provided, the E and N error is calculated based on the location near our data collection position. The location is  [22.322101, 114.208885, 0]. 

## Submission Format

**For every GPS Time and Every Satellite in the dataset**, submission files should contain two columns: `ID`, `East_error_base`, and  `North_error_base`. `ID` follows the same sequence with the testing dataset, and within each GPS Time, the satellite sequence should also be the same as that in the testing set. Every `East_error_base` and  `North_error_base` are float values, which mean the E and N based on the above location and your position produced by your model. 


The file should contain a header and have the following format:
```
ID,East_error_base,North_error_base
0,101,-10
1,25
2,0,-67
3,367,87
4,45,145
5,120,123
6,-261,68
7,72,98
8,49,81
9,192,105
10,238,-204
11,-85,-20
12,164,87
13,175.-107
```","# Dataset Description

Both training and testing dataset contain raw measurements from the f9p receiver. Please read and extract more features for your own machine learning training. The training data is collected in Tsim Sha Tsui (TST), Whampoa (WP), Mong Kok (MK). The data collection setup is as UrbanNav [1]. The testing data is collected in Kowloon Bay (KLB).

## Files

*   **GNSS_raw_train.csv** - the training set
*   **GNSS_raw_test.csv** - the testing set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format, where the number in the ""ID"" column is the ID, following the testing dataset sequence, **East_error_base and North_error_base are the E and N based on the location [22.322101, 114.208885, 0]**

## Columns

*   `GPS_Time(s)` - GPS time
*   `Satelite_code`?- code of satellite
*   `code_L1`?- code measurement in L1
*   `phase_L1`?- phase measurement in L1
*   `doppler_L1`?- doppler in L1
*   `cnr_L1`?- signal strength in L1
*   `code_L2`?- code measurement in L2
*   `phase_L2`?- phase measurement in L2
*   `doppler_L2`?- doppler in L2
*   `cnr_L2`?- signal strength in L2
*   `label`?- LOS/NLOS label of the satellite, where 1 represents LOS, 0 represents NLOS. This is gained by 3D building model and the ground truth location.
*   `Pr_Error`?- Pseudorange error (assumed mostly contributed by multipath/NLOS). The value is calculated by double-difference using the closest ground station measurement and the highest elevation angle satellite as in [2] equation (17).
*   `Latitude`?- Latitude of the ground truth location
*   `Longitude`?- Longitude of the ground truth location
*   `Height`?- Ellipsoidal height of the ground truth location

## Rows
* **For GNSS_raw_train.csv**
*   `2-10392` - Scenario Tsim Sha Tsui
*   `10393-38144`?- Scenario Whampo
*   `38145-74086`?- Scenario Mong Kok


[1] L.-T. Hsu, Kubo, Nobuaki, Wen, Weisong, Chen, Wu, Liu, Zhizhao, Suzuki, Taro, Meguro, Junichi, ""UrbanNav:An Open-Sourced Multisensory Dataset for Benchmarking Positioning Algorithms Designed for Urban Areas,"" in Proceedings of the 34th International Technical Meeting of the Satellite Division of The Institute of Navigation (ION GNSS+ 2021), St. Louis, Missouri, September 2021 2021, pp. 226-256, doi: https://doi.org/10.33012/2021.17895.
[2] B. Xu, Q. Jia, Y. Luo, and L.-T. Hsu, ""Intelligent GPS L1 LOS/Multipath/NLOS Classifiers Based on Correlator-, RINEX- and NMEA-Level Measurements,"" Remote Sensing, vol. 11, no. 16, p. 1851, 2019. [Online]. Available: https://www.mdpi.com/2072-4292/11/16/1851.",True,True
