5287,testcompvlad,"# Description

 §´§Ö§ã§ä§à§Ó§Ñ§ñ §ã§ä§â§Ñ§ß§Ú§è§Ñ §á§à §â§Ñ§Ù§Ò§à§â§å AT


## Acknowledgements

We thank §£§Ý§Ñ§Õ§Ú§ã§Ý§Ñ§Ó§Ñ §Ú §¦§Ü§Ñ§ä§Ü§â§Ú§ß§å =)
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is [Mean F1-Score](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/F-score). The F1 score, commonly used in information retrieval, measures accuracy using the statistics precision \( \text{p} \) and recall \( \text{r} \).

Precision is the ratio of true positives \(\text{tp}\) to all predicted positives \(\text{tp} + \text{fp}\). Recall is the ratio of true positives \(\text{tp}\) to all actual positives \(\text{tp} + \text{fn}\). The F1 score is given by:

\[ \text{F1} = 2\frac{\text{p} \cdot \text{r}}{\text{p}+\text{r}}\ \ \mathrm{where}\ \ \text{p} = \frac{\text{tp}}{\text{tp}+\text{fp}},\ \ \text{r} = \frac{\text{tp}}{\text{tp}+\text{fn}} \]

The F1 metric weights recall and precision equally, and a good retrieval algorithm will maximize both precision and recall simultaneously. Thus, moderately good performance on both will be favored over extremely good performance on one and poor performance on the other.

## Submission Format

**For every author in the dataset**, submission files should contain two columns: `AuthorId` and `DuplicateAuthorIds`. `DuplicateAuthorIds` should be a space-delimited list. Every `AuthorId` counts as its own duplicate, and every duplicate should be listed under each of its respective ids. For example, if you suspect author A, B, and C are the same, you should list `A,A B C`, `B,B A C`, `C,C A B`.


The file should contain a header and have the following format:
```
ID,CLASS
1,1
```","# Dataset Description

This page appears alongside the data files. It describes?what files have been provided and?the format of each. There is no single format for this page that is appropriate for all competitions, but you should strive to describe as much as you can here. A little time spent describing the data here can save a lot of time answering questions later.

## Columns

*   ` ID` - definition of example_id
*   `Class`?- definition of feature_1",True,True
5288,option-geostatistique-20212022,"# Description

The Jura data set comprises seven heavy metals measured in the top soil of the swiss Jura, along with consistently coded land use and rock type factors, as well as geographic coordinates.

The competition consist in predicting the Cobalt top soil concentration at the validation locations.
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is the mean squared error (MSE). The MSE is defined as:</p>
<p>\[ MSE = \frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^n (Z_i-Z^{\star}_i)^2 \]</p>
<p> where Z_i is the actual value, unknown and hosted here, Z_i* is the predicted value and i is the index.</p> 
<p>The MSE measures the accuracy of the prediction. The lower the better!</p>
<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p> submission files should contain two columns: the index of the predicted site with name V1 and the predicted cobalt concentration named V2 see </p>
[the sample submission](https://cloud.mines-paristech.fr/index.php/s/n1Vak3TiRvseX0o)","# Dataset Description

<p> The Jura data set comprises seven heavy metals measured in the top soil of the swiss Jura, along with consistently coded land use and rock type factors, as well as geographic coordinates. 
</p>
<h2>File descriptions</h2>
Two of the provided files will be useful
<ul>
<li><strong>jura_pred.csv</strong> - the training set</li>
<li><strong>jura_val_loc.csv</strong> - validation locations and covariables</li>
</ul>
<h2>Data fields</h2>

<ul>
<li><strong> Xloc </strong> X coordinate, local grid km </li>
<li><strong> Yloc </strong>Y coordinate, local grid km </li>
<li><strong>Landuse</strong> Land use: 1: Forest, 2: Pasture (Weide(land), Wiese, Grasland), 3: Meadow (Wiese, Flur, Matte, Anger), 4: Tillage (Ackerland, bestelltes Land) </li>
<li><strong>Rock</strong> Rock Types: 1: Argovian, 2: Kimmeridgian, 3: Sequanian, 4: Portlandian, 5: Quaternary. </li>
<li><strong>Cd</strong> mg cadmium kg^-1 topsoil </li>
<li><strong>Co</strong> mg cobalt kg^-1 topsoil </li>
<li><strong>Cr</strong> mg chromium kg^-1 topsoil </li>
<li><strong>Cu</strong> mg copper kg^-1 topsoil </li>
<li><strong>Ni</strong> mg nickel kg^-1 topsoil </li>
<li><strong>Pb</strong> mg lead kg^-1 topsoil </li>
<li><strong>Zn</strong> mg zinc kg^-1 topsoil</li>
</ul>",True,True
5289,molsim-2022-ml-challenge,"# Description

This is the competition for the MolSim 2022 machine learning workshop. 

Team up and build the best models to predict the high-pressure carbon dioxide uptake in MOFs!
We didn't provide you with all the data---we use this data to test the generalization performance of the model. 

## Getting started
1. Download the file with the features (they are also in the `data` folder on GitHub) 
2. Use your model to make predictions and create a submission `.csv`
3. Upload your prediction

Your submissions will be evaluated based on the mean absolute error on the subset from the same [database you used for training](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-019-1798-7#article-comments). 

Feel free to explore other model architectures!

## Acknowledgment
We thank Leopold Talirz for incredibly valuable feedback on the exercises
----------------------
# Evaluation

You will be scored using the MAE calculated on a holdout dataset. Make sure to not overfit to the public leaderboard which is a *small random* subset of this holdout set.","# Dataset Description

# File description 
* `features.csv` : contains all feature columns and is in the same format as the files we used for training the models",True,True
5290,coins,"# Description

# Introduction

Given images of rotated one-cent coins of varying (1) shininess/color, (2) years/mintmarks, and (3) levels of wear, as shown below, your objective is to detect the angle of rotation of each coin from the upright position.

There are two classes of coins: (1) the US Lincoln head one cent and (2) the US Lincoln memorial (tail) one cent.  It is expected that your algorithm can detect the rotation on both classes.

![Labeled Rotated Lincoln Heads](https://i.imgur.com/3ktjY8C.png)

![Labeled Rotated Memorial](https://i.imgur.com/sl6io3h.png)

# Dataset

The dataset consists of 568 images of rotated Lincoln heads and 532 images of Lincoln memorials.  All images are in color and have size 750 px by 750 px.  Each image is pre-cropped to only contain the circular coin; however, you may want to crop further to remove the coin edge.

![Rotated Dataset](https://i.imgur.com/6QFt3pM.png)
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is the absolute value of the difference between the submitted detected angle and the ground truth angle (fixed for 0-360 wrapping), summed over all the coins.  The lower this aggregated deviation is, the better.

![Clockwise Angle](https://i.imgur.com/dZTUNsm.png)

The angle should be in degrees rotated clockwise from upright position, as shown above.

## Submission Format

The submission should be a .csv file where the first column is the name of the png file that is being assessed and the second column is the detected angle of rotation.

The first line must be a header as follows:
> Id,Predicted

![Sample CSV](https://i.imgur.com/1ChQRsR.png)","# Dataset Description

The dataset directory consists of 1,100 images of rotated coins in .png format with a transparent background layer.

Each image is of a coin rotated a specific amount.

The Lincoln head images have the format, ""####_lincoln.png"", where ""####"" corresponds to the index of the image.  The index of the image defines the order of angles in the .txt file that will be submitted.   The Lincoln Memorial tail images have the format, ""####_memorial.png"".",True,True
