5296,where-is-the-toilet-the-right-one,"# Description

## About Image Retrieval and Disentangled Representation
This task is an Image Retrieval task with a focus on searching for items with special properties. This subfield of Image Retrieval benefits from Disentangled Embeddings (Disentangled Representations) whose goal is to search by a constructed embedding - some segments of the embedding are changed (manipulated) while others are fixed.

## Finding similar toilet signs for another gender
Your task is to find similar toilet signs (drawing style, concept, etc.) but for the opposite gender. Keeping in mind that images of opposite genders are very similar themselves because of lightning conditions and the background where they are drawn, the images were split into two parts: original and cropped versions. In this scenario it's more challenging to find a similar logo with the opposite gender and the model cannot simply cheat based on background.

Examples of original images:
![](https://i.imgur.com/O1NYoH1.png)
The same images but cropped:
![](https://i.imgur.com/OdInapf.png)
Expected TOP-1 retrieved items of opposite gender (cropped images are used as queries):
![](https://i.imgur.com/OFdIJRE.png)

Since the dataset is limited in size it's allowed to use pre-trained models trained on general datasets like ImageNet, JWT, etc. It's also allowed to use external data as well as pre-trained models trained specifically on datasets of toilet sign images (if you find them), but you have to note these resources in public Discussion or Code threads.

## Acknowledgements
The dataset was collected in two parts and I acknowledge the following authors:
1. Author's photos of toilet signs which were collected for about 2 years
2. Photos of toilet signs from the Pinterest collection of Anatolii TTJ: https://www.pinterest.ru/anatoliittj/toilet-signs/. Thanks the author for this clean set of images!
3. I thank all of the toilet signs' creators whom is hard to find and mention directly due to the complex process of finding the initial authors
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is [precision@k](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Evaluation_measures_(information_retrieval)) with k=1:
![precision@k](https://habrastorage.org/r/w1560/getpro/habr/upload_files/926/17c/c00/92617cc007671cbbd50c8c1441a941ca.png)
The averaging is done in macro mode, i.e. for each query `precision@1` is calculated and then averaged accross all queries.

## Submission Format
For submission your output should include `QueryId` for each query image, `DocumentNumber` of TOP-1 retrieved images and their similarities `Similarity` to the query image. The `Rank` field should be set to 0 since we are inreseted in TOP-1 result only. Other fields should be empty. Example:
| QueryId | Iteration | DocumentNumber | Rank | Similarity | RunId |
|-----|-----|-----|-----|-----|-----|
| 2 |  | 268 | 0 | 0.963030 | |
| 3 |  | 9 | 0 | 0.881817 | |
| 5 |  | 276 | 0 | 0.917736 | |

## Assessment
There are number of criterions that are important for us to consider a candidate to work in our company:
1. The solution should be better than 70% of submissions on private leaderboard, but not obligatory
2. It's expected for the organizer to see a clean documented code (reference is our baseline)
3. Usage of Metric Learning approaches and especially Embeddings Disentanglement is higly appreciated, even if they didn't work on this small training set, but you gave it a try
4. We encourage to use retrieval metrics calculation on training set inside the submission notebooks","# Dataset Description

The dataset contains 328 unique images of toilet signs. Each image has an original and cropped versions (656 images in total). The dataset is split into train and test by unique pairs in relation 328:328. The proportion of public/private sets in test set is 164:164. So, you will see 328 images (both crop and orig) in the `test.csv` for which you need to make predictions.

## Folders & Files
*   **orig** - the original images
*   **crop** - the same images but cropped (ids don't match)
*   **train.csv** - the training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

## Columns in `train.csv`
*   `pair_id` - unique id of toilet signs pair
*   `path_crop`?- path to the cropped version of an image
*   `path_orig`?- path to the original version of an image
*   `gender`?- either ""m"" or ""f"" meaning male or female gender respectively

## Columns in `test.csv`
*   `is_query`?- binary indicator saying either image is query (1) or non-query (0)
*   `path`?- path to the image (queries are always crops, non-queries are always original)",True,True
5297,sai-vessel-segmentation2,"# Description

Vessel Segmentation

Dice Score

-- Advanced baseline --0.80102

-- Basic baseline --0.59445
----------------------
# Evaluation

This competition is evaluated on the mean Dice coefficient. The Dice coefficient can be used to compare the pixel-wise agreement between a predicted segmentation and its corresponding ground truth. The formula is given by:

2?|X¡ÉY| / |X|+|Y|,

where X is the predicted set of pixels and Y is the ground truth. The Dice coefficient is defined to be 1 when both X and Y are empty. The leaderboard score is the mean of the Dice coefficients for each image in the test set.


** Submission File **
In order to reduce the submission file size, our metric uses run-length encoding on the pixel values.  Instead of submitting an exhaustive list of indices for your segmentation, you will submit pairs of values that contain a start position and a run length. E.g. '1 3' implies starting at pixel 1 and running a total of 3 pixels 1,2,3.

The competition format requires a space delimited list of pairs. For example, '1 3 10 5' implies pixels 1,2,3,10,11,12,13,14 are to be included in the mask. The metric checks that the pairs are sorted, positive, and the decoded pixel values are not duplicated. The pixels are numbered from top to bottom, then left to right: 1 is pixel 1,1, 2 is pixel 2,1, etc.

The file should contain a header and have the following format:

&gt; 
&gt; img,pixels

&gt; 1,1 1 5 1

&gt; 2,1 1

&gt; 3,1 1

&gt; etc.","# Dataset Description

This competition is evaluated on the mean Dice coefficient. The Dice coefficient can be used to compare the pixel-wise agreement between a predicted segmentation and its corresponding ground truth. The formula is given by:

2?|X¡ÉY| / |X|+|Y|,

where X is the predicted set of pixels and Y is the ground truth. The Dice coefficient is defined to be 1 when both X and Y are empty. The leaderboard score is the mean of the Dice coefficients for each image in the test set.


** Submission File **
In order to reduce the submission file size, our metric uses run-length encoding on the pixel values.  Instead of submitting an exhaustive list of indices for your segmentation, you will submit pairs of values that contain a start position and a run length. E.g. '1 3' implies starting at pixel 1 and running a total of 3 pixels 1,2,3.

The competition format requires a space delimited list of pairs. For example, '1 3 10 5' implies pixels 1,2,3,10,11,12,13,14 are to be included in the mask. The metric checks that the pairs are sorted, positive, and the decoded pixel values are not duplicated. The pixels are numbered from top to bottom, then left to right: 1 is pixel 1,1, 2 is pixel 2,1, etc.

The file should contain a header and have the following format:

> 
> img,pixels

> 1,1 1 5 1

> 2,1 1

> 3,1 1

> etc.",True,True
5298,lidchallenge,"# Description

Language Identification (LID) systems from voice are classification models that predict the spoken language from a given audio recording. The LID systems can facilitate the process of any speech processing system such as speech recognition (ASR) or speech translation systems. In speech-based assistant systems, LID works as a first step by selecting the appropriate grammar from a list of available languages for further semantic analysis. Also, these models can be employed in call centers in order to redirect an international user to an operator who is fluent in that identified language. 

The objective of this project is to use machine learning methods for constructing a LID model which can discriminate 4 languages; English, French, Arabic, Japanese. There are 3 expected phases.
The first phase is the data collection. It is expected to prepare the dataset by recording audio signals. The objective is to get familiar with the challenges of data collection, and understand the trade-off between the cost of having more data and the improvement of performance.
The second phase is constructing a classifier. It is expected to compare the performance of different models, optimize the hyperparameters, and practice of finding the best model.   
The third phase is the evaluation of the model in a simulated situation of real life deployment. The objective is to understand the challenge of generalization. It's also expected to analyze the result of the model¡¯s performance and make hypothesis about the weak and strong aspect of models. The competition among models' accuracy can provide a better understanding of performance.

----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is Accuracy

## Submission Format

It's expected to predict the label of all files and generate another *.txt file which contain two columns: wav file name and (separated by ,) language label (FR, EN, AR, JP).
There is a code at the end of TP subject that can help you.","# Dataset Description

It is proposed to collect the data from [TEDx talks YouTube](https://www.youtube.com/user/TEDxTalks) for the Language Identification task from audio.
The samples have to be a recorded audio of speaker speech from available TEDx talks videos.  
In order to have a standard sample's type, it is expected to respect the following convention.

- The length of recorded audio files should be around 5 seconds (5.00 - 5.99 seconds).
- The format of audio files should be *.wav.
- The sample rate of recording files should be 16 kHz (in mono format).


You can find a playlist of TEDx talk in different language in below links:

- [English](https://www.youtube.com/user/TEDxTalks/videos)
- [French](https://www.youtube.com/playlist?list=PLsRNoUx8w3rOzmp0qoc53u3vYrcFYsvKj)
- [Arabic](https://www.youtube.com/playlist?list=PLsRNoUx8w3rNMQsD857mE6awUQRkMj9IW)
- [Japanese](https://www.youtube.com/playlist?list=PLsRNoUx8w3rOHjXIU5EE4KOiIagv9yQaG)


For recording the audio samples, you can use phone with respect to the asked convention. It is suggested to record the speech file from a part of video that contain speech with the lowest background noise possible. Also, be careful to record your file in an as silent as possible environment.

In order to track the collected data it is expected to create a text file with 3 information of samples; sample's file name, TEDx talk video address, the starting time of recording, speaker's language. It would be recommended to at least record 5 samples per language. The recorded samples will be collected and a part of it would be shared with others.

You can find an example of data with 3 samples in each language.

Like the data example that is provided, You should upload a compressed folder contains your recording files (at least 5 files per languages) in asked format (*.wav, 16kHz, mono, 5-6 seconds) and a *.txt file same as info.txt in provided folder with 4 information for each recorded file (one file per line); The 1st column should be the name of *.wav file, the 2nd column should be the URL address of YouTube video, the 3rd column should be the starting time of recording from YouTube video, 4th column the label (language) of recorded speech.",True,True
5299,coepdsaiinductions,"# Description

Heart Failure Prediction
---

Cardiovascular diseases (CVDs) are the number 1 cause of death globally, taking an estimated 17.9 million lives each year. People with cardiovascular disease or who are at high cardiovascular risk need early detection and management wherein a machine learning model can be of great help. 

Using the given dataset, develop a model that can predict whether a person is at risk of heart disease. For each patient in the test set, you must predict a 0 (negative) or 1 (positive) for the target variable.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Metric
Your score is the percentage of patients you correctly predict. This is known simply as ""accuracy¡±.

Submission File Format
You should submit a CSV file with exactly 218 entries plus a header row
(as per the ""heart\_failure\_test.csv"" which will be used for your final submission). 
Your submission will show an error if you have extra columns (beyond Id and Prediction) or rows.

The file should have exactly 2 columns:

Id, Prediction (contains your binary predictions: 1 for positive of having a heart attack, 0 for negative)

Id,HeartDisease

1,0

2,1

3,0

Etc.

You can download an example submission file (""sample_submission.csv"") on the Data page.",# Dataset Description,True,True
5300,dru-kaggle-intro,"# Description

Hey! This is the first introductory Kaggle lab of the 6th module.
In this lab, you will classify the price range of mobile phones depending on their characteristics??¡ú??

You will be given [guidance](https://github.com/DataRootUniversity/ds-fundamentals/blob/master/dru-kaggle-intro/dru_kaggle_intro_template.ipynb) on how to do this lab, but you can add your own steps to improve the classification quality.

This competition uses a modified [Mobile Price](https://www.kaggle.com/iabhishekofficial/mobile-price-classification)  dataset.
----------------------
# Evaluation

<!-- Evaluation -->
<h1>Evaluation</h1>
<p>
  Submissions are evaluated on MeanFScore, which at Kaggle is actually a
  <a href=""https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/F-score"" target=""_blank""
     rel=""noopener noreferrer"">micro-averaged&nbsp;F1-score</a>.
</p>
<p>
  The F1 metric weights recall and precision equally, and a good retrieval
  algorithm will simultaneously maximise both.
</p>

<!-- Submission File -->
<h1>Submission File</h1>
<p>
  For each <code>index</code> in the test set, you must predict a price
  <code>price_range</code>. The file should contain a header and have the
  following format:
</p>

<pre><code>index,price_range
1812,0
624,1
678,3
etc.
</code></pre>

<blockquote>You can submit results up to 20&nbsp;times a day.</blockquote>

<h2>Happy coding&nbsp;!</h2>","# Dataset Description

## The data has been divided into 2 groups : 
* Train Set (**train_mobile.csv**) 
* Test Set (**test_mobile.csv**) 

The training set should be used to build your machine learning models. For the training set, you have `price_range` (it is our target or class label) for each mobile sales data point. The test dataset will be used to predict `price_range` on unseen data and compose your submission file. So, the test set does not have the `price_range` (target variable) for each mobile sales data point.

Use the model you trained to predict the price range for each data point in the test set. The training set contains 1500 data points. Each data point has 20 features and one target variable.

The test set contains 500 data points. Each data point has 20 features.

##The data contains the following fields:

* `battery_power` - total energy stored in the battery (in mAh)
* `blue` - has bluetooth (yes or no)
* `clock_speed` - processor speed when executes the instructions
* `dual_sim` - has dual sim capability (yes or no)
* `fc` - large resolution of front camera (in MegaPixels)
* `four_g` - has 4G connectivity (yes or no)
* `int_memory` - large internal memory (in GB)
* `m_dep` - mobile thickness (in cm)
* `mobile_wt` - mobile mass (in gram)
* `n_cores` - number of cores in the processors (pcs)
* `pc - large` resolution of rear camera (in MegaPixels)
* `px_height` - length of the pixel resolution
* `px_width` - width of the pixel resolution
* `ram` - capacity of Random Access Memory (in MB)
* `sc_h` - screen length (cm)
* `sc_w` - screen width (cm)
* `talk_time` - the longest call time that can be made from a fully charged mobile until it runs out (in hours)
* `three_g` - has 3G connectivity (yes or no)
* `touch_screen` - has touch screen (yes or no)
* `wifi` - has wifi (yes or no)
* `price_range` - this is the target variable with value of 0 (low cost), 1 (medium cost), 2 (high cost), 3 (very high cost)

## sample_submission.csv

The **sample_submission.csv** file is provided as an example of how you should structure your predictions. So, your submission should have:

* a `index` column containing unique id of each mobile from test_mobile.csv.
* a `price_range` column (that you will create!) with target values 0, 1, 2, 3.

Here is an example of [sample_submission.csv](https://www.kaggle.com/c/dru-kaggle-intro/overview/evaluation) file.

## Files

*   **train_mobile.csv** - the train set
*   **test_mobile.csv** - the test set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format",True,True
