# [Deprecated]

## Miniproject #1
For this Kaggle competition, you will use data about loans (amount, location, borrower history) to predict whether or not the borrower will pay back the loan. These loans originated from LendingClub, a peer-to-peer lending platform, from 2007 to 2020, ranging from personal to business loans. 

There can be 10 submissions per day per team, and the submission window will close on Wednesday 9 February 2022 at 2:00PM PST.

You may not use any additional data sources.

## Acknowledgements

We thank the LendingClub database for this data, in addition to Yash for the data cleaning. This dataset was sourced from a different Kaggle competition (Lending Club 2007-2020Q3). **It is not permitted to visit that Kaggle page, read/use the associated Notebooks in any way, nor use any code online that was made for modeling this dataset, in order to make the competition fair. Doing so will be considered a violation of the Honor Code. Thanks for understanding!**
----------------------
# Evaluation

### Metric

The evaluation metric for this competition is the Mean Column ROC AUC. This means we treat each loan as its own binary classification problem, and calculate the area under curve (AUC) of the receiver operating curve (ROC). It allows us to measure a balance of the false positive and true positive rate. It is elaborated on [here](https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/generated/sklearn.metrics.roc_auc_score.html).

The equivalent [sklearn command](https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/generated/sklearn.metrics.roc_auc_score.html) used by the Kaggle grader would be: 
```
sklearn.metrics.roc_auc_score(y_ground_truth, y_predicted_probability)
```

Submission Format
For every row of data in LOANS_TEST.csv, submission files should contain 2 columns:

```id```: contains integers equal to the id's in the LOANS_TEST.csv file
```loan_status```: contains an integer (0 or 1), with 0 representing that the loan is Fully Paid, and 1 representing that the loan is Charged Off. 

The file should contain a header and have the following format:

```
id,loan_status
200000,0.17
200001,0.20
...
242479,0.67
```
----------------------
# Tips

Credits to Jim Zhang for helping with these tips

## Anaconda
If using Python, pandas is recommended for data manipulation. For prediction, one can start with some typical models in scikit-learn. pandas, numpy, sklearn are all included in Anaconda.

## Useful pandas functions
```
pd.read_csv('filename.csv', index_col='id')
pd.DataFrame.to_csv('submission.csv', index_label='id')
```

## Don't be afraid to try things you haven't heard of before
After some basic models, one can progress to some more advanced models in other libraries. Maybe search around a bit for cool or successful models that people have used for classification.

## IDE/Shortcuts
Learn all the shortcuts to your development environment. Spyder and Jupyter are both included in Anaconda, and there are plenty of shortcuts to run specific code sections swiftly.

## Missing data
Think of how to fill in the missing/nan data. A starting point is `SimpleImputer`.

## Feature engineering
See if you can create new features out of the existing columns, that would make the model perform better / have an easier time fitting the data

## Model blending
Why use one model when you can take the average of multiple model outputs, or even use another model on top of the model outputs?

## Probability calibration
Some models don't have accurate classification probabilities, but one can calibrate them using various techniques. This doesn't help much for individual models due to the AUC metric, but allows for ""fairer"" model blending if you plan to take a simple average.

## Hyperparameter tuning
Gain some intuition and then try some automatic tuners! Be aware, poor model types usually will not improve much with tuning.","# Dataset Description

## Files

*   **LOANS_TRAIN.csv** - the training set, including labels
*   **LOANS_TEST** - the test set, excluding labels
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

## Columns

See **LCDataDictionary.xlsx** for column descriptions",True,True
5352,football-match-probability-prediction,"# Description

## Predicting the beautiful game
[Octosport](https://www.octosport.io/) and [Sportmonks](https://www.sportmonks.com/) are proud to present their first football prediction competition. Do you know how to code in python and are passionate about football? Do you want to test your knowledge in machine learning, sequence learning, or neural networks? Do you want to beat the bookmaker's performance? You are at the right place.

## Competition Description

Football has been at the heart of data science for more than a decade. If today's algorithms focus on event detection, player style, or team analysis, predicting the results of a match stays an open challenge.

Predicting the outcomes of a match between two teams depends mostly (but not only) on their current form. The form of a team can be viewed as their recent sequence of results versus the other teams. So match probabilities between two teams can be different given their calendar.

This competition is about **predicting the probabilities** of more than **150000** match outcomes using the recent sequence of 10 matches of the teams.

## Community
As a part of the community competition, we want to encourage published notebooks. So **the best-published notebook will also be rewarded**. See the prizes section for more information.

## Benchmark
To add more fun we also added the performance of the bookmakers as a benchmark. We turned their [odds into probabilities](https://medium.com/geekculture/how-to-compute-football-implied-probabilities-from-bookmakers-odds-bbb33ccf7c1d). Try to beat them!

## Practical skills
 - Coding in python
 - Dealing with heterogeneous and missing data
 - Dealing with imbalanced and noisy classes
 - Use advanced machine learning techniques  like sequence learning, neural networks (but not limited to)
 
## Acknowledgements

The data are provided by [Octosport](https://www.octosport.io/) and [Sportmonks](https://www.sportmonks.com/) 

Good luck!
----------------------
# Evaluation



The evaluation metric for this competition is a multinomial [Log-Loss](https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/generated/sklearn.metrics.log_loss.html). The log-loss measure the quality of the predicted probabilities. Check out our[ blog post ](https://medium.com/geekculture/football-prediction-performance-how-to-calculate-hit-ratio-and-log-loss-1e5e22310497)if you want to know more about it.

### Submission file

**For every match in the dataset**, submission files should contain four columns: `id`,  `home`, `draw`, and `away`. The columns home stands for the probability of the Home team to win the match, the column away for the Away team to win, and the columns draw for the probability of a Draw. Each column expects a double between 0 and 1, and the sum of the three probabilities to be equals one. The `id` column contains the id of the predicted match.

### Submission Format

The file should contain a header **in the correct order** and have the following format:
```
id,home,away,draw
1,0.3,0.5,0.2
2,0.45,0.25,0.3
...
```
