5373,21422netflix,"# Description

In this [Netflix Prize](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Netflix_Prize) assignment, participants need to predict a rating from a user (movie viewer) to a movie, i.e. rating|{user,movie}. See [&#127910;](https://youtu.be/ImpV70uLxyw), [&#127910;](https://youtu.be/8wLKuscyO9I). This problem is classified as a [**collaborative filtering**](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Collaborative_filtering) and assumes latent dependency between users and movies. Recall that typically we build models which assume weak or no dependency between observations, unless we model time series, video frames, etc. 
 
This problem is unique due to the high sparsity of ratings. In the original Netflix problem, ~99% of ratings were missing, but here we have a subsample with 85% sparsity (if measured as a fraction of NaN values in the user¡Ámovie matrix. So, the challenge might be viewed as missing value imputation. We can also apply clustering and dimensions reduction solutions, which classify this problem as unsupervised learning. Also, we can apply supervised learning techniques, which learn from rating labels and predict missing values. However, the challenge is to derive users' features, since users have no explicit features in this dataset.
 
* HSE students model in R 
* JHU students model in Python. 
 
See **Rules** tab for more details.
 
## Acknowledgements
1. The original dataset was derived from [Netflix Movie Rating Dataset](https://www.kaggle.com/rishitjavia/netflix-movie-rating-dataset?select=Netflix_Dataset_Movie.csv), [Netflix Prize data](https://www.kaggle.com/netflix-inc/netflix-prize-data), [Netflix Prize Shows Information (9000 Shows)](https://www.kaggle.com/akashguna/netflix-prize-shows-information?select=imdb_processed.csv), [TMDB](https://www.themoviedb.org/?language=en-US) and [IMDB](https://www.imdb.com/interfaces).
1. The image is https://www.herox.com/blog/839-history-of-crowdsourcing-the-netflix-prize
1. **Competition Authors**. [HSE](https://hse.ru/) [ML Teaching Team](https://www.hse.ru/edu/courses/?filial=22723&lecturer=409923484&year=2021): [Oleg Melnikov](https://www.linkedin.com/in/olegmelnikov/), [Alexey Boldyrev](https://www.hse.ru/org/persons/223985242), [Vladimir Kirillin](https://www.hse.ru/org/persons/499313273), [Sergey Chervontsev](https://www.hse.ru/org/persons/305134539), Margarita Lazareva, Tatyana Yazykova, Mike Ovyan, Lika Lomidze, Vladimir Novikov, Valeriya Rogovchenko
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric is [**accuracy score**](https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/generated/sklearn.metrics.accuracy_score.html), which is the default classification accuracy in `sklearn` library. There are 5 possible ratings. Target variable is has 1-based index which can is computed as a deterministic function of `uid` and `mid` (see Colab). Notice that this metric does not reward your predicted ratings for being close. You are rewarded for exact match of the rating. The submission format is:

        id,rtg
        1,1
        382,2
        763,3        
        1144,4
        1525,5

FYI: the original competition used RMSE, which defines deviation from true rating. The teams had to beat 0.95 RMSE.","# Dataset Description

The original dataset has been subsampled/oversampled and augmented. 
 
1. `XY_Netflix.csv`: Contains ~7.4 million movie ratings (with level 1,2,3,4,5) identified by user id (`uid`) and movie id (`mid`). The first 20K rows have NaN rating values, which need to be predicted and submitted to Kaggle with their IDs, which are computed from `uid` and `mid` (see Starter Colab).
1. `XY_Netflix_matrix.csv` is a matrix of user rows and movie columns. It contains the same ratings as `XY_Netflix.csv` does, but because all other NaN values are also specified, ratings required for Kaggle submission are trickier to identify.
1. `movie.csv` contains additional information on movies in the above datasets",True,True
