# Prizes

# Prizes

In order to qualify for prizes, participants must complete [registration](https://bit.ly/widsdatathon_phaseII_reg) for the WiDS Datathon Phase II.

## Phase II Excellence in Research Award Prizes:

- 1st Place in the Excellence in Research Award

  - $4,000 USD cash prize for the team
  - 1 complimentary VIP registration per team member for the WiDS Worldwide Conference in 2023
  - A WiDS Datathon Excellence in Research Award Certificate for each team member

- 2nd Place in the Excellence in Research Award
  - $3,000 cash prize for the team
  - 1 complimentary VIP registration per team member for the WiDS Worldwide Conference in 2023
  - A WiDS Datathon Excellence in Research Award Certificate for each team member

- 3rd Place in the Excellence in Research Award
  - $2,000 cash prize for the team
  - 1 complimentary VIP registration per team member for the WiDS Worldwide Conference in 2023
  - A WiDS Datathon Excellence in Research Award Certificate for each team member

*In addition to the Excellence in Research Award, up to **$7,000 USD** will be distributed for honorable mentions across submitted research papers. Outstanding teams will also be invited to submit their work for publication.*

*All winners will be recognized on WiDS social media channels and published on the WiDS website. Special thanks to Kaggle for providing and distributing cash prizes.*
----------------------
# FAQ

# FAQ

Feel free to ask questions in the [Kaggle Discussion Forum](https://www.kaggle.com/c/phase-ii-widsdatathon2022/discussion) throughout the competition. Below are some frequently asked questions and details. We look forward to reading your papers!

## Team Formation Questions: 

- **Question:** I plan to participate in the WiDS Datathon Excellence in Research Award with a team of up to 8 people. Do we all need to register separately?

  **Answer:** Yes, each individual who participates must register with us by filling out [this form](https://bit.ly/widsdatathon_phaseII_reg).<br><br>

- **Question:** What are the team formation guidelines? Do we have to work in teams?

  **Answer:**  We encourage Phase II individuals and author teams of up to 8 people to work together. You may collaborate with individuals in a ¡°new¡± team for the second phase of the Datathon, as long as (1) each person participated in the [first phase](https://www.kaggle.com/c/widsdatathon2022) of the datathon on Kaggle and (2) your new team still includes 50% or more individuals identifying as women. If you are submitting as a team, you do NOT need to merge within the Kaggle platform (it's actually disabled), but all team members must be listed as collaborators on your submitted research paper, and all team members must accept the [competition rules](https://www.kaggle.com/c/phase-ii-widsdatathon2022/rules) before the submission deadline of June 30th.<br><br>

- **Question:** Are folks encouraged to compete solo, or is there an opportunity to get matched to a team to learn from / maximize cross-functionally?

  **Answer:**  You can compete on your own, or as a team. You can attend an [office hours](https://www.kaggle.com/c/phase-ii-widsdatathon2022/overview/timeline-and-mentorship-office-hours) to meet other participants to form a team, or post in the Kaggle discussion board.<br><br>

- **Question:** Why do teams have to be at least 50% women?

  **Answer:** The WiDS Datathon is committed to encouraging women worldwide to get involved in data science.  By having at least 50% women on a team, it ensures that the women will feel like they belong as an integral part of the group and their voices will be heard.  <br><br>

- **Question:** Can we participate in a different team than we did for the WiDS Datathon Phase 1?

  **Answer:** Yes, you may participate in a new team. You can choose to do Phase 2 by yourself as well.  <br><br>

## Excellence in Research Award Questions:

- **Question:** What is the difference between WiDS Datathon Phase 1 and Phase 2 of the competition? 

  **Answer:** Phase I is the [Kaggle competition](https://www.kaggle.com/c/widsdatathon2022), which ended on February 26th. Phase II which is now open is important because it will examine the impact of our theme which is climate change across multiple domains not just focusing on one data set. This allows participants to pursue something that is deeper, more creative, more of scientific research.<br><br>

- **Question:** How many authors are allowed for the Excellence in Research Award submission?

  **Answer:** We encourage individuals and author teams of up to 8 people to work together, since we can support up to 8 VIP tickets and prizes for the [Excellence in Research Award winner](https://www.kaggle.com/c/widsdatathon2022/overview/datathon-phase-ii-excellence-in-research-award). You are welcome to acknowledge additional collaborators and mentors directly in the paper, through an extended blogpost or [Kaggle Discussion Forum](https://www.kaggle.com/c/phase-ii-widsdatathon2022/discussion) post, or other venues.<br><br>

- **Question:** I was part of a team for the first phase of the Datathon on Kaggle, but only a portion of the team is interested in writing a paper. Can I still participate in the Datathon Phase II Excellence in Research Award?

  **Answer:** Yes, you are welcome to collaborate with individuals in a ¡°new¡± team for the second phase of the Datathon<br><br>

- **Question:** Do participants in Phase II need to have participated in Phase I? 

  **Answer:** Yes, you do need to participate in Phase I to qualify for Phase II.<br><br>

- **Question:** Can Teams work on multiple research tracks during Phase II?

  **Answer:** No. Each team can work on only one research track during Phase II.<br><br>

- **Question:** Can Teams use external data?

  **Answer:** Yes, teams are welcome to use any publicly available data to enrich data provided for their chosen track.<br><br>

- **Question:** When/how will the papers be reviewed for the Excellence in Research Award? Can I submit my work for publication elsewhere?

  **Answer:** After the June 30, 2022 research paper deadline, submissions will be reviewed for their potential for real-world impact, rigor in scientific methodology, and clarity of communication, by subject matter experts from the WiDS Datathon Committee, the National Science Foundation Big Data Innovation Hubs, and Datathon partners. The paper content will be treated as confidential in order to enable participants to submit their work to scientific journals and conferences if they wish (please refer to specific guidance from publishing venues).<br><br>

- **Question:** Does my leaderboard standing impact my eligibility and consideration for the Excellence in Research Award?

  **Answer:** No, leaderboard placement does not impact the WiDS Datathon Excellence in Research Award. As long as you have submitted an entry in the first phase of the Datathon (by February 26, 2022), it does not matter if you are ranked first or last on the leaderboard. All are encouraged to write a paper that shares their perspective and contributions.<br><br>

- **Question:** Can I include results and work that I complete after the Kaggle leaderboard closes February 26, 2022, in the Excellence in Research Award paper submission?

  **Answer:** Yes, the part of second phase of the datathon is intended to encourage deeper exploration of the Phase I data. Please consider sharing your stories and future work with us using #WiDSDatathon and via email to widsdatathon@stanford.edu!

- **Question:** Where and how do I submit the paper for Phase II?

  **Answer:** Phase II Teams should submit their final papers to [Submittable](https://datahubs.submittable.com/submit/219905/wids-datathon-2022-excellence-in-research-award-phase-ii) by June 30th, 2022. See [submission instructions](https://www.kaggle.com/c/phase-ii-widsdatathon2022/overview/submission-instructions).


## Other Questions

- **Question:** What does it cost?

  **Answer:** Participation in the Datathon Phase II is free.<br><br>

- **Question:** What is the WiDS Datathon code of conduct?

  **Answer:** We adopt the ASA Code of Conduct:  The Datathon is a harassment-free space for participants of all races, gender and trans statuses, sexual orientations, physical abilities, physical appearances, body sizes, and beliefs. Harassment includes, but is not limited to: deliberate intimidation; stalking; unwanted photography or recording; sustained disruption of talks or other events; inappropriate physical contact; objectionable tweets/comments/utterances online; and unwelcome sexual attention. We ask you to be mindful. Harassment isn¡¯t about what you intend. It is about how your words and actions are received.<br><br>

- **Question:** What if I have questions about the data during the Datathon?

  **Answer:** ?Kaggle has a Discussion Board that you can post questions about data issues within the appropriate scope. The WiDS Datathon community, including other participants and the WiDS Datathon Committee, will reply to the questions throughout the contest.<br><br>



----------------------
# Submission Instructions

# Submission Instructions
You can make as many paper submissions as you like, but we will only consider your latest submission before the submission deadline of June 30th.

If you are submitting as a team, you do NOT need to merge within the Kaggle platform (it's actually disabled), but all team members must be listed as collaborators on the submitted research paper, and all team members must accept the competition rules before the submission deadline. You can submit your paper with up to 8 team members.

A valid submission will meet the [WiDS Datathon Paper Guidelines](https://www.kaggle.com/c/phase-ii-widsdatathon2022/overview/evaluation-and-paper-guidelines). If submitting as a team, all team members must be listed on the top of your paper.

### [Click here](https://datahubs.submittable.com/submit/219905/wids-datathon-2022-excellence-in-research-award-phase-ii) to go to the Submission form. Submissions are due June 30th @ 11:59pm PST
----------------------
# Resources & Tutorials For Research Milestones

# Resources & Tutorials For Research Milestones

In addition to themed office hours corresponding to research milestones, we provide participants with resources and tutorials to gain milestone related skills.

## Milestone 1: Exploratory Data Analysis
**Dates:** March 8th - April 15th

**Goals:** Understand the format, structure of the data; discover interesting patterns and trends in the data; generate scientific hypothesis based on structures, patterns and trends (e.g. variable y seem to be highly correlates with x, thus we may predict y from x).

**Resources:**
- Introduction to Exploratory Data Analysis:
  - [Notes on Exploratory Data Analysis](https://www.stat.cmu.edu/~hseltman/309/Book/chapter4.pdf)
- Exploratory Data Analysis with `python`:
  - [Exploratory Data Analysis and Visualisation in `python`](https://valdanchev.github.io/reproducible-data-science-python/data_exploration_visualisation.html#)
  - [`python` for Data Analysis](https://bedford-computing.co.uk/learning/wp-content/uploads/2015/10/Python-for-Data-Analysis.pdf)
  - [Example Code for EDA in `python`](https://github.com/PacktPublishing/Hands-on-Exploratory-Data-Analysis-with-Python)
- Exploratory Data analysis with `R`:
  - [Exploratory Data Analysis (from `R` for Data Science with `R`)](https://bookdown.org/ronsarafian/IntrotoDS/eda.html)
  - [Exploratory Data Analysis (from `R` for Data Science)](https://r4ds.had.co.nz/exploratory-data-analysis.html)

## Milestone 2: Building Basic Models
**Dates:** April 15th - May 13th

**Goals:** Based on your previous exploratory data analysis, form hypotheses about the trends you observed in the data. For example, you might hypothesize that some quantity `y` depends on other quantities `x_1, x_2, ...`; or you might hypothesize that the data `x_1, x_2, ...` can be clustered. Build simple models guided by your hypotheses and evaluate how well they capture the trends in the data. For example, you might build simple models to predict `y` from `x_1, x_2, ...`, or apply clustering methods on the data `x_1, x_2, ...`.

**Resources:**
- Clustering:
  - [Overview of Clustering](https://online.stat.psu.edu/stat555/node/11/)
- Clustering with `python`:
  - [An Introduction to Clustering Algorithms in Python](https://towardsdatascience.com/an-introduction-to-clustering-algorithms-in-python-123438574097)
- Clustering with `R`:
  - [K-means Cluster Analysis](https://uc-r.github.io/kmeans_clustering)
- Regression and Classification:
  - []()
- Regression and Classification with `python`:
  - [Scikit-learn tutorial: How to implement linear regression](https://www.educative.io/blog/scikit-learn-tutorial-linear-regression)
  - [Scikit-learn cheat sheet: methods for classification & regression](https://www.educative.io/blog/scikit-learn-cheat-sheet-classification-regression-methods)
- Regression and Classification with `R`:
  - [Linear Regression](http://r-statistics.co/Linear-Regression.html)
  - [Logistic Regression](http://r-statistics.co/Logistic-Regression-With-R.html)","# Dataset Description

# Research Tracks and Data

Participants must choose **one** of following three tracks to explore:

- **US Environmental Protection Agency (EPA):** weather, air pollutant, and census data
- **MIT Critical Data:** CDC county level COVID data
- **Climate Change AI:** Fine grained building energy usage data

Each track comes with a starter dataset as well as a list of suggested research questions. Some tracks require participants to download additional data in order to answer research questions. For example, the MIT Critical Data track requires participants to [download COVID outcomes](https://github.com/CSSEGISandData/COVID-19) in order to build predictive models. The necessary links to required additional datasets will be provided as part of the starter files (`data_links.txt`).

Participants are welcome to explore their own research questions as well as augment the starter dataset with additional data from any public source. For example, participants are encouraged to explore the relationship between factors involved in climate change and the outcomes in each track. To do this, participants are welcomed to enrich the dataset from their track with climate change related datasets (e.g. the [US Climate Extremes Index](https://www.climate.gov/maps-data/dataset/us-climate-extremes-index-graph-or-map)).

Throughout the competition, participants will have opportunities to interact with domain experts in their chosen track and receive feedback on their work. [Sign up](https://www.eventbrite.com/e/wids-datathon-excellence-in-research-award-phase-ii-office-hours-tickets-273303467027) to attend the Phase II mentorship office hours.",False,False
5403,kore-2022,"# Description

In this turn-based simulation game you control a small armada of spaceships. As you mine the rare mineral ¡°kore¡± from the depths of space, you teleport it back to your homeworld. But it turns out you aren¡¯t the only civilization with this goal. In each game two players will compete to collect the most kore from the board. Whoever has the largest kore cache by the end of 400 turns¡ªor eliminates all of their opponents from the board before that¡ªwill be the winner!

<img src=""https://i.imgur.com/BtSsuHD.gif"" style=""display: block;width: 475px; margin: 50px auto;"" >

Your algorithms determine the movements of your fleets to collect kore, but it's up to you to figure out how to make effective and efficient moves. You control your ships, build new ships, create shipyards, eliminate opponents, and mine the kore on the game board.

May your fleet live long and prosper!

----------------------
# Evaluation

Each day your team is able to submit up to 5 agents (bots) to the competition. Each submission will play Episodes (games) against other agents on the ladder that have a similar skill rating. Over time skill ratings will go up with wins, down with losses, or evened out with ties. 

Every agent submitted will continue to play episodes until the end of the competition, with newer agents playing a much more frequent number of episodes. On the leaderboard only your best scoring agents will be shown, but you can track the progress of all of your submissions on your Submissions page.

Each Submission has an estimated Skill Rating which is modeled by a Gaussian N(¦Ì,¦Ò2) where ¦Ì is the estimated skill and ¦Ò represents the uncertainty of that estimate which will decrease over time.

When you upload a Submission, we first play a Validation Episode where that Submission plays against copies of itself to make sure it works properly. If the Episode fails, the Submission is marked as Error and you can download the agent logs to help figure out why. Otherwise, we initialize the Submission with ¦Ì0=600 and it joins the pool of All Submissions for ongoing evaluation.

We repeatedly run Episodes from the pool of All Submissions, and try to pick Submissions with similar ratings for fair matches. Newly submitted agents will be given an increased rate in the number of episodes run to give you faster feedback.

After an Episode finishes, we'll update the Rating estimate for all Submissions in that Episode. If one Submission won, we'll increase its ¦Ì and decrease its opponent's ¦Ì -- if the result was a draw, then we'll move the two ¦Ì values closer towards their mean. The updates will have magnitude relative to the deviation from the expected result based on the previous ¦Ì values, and also relative to each Submission's uncertainty ¦Ò. We also reduce the ¦Ò terms relative to the amount of information gained by the result. The score by which your agent wins or loses an Episode does not affect the skill rating updates.

## Ranking System
After an Episode finishes, we'll update the Rating estimate for all Submissions in that Episode. If one Submission won, we'll increase its ¦Ì and decrease its opponent's ¦Ì -- if the result was a draw, then we'll move the two ¦Ì values closer towards their mean. The updates will have magnitude relative to the deviation from the expected result based on the previous ¦Ì values, and also relative to each Submission's uncertainty ¦Ò. We also reduce the ¦Ò terms relative to the amount of information gained by the result. The score by which your agent wins or loses an Episode does not affect the skill rating updates.

## Final Evaluation

At the submission deadline on July 12th, additional submissions will be locked. From July 13th to July 27th, we will continue to run games. At the conclusion of this period, the leaderboard is final and is used to determine who gets various ranking based prizes.
----------------------
# Timeline

* **April 12, 2022** - Start Date.
* **July 5, 2022** - Entry Deadline. You must accept the competition rules before this date in order to compete.
* **July 5, 2022** - Team Merger Deadline. This is the last day participants may join or merge teams.
* **July 12, 2022** - Final Submission Deadline.
* **July 13, 2022- July 27, 2022** - Estimated range when final games are played. 
* **July 28, 2022** - Winners announced.

The competition organizers reserve the right to update the contest timeline if they deem it necessary.

All deadlines are at 11:59 PM UTC on the corresponding day unless otherwise noted. The competition organizers reserve the right to update the contest timeline if they deem it necessary.
----------------------
# Prizes

* 1st Place - $3,000
* 2nd Place - $3,000
* 3rd Place - $3,000
* 4th Place - $3,000
* 5th Place - $3,000
----------------------
# Kore Rules

###Rules

In this game you control a small armada of spaceships, mining the rare mineral ¡°kore¡± from the depths of space and teleporting it back to your homeworld. But it turns out you aren¡¯t the only civilization with this goal ¨C in each game 2 players will compete to collect the most kore from the board. Whoever collects the most kore by the end of 400 turns, or eliminates all of their opponents from the board before that, will be the winner!

###Game Setup

![](https://i.imgur.com/idqedNQ.png)

At the start of the game, each player is assigned a random quadrant (and associated color) on the game board. Each player starts with 1 shipyard and 500 kore.

The game board is 21x21 cells large and wraps around on both the north/south and east/west borders. The southwest (bottom-left) corner of the board is designated (0,0) and the northeast (top-right) corner of the board is designated (20,20). 

*Strategy Tip: Due to the board wrapping, fleets sent out from the shipyard straight in any direction will return to that shipyard.*

The map is covered with a random distribution of kore. This distribution is symmetric both vertically and horizontally. In total, the game board starts with 2,750 kore on it. In the visualizer, the size of the kore icon is proportional to the amount of kore on that square.

###Summary

- The player with the most kore at the end of the game wins.
- You start the game with 1 shipyard and 500 kore.
- Creating a ship at a shipyard costs 10 kore.
- Fleets are launched with 1 or more ships.
- Converting a fleet of ships to a shipyard consumes 50 ships.
- Each turn you can issue a command to each of your shipyards to spawn more ships or launch a new fleet of ships with a flight plan.
- Each turn a fleet reads its flight plan. 
-- If it starts with one of NESW, it turns the fleet in that direction and the fleet moves
-- If it starts with an integer, decrement the integer and the fleet moves in its current direction
-- If it starts with a C, the fleet attempts to create a new shipyard at the given location.
-- If there are no characters remaining in the flight plan, the fleet will continue in its current direction
- As a fleet moves across the board, it mines kore underneath its current position. Mined kore is stored on the ship, and there is no maximum to how much kore a ship can hold.
- Kore in fleets cannot be spent to buy ships until deposited at a shipyard. At the end of the game - Players need to move their fleets over shipyards to deposit the kore and teleport it back to their homeworlds to count for the final kore total.
- Fleets that move onto the same square collide. First, allied fleets are absorbed into the largest fleet (most ships, or, if equal, the fleet with the most kore). The largest fleet (that is, the one with most ships) survives and all other fleets will be destroyed. The remaining fleet loses ships equal to the ship count of the second largest fleet. The largest fleet steals all of the kore from other fleets it collides with. If two or more fleets tie for the largest number of ships, they are both destroyed and their kore is left on the square. 
![](https://i.imgur.com/m81KdOX.gif)
- After fleet collision, a check then is performed for a fleet-shipyard collision. If the colliding enemy fleet has more ships than the shipyard, then the shipyard owner will change possession


###Turn Order

Each game of Kore lasts for 400 turns or until all but one player has been eliminated. Each turn, each agent will be given a copy of the board state (the ¡°observation¡±) with complete information about every aspect of the game (including how much kore each player has, the position of all fleets (and ship_count, kore carried and flight_plan) and how much kore is on every cell of the board). Each agent will then need to return a list of actions for their shipyards to take within a set amount of time.

If your agent does not return actions in time or encounters an error, it will be marked as errored and will immediately have all its fleets and shipyards removed from the game. 

Each turn each shipyard can take one of the following actions:

**Spawn Ship**
Actions: SPAWN_X
Cost: 10 kore per ship (10 * X)

This action will add X ships to the shipyard ship count. Initially, a shipyard can only spawn 1 ship at a time but the longer you control the shipyard the more you can spawn (up to 10). Shipyard turns_controlled is used to track how long a player has controlled a shipyard. Assuming a shipyard currently can spawn Y ships, after controlling it for Y^2 + 1 turns it can now produce Y+1 ships. See the following table:

| Turns Controlled | Spawn Maximum |
| --- | --- | 
| 0 | 1 |
| 2 | 2 |
| 7 | 3 |
| 17 | 4 |
| 34 | 5 |
| 60 | 6 |
| 97 | 7 |
| 147 | 8 |
| 212 | 9 |
| 294 | 10 |

**Launch**
Actions: LAUNCH\_X\_<FLIGHT_PLAN\>
Cost: 0 kore

This action will launch a fleet with X ships, which follows the given flight plan. Given the flight plan a fleet will either continue in its current direction, turn and move, or convert into a shipyard. To create a shipyard there must be at least 50 ships.

**Flight Plans**
Flight plans determine the path charted by the launching fleet. Their maximum length is determined by the size of the fleet with the following formula: `floor(2 * ln(num_ships)) + 1`. Length is measured by simple string length `len(flight_plan)`. Once launched flight plans are visible to all enemies and cannot be changed other than the once a turn update.

*Strategy Tip: Notice that the minimum number of ships for a fleet that can complete a circle (eg ¡°N5E5S5W¡±) is 21. Consider how the fleet coalescence mechanics might allow for collection with smaller fleets.*

**Converting**

When converting into a shipyard, any kore on the cell underneath the new shipyard will be destroyed. Any kore carried by the fleet converting itself will be deposited instantly and the fleet will lose 50 ships. Any remaining ships are added to the shipyard¡¯s ship count. 

If the fleet does not have at least 50 ships when it gets to this instruction (after a collision with an enemy for example) it will skip the convert instruction and will continue in its current direction and process following commands as normal. 

*Example: if a fleet with size 40 has the flight plan `NCW` it will act as though it has a flight plan of `NW` (skipping over the C to the next valid instruction).*

###Turn Resolution
After both players have submitted their actions for the turn, the system will automatically resolve the turn and update the board state. All player turns are resolved simultaneously across the following phases:

**1. Spawning and Launching**

All shipyards that ordered a spawn action are resolved with new ships being added to their ship_count. All shipyards that ordered a launch action have ships deducted from their ship count to create the new fleet and the new fleet is created. Note, after this the fleet will occupy the same square as the shipyard.

**2. Fleets Update Flight Plan**

All fleets process the next part of their flight plan (if present). This could result in a fleet updating its direction, or attempting to convert into a shipyard.
| Current Flight Plan | Fleet Affect | Next Flight Plan | 
| --- | --- | --- |
| ""N"" | Fleet direction set to North | """" | 
| ""N5E"" | Fleet direction set to North | ""5E"" | 
| ""5E"" | - | ""4E"" | 
| ""C"" | Fleet attempts to convert to shipyard | - | 
| ""10E"" | - | ""9E"" | 

Fleets then move 1 forward in their current direction.

**3. Fleets Convert to Shipyard**

When converting into a shipyard, any kore on the cell underneath the new shipyard will be destroyed. Any kore carried by the fleet converting itself will be deposited instantly and the fleet will lose 50 ships. Any remaining ships are added to the new shipyard¡¯s ship count.

**4. Fleets Move**

All fleets move forward one square in their current direction.

**5. Allied Fleets Coalesce**

Any allied fleets that currently occupy the same space are added to the largest allied fleet in that location. Ship size, current kore, and finally direction (NESW) are used to determine the largest fleet.

**6. Fleet Collisions**

Fleet collision is resolved, reducing the number of fleets in every cell on the board to one or less. In each collision the largest fleet is the survivor, with all others destroyed. The largest fleet loses ships equal to the ships in the seconds to largest fleet. The largest fleet is defined as the ship with the most ships. If multiple ships tie for having the most ships, all the fleets in the collision are destroyed and their carried kore is deposited onto the square. The surviving fleet loses ships equal to the size of the second to largest fleet, takes all the kore from the destroyed ships¡¯ cargoes, and retains its place on the board.

**7. Shipyard Collision**

If a fleet occupies the same square as an allied shipyard, that fleet¡¯s ships are added to the shipyard and its kore is added to the player's kore count. If a fleet and shipyard from different players occupy the same cell:
1.  if the fleet contains more ships than the shipyard, the shipyard changes hands (resetting turns controlled) to the fleet player and that player adds the kore from the fleet to their kore supply and the shipyard starts with the `fleet.ship_count - shipyard.ship_count` ships. 
2. if the shipyard contains more ships the fleet is destroyed and the defending shipyard loses ships equal to the attacking fleet's ship count. The defender also claims the kore from the attacking fleet. 

Losing your shipyard does not reduce your total kore - all deposited kore has already been teleported safely back to your homeworld.

**8. Fleet to Fleet Damage**

Each fleet does damage to all enemy fleets orthogonally adjacent to it (North, East, South, West) equal to its current ship count. Destroyed fleets drop 50% of their kore onto their currently occupied position with 50% being awarded to their attackers (proportionally to their attack if there are multiple). If the attacking fleet does not survive, the kore is dropped back onto the tile instead.

Strategy Note: This allows a single fleet to do damage to multiple fleets!

**9. Kore Mining**

All fleets on a square with kore mine it, moving a certain percentage of the cell¡¯s kore into that ship¡¯s storage. Kore in ship storage is indicated in the visualizer by a blue glow in the center of the fleet. The formula to determine the percentage mined is determined by the fleet¡¯s ship count with more ships mining more kore and tops out at 99%. The percentage (decimal) is given by this formula: ln(num_ships) / 20

| Number Ships | % mined  |
| --- | --- | 
| 1 | 0 |
| 2 | 3 |
| 3 | 5 |
| 5 | 8 |
| 8 | 10 |
| 13 | 13 |
| 21 | 15 |
| 34 | 18 |
| 55 | 20 |
| 91 | 23 |
| 149 | 25 |
| 245 | 28 |
| 404 | 30 |

**10. Kore Regeneration**
Every cell on the board that has more than 0 kore and no fleets on top of it will now regenerate by 2% of the existing amount of kore in the cell. Cells with 500+ kore do not regenerate. Note that a cell can contain substantially more than 500 kore if several fleets collide and deposit their carried kore.

**11. End Turn**
The ¡°step¡± number is incremented, ending the turn. The updated board is saved and redistributed to each agent as the ¡°observation¡±, moving to the next turn.

At this step, a player is eliminated if they are no longer able to viably compete in the game. This happens when the player no longer has any ships (across both fleets and shipyards) and has less than ship_cost kore remaining. If eliminated, all of a player¡¯s remaining shipyards are removed from the game.

At the end of each turn, the game also checks if a player has been eliminated or if the game has reached turn 400. If either of those is true instead of starting a new turn the game moves to the game end step.","# Dataset Description

Kore 2022 Starter Agent",True,True
