5417,mlrw-biomedicalhackathon,"# Description

# Introduction:

Omics repositories such as the NCBI Gene Expression Omnibus (GEO) and EBI ArrayExpress accumulate and serve gene expression data from thousands of studies. It is clear that these data contain much more information than what has typically been extracted from each individual dataset for the accompanying initial publication. However, currently, performing integrative analysis of large collections of gene expression studies to obtain a global integrated view of cellular regulation requires a significant data-wrangling effort, that is, manually unifying data formats, adding metadata, and converting the data to be more machine-readable.

Gene expression signatures are very useful in understanding underlying molecular causes of disease, effects of drugs, etc. Manual extraction of collections of gene expression signatures from GEO has been demonstrated to be highly useful. It was applied for drug repurposing, suggesting novel drugs for many diseases, and explaining mechanisms of action for many approved drugs. Several efforts have attempted to further annotate datasets from GEO manually; one example is Gene Expression data Mining Toward Relevant Network Discovery (GEM-TREND). The disadvantage of manual curation is that it does not scale up to cover the thousands of studies currently available. For similar challenges, crowdsourcing projects have been developed as a potential solution to overcome this obstacle.

Therefore, we need to automate this step of generating gene signatures. One way would be to classify samples in a dataset as being control or perturbation. Control in an experiment is a way to know that our experiment has worked for sure, for example, if we are treating something with a drug then control for that would be treated with water or DMSO and perturbation would be sample treated with that drug.

## Problem Statement:

[![Screenshot-2022-02-09-at-9-40-19-PM.png](https://i.postimg.cc/Ls9wt3HP/Screenshot-2022-02-09-at-9-40-19-PM.png)](https://postimg.cc/w1rwHJRx)

The target variables for classification are columns ctrl and pert which we have to correctly classify. Each row contains text which can be used for classifying it as a control or perturbation. The data is divided into multiple csv files, each csv file is a dataset containing samples. There are in total 20000 samples whose metadata is given, actual manual curated labels are given for 600 out of 20000. Now why is that, why not give labels for all the datasets, this is because we want to stress the fact that data is more important in situations where data is limited and it is costly to get data curated (example, chest x-ray), that's the fundamental thesis of data-centric AI.

You need to use 600 given labels to label the remaining training datasets in order to improve the model. Some folks call it weak supervised learning but please don¡¯t go by jargon. You could also pay other folks to manually curate the rest of the datasets, that¡¯s a fair approach but then you would miss a chance to explain your solution to many others through us.

You can get started with this [starter notebook](https://www.kaggle.com/yerramvarun/logistic-regression-sample-starter-notebook) to make your first submission!

## Acknowledgements
We thank Elucidata for providing this dataset.
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is [Mean F1-Score](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/F-score). 

## Submission Format
Take a look at the sample_submission.csv for better understanding. The submission file should be formatted as follows - 

```
geo_accession,ctrl
GSM1973514,0
GSM1973517,0
GSM1973515,1
GSM1973516,0

```
Take a look at the sample starter notebook to see how to create the submission file.
----------------------
# Timeline

**Feb 16, 2022**:
Start of the competition.

**Feb 25, 2022**:
Final submission deadline. The last date to accept rules, make teams and submit solutions.


[![Hackathon-Poster-5.png](https://i.postimg.cc/PqQqS1tW/Hackathon-Poster-5.png)](https://postimg.cc/yJWBdg3x)
----------------------
# Hints And Tips

## Hints and Tips:

Preprocessing and feature extraction are two very important steps in this problem. Not all information is relevant in classifying samples. 

Beware of data leaks while evaluating the model, the metrics that you choose for evaluation are important.

Don¡¯t focus on the models, you can use the simplest logistic regression or naive Bayes to solve this problem, trust us LSTM or transformers won¡¯t give you a competitive advantage, curating more data will be better. Look at the figure below, you have 2000 labeled samples, you should get around 0.8 accuracy, getting more data helps in increasing the accuracy.

[![samples-vs-acc.png](https://i.postimg.cc/7LKcmJ6W/samples-vs-acc.png)](https://postimg.cc/XZyQvJ9f)

If you don¡¯t do it right, the accuracy will not change much with samples. The important thing is to select the right columns for classification.

[![image-5.png](https://i.postimg.cc/jSrG5rmZ/image-5.png)](https://postimg.cc/bs36C4zt)

We are providing a template notebook for training a logistic model on the data, go crazy.

If you want to learn more about data-centric AI, please watch this [video](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=06-AZXmwHjo) 

## References:

Wang, Z., Monteiro, C., Jagodnik, K. et al. Extraction and analysis of signatures from the Gene Expression Omnibus by the crowd. Nat Commun 7, 12846 (2016). https://doi.org/10.1038/ncomms12846
