5450,ee4211,"# Description

This is the home page of the EE4211 course project - segmentation task. 

You need to perform the bird segmentation task using the given CUB-200-2011 dataset. 

This dataset is for internal academic research of the course only and cannot be used commercially.
----------------------
# Evaluation

# Evaluation
This competition is evaluated on the intersection over union (IoU). The IoU of a predicted set of object pixels and a set of true object pixels is calculated as:

??????(??,??)=(??¡É??)/(??¡È??).

# Submission File
In order to reduce the submission file size, our metric uses run-length encoding on the pixel values. Instead of submitting an exhaustive list of indices for your segmentation, you will submit pairs of values that contain a start position and a run length. E.g. '1 3' implies starting at pixel 1 and running a total of 3 pixels (1,2,3).

The competition format requires a space delimited list of pairs. For example, '1 3 10 5' implies pixels 1,2,3,10,11,12,13,14 are to be included in the mask. The pixels are one-indexed and numbered from top to bottom, then left to right: 1 is pixel (1,1), 2 is pixel (2,1), etc.

The metric checks that the pairs are sorted, positive, and the decoded pixel values are not duplicated. It also checks that no two predicted masks for the same image are overlapping.

The file should contain a header and have the following format. Each row in your submission represents a single predicted bird segmentation for the given ```<ImageId>```.

```
ImageId,EncodedPixels  
0114f484a16c152baa2d82fdd43740880a762c93f436c8988ac461c5c9dbe7d5,1 1  
0999dab07b11bc85fb8464fc36c947fbd8b5d6ec49817361cb780659ca805eac,1 1  
0999dab07b11bc85fb8464fc36c947fbd8b5d6ec49817361cb780659ca805eac,2 3 8 9
```","# Dataset Description

The dataset includes:

6788 training images and the corresponding segmentation ground truths.

5000 testing images.",True,True
5451,scet,"# Description

## Different Learning Backgrounds
""There's no single, prescribed academic path in the nascent field of data science. In this discussion, a panel of employed data scientists from different learning backgrounds share their stories and best advice."" (Jessica Li)
[Panel: Data Scientists with Different Learning Backgrounds | CareerCon 2019 | Kaggle](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=wXjiqb4HVu0)

## Context 
Traffic incidents pull crucial resources away from fires and some believe that traffic incidents or EMS response may lead to additional traffic incidents in the localized area due to rubbernecking and unfocused driving by the passersby. 
In this competition, you will use regression analysis to predict the number of injured victims in traffic collisions from the Statewide Integrated Traffic Records System (SWITRS) data maintained by the California Highway Patrol (CHP). SWITRS contains all crashes that were reported to CHP from local and governmental agencies. 

## Potential Impact
Through this Kaggle community competition, we hope to give Cal students the opportunity to ideate and innovate new technical solutions to solving some of the world's most pressing environmental problems.

## Prize Detail
Winning teams will have the opportunity to be selected to receive mentorship, and opportunities to work with first responders and industry experts in order to collect feedback and improve upon the prototypes of their technology in the Disaster X-Lab through X-Lab innovation challenges and SCET courses in the fall.

Points awarded toward [SCET Innovation Fellowship](https://scet.berkeley.edu/students/scet-student-innovation-fellows/) 
15 bonus points for 1st place submission
10 bonus points for 2nd place submission
5 bonus points for 3rd place submission
30 points for all participants who submit

## [Disaster Lab](https://disasterlab.berkeley.edu)

Disaster Lab facilitates X-Lab innovation challenges where UC Berkeley undergraduate and graduate students ideate solutions to key problems identified by first responders in regards to home hardening, defensible space, early detection, and rapid response in order to spur innovation in the disaster technology space. X-Lab challenges are facilitated by researchers, industry experts, and senior fire personnel.

The Disaster Lab will offer a new SCET course in the FA22 semester. See Berkeley Academic Guide [course catalog](https://classes.berkeley.edu) for updates. 
----------------------
# Evaluation

## Evaluation Metric
The evaluation metric for this competition is **Root Mean Square Error**.
The **RMSE** is calculated as: 
![](https://miro.medium.com/max/966/1*lqDsPkfXPGen32Uem1PTNg.png)

where:
- **??** is the total number of instances,
- **?????** is the predicted value of the target for instance (i), and 
- **????** is the actual value of the target for instance (i).

## Submission Format
For each Case Id in the test set, you must predict a value for the Injured victims variable. The file submission file should contain should have 54076 rows and two columns: `case_id` and `injured_victims`.  The `case_id` data type should be a string and the `injured_victims` data type should be a double for your submission.

The file should contain a header and have the following format:
```
case_id,injured_victims
1,1
```","# Dataset Description

## Codebook 
The SWITRS [codebook](https://tims.berkeley.edu/help/SWITRS.php#Codebook) provides a complete description of all the fields including the variable name and all possible values for the field.

If you want to download a Microsoft Word version of the codebook, please [click here](https://tims.berkeley.edu/help/files/SWITRS_codebook_20181203.doc).",True,True
