5453,datawiz-2022-r1,"# Description

As a part of an internship in the UUPC Trust for cancer patients across India, you were hired as a data scientist to help the organization to identify patients in the more severe stages of the disease. The Chennai-based NGO has surveyed over 10,000 patient reports in India and has compiled all of the data in the given dataset. 
With this dataset, you have to utilize machine learning algorithms and identify the patients¡¯ cancer stage in order to aid the NGO to support the patients who have a greater need of healthcare.

P.S: Head over to [credenz.in](https://credenz.in/) to register for Credenz if you haven't already!
----------------------
# Evaluation

#**Evaluation Metric**
The Evaluation Metric for this competition is **categorization accuracy**.","# Dataset Description

In the dataset provided, you have been given all the data needed for assessing a patient's condition.
You will be required to predict the **Tumor Stage** of a particular patient.

## File Descriptions

*   **r1_train.csv** - the training set
*   **r1_test.csv** - the test set
*   **sample_solution.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

## Data Fields

*   Patient ID: A unique identification number assigned to every patient.
*   Sample ID: The identification for samples collected from a particular patient.
*   Tumor Stage: The stage of cancer the tumor is in.
*   Type of Cancer: The type of cancer which the patient is suffering from.
*   Verbose Type of Cancer: The type of cancer, including any subtypes, which the patient is suffering from.
*   Sample Coverage: A measure of reliability of the sample taken.
*   Patient Age: At what age the patient was diagnosed with cancer. 
*   Number of Samples per Patient: The number of samples that were taken from a patient.
*   DNA Input: A metric calculated by sequencing the DNA of the patient and using research-based results to assess the severity of the cancer.
*   Mutation Count: The number of Mutations observed in the patient¡¯s cancer.
*   Sample Type: The type of sample taken from the patient.
*   Gender: Gender of the patient.
*   Alcohol/Tobacco Consumption: Whether the patient consumed alcohol or tobacco.
*   Specimen Preservation Type: The kind of preservation used to store the sample.
*   Specimen Type: The type of specimen taken from the patient.
*   TMB (nonsynonymous): The tumor mutation burden is the number of mutations in the cancer determined by next-*   generation gene-sequencing techniques.
*   Anti Raster Probability: A measure of probability of response to given treatment.
*   Oligo-Bertrand Symptoms: Set of symptoms for cancer stages as stated by Dr. Bertrand et al. in their research paper of 2014.
*   Treatment: The form of treatment the patient is undergoing.
*   Tumor Purity: The purity of the tumor present in the patient.
*   OLPG Rate: The measure of coherence to Oligo-Bertrand Symptoms.",True,True
5454,orbit-propagation-challenge-2,"# Description

<p style=""text-align: center;"">
<img src=""https://zupimages.net/up/22/08/vyif.png"" width=""150"" height=""200""  /></p>

Welcome to the Hackaton SDD 2022!
Please see the presentation to get all the informations. 
You can see this github repository to get useful scripts: https://github.com/TheoNguyen611/hackaton_2022_public

#Second challenge
The goal of the second challenge is to predict orbit thanks to the learning of the error between an analytical and a numerical propagator (considered as a reference).

## Getting Started
https://www.kaggle.com/theongy/getting-started

## Ressources
the full bibliography can be found here ?? https://github.com/TheoNguyen611/hackaton_SDD_ressources

https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/3316615.3316675
https://arxiv.org/abs/1605.09058
https://www.researchgate.net/publication/274618425_Application_of_Computational_Intelligence_in_Order_to_Develop_Hybrid_Orbit_Propagation_Methods
https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/2017amos.confE..15P/abstract





----------------------
# Evaluation

**You think that your model is ready to beat state of the art propagators?**
You have to evaluate the model on the test dataset that you can download in the competion data. In this dataset,only the first time step is present. You have to predict the 99 others. 
#Format
The submission should be a csv file with one line for each value predicted. Each line has a unique id with the following format : `orbitNb_timeStep_parameterNb` 
For example, for the first orbit, the second time step , and the parameter y, the submission file is:
```
id;predicted
0_1_1; value
```
#Scale
You have to predict only the cartesian parameters  (x,y,z,dx,dy,dz). Besides to ensure that each parameter is correctly evaluated with the MAE, **you have to divide the parameters in meter by the following scale factor: scale_factor= {""x"":1200,""y"":7000,""z"":7000,""dx"":1.4,""dy"":8.,""dz"":8.}**. This way, the cartesian parameters are all between -1000 and 1000 and they are fairly evaluated. 
By default, the loader loads the parameters with this scale factor but you may decide to work with the raw parameters, or to use cutom preprocessing. You are encouraged to do so, but don't forget to come back to the correct scale when it is time to prepare the submission
# Encoder
You can use the Submission encoder provided to create your submission. See [Getting Started Notebook](https://www.kaggle.com/theongy/getting-started)

# Winner 
After the end of the competion, the teams will be ranked on the MAE score. Depending on the position, they will get the following points:
?? First position : 8 points
?? Second position :  6 points
?? Third position : 4 points
Fourth position: 3 points
Fifth position: 2 points

The points of the 2 challenges will be summed to award the winner ! Have fun!","# Dataset Description

