5464,hydraulic-cross-section-2022,"# Description

The data set contains 1500 40x40 samples. Please subdivide them into a training and validation set to counter overfitting. The grading metric is RMSPE (Root Mean Percentage Square Error).

The deadline for this assignment is the 21'st of June, 23:59.","# Dataset Description

The cross-sections are represented as "".npy"" files. The input shape is [N,40,40,1]:

- 0th: number of samples (N)
- 1st: spatial dimension 1 (40)
- 2nd: spatial dimension 2 (40)
- 3rd: dummy, dimension (1) - required for fitting.

Please keep in mind that the value 0 represents a closed cell (no flow), and 1 represents an open cell (allows flow). The output is a data set with all hydraulic throughputs, which are matched by the index of the 0th dimension. 

So there are the following datasets:

- pub_input.npy - the input data that indicates the cell configuration
- pub_output.npy - the hydraulic throughputs corresponding to the data points above
- pri_input.**csv** - the input for the kaggle submissions.

**IMPORTANT**: Please note that Kaggle expects a specific data type:
The data should be uploaded as "".csv""-files, with labels for each column. See the example below.

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F5033067%2F423ff0a886f64e02ca1fd2e777ab6507%2FKaggle_submission_example.png?generation=1648909586668157&alt=media)",True,True
5465,hackathon-isae-supaero-2022,"# Description

Today, the weather is forecast using Numerical Weather Models (NWM), based on the equations of the physics of the atmosphere.

As you may have noticed, these models can make mistakes ! 

Your job will be to learn from these past errors to improve the forecast of the daily rainfall over the next 24 hours, on several observation stations.

## Data

To forecast the rain on the D day, you will have at your disposal, 2 types of data : 
* Observations from the D-1 day, measured at several ground stations
* Grids of forecasts made at the D-1 day, predicting the weather for the D day 

## Usefull links

* [Meteonet website](https://meteonet.umr-cnrm.fr/)
* [Link to download the forecast data](https://meteonet.umr-cnrm.fr/dataset/data/defi_ia_challenge/) on the Meteonet server
* [Meteonet's documentation](https://meteofrance.github.io/meteonet/)
* [Meteonet's GitHub page](https://github.com/meteofrance/meteonet/) with usefull notebooks
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation pages describes how submissions will be scored and how students should format their submissions. 

<h2>Evaluation metric</h2>
<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is the <a href=""https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mean_absolute_percentage_error""> Mean Absolute Percentage Error</a>. The MAPE score is closely related to a score often used in Meteo France to evaluate the predictions. 
It is given by:</p>

![](https://www.di.ens.fr/~sixzhang/mape.png)
where each forecast (Ft) is compared to the actual value (At) for the t-th item among n items. 

- **IMPORTANT**: To avoid zero on the denominator when computing the score, each (At) equals to the actual measurement of a ground-station + 1. To be consistent, your forecast (Fi) should be your actual prediction + 1. 

This score can also be computed with <a href=""https://github.com/Kaggle/kaggle-api"">Kaggle API</a>.

<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p>Submission files should contain two columns: Id and Prediction. <strong>Each line in the file corresponds to one item in the metric</strong>. 
</p><pre>Id,Prediction<br>14066001_149,2.4<br></pre>

- The **Id** represents the station and the day of a measurement. The list of Id can found in the `X_station_test` file in the ID column. You must remove the last part of the ID.  (e.g. `14047002_277_15` --&gt; `14047002_277`) You should have 85140 items in your submission file. 
- The **Prediction** represents the forecast value (Ft). No value in the Prediction column should be missing.","# Dataset Description

# Data description

The data is stored in two different places:

* The not voluminous data is on this Kaggle page, here in the 'Data' tab.
* The other part is stored on the [MeteoNet's server](https://meteonet.umr-cnrm.fr/dataset/data/defi_ia_challenge/)

## On Kaggle, in the tab 'Data'

* Other : directory containing various data 
    * ground station coordinates and 
    * land-sea and relief masks
    * a sample submission with random forecasts
* Train : all the ground station data and samples from the forecast data 
    * X_station_train.csv : observation data from the ground stations
    * Y_train.csv : the rainfall measured in each ground station
    * X_forecast directory : samples from the training forecast data for 1 day
* Test : all the ground station data and forecast data samples. 
    * X_station_test.csv : observation data from the ground stations
    * Id_month_test.csv : array indicating the month of each sample of the test set
    * X_forecast directory : samples from the test forecast data for 1 day

## On the MeteoNet's server

* Forecast features over a 2 year period of the training set
* Forecast features for the full test period

## Content of the files

### Y_train.csv

* date : one per day
* number_sta : station ID
* Ground_truth : accumulated rainfall value (in mm)
* Id : number_sta + index day -> need Xi to predict Yi

### X_station

* date : one per hour
* number_sta : station number
* several weather parameters : ff, t, td, hu, dd, precip. More information [here](https://meteofrance.github.io/meteonet/english/data/ground-observations/#3-meteorological-parameters).
* Id : number_sta + index day + hour -> need Xi to predict Yi

### X_forecast

Here you have 2 types of netCDF files : 

* 2D files from 2 numerical weather models, ARPEGE and AROME (finer resolution). Dimensions = latitude x longitude x forecast time. Weather parameters : 
    * at 2m above ground : temperature, humidity and dew point (*point de ros¨¦e*)
    * at 10m above ground : wind speed and direction, U and V wind components
    * Mean sea level pressure
    * Precipitation
* 3D files from ARPEGE. Dimensions = latitude x longitude x **height** x forecast time
    * Height files indicate the pressure at different altitudes
    * Isobar files indicate several weather parameters measures at different pressure levels : temperature, theta' w, humidity, wind speed and direction, vertical velocity, geopotential.

More information about the weather parameters in AROME and ARPEGE [here](https://meteofrance.github.io/meteonet/english/data/weather-models/).

<h2>Missing data</h2>
<p>There are missing data in ground station observations (NaNs). The sensors can be out of service, or some stations exist for some dates and not for all the studied period (2016-2019).</p>
<p>There are missing data in forecast data (some parameters and some dates).  It comes from the original files, so we cannot do anything about it (some problems during data archiving). </p>

<h2>Id system</h2>
<p>An Id system is set up to link easily the X and Y.</p>
<p> You have to use the X_i to predict the Y_i. </p>
<p>The ID has to the following structure according to data types:
<ul>
<li><strong>Y and baselines </strong> -> Id = station_number + day_index </li>
<li><strong>X_station </strong> -> Id = station_number + day_index + hour </li>
<li><strong>X_forecast </strong>-> Id = day_index + hour </li>
</ul>

<h3>Day index</h3>
The day index indicates a day number in the training and test sets.

* <strong>Train </strong> The day numbers are sorted by chronological order.
For example : 

    * <strong>Y_0 </strong> first day of the training set : 02/01/16 
    * <strong>Y_1 </strong> second day  of the training set : 03/01/16 
    * ...etc until 31/12/17
* <strong>Test</strong> The days are shuffled in a random way, so the ID doesn't mean anything. However, the month can be an interesting information to predict the rainfall, so you can find in the test directory a file called <strong>Id_month_test.csv</strong>, which gives you the corresponding month to each day index. </li>",True,True
