5470,ee4211-object-detection,"# Description

This is the home page of the EE4211 course project - detection task. 

You need to perform the bird detection task using the given CUB-200-2011 dataset. 

This dataset is for internal academic research of the course only and cannot be used commercially.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions on EE4211 course project - detection task are evaluated by computing Mean Squared Error (MSE). 

###  Submission File
For each image in the test set, you must predict a list of boxes describing objects in the image. Each box is described as
  ```
ImageID,PredictionString, PredictionString, PredictionString, PredictionString
ImageID,XMin, YMin, XMax, YMax

```","# Dataset Description

### The dataset includes:

- Training data: 1000 images.

- Testing data: 300 images.

- ### Training bounding boxes can be downloaded below: **train_boundingboxes.csv** - the training set

## Columns

*   `ImmageId` - definition of example_id
*   `PredictionString`?- definition of Xmin, Ymin, Xmax, Ymax",True,True
5471,ee4211-seg,"# Description

This is the home page of the EE4211 course project - segmentation task.

You need to perform the bird segmentation task using the given CUB-200-2011 dataset.

This dataset is for internal academic research of the course only and cannot be used commercially.
----------------------
# Evaluation

# Evaluation
This competition is evaluated on the intersection over union (IoU). The IoU of a predicted set of object pixels and a set of true object pixels is calculated as:

??????(??,??)=(??¡É??)/(??¡È??).

# Submission File
In order to reduce the submission file size, our metric uses run-length encoding on the pixel values. Instead of submitting an exhaustive list of indices for your segmentation, you will submit pairs of values that contain a start position and a run length. E.g. '1 3' implies starting at pixel 1 and running a total of 3 pixels (1,2,3).

The competition format requires a space delimited list of pairs. For example, '1 3 10 5' implies pixels 1,2,3,10,11,12,13,14 are to be included in the mask. The pixels are one-indexed and numbered from top to bottom, then left to right: 1 is pixel (1,1), 2 is pixel (2,1), etc.

The metric checks that the pairs are sorted, positive, and the decoded pixel values are not duplicated. It also checks that no two predicted masks for the same image are overlapping.

### We give the code for producing the run-length encoding [here](https://www.kaggle.com/guoxiaoqing/notebook72657674b4).

##### The example of submitted csv file can be found [here](https://www.kaggle.com/c/ee4211-seg/data), and the test csv should be similar to `SegTrain.csv'.","# Dataset Description

### The dataset includes:

- Training data: 1000 images, segmentation ground truths, and the corresponding run-length encoding.

- Testing data: 300 images.",True,True
