5473,teenmagi-2022,"# Description

This dataset is artificially generated. It is large in the sense that there is one million images and one thousand classes. The dataset is however conveniently small in the sense that ""images"" are only eight times eight pixels and single channel (gray level).

The dataset was made for the Tampere University DATA.ML.200 Pattern Recognition and Machine Learning course students, but also others can submit their results.
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is classification percent that measures the proportion of test images that are correctly classified, ideal value being 1.0.

## Submission Format

**For every author in the dataset**, submission files should contain two columns: `Id` and `Class`. An example result file is provided.","# Dataset Description

The dataset includes training data x and y so that your classifier f should produce the class number y=f(x). Test data is similar and your classifier is evaluated by the Kaggle server.

## Files

*   **training_x.dat** - the training images
*   **training_y.dat** - the training class ids
*   **validation_x.dat** - the test images

## Columns

The classes for the validation images must be returned in the CSV format including two columns:

*   `image_id` - corresponds to the index starting from 1 in validation_x.dat 
*  `classnum`?- number of the class 1-1000 (one of those in training_y.dat)",True,True
5474,mercatorocean,"# Description

Oceanic Eddies are  vortices of the order of ~10km in horizontal scale, whose signature is clearly visible in satellite products. Sea Level Anomalies (SLA) derived from altimetry is used for detecting such object (using numerical techniques), yet the horizontal and spatial resolution of the product limits the accuracy of detection. Eddy signature is also visible in other satellite-derived product such as Sea surface Temperature (SST) and Ocean Colour (OC) that do not suffer from such limitation (except for cloud coverage).

The objective here is to develop a Deep Learning approach to identify eddies using SST and OC. For this students will have a labelled eddy dataset consisting of  SLA images (which have been used for the detection), and the corresponding SST and OC images wherein the eddy signature is also present.
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is Category Accuracy.

## Submission Format

Submission files should contain two columns: id (date of an observation) and the category identified with your model.

Id : YYYYMMDD_xxx
Category : 0, 1, or 2","# Dataset Description

We provide SST, SLA and OC images in .pkl and .png formats. The recordings are spread over 60 days. Each image covers different regions. The folder images_pkl contains folders identified by a date. Each date folder contains 17 files with images, eddy center and contour coordinates, as well as coordinates of the studied zone and the label. In the images_png you can find images in .png format. There are also images provided under .png format, divided in train and test datasets.

You have to train your neural network to detect the eddy from the SST and/or Ocean Color images. There are three labels - 0 for no eddy image, 1 for anticyclonic eddy et 2 for cyclonic eddy. 
If the first task is done (classification), you could try more difficult application case  - you can detect not only the eddy type, but also it's boundaries and center. 

## Files

*   **TRAIN_png** - the training set with .png data
*   **TEST_png** - the test set with .png data
*   **LABLES_TRAIN** - labels for training set
*   **LABLES_TEST** - labels for test set
*   **train_images_pkl** - the training set with .pkl data
*   **test_images_pkl** - the test set with .pkl data",True,True
