# EN Description

In the first homework, we have learned how to implement a basic recurrent model for text classification. In this homework, we will further explore this direction, and try to develop more advanced models for text classification.

Towards this goal, we created this Kaggle in-class competition where the aim is to predict the category of scientific research papers. There are 20 labels, each given a numerical id. The labels correspond to categorisation into different subject areas. For each paper you are given an id, the title, and the abstract.

# Pipeline
You can do whatever you want on the training data (train.csv), and then make the predictions for the test data (test.csv). See the sample submission ('sample.csv') for what your submission should look like.

# More Hints on Training Techniques
You can practice all the techniques you learned in class. Other strategies you can use to improve the performance:
<li> Split your training data into train, validation, and test sections so that you can evaluate your model before testing it on test.csv with Kaggle.
<li> Data augmentation.
<li> Cross-validation and hyperparameter tuning.
<li> Learning rate decay.
<li> Early stopping on the validation set.
<li> Model ensemble.
