5526,wild-v2,"# Description

Wi-Fi devices are all around us, not just in smartphones but other IoT devices like smart-watches, smart-home appliances or smart sensors. With these Wi-Fi-connected devices increasing by the day, it becomes cumbersome to keep a tab on all of them manually, and GPS is not accurate enough to keep track of these devices indoors. Due to this widespread deployment of Wi-Fi and lack of GPS indoors, Wi-Fi-based localization for Indoor appliances becomes a necessity and requirement.

Current localization algorithms are tested on either too sparse data or use large datasets to fingerprint most of the locations. Furthermore, each algorithm is tested against its own data which makes coming up with a baseline really hard. This has further led to minimal to no deployment of about two decades of Wi-Fi localization into real-world systems. On the other hand, your smartphones are already using high-end computer vision algorithms that have been developed only in the last decade, all due to open-source competitions like ImageNet.

To that extent, we are open-sourcing our datasets and are discussing the importance of data open-sourcing for benchmarking, ease-of algorithm development, and reproducibility in the upcoming  1st International Wireless AI Perception ([WAIP](http://wcsng.ucsd.edu/waip/)) Workshop at [CVPR 2022](https://cvpr2022.thecvf.com/). Finally, the submission for this competition can also submit their papers for another International Workshop, details of which are available on [Workshop Submission](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/wild-v2/overview/workshop-submission) page.

## Dataset Description

This is the first iteration of the Wi-Fi-based Indoor Localization Dataset competition, where we provide you with labeled training data collected across two similar-looking environments of size \\( 40m \times 20m \\) each and has 6 WiFi access points deployed in each of them. 

In this competition we are planning to test the strength of Wi-Fi-based localization models and see their generalization across two specific aspects:
+ \\(G1\\) To see if within a given environment the algorithm developed using the training data can generalize to the test data in the region that has no representative data in the training set.
+ \\(G2\\) To see if given data from the 1st environment and a few sample data points from a different environment, can the algorithm be generalized through few-shot learning.

### Environment-1

We have collected data in Environment-1 whose map, the path taken for the training data and the approximate positions of the 6 Wi-Fi access points have been marked.
![4th Floor](http://drive.google.com/uc?export=view&amp;id=1PO5FV5dqsAn9oS8UGMIlb2a3RYWwuV-2)
We have collected about 28.8k data instances in this environment, amongst which 5.7k datapoints' path has not been displayed above and that is used for private leaderboard testing to test \\( G1 \\) generalization described above and are provided as `testing_data_env1-2.h5`

On the other hand, we provide randomly sampled 75% of the rest of the robot's path locations shown above are used for training and are provided as `training_data_env1.h5` and use the rest 25% for providing the public leaderboard test dataset provided as `testing_data_env1-1.h5` that can be monitored by the teams submitting their algorithms.

### Environment-2
Additionally, we have collected data in Environment-2 whose map, the path taken for the training data and the approximate positions of the 6 Wi-Fi access points have been marked similar to Environment-1
![5th Floor](http://drive.google.com/uc?export=view&amp;id=1lYEWlj7g7NjbM1hE5vM2wx1uALCOEC2i)
In this environment, we have collected 20k Wi-Fi data instances. Among this data, we provide one simple path of 5k data points that is u used to map the environment as sample data of this environment in `training_data_env2.h5`.
The rest of the 15k data points are provided in `testing_data_env2-1.h5` as test data for a private leaderboard which is used to test the generalizability \\(G2\\) as described above if the algorithm can generalize across environments with few-shot learning.

## Deployable Localization algorithms

Thinking about the above few-shot learning, it is important to note that while we need most of the data in any given environment to generalize for location estimates in that environment. With a few-host learning approach, we can utilize the data from the robot traversal that is utilized only for the mapping of the environment which never almost covers most of the space but only fewer labeled locations compared o Environment-1. This would make one's algorithm generalizable and deployable across multiple environments with minimal data requirements.

## Baseline

To provide a basic baseline for these results we provide the results across both the public and private leaderboard datasets tested using our existing <a href=""https://wcsng.ucsd.edu/dloc/"" target=""_blank"">DLoc</a> implementation

## Benchmark Results (Coming Soon)
Using our DLoc implementation, an example of which we provide as a sample submission and a [code example](https://www.kaggle.com/code/bharadwajkaggle/notebook246dfd408f) is used to get location predictions for the above datasets, which we provide as our benchmark results.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are scored on the mean of the 50th and 95th percentile distance errors. For every Floor data and at every LocationID, the horizontal distance (in meters) is computed between the predicted latitude/longitude and the ground truth latitude/longitude. 
Please use the following conversion formula to convert your  \\( X, Y \\) predictions to latitude/longitude predictions.

\\[ \{latitude, longitude\} = \{X, Y\} \times \frac{180}{\pi \times 6371000} \\]


These distance errors form a distribution from which the 50th and 95th percentile errors are calculated (i.e. the 95th percentile error is the value, in meters, for which 95% of the distance errors are smaller). The 50th and 95th percentile errors are then averaged for each Environment (if only one environment exists, only that is used). 
Lastly, the mean of these averaged values is calculated across all Environments in the test set.

Submission File
For each Environment and LocationID in the test set, you must predict the latitude and longitude (use the formula to convert XY meters to latitude and longitude predictions). You must provide estimations for all `LocationID`s. The sample submission contains a list of these `LocationID`s. The submission file should contain a header and have the following format:

## Submission Format

**For every Location in the dataset**, submission files should contain **four** columns: `Environment`, `LocationId`, `X`, and `Y`. `LocationId` should be an integer value assigned to it in the dataset provided. `Environment` is **&lt;&gt;** (empty) for all the datasets with **env1** in their name and **floor5** for all the datasets with **env2** in their name.

Every `LocationId` should have corresponding to its raw data a location prediction provided as two real numbers `X` and `Y`
 ### Note: The X and Y locations in the CSV file should correspond to the latitude and the longitude measurements as stated above.


The file should contain a header and have the following format:
```
Environment,LocationID,X,Y
5floor,1,1.2,3.4
```
----------------------
# Datasets

There are 5 different `.h5` files provided to the users: 
(A quick overview on how to use `.h5` can be found here [here](https://docs.h5py.org/en/stable/quick.html))

### Training Data to learn about Env-1
- **training\_data\_env1.h5**: It is a 75% subset of the path taken by the robot shown in blue in the image below:
![4th Floor](http://drive.google.com/uc?export=view&amp;id=1PO5FV5dqsAn9oS8UGMIlb2a3RYWwuV-2)

### Testing Data to evaluate the model in Env-1
- **testing\_data\_env1-1.h5**: The rest of the 25% data from the path shown above. This data is used for *Public Leaderboard*. 
- **testing\_data\_env1-2.h5**: There is some of the robot's traveled path that is not displayed in the image above. This is the data used for testing space generalization or \\( G1 \\). This data is used for *Private Leaderboard*
- *Note: Please use the LocationID provided within this file to label your* `X, Y` *predictions*

### Fine-Tuning Data for Env-2
-  **training\_data\_env2.h5**: A 20% sample of the data collected in Environment-2 shown below. The data is provided to enable re-training or fine-tuning or transfer-learning or few-shot learning purposes.
![5th Floor](http://drive.google.com/uc?export=view&amp;id=1lYEWlj7g7NjbM1hE5vM2wx1uALCOEC2i)

### Testing Data to evaluate the model in Env-2
- **testing\_data\_env2-1.h5**: The rest of the data collected in Env-2 that is used to evaluate the generalization of the model trained in Env-1 to be evaluated over Env-2, i.e., to verify \\( G2 \\). This data is used for *Private Leaderboard*
- *Note: Please use the LocationID provided within this file to label your* `X, Y` *predictions*

The metadata for the training and testing datasets described above is as follows:

