5539,mff-data-science-2-hw3,"# Description

## Task
In this assignment we have data about bank's clients' characteristics and for some of them (train dataset) also information if they have defaulted, i.e. have become unable to pay their loans, in some period in time. For test dataset, we lack this information and our task is to predict the probability that they default (measured over the same time period). We use tree boosting ensemble technique described below.

Note that the same dataset was used for HW1.

Denote TARGET the binary variable encoding information about default. Implement model for prediction of TARGET, *as a probability*, using xgboost.XGBClassifier model with hyperparameters optimalization.

More specifically,

* build a pipeline that will consist of two steps: mean target encoding of categorical parameters;?training of xgboost.XGBClassifier
* use sklearn.model_selection.HalvingRandomSearchCV with parameters of your choice to perform search of best parameters based on cross-validation score for both of the pipeline steps: optimize parameter **smoothing** for mean target encoding and some of the parameters for xgboost training as well

You can use the notebook from practicals

https://github.com/wenceslasdk/data-science-2/blob/main/Practicals/06_hyperparameter_optimization/hyperparameters_optimization.ipynb

as a source of some ideas.

## Expected outcomes

Until the competition deadline you are supposed to
*?Submit solution that exceeds benchmark solution (to be found in Leaderboard section)
* Send either .py or .ipynb file with working (and reasonably readable and commented) code that leads to the predicton to tybl@karlin.mff.cuni.cz. Send the script zipped and named as DS2\_22\_HW3\_XXX.zip, where XXX is the name of your team in this Kaggle competition. Use the same naming convention for the script and for the mail subject. Clearly indicate your names in the body of the mail.

After the competition deadline, the script of submissions that have passed the benchmark threshold will be revised. It may happen that you will be asked to correct some mistakes and/or answer some questions in some given period. Only after that the submission is considered is accepted.

## Further notes
* Keep in mind that xgboost.XGBClassifier can handle missing data
*?The class sklearn.model_selection.HalvingRandomSearchCV accepts only certain naming convention for parameters subject to optimization
* The class category_encoders.target_encoder.TargetEncoder can perform mean target encoding for you and is compatible with sklearn.pipeline.Pipeline
----------------------
# Evaluation

## Metric

The evaluation metric for this competition is [AUC](https://towardsdatascience.com/understanding-auc-roc-curve-68b2303cc9c5). The AUC score, commonly used in credit risk tasks, measures performance of models for prediction of binary targets.

You shall use sklearn package to evaluate the metric on your train dataset. See https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/generated/sklearn.metrics.roc_auc_score.html

## Submission Format

Submission csv should contain two columns: 
* **SK\_ID\_CURR** - column ID for the observation
* **prediction** - column consisting of the predicted value

Submission was tested using comma separated csv format.","# Dataset Description

We use the same dataset as for HW1.

## Files

*   **hw3_devsample.csv** - the train set
*   **hw3_outofsample.csv** - the test set (without target)
*   **hw3_columns_description** - short description of columns in the above files?

### Train set description

Consists of 80000 observations with 197 columns. Binary target, both categorical and continuous predictors.

*  **Index column name** - 'SK_ID_CURR'
*  **Target column name** - 'TARGET'
*  **Technical columns** - 'TIME', 'BASE',  'DAY', 'MONTH'
*  **Predictor columns** - all the remaining

### Test set description

Consists of 20000 observations with 196 columns.

The same structure as for train set, only target is missing.",True,True
