5553,ml-i3-mini-project-21-22,"# Description

## Goals

In this mini-project, you will apply machine learning algorithms on a specific dataset and try to determine the best solution. The goal is to understand the steps required when you want to recognize classes from a dataset with the best possible accuracy using machine learning algorithms.

A specific dataset is provided. This dataset contains data related to house/apartment price prediction in Switzerland. The dataset contains 6828 samples. Every sample is composed of 30 features. The goal is to determine the price of a house/apartment. 
You will create your classifier on this dataset and then score it on a test set of 1707 samples.
For this project, you must team up with 2 or 3 students only. 

The dataset is provided on tab ¡°Data¡±:
-	¡°train.csv¡±: contains the 6828 labeled samples
-	¡°test.csv¡±: contains the 1707 unlabeled samples

## Tasks

1)	Data Exploration:
You will explore this dataset in different ways. First, let¡¯s understand the data and the distribution of the different features using scatter plots or other tools. What can you tell about the data? Do you see correlations between features, between features and output? 

Hints:
a.	Visualize and understand the dataset
b.	Are there missing values among the samples?
c.	Do a feature need normalization?
d.	Are all features relevant?
e.	Can you combine features, modify them, or create new ones?
f.	Any correlation? 
g.	How data are distributed in each class?
h.	Any outliers?


2)	Classification: 
The objective is to create a classifier on the provided dataset. The goal of this classifier is to guess the price of a house/apartment in Switzerland. It should distinguish between the two following classes (LOW=Price less or equal to median of all prices and HIGH=price more than median to all prices). At the end of the project. Your goal is to train the classifier and test it with ¡°test.csv¡± that will obtain the best F1-score on the test set. Do not use the feature ¡°obj_price¡± in the classification process!
Hints:
a.	What about cross-validation?
b.	Compute different metrics to evaluate you model, it will help you understand how to improve the model.
c.	Test different algorithms 
d.	Tune the hyperparameters
e.	Analyze well the results (confusion matrix, different metrics)

3)	Optional: Regression
The goal is now to estimate the real price of each apartment/house of the dataset. Instead of using the label you created (LOW vs. HIGH), now use the feature ¡°obj_price¡± as target to predict.

All the information about the dataset and the competition are described on the competition pages. See you there!

Advice: Build your pipeline, submit your result, and only then tune your model.

## Technologies

Regarding the technologies, you¡¯re pretty much free to use whatever you want. But keep this in mind: 
- You can use the technologies seen during the course (Scikit-learn with Jupyter)
- You can also use another tool or libraries. (It helps you to get a Bonus)

Regarding the classifiers, you have to use a classifier that is part of the course (k-NN, Na?ve Bayes, Decision Tree/Random Forest). But you can also experiment with an additional classifier (not part of the course) like Neural Networks or SVM for example. If you do so, you have to explain how it works, and you¡¯ll be given a bonus of 0.1 to the mini-project grade.

## Rules

A complete report presenting your work and your results must be delivered on Moodle. 
You should have done a minimum of one successful submission on the competition page. You can submit a maximum of 3 times on Kaggle. 
The 3 best scores on Kaggle will be rewarded a bonus on the grade:
- +0.3 for the first group
- +0.2 for the second group
- +0.1 for the third group

Be sure to follow these guidelines:
- The report must contain the explanations of the steps you made to achieve the goal. Please add figures or screenshots of your results with the relevant metrics to illustrate what you explain.
- We will reproduce your results. So, if you used a Jupyter notebook, zip it with your report. The same applies to other type of code files. You can also give us a link to a Git repo.
- The report must be a PDF file.

Note: Cheating implies a grade of 0, as usual.

Enjoy!

----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is [Mean F1-Score](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/F-score). The F1 score, commonly used in information retrieval, measures accuracy using the statistics precision \( \text{p} \) and recall \( \text{r} \).

Precision is the ratio of true positives \(\text{tp}\) to all predicted positives \(\text{tp} + \text{fp}\). Recall is the ratio of true positives \(\text{tp}\) to all actual positives \(\text{tp} + \text{fn}\). The F1 score is given by:

\[ \text{F1} = 2\frac{\text{p} \cdot \text{r}}{\text{p}+\text{r}}\ \ \mathrm{where}\ \ \text{p} = \frac{\text{tp}}{\text{tp}+\text{fp}},\ \ \text{r} = \frac{\text{tp}}{\text{tp}+\text{fn}} \]

The F1 metric weights recall and precision equally, and a good retrieval algorithm will maximize both precision and recall simultaneously. Thus, moderately good performance on both will be favored over extremely good performance on one and poor performance on the other.

## Submission Format


The file should contain a header and have the following format:
```
Id,predicted_class
6377,1
```

Id = Id of the property contained in test set
predicted_class = the class predicted by the algorithm you train on the training set (0 or 1)

**0 : LOW = Price less or equal to median of all prices**
**1 : HIGH = Price more than median of all prices**","# Dataset Description

This page describes?what files have been provided and?the format of each. You also have details on features contained in the dataset.

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

## Columns

*   `id` - ID of property
*   `obj_add_date`?- date the property was added to the database
*   `obj_main_category` - category of property (apartment or house)
*   `obj_category` - detailed category of property
*   `obj_deal` - type of deal (rent or sale)
*   `obj_postalcode` - postal code of property
*   `obj_city` - city of property
*   `obj_canton - canton of property
*   `obj_price` - price of property (**Use to create the target for classification and remove before training**)
*   `obj_price_hidden` - price hidden from the ad (0 = No, 1 = yes)
*   `obj_building_year` -  year of construction of the property
*   `obj_restoration_year` - year of renovation of the property
*   `obj_bathrooms` - number of bathrooms in property
*   `obj_rooms` - number of rooms in property
*   `obj_bedrooms` - number of bedrooms in property
*   `obj_volume` - volume of property
*   `obj_standing` - standing of property (simple, standard, luxurious)
*   `obj_condition` - condition of property
*   `obj_floor` - floor of property
*   `obj_surface_living` - total living surface of property?
*   `obj_surface_ground` - total ground of garden ?
*   `obj_surface_ground_floor` - 
*   `obj_surface_balcony` - surface of balcony
*   `obj_surface_terrace` - surface of terrace
*   `obj_surface_loggia` - surface of loggia
*   `obj_surface_garden` - surface of garden
*   `obj_lat` - latitude of property
*   `obj_lgn` - longitude of property",True,True
5554,elemeno-sfhacks,"# Description

We challenge you to develop a machine learning model to detect whale movements in blockchain data. The data set contains time-stamped transactions between addresses on the Ethereum blockchain. Transactions are exclusively NFT transfers, and we filtered only top 5 trending NFT smart-contracts. Your task is to identify addresses that are associated with whales, and build a model that predicts the movement of whales over time.

## Context

Whales ?? in the NFT space are entities that hold a large amount of NFTs. These entities can be individuals, companies, or other organizations. Whales can wield a large amount of power in the NFT space due to their holdings. They can use this power to influence the crowds and gain control over aspects of secondary markets.

A whale can influence an NFT secondary market by buying or selling large amounts of NFTs on exchanges. This can cause the price of NFTs on the exchange to rise or fall, depending on the demand for NFTs from the whale.

This challenge does not follow the traditional Kaggle challenge format, in this contest you will be required to adopt an IRL approach. Create your solution as it would be either used by companies or provided by you to an internal team in your company. One possible use-case to keep in mind is that you and your friends have developed a web2 (or 3) app that is available to the masses, and the model you will create will need to be serving inferences for such app. To help you with such task, we want you to use Elemeno ML Ops cloud platform, and by doing so easily deploying a REST API with your end-solution.

The features you will be using on the ML Ops platform are:
 - **Data Source** - This allows you to connect to an external data source and is necessary for configuring the feature store. In this challenge, you will be using the data source type BigQuery. If your solution includes additional data, you're allowed to configure additional data sources as necessary.
 - **Feature Store** - Feature stores are a key part of machine learning, as they allow models to be quickly trained and updated. A feature store is a collection of data that is stored in a way that allows it to be accessed quickly and easily. This data can be used to train machine learning models, and can be updated as new data becomes available.
 - **Inference Service** - You will be able to easily create a REST HTTP API without coding just by using Elemeno ML Ops. In this step you will configure what are the features needed at inference time (input space). You have the option to read features from the feature store or from the request body (using jsonpath). Make sure to specify at least one search key, so that the platform is able to find appropriated data in your feature stores.

## Instructions

### Data
 - There's a sample of the dataset attached in this competition, we recommend that you use the sample just for exploration. Remember, in real-life you will rarely deal with CSV files if you're building a data product.
 - The complete dataset, that should be imported to your Elemeno ML Ops account is publicly available on Big Query. The dataset name is `elemeno-public.sfhacks.eth_nft_transactions`

### Challenge

You're required to create a model that's able to predict the transactions of an account classified as whale. Your model should be able to predict transactions for one day in the future, you're not required to predict for less or more than one day. There's at least one feature that you will need to calculate: the label to classify if a transaction was made by a whale or not. To restrict the solutions, we define whale in this competition as: any account (`to_address`) which the aggregate value of `transaction_value` for a given `token_address` lays within the 75th percentile of the group. So, as a suggestion, we recommend that you group transactions by `token_address` and `to_address` and calculate the percentiles of the sum of `transaction_value`, once you know where the 75th percentile starts for each group you will know how to determine if an account is a whale or not.

The required deliverable is:
 - A CSV file with your predictions for one day after the most recent day in the dataset. We will be calculating the **RMSE** (Root Mean Square Error) from your predicted values and the truth value that are expected. The file should have two columns, one column with the title `Id` which should contain the string concatenation of the `token_address` and `to_address`, and a column with the title `Predicted` with your prediction in ETH scale (tip: when training your model instead of using `transaction_value`, you should use `transaction_value/1e18`).

To score additional points:
- A feature store configured on Elemeno ML Ops, that ingests data from BigQuery.
- A REST HTTP API with your model, deployed on Elemeno ML Ops inference service.

### Criteria

In this challenge we will not evaluate the code quality. Winners will be evaluated by:
 - Best RMSE (less is better, tends to 0)
 - The ability of your model to generalize to a new set of data not used during training
 - The usage of Elemeno ML Ops feature store
 - The usage of Elemeno ML Ops inference service deployment.

## Additional information

If you are using our public dataset on BigQuery through Elemeno ML Ops you will need a service account in order to configure your data source. Use this file: [https://storage.googleapis.com/sfhacks-elemeno/elemeno-public-37205800cc93.json](https://storage.googleapis.com/sfhacks-elemeno/elemeno-public-37205800cc93.json)

If you need a more in-depth example on how to use Elemeno ML Ops tool, refer to this tutorial:
[https://www.kaggle.com/lucasbonattomiguel/model-deployed-as-rest-api-using-feature-store](https://www.kaggle.com/lucasbonattomiguel/model-deployed-as-rest-api-using-feature-store)


----------------------
# Evaluation

We will be calculating the **RMSE** (Root Mean Squared Error) from your predicted values and the truth value that are expected. The file should have two columns, one column with the title **Id** which should contain the string concatenation of the token\_address and to\_address, and a column with the title Predicted with your prediction in ETH scale (tip: when training your model instead of using transaction\_value, you should use transaction\_value/1e18).

## Submission Format

You will be submitting a single file. The file should have two columns: `Id` and `Predicted`. `Id` should be the concatenation of the fields `token_address` and `to_address` (the address of the whale wallet). The column `Predicted` is your model's prediction for what would be the `transaction_value` of the given whale in the given token for one day after the last day in the dataset.


The file should contain a header and have the following format:
```
Id,Predicted
0xbc4ca0eda7647a8ab7c2061c2e118a18a936f13d0x5bd8eff5967b1f1812fda2d5e5802d28d1cd793c,74.69
```","# Dataset Description

We provide a sample of the dataset attached in this competition, we recommend that you use the sample just for exploration. 

Remember, in real-life you will rarely deal with CSV files if you're building a data product. 

The complete dataset, that should be imported to your Elemeno ML Ops account is publicly available on Big Query. The dataset name is `elemeno-public.sfhacks.eth_nft_transactions`

## Files

*   **transactions_sample.csv** - the sample dataset
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

## Columns

* token_address	| STRING | The ERC721 smart contract address
* from_address	| STRING	| The originating Ethereum wallet address
* to_address	| STRING	| The destination Ethereum wallet address	
* transaction_value	| NUMERIC	| The transaction value in WEI (101?) 	
* transaction_hash | STRING | The blockchain transaction hash
* block_timestamp	| TIMESTAMP	| The timestamp of when the transaction was processed	
* block_number	| INTEGER	| The block number of the transaction	
* gas	| INTEGER	| The amount of gas paid for the transaction in WEI	
* gas_price	| INTEGER	| The price of the gas at the moment of the transaction in WEI	
* transaction_type	| INTEGER 	| The type of transaction",True,True
5555,corrected-bike-sharing-regression-otm-714-2022,"# Description

<p>This assignment will take the form of an in-class competition to forecast demand for bikes in a bike-sharing system. You are provided hourly rental data spanning two years. For this competition, the training set is comprised of the first 13 days of each month, while the test set is the 14th through the 19th of the month. You must predict the total count of bikes rented during each hour covered by the test set. Your forecasts will be assessed based on RMSE.</p>

Here is a handy link to Kaggle's [competition documentation](https://www.kaggle.com/docs/competitions), which includes, among other things, instructions on [submitting predictions](https://www.kaggle.com/docs/competitions#making-a-submission).


----------------------
# Evaluation

<h2>Evaluation Metric</h2>
<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is root mean squared error (RMSE) of the submitted forecasts.

</p><h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p>For every date and time in the test set (i.e., for the 14th of the month to the 19th of the month for both 2011 and 2012, and for every hour of the day), submission files should be CSV files containing two columns (with headers):  datetime and count. A sample submission file is provided with the data set.</p>","# Dataset Description

<p>You will be provided with three data files. The first is a training set, which gives usage data for the first 13 days of each month over 2 years, broken down by hour and by type of user. The second is a test set, which gives all of the ""X"" variable data for days 14 through 19 of each month, but does not include actual bike usage. You will try to forecast usage for these days/times. The third is a sample submission file that you can use as a template for submitting your forecasts.

<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>bike sharing training for students.csv</strong> - the training set</li>
<li><strong>bike sharing test for students.csv</strong> - the test set</li>
<li><strong>bike sharing sample submission.csv</strong> - a sample submission file in the correct format</li>
</ul>

<h2>Data Description</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>datetime</strong> - hourly date + timestamp</li>  
<li><strong>season</strong> -  1 = spring, 2 = summer, 3 = fall, 4 = winter</li> 
<li><strong>holiday</strong> - whether the day is considered a holiday</li>
<li><strong>workingday</strong> - whether the day is neither a weekend nor holiday</li>
<li><strong>weather - </li>
<p>   1: Clear, Few clouds, Partly cloudy, Partly cloudy</p> 
<p>   2: Mist + Cloudy, Mistt + Broken clouds, Mist + Few clouds, Mist</p> 
<p>   3: Light Snow, Light Rain + Thunderstorm + Scattered clouds, Light Rain + Scattered clouds</p>
<p>   4: Heavy Rain + Ice Pallets + Thunderstorm + Mist, Snow + Fog</p> 
<li><strong>temp</strong> - temperature in Celsius</li>
<li><strong>atemp</strong> - ""feels like"" temperature in Celsius</li>
<li><strong>humidity</strong> - relative humidity</li>
<li><strong>windspeed</strong> - wind speed</li>
<li><strong>casual</strong> - number of non-registered user rentals initiated</li>
<li><strong>registered</strong> - number of registered user rentals initiated</li>
<li><strong>count</strong> - number of total rentals</li>
</ul>

<h2>Acknowledgements</h2>
<p>This data set was part of an earlier public Kaggle competition.</p>",True,True
