5560,heart-disease-prediction-competition,"# Description

This dataset is from IntelliMed healthcare analytics vendor with medical decision support systems in several major hospitals across Victoria. Their major focus is on enhancing patient care by assisting medical practitioners with clinical insights using data analytics. They have a need to develop a feature into their platform for prediction of cardiac arrest, one of the leading causes of death in Australia. 

Assume you are a data analytics professional in the IntelliMed analytics team, and you have been asked to develop a machine learning model to predict the likelihood of a heart attack.
Your analysis should adhere to the data analytics lifecycle and is expected to focus on,

?	Data pre-processing 
?	Data exploration (descriptive analytics) 
?	Feature engineering 
?	Predictive modeling (predictive analytics) 
?	Model Explainability (XAI)

The train dataset provided to you has 734 records, while the remainder 184 records have been kept for evaluation purposes.

----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is Area Under the Curve (AUC). AUC curve is a performance measurement for classification problem at various thresholds settings. AUC represents degree or measure of separability. It tells how much model is capable of distinguishing between classes. Higher the AUC, better the model is at predicting 0s as 0s and 1s as 1s. By analogy, Higher the AUC, better the model is at distinguishing between patients with disease and no disease.

## Submission Format

**For every author in the dataset**, submission files should contain two columns: `Id` and `HeartDisease`. A sample submission file is available in data section.


The file should contain a header and have the following format:
```
Id,HeartDisease
PT1802,1
```","# Dataset Description

The train dataset provided to you has 784 records, while the remainder 184 records is used for evaluation purposes.

## Files

*   **train_dataset.csv** - the training set
*   **test_dataset.csv** - the test set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format
*   **DataDescription.csv** - supplemental information about the data",True,True
5561,pvelad,"# Description

# Introduction
The anomaly detection in photovoltaic (PV) cell electroluminescence (EL) image is of great significance for the vision-based fault diagnosis. Many researchers are committed to solving this problem, but a large-scale open-world dataset is required to validate their novel ideas. We build a PV EL Anomaly Detection (PVEL-AD) dataset for polycrystalline solar cell, which contains 36,543 near-infrared images with various internal defects and heterogeneous background. This dataset contains 1 class anomaly-free images and anomalous images with 12 different categories. Moreover, **40358 ground truth bounding boxes are provided for 12 types of defects**. We also carry out a comprehensive evaluation of the state-of-the-art object detection methods based on deep learning. The evaluation results on this dataset provide the initial benchmark, which is convenient for follow-up researchers to conduct experimental comparisons. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first public dataset for PV solar cell anomaly detection that provides box-wise ground truth and focuses on industrial application. Furthermore, this dataset can also be used for the evaluation of many computer vision tasks such as few-shot detection, one-class classification and anomaly generation. [**Competition dataset download**](https://github.com/binyisu/PVEL-AD)

----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated by computing mean Average Precision (mAP), modified to take into account the annotation process of PVELAD dataset (mean is taken over per-class APs). The metric is described on the  [**website**](https://github.com/binyisu/PVEL-AD).

The final mAP is computed as the average AP over the 8 classes. The participants will be ranked on this final metric.

The metric is implemented as a part of Tensorflow Object Detection API. See this Tutorial on running the evaluation in Python.

**# Submission File**
For each image in the test set, you must predict a list of boxes describing objects in the image. Each box is described as

The file should contain a header and have the following format:
```
ImageID,PredictionString
ImageID,{Label Confidence XMin YMin XMax YMax} {...}
```","# Dataset Description

The train and test sets of images and their ground truth (bounding boxes and labels) should be downloaded from **[here](https://github.com/binyisu/PVEL-AD)**. 

# File descriptions
- images.zip - parts of test set
- sample_submission.csv - a sample submission file in the correct format",True,True
5562,ieee-bigdata-cup-2022-destination-prediction,"# Description

## Background
Destination prediction plays a crucial role in a wide range of applications, including city governance, emergency regulation, and location-based services. It is the key to understanding human mobility and supporting policymaking from travel demand estimation, traffic congestion, and transit planning. To answer these questions, agent-based models are widely applied to simulate human mobility at the citywide level. However, present studies are facing two major limitations. First,  the modeling paradigm involves observed partial trajectories or historical visited locations for training and prediction. Even though recent location-based services enable communication, navigation, and smartphone application companies to collect human mobility data, it is still challenging to apply existing models to the population without historical mobility data. Second, the differences in city topologies and functions prevent us to transfer the trained models from city to city.

Therefore, the objective of this competition is two-fold. First, using the data collected from multiple metropolitan areas in Japan, you are challenged to develop a robust and generalizable model to predict the individual's daily trip destination based on easy-collected information and features. Second, using the models trained on the data before, predict the trip destination for a new metropolitan area.

## IEEE BigData Cup 2022
This competition is part of the IEEE BigData Cup Challenge at the IEEE International Conference on BigData. A panel will review the top submissions for the competition based on the description of the methods provided. From this, a subset may be invited to present their results at the workshop of the conference. Attending the workshop is not required to participate in the competition; however, only teams that are attending the workshop will be considered to present their work.

You can see a list of all of the IEEE BigData 2022 competitions [here](https://bigdataieee.org/BigData2022/BigDataCupChallenges.html).
----------------------
# Evaluation

## Evaluation metric

Participants are asked to predict the trip destination based on their basic personal attributes (including age, gender, occupation), departure time, and departure location. Submissions are evaluated on the **Categorization Accuracy**.

## Submission Format

**For every trip in the dataset**, submission files should contain two columns: `Tripid` and `Destination`. `Destination` should be the `ZONE ID` corresponding to the shapefile. 


The file should contain a header and have the following format:
```
Trip ID, zone 1, zone 2, ..., zone n
1, 0.001, 0.0041, ..., 0.0002
```
----------------------
# Timeline

* **June 20, 2022,** Start Date.
* **September 30, 2022,** BigData Cup Challenge Participation Deadline. You must accept the competition rules before this date in order to compete.
* **October 30, 2022,**: Announcement of winning teams and invitation for paper submission for the special track during the IEEE BigData 2022 conference.
* **November 10, 2022,**: Deadline for the submission of the papers.
* **November 14, 2022**: Notification of paper acceptance.
* **November 22, 2022,**: Camera-ready version of accepted papers. (Firm deadline)
* **December 17 ¨C December 20, 2022,**: IEEE BigData 2022 Conference, Osaka, Japan.

All deadlines are at 11:59 PM UTC on a corresponding day unless otherwise noted. The competition organizers reserve the right to update the contest timeline if they deem it necessary.
----------------------
# Supplementary 

## Supplementary

The goal of the competition is to predict the trip destination on a citywide level. In order to make the solution has more widespread application in the real-world scenario, the problem setting is different from the most existing approaches, particularly with regard to the following:
* The destination prediction is mainly based on the traveler's personal attributes (i.e., age, gender, occupation), trip occupation, departure time, and departure location. Traveler's historical mobility profile and ongoing partial trajectories are not available on this task. 
* Due to privacy concerns, the dataset uses zones as basic spatial units to avoid the reproduction of significant locations (i.e., home location, office location). Each zone represents an area containing a similar population. 
*  The training data is provided by four metropolitan areas. Participants are asked to develop a robust and generalizable model to predict the trip destination in two different cities. We explain these details on the Data page.

Besides, freely & publicly available external data is allowed, including pre-trained models.

## Further reading

* Sekimoto, Y., Shibasaki, R., Kanasugi, H., Usui, T., & Shimazaki, Y. (2011). Pflow: Reconstructing people flow recycling large-scale social survey data. IEEE Pervasive Computing, 10(4), 27-35.
* Kashiyama, T., Pang, Y., Sekimoto, Y., & Yabe, T. (2022). Pseudo-PFLOW: Development of nationwide synthetic open dataset for people movement based on limited travel survey and open statistical data. arXiv preprint arXiv:2205.00657.
