# Join The IEEE Big Data Challenge

- After the competition phase is completed, an invitation for submitting the accompanying academic paper (4-10 pages) will be provided to the top 10 participants as ranked by the leaderboard. Accepted papers will be invited to give talks at the IEEE BigData 2022 on December 17-20, 2022 in Osaka, Japan. 
- Only teams that are attending the workshop will be considered to present their work and got the prize.
- Three teams will be selected as the winners based on the results and submitted paper of the challenge and awarded the following prizes:
   - 1st Place $ 1,000
   - 2nd Place $ 500
   - 3rd Place $ 200
- The papers are expected to conform to the IEEE 2-column format set by the conference, which can be found at [Big Data CFP](https://www.ieee.org/conferences/publishing/templates.html). Paper be submitted to the following link: https://wi-lab.com/cyberchair/2022/bigdata22/scripts/ws_submit.php?subarea=SC
- Peer reviewers will review the academic papers.
- Important Dates
   - October 30, 2022: Announcement of winning teams and invitation for paper submission for the special track during the IEEE BigData 2022 conference.
   - November 10, 2022: Deadline for the submission of the papers.
   - November 14, 2022: Notification of paper acceptance.
   - November 22, 2022: Camera-ready version of accepted papers. (Firm deadline)
   - December 17 ¨C December 20, 2022: IEEE BigData 2022 Conference, Osaka, Japan.


If you have questions, please feel free to contact the lead organizer pybdtc@iis.u-tokyo.ac.jp.","# Dataset Description

This competition provides *[People Flow dataset](https://pflow.csis.u-tokyo.ac.jp/data-provision-service/about-people-flow-data/)* which was processed based on Person Trip Survey Data (PT Data) collected by a country or a local authority of each area.  These data sources are used with the consent of the Minister of Land, Infrastructure, Transport and Tourism, the Metropolitan Area Traffic Planning Association of each area in Japan. 

Each record in this dataset represents a daily trip in the metropolitan area and concludes the traveler's person ID, demographic attributes (i.e. age, gender, occupation), origin, and destination.  Due to privacy concerns, the dataset uses zones as basic spatial units to avoid the reproduction of sensitive significant locations (i.e., home location, office location). The cartographic representation of zones is provided by a [shapefile](https://doc.arcgis.com/en/arcgis-online/reference/shapefiles.htm). Further, we also provide an open data-based dataset to represent each zone including population, # of employees (secondary industry), # of employees (tertiary industry), # of offices (secondary industry), and # of# of offices (tertiary industry). Besides, freely & publicly available external data is encouraged.

## Train set
The train set is meant to be for model development. The train set is composed of 4 metropolitan areas' subsets as follows
* **Tokyo metropolitan area** is the most populous metropolitan area in Japan including Tokyo Metropolis and the prefectures of Chiba Ibaraki, Kanagawa, and Saitama. The dataset consists of 470,140 travelers and 790,613 trips.

* **Chukyo metropolitan area** is a major metropolitan area in Japan that is centered in the city of Nagoya in Aichi Prefecture. The dataset consists of 135,910 travelers and 205,435 trips. 

* **Kyushu metropolitan area** is the region comprising the metropolitan areas of the cities of Fukuoka and North Kyushu Prefecture. This dataset consists of 147,968 travelers and 343,752 trips.

* **HIgashisurugawan metropolitan area**  is a city cluster located in the North of Shizuoka Prefecture. This dataset consists of 21,968 travelers and 34,496 trips.

## Test set
The test set is used for evaluating the model developed from the train set. 

* **Kinki metropolitan area** lies in the southern-central region of Japan's mainland. The region includes the prefectures of Nara, Wakayama, Kyoto, Osaka, Hyogo, and Shiga. This dataset consists of 624,511 travelers and 967,459 trips.

Since all of the train and test sets are collected from different areas, learning strategies such as transfer learning and domain adaptation. 

## Folders

* **zones/** - a folder of shapefiles corresponding to each zone. Each shapefile records zone id and geometry of zone polygon information.
* **zone_features/** - a folder of feature files corresponding to each zone. The features consist of population, # of employees (secondary industry), # of employees (tertiary industry), # of offices (secondary industry), and # of# of offices (tertiary industry) corresponding to the zone id.
* **train/** - a folder of train set files.

## Files

*   **test.csv** - the test set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format
*   **data_specification.csv** - supplemental information about the data",True,True
5563,house-pricing-ml-matfac-0,"# Description

Today you need to predict the house prices in the USA. You can use any model from the course, except Random Forests, Bagging Aggregation, Gradient boosting and any DL models. Use of raw features is not the best idea, but you may create your own ones. Be creative and remember what you were taught!
----------------------
# Evaluation

R2 score is used as metric. Public set is only 30% of overall test

The submission file should contain a header and have the following format:
```
Id,price
1,1000.0
2,9000.0
```
