5590,the-table-fan-dilemma,"# Description

<a href=""https://bpgc-cte.org/""><img src=""https://i.imgur.com/5mvZeDQ.png"" alt=""CTE"" width=""225""/></a> <a href=""https://www.saidl.in//""><img src=""https://i.imgur.com/KdKEwxw.png"" alt=""SAiDL"" width=""225""/></a> <img src=""https://i.imgur.com/QCNpRqY.png"" alt=""GFG"" width=""225""/>
Goa can get extremely hot in the summers. You are tired of the heat and a table fan seems like your only salvation. Little did you know, table fans are not allowed in rooms.
You have hidden the fan safely and only use it only when necessary.
It takes a lot of time to get the fan from where it is hidden. If only you could predict the weather to be more prepared.
<hr /> 
Formally, you are given the dates, maximum temperature, and some other features which may or may not be relevant to the task and your goal is to build a model/solution to find the mean temperature on a particular day.
**NOTE - You are required to first predict the minimum temperature and then compute the mean temperature ( (_max_temp_+_min_temp_)/2 )for the final submission.**

**Head over to ""Rules"" to understand further details**

Authors: Shreyas Bhat(9082080984), SAiDL and Aditya Agrawal (8879459970), SAiDL
Event POC: Yash Khanna, CTE (9958743212)
----------------------
# Evaluation

## Metric
This competition is evaluated on the RMSE of your predictions.
RMSE expects an Id column identifying the row and an Expected column specifying the true value which competitors are attempting to predict. 
## Submission Format



The file should contain a header and have the following format:
```
id, mean_temp
1,1
```","# Dataset Description


## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

## Columns

*   `date` - Date when the temperature was measured
*   `max_temp`?- The maximum temperature measure on that day
*   `min_temp`?- The minimum temperature measure on that day
*   etc.",True,True
