5604,mais-202-winter-2022,"# Description

Each image is 28 pixels in height and 28 pixels in width, for a total of 784 pixels in total. Each pixel has a single pixel-value associated with it, indicating the lightness or darkness of that pixel, with higher numbers meaning darker. This pixel-value is an integer between 0 and 255.

The file `train_images.npy` contains all 50 000 training images.
The file `train_labels.csv` contains all 50 000 training labels.
The file `label_int_to_str_mapping` contains the mapping between integer labels and plain text labels.
The file `test_images.npy` contains all 20 000 testing images. Please use this file to generate your solutions.

To load your npy files use the following code:
```
import numpy as
train_images = np.load(""train_images.npy"")
```
To visualize an image you can use the following code block:
```
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt

def show_image(arr):
    two_d = (np.reshape(arr, (28, 28)) * 255).astype(np.uint8)
    plt.imshow(two_d, interpolation='nearest')
    plt.show()

show_image(train_images[0]) # 0 is the index of the training image you want to display
```

Labels
Each training and test example is assigned to one of the following labels:

0 T-shirt/top
1 Trouser
2 Pullover
3 Dress
4 Coat
5 Sandal
6 Shirt
7 Sneaker
8 Bag
9 Ankle boot

Submission
You can use the following code to submit your predictions on the test set (adapted to your pipeline as necessary):
```
x_test = np.load('test_images.npy')
x_test = x_test.reshape(len(x_test),1,28,28)
y_test = model.predict(x_test)

df_test = pd.read_csv('sample_submission.csv')
df_test['label'] = y_test
df_test.to_csv('submission.csv', index=False)
```
Resources
Feel free to use any online resources you find, this assignment is used to mimic some real life challenges you may run into and also allow you to work in groups with your fellow bootcampees. It is important not to worry about your ranking, take this opportunity to learn



----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is class label accuracy.

## Submission Format

Submission files should contain two columns: ID and labels. Each ID in the submission should be unique and be in increasing order. The IDs must be ordered from lowest to highest. Please see sample_submission.csv as an example.
The file should contain a header and have the following format:
```
ID,label
0, 1
1, 8
¡­, ¡­
```","# Dataset Description

## File descriptions
- train_labels.csv - contains the ground truth labels for all training images, indices correspond to the indices in train_images.npy
- train_images.npy - contains the training images, use np.load to load this file
- test_images.npy - contains all the unlabelled test images, use np.load to load this file
- label_int_to_str_mapping.csv - contains the mapping between integer labels to string labels
## Data fields
- ID - the index of the image in the train or test npy file
- label - the label of the image in the train npy file",True,True
5605,unifesp-fatty-liver,"# Description

## What is the liver?

The liver is a major organ located in the right upper quadrant of the abdomen. It is currently thought to be responsible for up to 500 separate biological functions, such as detoxification, synthesis of proteins and biochemicals necessary for digestion and growth, glycogen storage, production of hormones, among others.

## What about fatty liver?

Hepatic steatosis, or fatty liver, is a medical condition in which there is an increase in intracellular fat in the liver. Some of its most common causes include alcohol abuse, obesity, high cholesterol, and diabetes. As all of these are very common in today's society, studies have found hepatic steatosis affects about 45% of the western population to some degree. Although most patients don't have symptoms and won't have serious problems, in some people it can get worse over time and progress to hepatitis, fibrosis, and eventually cirrhosis. 

## But how do we diagnose it on CT Scans?

On CT, hepatic steatosis can be quite easy to diagnose, especially the moderate to severe cases. Based on the imaging findings, it can be classified into two groups: focal hepatic steatosis and diffuse hepatic steatosis. This competition is about the diffuse type. On non-contrast CT, moderate to severe diffuse steatosis (at least 30% fat fraction) is predicted by:
- relative hypoattenuation: liver attenuation lower than 10 Hounsfield Units (HU) less than that of spleen.
- absolute low attenuation: liver attenuation lower than 40 HU.

## Oh, not that hard... so why create a Machine Learning model to predict this?

Chest CT Scans are usually extended until the upper abdomen, so at least a part of the liver is depicted. Although relatively easy to diagnose, hepatic steatosis can be missed by the reporting radiologist in these studies, especially in mild cases, because the focus is on the chest organs, like the lungs, heart, and thoracic blood vessels and if there is a discrete hypoattenuation, the radiologist won't notice unless it is actively measured.

In this competition, you will be challenged to create an algorithm that predicts whether or not there is hepatic steatosis on a Chest CT Scan, so we can help radiologists who read these studies to increase their diagnostic accuracy for this condition.

Here is a handy link to Kaggle's [competition documentation](https://www.kaggle.com/docs/competitions), which includes, among other things, instructions on [submitting predictions](https://www.kaggle.com/docs/competitions#making-a-submission).

## Acknowledgements
Mariana Scolari, MD, radiology resident at our department, for annotating this dataset. 
Nitamar Abdala, MD, PhD, head of IT discipline at our department, for supporting this work.
Ernandez Rodrigues, our PACS admin.
Jefferson Silva, our IT manager.

## Competition Hosts

Augusto Sarquis Serpa, MD and Felipe Campos Kitamura, MD, PhD.
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is [log loss / binary cross entropy (LL)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cross_entropy).

For each patient which underwent one or multiple CT Scans, you will have to submit a number between 0 (not steatosis) and 1 (steatosis). The ground truth for each one is either 0 or 1 and the closer you get to the ground truth for each patient, the lower your LL will be. 

The LL is calculated by the following expression:

![](https://github.com/kitamura-felipe/competitions/blob/main/logloss.png?raw=true)

Where:
`N` is the number of patients in the testing set.
`y_i` is the ground truth for the ith patient in the testing set.
`p(y_i)` is the prediction between 0 and 1 submitted for that patient.
`log` is the [natural logarithm](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Natural_logarithm). 

Although it looks somewhat complicated, it is simple to understand what the above expression is doing. It is basically averaging the individual losses for each one of the testing examples. If we have a negative testing example, the loss for that particular patient becomes:

$$ LL = -log(1 - p(y)) $$

And the graph for the loss vs. prediction becomes the following:

![](https://github.com/augusto-sarquis-serpa/images/blob/main/ll.png?raw=true)

As you can see, as the prediction approaches 0, the loss approaches 0, and as the prediction gets further away from 0 and closer to 1, the loss approaches infinity.

Now, if we have a positive example, the loss becomes: 

$$ LL = -log(p(y)) $$

And the corresponding graph:

![](https://github.com/augusto-sarquis-serpa/images/blob/main/ll2.png?raw=true)

Again, quite intuitive. As the prediction approaches 1, the loss approaches 0, and as the prediction approaches 0, the loss goes to infinity. Pretty much what you would expect from a loss function.

Now, if we sum the losses for every patient and average them, we get the final metric. The competitor with the lowest metric, wins the competition!

## Submission Format

Submission files should contain one row for each exam in the test set and two columns: `Id` and `Predicted`.

The file should contain a header and have the following format:
```
Id,Predicted
1,0.76
```","# Dataset Description

The dataset for this competition comprises of 152 Chest CT Scans patients (CS) and 112 Abdomen or Abdomen + Chest CT Scans patients (AS). The data is splitted between a traning set and a testing set. The training set contains 226 patients, comrpising of 114 CS and all the 112 AS. Note that we will be using the AS only for training, as we want to test the performance of our model on CS. The testing set comprises of the remaining 38 CS. There is a .zip file which contains all the patient folders inside the directory 'd_2'. The folder's names are the patient Ids. 

Inside each patient folder, there are one or more subfolders, each one corresponding to a different date when the patient underwent a CT Scan. Inside these subfolders, we have another set of one or more subfolders, where each one represents a different slice thickness of the same Scan. Finally, when we open this last set of subfolders, we have our dicom files, each one containing the pixel array of a specific slice.

The dicom files were carefully anonymized, so no private information from the patient is depicted there. The Patient's Name and Patient ID Tags contain the patient Id for the dataset, and the Study Description Tag is either 'Abdomen', if it is a AS patient, or 'Chest', if it is a CS patient.

The last piece of the data is a train.csv file, which contains the ground truth for each patient in the traning set, as annotated by Radiologists. The sample_submission.csv contains only the patients in the testing set . The column 'Id' is the number on the name of the patient folder.

You will submit your predictions in the sample_submission.csv format. Each prediction is a float number between 0 and 1. 

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the ground truth for the training set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format


## Columns

*   `Id` - patient id
*   `ground_truth`?- whether the patient from the training set has (1) steatosis or not (0)
*    `Predicted` - the probability that the patient from the testing set has steatosis",True,True
5606,coms4771-spring-2022-regression-competition,"# Description

Welcome to COMS 4771 Spring 2022 large-scale regression task. Your goal for this assignment is to develop a good quality regressor for the given dataset.

The given dataset comes from a Uber-esque transportation service. It consists of about 400000 rides taken by users. The task is to predict the duration (in seconds) of the ride.

You can use any resource/code/package/toolbox that is publicly available to develop your regressor. No external data sources can be used. Your primary goal for this assignment is to develop a good quality trip-duration predictor. (This of course will be only possible if you employ good Machine Learning practices).
----------------------
# Evaluation

##Evaluation Criteria
You must get a Mean Absolute Error (MAE) score of at most 500 on the private holdout test-dataset to get full credit. If your MAE score is over the simple baseline (see leaderboard), then you will get zero credit. If your MAE score is between the simple baseline and the full credit threshold, then you will be awarded points linearly proportional to your deviation from the full credit threshold. For example, if your MAE is halfway between the simple baseline and full credit threshold, then you will get half credit. Extra points will be awarded to the highest performing students, using the following scoring mechanism:

$$\text{Extra Credit Points}= 5(1?\frac{\text{Your Rank}?1}
{\text{Number of Participants with MAE &lt; 300 }}
)?1[\text{Your MAE}&lt; 300]$$","# Dataset Description

There are three data files: train_examples.csv, train_labels.csv, test_exampes.csv.

- train_examples.csv contains 400,000 training examples, where each example has 11 features
- train_labels.csv contains 400,000 training labels, in the same order as train_examples (and with ids).
- test_examples.csv contains 100,000 training examples.",True,True
