5612,procom22-data-science-competition,"# Description

The shape of bacteria can be classified and identified using number of features. The bacterial can exist in three different shapes: coccus (spherical), bacillus (rod-shaped), and spiral (twisted).

- Cocci are bacteria that are spherical or ovoid in shape. Some cocci remain attached after binary fission, even though separate cells have been formed. For example, diplococci are cocci in pairs, streptococci are chains, and staphylococci are clusters of multiple cocci. Tetrads are square arrangements of four cocci, while sarcinae are cubes of eight cocci.
- Spiral bacteria are, as the name suggests, spiral-shaped. Spirillums are thick, tough spirals. Spirochetes are spirals that are thin and flexible. Vibrios are comma-shaped rods with a small twist.
- Bacilli are rod-shaped bacteria. Like cocci, bacilli can be solitary or arranged together. Diplobacilli are two bacilli arranged next to each other, and streptobacilli are chains of bacilli.

In our problem we are actually focusing on only 2 of them. There are number of features given that can be used to determine what category it belongs to.
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is Mean F Score.

## Submission Format
The file should contain a header and have the following format:
```
ID,Class
324,b
```","# Dataset Description

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

## Predict
You need to predict if the shape is a (Cocci) or b (Spiral)",True,True
5613,machathon-3,"# Description

Welcome to Machathon 3.0; this year we have a very exciting real-world problem. Car plate recognition.

On your first day of work as a Machine Learning Engineer at STP, a client approached you (on Saturday 19th of March 2022) and requested a system that can read car plates to be used in a smart city; however, the client has no data and is unable to collect any. Moreover, they want the project finished within one week only (they are ready to pay any amount of money to make it happen??). 

You learn that the client has approached multiple companies with the offer and the two companies with the most robust systems will end up getting paid (10,000 and 5,000 respectively), so you agreed to the offer.

You decided to gather the data yourself; you were able to gather a team of 20 and managed to collect and label some data (around 1000 images); however, that is not nearly enough, so you decided to generate some images and managed to generate around 5000 images. This process has taken you four days; today is Wednesday 23rd of March 2022. You agreed to deliver the system by Friday 25th of March 2022 and will meet the client on Saturday 26th of March to discuss the details of the system.

Can you finish the system in time with enough accuracy that the client is happy and actually ends up paying you?
----------------------
# Evaluation

## Evaluation Metric
The evaluation metric for this competition is [Levenshtein Distance](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Levenshtein_distance#:~:text=Informally%2C%20the%20Levenshtein%20distance%20between,considered%20this%20distance%20in%201965.). 

For each image, the Levenshtein distance is evaluated and the average of all the images is your score with the best score possible being 0, worse scores are higher than 0.

## Submission Format

You should submit only 1 .csv file that contains the following header the format:
```
img_name,label
00000.jpg,1 2 3 ? ? ?
```

Be careful with the labels since it contains a mix of Arabic and English characters. Make sure that you submit them in the same way/order as the given training set. The order is the same as reading each character one by one from the right to the left with 1 space between each character and no preceding or leading spaces.","# Dataset Description

After spending four days collecting data and creating a generator, you were able to obtain the following files.

## Files

*   **train_images** - the training set of images
*   **test_images** - the testing set of images
*   **train_labels.csv** - the labels for all the images in the training set
*   **train_bbxs** - folder containing bounding boxes on each character for most of the images in the training set (not all of them)

## Columns for train_labels.csv

*   `img_name` - name of the image
*   `label`?- label associated with the image, characters are read one by one from the right to the left, be careful that how they are printed might be different from how they are actually ordered since a mix of English and Arabic characters can be printed differently according to the editor you are using

## Format of training bounding boxes
Each .txt file has the name of the image it is associated with in the training set (no images from the testing set have bounding boxes provided).

Each .txt file contains N rows, where N is the number of characters in that image
Each row contains four values (xmin, ymin, xmax, ymax), these represent the corners of a bounding box on one of the characters",True,True
