5615,foursquare-location-matching,"# Description

When you look for nearby restaurants or plan an errand in an unknown area, you expect relevant, accurate information. To maintain quality data worldwide is a challenge, and one with implications beyond navigation. Businesses make decisions on new sites for market expansion, analyze the competitive landscape, and show relevant ads informed by location data. For these, and many other uses, reliable data is critical.

Large-scale datasets on commercial points-of-interest (POI) can be rich with real-world information. To maintain the highest level of accuracy, the data must be matched and de-duplicated with timely updates from multiple sources. De-duplication involves many challenges, as the raw data can contain noise, unstructured information, and incomplete or inaccurate attributes. A combination of machine-learning algorithms and rigorous human validation methods are optimal to de-dupe datasets.

With 12+ years of experience perfecting such methods, Foursquare is the #1 independent provider of global POI data. The leading independent location technology and data cloud platform, Foursquare is dedicated to building meaningful bridges between digital spaces and physical places. Trusted by leading enterprises like Apple, Microsoft, Samsung, and Uber, Foursquare¡¯s tech stack harnesses the power of places and movement to improve customer experiences and drive better business outcomes.

In this competition, you¡¯ll match POIs together. Using a dataset of over one-and-a-half million Places entries heavily altered to include noise, duplications, extraneous, or incorrect information, you'll produce an algorithm that predicts which Place entries represent the same point-of-interest. Each Place entry includes attributes like the name, street address, and coordinates. Successful submissions will identify matches with the greatest accuracy.

By efficiently and successfully matching POIs, you'll make it easier to identify where new stores or businesses would benefit people the most. 

> **This is a Code Competition. Refer to [Code Requirements](https://www.kaggle.com/c/foursquare-location-matching/overview/code-requirements) for details.**
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated by the mean [Intersection over Union](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jaccard_index) (IoU, aka the Jaccard index) of the ground-truth entry matches and the predicted entry matches. The mean is taken sample-wise, meaning that an IoU score is calculated for each row in the submission file, and the final score is their average.

## Submission File ##

For each place entry `id` in the test set, you should submit a space-delimited list of matching place `id`s. *Places always self-match*, so the list of matches for an `id` should always contain that `id`.

The file should contain a header, be named `submission.csv`, and have the following format:
```
id,matches
E_00001118ad0191,E_00001118ad0191
E_000020eb6fed40,E_000020eb6fed40
E_00002f98667edf,E_00002f98667edf
E_001b6bad66eb98,E_001b6bad66eb98 E_0283d9f61e569d
E_0283d9f61e569d,E_0283d9f61e569d E_001b6bad66eb98
```
You should predict matches for every `id`. For example, if you believe `A` matches `B` and `C`, your submission file should include rows `A,A B C`, but also `B,B A C` and `C,C A B`.
----------------------
# Timeline

* **April 14, 2022** - Start Date.
* **June 30, 2022** - Entry Deadline. You must accept the competition rules before this date in order to compete.
* **June 30, 2022** - Team Merger Deadline. This is the last day participants may join or merge teams.
* **July 7, 2022** - Final Submission Deadline.

All deadlines are at 11:59 PM UTC on the corresponding day unless otherwise noted. The competition organizers reserve the right to update the contest timeline if they deem it necessary.
----------------------
# Prizes

* 1st Place - $12,500
* 2nd Place - $7,500
* 3rd Place - $5,000
----------------------
# Code Requirements

<img src=""https://storage.googleapis.com/kaggle-media/competitions/general/Kerneler-white-desc2_transparent.png"" style=""float: right; width: 70px; padding: 10px"">

### This is a Code Competition

Submissions to this competition must be made through Notebooks. In order for the ""Submit"" button to be active after a commit, the following conditions must be met:

- CPU Notebook <= 9 hours run-time
- GPU Notebook <= 9 hours run-time
- Internet access disabled
- Freely & publicly available external data is allowed, including pre-trained models
- Submission file must be named `submission.csv`

Please see the [Code Competition FAQ](https://www.kaggle.com/docs/competitions#notebooks-only-FAQ) for more information on how to submit. And review the [code debugging doc](https://www.kaggle.com/code-competition-debugging) if you are encountering submission errors.","# Dataset Description

The data presented here comprises over one-and-a-half million place entries for hundreds of thousands of commercial **Points-of-Interest** (POIs) around the globe. Your task is to determine which place entries describe the same point-of-interest. Though the data entries may represent or resemble entries for real places, they may also contain artificial information or additional noise.

## Training Data
- **train.csv** - The training set, comprising eleven attribute fields for over one million place entries, together with:
  - `id` - A unique identifier for each entry.
  - `point_of_interest` - An identifier for the POI the entry represents. There may be one or many entries describing the same POI.  Two entries ""match"" when they describe a common POI.
- **pairs.csv** - A pregenerated set of pairs of place entries from `train.csv` designed to improve detection of matches. You may wish to generate additional pairs to improve your model's ability to discriminate POIs.
  - `match` - Whether (`True` or `False`) the pair of entries describes a common POI.

## Example Test Data
To help you author submission code, we include a few example instances selected from the test set. When you submit your notebook for scoring, this example data will be replaced by the actual test data. The actual test set has approximately 600,000 place entries with POIs that are distinct from the POIs in the training set.

- **test.csv** - A set of place entries with their recorded attribute fields, similar to the training set.
- **sample_submission.csv** - A sample submission file in the correct format.
  - `id` - The unique identifier for a place entry, one for each entry in the test set.
  - `matches` - A space delimited list of IDs for entries in the test set matching the given ID. Place entries always self-match.",True,True
5616,SalesAnalysis,"# Description

The given data contains transaction and household level data for around 21 months. There is a group of 2,500 households who are frequent shoppers at a retail store. For certain households, demographic information as well as direct marketing/ campaign history is included.
Please use EDA to answer below questions. Analysis on any other point/category apart from questions mentioned below is always welcome.

1. How many customers are spending more over the time? Lesser over the time? Describe these customers.

2. Of those customers who are spending more over the time, which categories are growing at a faster rate?

3. Of those customers who are spending lesser over the time, with which categories are they becoming less engaged?

4. Which demographic factors (e.g. household size, presence of children, income) appear to affect customer spend? Engagement with certain categories?

5.  Is there evidence to suggest that direct marketing improves overall engagement?

6. Retail store is planning to send out a campaign for Nutritional Products and wants a list of potential customers who are more likely to buy in next three months. Use the available data to build a model which predicts if a customer is likely to buy a NUTRITIONAL product in next 3 months. A list of all 2500 household keys followed by prediction in 1/0 needs to be provided in an excel file. Use DEPARTMENT = ¡°NUTRITION¡± to filter out the products.

Please provide proper analysis based on data and it should be backed up with relevant tables/graphs.","# Dataset Description


**hh_demographic :**
This table contains demographic information for a portion of households. Due to nature of the 
data, the demographic information is not available for all households.

**train_transaction**
This table contains all products purchased by households within this study. Each line found in 
this table is essentially the same line that would be found on a store receipt. Data is available for 639 days.

**campaign_table**
This table lists the campaigns received by each household in the study. Each household 
received a different set of campaigns

**coupon_redempt**
This table identifies the coupons that each household redeemed.",True,True
5617,chisquarexhiring,"# Description

This is a straightforward competition aimed at testing your knowledge of Machine Learning, your implementation skills and your feature engineering finesse.

You are given a tabular dataset with the following columns, and you are tasked with predicting the _duration_ column. Predictions will be evaluated using the F1 score, calculated using the standard scikit-learn implementation.   

- type
- time	
- day of week
- models
- params
- queue_len
- trials

In addition to your predictions file (to be submitted here on Kaggle) which will indicate your leaderboard position, you're required to submit - 
- A detailed, well commented Python Notebook (hosted on Google Colab only, no .ipynb files will be accepted)
- A very short report ( 300 words) describing your system
- A short presentation ( ppt only) outlining your approach, feature engineering choices and the intuitions behind them

In case there is any ambiguity, use your best guess and explain your assumptions clearly in your presentation. 

----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation pages describes how submissions will be scored and how students should format their submissions. You don't need a sub-title at the top; the page title appears above. For an example of a high quality evaluation page, see the 2013 KDD Cup page, which we have included below:

---

The evaluation metric for this competition is [Mean F1-Score](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/F-score). The F1 score, commonly used in information retrieval, measures accuracy using the statistics precision \( \text{p} \) and recall \( \text{r} \).

Precision is the ratio of true positives \(\text{tp}\) to all predicted positives \(\text{tp} + \text{fp}\). Recall is the ratio of true positives \(\text{tp}\) to all actual positives \(\text{tp} + \text{fn}\). The F1 score is given by:

\[ \text{F1} = 2\frac{\text{p} \cdot \text{r}}{\text{p}+\text{r}}\ \ \mathrm{where}\ \ \text{p} = \frac{\text{tp}}{\text{tp}+\text{fp}},\ \ \text{r} = \frac{\text{tp}}{\text{tp}+\text{fn}} \]

The F1 metric weights recall and precision equally, and a good retrieval algorithm will maximize both precision and recall simultaneously. Thus, moderately good performance on both will be favored over extremely good performance on one and poor performance on the other.",# Dataset Description,True,True
5618,value-prediction-challenge,"# Description

### KBC Prize Prediction Challenge

Kaun Banega Crorepati (KBC) is a popular Hindi-language television game show in India where participants answer questions to win money. It has been a significant force in popular culture for years now.
The quizmasters of the show frame and collect the questions spanning a range of different genres. Have you ever thought about how they decide the worth of every question in the game? 
Imagine yourself as the technical expert in KBC's organizing committee. Now your job is to use your expertise in the domain of NLP to predict the prize on every question framed. Are you up for the challenge?

### Problem Statement 

You will be given various details about a question. Your task is to train an NLP model to predict the prize on the question in rupees. 

### Dataset

You are provided two splits of data - train and test. Navigate to the Data Section for more details.

### Submission

You must submit the solution in the form of a .csv file, with the predicted values for each test case (using the ¡®Id¡¯ column to denote the case). 
You also need to submit the link to your Colab Notebook (https://colab.research.google.com/) 

**Important:** In case you are using Kaggle notebooks, then strictly keep your notebook private. Sharing your Kaggle notebook publicly will make you liable for disqualification. Also, even if you are using Kaggle notebooks for writing your code, you need to first import it to Google Colaboratory and then share the link of the same, as mentioned above.

In case you are new to Kaggle, here is a handy link to Kaggle's [competition documentation](https://www.kaggle.com/docs/competitions), which includes, among other things, instructions on [submitting predictions](https://www.kaggle.com/docs/competitions#making-a-submission).


----------------------
# Evaluation

### The evaluation procedure for this challenge is as follows:

Submit your prediction file in the format as described in the Data section.
We will evaluate participants' performances based on the Macro-F1 score on the Test Set","# Dataset Description

## Description

You are provided with the training and testing splits. The training dataset consists of 1,73,594 questions with the corresponding prizes in rupees. The Test dataset contains 42,610 questions. Your predictions on the test data have to be submitted in the same format as the **submission.csv** file.

## Files

#### **1. train.csv (1,73,594 examples)**
The training set consists of the following columns:

1. Id (str) 

2. Game number (int)

3. Date (str) 

4. Round (str) 

5. Category (str) 

6. Question (str) 

7. Answer (str)

8. Prize (str) 

#### **2. test.csv (42,610 examples)**
The test split includes all of the training dataset's feature columns. Your task is to predict the prizes (Target column) for each of these questions corresponding to their respective 'Id' and submit the data in the same style as **submission.csv**.

#### **3. submission.csv**
This is the format in which you have to submit your predictions on the test dataset. Your final submission file should have only two columns. The first column should contain the 'Id' of the question from the training dataset. The second column should have predictions of the prize.
Remember that the numbers in submission.csv are only placeholders; you must build your submission file for the whole test set.",True,True
5619,aacc-2022-predicting-pthrp-results,"# Description

## The Challenge
The Informatics Section of the Department of Pathology and Immunology of Washington University School of Medicine and the Data Analytics Steering Committee of the American Association for Clinical Chemistry welcome you to the ***Predicting PTHrP Results Competition***. 

Through this competition, we hope to engage our community of laboratory medicine practitioners in a fun and friendly environment where we can practice our skills, learn from each other, and get a sense of the variety in how we approach problems in the data-driven side of our field. 

The competition will run from May 8 to July 1, when submissions must be finalized. On July 5, the winner will be announced. A representative of the winning team will be invited to present their solution at the AACC Informatics Division Annual Meeting during the [AACC Annual Scientific Meeting &amp; Clinical Lab Expo](https://meeting.aacc.org/) on July 25, 2022, in Chicago.

## The Background
Measurement of parathyroid hormone-related peptide (PTHrP) can help to establish a diagnosis of humoral hypercalcemia of malignancy. However, PTHrP testing is often ordered on patients with a low pretest probability of this condition. This poor laboratory utilization practice drives up healthcare costs, diverts scarce resources, and can cause anxiety, unwarranted cancer work-ups, and treatment delays in the event of a false positive. 

At Washington University in St. Louis, we use a manual, rules-based approach in which the lab medicine resident reviews PTH and calcium results to try to identify inappropriate orders where the probability of an abnormal PTHrP value is low. This process is labor-intensive and the ordering physician is often unwilling to cancel the order when contacted by the lab medicine resident.

In this friendly competition, we challenge you to develop an algorithm that predicts the PTHrP result (normal vs abnormal) using laboratory data available at the time of order to test our hypothesis that many of the PTHrP tests we send out fail to provide any novel information. The potential utility of this predictive algorithm would be to help convince the ordering physicians to improve utilization or to provide more timely clinical information while we wait for the result to return from the reference laboratory. 

![](https://i.imgur.com/zLscDSs.png) 

----------------------
# Evaluation

## Evaluating Performance

The evaluation metric for this competition is the [F1 Score](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/F-score). The F1 score measures performance by calculating the harmonic mean of precision and recall.

![](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/4179c69cf1dde8418c4593177521847e862e7df8)


## Submission Format

You will submit your predictions in the same format as the *submission\_template.csv* file on the **[Data](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/aacc-2022-predicting-pthrp-results/data)** page. This file should have two columns, *PATIENT\_ID* and *PREDICTION*. Your predictions should be a binary (0 or 1), with 0 being a normal PTHrP result. 


The file should contain a header and have the following format:
```
PATIENT_ID,PREDICTION
123,1
```
----------------------
# Timeline

![](https://i.imgur.com/wUJQqsO.png)
----------------------
# Prizes

**1st Place:** One representative of the team whose model most accurately predicts PTHrP results will be invited to present their method at the AACC Informatics Division Annual Meeting during the AACC Annual Scientific Meeting & Clinical Lab Expo, on July 25, 2022, in Chicago. The representative will receive complimentary meeting registration and travel support (airfare and one night¡¯s hotel) of up to $1,000.","# Dataset Description

*This is a real, de-identified, clinical dataset. The use of these data for this competition has been reviewed and deemed not human subjects research by the Institutional Review Board at Washington University in St Louis (approval numbers 202204007 and 202202087). The data is intended for educational use only, and any other use is prohibited.*

## The Data
Results for all PTHrP orders since 2012 were retrieved from the laboratory information system along with all other results that would have been available at the time of the PTHrP order. For patients that had multiple PTHrP orders, we retained only the first order and its associated data. The data has been de-identified to protect the privacy of the patients, physicians, and institutions, but otherwise appears as it would in our laboratory information system, without additional cleaning or preprocessing. That part will be up to you!

Each row of the data represents a single laboratory result, and the columns are explained below. 

## The Target
You should be predicting the PTHrP result as a binary classification, normal or abnormal, where abnormal is defined as above the reference range. Note that the reference range changed during this study period, as displayed in the **REFERENCE_RANGE** column. Your predictions should encode normal PTHrP results as 0, and abnormal results as 1.

## The Features
You decide! We have provided an enormous feature space on which you can experiment. Use your domain expertise, favorite feature selection algorithms, and your creativity to engineer the best possible set of features to produce your predictions. 

If you are unsure of where to start, take a look at the example we have provided on the [*Code*](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/aacc-2022-predicting-pthrp-results/code) page. This example uses some markers in calcium homeostasis and a boosted tree model to make predictions. This is meant to act as a guide, but there is plenty of optimization that can be done to improve the performance. 

## The Files

*   **input_data.txt** - A tab-separated, flat file containing the laboratory results available for each patient prior to the first PTHrP order, as described above. 
*   **PTHrP_train_results.txt** - The results of each PTHrp order for the training samples. Use this file to build and validate your models. This also contains some additional information about the PTHrP orders that might prove useful.
*   **PTHrP_test_results_masked.txt** - The results file for the testing set. Your submission file should contain one prediction for each of the orders in this file. A random subset of these will be graded and used for the final standings. 
*   **submission_template.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format.

## Columns

*   `PATIENT_ID` - anonymized ID unique to each patient in the data. 
*   `ORDERABLE`?- panel from which the lab was ordered (e.g. CBC, BMP). 
*   `TASK_ASSAY`?- the discrete lab assay (e.g. hemoglobin, sodium). 
*   `RESULT_VAL`?- the numeric or character result of a task_assay (e.g. 12.5, 141).
*   `RESULT_UNITS`?- units for the corresponding result (e.g. g/dL, mmol/L).
*   `NORMAL_LOW`?- the lower limit of normal for this result from our LIS.  
*   `NORMAL_HIGH`?- the upper limit of normal for this result from our LIS. 
*   `NORMALCY`?- a column that holds flags for high, low, abnormal, and critical results based on LIS rules.
*   `RESULT_STATUS`?- the step in the final verification process for each result. 
*   `ORDER_STATUS`?- whether the order was completed, modified, or canceled. 
*   `ORDER_PROVIDER`?- an anonymized ID for the provider who placed the order in our EMR.
*   `ORDER_DT_TM`?- the time that the order was placed in the EMR.
*   `PERFORMED_DT_TM`?- the time that the test was performed and first resulted back to the EMR. 

*Additional columns in the results files*

*   `REFERENCE_RANGE`?- the normal range for that particular result, either <2.0 or <=4.2 pmol/L.
*   `MEDICAL_SERVICE`?- the service linked to the encounter from which the order is placed (e.g. Medicine, Surgery, Emergency). 
*   `ENCOUNTER_TYPE`?- the type of encounter from which the order was placed (e.g. Inpatient, Outpatient, Emergency).",True,True
