----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is [Categorical Accuracy](https://www.tensorflow.org/api_docs/python/tf/keras/metrics/CategoricalAccuracy), which calculates the percentage of predicted values (yPred) that match with actual values (yTrue) for one-hot labels.

The [CIFAR-10 dataset](https://www.cs.toronto.edu/~kriz/cifar.html) used in this competition is divided into 10 different classes and there are 50000 images for training and 10000 images for testing. The ground truth labels are available for both train and test sets and automatic scoring metric in this competition can only measure how accurately you predict the ground truth labels for test dataset from your submissions. Therefore, 100% accuracy can be reached by just submitting ground truth labels. For discouraging this unfair submissions, we want you to make submissions by using the [challenge\_test\_Keras](https://www.kaggle.com/code/maktas/challenge-test-keras) notebook publicly available. This notebook simply downloads your pre-trained network from a given url in Keras hdf5 format and evaluate the accuracy of your model and create the submission file. You just need to change the `url` assignment line at the end of the second code cell in this notebook. If you store your trained model in Goggle Drive, please ensure that you give the direct link instead of shared link. You can use [Google Drive Direct Link Generater](https://sites.google.com/site/gdocs2direct/) to convert the shared link into direct url link to use in the challenge\_test\_Keras notebook.

## Submission Format

Submission file should contain two columns: `Id` and `Category`. The first row in Id column is tagged as `Trained_Net_Path` and the first row in Category column is the url of your trained model in Keras hdf5 format. Starting from second row, `Id` column should contain the number of image in test set and `Category` column should contain the corresponding predicted label within [0-9] obtained from your trained model output. 

The url given in your submission will be used to check the validness of your submission. We will run the [challenge\_test\_Keras](https://www.kaggle.com/code/maktas/challenge-test-keras) notebook with the given url in your submission file and get the actual score for your submission. If there is any mismatch, we will regretfully invalidate your submission. We encourage all the participants to submit directly the output of their trained model in inference mode, instead of submitting known ground truth labels.
----------------------
# Templates

This competition is a challenge to train a given neural network, without any modification at least in inference mode. You are free to use any data augmentation methods and optimization tricks to obtain the best accuracies, but it is forbidden to modify any part of the original model architecture. For that reason, we shared the Python scripts to define the model architecture and training and testing templates. The paths and descriptions of these scripts are given as follows.

- [challenge\_train\_template\_TF2\_Keras](https://www.kaggle.com/code/maktas/challenge-train-template-tf2-keras): This notebook contains the general training pipeline for CIFAR-10 dataset written in Tensorflow 2.X with Keras backend. The trained model weights are saved in Keras hdf5 format.
- [challenge\_train\_template\_TF2](https://www.kaggle.com/code/maktas/challenge-train-template-tf2): This notebook contains the general training pipeline for CIFAR-10 dataset written in Tensorflow 2.X without Keras backend. The trained model weights are saved in Keras hdf5 format.
- [challenge\_train\_template\_TF1](https://www.kaggle.com/code/maktas/challenge-train-template-tf1): This notebook contains the general training pipeline for CIFAR-10 dataset written in Tensorflow 1.X without Keras backend. The trained model weights are saved first in Tensorflow 1.X checkpoint format and then converted into Keras hdf5 format.
- [challenge\_test\_Keras](https://www.kaggle.com/code/maktas/challenge-test-keras): This notebook downloads the pre-trained model from a given url and then create submission file and report the measured  test accuracy.

The first three notebooks give you a starting point to create your own training pipeline with your favoured framework. You should modify the losses, optimization methods and may be constraints in the training part of these templates to get your best result. The last notebook can be used to generate a submission.csv file and get the test accuracy of your trained model.","# Dataset Description

The CIFAR-10 dataset consists of 60000 32x32 colour images in 10 classes, with 6000 images per class. There are 50000 training images and 10000 test images.

The dataset is divided into five training batches and one test batch, each with 10000 images. The test batch contains exactly 1000 randomly-selected images from each class. The training batches contain the remaining images in random order, but some training batches may contain more images from one class than another. Between them, the training batches contain exactly 5000 images from each class.

The classes in the dataset are ""airplane"", ""automobile"", ""bird"", ""cat"", ""deer"", ""dog"", ""frog"", ""horse"", ""ship"", ""truck"".										

The classes are completely mutually exclusive. There is no overlap between automobiles and trucks. ""Automobile"" includes sedans, SUVs, things of that sort. ""Truck"" includes only big trucks. Neither includes pickup trucks.

For more information please visit https://www.cs.toronto.edu/~kriz/cifar.html

The Python version of CIFAR-10 dataset is used in this competition and converting the image format is handled in the script ""cifar10_input.py"".

## Files

*   **Dataset** - the folder containing train and test sets
*   **cifar10_input.py** - the Python script containing functions for converting dataset into image and various data augmentations. The codes are taken from [MadryLab](https://github.com/MadryLab/cifar10_challenge/blob/master/cifar10_input.py)
*   **model_challenge.py** - the Python script containing functions for defining the Deep Neural Network that should be trained in this challenge
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

## Columns

*   `Id` - the id of the test image.
*   `Category`?- predicted class number of the test image within [0,9]

The first row in `Id` column is tagged as `Trained_Net_Path` and the first row in `Category` is the url of the pre-trained model in Keras hdf5 format. This data will be used to check the validness of the submission. For further information please look at [Evaluation](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/ai-training-challenge-1/overview/evaluation) section.",True,True
5627,classification-of-an-out-of-distribution-dataset,"# Description

In this assignment you are asked to classify an ""Out-of-Distribution"" hold-out test set using a set of data:   **'train-set.7z'**, and **'train\_labels.csv'**. '**train-set.7z**' is a zip container (you may need to install [7zip](https://www.7-zip.org/download.html)) of the training data and their labels can be found in **'train\_labels.csv'**. Through carefully studying the dataset at hand, the task is to come up with a classifier with decent performance on the **'test\_set.7z'** for which you don't have labels. Notice that in the **'train\_labels.csv'** in addition to intended labels (label2) you have also been provided with a helper label (label1). But, keep in mind that your task is to predict label2. Hence, your submission should be formatted this way:
| id|label|
| --- | --- |
| 0.png| 0 |
| 1.png| 0 |
| ... | ... |
| 3928.png| 0 |
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is [Accuracy](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Accuracy_and_precision).

## Submission Format

The file should contain a header and have the following format:
| id|label|
| --- | --- |
| 0.png| 0 |
| 1.png| 0 |
| ... | ... |
| 3928.png| 0 |","# Dataset Description

In this assignment you are asked to classify an ""Out-of-Distribution"" hold-out test set using a set of data:   **'train-set.7z'**, and **'train_labels.csv'**. **'train-set.7z' ** is a zip container (you may need to install [7zip](https://www.7-zip.org/download.html)) of the training data and their labels can be found in **'train_labels.csv'**. Through carefully studying the dataset at hand, the task is to come up with a classifier with decent performance on the **'test_set.7z'** for which you don't have labels. Notice that in the **'train_labels.csv'** in addition to intended labels (label2) you have also been provided with a helper label (label1). But, keep in mind that your task is to predict **label2**. 

**Keep in mind that your daily maximum submissions is 2/day !**",True,True
5628,principios-de-machine-learning-regresion-2210,"# Description

Se tiene informaci¨®n sobre 1600 zonas ordenadas de manera contigua durante un a?o de un detallista de ropa y joyer¨ªa que vende por cat¨¢logo.

De acuerdo con lo indicado por el cuerpo directivo, el objetivo es realizar un pron¨®stico de ventas anual para la ropa de mujer basado en la informaci¨®n de la sucursal. De gran importancia es saber si los recursos que se asignan tienen poder para modelar la demanda en las zonas respectivas.

Para ello desean que usted haga un pron¨®stico para las 400 zonas que se encuentran en el archivo de sometimiento (ver datos), cuya informaci¨®n corresponde al mismo a?o de las 1600 zonas originales. El pron¨®stico ser¨¢ evaluado con base en la ra¨ªz del error cuadr¨¢tico medio (RMSE) que se obtenga en estas 400 sucursales.

##?Qu¨¦ deben presentar?

- Talleres individuales acorde a CRISP-DM con longitud m¨¢xima de 8 p¨¢ginas: 50%.
- Predicci¨®n: 50%.

----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>La mejor predicci¨®n obtendr¨¢ un 50 en el 50% del taller, y todos aquellos que igualen un benchmark ingenuo obtendr¨¢n 20. Valores intermedios se ubicar¨¢n de acuerdo a una escala. Si no se supera el benchmark ingenuo la nota ser¨¢ inferior a 20. La predicci¨®n ser¨¢ valorada basada en el ingenuo ya publicado y usando la m¨¦trica RMSE.

La colaboraci¨®n en los kernels y foros en el espacio del concurso puede darle puntos adicionales, valorados por los profesores. Colaborar no necesariamente es responder, tambi¨¦n preguntar y comentar. S¨®lo se permitir¨¢n notebooks de exploraci¨®n/visualizaci¨®n.

El taller corresponde al otro 50% de la nota.
</p>","# Dataset Description

El archivo de datos que se entrega est¨¢ en formato Excel (xlsx) y tiene dos hojas: Train, para desarrollar el modelo (1600 registros) y Test (400 registros) con todas las variables independientes necesarias para predecir la variable de respuesta (ropamujer). Las predicciones deben ser sometidas a kaggle en formato .csv con dos columnas con encabezados idloc y ropamujer (esta ¨²ltima contiene su predicci¨®n). La separaci¨®n de las columnas es con coma y el decimal debe marcarse con punto(.).

##Campos de datos

- ropamujer: ventas de prendas para mujer en millones de pesos durante el a?o.
- correo: n¨²mero de cat¨¢logos enviados durante el a?o.
- p¨¢ginas: n¨²mero de p¨¢ginas del cat¨¢logo.
- tel¨¦fono: n¨²mero promedio de l¨ªneas para llamada abiertas para pedidos.
- impresa: cantidad gastada en publicidad impresa.
- servicio: n¨²mero de representantes del servicio al cliente.
- idmercado: tipo de mercado. Solo se listan n¨²meros de clases o tipos de mercado que se desea atacar. No se proveer¨¢ informaci¨®n adicional.
- tama?omer: tama?o potencial del mercado, proyectado de acuerdo a cifras del ¨¢rea de marketing.
- Idloc: ID de la tienda.
- Edadloc: a?os transcurridos desde la llegada a la zona.
- promo (promoci¨®n): tipo de promoci¨®n que se llev¨® a cabo durante el a?o. De nuevo, informaci¨®n descriptiva sobre estas promociones es reservada.
- nomina: valor total de la n¨®mina durante el a?o.",True,True
5629,cse151b-spring2022,"# Description

This is the home page of the deep learning class competition (CSE 151b Spring 2022). 
Here is a handy link to Kaggle's [competition documentation](https://www.kaggle.com/docs/competitions), which includes, among other things, instructions on [submitting predictions](https://www.kaggle.com/docs/competitions#making-a-submission).


## Background
Autonomous vehicles (AV) are expected to dramatically redefine the future of transportation. However, there are still significant engineering challenges to be solved before one can fully realize the benefits of self-driving cars. One such challenge is building models that reliably predict the movement of traffic agents around the AV, such as cars, cyclists, and pedestrians.

In this competition, you¡¯ll apply your deep learning skills to build motion forecasting models for self-driving vehicles. You'll have access to a large AV motion dataset to train and test your models. The goal of the prediction task is to predict the positions of a tracked AV 6 seconds into the future, given an initial 5-second observation. 

The training, validation, and test sequences are taken from different areas of 6 cities so that there is no geographical overlap. Each sequence follows the trajectory of the main agent for 11 seconds, while keeping track of all other traffic agents including cars and pedestrians. Agents follow very complex trajectories, such as changing lanes, navigating intersections, and turning. 

## Team Guideline
This is a group project for the class. You can request the computational resources from [UCSD Data Science/Machine Learning platform](https://datahub.ucsd.edu/hub/login) allocated for this course. See more details on the [Course Website](https://sites.google.com/view/cse151b/competition).

Maximum submission per day: 10
Submission deadline: 11:59 pm UTC May 29, 2021. 

Please note UTC is 7 hours ahead of PST,

## Evaluation Guideline
See evaluation page for submission format and evaluation metric. The final rank counts towards 10% of the final score. 

## Acknowledgements

We thank [Argo](https://www.argo.ai/) for sharing this dataset.
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation page describes how submissions will be scored and how you should format your submissions. 

---

The evaluation metric for this competition is [Mean Squared Error](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mean_squared_error). The MSE score measures the average squared difference between the estimated values and the actual value. 


## Submission Format

You will make predictions for each scene in the test set, where only the input is provided. You will generate the prediction and submit your solution in a single csv file. In the solution file, each row starts with a ID of the scene, followed by 120 columns.  These 120 columns are the x and y coordinates for the future 60 time steps (6 seconds). The submission file has the following format.
```
# ID      v1   v2   v3  v4 ...
0_austin  x0   y0   x1  y1 ..
1_austin
```
The ID contains two parts. The prefix is the index in the input file, followed by the name of the city. You can also refer to the sample submission file as an example.","# Dataset Description

This page describes the details of the dataset. We provide the training set and the input of the test set. You are requested to submit the output (predictions) for the test set. The test set is further split into 80% for the public leaderboard and 20% for the private leaderboard.

We also provide a sample submission file with zero predictions. You are expected to replace the zeros with the predictions generated by your deep learning model.



## Files

*   **train** - the training set folder
*   **test** - the test set folder
*  **Load_Argo2_Publlic.ipynb** - a jupyter notebook to load the data 
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

## Format

*   `austin_input` - input trajectories in Austin. A numpy array with the shape ( N, 50, 2). N is the number of scenes. 50 is the input 50 time steps. 2 is the x and y dimension.
*   `austin_output`?- output trajectories in Austin. A numpy array with the shape ( N, 60, 2). N is the number of scenes. 60 is the output 60 time steps. 2 is the x and y dimension.
*   same for all other cities",True,True
