5636,iba-ml2-final-project,"# Description

Welcome to our final project! This project is hosted as a Kaggle InClass competition. 
     <p>?Time series analysis and forecasting plays significant role in business and financial planning, resources allocation, investments decision making, portfolio distribution, weather forecasting, stock prices forecasting and other applications.
     </p>?In this competition you will analyze the daily electricity consumption time series of Germany Open Power System Data (OPSD). The goal would  be to make a 7-days (1 week) electricity consumption prediction based on daily data. Please follow link below to find details on the dataset and several tips on data preprocessing:
https://www.dataquest.io/blog/tutorial-time-series-analysis-with-pandas/ 
     <p></p>?Work on this competition is individual.

## Requirements
In order to receive a passing grade on the project, you need to complete the following requirements (you can organize your notebook in whichever way you like): 
- Successful submission to the leaderboard, with the public leaderboard score no worse than median public leaderboard score minus 5%.
- EDA analysis on the data
- Demonstration of an RNN-based model for the required task. Please also feel free to combine several layers (RNN/LSTM, Dense, Dropout, etc.) in your sequential model.

For the final submissions you can use whichever model you like.
Take care to explain all the decisions you make and all steps of the analysis and prediction.

## Awards
Several awards (i.e. course grade bonuses) will be chosen:
- 3 best submissions (based on the private leaderboard)
- 2 best explanations of neural network architecture choice (picked by course staff)

## Leaderboard
The competition has 2 leaderboards - public and private. The public leaderboard consists of about 50% of the test set, while the rest makes up the private leaderboard. During the competition, only the public leaderboard score will be presented, but the final choice of awards will be based on the private leaderboard. Note that there is a daily limit on the amount of submissions you can make.
Good luck, and enjoy!

<p></p>
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is root mean squared error (rmse) https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Root-mean-square_deviation
This metric is commonly used when forecasting numerical variables.
The lower the rmse the better your model is.

Structure of the submission file should be as demonstrated below.
""Consumption\_day\_1"" refers to an electricity consumption  on a day specified by ""Date"" column, ""Consumption\_day\_2"" refers to ""Date""+1 day and so on.
```
Date,Consumption_day_1, Consumption_day_2, Consumption_day_3, Consumption_day_4, Consumption_day_5, Consumption_day_6, Consumption_day_7
2017-05-09,1457.217,1550.172,1443.71,1326.325,1432.974,1289.427,1375.759
```","# Dataset Description

The data for this competition consists of electricity consumption time series.

The files provided for the competition are:

*   **train.csv** - the training set. Contains electricity consumption time series (with daily resolution)
*   **test.csv** - the test set. Contains list of start dates for each 7 days forecasting period.
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format. ""Consumption_day_1"" refers to an electricity consumption on a day specified by ""Date"" column, ""Consumption_day_2"" refers to ""Date""+1 day and so on.

## Columns

*   `Date` - The date (yyyy-mm-dd format)
*   `Consumption`?- electricity consumption in GWh",True,True
