5648,vmware-zero-shot-information-retrieval,"# Description

This competition is brought to you by VMware's [Machine Learning Program Office (MLPO)](https://octo.vmware.com/author/mlpocomm/) and R&D AI Lab (RAIL).  This is an [Information Retrieval](https://nlp.stanford.edu/IR-book/information-retrieval-book.html) task [(papers-with-code link)](https://paperswithcode.com/task/information-retrieval), using real queries from our Google Search Console data, searching our highly-technical corpus of documentation, product material, blogs, etc.  We have ~2.3k queries that more or less resemble questions and ~325k documents in the corpus. **Your challenge is to use state-of-the-art information retrieval techniques to find the top five documents that do the best job of answering each question.**

We've limited the query set to queries that more or less resemble questions because they are far more challenging.  Any old keyword-based search engine can find the correct document for ""vSphere 7.0 release notes"", but will fall flat when faced with something like, ""why is everyone doing this isolate x copy and paste"".

The intent of this competition is to spur academic research in the field of ""domain-specific information retrieval"" (as a complement to all of the research into ""general information retrieval"", ¨¤ la MS MARCO).  Long term, our hope is that this competition spurs other companies to release similar datasets.  Then, after more datasets are available, someone can create a comprehensive benchmark, much like SuperGLUE, but for IR.

## Training Set

This is a zero-shot challenge, therefore there is no labeled training set ¨C just the document corpus, which is comprised of our documentation, KBs, and other publicly available content. Along with the page content, the metadata for *most* files is available.
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is [nDCG@5](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Discounted_cumulative_gain). Normalized Discounted Cumulative Gain is commonly used in Information Retrieval and measures the quality of your retrieved documents up to the limit of the 5th document.  We cap measuring at the 5th result to force the correct/best page(s) to be in the top half of the first page of search results. 

## Submission Format

**For every query in the test set**, submission files should contain 5 rows and two columns: `QueryId` and `DocumentId`.  `QueryID` is the ID of the query from the test set.  `DocumentId` is the filename (`f_name` in the content CSV) of the responsive document.  The 5 rows per query represent the top 5 documents per query and should be ordered according to their relevance.

Your submission file should contain a header and have the following format:
```
QueryId,DocumentId
0,38.txt
0,49.txt
0,69.txt
0,80.txt
0,101.txt
1,https---www.vmware.com-.txt
1,https---www.vmware.com-cloud-economics.txt
1,https---www.vmware.com-cloud-services-engagement-platform.txt
1,https---www.vmware.com-cloud-solutions-app-modernization-cloud-native-apps.txt
1,https---www.vmware.com-cloud-solutions-app-modernization-devsecops.txt
```

This example shows what it would look like if the top 5 responses to the first query were the first 5 KB articles and the top 5 for the second were the first 5 pages from vmware.com.

## Accounting for duplicate documents via GUID

Our documentation is highly duplicative.  This is a product of the same page existing for each version of a product.  For example, let's say the following document was the top result for a given query:

`en-2021-VMware-vSphere-6-7-com-vmware-vsphere-vcsa-doc-GUID-F972C583-1423-4A14-AECB-0A588F918DFB-html.txt`

One could expect the next two entries to be:

`en-2021-VMware-vSphere-6-5-com-vmware-vsphere-vcsa-doc-GUID-F972C583-1423-4A14-AECB-0A588F918DFB-html.txt`

and

`en-2021-VMware-vSphere-6-0-com-vmware-vsphere-vcsa-doc-GUID-F972C583-1423-4A14-AECB-0A588F918DFB-html.txt`

That's really just the same content across 3 versions of vSphere (6.0, 6.5, and 6.7).  You can tell it's the same page because the GUID is the same for all three files.  In terms of user experience, showing the same content for the first three entries would be less than ideal.  So, we dedupe by GUID.  Whenever you have documentation in your top five, strip the filename to the GUID.

```
# Example pseudo-code

result = ""en-2021-VMware-vSphere-6-7-com-vmware-vsphere-vcsa-doc-GUID-F972C583-1423-4A14-AECB-0A588F918DFB-html.txt""

if ""GUID"" in result:
    # Use Regex to match the GUID, then postpend the file extension
    result = result.match(/GUID-[A-Z\d\-]+-html/) + "".txt""
print result

# Expected output
# GUID-F972C583-1423-4A14-AECB-0A588F918DFB-html.txt
```

The stripped filename is what you enter as the `DocumentId`.  Don't forget to dedupe your `DocumentId` column for each query.  Submitting the same GUID three times won't get you extra credit, and will rob you of 2 additional `DocumentId` entries for that query.

### Bad Examples

```
QueryId,DocumentId
0,en-2021-VMware-vSphere-6-7-com-vmware-vsphere-vcsa-doc-GUID-F972C583-1423-4A14-AECB-0A588F918DFB-html.txt
0,en-2021-VMware-vSphere-6-5-com-vmware-vsphere-vcsa-doc-GUID-F972C583-1423-4A14-AECB-0A588F918DFB-html.txt
0,en-2021-VMware-vSphere-6-0-com-vmware-vsphere-vcsa-doc-GUID-F972C583-1423-4A14-AECB-0A588F918DFB-html.txt
0,other-document-1.txt
0,other-document-2.txt
```

This is wrong because the full filenames were submitted.  This will get a score of 0.00 because it won't match.

```
QueryId,DocumentId
0,GUID-F972C583-1423-4A14-AECB-0A588F918DFB-html.txt
0,GUID-F972C583-1423-4A14-AECB-0A588F918DFB-html.txt
0,GUID-F972C583-1423-4A14-AECB-0A588F918DFB-html.txt
0,other-document-1.txt
0,other-document-2.txt
```

This is better, but still wrong because the GUIDs weren't deduped.  You'll get credit for having the correct GUID in the first place, but the second and third entries will be ignored.

### Good Example

```
QueryId,DocumentId
0,GUID-F972C583-1423-4A14-AECB-0A588F918DFB-html.txt
0,other-document-1.txt
0,other-document-2.txt
0,other-document-3.txt
0,other-document-4.txt
```

This is perfect.  The filename was stripped to the GUID and deduped.","# Dataset Description

## Files

*   **vmware_ir_content.csv** - the search corpus.  See the column description below.
*   **test.csv** - the list of queries you'll use to search
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format (with very wrong answers)

<br>

## **`vmware_ir_content.csv`** Columns

### Column **`document_group`**

This is the portal the document came from:

- **`blog`**
  - Origin: **Blogs**
- **`cb`**
  -  Origin: **Carbon Black**
- **`docs`**
  - Origin: **Documentation, Release Notes, & Solution Guides**
- **`glossary`**
  -  Origin: **Glossary**
- **`kb`**
  -  Origin: **KB Articles**
- **`sdw`**
  -  Origin: **SD-WAN**
- **`tanzu`**
  -  Origin: **Tanzu**
- **`vmw-com`**
  -  Origin: **VMware main site**

### Column **`f_name`**

This is the filename you'll use for `DocumentId` in your submission CSV.

### Column **`raw_text`**

This is the raw text from the webpage.

### Column **`raw_meta`**

This is the metadata for each page in JSON.

<br>

## **`test.csv`** Columns

*   **`QueryId`** - The ID for the query
*   **`Query`**?- The query itself

<br>

## **`sample_submission.csv`** Columns

*   `QueryId` - The ID for the query from test.csv
*   `DocumentId ` - The **`f_name`** (or deduped GUID) from vmware_ir_content.csv

<br>

## Example Notebook

See the Example Notebook for how to load the data and parse the JSON.",True,True
5649,university-of-stavanger-beans-classification,"# Description

- The original dataset is from the [Dry Beans dataset](https://archive.ics.uci.edu/ml/datasets/Dry+Bean+Dataset) from the University of Irvine, California (UCI) datasets archive, description:
    - Seven different types of dry beans were used in this research, taking into account the features such as form, shape, type, and structure by the market situation. A computer vision system was developed to distinguish seven different registered varieties of dry beans with similar features in order to obtain uniform seed classification. For the classification model, images of 13,611 grains of 7 different registered dry beans were taken with a high-resolution camera. Bean images obtained by computer vision system were subjected to segmentation and feature extraction stages, and a total of **16 features; 12 dimensions and 4 shape forms**, were obtained from the grains
----------------------
# Evaluation

Evaluation is done on a held-out test set using Categorization Accuracy","# Dataset Description

- The original dataset is from the [Dry Beans dataset](https://archive.ics.uci.edu/ml/datasets/Dry+Bean+Dataset) from the University of Irvine, California (UCI) datasets archive, description:
