5652,fmi-su-recommender-system-hw-22022,"# Description

This kaggle competition is part of the ""Recommender Systems"" course at Sofia University, FMI, 2022 year.

The domain of the competition is books recommedation as you have data about:

Users - id, location, age
Books - id, title, author, year, publisher
and you have ratings from 0 to 10 which user id gave to book id

Users and Books files are the same for training and testing as
for training you have 862,335 ratings which the users gave to the books  and for testing you have 287,445 ratings which you need to predict.

The expectation is that you will try content based, collaborative filtering and/or hybrid approach as the evaluation will be with RMSE.

----------------------
# Evaluation


The evaluation metric for this competition is RMSE

## Submission Format

**For every userid,bookid in the test_dataset**, submission files should contain 3 columns: `Id`,`Rating`. The ratings should be integer.


The file should contain a header and have the following format:
```
`Id`,`Rating`
0,9
1,10
2,5
3,10
4,0
```","# Dataset Description

## Files

*   **users.csv** - list with all users and their features which are at the train and/or test set
*   **books.csv** - list with all books and their features which are at the train and/or test set
*   **ratings.csv** - file which shows ratings given by the users to the books
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format",True,True
5653,2022bmi707-assignment-2-q5,"# Description

<h2>Question 5: Multiclass classification</h2>
<p>In this question, we will build multiclass classifiers to distinguish different types of lung diseases using the NIHCXR8 data.</p>
<p>Please download the training set and the validation set from the Data tab (https://www.kaggle.com/c/2022bmi707-assignment-2-q5/data). Please note that this dataset is different from the one we used in Questions 3 and 4. Please <b>DO NOT</b> use any additional dataset to train or augment your models.</p> 
<p>Feel free to use any (ImageNet or any custom) architecture to classify all available classes.</p>
<p>In addition to participating in this BMI707 internal Kaggle contest, you would need to provide your answers to the questions and codes in the assignment writeup.</p>

----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is <a href=""https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sensitivity_and_specificity#Confusion_matrix"">accuracy</a>.</p>
<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p><strong>For every author in the dataset</strong>, submission files should contain two columns: Id and Category.</p>
<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>Id,Category<br>00000041_004.png,0<br></pre>","# Dataset Description

<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>train.tar</strong> - the training set (Dropbox link: <a href=""https://www.dropbox.com/s/feaakvusdvkep3n/train.tar"">https://www.dropbox.com/s/feaakvusdvkep3n/train.tar)</a></li>
<li><strong>train.csv</strong> - the labels of the training set (Dropbox link: <a href=""https://www.dropbox.com/s/9kebfecemhfkj7k/train.csv"">https://www.dropbox.com/s/9kebfecemhfkj7k/train.csv)</a></li>
<li><strong>testPublic.tar</strong> - the public test set (the labels are not given). This dataset is used to generate the evaluation results for the leaderboard. (Dropbox link: <a href=""https://www.dropbox.com/s/20d8b9z8znc2lmp/testPublic.tar"">https://www.dropbox.com/s/20d8b9z8znc2lmp/testPublic.tar</a>) </li>
<li><strong>testPrivate.tar</strong> - the private test set (the labels are not given). This dataset is used to generate the final evaluation. (Dropbox link: <a href=""https://www.dropbox.com/s/lun96wxiq84z7eg/testPrivate.tar"">https://www.dropbox.com/s/lun96wxiq84z7eg/testPrivate.tar</a>) </li>
<li><strong>sampleSubmission.csv</strong> - a sample submission file in the correct format. (Dropbox link: <a href=""https://www.dropbox.com/s/a9o0lkszeusdmr9/sampleSubmission.csv"">https://www.dropbox.com/s/a9o0lkszeusdmr9/sampleSubmission.csv</a>)</li>
</ul>
<h2>Data fields in the submission</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Id</strong> - the file name of the image</li>
<li><strong>Category</strong> - the diagnostic category of the image</li>
</ul>",True,True
5654,data-science-homework,"# Description

# Pima Indians Diabetes Database

#### Number of Instances: 768
#### Number of Attributes: 8 plus class

### Columns Description:
1. Number of times pregnant
2. Plasma glucose concentration a 2 hours in an oral glucose tolerance test
3. Diastolic blood pressure (mm Hg)
4. Triceps skin fold thickness (mm)
5. 2-Hour serum insulin (mu U/ml)
6. Body mass index (weight in kg/(height in m)^2)
7. Diabetes pedigree function
8. Age (years)
9. Class variable (0 or 1)

### Class Distribution: (class value 1 is interpreted as ""tested positive for diabetes"")
0 --- 500
1 ---- 268


----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated on area under the ROC curve between the predicted probability and the observed target.

## Submission Format

For each `p_id` in the test set, you must predict a probability for `diabetes`. The file should contain a header and have the following format:
```
p_id, diabetes
437, 0.155
411, 0.343
639, 0.853
etc.
```","# Dataset Description

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format",True,True
