5679,advanced-nlp-l1-hackthon-1,"# Description

<h1>Advance NLP - Hackathon 1</h1>

Classification of text comments as Constructive vs Non-Constructive.

The main intention of this hackathon is to assess the understanding of concepts discussed so far in this course. Here the problem statement is a simple text classification on whether the provided comment is constructive or not. 
These comments are grabbed from online competing platforms where a user will have multiple emotions and articulating approaches in describing the same feeling. Now our task is to classify all those comments into two specific classes.
<li> Constructive - 1</li>
<li>Non-Constructive - 0</li>

<h2>Steps to Follow : </h2>

<li>Read train.csv file which contains two columns id and comment text. Then build a classification model on top of this dataset .</li>
<li>Using the model create class labels for each record in test.csv and save it as in the form of provided sample submission file. In the 1st hackthon, you are allowed to use only ML based models. </li>
<li>Now click on submit button.</li>


More Details about dataset: https://www.kaggle.com/mtaboada/c3-constructive-comments-corpus





----------------------
# Evaluation

**1. Evaluation Metric**

The evaluation metric for this competition is Mean F1-Score. The F1 score, commonly used in information retrieval, measures accuracy using the statistics precision p and recall r. Precision is the ratio of true positives (tp) to all predicted positives (tp + fp). Recall is the ratio of true positives to all actual positives (tp + fn). The F1 score is given by:


$$F1 =2\frac{p.r}{p+r}$$  

where  $$p=\frac{tp}{tp+fp}$$,  $$r=\frac{tp}{tp+fn}$$ 

**2. Submission Format**
For every text in the test set, submission files should contain two columns: id and target.

The submission file should contain the column headers: id, predicted

Example:
id, 	target
source\_30306595\_50, 0 
source\_28078752\_39, 1","# Dataset Description

**What files do I need?**

You'll need train.csv which contains the input data to train ml model, Use test.csv data for predictions, you can refer to sample_submission.csv to understand the output format

**What is the data format?**

train.csv : Contains id, text, target
 
(id: unique Id for the text, text: the comments text, target: Indicator whether given comment text is Constructive or not)

**What am I predicting?**

You will be predicting the target for the given corpus of comments text in test.csv. Make sure you submit your predictions with correct label names referenced in the description.",True,True
5680,gem-predictive-maintenance-2022,"# Description

## Aim of the challenge

The goal of this challenge is to predict the RUL (Remaining Useful Life) of turbofan engines.

The dataset consists of different multivariate time-series. These different time-series refer to different engines ( engineno in the dataset). The sampling of the time series is 1 point per engine cycle  timein_cycles in the dataset).

The dataset is split into train data and test data to evaluate your model.

In the train dataset: the engine runs until failure. It means that for each data point we can associate the RUL (Remaining Useful Life in cycles). This column is present in the train dataset (RUL).

In the test dataset: The engine runs until a certain point. What you need to predict is the RUL at the last point of the dataset.
----------------------
# Evaluation

You will give a submission file containing one column:

result : containing the RUL of engine #i (where i is the line number)

The evaluation will be based on the RMSE. https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Root-mean-square_deviation","# Dataset Description

The dataset consists of different multivariate time-series. These different time-series refers to different engine ('engine_no' in the dataset). The sampling of the time series is 1 point per engine cycle ('time_in_cycles' in the dataset).

The time-series contain some operational features and some sensor features (explicitly mentionned in the header).


README:


Data Set: FD001
Train trajectories: 100
Test trajectories: 100
Conditions: ONE (Sea Level)
Fault Modes: ONE (HPC Degradation)

Data Set: FD002
Train trajectories: 260
Test trajectories: 259
Conditions: SIX 
Fault Modes: ONE (HPC Degradation)

Data Set: FD003
Train trajectories: 100
Test trajectories: 100
Conditions: ONE (Sea Level)
Fault Modes: TWO (HPC Degradation, Fan Degradation)

Data Set: FD004
Train trajectories: 248
Test trajectories: 249
Conditions: SIX 
Fault Modes: TWO (HPC Degradation, Fan Degradation)



Experimental Scenario

Data sets consists of multiple multivariate time series. Each data set is further divided into training and test subsets. Each time series is from a different engine ? i.e., the data can be considered to be from a fleet of engines of the same type. Each engine starts with different degrees of initial wear and manufacturing variation which is unknown to the user. This wear and variation is considered normal, i.e., it is not considered a fault condition. There are three operational settings that have a substantial effect on engine performance. These settings are also included in the data. The data is contaminated with sensor noise.

The engine is operating normally at the start of each time series, and develops a fault at some point during the series. In the training set, the fault grows in magnitude until system failure. In the test set, the time series ends some time prior to system failure. The objective of the competition is to predict the number of remaining operational cycles before failure in the test set, i.e., the number of operational cycles after the last cycle that the engine will continue to operate. Also provided a vector of true Remaining Useful Life (RUL) values for the test data.

 The columns correspond to:
1)	unit number
2)	time, in cycles
3)	operational setting 1
4)	operational setting 2
5)	operational setting 3
6)	sensor measurement  1
7)	sensor measurement  2
...
26)	sensor measurement  26


Reference: A. Saxena, K. Goebel, D. Simon, and N. Eklund, ?Damage Propagation Modeling for Aircraft Engine Run-to-Failure Simulation?, in the Proceedings of the Ist International Conference on Prognostics and Health Management (PHM08), Denver CO, Oct 2008.",True,True
5681,the-price-is-right,"# Description

With your new Machine Learning skills you're now ready to tackle a real-world problem!

You're in the market for a new house and your budget is between $170,000 and $190,000. Can you create a model based on the Ames Housing dataset that will correctly predict which houses are within your budget?

Use a 'Classification' method to predict which houses should have a sale price between $170,000 and $190,000 (inclusive). 

Your submissions will be graded using the F1-score metric. 

Here is a handy link to Kaggle's [competition documentation](https://www.kaggle.com/docs/competitions), which includes, among other things, instructions on [submitting predictions](https://www.kaggle.com/docs/competitions#making-a-submission).

----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is [Mean F1-Score](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/F-score). 

## Submission Format

**For every house in the dataset**, submission files should contain two columns. The first column contains the ID of the house, and the second column contains a 1 if the house is in the budget range, or a 0 if it is outside the budget range.


The file should contain a header and have the following format:
```
house_id, in_budget,
1,1
2,0
3,1
4,1
5,0
.
.
.
```","# Dataset Description

We will be using the same Ames Housing dataset we looked at earlier.

## Files

*   **housing_data.csv** - the dataset
*   **data_description.txt** - the data dictionary",True,True
5682,2022-spring-competition-psych-5710,"# Description

<h2>Introduction</h2>

Hi, it's time to apply everything you learned in our Introduction to Machine Learning and Data Mining course.

<p>In this competition you'll work with a dataset containing drug reviews (i.e. textual information that people that used some medications posted in an online medical portal). The dataset provides patient reviews on specific drugs along with related conditions and a 10 star patient rating reflecting overall patient satisfaction. Your goal is to predict the patient satisfaction score.</p>

We'll have two leaderboards: Public and Private.

The public leaderboard will calculate the RMSE based on 50% of the test set, while the private leaderboard will be based on the remaining 50%.

You can make up to 10 submissions per day. Participants will need to wait until the next UTC day after submitting the maximum number of daily submissions.

Also, you can select up to 2 submissions as the number of submissions eligible for the final private leaderboard. Users can hand-select the eligible submissions, or will otherwise default to the best public scoring submissions.

Good luck, and have fun!


<h2>Grading</h2>

<p>**The full grade for this competition will be worth 40 points**. Your final grade will depend on your** final RMSE**, i.e. in the RMSE **calculated using the complete test set (average of the RMSE obtained in the Public and in the Private Leaderboard)**. Notice that once you submit your predictions to the Kaggle platform, the resulting RMSE will be calculated using only half of the test set. So, make sure that you are paying attention to weather or not your predictive model is stable (i.e. presents low variance) when applied to new, unseen datasets.</p>

<p>Your grade will depend on your performance. You will receive a **full grade** (100%) if your final **RMSE is equal to or below 0.8795**. </p>

<p> If your final **RMSE is between 0.8795 and 0.8825** you'll receive **90% of the total grade** for the competition.</p>

<p> If your final **RMSE is between 0.8826 and 0.8910** you'll receive **70% of the total grade** for the competition.</p>

<p> If your final **RMSE is between 0.8911 and 0.8964** you'll receive **50% of the total grade** for the competition.</p>

<p> If your final **RMSE is between 0.8965 and 0.901** you'll receive **25% of the total grade** for the competition.</p>

<p> Finally, if your RMSE is above 0.902 you'll receive 0% of the total grade for the competition</p>

<h2>Data Set Information:</h2>

<p> The dataset provides patient reviews on specific drugs along with related conditions and a 10 star patient rating reflecting overall patient satisfaction. The data was obtained by crawling online pharmaceutical review sites. The original data was downloaded from the UCI Machine Learning repository here:</p>

<a href=""https://archive.ics.uci.edu/ml/datasets/Drug+Review+Dataset+%28Drugs.com%29""> Original Drug Review Dataset</a>

<h2>Important notes:</h2>

<p> When using this dataset, you agree that you

1) only use the data for research purposes

2) don't use the data for any commercial purposes

3) don't distribute the data to anyone else

4) cite the authors.</p>


<h2>Attribute Information:</h2>

<p>doc_id: ID of the patient</p>

<p> text: patient review</p>

<p>rating (numerical): the Z-score of the 10 star patient rating</p>

<p>Weekday: what was the day of the week when the review was posted</p>

<p>Month: what was the month when the review was posted</p>

<p>Year: what was the year when the review was posted</p>

<p>usefulCount (numerical): the Z-score of the number of users who found review useful</p>

<p> DataGroup: Does the review belongs to the ""Training"" or to the ""Testing"" sets? You'll need to train your models in the ""Training"" set, and generate predictions of ""rating"" for the ""Testing"" set. </p>
----------------------
# Evaluation

Grading

**The full grade for this competition will be worth 40 points**. Your final grade will depend on your** final RMSE**, i.e. in the RMSE **calculated using the complete test set (average of the RMSE obtained in the Public and in the Private Leaderboard)**. Notice that once you submit your predictions to the Kaggle platform, the resulting RMSE will be calculated using only half of the test set. So, make sure that you are paying attention to weather or not your predictive model is stable (i.e. presents low variance) when applied to new, unseen datasets.

Your grade will depend on your performance. You will receive a **full grade** (100%) if your final **RMSE is equal to or below 0.8795**.

If your final **RMSE is between 0.8795 and 0.8825** you'll receive **90% of the total grade** for the competition.

If your final **RMSE is between 0.8826 and 0.8910** you'll receive **70% of the total grade** for the competition.

If your final **RMSE is between 0.8911 and 0.8964** you'll receive **50% of the total grade** for the competition.

If your final **RMSE is between 0.8965 and 0.901** you'll receive **25% of the total grade** for the competition.

Finally, if your RMSE is above 0.902 you'll receive 0% of the total grade for the competition","# Dataset Description

