* `gfs_total_clouds_cover_low_next`:  next horizon cloud coverage (between horizons, divisible by 6) at low level, %
* `fact_temperature`: temperature, C [targets]


## Copyright 
This data is created starting from the available dataset used for the **Shift Competition**, held during the [NeurIPS conference 2021](https://neurips.cc/Conferences/2021/CompetitionTrack).
The original Shifts Weather Prediction Dataset is released under CC BY NC SA 4.0 license. This dataset was constructed by combining features from publicly available weather prediction services and models. Specifically, we combined data from NOAA/NWS servers, data generated by the WRF model from NCAR/UCAR, and data from the Meteorological Service of Canada. Ground station readings were taken from [NOAA](https://www.weather.gov/disclaimer). 
The data was further cleaned and subsampled to meet the requirements of the AML challenge.",True,True
5719,uwi-poisonous-mushorooms,"# Description

This dataset includes descriptions of hypothetical samples corresponding to 23 species of gilled mushrooms in the Agaricus and Lepiota Family Mushroom drawn from The Audubon Society Field Guide to North American Mushrooms (1981). Each species is identified as definitely edible, definitely poisonous, or of unknown edibility and not recommended. This latter class was combined with the poisonous one. Your job is to create a model that can predict wheter a mushroom is poisonous or edible.


## Acknowledgements

This dataset was originally donated to the UCI Machine Learning repository. You can learn more about past research using the data here.
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for the compeition is Accuracy.

## Submission Format

Submission file should have two columns id and class. The ids would have been retrieved from the test file and the class created from predicitions of the test dataset.


The file should contain a header and have the following format:
```
id,class
1,1
```","# Dataset Description

You have been given three files namely **mushroom_train.csv**,  **mushroom_test.csv** and lastly **mushroom_submission_sample.csv**. The mushroom_train.csv contains data on 6499 mushrooms. The class column has the information for the labels. 1 = poisonous, 0 = edible. The mushroom_test.csv has all the columns of the mushroom_train.csv file but does not include the class column. You are supposed to use your model to make predictions from the mushroom_test.csv file and the predicitions output to a csv of the same format as mushroom_submission_sample.csv file.

## Files

*   **mushroom_train.csv** - the training set
*   **mushroom_test.csv** - the test set
*   **mushroom_submission_sample.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format",True,True
5720,gdsc-data-science-track,"# Description

# this is First ML project 
You have been hired by Inn the Neighborhood, an online platform that allows people to rent
out their properties for short stays. Currently, the webpage for renters has a conversion rate of
2%. This means that most people leave the platform without signing up.
The product manager would like to increase this conversion rate. They are interested in
developing an application to help people estimate the money they could earn renting out
their living space. They hope that this would make people more likely to sign up.
The company has provided you with a dataset that includes details about each property
rented, as well as the price charged per night. They want to avoid estimating prices that are
more than 25 dollars off of the actual price, as this may discourage people.
You will need to present your findings in two formats:
1. You must submit a written report summarising your analysis to your manager. As a
data science manager, your manager has a strong technical background and wants
to understand what you have done and why.
2. You will then need to share your findings with the product manager in a 10 minute
presentation. The product manager has no data science background but is familiar
with basic data related terminology.","# Dataset Description


## Files

*   **ren.csv** - the training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set
*   **sub_simple.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format __ i made it's name wrong ?? 

## Columns

* id ==> Numeric, the unique identification number of the property
* latitude ==> Numeric, the latitude of the property
* longitude ==> Numeric, the longitude of the property
* property_type ==> Character, the type of property (e.g., apartment, house, etc)
* room_type ==> Character, the type of room (e.g., private room, entire home, etc)
* bathrooms ==> Numeric, the number of bathrooms
* bedrooms ==> Numeric, the number of bedrooms
* minimum_nights ==> Numeric, the minimum number of nights someone can book
* price ==> Character, the dollars per night charged ==> Target",True,True
5721,denoising-shabby-pages,"# Description

# **Your Challenge**
Your task is to denoise ¡°shabby¡± documents, reversing the effects imposed on them by [Augraphy](https://github.com/sparkfish/augraphy). There are several paths one might take to accomplish this goal, so feel free to experiment and find which leads to doom and which leads to glory.

![](https://miro.medium.com/max/1400/1*-Gif4QqHRoUotVE_AVvv6A.png)
### The task is simple: denoise shabby documents, like the right image, to make them look as clean as possible, like the left image.

# **Historical Significance**
Documents are exposed to harsh conditions once scribed and enter the world. Most of human history is captured in paper form, some going back thousands of years -- yet the knowledge they hold often degrades very quickly. Even in the modern world, as those documents are scanned, faxed, copied, and printed, there are many distortions that get introduced that obscure their original form. Those distortions, or ""noise"", prevent them from being captured in a more immortal, digital form. That is the problem we intend to solve, with your help.

Depart with us on the hero's journey, to chart the uncharted path that restores luster to that which was once lost.

# **Due Date**
The competition is set to end based on the accuracy of the top denoising model. The winner of the competition will be the first person with a denoising model with an RMSE value of less than 0.05. To train models with this accuracy, look at how you can create more data under the Data tab.

# **Rules**
All code submissions must be written in Python, but any library can be used
No online solutions-as-a-service may be used
Groups of up to 3 people are allowed

# **Augraphy Workshop**
Unlike most competitions, this one will feature a workshop that describes how Augraphy works and how you can use it to supercharge your submissions to improve your odds of winning. Once the competition receives more interest, we'll poll competitors on Slack and determine a date and time for the workshop. Check back here or in the Slack channel for the announcement!

# **Communication**
All official communication will be done through Slack.
[Join in the discussion](https://join.slack.com/t/denoisingshabby/shared_invite/zt-160i3x0c7-3mGB81~5M0mt2t0zcq2N_Q) happening right now on Slack to see how others are approaching a solution and to access other opportunities to learn!

# **Prizes**
The grand prize offered by our sponsors are [detailed separately¡­](https://denoising-shabby.com/prizes/)
We are awarding the author of the best write-up and code documentation with the chance to meet and collaborate with the Augraphy Team on the Augraphy paper, which will be submitted to global AI conferences.
We are also awarding 20 Augraphy T-shirts! 10 for the highest-scoring submissions on the leaderboard, and 10 for the most active participants in the Kaggle competition discussion.

# **Website**
All of this information and more is accessible at [https://denoising-shabby.com/](https://denoising-shabby.com/).

# **Shabby Pages**
The full Shabby Pages dataset is under active development, and you can help by participating in this competition! We'll release the full set later, but for now, you can grab the cropped set available on the Data page of this competition, or you can grab that and the full-size set from [here](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/12TtuZHgtTeFyJEhV3DECBXYFlYt4tGUC?usp=sharing).
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated on the root mean squared error between the pixel intensities of the cleaned images and the pixel intensities of the original images, where both sets of images have been grayscaled.

# Submission File
Form the submission file by grayscaling your cleaned images, assigning each image an ID number, and producing a row per pixel in each image, in the following format:

```
1_1_2,0
```

corresponding to the following data:
1. which picture the data belongs to (filename)
2. the pixel's row image
3. the pixel's column
4. the intensity of the pixel (0, black, to 1, white).


The file should contain a header and have the following format:
```
id,value
1_1_1,1
1_2_1,1
1_3_1,1
```
etc.

A sample submission file is provided in the competition data.
----------------------
# Augraphy

# **The Augraphy Project**


Augraphy is a Python library that creates multiple copies of original documents through an augmentation pipeline that randomly distorts each copy ¨C degrading the clean version into dirty and realistic copies rendered through synthetic paper printing, faxing, scanning, and copy machine processes.

The dataset created for the Denoising Shabby Pages competition was generated using Augraphy pipelines to create realistic old and noisy documents from ¡°born digital¡± sources. This simulation of realistic paper-oriented process distortions creates large amounts of training data for AI/ML processes to learn how to remove those distortions.

Fork and star [Augraphy](https://github.com/sparkfish/augraphy) on GitHub to help the team and improve the project!

[![GitHub forks](https://img.shields.io/github/forks/sparkfish/augraphy.svg?style=social&amp;label=Fork&amp;maxAge=2592000)](https://GitHub.com/sparkfish/augraphy/network/)
[![GitHub stars](https://img.shields.io/github/stars/sparkfish/augraphy.svg?style=social&amp;label=Star&amp;maxAge=2592000)](https://GitHub.com/sparkfish/augraphy)

![The Augraphy Project](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/sparkfish/augraphy/dev/images/logo/augraphy.png)

The ShabbyPages dataset is compiled through the Augraphy pipeline. If you would like to run your own documents through the pipeline to create your own training/testing data, we encourage you to do so! [You can access the pipeline here.](https://github.com/sparkfish/shabby-pages/blob/dev/shabbypipeline.py)

[You can use these clean documents to run through the pipeline.](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1NJhrxo5wyQnyPUKuRkIMOmcW7Zkd2J_0?usp=sharing)","# Dataset Description

You are provided 2 sets of images, train, and test. Each of the images in those two folders has a clean and shabby version, making it possible to train and test your own denoising model. These images are of documents synthetically noised with the [Augraphy](https://github.com/sparkfish/augraphy) library, which simulates real-world paper printing, faxing, scanning, and copy machine processes. Your task is to create an algorithm to remove the noise from images in the test set.

**Note:** You **MUST** use make-submission.py to produce your submission file.

## Files

*   **train/train_shabby** - The training data ran through the Augraphy pipeline.
*   **train/train_clean** - The training data ground truth.
*   **validate/validate_shabby** - The validation data ran through the Augraphy pipeline.
*   **validate/validate_cleaned** - The validation data ground truth.
*   **test/test_shabby** - The test data ran through the Augraphy pipeline.
*   **test/test_cleaned** - The test data ground truth.
*   **competition_images.zip** - The shabby versions of the solutions. Denoise these documents, run your solution through make-submission.py, and submit the CSV file.
*   **sample_submission.csv** - A sample submission file in the correct format.
*  **make-submission.py** - Change the cleaned_images_dir path in this file and run it to generate your submission file.",True,True
5722,asdfasdfasdfasdfasdfasdfasdf,"# Description

This iasdfasdfasdfasdfs the home page of the competition. You don't need a subtitle here. The competition sub-title will appear above. This is where you introduce the problem. You can upload images using the ""Insert Photo"" icon on the bar that displays above this textbox when in edit mode.

If you are copy-pasting from another application, like Word or your browser, you may need to clean up the markdown or html for things to display properly.

Here is a handy link to Kaggle's [competition documentation](https://www.kaggle.com/docs/competitions), which includes, among other things, instructions on [submitting predictions](https://www.kaggle.com/docs/competitions#making-a-submission).


## This is a subtitle
To format pages, stick to the following conventions:
- Inline `code` should be encapsulated by back-ticks \``. 
- Block code should have  \``` as the first and last line of the block to produce
```
# code to print hello
print('hello')
```
- Subtitles are prefixed with \##


You can display block equations using LaTeX encapsulated between `\[` and `\]`, and display inline encapsulated between `\(` and `\)` . Note that underscores should be escaped with a `\` for the LaTeX to render properly. For example, this:

\[ \epsilon = \sqrt{\frac{1}{n} \sum\_{i=1}^n (\log(p\_i + 1) - \log(a\_i+1))^2 } \]

is created by this:

`\[\epsilon = \sqrt{\frac{1}{n} \sum\_{i=1}^n (\log(p\_i + 1) - \log(a\_i+1))^2 }\]`


## Acknowledgements

We thank Professor Plum, Ph.D. for providing this dataset.
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation pages describes how submissions will be scored and how students should format their sasdffdsubmissions. You don't need a sub-title at the top; the page title appears above. For an example of a high quality evaluation page, see the 2013 KDD Cup page, which we have included below:

---

The evaluation metric for this competition is [Mean F1-Score](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/F-score). The F1 score, commonly used in information retrieval, measures accuracy using the statistics precision \( \text{p} \) and recall \( \text{r} \).

Precision is the ratio of true positives \(\text{tp}\) to all predicted positives \(\text{tp} + \text{fp}\). Recall is the ratio of true positives \(\text{tp}\) to all actual positives \(\text{tp} + \text{fn}\). The F1 score is given by:

\[ \text{F1} = 2\frac{\text{p} \cdot \text{r}}{\text{p}+\text{r}}\ \ \mathrm{where}\ \ \text{p} = \frac{\text{tp}}{\text{tp}+\text{fp}},\ \ \text{r} = \frac{\text{tp}}{\text{tp}+\text{fn}} \]

The F1 metric weights recall and precision equally, and a good retrieval algorithm will maximize both precision and recall simultaneously. Thus, moderately good performance on both will be favored over extremely good performance on one and poor performance on the other.

## Submission Format

**For every author in the dataset**, submission files should contain two columns: `AuthorId` and `DuplicateAuthorIds`. `DuplicateAuthorIds` should be a space-delimited list. Every `AuthorId` counts as its own duplicate, and every duplicate should be listed under each of its respective ids. For example, if you suspect author A, B, and C are the same, you should list `A,A B C`, `B,B A C`, `C,C A B`.


The file should contain a header and have the following format:
```
AuthorId,DuplicateAuthorIds
1,1
```","# Dataset Description

This page appears alongside the data files. Itasdfasdfasdfasdf describes?what files have been provided and?the format of each. There is no single format for this page that is appropriate for all competitions, but you should strive to describe as much as you can here. A little time spent describing the data here can save a lot of time answering questions later.

Participants should be able to answer these types of questions after reading the data description:

What files do I need?  
What should I expect the data format to be?  
What am I predicting?  
What acronyms will I encounter?

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format
*   **metaData.csv** - supplemental information about the data

## Columns

*   `example_id` - definition of example_id
*   `feature_1`?- definition of feature_1
*   etc.",False,True
