5764,cme250-final-project,"# Description

For the final project of CME250, you will be predicting whether patients will progress to Severe Dengue.

Patients come from 11 different dataset cohorts. For testing, we reserve two of those cohorts. Although it is not necessary, we recommend that in any validation (or cross validation), cohorts be separated.

We thank Yiran Liu for providing this dataset.
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is the Area Under the ROC Curve [AUC](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Receiver_operating_characteristic). 

## Submission Format

The submission file should be a csv that contains two columns: `SampleID` and `class`. `class` should be a probability, i.e., a number between zero and one.

The file should contain a header and have the following format:
```
SampleID,class
GSM1071039,0.79
```","# Dataset Description

We have two files, **train.csv** contains the training data which contains the feature and target of each observation, **test.csv** contains the features of the test data (but not the target, which you must predict).

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

## Columns

*   `class` - whether the patient progressed to Severe Dengue or not
*   `dataset`?- the dataset cohort used to collect this observation
*  `SampleID` - the ID for the observation",False,True
5765,110-2-uta-dl-hw3,"# Description

In homework, we are going to train models that can recommend hashtags based on the content of an image. We will use the dataset ""HARRISON"". Please refer to the paper (https://arxiv.org/abs/1605.05054) for more details about the dataset.

The original data of HARRISON can be downloaded here: https://drive.google.com/file/d/1B9NZf42J_GpslRNlTTAxvM5c9WRVl_1Z/view?usp=sharing. 

You can download the images and take a look, but this is not mandatory. We already extracted the image features, compressed them as ""harrison_features.npz"", and upload to Kaggle:
https://www.kaggle.com/kuantinglai/harrison-dataset


The training process is split into two stages: hashtag2vec training and image-to-word2vec training. Please refer to the notebook of this competition. The steps are
1. Run https://www.kaggle.com/kuantinglai/train-htag2vec-tf1
2. Download ""tag\_list\_word2vec\_64.npz"" (The number 64 may be changed, it depends on your vector dimension)
3. Run https://www.kaggle.com/kuantinglai/train-devise-w-ur-htag2vec, upload your  ""tag\_list\_word2vec\_64.npz""
4. Download ""harrison\_test\_predict.csv"" and submit

To evaluate the model performance, we use the mean Average Precision (mAP) @ 10. Enjoy the homework and Have fun!
----------------------
# Evaluation

We use mean Average Precision (mAP) to evaluate the performance. Please refer to 

<a href=""https://www.educative.io/answers/what-is-the-mean-average-precision-in-information-retrieval"">www.educative.io/answers/what-is-the-mean-average-precision-in-information-retrieval</a>

Or 

<a href=""https://darren1231.pixnet.net/blog/post/347752732-machine-learning-map"">MAPÅcÙYÁÏ™zË÷Ïµ½y</a>

for detailed explanation of Average Precision.","# Dataset Description

Please search and add the HARRISON dataset directly in your Kaggle notebook.

https://www.kaggle.com/kuantinglai/harrison-dataset",False,True
5766,dataquest-fe-se-r2,"# Evaluation

Evaluation
The evaluation metric for this competition is Mean Squared Error.
----------------------
# Description

It's the end of the world :/
The year is 2035. Climate change has destroyed the planet. A strong presence of oxygen has been discovered on a planet named Keruroid. On observing the planet through satellites, it is seen that many explosions occur here regularly with variable intensities. An expedition has been planned to explore these regions, but to ensure the safety and survivability of the expedition, we need to be able to predict the nature of these explosions. Given is the satellite data of Keruroid captured at different times of the day. Your job is to use this data to predict the intensity of explosions.

The fate of humanity lies in your hands. And kaggle GPUs.","# Dataset Description

File descriptions
train.csv - the training set
test.csv - the test set
sampleSubmission.csv - a sample submission file in the correct format
Labels
Latitude - Center of explosion pixel
Longitude - Center of explosion pixel
Brightness_t21 - Channel 21 brightness temperature of the explosion pixel measured in Kelvin
Brightness_t31 - Channel 31 brightness temperature of the explosion pixel measured in Kelvin
Scan - Along Scan pixel size: Scan and track reflect actual pixel size.
Track - Along Track pixel size: Scan and track reflect actual pixel size.
Date - Acquisition Date
Time - How long the explosion was
Region - Region of planet being observed
frp - Fire Radiative Power: Depicts the pixel-integrated fire radiative power in MW (megawatts).
Day/Night - Indicates Daytime/ Nighttime
Explosion Intensity - Value of intensity on a scale of 1-100",False,True
5767,used-car-price-prediction,"# Description

<p>This competition is an in-class competition for the course, ""Artificial Intelligence and Machine Learning (Spring 2022)"", in Renmin University of China.</p>
<p>The competition is to predict the prices for used cars.</p>
<h2>Acknowledgements</h2>
<p>The data was adapted from that of <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/austinreese/craigslist-carstrucks-data""> Used Cars Dataset </a>, which was constructed by scraping data on car sales from Craigslist.org every few months.</p> The current version of the data set is V10.
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is <a href=""https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Root-mean-square_deviation""> Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE)</a>.</p>
<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p>For <strong>every listing</strong> in the test dataset, submission files should contain two columns: <strong><i>id</i></strong> and <strong><i>price</i></strong>, i.e. the id and the predicted price for the used car in the listing.</p>
<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>id,price<br>10767,900<br>378247,8000<br>379910,60500<br>......</pre>","# Dataset Description

Craigslist is an American classified advertisements website with sections devoted to jobs, housing, for sale, items wanted, services, discussion forums, and so on. The Used Cars Dataset was constructed by scraping every used vehicle entry within the United States on Craigslist. It contains almost all relevant information that Craigslist provides on car sales including columns like price, condition, manufacturer, odometer, latitude, longitude, and 18 other categories.

## Files

*   **used_cars_train.csv** - the training set
*   **used_cars_test.csv** - the test set
*   **used_cars_sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

## Columns

*   `id` - a unique identifier for each row
*   `url`?- listing URL
*   `region`?- craigslist region
*   `region_url`?- region URL
*   `price`?- entry price
*   `year`?- year of vehicle
*   `manufacturer`?- manufacturer of vehicle
*   `model`?- model of vehicle
*   `condition`?- condition of vehicle
*   `cylinders`?- number of cylinders
*   `fuel`?- fuel type
*   `odometer`?- miles traveled by vehicle
*   `title_status`?- title status of vehicle
*   `transmission`?- transmission of vehicle
*   `VIN`?- vehicle identification number
*   `drive`?- type of drive
*   `size`?- size of vehicle
*   `type`?- generic type of vehicle
*   `paint_color`?- color of vehicle
*   `image_url`?- image URL
*   `description`?- listed description of vehicle
*   `state`?- state of listing
*   `lat`?- latitude of listing
*   `long`?- longitude of listing
*   `posting_date` - date of posting the listing",False,True
