5775,math-356-in-class-competition,"# Description

This Kaggle competition is an assignment from the course [MATH 356 - Statistical Methods in Machine Learning](https://blackboard.hamilton.edu/webapps/blackboard/content/listContentEditable.jsp?content_id=_1031824_1&course_id=_32358_1) taught in Spring 2022 at [Hamilton College](https://www.hamilton.edu/).  

As a student of MATH 356, you must create a Kaggle account to be able to submit a solution. I recommend that you use your personal email when you create a Kaggle account. In this competition, you will decide if a case is in class 1 or 0. Cases do not have any meaning. The dataset is simulated. You can use any binary classification algorithm we have learned in class. You are able to submit up to 20 solutions per day. 

Here is a handy link to Kaggle's [competition documentation](https://www.kaggle.com/docs/competitions), which includes, among other things, instructions on [submitting predictions](https://www.kaggle.com/docs/competitions#making-a-submission).


## Acknowledgements

We thank [Professor Jeff Heaton from Washington University in St. Louis](https://sites.wustl.edu/jeffheaton/) for [his clear explanation](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rtzUHuGOZ7o&t=162s) of setting up a Kaggle competition. 

