5822,test-ml-in-physics-uofa-2022,"# Description

In many subsurface engineering applications, water is injected underground. One example is
geothermal energy extraction: cold water is injected at one spot, and hot water is produced from
another. Other examples include oil/gas reservoir development or solution mining processes. The
physical process is generally complex, involving highly nonlinear relationships among different
variables. Here, we simplify the problem by considering only single-phase water in a setting with
very low compressibility flow and constant temperature. A simple way to look at it is that this is
a water injection and production problem in a sandstone reservoir.

The task of this competition is to create a data-driven model to predict the flow rate of 4 specific
producers: 19, 20, 24 and 25 for the 365 days. The inputs are the drilled location¡¯s static
information and the pressure time series.

We will use Mean Absolute Error (MAE) to evaluate the performance of the submitted
prediction results and rank the best-performed models.","# Dataset Description

This problem describes a water injection and production problem in a sandstone reservoir. In each
reservoir, 61 wells (36 water injectors and 25 producers) are located following a 5-spot pattern. Water is
injected and produced since time zero, and the first 365 days of the data are recorded. 

The data for 10 different reservoirs are provided: 9 for training and 1 for testing. In the train/test folder,
each subfolder contains three .csv files, &quot;well_info.csv&quot;, &quot;rate_and_pressure.csv&quot; and &quot;expected_ouput.csv&quot;
""rate_and_pressure.csv"" and ""well_info.csv"" are the input, while ""expected_output.csv"" is the output you are trying to achieve. 

 &quot;well_info.csv&quot; contains the drilled location&#39;s static (not varying with time) information: x-
coordinate, y-coordinate, porosity, permeability, and formation thickness.

 &quot;rate_and_pressure.csv&quot; contains the time-series data, including each well&#39;s flow rate and
bottom hole pressure. The data is given on a daily basis over the 365-day duration.

""expected_output.csv"" contains Producer 19, 20, 24 and 25's production rate presented in a time series format with 365 timesteps (similar to 365 timesteps of the input). 

Due to Kaggle limitations, you have to ""flatten"" your output into two columns. Please refer to ""sample_submission.csv"" to understand the required format. The file contains 365 x 4 = 1460 entries, where each entry is in the format [injector #]-[timestep], [predicted flow rate].",False,True
