5874,challenge-datascience-wind-turbine-cs,"# Description

# Welcome to the 2022 CS Data Challenge


This year the challenge takes place on the Kaggle In-Class platform. It provides some really interesting features which should help you to work from home. 

It allows you to : 
- work as a team
- access data
- code online (notebook or scripts)
- submit your predictions

We will of course guide you for your first steps on that platform.

## Data context

We will dive into wind turbines world in order to predict more accurately the wind power production of turbines in terms of kW. The data was measured with sensors inside the turbines. The idea here is to determine a solution to predict a production (in kW) based on values measured by sensors within the wind turbines.",# Dataset Description,False,True
5875,horse-racing-prediction,"# Description

The competition is simple: use machine learning to create a model that predicts which horse will win each race.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Goal
It is your job to predict which horse will win the race.
For each in the test set, you must predict a 0 or 1 value for the variable.

Metric
Your score is the horses that you predicted correctly. This is known as accuracy.","# Dataset Description

The data has been split into two groups:

training set (train.csv) 
test set (test.csv)

The training set should be used to build your machine learning models. For the training set, we provide the outcome (also known as the ¡°ground truth¡±) for each Race. Your model will be based on ¡°features¡±. You can also use feature engineering to create new features.

The test set should be used to see how well your model performs on unseen data. For the test set, we do not provide the ground truth for each Race. It is your job to predict these outcomes. For each Race in the test set, use the model you trained to predict which horse will win the race.",False,True
5876,health-impact-classification,"# Description

In this challenge, you use NLP to build understanding on how any business impacts our lives, and more specifically our health.

## Context

The Upright project enables smarter decision-making for investors, companies, employees and governments by quantifying the net impact of companies. Companies exist to create value, but use resources. Net impact considers both the positive and negative impacts in 19 categories. Currently Upright model covers over 14k companies of which more than 700 are openly available on [uprightplatform.com](https://uprightplatform.com/).
 
Upright evaluates the impact of companies based on the products and services sold. One key method in evaluating the impact of different products is Bibliographic Causality Analysis (BCA). A simple way to implement BCA is to answer questions like: ""Out of all the articles that discuss tobacco and cancer, how many suggest that tobacco has a negative effect towards cancer? How many suggest a positive effect?"". The main data source for BCA is over 180M scientific articles.


## Task

In this NLP challenge, you implement BCA by classifying impacts of different products on one important impact dimension: physical diseases.

## Results and Rewards

If the winning team beats Upright's existing classification model, their solution may be adjusted and applied as part of the Upright Model and have a real world impact on how thousands of companies are ranked by investors.
----------------------
# Evaluation

The task is evaluated with [Macro averaged F1-Score](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/F-score). The F1 score, commonly used in information retrieval, measures accuracy using the statistics precision. The macro averaged score is computed by taking the arithmetic mean of all the per-class F1 scores.

Precision is the ratio of true positives to all predicted positives. Recall is the ratio of true positives to all actual positives. For a single class, te F1 score is given by:

`F1 = 2 * precision * recall / (precision + recall)`

The F1 metric weights recall and precision equally, and a good retrieval algorithm will maximize both precision and recall simultaneously. Thus, moderately good performance on both will be favored over extremely good performance on one and poor performance on the other. Macro averaging the score over different classes emphasises performance in the smaller classes over overall performance. 

## Submission Format

For every row in the test set, the submission file should contain two columns: `id` and `effect`. The `effect` column should contain the predicted class (`0`, `1`, or `2`) of the test sample with the corresponding id from the `test.csv`.

The file should contain a header and have the following format:
```
id,effect
1,0
```","# Dataset Description

## Overview

The training set includes 4000 articles from the CORE research database and wikipedia articles, together with product and impact terms of interest.

The samples have been classified in three categories:`1` (positive effect), `2` (negative effect) and `0` (other). Positive label means that the abstract suggests that the product or service has a positive effect on human health with regard to the impact term in question. This can mean that the product prevents the health issue (represented by the impact term), or treats it. A negative label means that the article implies that the product or service causes or contributes to the health issue. The ¡®other¡¯ label means that neither of the above is the case, and the article either implies nothing about a causal effect between the product and the health issue, or it implies no causation.

## Example

Let¡¯s examine at one training sample:

`productTerm: Tobacco`

`healthIssue: Cancer`

`title: Tobacco use and cancer causation: association by tumour type`

`text: In the second part of our review we describe the association between tobacco use and risk of specific cancer types. There is evidence for an established association of tobacco use with cancer of the lung and larynx, head and neck, bladder, oesophagus, pancreas, stomach and kidney. In contrast, endometrial cancer is less common in women who smoke cigarettes. There are some data suggesting that tobacco use increases the risk for myeloid leukaemia, squamous cell sinonasal cancer, liver cancer, cervical cancer, colorectal cancer after an extended latency, childhood cancers and cancer of the gall bladder, adrenal gland and small intestine. Other forms of cancer, including breast, ovarian and prostate cancer, are unlikely to be linked to tobacco use`

`effect: 2`


The article clearly suggests that tobacco has negative effects on health by causing cancer. While it also suggests possible positive effects, it is labeled as negative in this single label training set. 

## Files

The data is provided in csv format and includes the following files

- **train.csv** - the training set
- **test.csv** - the test set
- **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format


## Columns

- `id`
- `productTerm` - a term representing the product or service whose effect is being considered
- `healthIssue` - a term representing the health issue being considered
- `effect` - a classification of the causation between `productTerm` and `healthIssue` as either `1` (positive), `2` (negative) or `0` other.
- `title` - Title of the article
- `text` - Abstract or extract from the article
- `wikiPageId`- Wikipedia page number (only present for wikipedia page extracts)
- `doi` - Digital Object Identifier of the article, if any
- `oai`- Open Archives Initiative identifier, if any
- `year` - Year of the article, if any",False,True
