5908,the-bridge-ml-1,"# Description

Hello The bridgers , time to get ready to know which kind of pollutant the european countries are expelling, keep this mapping in mind when making a submission:
Carbon dioxide (CO2):1
Nitrogen oxides (NOX):0
Methane (CH4):2
----------------------
# Evaluation


The evaluation metric for this competition is [Mean F1-Score](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/F-score). The F1 score, commonly used in information retrieval, measures accuracy using the statistics precision \( \text{p} \) and recall \( \text{r} \).","# Dataset Description

This dataset contains data on industrial facilities and emissions across Europe.
The dataset will consist of the following variables of interest:

countryName: Country in which the facility is located

EPRETRSectorCode: Code of the sector in which the company specialises

eptrSectorName: Name of the sector in which it specialises

EPRTRAnnexIMainActivityCode: Code of the specialisation within the sector in which they operate

EPRTRAnnexIMainActivityLabel: Specialisation within the sector in which they operate

FacilityInspireID: Building identifier

facilityName: Name of the building in which the activity takes place

City: City in which the facility is located

CITY ID: ID to confirm location

targetRelease: Type of polluter to study

pollutant: Type of pollutant emitted (Target variable). In order to follow the same standard, you must encode this variables as follows:

pollutant	number
Nitrogen oxides (NOX)	0
Carbon dioxide (CO2)	1
Methane (CH4)	2
DAY: Day on which the report is made

MONTH: Reporting month

reportingYear: Reporting year

CONTINENT: Continent on which the company is located

max_wind_speed: Maximum wind speed

avg_wind_speed: Average wind speed

min_wind_speed: Minimum wind speed

max_temp: Maximum temperature

avg_temp: Average temperature

min_temp: Minimum temperature

DAYS WITH FOG: Total days of the month recorded in the area

REPORTER NAME: Reporter's name",False,True
5909,electric-transmission-imagery,"# Description

The Electric Power Research Institute (EPRI) is building a community to advance utility infrastructure inspection.  To jumpstart collaboration EPRI is sharing labeled datasets.  These data may support immediate and future research.  Advances in automated inspection workflows can benefit society.  Future tools may revolutionize traditional workflows and have safety, quality, and efficiency benefits to utilities and consumers.  More situational awareness of eclectic utility infrastructure can reduce the likelihood of in-service equipment failures.

Visually inspecting high voltage electrical infrastructure is complex.  There are several components and areas of interest each with unique degradation modes.  Automated inspection technologies may require large datasets to handle the real-world complexities.  It is very likely these datasets do not exist in 2022. EPRI is addressing the data gap by creating and sharing datasets to support research and commercial innovation.  

The **Transmission H-frame Dataset 1.0** dataset contains 12,158 drone collected images of one transmission structure type, an ""H-frame"" structure type. 11,483 have been labeled and split 8,038 for public training and 3,445 for withheld testing.
These images can be obtained by completing the form below.
https://forms.office.com/r/7uuD1putv1 

##Data Description
![2](https://publicstorageaccnt.blob.core.windows.net/web-photos/Slide2.JPG)
![3](https://publicstorageaccnt.blob.core.windows.net/web-photos/Slide3.JPG)
![4](https://publicstorageaccnt.blob.core.windows.net/web-photos/Slide4.JPG)
The **Transmission H-frame Dataset 1.0** dataset contains 12,158 drone collected images of one transmission structure type, an ""H-frame"" structure type.  11,483 have been labeled and split 8,038 for public training and 3,445 for withheld testing.<br>**These images can be obtained by completing the form below.**
https://forms.office.com/r/2Qy0n4NnVA

After the form is complete, an email is generated containing hyperlinks to the image datasets.  The full resolution (5280x2970) imagery, as well as, lower resolution versions will be shared.  The imagery has been labeled with seven classes.  Five of these are bounding boxes, and two are polylines defined by points.  Each class contains metadata denoting obscuration, truncation, or both.  


Some image pixels have been obscured to limit personally identifiable information.  Features include faces, licenses plates, company logos, and other objects based on labeler discretion.  EPRI requests that if you identify an image that you think contains sensitive information, please email AI@epri.com with subject ""Sensitive Image, H_Structure_Vx, Hframe(xxxxx).jpg"".  Should any edits be made, EPRI will update the dataset accordingly. 
![PII Obscure Example](https://publicstorageaccnt.blob.core.windows.net/web-photos/PII_Example.jpg)

Additionally, the GPS information has been translated in an attempt to anonymize the location.  Relative position information between each image remains.  The code used for the GPS translation can be found here, https://github.com/pkulkarni-epri/DistributionTaxonomy/blob/main/manipulate_exif.py
![GPS Translation](https://publicstorageaccnt.blob.core.windows.net/web-photos/GPS_Translation.jpg)

## Acknowledgements

The electric utilities that support EPRI's research project 1-114205, AI for Transmission Images.
----------------------
