5929,detecting-generated-scientific-papers,"# Description

## Description

This competition is a part of the shared task hosted within the third workshop on Scholarly Document Processing ([SDP 2022](https://sdproc.org/2022/)), being held in association with the 29th International Conference on Computational Linguistics ([COLING 2022](https://coling2022.org/)).

## Competition report 

**Yury Kashnitsky, Drahomira Herrmannova, Anita de Waard, George Tsatsaronis, Catriona Catriona Fennell, and Cyril Labbe. 2022. [Overview of the DAGPap22 Shared Task on Detecting Automatically Generated Scientific Papers](https://aclanthology.org/2022.sdp-1.26/). In Proceedings of the Third Workshop on Scholarly Document Processing, pages 210¨C213, Gyeongju, Republic of Korea. Association for Computational Linguistics.**

There are increasing reports that research papers can be written by computers, which presents a series of concerns (e.g., see [1]). In this challenge, we explore the state of the art in detecting automatically generated papers. We frame the detection problem as a binary classification task: given an excerpt of text, label it as either human-written or machine-generated. We provide a corpus of over 5000 excerpts from automatically written papers, based on the work by Cabanac et al. [2], as well as documents collected by Elsevier publishing and editorial teams. As a control set, we provide a 5x larger corpus of openly accessible human-written as well as generated papers from the same scientific domains of documents. We also encourage contributions that aim to extend this dataset with other computer-generated scientific papers, or papers that propose valid metrics to assess automatically generated papers against those written by humans.

## Data

Check out the [Data](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/detecting-generated-scientific-papers/data) tab for the description of the provided data. The competition allows the use of external data, so feel free to augment the training set with generated content coming from generator/summarizer/paraphrazer and other Deep Learning models.

## Baseline

Check out the [Tf-Idf & logistic regression baseline](https://www.kaggle.com/code/kashnitsky/fake-papers-tf-idf-logistic-regression-baseline/) for the task in hand.

## Rules

Check out the [Rules](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/detecting-generated-scientific-papers/rules) tab, in particular, it mentions the SDP workshop timelines. 


## Acknowledgements

We thank Cyril Labb¨¦, Basile Dubois-Binnaire, Guillaume Cabanac, and Alexander Magazinov for their input in the ideation phase of the task preparation. 

## Links
[1] Holly Else. (2021). [""'Tortured phrases' give away fabricated research papers.""](https://www.nature.com/articles/d41586-021-02134-0) Nature.
[2] Guillaume Cabanac, Cyril Labb¨¦, and Alexander Magazinov. (2021). [""Tortured phrases: A dubious writing style emerging in science. Evidence of critical issues affecting established journals.""](https://arxiv.org/abs/2107.06751)
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is [Mean F1-Score](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/F-score). The F1 score, commonly used in information retrieval, measures accuracy using precision and recall. 

Precision is the ratio of true positives TP to all predicted positives TP + FP. Recall is the ratio of true positives TP to all actual positives TP + FN. The F1 score is given by:

F1 = 2 p r / (p + r), where p = TP / (TP + FP), r = TP / (TP + FN).

The F1 metric weights recall and precision equally, and a good retrieval algorithm will maximize both precision and recall simultaneously. Thus, moderately good performance on both will be favored over extremely good performance on one and poor performance on the other.

## Submission Format

**For every paper excerpt in the dataset**, submission files should contain two columns: `id` and `fake`. `id` is a list of test set excerpt IDs and can be copied from the provided **sample_submission.csv**. `fake` is a binary vector, accepted values are 0 and 1. 

The file should contain a header and have the following format:
```
id,fake
0,1
3,0
```

The submission file can be prepared the following way
```
import pandas as df
subm_df = pd.read_csv(PATH_TO_SAMPLE_SUB.csv, index_col='id')
subm_df['fake'] = 1 # change to an array with your model predictions
subm_df.to_csv('subm.csv')
```","# Dataset Description

The data provided for this competition is structured in a simple way. There's a text excerpt from a scientific paper and an indication of whether this text is ""fake"" (probably generated) or real, i.e. human-written. The data comes from both published and retracted Scopus papers. 

## Files

*   **fake_papers_train_part_public.csv** - the training set (5327 records)
*   **fake_papers_test_public.csv** - the test set (21310 records)
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format (21310 records)

## Columns

*   `text` - text input, excerpt from a scientific paper
*   `fake`?- whether the text is generated or not, i.e. 1 stands for the generated content and 0 stands for human-written content (only present in the training set)",False,True
5930,adams-sose22,"# Description

## Overview
The learning goal of ADAMS is to familiarize you with recent developments in deep learning and to enable you to use the corresponding methodology for solving real-world problems. In accordance with this objective, the evaluation of the module is based on an assignment.

Your submission will consist of a Jupyter notebook that includes all steps that we typically encounter in a machine learning pipeline (explanatory data analysis, data preparation, parameter tuning, etc.). Grading of the notebook will be based on several criteria including:
- the quality of the exposition (e.g., a healthy mix of prose, code, comments, math. formalism)
- the organization of the notebook 
- the scope of your analysis
- whether you have solved the task in a sound and rigorous manner

The final assignment is hosted as a Kaggle in-class competition. The main purpose of this setting is to give you the possibility to interactively query the quality of your modeling pipeline by submitting the model predictions to the online leaderboard. You may submit up to 5 entries daily to track the quality of your solution on unseen test data. Please remember that the actual accuracy of your solution is not the main factor that will determine your grade.


## Modeling task
Airbnb is an online marketplace for accommodation rental founded in 2008. Properties hosted on the Airbnb platform are owned by private companies and individuals. The hosts determine the rental price themselves and pay a service fee to Airbnb.

In this assignment, you will predict property prices for Airbnb listings in London. You will be provided with data on more than 55.000 properties containing the listing attributes (e.g., location, number of rooms, property type), texts with the listing description, and rental price per night.

Using a broad range of NLP techniques, you will need to build an NN-based model that predicts the price of listings in the test set based on both text and tabular data. If you wish to further refine the accuracy of your model, you will have access to additional data containing the listing reviews and property images.

Please refer to other sections of the competition page to download the data and read more about competition rules, evaluation and data description.

## Deadline
The submission deadline is the 9th of September 2022
----------------------
# Evaluation

## Evaluation Metric

Your submissions will be evaluated by computing [Root Mean Squared Error](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Root-mean-square_deviation) between the actual property prices and submitted price predictions.


## Submission File

You should submit your predictions in `.csv` format. For every `listing_id` in the test set, you must predict the price of the corresponding listing per night. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

```
listing_id, price
0FEMC4VA5U, 100.00
PQ2BYEO0QR, 100.00
3FP6WFHUBN, 100.00
...
```

### Sample Submission

A demo notebook that illustrates how to create a submission will be made available asap.","# Dataset Description

# Data partitioning

The data has been randomly partitioned into a training set and a test set. You should use the labeled training set for developing your models and validating their quality. Next, you should use the developed models to predict the property prices for the listings in the unlabeled test set.

To prevent overfitting, the test set is divided into a public and a private subset. The leaderboard score of your submission is computed on a private part of the test set. Once the competition is finished, you will see a private score of your submissions.


# File descriptions

The data consists of three `.csv` files containing different information:

- **train.csv**: training set with listing features, description and prices
- **test.csv**: test set with listing features and description
- **reviews.csv**: [optional] reviews for listings in both training and test set

# Data fields

Files `train.csv` and `test.csv` have the same columns except for the `price` variable:

- `listing_id`: unique property identifier
- `price`: **[target variable]** property price per night in US dollars
- `summary`: **[text]** listing summary
- `description:` **[text]** listing description
- `space`: **[text]** space description
- `neighborhood_overview`: **[text]** neighborhood description
- `picture_url`: **[optional]** URL to the property image

As indicated above, the text data is provided in several columns devoted to different topics. You will need to think whether to merge them into a single text or consider separately in order to improve your solution.

The remaining columns contain numeric/categorical features with the listing attributes such as location, host information, reviewer scores and more. You would need to perform some data preprocessing and think how to incorporate the tabular data into your modeling pipeline.


# Optional additional data

The data on property reviews in `reviews.csv` and property images in `picture_url` is optional. You may use it to further refine the quality of your solution but may also choose to ignore it.

### Reviews

Data dictionary for `reviews.csv`:

- `listing_id`: unique property identifier
- `review_id`: unique review identifier
- `reviewer_id`: unique reviewer identifier
- `comments`: **[text]** review

Reviews can be matched with `train.csv` by `listing_id`. You would need to think how to deal with the following challenges:

- reviews may come in different languages
- listings have a different number of reviews
- aggregating signal from multiple reviews


##  Images

The column `picture_url` in `train.csv` contains links to image properties that can be used to extract additional information. You would need to think how to deal with the following challenges:

- writing a script to loop through the URLs and download images
- incorporating signal from image data in your pipeline",False,True
5931,predict-the-weekly-price-of-a-product,"# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is simple RSME. 

All we want to know how different approaches can reduce the overall prediction errors, despite being high volatility in the price trend.
----------------------
# Description

## Description

Sales (or Revenue) prediction is central to managing demand and inventory for a product that any company is manufacturing. Prediction allows managers to optimize manufacturing decisions, which leads to a business economics (profitability).

Currently, there exists 100s of models from simple moving averages to variants of ARIMA as well as advanced deep learning for making predictions. But when high volatility is seen in the price movements, it becomes difficult for more algorithms to efficiently make predictions for future movements

But it's possible to create better models that can outperform those currently in use. In this competition, we would like to see how the different competitors compete to build some models better than the existing ones","# Dataset Description

## Files

*   **TimeSeriesData.csv** - the training set consisting of date and price for a product

## Columns

