5946,nlp1000-ml-challenge,"# Description

For your final programming assessment for NLP1000, you are to tackle a sentiment classification task using Tweets from the Philippines.

## Data Background
The data used for this challenge was originally collected via Twitter's Streaming API and the period of collection was from March 2020 to December 2020. The original data was about 17 million tweets.

The same dataset was used by my CS-ST advisees (Co, Custodio, Dela Cruz, Sanchez) in their thesis, entitled *Sentiment Classification of Tweets from the Philippines with Emoji-based Data Annotation*. To adhere to Twitter Development Policies, we re-crawled the tweets around the middle of 2021 and this resulted in the re-collection of about 9 million tweets.

From the 9 million tweets, the group then extracted all tweets with emojis, which was around 2.5 million tweets. For each tweet, they extracted each emoji's respective sentiment score based on the Emoji Sentiment Ranking ([see here for more information](http://kt.ijs.si/data/Emoji_sentiment_ranking/)). They then summed the scores and created labels based on summed sentiment scores. Anything above zero was labeled as positive, while anything below zero was labeled negative. 

Additionally, for the sake of creating an assessment that isn't too taxing on your available computing, the original 2.5 million labeled tweets was further reduced by 82% (2.5m -> 454k). Of the 454k, 20% was dedicated to testing, while 80% was dedicated to training. The training data was then split with 20% of heading to a validation set and the remaining forming the training data (i.e. training.csv). Once the three data splits were made, the classes (i.e. positive, negative) were balanced. The validation and testing sets were then combined into one file (i.e. testing.csv), where all validation documents were labeled for public usage in this Kaggle competition and all testing documents were labeled private. Scores on the private / test set will be released after the deadline.

## Dataset Considerations
- Please note that the labeling technique is not perfect, so please don't expect to get a high score when participating in the challenge.
- The data for training and testing has been balanced. Usage of accuracy for the testing should be sufficient because of the data balancing.
- When loading in your data, please consider loading in the csv files as such
```
import pandas as pd
import csv

data_df = pd.read_csv('path/to/file.csv', quoting=csv.QUOTE_ALL, encoding=""utf-8"", index_col=0)
```
- Other considerations are outlined in the Canvas assessment page (i.e. no usage of dummy learners, pipeline constraints, etc.)
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is [Accuracy](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Accuracy_and_precision). While F1 Score might be more used, Accuracy is acceptable for our dataset given the validation and test sets are balanced.

The accuracy of a list of predicted labels is computed as such

$$\text{Accuracy} = \frac{\text{TP} + \text{TN}}{\text{TP} + \text{TN} + \text{FP} + \text{FN}} $$

## Submission Format

**For every author in the dataset**, submission files should contain two columns: `DocumentId` and `IsPositive`. DocumentId is a unique identifier for each individual tweet. IsPositive is a Boolean value (i.e. True or False). True signals a document is positive in its sentiment, while False signals that a document is negative in its sentiment.

When exporting your predictions, please ensure the output file is labeled as ""submission.csv"". You're also encouraged to use the following code for generating the CSV file if you have the predictions via a DataFrame:
```
import pandas as pd
import csv
df.to_csv('submission.csv', quoting=csv.QUOTE_ALL)
```

The file should contain a header and have the following format:
```
""DocumentId"", ""IsPositive""
""1"",""True""
```","# Dataset Description

A brief description of the data is found in the overview. The following describes the files present and the columns included.

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set (also includes a validation set; validation -> public scores, test -> private scores)
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

## Columns

*   `DocumentId` - unique identifier of a tweet
*   `Text`?- the text of the tweet
*   `IsPositive` - a Boolean value describing whether a tweet is positive (i.e. True) or negative (i.e. False)",False,True
