5964,formation-dsfdx-bouigues-mnist,"# Description

<h2>MNIST Formation DSFDX: Description</h2>
<p>MNIST (""Modified National Institute of Standards and Technology"") est le ? hello world ? de la vision par ordinateur. Il s'agit d'un dataset d'images manuscrites de chiffres, datant de 1999 et ayant servi de benchmark ¨¤ de nombreux algorithms de computer vision.</p>
<p>Dans ce TD, l'objectif est d'identifier correctement les chiffres ¨¤ partir d'un ensemble de donn¨¦es de dizaines de milliers d'images manuscrites.</p>
<h2>Remerciements</h2>
<p> L'ensemble de donn¨¦es est mis ¨¤ disposition sous une licence <a href=""https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/"">Creative Commons Attribution-Share Alike 3.0</a>.</p> 
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>Le but de ce concours est de d¨¦terminer le chiffre repr¨¦sent¨¦ sur une image manuscrite.</p>
<p>Pour chaque ¨¦l¨¦ment de l'ensemble de test, vous devez pr¨¦dire le bon label.</p>
<hr>
<h2>M¨¦trique d'¨¦valuation</h2>
<p>La performance de l'algorithme est ¨¦valu¨¦e suivant la <a href=""https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Confusion_matrix""> justesse (accuracy)</a> des pr¨¦dictions (le pourcentage d'images correctement identifi¨¦).</p>
<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p>Le fichier texte soumis doit contenir un en-t¨ºte et avoir le format suivant :</p>
<pre>id,label<br>1,0<br>2,7<br>3,1<br>etc.<br></pre>
<p>Avec : </p>
<ul>
<li>id : Identifiant de l'image</li>
<li>label : Label attribu¨¦ ¨¤ l'image (entre 0 et 9)</li>
</ul>","# Dataset Description

<p>Les fichiers de donn¨¦es mnist_train.csv et mnist_test.csv contiennent des images en niveaux de gris de chiffres dessin¨¦s ¨¤ la main (0 ¨¤ 9).</p>
<p>Chaque image mesure 28 pixels de hauteur et 28 pixels de largeur, pour un total de 784 pixels au total. Chaque pixel est associ¨¦ ¨¤ une seule valeur de pixel, indiquant la luminosit¨¦ ou l'obscurit¨¦ de ce pixel (le niveau de gris), les nombres les plus ¨¦lev¨¦s traduisant une plus forte obscurit¨¦. Cette valeur de pixel est un entier compris entre 0 et 255 (inclus).</p>
<p></p>
<p>L'ensemble de donn¨¦es d'entra?nement (mnist_train.csv) comporte 785?colonnes. La premi¨¨re colonne, appel¨¦e ""label"", est le chiffre qui a ¨¦t¨¦ dessin¨¦ par l'utilisateur. Le reste des colonnes contient les valeurs de chaque pixel de l'image associ¨¦e.</p>
<p>Ces colonnes associ¨¦es aux pixels portent un nom tel que ""pixelx"", o¨´ x est un entier compris entre 0 et 783 (inclus). La position d'un pixel sur l'image est indiqu¨¦ par la valeur de x, suivant une num¨¦rotation de l'image qui partirait d'en haut ¨¤ gauche, se d¨¦placerait de gauche ¨¤ droite, et passererait ¨¤ la ligne inf¨¦rieure une fois la derni¨¨re colonne atteinte.</p>
<p>Par exemple, pixel30 indique le pixel qui se trouve dans la troisi¨¨me colonne en partant de la gauche et la deuxi¨¨me ligne en partant du haut, comme dans le diagramme ci-dessous:</p>
<pre>000 001 003 ... 027<br>028 029 030 ... 055<br>etc<br>756 757 758 ... 783<br></pre>
<p></p>
<p>Le fichier de test (mnist_test.csv) comporte 785?colonnes. La premi¨¨re colonne, appel¨¦e ""id"", est l'identifiant de l'image ¨¤ cat¨¦goriser. Le reste des colonnes contient les valeurs de chaque pixel de l'image associ¨¦e. Il n'y a pas de 'label' puisque le but est de d¨¦terminer celui-ci.</p>
<h2>Format soumission</h2>
<p>Le fichier texte soumis doit contenir un en-t¨ºte et avoir le format suivant (cf. mnist_answer_sample.csv) :</p>
<pre>id,label<br>1,0<br>2,7<br>3,1<br>etc.<br></pre>
<p>Avec : </p>
<ul>
<li>id : Identifiant de l'image</li>
<li>label : Label attribu¨¦ ¨¤ l'image (entre 0 et 9)</li>
</ul>",False,True
5965,Covid19-Death-Predictions,"# Description

There have been more than half a billion cases of COVID-19 cases resulting in more than 6 million deaths. The goal of this competition is to predict next week's deaths to hopefully provide insight into epidemiology.  We believe this problem is also a great way to practice data science skills.

Here is a handy link to Kaggle's [competition documentation](https://www.kaggle.com/docs/competitions), which includes, among other things, instructions on [submitting predictions](https://www.kaggle.com/docs/competitions#making-a-submission).

You may see [important dates](https://fraser.stlouisfed.org/timeline/covid-19-pandemic) about COVID-19 pandemic.

You may get more information about [COVID-19](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/COVID-19).

We will be sharing the notebook that we used to create the dataset after the competition ends for use by anyone interested.

## Acknowledgements

We thank Our World in Data for providing the datasets that are used to create the competition.
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is [RMSE](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Root-mean-square_deviation). 

The Root Mean Square Error or Root Mean Square Deviation is the square root of the Mean Square Error(MSE). Mean Square Error is the sum of squares of differences between predictions and true values, divided by the number of prediction/true values.

 RMSE = \sqrt{\frac{\sum_{1}^{^{T}} (y_{true}-y_{prediction})^{2}}{T}}

## Submission Format

Submission files should contain two columns `Id` and `Next Week's Deaths`.

The submission file should contain a header.
----------------------
# Timeline

**- 08/19/2022 11:00 PM UTC** - Final Submission Deadline
**- 08/19/2022 11:01 PM UTC** - Private Leaderboard Release Date","# Dataset Description

## Overview 

The data contains cases, deaths, and vaccinations information about a region in a week. The weeks do not necessarily start with Monday.

The timeframe for the data is between the 27th of January 2020 to the 30th of June 2022 for feature columns, and between the 3rd of February 2020 to the 7th of July 2022 for the ""Next Week's Deaths"" column.

## What am I predicting?

Participants are predicting the next week's deaths for a region.

## Important notes

The rows containing non-available(n/a) data for the ""Next Week's Deaths"" column have been removed from the dataset. However, there are still rows containing zero value in the ""Next Week's Deaths"" column.

There are many n/a values in feature columns in both train and test datasets, so it is definitely worth checking into.

Month and day part of the date has been removed from datasets to prevent leakage.

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

## Columns

Id => Unique id for each row

Location => Name of the country/region/group of people

Year  => Year of the start of the week is in

# Cases
Weekly Cases  => Weekly new confirmed cases of COVID-19. Counts can include probable cases, where reported. In rare cases where the source reports a negative daily change due to a data correction, the metric is set to n/a.

Weekly Cases per Million => ""Weekly Cases"" per 1,000,000 people in the total population of the location

# Deaths
Weekly Deaths => Weekly new deaths attributed to COVID-19. Counts can include probable deaths, where reported. In rare cases where our source reports a negative daily change due to a data correction, the metric is set to n/a.

Weekly Deaths per Million => ""Weekly Deaths"" per 1,000,000 people in the total population of the location

Next Week's Deaths => Weekly Deaths for the next week. Counts can include probable deaths, where reported

# Vaccinations

Total Vaccinations => Total number of doses administered. For vaccines that require multiple doses, each individual dose is counted. If a person receives one dose of the vaccine, this metric goes up by 1. If they receive a second dose, it goes up by 1 again. If they receive a third/booster dose, it goes up by 1 again

People Vaccinated => Total number of people who received at least one vaccine dose. If a person receives the first dose of a 2-dose vaccine, this metric goes up by 1. If they receive the second dose, the metric stays the same

People Fully Vaccinated => Total number of people who received all doses prescribed by the initial vaccination protocol. If a person receives the first dose of a 2-dose vaccine, this metric stays the same. If they receive the second dose, the metric goes up by 1

Total Boosters => Total number of COVID-19 vaccination booster doses administered (doses administered beyond the number prescribed by the initial vaccination protocol)

Daily Vaccinations => New doses administered per day (7-day smoothed). For countries that don't report data on a daily basis, we assume that doses changed equally on a daily basis over any periods in which no data was reported. This produces a complete series of daily figures, which are then averaged over a rolling 7-day window

Total Vaccinations per Hundred => ""Total Vaccinations"" per 100 people in the total population of the location

People Vaccinated per Hundred => ""People Vaccinated"" per 100 people in the total population of the location 

People Fully Vaccinated per Hundred => ""People Fully Vaccinated"" per 100 people in the total population of the location

Total Boosters per Hundred => ""Total Boosters"" per 100 people in the total population of the location

Daily Vaccinations per Hundred => ""Daily Vaccinations"" per 100 people in the total population of the location

Daily People Vaccinated => Daily number of people receiving a first COVID-19 vaccine dose (7-day smoothed).

Daily People Vaccinated per Hundred => ""Daily People Vaccinated"" per 100 people in the total population of the location",False,True
