5967,start-ml-dl-track,"# Description

Feel free to use any framework and instruments you like above all or find most appropriate.

This competition is based on data collected by mini-course ""Start in ML"" participants. There are 49 classes of people.
Images are B&W, all in .png format.

Here is a handy link to Kaggle's [competition documentation](https://www.kaggle.com/docs/competitions), which includes, among other things, instructions on [submitting predictions](https://www.kaggle.com/docs/competitions#making-a-submission).


## Acknowledgements

We thank all online-course participants for active involvement, incl. distributed dataset collection. And Evgeny Sokolov for various support.
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is Accuracy. 

## Submission Format

**For every image in the dataset**, submission file should contain two columns: `img_name` and `pred_class`. `img_name` should be person's picture title (for instance, image1.jpg). Every `pred_class` should be a class prediction for corresponding image.

The file should contain a header and have the following format:
```
img_name,pred_class
image1.png,1
image2.png,1
.
.
.
image22059.png,1
```","# Dataset Description

## Files

*   **DL_track_train.zip** - the training set (images with specific filename: name contains true label after '_' sign)
*   **DL_track_test.zip** - the test set (only images)
*   **sample_submission_DL.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format",False,True
5968,mesio-summer-school-2022,"# Description

<p>Welcome to the <strong>MESIO Summer School 2022</strong> competition.</p>

<p>This contest will be used to evaluate the performance of the models developed by the students of the course <strong>""Applied Machine Learning to solve Real Life Problems""</strong>.</p>

<p>A <strong>rating (classification) model</strong> should be constructed on the variable ""TARGET"" that takes values 0 (not default) or 1 (default, which is bankruptcy status after 1 year). Each column represents a characteristic (ratio) of the company and can contain missing values. There are both numerical and categorical variables.</p>

<p>The output will be a default probability (it does not need to be calibrated, only the sorting matters).</p>
----------------------
# Evaluation

<h2>Evaluation Metric</h2>
<p> The metric will be the <strong>area under the ROC curve: <a href=""https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Receiver_operating_characteristic#Area_under_the_curve""> AUC </a> </strong></p>
<p>It measures the quality of a sorting (which in our case will be given by a value between 0 and 1, a probability) with respect to a binary variable, so that the optimum will be to assign the lowest probabilities to the non-defaults (TARGET = 0) and the highest odds for default (TARGET = 1). A random rating (or probability allocation) takes a value of 0.5, and a perfect rating will score 1 (or 0 if the probabilities are reversed).</p>
<p>Important: Usually, the <strong>AR ""Accuracy Ratio"" metric is also used, which is the area under the <a href=""https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cumulative_accuracy_profile"">CAP</a> curve  </strong> (also known as GINI or Somers' D).</p>

<p> The relationship is the following:
\[ AR = 2 ¡¤ AUC-1 , \]
so that, if we have an AUC of 94%, it is equivalent to an IP or GINI of 88%. </p>

<p><strong>Note:</strong> This metric goes from -100% to 100%, with 0 being a random model, while in AUC the random is 50%.</p>


<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p><strong>The submission file must contain two columns: </strong> ""ID"" and ""Pred"", so that ""ID"" will be a string that identifies the company and ""Pred"" will be your PD prediction. </p>

<p>The file must have a ""header"" and come with the following csv format:</p>
<pre>ID,Pred<br>A2,0.590668280319669<br>A5,0.714666990126745<br>...<br>
A5909,0.0744845547164769<br></pre>


<p>50% of the submission file is selected for the ""Public"" Leaderboard, while the other 50% is for the ""Private"" Ranking, being the latter the one that will determine the competition results. Each participant will be asked, at the end, to choose 5 submissions in order to qualify for the final results.</p>
----------------------
# Course Evaluation

<h2>Course Evaluation Requirements</h2>
<p> In order to be evaluated you will need to <strong>upload at least one submission with a Leaderboard position better than the ""Benchmark""</strong> (an in-class submission made by the professor with a simple publicly available code).</p>
<p><strong>All students have to upload an executable code</strong>, which produces their Leaderboard submission, via a Notebook in Python 3 or R. There is also the option to send the .py or .r file directly to the professor.</p>
<p> <strong>The final Leaderboard standings and the delivered code will determine the course scores</strong> (the assignation will be discussed in class).</p>","# Dataset Description

<h2>Provided Data</h2>
<p><strong>Train</strong> and <strong>test</strong> files and an example of <strong>submission</strong> with random default probabilities (""Pred"" column).</p>
<p>To train the model, use the <strong>train</strong> file: The variable to predict (""TARGET"" column) is the 12-month default from the observation point of the data. 0 is healthy, 1 is default. Each line represents a company. The predictors have not been previously chosen or treated and there can be missing values.</p>

<p>To make predictions, take the <strong>test</strong> data, which no longer has the ""TARGET"" column, but the names of the predictors are exactly the same.<p>

<p>Finally, for each ""ID"" of the <strong>submission</strong> there is a corresponding ""ID"" in the <strong>test</strong> set. You must put the default probability in the ""Pred"" column (a real number between 0 and 1). It is not necessary the calibration of the probability, only the sorting matters.</p>

<h2>File Descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>train.csv</strong> - the training set</li>
<li><strong>test.csv</strong> - the test set</li>
<li><strong>submission_sample.csv</strong> - a sample submission file (with random predictions) in the correct format</li>
<li><strong>Dataset Description.txt</strong> - text file with the description of the data</li>",False,True
5969,ai200-may22-lendingclub-loan-default,"# Description

<img src=""https://encrypted-tbn0.gstatic.com/images?q=tbn:ANd9GcSctD44gMy0L_11BdiQhMYLE4Lr_SiIt9ALCVrX5mb61U0FSeFtgFIm9MQ6Pz62NebC2Q&usqp=CAU"" style=""margin:0"">
<br>
<h2>Welcome, students of <a href='https://heicodersacademy.com/'>Heicoders Academy</a>'s AI200 course!</h2>
<br>
<p>For this competition, imagine that you work for the LendingClub company, which is headquartered in San Francisco, California. As the world's largest online loan marketplace, LendingClub specialises in facilitating personal loans, business loans, and financing of medical procedures.</p>

<p>When LendingClub receives a loan application, the company has to make a decision for loan approval based on the applicant¡¯s profile. Two types of risks are associated with the bank¡¯s decision:</p>

<ul>
<li>If the applicant is likely to repay the loan, then not approving the loan results in a loss of business to the company</li>
<li>If the applicant is not likely to repay the loan, i.e. he/she is likely to default, then approving the loan may lead to a financial loss for the company.</li>
</ul>


<p>Like most other lending companies, extending loans to ¡®risky¡¯ applicants is the largest source of financial loss. Each year, the financial industry loses billions of dollars due to loan defaults. This is why your Machine Learning team has been entrusted with the important task of mitigating this costly risk.</p><br>

<p><b>The goal of this competition? Work with your team to build the optimal model that best predicts which loans are likely to be defaulted.</b> All the best, folks!</p><br>

<p>Please note:</p>
<ul>
<li>You must compete in the same teams as previously registered at the start of the course.</li>
<li>Individual submissions will be invalidated.</li>
</ul>
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>Submissions are judged on <a href=""http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Receiver_operating_characteristic"">area under the ROC curve</a>.</p>
<hr>
<h2>Submission File</h2>
<p>Each line of your submission should contain an Id and a prediction of the probability that the loan application will be defaulted. Your submission file must have a header row. The file should have the following format:</p>
<pre>Id,Predicted
0,0.3
1,0.8
2,0.9
...
78236,0.1
</pre>
----------------------
# Timeline

* 2 July 2022 (Saturday) - You must make your first team submission by this deadline.
* 7 July 2022 (Thursday) - Final Submission deadline.

All deadlines are at 9:00 PM SGT on the corresponding day unless otherwise noted. The organizers reserve the right to update the contest timeline if they deem it necessary.","# Dataset Description

<h2>File description</h2>

<ul>
<li><b>lc_trainingset.csv</b> - the training set (dataset you will use for training & evaluation)</li>
<li><b>lc_testset.csv</b> - the test set (only used for generating predictions for the leaderboard ranking)</li>
<li><b>sample_submission.csv</b> - a sample submission file in the correct format</li>
</ul>

<h2>Column descriptions</h2>

<table border=""1"" class=""dataframe"">
  <thead>
    <tr style=""text-align: right;"">
      <th>Column Name</th>
      <th>Description</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>loan_amnt</td>
      <td>The listed amount of the loan applied for by the borrower. If at some point in time, the credit department reduces the loan amount, then it will be reflected in this value.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>term</td>
      <td>The number of payments on the loan. Values are in months and can be either 36 or 60.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>int_rate</td>
      <td>Interest Rate on the loan</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>installment</td>
      <td>The monthly payment owed by the borrower if the loan originates.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>grade</td>
      <td>LC assigned loan grade</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>sub_grade</td>
      <td>LC assigned loan subgrade</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>emp_title</td>
      <td>The job title supplied by the Borrower when applying for the loan.*</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>emp_length</td>
      <td>Employment length in years. Possible values are between 0 and 10 where 0 means less than one year and 10 means ten or more years.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>home_ownership</td>
      <td>The home ownership status provided by the borrower during registration?or obtained from the credit report.?Our values are: RENT, OWN, MORTGAGE, OTHER</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>annual_inc</td>
      <td>The self-reported annual income provided by the borrower during registration.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>verification_status</td>
      <td>Indicates if income was verified by LC, not verified, or if the income source was verified</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>issue_d</td>
      <td>The month which the loan was funded</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>loan_status</td>
      <td>Current status of the loan</td>
    </tr>
