5974,hyperspectral-classification-ii,"# Description

Welcome to the Hyperspectral Image Classification Challenge Phase 2. Here, you will have a chance to apply any techniques, deep learning, convolution neural network, and more to a hyperspectral image!

## What is Hyperspectral Imaging?
Hyperspectral imaging collects and processes information from across the electromagnetic spectrum. Typically, light is collected in the visible, near infrared, and short-wave infrared. That is 400 nanometers to 2500 nanometers. Your cell phone collects light from 400 nanometers to 700 nanometers, for example, as a band of blue, a band of green and a band of red (i.e., 3 bands). However, Hyperspectral data consists of hundreds of narrow bands, not just three. This allows each pixel in an image to have, not just 3 values (RGB), but hundreds of values, narrowly spaced. Thus we can have a ""spectrum"" for each pixel. We can think of each pixel as having a unique spectral finger print unique to that pixel. The spectrum is directly related to the material content in the pixel. The goal of hyperspectral imaging is to obtain this spectrum for each pixel in an image with the purpose of finding objects, identifying materials, detecting processes, performing change detection, etc. The sensor systems that collect this type of data (usually in the form of a ""cube"", x-spatial, y-spatial, z-spectral) can be complicated and are very different from your typical camera phone of SLR professional camera.

Excerpt from https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hyperspectral_imaging

##What is This Pixel?
We challenged you to build a model that can classify each pixel into an appropriate category. There are three possible categories for each pixel. (1) Red object, (2) Blue object, (3) Background. You can use any methods to come up with the highest ranking in this competition!

##Important!
Please keep in mind that currently that we are in **Phase 2**, so the ground truth is based on the `train_without_leak.csv` and `test_without_leak.csv` with **three possible classes** ((1) Red object, (2) Blue object, (3) Background) for the output.

##Acknowledgement
We would like to thanks Dr. Emmett J. Ientilucci for the dataset provided to use in this competition.
[Dr. Emmett J. Ientilucci (ientilucci)](https://www.rit.edu/directory/ejipci-emmett-ientilucci)
Associate Professor | [Graduate Admissions](https://www.rit.edu/study/imaging-science-phd) Chair
