4. Please try this hack without any external help, if found copied then the completion certificate of the summer school will be issued.
5. For any other issue  email me (prashant.singh@thapar.edu).

----------------------
# Evaluation

## The evaluation metric

- The evaluation metric for this competition is [Accuracy](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Accuracy_and_precision#In_binary_classification).

## Submission Format

- **Submission file contain two columns:** `id` and `target`.
- **`id`**: Don't change it.
- **`target`**: You need to predict it, it can be either 0 or 1.
- The submission file should contain a header and have the following format:
```
id,target
nc3dea071da1944b,1
n22d95133dce7449,0
ndb9c52980cd9476,1
```","# Dataset Description

*   **train_data.csv** - Train File
*   **test_data.csv** - Test File
*   **sample_submission.csv** - Submission File | [Click here](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1eVQaOu7FkBXEryyCRofpVeTbd8IqBSl_/view?usp=sharing) to download",False,True
5976,artistic-head-detection,"# Description

Computer vision models may not be good art critics, but they certainly can be trained to be good art recognizers. In this competition, we ask you to build an object detection model for identifying heads. The twist? None of the heads are real! Rather than photos of real life, each image is a painting, print, or sketch from public domain artwork. 

**This dataset was created by [Scale Rapid](https://scale.com/rapid)**, the fastest annotation platform to help you obtain production-quality labels with no data minimums. We support a wide range of use cases, including classification, transcription, semantic segmentation, polygon image annotations, and more. **To get started, visit [this link](https://scale.com/kaggle) to receive $250 of free labeling credits!**

Each head is at least 50px wide and 50px tall, and labelers were instructed to ignore heads where no part of the face is visible (i.e. only the back of the head is visible to the viewer). Your training data will include bounding box information for all the human heads in the dataset, and for the test set you will be asked to predict the number of human heads without the bounding boxes given to you.

Here is a handy link to Kaggle's [competition documentation](https://www.kaggle.com/docs/competitions), which includes, among other things, instructions on [submitting predictions](https://www.kaggle.com/docs/competitions#making-a-submission).


## Acknowledgements

We thank the New York Metropolitan Museum of Art for supplying the original images for this dataset
----------------------
# Evaluation

For simplicity, the evaluation metric for this competition is root mean square error, or [RMSE](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Root-mean-square_deviation). RMSE is a common regression metric that penalizes the deviance from ground truth for both overestimates and underestimates. For each of the ids in the test csv (which correspond to the images in the test folder), you must output a prediction for the number of human heads larger than 50px by 50px and not facing away from the viewer that are present in the image.

## Submission Format

**For every image in the test folder**, submission files should contain two columns: `Id` and `guess`. 

The file should contain a header and have the following format (see `sample_submission.csv` for a more in-depth example):
```
Id,guess
034abc,1
```","# Dataset Description

The `train/` and `test/` folders each contain the image files where the filename is the unique id. 

`train_with_boxes.csv` contains an entry for every image in the `train/` folder, where each entry has three columns: `id`, `num_human_heads`, and `boxes`.
The columns contain the following info:

`id` corresponds to the filename in the `train/` folder

`num_human_heads` is the number of heads matching the criteria (at least 50x50 and at least partially facing the viewer) in the image

`boxes` is a list of bounding boxes, where each bounding box has the format `(x_min, x_max, y_min, y_max)` that specify the pixel coordinates of the box, measured from the upper left hand corner of the image (e.g. the upper left corner of the box has the coordinates `(x_min, y_min)` and the bottom right corner is `(x_max, y_max)`).

`test.csv` contains an entry for every image in the `test/` folder, and contains only the `id` column without boxes or number of heads.

`sample_submission.csv` shows what your submission should look like. (The numbers in the `guess` column were generated uniformly at random from the integers 0-20 so probably won't be that helpful for you!)",False,True
5977,linux-kernel-size,"# Description

# Linux Kernel Size Challenge

Predict the size of a Linux Kernel based on its options ! 

## Context - The Linux Kernel

<img src=""https://ensiwiki.ensimag.fr/images/a/af/Tux.png"" alt=""drawing"" style=""width:250px;""/>

The [Linux](https://www.linux.org/) kernel has been an open initiative for 30 years and is the basis for most modern operating system distributions: [Debian](https://www.debian.org/), [Fedora](https://getfedora.org/en/), [Ubuntu](https://ubuntu.com/download), etc.
Linux kernels are used in a wide variety of systems, ranging from [embedded devices](https://www.seeedstudio.com/blog/2021/01/20/beginners-guide-to-embedded-linux/), cloud services to [powerful supercomputers](https://itsfoss.com/linux-runs-top-supercomputers/). 
Many of these systems have strong requirements on the kernel size due to constraints such as limited memory or instant boot. 
To match these requirements, users can choose to [activate configuration options of the kernel](https://tldp.org/HOWTO/SCSI-2.4-HOWTO/kconfig.html) - more than [15,000 options in the last versions](https://www.slideshare.net/acher/transfer-learning-across-variants-and-versions-the-case-of-linux-kernel-size/9). 
Depending on the chosen options, the generated kernel will have different functionalities and sizes. 
Obtaining a suitable trade off between kernel size, functionality and security is an extremely hard problem.

## Practice skills

- Regression techniques
- Creative feature engineering and feature selection

## Goal

In this challenge, the goal is to accurately **predict the size of a Linux kernel** based on its activated options. 

Several objectives:
1. **Train the best model** predicting the size of the Linux Kernel 
2. **Find the list of options** leading to the best results
3. **Build additional features** improving the predictive model

Thanks for contributing!

## Contact

If you want any additional information, please send a mail to <A HREF=""mailto:luc.lesoil@irisa.fr"">luc.lesoil@irisa.fr</A>

## Acknowledgements

This dataset is the result of a collaboration with members of the [DiverSE team](https://www.diverse-team.fr/), namely [Mathieu Acher](https://www.mathieuacher.com/), [Hugo Martin](https://github.com/HugoJPMartin), [Juliana Alves Pereira](http://www.inf.puc-rio.br/blog/professor/juliana-alves-pereira/), [Arnaud Blouin](https://people.irisa.fr/Arnaud.Blouin/), [Jean-Marc J¨¦z¨¦quel](https://people.irisa.fr/Jean-Marc.Jezequel/), [Djamel Eddine Khelladi](http://people.irisa.fr/Djamel-Eddine.Khelladi/) and [Olivier Barais](https://olivier.barais.fr/). 
You can reuse it for your own projects.

If you want to quote this dataset in a research article, please use the following bibtex entry :

```
@techreport{acher:hal-02314830,
  TITLE = {{Learning Very Large Configuration Spaces: What Matters for Linux Kernel Sizes}},
  AUTHOR = {Acher, Mathieu and Martin, Hugo and Pereira, Juliana Alves and Blouin, Arnaud and J{\'e}z{\'e}quel, Jean-Marc and Khelladi, Djamel Eddine and Lesoil, Luc and Barais, Olivier},
  URL = {https://hal.inria.fr/hal-02314830},
  TYPE = {Research Report},
  INSTITUTION = {{Inria Rennes - Bretagne Atlantique}},
  YEAR = {2019},
  MONTH = Oct,
  PDF = {https://hal.inria.fr/hal-02314830/file/LinuxSizePrediction.pdf},
  HAL_ID = {hal-02314830},
  HAL_VERSION = {v1},
}
```

----------------------
# Evaluation

Any regression model or attempt to predict the size of the model will be appreciated, even if it does not beat the best model.
Besides the implementation of new models or the search of hyperparameters, two types of contributions are also encouraged :
- those proposing a feature selection step, the goal being to find the best list of features to feed the machine learning model
- those creating new synthetic features prior to the training to improve the model

## Metric

We evaluate the results of the final model on the test set with the Mean Absolute Percentage Error (MAPE), defined by the following formula:

$$MAPE = \frac{100\%}{n}*\sum_{i=1}^n \left\lvert{\frac{y{i}-?{i}}{y{i}}}\right\rvert$$

where:
- n is the size of the test set
- **y** is the real Linux Kernel size distribution
- **?** is your prediction of the Linux Kernel size distribution

## How to submit

The resulting estimations of your model should be stored in a *submission.csv* file, with an *Id* column  (string) corresponding to the configuration ids of the test set and a *Predicted* column (double) for the estimation of the related Kernel size.

A notebook with a simple Decision Tree model is proposed as an example [here](https://www.kaggle.com/code/lllmmm45/simple-decision-tree)","# Dataset Description

## Description

Our dataset is composed of a unique table. 
The table lists 92,562 configurations of the Linux Kernel (=row) with their 9,467 kernel options (=column). 

- These 9467 kernel options are our explaining variables. We encoded them as boolean features. Each has its own id - see the following picture.
The value of each column is set to 1 if the related kernel option is activated in the configuration and to 0 if the option is deactivated. We also provide the list of kernel options in the *options.json* file. 

![test](https://github.com/TuxML/size-analysis/blob/master/output-figs/data2.png?raw=true)

- The last column **vmlinux** contains the size of the generated kernels (in bytes), our variable of interest. 

![test](https://github.com/TuxML/size-analysis/blob/master/output-figs/data1.png?raw=true)

## Training & Test

The dataset is separated into a training set *train.csv*  (70% of the configurations) and a test set *test.csv* (30% of the configurations).
The model should be trained using the training set and tested on the test set.

## Load the data

We provide an example of code loading the training and test datasets once they are in the notebook directory.

df_train is the training set and df_test the test set.


    import numpy as np
    import pandas as pd
    import json

    # load the options
    with open(""../input/linux-kernel-size/options.json"",""r"") as f:
        linux_options = json.load(f)

    # load csv file by setting options as int8 to save memory

    df_train = pd.read_csv(""../input/linux-kernel-size/train.csv"", dtype={f:np.int8 for f in linux_options}, index_col = 0)

    df_test = pd.read_csv(""../input/linux-kernel-size/test.csv"", dtype={f:np.int8 for f in linux_options}, index_col = 0)


    # for the train set, X_train is the dataframe of explaining variables...
    X_train = df_train[linux_options]

    # ... and y_train the variable of interest, the kernel size vmlinux for the training set
    y_train = df_train[""vmlinux""]

    # for the test set, X_test is the dataframe of explaining variables...
    X_test = df_test[linux_options]

    # Now it is your turn...
    # Your goal is to estimate y_test, the kernel size vmlinux for the test set
    # Have a good challenge!",False,True
5978,hackathon-28-czerwca,"# Description

## Can we predict if a visitor, in a session, will add item(s) to the cart?
The [Google Merchandise Store](https://www.googlemerchandisestore.com/shop.axd/Home) sells Google branded merchandise, a real ecommerce store. Using obfuscated Google Analytics data on each visitor session, you will use BigQuery ML to try and build a model that predicts whether each session will result in the visitor performing an action to add an item to the cart.

## Where's the data?
The data is split into two public tables on BigQuery: You will use the **training data** to build your model, and **testing data** to make your predictions using your model. 
Go to the [data](../data) page for more information on the tables.

## How to create your first model and make your submission:

### Step 1. 
Launch one of the notebooks below on AI Platform Notebooks:

- <a href=""https://console.cloud.google.com/ai-platform/notebooks/deploy-notebook?name=Online%20Retail%20Store%3A%20Predicting%20Visitor%20Behaviour%20-%20Starter%20Notebook%20%5BPython%5D&download_url=https%3A%2F%2Fstorage.googleapis.com%2Fstarter_notebooks%2Fgoogle_analytics_sample_bqml_python.ipynb&url=https%3A%2F%2Fgist.github.com%2Fpolong-lin%2F51bb01bac82513294e10fb9856c4fdb0"" target=""_blank""><img src=""https://cloud.google.com/images/products/ai/ai-solutions-icon.svg"" alt=""AI Platform Notebooks"">Starter Notebook [Python]</a>  
- <a href=""https://console.cloud.google.com/ai-platform/notebooks/deploy-notebook?name=Online%20Retail%20Store%3A%20Predicting%20Visitor%20Behaviour%20-%20Starter%20Notebook%20%5BR%5D&download_url=https%3A%2F%2Fstorage.googleapis.com%2Fstarter_notebooks%2Fgoogle_analytics_sample_bqml_r.ipynb&url=https%3A%2F%2Fgist.github.com%2Fpolong-lin%2F568c0984abdf169c91d475da1ddb3489"" target=""_blank""><img src=""https://cloud.google.com/images/products/ai/ai-solutions-icon.svg"" alt=""AI Platform Notebooks"">Starter Notebook [R]</a> Note: Select ""R"" under ""Advanced Options"" > ""Environment"".

If you'd like to only use SQL instead, you can follow the [instructions on using the BigQuery Console UI](https://www.kaggle.com/c/online-retail-store-20201008/overview/can-i-just-use-the-bigquery-console-ui)

### Step 2. 
Upload your CSV submission file to Kaggle via ""Submit Predictions""

----------------------
# Evaluation

## Goal
It is your job to predict for each visitor session, **whether it will result in addedToCart (1) or not (0). Your predictions should be a probability between 0 and 1.0**.

## Metric
Submissions are evaluated on [AUC](https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course/classification/roc-and-auc), which is a number between 0 and 1, where 0 means 100% of the predictions are wrong and 1 means 100% of the predictions are correct. Randomly guessing should give a score of 0.5.

## Submission File Format
The file should contain a header and have the following format:
```
sessionId,addedToCart
121212121212,0.30
3434343434,1.0
...
```
You can download an example submission file (csv) on the [Data page](../data).

----------------------
# How Do I Evaluate My BigQuery ML Model

# How do I evaluate my BigQuery ML model, before making predictions?

To evaluate your model after training completes, run:
```
SELECT
  *
FROM
  ML.EVALUATE(MODEL bqmlretail.MY_MODEL_NAME)
```
Hint: Look at the **AUC** metric to compare performance between your models.

## How does ML.EVALUATE work if I never split the data?
When you use CREATE MODEL, BigQuery ML automatically splits your training data into its own training/test data so that you can evaluate the performance of the model immediately when the model has finished training. 

_For more information on how BigQuery ML automatically splits your training data, read the [docs here on the `DATA_SPLIT_METHOD` parameter](https://cloud.google.com/bigquery-ml/docs/reference/standard-sql/bigqueryml-syntax-create#data_split_method) when using CREATE MODEL._

----------------------
# How Can I Improve My Model?

# How can I improve my predictions? My rank is not going up.
### Feature selection
- try selecting the features that you think are most important to the model

### Model selection and hyperparameter tuning
- have you tried choosing different classification models? have you tried tuning some of its hyperparameters? Check out the docs on each of the models below:
 - [`logistic_reg`](https://cloud.google.com/bigquery-ml/docs/reference/standard-sql/bigqueryml-syntax-create)
 - [`boosted_tree_classifier`](https://cloud.google.com/bigquery-ml/docs/reference/standard-sql/bigqueryml-syntax-create-boosted-tree)
 - [`dnn_classifier`](https://cloud.google.com/bigquery-ml/docs/reference/standard-sql/bigqueryml-syntax-create-dnn-models)
 - [`automl_classifier`](https://cloud.google.com/bigquery-ml/docs/reference/standard-sql/bigqueryml-syntax-create-automl)

### Feature engineering
- could the day of week have an impact on purchasing patterns? use the [EXTRACT](https://cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/reference/standard-sql/timestamp_functions#extract) function to retrieve the year/month/day/hour/minute/etc. from a TIMESTAMP column
- can you multiple, divide, add, or subtract numerical features to create new features?
- can you augment the weight of outliers by squaring numerical features, or on the other hand, taking the square root?
- could combinations of features impact purchasing patterns more than individual columns? You can use [ML.FEATURE_CROSS](https://cloud.google.com/bigquery-ml/docs/reference/standard-sql/bigqueryml-preprocessing-functions#mlfeature_cross) to create interactions on category variables 
- would it help to scale quantitative variables using [ML.STANDARD_SCALER](https://cloud.google.com/bigquery-ml/docs/reference/standard-sql/bigqueryml-preprocessing-functions#mlstandard_scaler)?
- are numerical features too granular? Could you bucketize them using [ML.QUANTILE_BUCKETIZE](https://cloud.google.com/bigquery-ml/docs/reference/standard-sql/bigqueryml-preprocessing-functions#quantile_bucketize)?
- check out all of the [pre-processing features built into BigQuery ML](https://cloud.google.com/bigquery-ml/docs/reference/standard-sql/bigqueryml-preprocessing-functions)


### Ensembling models
- the combination of multiple model outputs may be better than any single model alone. As a simple example, you can try to take the mean/median of the probability outputs of ML.PREDICT across multiple models. For example, for a specific fullVisitorId, if model_1 and model_2 predict 0.5 and 1.0 respectively, then the averaged prediction would be 0.75.

```
## Ensemble two models (model_a, model_b) by averaging their predictions
WITH pred1 AS (
  SELECT
    sessionId, 
    prob as addedToCart
  FROM
    ML.PREDICT(MODEL bqmlretail.model_a, ## replace model name
      (
      SELECT 
        CONCAT(fullVisitorId, CAST(visitStartTime as string)) as sessionId, 
        *
      FROM
      `kaggleworkshops.google_analytics_sample_eu.test`
    )),
  UNNEST(predicted_addedToCart_probs)
  WHERE label = 1),
  
  pred2 AS (
  
  SELECT
    sessionId, 
    prob as addedToCart
  FROM
    ML.PREDICT(MODEL bqmlretail.model_b, ## replace model name
      (
      SELECT 
        CONCAT(fullVisitorId, CAST(visitStartTime as string)) as sessionId, 
        *
      FROM
      `kaggleworkshops.google_analytics_sample_eu.test`
    )),
  UNNEST(predicted_addedToCart_probs)
  WHERE label = 1)

SELECT
  sessionId,
  AVG(addedToCart) addedToCart
FROM (
  SELECT
    *
  FROM pred1
  UNION ALL
  SELECT
    *
  FROM pred2
  )
GROUP BY sessionId

----------------------
# Error: Unsupported Column Date?

# I got an error: _Unsupported column date: model {} doesn't support TIMESTAMP data type._
The `date` column is a TIMESTAMP column, which some BigQuery ML models (e.g. `boosted_tree_classifier`, `dnn_classifier`) do not accept. You will need to manually extract out the values you'd like as separate features. 

Here's how to extract various date-time features from a TIMESTAMP column:
```
CREATE OR REPLACE MODEL bqmlretail.xgb_extract_time
TRANSFORM(
  EXTRACT (dayofweek FROM date) AS dayofweek,
  EXTRACT (day FROM date) as day,
  EXTRACT (month FROM date) as month,
  EXTRACT( hour FROM TIMESTAMP_SECONDS(visitStartTime) ) as hour,
  addedToCart
OPTIONS(
  model_type='boosted_tree_classifier', 
  INPUT_LABEL_COLS=[""addedToCart""]) AS
SELECT 
  *
FROM
  `kaggleworkshops.google_analytics_sample_eu.train`
```

Check the [EXTRACT](https://cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/reference/standard-sql/timestamp_functions#extract) documentation for more info.

----------------------
# Can I Just Use The BigQuery Console UI?

Yes! You will only need to know SQL to use the BigQuery Console UI. Follow the instructions below:

## Create your first BigQuery ML model

### Step 0:
Open the [BigQuery Console](https://console.cloud.google.com/bigquery).

### Step 1:
First, create a dataset in BigQuery called `bqmlretail` in the `EU` region. If you create it in a different region, Step 2 will not work.
![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F3399668%2F23bd8fee4e4828a49bf36bab54f74141%2Finstructions_createbqdataset.png?generation=1594202939078531&amp;alt=media)



### Step 2:
Run the CREATE MODEL query below. It should take ~1 min to train.

```
CREATE OR REPLACE MODEL bqmlretail.logreg_sample
TRANSFORM(
  LOWER(country) country_lower,
  totalPageviews,
  addedToCart
)
OPTIONS(
  model_type='logistic_reg', 
  INPUT_LABEL_COLS=[""addedToCart""]) AS
SELECT 
  *
FROM
  `kaggleworkshops.google_analytics_sample_eu.train`
WHERE date BETWEEN TIMESTAMP(""2016-08-01"")
  AND TIMESTAMP(""2016-08-31"")
```

## How to create your submission file
### Step 3. Run this code
Using your model (e.g., `your_project_id.addtocart_model_name`), run the following code to make predictions on all the test data. This returns the probability that addedToCart = 1. Make sure to use at least the same features you used to create your model.

```
SELECT
  sessionId, 
  prob as addedToCart
FROM
  ML.PREDICT(MODEL bqmlretail.YOUR_MODEL_NAME, ## replace model name
    (
    SELECT 
      CONCAT(fullVisitorId, CAST(visitStartTime as string)) as sessionId, 
      *
    FROM
    `kaggleworkshops.google_analytics_sample_eu.test`
  )),
UNNEST(predicted_addedToCart_probs)
WHERE label = 1
```

### Step 4. Save your results as a CSV
Once your ML.PREDICT has finished running, click on **Save Results**, then choose **CSV (Google Drive)**:
![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F3399668%2F6b98c796f91435ccab4687dd5df98c2f%2Fsave_results.png?generation=1581284671726951&alt=media)
![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F3399668%2Fe86add0ff58b73240d5f736a914d6bf1%2Fsave_results_popup.png?generation=1581284672753246&alt=media)

_If you missed the link, you can re-export, or in the ""Jobs history"" section in the BigQuery Console, navigate to your export job, find the filename, and search in your Google Drive for the file._

### Step 5. Open the file in Google Drive
Wait for the ""Open in Drive"" link to appear in the bottom-left corner of your screen and click on it.
![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F3399668%2F4ea482fcbdd6763aef45215eec2973f8%2Fopen_in_drive.png?generation=1581285106012139&alt=media)
(**Can't find it?** Easiest way is to re-do Step 2. Otherwise, you can find the output filename under _Job History_ on the left-hand menu on the BigQuery Console, then check under _Personal History_.)

### Step 6. Download the CSV
Download the results from your Google Drive (~8MB):
![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F3399668%2F107b5d000d77eba26d0a85d451c3c3a3%2Fdownload_drive.png?generation=1581285350804991&alt=media)

### Step 7. Make your submission!
On the Submit Predictions page, follow the following instructions. Pasting the SQL query in the description will allow you to keep track of what models you used, and help the competition admins audit your queries.
![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F3399668%2Faa300b2d2b341ee3e7d386e4e2885c77%2Finstructions_makesubmission.png?generation=1594202366445976&alt=media)","# Dataset Description

### What is the table schema?
- **fullVisitorId**: The unique visitor ID.
- **visitStartTime**: The timestamp (expressed as POSIX time).
- **date**: The date of the session.
- **deviceCategory**: The type of device (Mobile, Tablet, Desktop).  
- **isMobile**: If the user is on a mobile device, this value is true, otherwise false.	
- **operatingSystem**: The operating system of the device (e.g., ""Macintosh"" or ""Windows"").	
- **browser**: The browser used (e.g., ""Chrome"" or ""Firefox"").
- **country**: The country from which sessions originated, based on IP address.
- **city**: Users' city, derived from their IP addresses or Geographical IDs  
- **trafficSource**: The source of the traffic source. Could be the name of the search engine, the referring hostname, or a value of the utm_source URL parameter.  
- **trafficMedium**: The medium of the traffic source. Could be ""organic"", ""cpc"", ""referral"", or the value of the utm_medium URL parameter.	
- **trafficCampaign**: The campaign value. Usually set by the utm_campaign URL parameter.  	
- **isFirstVisit**: 1 if this is the first visit made by the visitor, otherwise 0.
- **isBounce**: 1 if the visitor went to only one webpage and did not come back, otherwise 0.
- **totalVisits**: The total number of visits by the visitor across sessions.  
- **totalHits**: The total number of interactions across sessions. 
- **totalPageviews**: The total number of page views across sessions. 	
- **totalTimeOnSite**: The total time on site.
- **totalTransactions**: The total number of interactions across sessions. 	
- **productPagesViewed**: The number of product pages viewed in the session.
- **addedToCart**: If the visitor added an item to the cart or made further downstream actions (checkout, transaction), otherwise 0 (if the visitor only browsed but never added an item to the cart).  

### What does the training data look like?
```
 SELECT   
   *   
 FROM  
   kaggleworkshops.google_analytics_sample_eu.train  
 LIMIT 20
```

### What does the testing data look like?
```
 SELECT   
   *   
 FROM  
   kaggleworkshops.google_analytics_sample_eu.test  
 LIMIT 20
```",False,True
5979,telecom-churn-case-study-hackathon-gc3,"# Description

# Problem Statement

In the telecom industry, customers are able to choose from multiple service providers and actively switch from one operator to another. In this highly competitive market, the telecommunications industry experiences an average of 15-25% annual churn rate. Given the fact that it costs 5-10 times more to acquire a new customer than to retain an existing one, customer retention has now become even more important than customer acquisition.

For many incumbent operators, retaining high profitable customers is the number one business
goal. To reduce customer churn, telecom companies need to **predict which customers are at high risk of churn**. In this project, you will analyze customer-level data of a leading telecom firm, build predictive models to identify customers at high risk of churn.

In this competition, your goal is to build a machine learning model that is able to predict churning customers based on the features provided for their usage.

----------------------
# Evaluation

## Goal

It is your job to predict if a customer will churn, given the ~170 columns containing customer behavior, usage patterns, payment patterns, and other features that might be relevant. Your target variable is ""churn_probability""

Note: Make sure your accuracy is greater than the sample submission that is present in the leaderboard

## Metric

Submissions are evaluated on [Classification Accuracy](https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/model_evaluation.html#accuracy-score) between the value of the predicted value and the actual value of churn for each of the customers. 

![Accuracy score formula](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/7bfe40cea126a04004b82f729cf7df1ec435fbf6)

The public leaderboard is going to rank your submission against other users while the competition is active, however, once the competition is ended, the final ranks will be calculated on the private leaderboard.

## Submission file format

The file should contain a header and have the following format (CSV):

```
id,churn_probability
70005,0.0
70006,1.0
70007,0.0
etc.
```
A sample file can be found attached in the Data section of this competition.","# Dataset Description

## File descriptions:
- **train.csv**: Contains 172 columns. The primary key that represents each customer is `id`. The target variable that you need to predict is `churn_probability` which contains a value of 0 or 1. This data is what you are going to use for EDA, cleaning, feature engineering, model building, model evaluation, model selection, and finally model training.


- **test.csv**: Contains 171 columns, doesn't contain the target variable `churn_probability`. You will apply all the necessary preprocessing steps to get this data into the right format and then use the model trained using the *train.csv* file to make predictions with this. This is unseen data! Only the competition hosts know the actual values of the target feature for this data and therefore your submissions will be evaluated on how well your model does with this dataset.


- **sample.csv**: This contains the format in which you need to submit the solutions to Kaggle. The `id` column in this dataset exactly the same as the `id` column in *test.csv*. You will make your predictions on the *test.csv* data and store them in a submission file that has the same format as this file. Check the **Overview>Evaluation** tab for mode details.


- **data_dictionary.csv**: This contains the definitions for the various acronyms that you will need to understand each variable. For example, the variable `total_og_mou_7`, contains the acronyms `total`, `og`, `mou`, and `7`, which can be translated as `the total outgoing minutes of voice calls made by the user in month of July`.

## Data Definitions

The definitions are also listed down below:

- CIRCLE_ID : Telecom circle area to which the customer belongs to
- LOC : Local calls - within same telecom circle
- STD : STD calls - outside the calling circle
- IC : Incoming calls
- OG : Outgoing calls
- T2T : Operator T to T, i.e. within same operator (mobile to mobile)
- T2M : Operator T to other operator mobile
- T2O : Operator T to other operator fixed line
- T2F : Operator T to fixed lines of T
- T2C : Operator T to it¡¯s own call center
- ARPU : Average revenue per user
- MOU : Minutes of usage - voice calls
- AON : Age on network - number of days the customer is using the operator T network
- ONNET : All kind of calls within the same operator network
- OFFNET : All kind of calls outside the operator T network
- ROAM : Indicates that customer is in roaming zone during the call
- SPL : Special calls
- ISD : ISD calls
- RECH : Recharge
- NUM : Number
- AMT : Amount in local currency
- MAX : Maximum
- DATA : Mobile internet
- 3G : 3G network
- AV : Average
- VOL : Mobile internet usage volume (in MB)
- 2G : 2G network
- PCK : Prepaid service schemes called - PACKS
- NIGHT : Scheme to use during specific night hours only
- MONTHLY : Service schemes with validity equivalent to a month
- SACHET : Service schemes with validity smaller than a month
- *.6 : KPI for the month of June
- *.7 : KPI for the month of July
- *.8 : KPI for the month of August
- FB_USER : Service scheme to avail services of Facebook and similar social networking sites
- VBC : Volume based cost - when no specific scheme is not purchased and paid as per usage",False,True
5980,10botics-house-prices,"# Description

apply you regression modelling skill!
----------------------
# Evaluation

Goal

It is your job to predict the sales price for each house. For each Id in the test set, you must predict the value of the SalePrice variable. 
Metric

Submissions are evaluated on Root-Mean-Squared-Error (RMSE) between the logarithm of the predicted value and the logarithm of the observed sales price. (Taking logs means that errors in predicting expensive houses and cheap houses will affect the result equally.)","# Dataset Description

File descriptions

    train.csv - the training set
    test.csv - the test set
    data_description.txt - full description of each column, originally prepared by Dean De Cock but lightly edited to match the column names used here
    sample_submission.csv - a benchmark submission from a linear regression on year and month of sale, lot square footage, and number of bedrooms

Data fields

Here's a brief version of what you'll find in the data description file.

    SalePrice - the property's sale price in dollars. This is the target variable that you're trying to predict.
    MSSubClass: The building class
    MSZoning: The general zoning classification
    LotFrontage: Linear feet of street connected to property
    LotArea: Lot size in square feet
    Street: Type of road access
    Alley: Type of alley access
    LotShape: General shape of property
    LandContour: Flatness of the property
    Utilities: Type of utilities available
    LotConfig: Lot configuration
    LandSlope: Slope of property
    Neighborhood: Physical locations within Ames city limits
    Condition1: Proximity to main road or railroad
    Condition2: Proximity to main road or railroad (if a second is present)
    BldgType: Type of dwelling
    HouseStyle: Style of dwelling
    OverallQual: Overall material and finish quality
    OverallCond: Overall condition rating
    YearBuilt: Original construction date
    YearRemodAdd: Remodel date
    RoofStyle: Type of roof
    RoofMatl: Roof material
    Exterior1st: Exterior covering on house
    Exterior2nd: Exterior covering on house (if more than one material)
    MasVnrType: Masonry veneer type
    MasVnrArea: Masonry veneer area in square feet
    ExterQual: Exterior material quality
    ExterCond: Present condition of the material on the exterior
    Foundation: Type of foundation
    BsmtQual: Height of the basement
    BsmtCond: General condition of the basement
    BsmtExposure: Walkout or garden level basement walls
    BsmtFinType1: Quality of basement finished area
    BsmtFinSF1: Type 1 finished square feet
    BsmtFinType2: Quality of second finished area (if present)
    BsmtFinSF2: Type 2 finished square feet
    BsmtUnfSF: Unfinished square feet of basement area
    TotalBsmtSF: Total square feet of basement area
    Heating: Type of heating
    HeatingQC: Heating quality and condition
    CentralAir: Central air conditioning
    Electrical: Electrical system
    1stFlrSF: First Floor square feet
    2ndFlrSF: Second floor square feet
    LowQualFinSF: Low quality finished square feet (all floors)
    GrLivArea: Above grade (ground) living area square feet
    BsmtFullBath: Basement full bathrooms
    BsmtHalfBath: Basement half bathrooms
    FullBath: Full bathrooms above grade
    HalfBath: Half baths above grade
    Bedroom: Number of bedrooms above basement level
    Kitchen: Number of kitchens
    KitchenQual: Kitchen quality
    TotRmsAbvGrd: Total rooms above grade (does not include bathrooms)
    Functional: Home functionality rating
    Fireplaces: Number of fireplaces
    FireplaceQu: Fireplace quality
    GarageType: Garage location
    GarageYrBlt: Year garage was built
    GarageFinish: Interior finish of the garage
    GarageCars: Size of garage in car capacity
    GarageArea: Size of garage in square feet
    GarageQual: Garage quality
    GarageCond: Garage condition
    PavedDrive: Paved driveway
    WoodDeckSF: Wood deck area in square feet
    OpenPorchSF: Open porch area in square feet
    EnclosedPorch: Enclosed porch area in square feet
    3SsnPorch: Three season porch area in square feet
    ScreenPorch: Screen porch area in square feet
    PoolArea: Pool area in square feet
    PoolQC: Pool quality
    Fence: Fence quality
    MiscFeature: Miscellaneous feature not covered in other categories
    MiscVal: $Value of miscellaneous feature
    MoSold: Month Sold
    YrSold: Year Sold
    SaleType: Type of sale
    SaleCondition: Condition of sale",False,True
5981,cs4210-summer2022-assignment-2,"# Description

<h2><strong>In-Class Competition: Facial Expression Classification</strong></h2>
<p>Automatic human facial expression classification has lots of potential real life applications, such as customer service systems, personalized advertisement, and entertainment. In our assignment 2, we will focus on building a deep learning model for <strong>Facial Expression Classification</strong>.</p>

<p>The training dataset and testing dataset are made available in the Data section. The training set consists of 16,175 examples for you to build your model. The test set consists of 3,965 examples which will be used to determine your score. Please check the Data section for more details.</p> 

<p> Please note that the data are from a real Kaggle competition, https://www.kaggle.com/c/challenges-in-representation-learning-facial-expression-recognition-challenge/data</p>

<p><strong>Specific Tasks:</strong><br />1. Develop your Deep Learning models for facial expression classification using Keras.<br />2. Gain experience in training neural network models.<br />3. Follow the submission file format and instructions to submit your predictions in Kaggle.<br />4. Zip your code and your final prediction file, and submit the zipped file in our course blackboard.</p>

<p><strong>Some Useful Resources:</strong><br /></p>
<ul>
<li><a href=""https://pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/basics/intro.html"">Pytorch: LEARN THE BASICS
</a></li>
<li><a href=""https://pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/blitz/cifar10_tutorial.html"">Pytorch: TRAINING A CLASSIFIER
</a></li>
<li><a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/code/basu369victor/pytorch-tutorial-the-classification/notebook"">PyTorch-Tutorial (The Classification)
</a></li>
<li><a href=""https://pytorch.org/tutorials/recipes/recipes/custom_dataset_transforms_loader.html?highlight=custom%20dataset"">DEVELOPING CUSTOM PYTORCH DATALOADERS
</a></li>
<li><a href=""https://towardsdatascience.com/pytorch-tabular-multiclass-classification-9f8211a123ab"">PyTorch [Tabular] ¡ªMulticlass Classification
</a></li>
<li><a href=""https://pytorch.org/vision/stable/models.html"">Models and pre-trained weights</a></li>
</ul>
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>This evaluation page describes how submissions will be scored and how students should format their submissions.  
 <p><strong>Metric</strong>: Categorization Accuracy</p>
 - <p><strong>The submission files should contain two columns: Id and
   Expected. </strong>,</p><p>The format is as follows:</p>
   <pre>Id,Category<br />0,2<br />1,1<br />2,0<br />3,2<br />etc.</pre>
 - <p><strong>You may submit a maximum of 20 entries per day. You may select up to 2 final submissions for judging.</strong></p>","# Dataset Description

<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>train_data.csv</strong> - the training?data</li>
<li><strong>train_target.csv</strong> - the corresponding?training?targets</li>
<li><strong>test_data.csv</strong> - the testing data</li>
<li><strong>sample-submission.csv</strong> - a sample submission file in the correct format</li>
</ul>

<p>The data has been randomly separated into two parts: training set and testing set. The training set consists of 16,175 examples for you to build your model. The test set consists of 3,965 examples which will be used to determine your score.</p>
<p>Please note:?</p>
<p>1. The provided csv files have no index column and header row.</p>
<p>2. In ""train_data.csv"" and ""test_data.csv"", each row contains 2304 pixel values (as each face image is of size 48*48 in pixels).</p>
<p>3. ""train_target.csv"" stores the corresponding target to each row data in ""train_data.csv"" (0-Angry, 1-Happy, and 2-Neutral).</p>
<p>4. When you save your predictions into a csv file, please follow the submission file format as shown in ""Evaluation"" section (the submission file requires the specific index column and header row, where the ""Id"" is a zero-based index corresponding to the order of testing data in the ""test_data.csv"" file).</p>",False,True
