5991,intent-detection-by-sinch,"# Description

# We are [Sinch](https://www.sinch.com/)

We're not just experts in messaging...we¡¯re #1 in Conversational Platforms. Our cloud communications platform reaches every mobile?phone on the planet -?in seconds or less. What are Conversational Platforms? Conversational platforms can be used by developers to build conversational user interfaces, chatbots and virtual assistants for integration into messaging platforms, social media, SMS, website chat.

In this messaging context, we have the challenge of correctly identify the intent of a message, task called **Intent Detection**. In this competition, you are going to explore a dataset for Intent Detection with pre-labeled messages (train set) and non-labeled messages (test set). 

## Want to join us? Well, lucky you - we're hiring! 

If you want to become an intern at Sinch, join in this competition and apply [here](https://apply.workable.com/sinch/j/4345ED85FC/)!

> Your task is to build an intent classifier on top of sentence embeddings. 

There is no real deadline for the competition. Every good submission will result in an interview and possibly an internship ;-)

If you want to see all of open jobs, please access our [career website](https://www.sinch.com/careers/).

----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is [Micro-Averaged F1-Score](https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/generated/sklearn.metrics.f1_score.html). The F1 score, commonly used in information retrieval, measures accuracy using the statistics precision \( **p** \) and recall \( **r** \). Precision is the ratio of true positives \( **tp** \) to all predicted positives \( **tp** + **fp** \). Meanwhile, Recall is the ratio of true positives \( **tp** \) to all actual positives \( **tp** + **fn** \). 

The F1-Score is given by:

$$
F_1 = 2 * \frac{precision * recall}{precision + recall}
$$

where:

$$
precision = \frac{TP}{TP + FP}
$$

$$
recall = \frac{TP}{TP + FN}
$$

The F1 metric weights recall and precision equally, and a good retrieval algorithm will maximize both precision and recall simultaneously. Thus, moderately good performance on both will be favored over extremely good performance on one and poor performance on the other.

## Submission Format

Check `sample.csv` to see the submission format","# Dataset Description

In total there are 45 classes. The training set consists out of 1663 sentence embeddings and 713 sentence embeddings are in the test set.

To load the data download the `X_train.npy`, `X_test.npy` and `y_train.csv` files and open them as follows:
   
> import numpy as np   
> import pandas as pd
> 
> X_train = np.load('X_train.npy')   
> X_test = np.load('X_test.npy')
> 
> y_train = pd.read_csv('y_train.csv').Predicted.to_numpy()",False,True
5992,what-the-sketch,"# Description

Image classification is a prevalent task in the machine learning world, and there are a lot of content/articles on this topic (I wrote one a few months ago [here](https://www.the-odd-dataguy.com/2021/09/25/fastai-image-classifier/)). This problem is, for me, exciting and fun because it's a very tangible outcome to associate an image with a class, but it can also bring many challenges.

A few years back, Kaggle hosted a [competition](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/quickdraw-doodle-recognition) based on a dataset extracted from the [quick draw application](https://quickdraw.withgoogle.com/) (you have a few seconds to draw specific entities like a sheep, for example). The competition was exciting, but the data setup was huge, with plenty of formats and classes to predict, and it could be overwhelming for newcomers.

This competition leverages a smaller dataset called [Sketch](http://cybertron.cg.tu-berlin.de/eitz/projects/classifysketch/); it contains over 20,000 sketches evenly distributed over 250 object categories and is released in 2012  (so before quick draw, maybe an inspiration). 

The setup of the collection of these images is different with:
- Using [Amazon Mechanical Turk](https://www.mturk.com/) for the collection
- 1350 participants in this data collection
- the median time for each drawing is around 86 seconds (with the 10th and 90th percentile at 31 and 280 seconds)

If you are more interested in the experiment and dataset, you can look at this [paper](http://cybertron.cg.tu-berlin.de/eitz/pdf/2012_siggraph_classifysketch.pdf) associated with the original dataset.

So the goal of this competition will be to classify images/sketches in the different classes possible., pretty simple :)
----------------------
# Evaluation

Categorization accuracy is the metric for this competition.

The goal is to predict the right class associated with an image:

- if the class is right accuracy = 1
- if not right , accuracy = 0

Overall the categorization_accuracy will be the average of the accuracy on all the test set. If we compare the metric to quick draw recognition challenge it's a little bit harder but easier to understand.

## Submission Format

The file should contain a header and have the following format:
```
image_id,class
1,sheep
```
----------------------
# Acknowledgement

First of all, I would like to thank the work of Mathias Eitz, James Hays and Marc Alexa, who built the [Sketch](http://cybertron.cg.tu-berlin.de/eitz/projects/classifysketch/) dataset.

Also, this competition is inspired by the [Quick, Draw! Doodle Recognition Challenge](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/quickdraw-doodle-recognition) by Google, so thanks :) 
----------------------
# Tips And Tricks

There are a few things to keep in mind from my perspective:

- the number of samples to train in each class is ""small"" so data augmentation is your friend
- Balanced dataset
- Be careful on classes that can be ambiguous, like seagulls VS the other bird's classes (flying bird, standing bird and pigeon)

A good starting point in terms of resources could be to have a look at the [discussion section](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/quickdraw-doodle-recognition/discussion?sort=votes) of the [Quick, Draw! Doodle Recognition Challenge](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/quickdraw-doodle-recognition); there are a few links that I selected:
- [1st solution](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/quickdraw-doodle-recognition/discussion/73738)
- [FastAi v1 (PyTorch nightly) Starter Pack [updated - LB 0.856 with 1k examples per class]](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/quickdraw-doodle-recognition/discussion/69409)
- [5th place solution](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/quickdraw-doodle-recognition/discussion/73708)

To submit, there is a [notebook](https://www.kaggle.com/code/jeanmidev/build-submission) to illustrate the overall flow.","# Dataset Description

The data is split between the 250 classes with:

- 74 images per class in images/train in one folder
- 6 images per class in images/test in one folder (with a 50/50 split between private and public leaderboards)

The images keep the same shape as the original one, with a grid of 1111x1111 pixels in a greyscale bitmap.

To make the association from class to image there is::

-  train.csv with an image_id and the classes , to get the image just need to read from ""./images/train/{image_id}.csv""
-  test.csv with an image_id, to get the image just need to read from ""./images/test/{image_id}.csv""

The list of all the classes is available in the classes.csv file.",False,True
5993,sc201-june-2022,"# Description

# Housing Price in Boston
The medv variable is the target variable.

# Data Description
The Boston data frame has 506 rows and 14 columns.
This data frame contains the following columns:

## crim
per capita crime rate by town.

## zn
proportion of residential land zoned for lots over 25,000 sq.ft.

## indus
proportion of non-retail business acres per town.

## chas
Charles River dummy variable (= 1 if tract bounds river; 0 otherwise).

## nox
nitrogen oxides concentration (parts per 10 million).

## rm
average number of rooms per dwelling.

## age
proportion of owner-occupied units built prior to 1940.

## dis
weighted mean of distances to five Boston employment centres.

## rad
index of accessibility to radial highways.

## tax
full-value property-tax rate per \$10,000.

## ptratio
pupil-teacher ratio by town.

## black
1000(Bk - 0.63)^2 where Bk is the proportion of blacks by town.

## lstat
lower status of the population (percent).

## medv
median value of owner-occupied homes in \$1000s.


#Source

Harrison, D. and Rubinfeld, D.L. (1978) Hedonic prices and the demand for clean air. J. Environ. Economics and Management 5, 81¨C102.

Belsley D.A., Kuh, E. and Welsch, R.E. (1980) Regression Diagnostics. Identifying Influential Data and Sources of Collinearity. New York: Wiley.
----------------------
# Evaluation

The scoring is based on the RMSE, that is
```
RMSE(y) = (¡Æ(hi -yi)^(2))^(0.5)
```","# Dataset Description

## Files

*   **submission_example.csv** -  a sample submission file in the correct format
*   **test.csv** - the test set
*   **train.csv** - the training set",False,True
5994,first-arrivals-ml-in-physics,"# Description

The goal of this challenge is to determine the First Arrivals of earthquakes. Before formulating our specific problem, let us consider a short example of a similar problem which may help to understand the general idea.

We have a smartphone and we want to ask an audio assistant (e.g. ""Ok Google!"") to find some information on
the internet.

The audio assistant has to continuously record our voice from the microphone and when we speak the keywords
""Ok Google!"", it activates speech recognition. To catch the keywords, the program inside of your smartphone
has to identify your voice among background noises and understand when your speech starts and ends. This
is called voice activity detection. 

Let us come back to our problem, where ""voice"" is earthquakes and ""microphone"" is geophones recording quakes (receiver for short). Instead of detecting full voice activity, we want to identify only when it starts. In seismology, this procedure is called first-arrival picking. In other words, we want to understand when an earthquake starts in time.

We will use Mean Absolute Error (MAE) to evaluate the performance of the submitted prediction results and rank the best-performed models.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Similarly to the first challenge, this challenge will also use MAE to determine the scoreboard.","# Dataset Description

There are 24 simulations in the train folder. each simulation folder contains 127 csv files, standing for 127 receivers. Each receiver records fluctuations in X, Y and Z components over a 30000 timesteps period. There can be from 0 (minimum) to 5 (maximum) earthquakes in each simulation. Your task is to determine when a receiver first picks up the energy from an earthquake. 

For each simulation, there is a ""simulation_[X]_output.csv"". In this file, there is a 5 by 127 matrix, where each row corresponds for an ""event"" (an earthquake), and each column represents a receiver respectively. Each value is the exact timestep when the receiver recognises the first arrival of an earthquake. If there is less than 5 earthquakes in a simulation, you must fill everything else with -1. 

Due to Kaggle restrictions, the submission file cannot be in the matrix form but in a flattened format. Please refer to the sampleSubmission.csv to understand the layout. The file contains 127 x 5 =  635 entries, where each entry is in the format R[receiver #]-E[event #], [predicted timestep].",False,True
5995,pitc-2122-clustering,"# Description


----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation pages describes how submissions will be scored and how students should format their submissions. 
---

The evaluation metric for this competition is [CategoricalAccuracy]","# Dataset Description

Dear **Partecipant** Welcome to the clustering **PITC 21/22** competition.

In this section you will find the needed files  to traning your model, a data set to  perform the clusteriztion against with and  a sample file with the expected submition format. 

##Files Description

*   **train.csv** - the training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file
___

##train.csv file format

Format: CSV , Separator: *','* (comma)

## Columns
- `user`:  customer identifier 
- `cluster`:  energy consumption profiling (energy being used at particular times of the day). Available options:  [` 0` (mono),  `1 ` (after mid-day), `2 ` (nigth) )]`
- `events`:  list of available previous bill in json format.

___

##test.csv file format
Format: CSV , Separator: *','* (comma)

## Columns
- `user`:  customer identifier 
- `events`:  list of available previous bill in json format. 

___

##Events field schema 
Fields having high impact on the model below. Make sure to extract at least this field.

* `bamData.items.bamItem.addresses`: List of Address 
* `bamData.items.bamItem.addresses.city`: (`{code, value}`)  **code** city code, **value** city name 
* `bamData.items.bamItem.addresses.type`: (`{code, value}`) **code** set to *2* for customer redidential, *6* for consumption location
*  `bamData.items.customData`: energy consumption information
* `bamData.items.customData.billing_range`:   Billing period
* `bamData.items.customData.billing_range.from_date`:   The date the billing period is starting from
* `bamData.items.customData.billing_range.to_date`:   Date the billing period is ending to
* `bamData.items.customData.energy`: Energy Consumption related for the period in scope
*  `bamData.items.customData.energy.fasciaF0ConsumoFatturato`: Energy Consumption related to F0 level (**Not Used**)
*  `bamData.items.customData.energy.fasciaF1ConsumoFatturato`: Energy Consumption related to F1 level
*  `bamData.items.customData.energy.fasciaF2ConsumoFatturato`: Energy Consumption related to F2 level
*  `bamData.items.customData.energy.fasciaF3ConsumoFatturato`: Energy Consumption related to F3 level
*  `bamData.items.customData.energy.fasciaF4ConsumoFatturato`: Energy Consumption related to F4 level (**Not Used**)
*  `bamData.items.customData.energy.fasciaF5ConsumoFatturato`: Energy Consumption related to F5 level (**Not Used**)
*  `bamData.items.customData.energy.fasciaF6ConsumoFatturato`: Energy Consumption  related to F6 level (**Not Used**)

___

##sample_submission.csv file format
Format: CSV , Separator: *','* (comma)

## Columns
- `user`:  customer identifier 
- `cluster`:  energy consumption profiling",False,True
5996,pitc-2122-prediction,"# Description

Let's summarize the datasets of the two competitions.

**Folder ""task_1"":** consumptions from July 2021 to June 2022 (there are missing data)
**Folder ""task_2"":** consumptions from July 2022 to June 2022
