6001,forcastingjudgement,"# Description

You might have wondered the difference between prediction and forecasting. Forecasting is an estimation of future events which one can make by incorporating and casting forward data related to the past. In contrast, Prediction is an estimate of future events driven only by subjective considerations. 

Forecasting is a key tool as it helps make accurate predictions of future events backed with data which is of great importance for firms in order to estimate the future demand and make decisions on the manufacturing of the supply chain. However, it can generate inaccurate forecasts, especially if carried out by humans, as there may be various biases which affect the judgement of the people carrying it out.

This experiment is carried out by @?University Research Team in order to study the biases that may affect forecasters¡¯ judgement under different environmental conditions.

By completing this competition you will help to find ways to make forecasts more accurate and less biased affected.","# Dataset Description

This dataset represents different environmental conditions for an individual during an economic cycle. The objective of this competition is to use forecasting methods in order to estimate the average spending for good x for July 2022.

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format
*   **metaData.csv** - supplemental information about the data",False,True
6002,covid-19-ai-detection-challenge,"# Description


**# Motivation**
COVID-19 has profoundly impacted the world we live in. We must adapt to this news and we need an exceptional initiative in innovation across the globe. Around the world, industry, academia, governments, and non-profit organizations are joining forces to fight the pandemic. These partnerships are paramount in struggling with the disease and investigating options that meet the varying needs of patients and healthcare professionals.

**# Task Description**
The 7th IDSS (Intelligent Decision Support Systems) 2022 [**COVID-19 AI Detection Challenge**](https://itsis.org/?page_id=34) in conjunction with the IEEE International Conference on Information Technologies and Smart Industrial Systems [**ITSIS**](https://itsis.org/) 2022 invites participants to help medical professionals around the world quickly diagnose COVID-19. Participants
are asked for building models to detect COVID-19 using Computed Tomography (CT) scan images and classify images into COVID-19, NON-COVID-Normal, and Pathological-NON-COVID.



## Acknowledgements
We thank all team members for providing this dataset, namely Zeineb Fki, Boudour Ammar, Rahma Fourati, Hela Fendri, Amir Hussein, and Mounir Ben Ayed.
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is [Accuracy]

## Submission Format

Submission Format
For every unique combination in the dataset, submission files should contain two columns: Id and Class.

The file should have the following format:
Id (the same column index in the test set file) column and Class column.

The Class column contains values that can be 0, 1, or 2 which correspond to COVID-19, NON-COVID-Normal, and Pathological-NON-COVID classes, respectively.","# Dataset Description

# **Data**
The competition data include Computed Tomography (CT) scan images. Each image has one label that describes a clinical state of an individual.
The competition labels consist of three types:
-**COVID-19** label indicates whether experts detect the presence of COVID-19 lesion.
-**NON-COVID-Normal** label indicates the normal clinical outcome diagnosed by the medical experts.
-**Pathological-NON-COVID** label indicates the presence of a disease other than COVID-19 detected by experts.

The competition data were collected from **Habib Bourguiba Hospital of Sfax** in 2022, thereby approved by medical experts.

The competition data are organized into three distinct sets: training, validation, and test sets. All labels are included in the different sets. We have publicly released 80% of the dataset as the training set for the competition, and we have retained the remaining 20% as test set.

**# Files**
train - Training images
train_label.csv - Tagged class for an image in train folder
test - Test Images
sample_submission.csv - a sample submission file in the correct format",False,True
