6012,nus-fintech-recruitment,"# Description

Fraudulent transactions as a result of compromised credit cards and phishing attacks on merchant terminals pose a great challenge for businesses, payment providers and consumers. With the increasing number of card transactions, it is almost impossible for financial institutions to check and detect fraudulent transactions manually. Hence, payment providers are increasingly relying on machine learning techniques and models to screen through the vast amount of transactions and flag suspicious transactions for further investigations by humans.

In this challenge, we seek to design a credit card fraud detection system that is able to detect fraudulent transactions in the dataset. 

We are not looking for the best-performing model. Rather, we are interested in how you approach machine learning problems and your creativity in data engineering.

## Acknowledgements

- Le Borgne, Yann-A?l & Bontempi, Gianluca. (2021). Reproducible Machine Learning for Credit Card Fraud Detection - Practical Handbook. 
- Y¨¹ksel, K. (2020, March 10). Accuracy, precision, recall and F1 score. Medium. https://emkademy.medium.com/accuracy-precision-recall-and-f1-score-62ff0a1a3cae 
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is [Precision](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Precision_and_recall). Precision is the ratio of true positives to all predicted positives. Mathematically, $$precision = {True Positives \over True Positives + False Positives}$$

This metric ranges from 0.0 to 1.0, with a higher value indicating better performance.

## Submission Format

The submission file should contain two columns: `TRANSACTION_ID` and `TX_FRAUD`. For each `TRANSACTION_ID` in the test set that **occurred in Apr 2022**, you must predict if the transaction is fraudulent or legitimate (1 for fraudulent and 0 for legitimate).  There should be 55091 such transactions.


The file should contain a header and have the following format:
```
TRANSACTION_ID,TX_FRAUD
524230, 0
524231, 1
...
579318, 0
579319, 1
579320, 0
```
----------------------
# Timeline

- July 17, 2022 - Start Date

- August 10, 2022 - Final Submission Deadline

All deadlines are at 11:59 PM UTC on the corresponding day unless otherwise noted. The competition organizers reserve the right to update the contest timeline if they deem it necessary.","# Dataset Description

The objective of this competition is to predict whether a transaction is fraudulent or legitimate. The target label is `TX_FRAUD`, where 1 indicates a fraudulent transaction and 0 indicates a legitimate transaction.

Please take note that the **test.csv** file contains credit card transactions that occurred from Jan - Apr 2022. However, for the final submission of this competition, **please only submit the predictions for transactions that occurred in Apr 2022** and sort them by transaction ID in ascending order (from `transaction_id = 524230` at the top to `transaction_id = 579320` at the bottom). Please refer to the sample_submission.csv file for your final submission. 

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training set. Contains credit card transactions that occurred from Aug - Dec 2021
*   **test.csv** - the test set for submission. Contains credit card transactions that occurred from Jan - Apr 2022. Only predictions for transactions that occurred in Apr 2022 need to be submitted. Please sort your submission by the transaction ID in ascending order
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format
*   **customer.csv** - list of customers and their respective customer IDs. A pair of coordinates `(x_customer_id, y_customer_id)` is provided to indicate the location of the customer
*   **terminal.csv** - list of merchants (terminals) and their respective terminal IDs. A pair of coordinates `(x_terminal_id, y_terminal_id)` is provided to indicate the location of the terminal

## Columns in train.csv

*   `TRANSACTION_ID` - transaction ID
*   `TX_DATETIME` - date and time of transaction
*   `CUSTOMER_ID` - customer ID involved in the transaction
*   `TERMINAL_ID` - terminal ID where transaction occurred
*   `TX_AMOUNT` - amount transacted
*   `TX_FRAUD`?- indicates if the transaction is fraudulent. 1 for fraudulent and 0 for legitimate

## Columns in customer.csv

*   `CUSTOMER_ID` - customer ID
*   `x_customer_id` - x-coordinate of the customer
*   `y_customer_id` - y-coordinate of the customer
*   `mean_amount` - mean amount spent by the customer
*   `std_amount` - standard deviation of the amount spent by the customer
*   `mean_nb_tx_per_day` - mean number of transactions made by the customer per day
*   `available_terminals`?- terminals where the customer is able to make transactions. We assume that customers can only make transactions at terminals within a radius of 5 units from the location of the customer
*   `nb_terminals` - number of terminals that the customer can make transactions

## Columns in terminal.csv

*   `TERMINAL_ID` - terminal ID
*   `x_terminal_id` - x-coordinate of the terminal
*   `y_terminal_id` - y-coordinate of the terminal",False,True
6013,cmhstm22t1,"# Description

This competition is organized in conjunction with the International Conference of Frontiers in Handwriting Recognition [ICFHR2022](http://icfhr2022.org/), which will be held in Hyderabad,  India, on December 4-7.

### The competition consists of two tasks: (1) hard classification and (2) soft classification. Each participant can participate in both or only one of them.  Here,  you are competing in TASK 1. To register and compete in [TASK 2](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/cmhstm22t2), follow this[ link](https://www.kaggle.com/t/3453f441f1f44bb1b9b8008ba832c839).

Automatic script classification has been an active area of research in recent years.  It is essential for understanding, authenticating, and dating historical texts. Script classification is a prerequisite for many historical document processing tasks, such as paleography analysis, writer identification, and handwritten text recognition (HTR).

This competition adds the challenge of classification of medieval Hebrew scripts based on their type and mode.

The task to be evaluated in the present competition is classifying image pages written in Hebrew script. There are 508 pages in the training set that were picked from 125 manuscripts dating from the 10th to 16th centuries. The results will be evaluated on 140 image pages picked from manuscripts that are not included in the training dataset.

In the hard classification task (TASK 1), there is one label for each page image in the data set.


The organizers provide a training data set together with their ground truth in the form of hard labels. 



----------------------
# Evaluation


### Evaluation 
The evaluation of Task 1 (hard classification) is based on average accuracy. [Accuracy](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Accuracy_and_precision) is  the fraction of correct classifications:

$$  Accuracy = \frac{correct\ classifications}{all\ classifications}$$

In addition, each participant is required to provide a short description of the method.

To check the applicability of the proposed systems for real-time application, applicants are also requested to report the processing time per document page. 

### Input and output format specifications

The input to the classifier is a document page image. The output is a single value (class) for TASK 1.

### Submission Format
**For every image in the test set**, the submission file should contain two columns:  `ImageID` and `SCRIPT TYPE,` as illustrated in the  ""sample_submission.csv"" file.  The file should contain a header and have the following format:
![](https://imgur.com/fcjSiZw.png)

**Figure 1: Submission format.**

