6034,what-card-should-i-put-next,"# Description

#WIP is rebuilt with fresh data [HERE](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/what-card-should-i-select-next)

## Context (based on [Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hearthstone))
Hearthstone is a free-to-play online digital collectible card game developed and published by Blizzard Entertainment. Originally subtitled Heroes of Warcraft, Hearthstone builds upon the existing lore of the Warcraft series by using the same elements, characters, and relics. It was first released for Microsoft Windows and macOS in March 2014, with ports for iOS and Android released later that year. The game features cross-platform play, allowing players on any supported device to compete with one another, restricted only by geographical region account limits.

 <img src=""https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/f/f2/Hearthstone_2016_logo.png"" alt=""animated"" style=""display: block; margin: 0 auto"">

The game is a turn-based card game between two opponents, using constructed decks of 30 cards and a selected hero with a unique power. Players use their limited mana crystals to play abilities or summon minions to attack the opponent, to destroy the opponent's hero. Winning matches and completing quests earn in-game gold, rewards in the form of new cards, and other in-game prizes. Players can then buy packs of new cards through gold or microtransactions to customize and improve their decks. The game features several modes of play, including casual and ranked matches, drafted arena battles, and single-player adventures. New content for the game involves the addition of new card sets and gameplay, taking the form of either expansion packs or adventures that reward the player with collectible cards upon completion.
In contrast to other games developed by Blizzard, Hearthstone was an experimental game developed by a smaller team based on the company's appreciation of collectible card games. The game was designed to avoid the pitfalls of other digital collectible card games by eliminating any possible plays from an opponent during a player's turn and replicating the feel of a physical card game within the game's user interface. Many concepts and art assets were based on those previously published in the physical World of Warcraft Trading Card Game.

## Competition
I started to work on a project around Hearthstone a few years ago (I built an article on it), and I wanted to design a project around recommendation with it. The idea is inspired by what I see in my work at Ubisoft on recommender systems but applied to the gameplay aspect of a game.

Blizzard builds functionalities to help the user complete their deck during conception.

<img src=""https://media.giphy.com/media/HgGGYiczyiaCgX0SrX/giphy.gif"" alt=""animated"" style=""display: block; margin: 0 auto"">

The functionality is pretty cool but is very limited for the input required by the user (that is a good thing in some way), so the competition will be similar to the conception of a recommender of the last card to put in a deck.

The data is based on a dump of the website at [hearthpwn](https://www.hearthpwn.com/), very similar to the one that I used for this [article](https://www.the-odd-dataguy.com/2020/01/15/a-gentle-introduction-on-hearthstone/); big thanks to the community; you are the best :)

<img src=""https://cdn.myminifactory.com/uploads/tinymce-images/heartpwn.png"" alt=""animated"" style=""display: block; margin: 0 auto"">

The evaluation metric ([MAP@3](https://medium.com/@misty.mok/how-mean-average-precision-at-k-map-k-can-be-more-useful-than-other-evaluation-metrics-6881e0ee21a9)) is related to the design that I think can fit Hearthstone UI/UX. The feature, for me (with my UX designer hat :) ), should be a simple display of the three best choices to complete your deck that should be displayed if the user is taking some time to fill his deck. The evaluation will be on a specific week (predict the future), and the goal is to optimize the metric MAP@3 for this period.

The training of the recommender system is based on the various decks provided by the community before the test period (no win rate implied or interest in the decks like rating).

----------------------
# Evaluation

the evaluation metric is the [mean average precision](https://blog.paperspace.com/mean-average-precision/) at rank 3

## Submission Format

**For every deck in the dataset**, submission files should contain two columns: `deckid` and `recommendations`. 


The file should contain a header and have the following format:
```
deckid,recommendations
1,100 101 102
```
