6048,drinking-water-quality,"# Description

Estonian government AI testbed competition invites everyone with expertise in data science and analysis to join an experimental testbed to find solutions to real-world problems using open data. We look forward to welcoming a range of specialists to come and work with us and become part of one of the most technologically advanced government projects in Europe contributing to the development of AI Gov-stack that benefits people across the globe. The AI testbed competition encompasses various works in the fields of data science, machine learning, and language technology, that open a way to contribute and become a part of developing the open-source AI Gov-stack. 
Pick a competition you are interested in and help us to build the coolEST and brightEST digital state!

#  Background
Despite the fact that most of our planet is covered with water, not more than 3 % of this amount is fresh. To make sure that the water is safe to drink the Estonian Health Board has been measuring its quality in more than thousand water stations across the country thereby making sure that every citizen will get the freshest water right from their tap. 
To bring water quality measurement to the next level and automate working process of Estonian water inspectors, we would like to invent predictive water quality model that would enable us to prioritize the tests or react proactively to the deterioration of the water conditions. Therefore, enhancing the role of scientific and data-driven approach on a governmental level.

# Goal
The goal of this competition is to create a model that predicts the water quality in Estonian water stations based on the government¡¯s open data of the previous measurements. 

# Praise
* The best solutions will be publicly introduced to Estonian public sector.
* The best solutions become a part of Estonian e-government [code repository]( https://koodivaramu.eesti.ee/explore/projects).
* Social impact by creating a solution which would benefit a society as a whole.
* Chance to virtually meet top Estonian data scientists and build your network.

----------------------
# Evaluation

# Metric
The evaluation metric for this competition is [Accuracy]( https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Accuracy_and_precision#In_binary_classification) ¨C the number of correct predictions divided by the number of all predictions.

# Submission format
You should submit a csv file with the same number of rows + header row as in test.csv file. For every row in the dataset, submission files should contain two columns: ``station_id`` (representing the id of the water station) and ``compliance_2021`` (representing the prediction about the compliance of the water quality):
* 0 ¨C water quality in 2021 is **compliant** with the regulation.
* 1 ¨C water quality in 2021 is **not-compliant** with the regulation.
The file should contain a header and have the following format:

```
station_id, compliance_2021
165, 1
123, 0
Etc.
```
NB: the `compliance_2021` values will be treated as strings. Make sure that your result is presented without commas (1.0 is not the same as 1).","# Dataset Description

# Overview
The data has been split into two groups:

* **Training set (train.csv)**
* **Test set (test.csv)**

**The training set** should be used to build your machine learning models. The training set includes both the features and the result variable (`compliance_2021`).

If you succeed in building a good machine learning model, then you have created a model that enables to predict the quality of the next test based on the metrics of previous tests.

**The test set** should be used to see how well your model performs on unseen data. The test set doesn¡¯t include the result variable. It is your job to predict it using the model you built on the training data!

We also include **sample_submission.csv** - an example of what the submission file should look like (the predictions in the sample file are picked randomly).

# Features
The features include various numeric metrics (e.g. color, smell, ph-level, coli-type bacteria) from previous water tests that are used by the inspectors to define whether the quality is compliant with the regulation. For each water station, you have information about the test results of the years 2019 and 2020. The corresponding year number is added at the end of each variable name.  In addition, you have the information about the compliance of the station in 2019 (`compliance_2019`) and 2020 (`compliance_2020`). The result variable is `compliance_2021`.

NB: There are many missing values (not all of the variables are always tested in each station). Choose your own approach of how to deal with them.",False,True
6049,multi-intent-detection,"# Description

Estonian government AI testbed competition invites everyone with expertise in data science and analysis to join an experimental testbed aims to find solutions to real-world problems using open data. We look forward to welcoming a range of specialists to come and work with us and become part of one of the most technologically advanced government projects in Europe contributing to the development of AI Gov stack that benefits people across the globe. The AI testbed competition encompasses various works in the fields of data science, machine learning, and language technology, that open a way to contribute and become a part of developing the open-source AI Gov-stack. 

Pick a competition you are interested in and help us to build the coolEST and brightEST digital state!

# Background
Remember the last time you had to interact with the government?  Was it easy, fast, and enjoyable? In Estonia, your answer would most probably be YES! In the government we tend to put people¡¯s needs first and offer public services in the most accessible way. Starting from early 90s we¡¯ve chosen the path of e-development and during the last 30 years there has been a huge leap, which allowed Estonia to become one of the most digitally advanced states in the world. Nowadays we want not only to rely on and cherish our previous success but also continue moving further in offering top tier solutions to our citizens. 

Today we are developing our own virtual assistant [B¨¹rokratt] (https://youtu.be/T9dFn9T2QAg) which already slowly is and soon will continue to revolutionize the way people interact with a government. B¨¹rokratt allows citizens to contact the government as a whole, erasing boarders between different authorities and reducing bureaucracy to the max. When developing B¨¹rokratt our number one priority is user-friendliness. We want to give people an opportunity to solve all the problems with one conversation with B¨¹rokratt. That requires B¨¹rokratt to understand multiple intents, which it is still learning to do.

Take part of the competition and help Estonia¡¯s virtual assistant B¨¹rokratt to understand multiple intents by creating a ML model for multi-intent detection in the Estonian language.

# Goal
The goal of this competition is to create a model that can detect multiple intents from user queries sent to Estonian government by its citizens.

# Praise
?	The best solutions will be publicly introduced to Estonian public sector.
?	The best solutions become a part of Estonian e-government [code repository]( https://koodivaramu.eesti.ee/explore/projects).
?	Social impact by creating a solution which would benefit a society as a whole.
?	Chance to virtually meet top Estonian data scientists and build your network.


----------------------
# Evaluation

# Metric
The evaluation metric for this competition is [Mean F-Score](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/F-score) or the arithmetic mean of individual F-scores. The F-score weights recall and precision equally. 

*-Precision is the ratio of true positives to all predicted positives.*
*-Recall is the ratio of true positives to all actual positives.* 

# Submission format
You should submit a csv file with the same number of rows + header row as in test.csv file. For every row in the dataset, submission files should contain two columns: ``query_id`` (representing the id of the user¡¯s query) and ``intents`` (representing the comma separated list of all user intents in the query). Example format:

```
query_id, intents
query1, i0 i1 i5
query103, i11 i6
Etc.
```
In the example above, we say that ``query1`` includes three different intents with the id numbers ``i0 i1 i5``.","# Dataset Description

# Files

## user_queries.csv 
This file represents the original user queries. The file has 2 columns:

* ``query_id`` ¨C the id of the query (NB: you need to use the query_id in the submission file),
* ``query`` ¨C the original query in Estonian.

## intents.csv 
This file represents the unique intents that can be found in the queries. The file has 2 columns: 

* ``intent_id`` ¨C the identificator of the intent. (NB: you need to use the intent_ids in the submission file).
* ``intent`` ¨C intent description in Estonian.

## train.csv 
Training set that should be used to build your machine learning models. The training set includes both the ``query_id`` and the corresponding intents (provided as a space separated list):

* ``query_id`` ¨C the id of the query (NB: use user_queries file to map the query_ids with the actual query text),
* ``intents`` ¨C space separated list of intent ids that correspond to this query.

## test.csv 
Test set should be used to see how well your model performs on unseen data. The test set includes ``query_id`` column but doesn¡¯t include the intents. It is your job to predict it using the model you built on the training data!

## sample_submission.csv
An example of what the submission file should look like. For every row in the test.csv, submission files should contain two columns: 

* ``query_id`` - representing the id of the user¡¯s query,
* ``intents`` - representing the space separated list of all user intents in the query. 

Example format:

``query_id, intents``

``query1, i0 i1 i5``

``query103, i11 i6``

``Etc.``

*NB: the intents in the sample file are picked randomly*.",False,True
6050,tartu-object-detection,"# Description

Estonian government AI testbed competition invites everyone with expertise in data science and analysis to join an experimental testbed to find solutions to real-world problems using open data. We look forward to welcoming a range of specialists to come and work with us and become part of one of the most technologically advanced government projects in Europe contributing to the development of AI Gov stack that benefits the people across the globe. The AI testbed competition encompasses various works in the fields of data science, machine learning, and language technology, that open a way to contribute and become a part of developing the open-source AI Gov-stack. 

Pick a competition you are interested in and help us to build the coolEST and brightEST digital state!

# Background
Tartu or as it is also known as ¡°The City of great thoughts¡± is the second most populated city in Estonia. It is famous for its university, rich history and aspiration to innovate, and make citizens¡¯ lives better. 
Tartu has its own image bank, which contains 134 000 images divided into folders by the general topics. Unfortunately, images are not systematically labeled, which makes searching for the right picture complicated.

The accurate prediction model would help Tartu to make the public images searchable and reusable.

# Goal
The goal of this competition is to label the images from the image bank of the city of Tartu. 


# Praise
?	The best solutions will be publicly introduced to Estonian public sector.
?	The best solutions become a part of Estonian e-government [code repository]( https://koodivaramu.eesti.ee/explore/projects).
?	Social impact by creating a solution which would benefit a society as a whole.
?	Chance to virtually meet top Estonian data scientists and build your network.


----------------------
# Evaluation

# Metric
The evaluation metric for this competition is [Mean F-Score](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/F-score) or the arithmetic mean of individual F-scores. The F-score weights recall and precision equally. 

*-Precision is the ratio of true positives to all predicted positives.*
*-Recall is the ratio of true positives to all actual positives.* 

# Submission format
You should submit a csv file with the same number of rows + header row as in test.csv file. For every row in the test data, submission files should contain two columns: ``image_id`` (representing the id of the image) and ``labels`` (a **space separated list of the labels** of the image). Example format:

``image_id, labels``

``img102, l0 l1 l41``

``img103, l44``

``Etc.``

In the example above, we say that image with id **img102** includes labels **l0 l1 and l41**. All unique labels are described in ``labels.csv`` (read more under data section).","# Dataset Description

# Files

## images.zip
The compressed folder with all the images used in this challenge.

## labels.csv 
This file represents the unique labels that you should try to link to images. (NB: you should only link images with the labels found in this file). The file has 2 columns:

* ``label_id`` - the unique identifier of the label (should be used in the list of labels that you provide as the result variable).
* ``object`` - object that corresponds to this id (defined by human specialist).

## train.csv 
Training set that should be used to build your machine learning models. The training set includes both the image identificator and the ground truth about the ``labels`` of the image:

* ``image_id`` ¨C the id of the image (the image names match the ids),
* ``labels`` ¨C space separated list of label ids that correspond to this image.

## test.csv 
Test set should be used to see how well your model performs on unseen data. The test set includes the ``image_id`` but not the ``labels`` of the image. It is your job to predict the labels using the model you built on the training data!

## sample_submission.csv
An example of what the submission file should look like. For every row in the test data, submission files should contain two columns: ``image_id`` (representing the id of the image) and ``labels`` (a **space separated list of the labels** of the image). Example format:

``image_id, labels``

``img102, l0 l1 l41``

``img103, l44``

``Etc.``

In the example above, we say that image with id **img102** includes labels **l0 l1 and l41**. All unique labels are described in ``labels.csv`` (read more under data section).",False,True
6051,caps-in-museums-data,"# Description

Estonian government AI testbed competition invites everyone with expertise in data science and analysis to join an experimental testbed to find solutions to real-world problems using open data. We look forward to welcoming a range of specialists to come and work with us and become part of one of the most technologically advanced government projects in Europe contributing to the development of AI Gov stack that benefits people across the globe. The AI testbed competition encompasses various works in the fields of data science, machine learning, and language technology, that open a way to contribute and become a part of developing the open-source AI Govstack. 

Pick a competition you are interested in and help us to build the coolEST and brightEST digital state!

# Background 
Among European countries, Estonia has the most museums per 100,000 inhabitants.  
You can visit them physically or take a virtual trip via the Estonian museums information system to explore the abundant choice of collections. 

The Estonian museums information system [MuIS](https://www.muis.ee/en_GB/) is the online gateway to Estonian museums, giving everybody an opportunity to study the collections of the institutions that have joined the system. 

The information system contains almost 4 million objects, but not all of the objects are labeled ideally. Manual improvement of such state would take years. Thus, we ask you to help us by creating a smart model to fill in the gaps in the data based on existing descriptive variables.  
Let¡¯s improve Estonian museums information system and preserve Estonia¡¯s cultural heritage altogether!

# Goal
The goal of this competition is to create a model to predict the *`type`* of the museum object based on its descriptive variables. 


# Praise
* The best solutions will be publicly introduced to Estonian public sector.
* The best solutions become a part of Estonian e-government [code repository]( https://koodivaramu.eesti.ee/explore/projects).
* Social impact by creating a solution which would benefit a society as a whole.
* Chance to virtually meet top Estonian data scientists and build your network.




----------------------
# Evaluation

# Metric
The evaluation metric for this competition is [Accuracy]( https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Accuracy_and_precision#In_binary_classification) ¨C the number of correct predictions divided by the number of all predictions.

# Submission format
You should submit a csv file with the same number of rows + header row as in test.csv file. For every row in the test data, submission files should contain two columns: ``id`` (representing the id of the museum object) and ``type`` (representing the **type** of the object). Example format:

``id, type``

``2652198, foto``

``3851731, kiri``

``Etc.``

In the example above, we say that object with id **2652198** has a type **foto**. All unique types are described in ``unique_types.csv`` (read more under data section).","# Dataset Description

# Files

## unique_types.csv 
This file represents the unique museum object types that can be predicted. All types are in Estonian language.

## train.csv 
Training set that should be used to build your machine learning models. The training set includes both the descriptive variables of the museum object and the ground truth about the ``type`` of the object. 

## test.csv 
Test set should be used to see how well your model performs on unseen data. The test set includes the descriptive variables about the objects but not the ``type`` of the object. It is your job to predict it using the model you built on the training data!

## sample_submission.csv
An example of what the submission file should look like. For every row in the test data, submission files should contain two columns: ``id`` (representing the id of the museum object) and ``type`` (representing the **type** of the object). Example format:

``id, type``

``2652198, foto``

``3851731, kiri``

``Etc.``

*NB: the types in the sample file are picked randomly*.",False,True
