6055,twitter-likes-prediction-famous,"# Description

Welcome to the Twitter Likes Prediction Challenge! 

In this competition, your goal is to **develop a predictive model that can accurately estimate the number of likes received by individual tweets.**

Twitter is a popular social media platform where users can post short messages called tweets. The number of likes a tweet receives is a crucial metric that indicates its popularity and engagement level. By accurately predicting tweet likes, we can gain valuable insights into user preferences and behavior, which can be useful for content creators, marketers, and researchers.

## **You're allowed to use your own dataset to build the model. But don't try to search test data on twitter.**
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition will be **Root Mean Square Error (RMSE)**, which is a widely used measure to assess the performance of predictive models. RMSE calculates the square root of the average of squared differences between the predicted and actual number of likes. By taking the square root, RMSE provides a measure of the average magnitude of the prediction errors, giving us an indication of how close the predicted values are to the actual values. A lower RMSE indicates a better performance of the model in terms of accuracy.

RMSE has some advantages over Mean Absolute Error (MAE) as an evaluation metric. Unlike MAE, RMSE emphasizes larger errors due to the squaring operation, which can be beneficial in scenarios where large errors are considered more critical or impactful. RMSE is also more sensitive to outliers in the data, as their squared differences have a more pronounced effect on the overall metric.

In summary, for this competition, the lower the RMSE, the better the model's performance, indicating its ability to accurately predict the number of likes. Participants should focus on minimizing the RMSE by optimizing their models to make more precise predictions and reduce the overall prediction errors.
----------------------
# Data Card

In this competition, you will be provided with a large dataset of tweets from the popular social media platform Twitter. The goal is to develop a predictive model that can accurately estimate the number of likes received by individual tweets.

**You're allowed to use your own dataset to build the model. But don't try to search to test data on twitter.**

**Dataset:**
The dataset consists of a diverse collection of tweets, each associated with 2 features such as keyword for the tweet, tweet's content. Additionally, the dataset includes the actual number of likes received by each tweet, which serves as the target variable for prediction.

Clike [here](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/twitter-likes-prediction-famous/data) for detail description.

**To make this Kaggle competition more challenging, we add three more keywords that there're unseen in the training dataset.**

**Target Variable:**
The target variable for this challenge is the number of likes received by each tweet.

**Submission Format:**
Participants are required to submit a CSV file containing their predicted number of likes for the provided test dataset. The submission should include the tweet ID and the corresponding predicted number of likes.","# Dataset Description

Sure! Here's a description of the datasets provided for the Twitter Likes Prediction Challenge:

1. `train.csv`:
   - This dataset contains the training data for the competition.
   - Columns:
     - `keyword`: Represents the keyword associated with each tweet.
     - `likes`: Indicates the number of likes received by each tweet (the target variable).
     - `tweets`: Contains the text of each tweet.

2. `test.csv`:
   - This dataset contains the test data for which participants need to make predictions.
   - Columns:
     - `id`: Represents the unique identifier for each tweet in the test dataset.
     - `keyword`: Represents the keyword associated with each tweet.
     - `tweet`: Contains the text of each tweet.

3. `sample_submission.csv`:
   - This dataset provides a template for submitting predictions.
   - Columns:
     - `id`: Represents the unique identifier for each tweet in the test dataset.
     - `likes`: Participants need to fill in this column with their predicted number of likes for each tweet.

Participants are expected to train their predictive models using the `train.csv` dataset, make predictions for the tweets in the `test.csv` dataset, and submit their predictions in the same format as the `sample_submission.csv` file.

Note: It's important to carefully review the datasets to understand the structure, potential missing values, and any other specific requirements or characteristics that may affect the prediction task.",False,True
6056,spam-detection-using-tree-based-models,"# Description

In recent years social websites have become important components of the web. With their success, however has
come a growing influx of spam. If left unchecked, spam threatens to undermine resource sharing, interactivity and
openness. This article focuses on various countermeasures that are based on detection, demotion and prevention.
Although various countermeasures have been proposed for email and web spam but still there are challenges.

There are six main forms of spam, and they have different effects on Internet users like: 
(1) E-mail spam 
(2) comment spam
(3) Instant Messenger Spam
(4) Unsolicited text message
(5) social networking spam

Here the problem is to detect spam in emails. 
- Instead of the actual email text data, several features have been derived and provided in the training file ( like word frequency, capitalized word length etc).  
- Each row of data is content from one email.
- The challenge is to build a tree based model , to correctly identify the rows as spam(1) or non-spam(0).  
- F-Score will be used for model evaluation.

The competition is hosted by AccelerateAI , and is open to all to participate. 

Best Wishes
Team AccelerateAI 
Link: https://www.linkedin.com/company/74110157/","# Dataset Description

This is a spam detection challenge. Data is provide as word count of words that are deemed to be predictive of identifying a spam email. 

## Files
*   **train.csv** - the training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

## Column to  predict
*   `spam` - 1 if email is spam, 0 otherwise

## Other columns
*   `ID`?- row identifier 
*   `All other columns`?- word frequency (WF) and other useful features like capitalized test length etc.",False,True
6057,2022tae,"# Description

## Your Task
Each team is to develop an algorithm that determines what sort of sentiment a given set of tweet references - does the tweet have a *negative*, *neutral* or *positive* sentiment?

## Datasets
The following datasets are provided:

- **train.csv**: 20,610 tweets with the corresponding classification (sentiment) - integers ""1"", ""2"" and ""3"" indicate *negative*, *neutral* and *positive* sentiment, respectively.
- **test.csv**: 6,871 tweets with no labels - to be used for quantifying the performance of the algorithms.

## Rules
Please refer to the tab on 'Rules' and class slides provided on eDimension for more details.

----------------------
# Evaluation

##Performance Metric
The performance of the algorithms will be evaluated based on *classification accuracy* (the ratio between the number of correctly classified samples to the total number of samples). Randomly assigning labels to the tweets in the test dataset gives an accuracy of approximately 30%.

##Leaderboards
Kaggle will calculate the accuracy on two subsets of the test dataset - *public* and *private*. The *public* leaderboard will be available during the competition while the *private* leaderboard will only be available at the end of the competition.
----------------------
# Submission (Kaggle)

##Submission Format
Submit a .csv with two columns named ```id``` and ```sentiment```. Sticking with the exact capitalisation is advised as these headers must be present for evaluation.

Each entry of ```id``` corresponds to a single tweet as given in the test dataset **test.csv** with ```id``` sequentially increasing from 1 to 6871.

The ```sentiment``` is the sentiment label as predicted by you, an integer of 1, 2 or 3 (check that they are integers).

The first 5 lines of a .csv submission should have a format like the following:
| id   | sentiment |
| --- | ---             |
|     1 |               3 |
|     2 |               2 |
|     3 |               2 |
|     4|                 1|
|     5 |               3 |

Each team can submit up to 2 entries a day. The final day of submission is Aug 11, 23:59.##
----------------------
# Submission (eDimension)

Each team is to submit on eDimension a report along with your code and a README file (in a zipped folder).

The report is a short document (maximum 4 pages, font size 12) containing:

- a high-level description of the approach developed
- a short description of the results
- a brief discussion on interpretability and limits of the approach

The README file should contain a list of all dependencies and files need to run your code. Do note that your code must produce the same results as what you have uploaded on Kaggle.

The reports and code are due on Aug 14 23:59 (SGT).
----------------------
# Schedule

##Competition Schedule

| Date  | Event |
| --- | --- |
| August 3, 2022 | Announcement of the Data Competition  |
| August 3, 2022 15:00| Publication of problem details and competition rules |
| August 11, 2022 23:59 | Last opportunity for submitting the results on Kaggle |
| August 14, 2022 23:59 | Submission of reports, code, and peer evaluation form on eDimension|","# Dataset Description

##Files
All teams are provided with the following files - training dataset (**train.csv**) and test dataset (**test.csv**).

##Training dataset
- ```tweet``` - (character) a string of letters and symbols representing a tweet
- ```sentiment``` - (integer) an integer representing a sentiment corresponding to a tweet: 1 indicates *negative* sentiment, 2 indicates *neutral*, 3 indicates *positive*

##Test dataset
- ```id``` - (integer) id corresponding to a tweet in the test dataset
- ```tweet``` - (character) a string of letters and symbols representing a tweet which sentiment needs to be predicted",False,True
6058,cryptography,"# Description

This is hard machine learning problem. The task is to predict cryptographic SHA-256  hash output (10 first bits) using machine learning methods and without using detailed  knowledge of SHA-256 function. It is interesting to find out which methods can learn very complex non-linearities.

Input data is 10 dimensional vectors / strings of ASCII values (message) and output is 10 bits of y = SHA-256(message). Mean and standard deviation of SHA-256 bits are 0.50 meaning there are no easy statistics machine learning methods can use.

Have fun!
----------------------
# Evaluation

10 first bits of SHA-256 values calculated from an testing dataset must stored in CSV formated text file. Each line of ten 0-1 valued data contains a single SHA-256 10 bit hash value.

Competitors are ranked using mean absolute error of the testing dataset. The earlier submitter is ranked first if there are ties.

Please don¡¯t calculate correct values using SHA-256 hash function but predict the values using machine learning methods.","# Dataset Description

The task is simply to learn the function y=f(x). Input values x are in a CSV file **hash_train_input.csv** and output values y are in a **hash_train_output.csv**. There is an Id column to match x and y values in separate files although the rows are in order so you can just use x and y that are on the same row of the files.

For submission, you use file **test.csv** as x values and predict hash values y to a submission CSV file. An example of a submission file is in **sample_submission.csv**. The submission file must have a header row and the columns Id, Bit1, Bit2, Bit3, Bit4, Bit5, Bit6, Bit7, Bit8, Bit9, and Bit10.

## Files

*   **hash_train_input.csv** - the train input (x) dataset (ASCII strings)
*   **hash_train_output.csv** - the train output (y) dataset (SHA-256 hash values)
*   **test.csv** - the test input (x) dataset (inputs for submission)
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format (y values, predicted SHA-256 hash values)

## Columns

*   `Id` - Id number field of the vector
*   `Char%d` - %d:th character column of the input x (10 letter ASCII string)  
*   `Bit%d` - %d:th bit column of the output y (SHA-256 hash first 10 bits)",False,True
6059,predict-sleep-interruption,"# Description

We have two different datasets which will help predict if the person will wake up in middle of night or not. The hypothesis behind this competition is that  food consumed that day or that day + few last day AND few previous night sleeps should have effect on if a person will wake up or not. You can also bring external data sources like moon position, external weather condition as well. Geographical location for this data set is Dallas, Tx, USA.

**Dataset 1**
this dataset contains information on sleep. it contains following fields:
1. time to bed: this field tells when the subject went to bed. e.g. if can be like 8:30 PM etc.
2. time to fall asleep: this field tells how long it took the subject to fall asleep. (e.g. 30 min or 45 min et.)
3. Mid night predictions: this field will tell if the person woke up in middle of night. 0 means they woke up once, 1 means means woke up once, 2 means woke up twice and so on.
4. Final wake up time.
5. calendar date: this will tell which day data belongs to. if it says 12 june, that means it was the night between 11 and 12.

**Dataset 2:**
This will contain information on what they person eats on a given day. some of the key fields are:
1. calender date: date when meal was taken
2. mealTime: time when meal was taken
3. meailType: if this was breakfast/lunch/dinner/snacks
4. food: food that was taken
5. set of fields that show different nutrition amounts in each of the foods.

we will judge the best model based on the F1 score for prediction. 
----------------------
# Evaluation



The evaluation metric for this competition is [Mean F1-Score](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/F-score). The F1 score, commonly used in information retrieval, measures accuracy using the statistics precision and recall.


The F1 metric weights recall and precision equally, and a good retrieval algorithm will maximize both precision and recall simultaneously. Thus, moderately good performance on both will be favored over extremely good performance on one and poor performance on the other.

## Submission Format

**For every calendar date in the dataset **, submission files should contain two columns: `calendarDate` and midNightWakeUp. 


The file should contain a header and have the following format:
```
calendarDate,MidNightWakeUp
2022-07-22,1
```","# Dataset Description

**Sleep Data**
this dataset contains information on sleep. it contains following fields:

time to bed: this field tells when the subject went to bed. e.g. if can be like 8:30 PM etc.
time to fall asleep: this field tells how long it took the subject to fall asleep. (e.g. 30 min or 45 min et.)
Mid night predictions: this field will tell if the person woke up in middle of night. 0 means they woke up once, 1 means means woke up once, 2 means woke up twice and so on.
Final wake up time.
calendar date: this will tell which day data belongs to. if it says 12 june, that means it was the night between 11 and 12.
**Diet Data:(historical and future)**
This will contain information on what they person eats on a given day. some of the key fields are:

calender date: date when meal was taken
mealTime: time when meal was taken
meailType: if this was breakfast/lunch/dinner/snacks
food: food that was taken
set of fields that show different nutrition amounts in each of the foods.",False,True
6060,competencia-ml-economistas-usta,"# Description

# **Competencia ML - Clasificaci¨®n Econom¨ªa Laboral** ??

Dentro de la econom¨ªa laboral surgen problemas que podr¨ªan ser analizados con herramientas extra¨ªdas del Machine Learning. En esta competencia se exponen datos que permiten dar uso a diferentes algoritmos de clasificaci¨®n con el fin de separar a individuos en dos grupos (empleados y desempleados) sujeto a una gama de variables.  

## **?Cu¨¢l es el reto?** ??

Esta competencia busca la creaci¨®n de algoritmos de clasificaci¨®n que logre categorizar a un grupo de individuos en empleado o desempleado, para esto se brinda un set de datos de entrenamiento `train.csv` con 15516 observaciones y 10 variables con informaci¨®n relevante. Usted puede realizar cualquier tipo de selecci¨®n, transformaci¨®n o estandarizaci¨®n sobre las variables. 

Se recomienda ser cuidadoso con el manejo de los datos tanto de entrenamiento como de testeo, puesto que estos presentan algunos errores naturales en el proceso de minado de datos (Missing values, duplicados etc.)

----------------------
# Evaluation

# **Evaluaci¨®n Algoritmos** ??

La evaluaci¨®n de los algoritmos creados se realizar por medio de la m¨¦trica [accuracy](https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/generated/sklearn.metrics.accuracy_score.html), la cual simplemente determina el numero de observaciones clasificadas correctamente. Esta m¨¦trica se aplica a los resultados que genera la aplicaci¨®n del algoritmo sobre el grupo de testeo `test.csv`.

## **Formato de Subida Resultados** ??


Para subir los resultados y conocer el puntaje obtenido seleccione la opci¨®n de `Sumbit Predictions`, el formato de subida debe ser un archivo con formato `.csv` con dos columnas, la primera `id`, la cual corresponde a los n¨²meros de identificaci¨®n de los individuos, estos pueden ser extra¨ªdos del mismo archivo `test.csv`, la otra columna se debe llamar 'empleado' y corresponde a las predicciones de los grupos sobre los datos de testeo.

Ejemplo de formato del archivo:

```
id,empleado
125,0
407,1
302,1
```","# Dataset Description

