6098,forta-protect-web3,"# Description

# What is Forta?

[Forta](https://forta.org/) is a real-time detection network for security & operational monitoring of blockchain activity
As a decentralized monitoring network, Forta detects threats and anomalies on DeFi, NFT, governance, bridges, and other Web3 systems in real-time to protect the web3 ecosystem.

[![Forta Intro Youtube Video](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/3497137/188163437-70d0ae07-ce0c-4840-aad8-08612bcaf211.gif)](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FNj_Kqc3fe8)

[Forta Blog Post introducing this competition](https://forta.org/blog/predicting-phishing-kaggle/)

[Watch a 2-minute clip on how Forta protects Web3](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FNj_Kqc3fe8)

# Problem Statement

Chainalysis estimated scam losses of 7.7 billion dollars for 2021 in [their 2022 cyber crime report](https://go.chainalysis.com/2022-Crypto-Crime-Report.html). In August alone, [Web3Rekt](https://www.web3rekt.com/) recorded 34 scams, targeting web3 users ranging from NFT owners to De-Fi users. The owners of [NFT: Bored Ape Yacht Club](https://opensea.io/collection/boredapeyachtclub) fell victim to a scam (see [New BAYC Owner Scammed Just Two Hours After Purchasing $116K NFT
](https://beincrypto.com/new-bayc-owner-scammed-just-two-hours-after-purchasing-116k-nft) ). On August 9th, 2022, Curve Finance users were tricked into token approvals, and their wallets were quickly drained (see [Curve Finance Rekt](https://rekt.news/curve-finance-rekt/)).

These attacks are commonly referred to as [ice phishing attacks](https://forta.org/blog/how-to-derail-a-120-million-dollar-hack/). On the Forta Network, there is a heuristic-based bot that attempts to identify these ice phishing attacks (see [Ice Phishing 2.0 bot](https://explorer.forta.network/bot/0x8badbf2ad65abc3df5b1d9cc388e419d9255ef999fb69aac6bf395646cf01c14)). However, the bot emits false positive alerts, identifying many approval events that are not part of an actual scam.

Machine learning can surface potential threats with high confidence and reduce most false alarms often seen in heuristic based systems. For example, ML can detect future phishing activity by analyzing past phishing attacks and transaction flow. The authors of a research paper: [¡°Who Are the Phishers?¡°](https://arxiv.org/abs/1911.09259) gathered phishing related labels from [Etherscan](https://etherscan.io/accounts/label/phish-hack) and [CryptoScamDB](https://github.com/CryptoScamDB/EtherScamDB), and created a ML solution that detects phishing attacks with 92.7% precision and 89.3% recall. A ML model like this can be deployed in a [Forta detection bot](https://docs.forta.network/en/latest/getting-started/). Block by block or for every incoming transaction, the bot can invoke the model to predict if any addresses are involved in a phishing attack. 

# Objective

Your goal is to develop a machine learning model that can accurately predict if an Ethereum Externally Owned Account (EOA) is a phishing scammer. For each `Id` in the test set, your task is to predict the value of the `target`: 0 for benign EOAs, 1 for phishing scammers. 

The training set includes a random sample of unlabelled EOAs and a small sample (<1%) of EOAs involved in phishing scams. The labels were derived from addresses labeled with `phish/hack` on [Etherscan](https://etherscan.io/accounts/label/phish-hack). 

We provided train/test EOA transactions, traces, logs, and even statistics on one-degree neighbors' transactions for a given timeframe. The data is time-sliced in the following way:

-  Train transactions and metadata executed before January 16, 2022. 
-  Test transactions and metadata executed before July 18, 2022.

You are free to use all or some of the provided data. We also provided additional data sources you can tap into in the [Tutorial and Resources section](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/forta-protect-web3/overview/tutorials-and-resources).

----------------------
# Evaluation

## Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated on [Average Precision](https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/generated/sklearn.metrics.average_precision_score.html). Average Precision calculates average precision of all prediction scores at different thresholds. This metric produces a value between 0 and 1 where a value closer to 1 indicates that your model can accurately detect all past phishing scammer EOAs without mislabelling too many benign EOAs as phishing scammers. 

## Submission File Format

The file should contain a header and have the following format:
```
Id,Predicted
42,0.99
43,0.25
44,0.53
....
```
You can download an example submission file (sample_submission.csv) on the [Data page](http://www.kaggle.com/competitions/forta/data).
----------------------
# Tutorials And Resources

# Ethereum Intro

- [Intro to Ethereum](https://ethereum.org/en/developers/docs/intro-to-ethereum/)
- [Solidity, Blockchain, and Smart Contract Course ¨C Beginner to Expert Python Tutorial](https://youtu.be/M576WGiDBdQ)

# Additional Data Sources

- [Luabase](https://luabase.com) - Query blockchain data with SQL and request data in real-time via an API.
- [Etherscan API](https://etherscan.io/apis) - Data from Etherscan's Ethereum (ETH) Block Explorer
- [Ethplorer API](https://github.com/EverexIO/Ethplorer/wiki/Ethplorer-API) - Query information about Ethereum tokens, balances, addresses, history of transactions, contracts, and more.
- [BigQuery Public Blockchain Datasets](https://github.com/blockchain-etl/public-datasets) - Available to query using BigQuery on Google Cloud Platform.

# Phishing Detection Academic Research

- [Who Are the Phishers? Phishing Scam Detection on Ethereum via Network Embedding](https://arxiv.org/abs/1911.09259)
- [Phishing Scam Detection on Ethereum: Towards Financial Security for
Blockchain Ecosystem](https://www.ijcai.org/proceedings/2020/0621.pdf)

## Notebooks and Tutorials

- [Benchmark model: Easy Ensemble with One-order neighbor features](https://www.kaggle.com/code/mgw917/benchmark-model-easyensemble)
- [How to tell if an address is a smart contract or an EOA?](https://web3py.readthedocs.io/en/stable/web3.eth.html#web3.eth.Eth.get_code)

# More about Forta

- [Forta Docs](https://docs.forta.network/en/latest/)
- [Doc: Data Science and Machine Learning on Forta](https://docs.forta.network/en/latest/ds-and-ml/)
- [Blog: Three Ways Machine Learning Can Detect Web3 Security Threats](https://forta.org/blog/three-ways-machine-learning-can-detect-web3-security-threats/)

----------------------
# Prizes

# Competition Prizes

**1st Prize:** A Unique Forta POAP and getting featured on [Forta Blogs](https://forta.org/blog/) and on [Twitter](https://twitter.com/FortaNetwork). 
**2nd Prize**: A Unique Forta POAP and getting featured on [Forta Blogs](https://forta.org/blog/) and on [Twitter](https://twitter.com/FortaNetwork)
**3rd Prize**: A Unique Forta POAP and getting featured on [Forta Blogs](https://forta.org/blog/) and on [Twitter](https://twitter.com/FortaNetwork)

To be eligible for a prize, you will be asked to:
- share code with documentation
- submit a valid email and Discord username through this [Google form](https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSet5F1DeO52wDavMEB2a1sI1vyH8qc-SRJ6l7IkkoRVYeCw4Q/viewform)

For any questions, please contact a Forta Moderator on Discord: https://discord.gg/ehKhTDmbHT","# Dataset Description

## File Descriptions

### Training and Test Data

*   **train.csv** - Ethereum Externally Owned Accounts and their ground truth values.
*   **test.csv** - Ethereum Externally Owned Accounts to identify as phishing scammers. 
*   **eoa_tx_(train|test).zip** - List of transactions where Ethereum EOAs were involved as a sender or a receiver. All transactions were executed before January 16, 2022. For the test set, all transactions were executed before July 18, 2022. 
*   **eoa_trace_(train|test).zip** - List of internal transactions triggered inside EOA¡¯s transactions and internal transactions where EOA were involved as a sender or receiver. All internal transactions are executed before January 16, 2022. For the test set, all internal transactions were executed before July 18, 2022. 
*   **eoa_log_(train|test).zip** - List of event logs produced by EOA¡¯s transactions and internal transactions.
* **one_order.zip** - Folder of csv files on EOAs¡¯ first-degree neighbor¡¯s transaction activity. 
*   **one_order_from_in_stats_(train|test).csv** - Incoming transaction value and time related statistics for EOAs¡¯ first-degree neighbors who sent a transaction to the EOAs (aka ¡°from neighbor¡±)
*   **one_order_from_out_stats_(train|test).csv** - Outgoing transaction value and time related statistics for EOAs¡¯ first-degree neighbors who sent a transaction to the EOAs (aka ¡°from neighbor¡±)
*   **one_order_to_in_stats_(train|test).csv** - Incoming transaction value and time related statistics for EOAs¡¯ first-degree neighbors who received a transaction from the EOAs (aka ¡°to neighbor¡±)
*   **one_order_to_out_stats_(train|test).csv** - Outgoing transaction value and time related statistics for EOAs¡¯ first-degree neighbors who received a transaction from the EOAs (aka ¡°to neighbor¡±)

### Sample Submission & Data Description
*   **sample_submission.csv** - example submission.
*   **data_description.md** - Full description of each column.",False,True
6099,classification-of-covid-19-tweets-capstonefall2022,"# Description

Please don't post your solutions before the competition is over.

###File descriptions

- training_data.csv - 240,000 unique tweets about Covid-19 coming from six different English-speaking countries (each country has 40,000 tweets in total). Categorical outcome variable is called 'country'.

- test_data.csv - 60,000 unique tweets for which you will be predicting the 'country' variable. 'Id' variable shows the index values associated with each tweet.

- sample_submission.csv - a .csv file that shows the format using which you will submit your solutions for the competition ('Id' variable corresponds to the tweets found in the test dataset).

###Data fields

- text: The text of tweet (including emojis, htmls, hashtags)
- reply_to_screen_name: The Twitter screen name of the user the owner of the tweet is replying to (if any)
- is_quote: A Boolean variable that indicates if the owner of the tweet is quoting someone else¡¯s tweet
- is_retweet: A Boolean variable that indicates if the owner of the tweet is retweeting someone else¡¯s tweet
- hashtags: A list of hashtags included in the tweet
- country: The label of the country in which the tweet was posted (found only in the training dataset) 
- Id: An index number associated with tweets (found only on the test dataset)
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is <a href=""https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Accuracy_and_precision""> Accuracy</a>. Accuracy is a frequently used cost function to calculate how close or far off a given set of measurements (observations or readings) are to their true value.</p>",# Dataset Description,False,True
6100,telecom-churn-case-study-hackathon-C39,"# Description

# Problem Statement

In the telecom industry, customers are able to choose from multiple service providers and actively switch from one operator to another. In this highly competitive market, the telecommunications industry experiences an average of 15-25% annual churn rate. Given the fact that it costs 5-10 times more to acquire a new customer than to retain an existing one, customer retention has now become even more important than customer acquisition.

For many incumbent operators, retaining high profitable customers is the number one business
goal. To reduce customer churn, telecom companies need to **predict which customers are at high risk of churn**. In this project, you will analyze customer-level data of a leading telecom firm, build predictive models to identify customers at high risk of churn.

In this competition, your goal is to build a machine learning model that is able to predict churning customers based on the features provided for their usage.

----------------------
# Evaluation

## Goal

It is your job to predict if a customer will churn, given the ~170 columns containing customer behavior, usage patterns, payment patterns, and other features that might be relevant. Your target variable is ""churn_probability""

Note: Make sure your accuracy is greater than the sample submission that is present in the leaderboard

## Metric

Submissions are evaluated on [Classification Accuracy](https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/model_evaluation.html#accuracy-score) between the value of the predicted value and the actual value of churn for each of the customers. 

![Accuracy score formula](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/7bfe40cea126a04004b82f729cf7df1ec435fbf6)

The public leaderboard is going to rank your submission against other users while the competition is active, however, once the competition is ended, the final ranks will be calculated on the private leaderboard.

## Submission file format

The file should contain a header and have the following format (CSV):

```
id,churn_probability
70005,0.0
70006,1.0
70007,0.0
etc.
```
A sample file can be found attached in the Data section of this competition.","# Dataset Description

## File descriptions:
- **train.csv**: Contains 172 columns. The primary key that represents each customer is `id`. The target variable that you need to predict is `churn_probability` which contains a value of 0 or 1. This data is what you are going to use for EDA, cleaning, feature engineering, model building, model evaluation, model selection, and finally model training.


- **test.csv**: Contains 171 columns, doesn't contain the target variable `churn_probability`. You will apply all the necessary preprocessing steps to get this data into the right format and then use the model trained using the *train.csv* file to make predictions with this. This is unseen data! Only the competition hosts know the actual values of the target feature for this data and therefore your submissions will be evaluated on how well your model does with this dataset.


- **sample.csv**: This contains the format in which you need to submit the solutions to Kaggle. The `id` column in this dataset exactly the same as the `id` column in *test.csv*. You will make your predictions on the *test.csv* data and store them in a submission file that has the same format as this file. Check the **Overview>Evaluation** tab for mode details.


- **data_dictionary.csv**: This contains the definitions for the various acronyms that you will need to understand each variable. For example, the variable `total_og_mou_7`, contains the acronyms `total`, `og`, `mou`, and `7`, which can be translated as `the total outgoing minutes of voice calls made by the user in month of July`.

## Data Definitions

The definitions are also listed down below:

- CIRCLE_ID : Telecom circle area to which the customer belongs to
- LOC : Local calls - within same telecom circle
- STD : STD calls - outside the calling circle
- IC : Incoming calls
- OG : Outgoing calls
- T2T : Operator T to T, i.e. within same operator (mobile to mobile)
- T2M : Operator T to other operator mobile
- T2O : Operator T to other operator fixed line
- T2F : Operator T to fixed lines of T
- T2C : Operator T to it¡¯s own call center
- ARPU : Average revenue per user
- MOU : Minutes of usage - voice calls
- AON : Age on network - number of days the customer is using the operator T network
- ONNET : All kind of calls within the same operator network
- OFFNET : All kind of calls outside the operator T network
- ROAM : Indicates that customer is in roaming zone during the call
- SPL : Special calls
- ISD : ISD calls
- RECH : Recharge
- NUM : Number
- AMT : Amount in local currency
- MAX : Maximum
- DATA : Mobile internet
- 3G : 3G network
- AV : Average
- VOL : Mobile internet usage volume (in MB)
- 2G : 2G network
- PCK : Prepaid service schemes called - PACKS
- NIGHT : Scheme to use during specific night hours only
- MONTHLY : Service schemes with validity equivalent to a month
- SACHET : Service schemes with validity smaller than a month
- *.6 : KPI for the month of June
- *.7 : KPI for the month of July
- *.8 : KPI for the month of August
- FB_USER : Service scheme to avail services of Facebook and similar social networking sites
- VBC : Volume based cost - when no specific scheme is not purchased and paid as per usage",False,True
