6111,com3025-2022-challenge-week-6-7-extended-extend,"# Description

# Overview
This challenge concerns the automated recognition of visual objects in images using machine learning techniques. The dataset you will work including 23 types of objects in realistic scenes, where training samples and their ground truth are provided. You will develop a machine learning model that will make predictions on unseen test data. The performance will be measured upon submission of the prediction results. A leaderboard will be published after each submission. **You need to use your URN as the Team Name** in the Kaggle page. **Otherwise, your score won't be recorded.** Multiple submissions are encouraged if you wish to improve your models. The latest model for each student will be used for the assessment. You may save multiple prediction results and choose the best one for the final submission.

You can use Swin-Transformer in this week's lab sheet to do this challenge.

# Important Rule
You need to use your URN as the Team Name in the Kaggle page. Otherwise, your score won't be recorded.

# Code Submission
You need to submit your code to Surreylearn before the code submission due date (see SurreyLearn for further detail)

# Participation
Please click the join competition button on the page to join it. Otherwise, you cannot submit your result.


# Task 
### Overview
This is a dataset about **Visual Object Classification**. The goal of this challenge is to recognize objects from a number of visual object classes in realistic scenes. There are twenty-three object classes in the datasets:
* person, hand, foot, head
* bird, cat, cow, dog, horse, sheep
* aeroplane, bicycle, boat, bus, car, motorbike, train
* bottle, chair, dining table, potted plant, sofa, tv/monitor


### Dataset Size
- Training set: 5011 images
- Testing set: 4952 images
### Training and Testing Data
We will provide the images and labels for the training set and images for the testing set.

# Evaluation
We will evaluate your prediction result on the testing set. So you will need to submit the result on this competition page. **The minimum requirement of this challenge is 1.0 Mean ColumnWise Log Loss (lower result indicate better performance).**
 
# Submission Format
Your submission should be a csv file, with 24 columns in it, the first is the id of the test image and the second to 24th are the predicted probability of each visual object class (e.g., person 0.49, cat 0.97, dog 0.01). We will provide an example of the submission file in the Data Section.
----------------------
# Evaluation

# The evaluation metric for this competition is Mean ColumnWise Log Loss.

## Submission Format

submission files should contain twenty-four columns: `file_name` and `label` for each type of visual object. A header will be needed for the submission, so the first line will start with the header information.

We provide a template of the submission file in the Data Section. **You must follow the order of tags in the template in your submission**.

The file header **must** contain a header and have the following format:
```
file_name, person, hand, foot, ...
000191.jpg, 0.48365255458278655, 0.055888978482053875, 0.5992317190609185 ...
003498.jpg, 0.6553363657832338, 0.8178946666280492, 0.8834546850516083, ...
005957.jpg, 0.8320555710717549, 0.8207940776278797, 0.21887284952453545, ...
...
```

An example submission is provided on the data page.","# Dataset Description

# Directory Structure
- train_data
  - The image files for the training set
- train.csv
  - The labels for the training set
- test_data
  - The image files for the test set
- submission_sample.csv
  - The sample of submission file

---",False,True
6112,etu-ai-club-competition-1,"# Description

In this competition, you need to make a binary classification on an imbalanced dataset. We recommend to follow three main steps for beginners:

-Feature Engineering
-Model Selection
-Hyperparameter Tuning
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is [Macro F1-Score](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/F-score). The F1 score, commonly used in information retrieval, measures accuracy using the statistics precision and recall.

The F1 metric weights recall and precision equally, and a good retrieval algorithm will maximize both precision and recall simultaneously. Thus, moderately good performance on both will be favored over extremely good performance on one and poor performance on the other.

## Submission Format

**For every instance in the dataset**, submission files should contain two columns: `id` and `Label`.","# Dataset Description

""train.csv"", ""test.csv"" and ""sample_ submission.csv"" files can be downloaded here. You need to predict the Label which consists of 0s and 1s.
 
## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

## Columns

*   `id` - unique identifier for each instance
*   `feature_0`?- feature_0 to be trained ...
*   `Label`?- the class to be predicted",False,True
6113,eeg-biometric-competition,"# Description


# 1. Challenge Description

In the current digital information society, the biometrics technique is an essential tool in many personal and enterprise security systems. Biometrics is an important personal authentication or identification technique based on biological measurements, including physical, physiological, or behavioral characteristics. Compared with existing voice recognition, fingerprint scanning, and facial recognition, EEG-based biometrics has unique advantages (e.g., it is more difficult to be stolen, forged, or damage, and it must be detected in vivo). Hence, EEG-based biometrics is capable of providing a more secure biometric method for identity recognition.

However, at present, EEG-based biometric technique is still in the exploratory stage. Current EEG-based biometric studies are mostly focused on a small number of samples and lack evaluation of multi-session and multi-task EEG recordings, which made the development of EEG-based biometric systems challenging with their uniqueness, permanence, and robustness. At the same time, there is no open EEG-based biometric competition and a suitable database. The lack of a unified test benchmark and platform seriously hinders the development of this field. To this end, we collected the M3CV (A **M**ulti-subject, **M**ulti-session, and **M**ulti-task Database for Investigation of EEG **C**ommonality and **V**ariability) dataset and launched an EEG-based biometric competition.

Compared with the existing open dataset, this competition list four challenges as follows:

1. Larger population size
2. Multiple sessions of EEG recording
3. Multiple tasks of EEG recording
4. The intruders for the identification and verification

# 2. Task Definition
With the 106 subjects, 2 sessions, and 14 types of EEG recording in M3CV database, we segmented the EEG signal and sorted them out of order.  The  EEG-based biometric competition was launched by focusing on the problems of personal identification and verification. Based on the enrollment dataset, the participant needs to train the prediction model for the biometric system. After the model has been trained, the participant would be asked to complete the following two prediction tasks:
- **Identification**: Determine whether a given EEG signal comes from a subject in the Enrollment Set, and further determine the subject ID if he or she is not an intruder.
- **Verification**: Determine whether a given EEG signal comes from a certain subject.


# 3. Dataset Provider
**HUANG Gan** Assistant Professor
**LIANG Zhen** Assistant Professor
**ZHANG  Zhiguo** Professor
<img src=""https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F10539915%2F347d5e94941e0daad9e85f2e406bbaa8%2F0101151s.png?generation=1662828199475233&alt=media"" width=""450"" height=""60"">

----------------------
# Evaluation

For each epoch in the Testing.zip file, participants need to make the corresponding prediction for each epoch according to different usage in the Testing_Info.csv file and submit result_name_date.csv file

* The usage 3 indicates the Identification mode. Participants need to judge whether the epoch is from an intruder. If yes, returns 0. If not, return the predicted subject ID.
* The usage 4 indicates the verification mode. Participants needs to judge whether the epoch is from subject as he or she claimed. If yes, return 1. If not, return 0.

A baseline method is provided with the following link,
[Baseline.zip](https://storage.googleapis.com/kaggle-competitions-data/kaggle-v2/37941/4046133/compressed/Baseline.zip?GoogleAccessId=web-data@kaggle-161607.iam.gserviceaccount.com&amp;Expires=1659170547&amp;Signature=xCow0WpxZ%2FJLcy48CSpMBPpRxFLiJtduvJ1NPrv6jo0Uw80OPCAmODe5OfnWOTD2l40cPiIQRAq8A16FPm2GuV%2FuvQtDZ2S%2F6MQNR7IBcbxG9vBFX3xzzE%2Ffw2EarkZmsENkFLQkC%2FNQ9EsD0vBeZqsoI80WWV19u47iOJN5dkrvGBfF7kh%2B2z5VUVzVHjdCIyIHfrgx8dc3SeRs6d1%2B9IosVGpVG2%2B5olkhABafafYjGQI1wf%2BclQQwlnAPX2v7F0Z5ei3uOcTAGzUHPMBhGh5MLTnNwAh38rRD%2BNux%2F5QuvU6p6ZX1D6RS8Q5t%2FNb%2BYBFj%2F%2BbEbzgZ734ahhKCXw%3D%3D&amp;response-content-disposition=attachment%3B+filename%3DBaseline.zip)

The mean recognition accuracy for all the identification and verification tasks is used as the performance metric for this competiution.. 


## Submission Format

**For every author in the dataset**, submission files should contain two columns: `EpochID` and `Prediction`. `EpochID` should be the ID of the epoch. `Prediction` shoule be the prediction result for each epoch.


The file should contain a header and have the following format:
```
EpochID,Prediction
epoch057852,43
epoch057853,43
epoch057854,1
epoch057855,6
epoch057856,0
epoch057857,0
epoch057858,0
epoch057859,52
```

An example submission is also provided,
[Result\_example\_0909\_232857.csv](https://storage.googleapis.com/kagglesdsdata/competitions/38725/4244254/Result_example_0909_232857.csv?GoogleAccessId=web-data@kaggle-161607.iam.gserviceaccount.com&amp;Expires=1663128258&amp;Signature=EiMYqH3wVjkKSVtxlz7DskJJNnq3eYz%2BAO7zm6srrIdmTh8J6JhiP4gtErgUKzYmyeVAi7DLIy6c4JVo%2BWi3TOUI975oO2j0MKPzMDJaFH3xL7bUvpv7pYSgb3lh7UCUQgA%2B%2BY96%2FSNB%2FhXtz0x05uRziqgATJxYCxHlWc8lZ7J8%2Bzx3fYCMci5rr5F1Lvq5xVORTxm0lp1ckivSxh2IwU6OA5ZVWOJwJ9Rra1Gx7A3tzpZTUXi9M7ZijUzyyZNPFlBz90%2Fcr4aNvlQsrNunxrQsQOJU1coL3YdMzLi4IFH3JVzp068Z58PTavtGZKwfSK1BYbMloCIkcBx6ZIuIGA%3D%3D&amp;response-content-disposition=attachment%3B+filename%3DResult_example_0909_232857.csv)

## Reference:
Gan Huang, Zhenxing Hu, Weize Chen, Zhen Liang, Linling Li, Li Zhang, Zhiguo Zhang. M3CV:A Multi-subject, Multi-session, and Multi-task database for EEG-based Biometrics Challenge. doi: [https://doi.org/10.1101/2022.06.28.497624](https://www.biorxiv.org/content/10.1101/2022.06.28.497624v1)[[PDF](https://www.biorxiv.org/content/10.1101/2022.06.28.497624v1.full.pdf)]


----------------------
# Experiment

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F10539915%2F33d8d24f7942a638e6ef616e5b888881%2Ffig2.png?generation=1658843768532966&alt=media)


A reliable EEG-based biometric system should be able to withstand changes in an individual's mental state (cross-task test) and still be able to successfully identify an individual after several days  (cross-session test). Here we built an EEG dataset M3CV with 106 subjects, two sessions of experiment on different days, and multiple paradigms. Ninety-five of the subjects participated in two sessions of the experiments, separated by more than 6 days. The experiment includes 6 common EEG experimental  paradigms including resting state,  sensory and cognitive task, and brain-computer interface. To the best of our knowledge, no previous studies have evaluated the test performance of EEG-based biometric algorithms in cross-paradigm and cross-session scenarios  on the public dataset with more than 100 subjects.



1. <font color=#3A729A>**Resting-state**</font>: Runs 01 and 14 were resting-state EEG signals with eyes-closed (<font color=red>**EC**</font>), while Runs 02 and 15 were resting-state EEG signals with eyes-open (<font color=red>**EO**</font>), each lasting for 1 min. In the eyes-open run, the subjects were required to keep their eyes fixed in front and blink as little as possible.
3. <font color=#3A729A> **Transient-state sensory**</font>: EEG elicited by visual, auditory, and somatosensory stimuli were recorded in Runs 04 and 11. For each run, 30 trials of <font color=red>**VEP**</font>,<font color=red> **AEP**</font>, and <font color=red>**SEP**</font> were arranged in random order. Each stimulus lasted 50 milliseconds, and the inter-stimulus interval (ISI) was set at 2¨C4 s. On average, each run lasted 4.5 mins.
4. <font color=#3A729A>**Steady-state sensory**</font>: A train of visual, auditory, and somatosensory stimuli were released in Runs 05, 09, and 12 respectively. Considering the different frequency responses of different modality stimuli, and the higher signal-to-noise ratio of SSVEP compared to SSAEP and SSSEP, the stimulation frequencies and recording times were different. They were 10 Hz and 1 min for <font color=red>**SSVEP**</font> in Run 05, 45.38 Hz and 2 mins for<font color=red> **SSAEP**</font> in Run 09, and 22.04 Hz and 2 mins for <font color=red>**SSSEP**</font> in Run 12.
5. <font color=#3A729A>**P300**</font>:: A visual oddball experiment was arranged in Run 07 with the red square as the <font color=red>**target**</font> stimuli and a white square as the <font color=red>**nontarget**</font> stimuli on the screen. Each square lasted 80 ms with the ISI 200 ms. Six hundred trials of stimuli were arranged in 2 mins, in which target stimuli had a possibility of 5% (30 trials of target stimulation and 570 trials of nontarget stimulation). The subjects were asked to count the number of red squares and report after the run so that their attention would remain on the screen.
6.	<font color=#3A729A>**Motor Execution**</font>: Executed movement was performed in Runs 03, 06, 10, and 13. During these runs, the subjects were instructed to respond to a visual cue by gripping their left hand (<font color=red>**LH**</font>) or right hand (<font color=red>**RH**</font>), or by lifting their right ankle (<font color=red>**FT**</font>) for a duration of 3 s, i.e., until cue offset. No feedback was provided during the online recording. To minimize the intra- and inter-subject variability, we tried to ensure the consistency of the movements for all subjects. Hence, the executed movement was used instead of motor imagery in classical BCI experiments, and no feedback or training was given to the subjects.
7. <font color=#3A729A>**SSVEP with selective attention**</font>: Six white squares flashed simultaneously at different frequencies in Run 08. First, the target square turned red for 200 ms. After 500 ms, all squares with the frequency of 7 Hz, 8 Hz, 9 Hz, 11 Hz, 13 Hz, and 15 Hz began to flash and lasted 10 s. The subject was asked to focus on the target square during the 10 s. In total, there were 12 trials of <font color=red>**SSVEP-SA**</font> arranged in Run 08.","# Dataset Description

## Enrollment, Calibration and Testing Set
In this competition, the M3CV dataset is splited into the Enrollment Set, Calibration Set, and Testing Set:

1. Enrollment Set: the EEG data of session 1 from 95 subjects 
    - For the participants to train the biometric model.
2. Calibration Set: the EEG data of session 2 from 20 subjects
    - For the participants to observe the EEG data, analyze the commonalities and variability between different subjects, different days, and different paradigms, and conduct offline comparison between different prediction models.
    - Not all the EEG epoches in session 2 in these 20 subjects are provided in the calibration set. Part of the other EEG epoches has been arranged in testing set with subject ID unlabeled.
3. Testing Set: the EEG data of session 2 from 86 subjects
    - Public leaderboard : around 10% of epochs in testing set
    - Private leaderboard : around 90% epochs in testing set
    - 11 intruders have been contained in the testing set, which does not appear in the enrollment set.
    - The information for which epoch belongs to the public leaderboard  or the private leaderboard is hidden.

![Data format.jpg](https://pickle-ceramic-98c.notion.site/image/https%3A%2F%2Fs3-us-west-2.amazonaws.com%2Fsecure.notion-static.com%2F4d5fdde1-d452-417f-bf23-46cb72e6cfdc%2FData_format.jpg?table=block&id=661b7e86-59fa-44d1-975a-ea200141c05a&spaceId=9b832dc9-0403-4155-a62e-d4bf0be641dd&width=2000&userId=&cache=v2)




## Data format and signal preprocessing
All epoches were arranged in the mat file,named epochxxxxxx.mat, with a 2D-matrix epoch_data £¨$65\times 1000$ £©in it. Rows 1-64 correspond to 64 channels of EEG signal, row 65 corresponds to the marker information. xxxxxx are the random ID for each epoch. BrainAmp (brain products GmbH, Germany) was used for EEG recording with the original sampling rate 1000Hz. Each epoch are segmented from the preprocessed EEG signal, the preprocessing step including
  - Bad channel repairing
  - Rereference to TP9/TP10
  - Bandpass filter: 0.01-200Hz
  - ICA filter for artifact removing
  - Downsampling to 250Hz

## Files
Three zip files are provided with all the epoches used in this competition, which are

*   **Enrollment.zip** - the epochs in enrollment set
*   **Calibration.zip** - the epochs in calibration set
*   **Testing.zip** -the epochs in testing set
*   **Enrollment_Info.csv** -the label information for enrollment Set
*   **Calibration_Info.csv** -the label information for calibration Set
*   **Testing_Info.csv** -the label information for testing Set
*   **Baseline.zip** -matlab version code of the baseline methods
*   **Result_example_0907_181703.csv** -an example sumbission *.csv file 
 
## Columns

*   `Epoch_ID` - The ID of the epoch, 
    - ranged from epoch000001 to epoch120000
*   `Subject_ID`?- The ID of the subject
    - ranged from sub001 to sub106, or None
    - if usage is 1 or 2, the Subject_ID is the real ID of the subject
    - if usage is 3, the Subject_ID is None
    - if usage is 4, the Subject_ID is the ID that the subject claim he or she is.
*   `Session`?- The number of the session, from 1 to 2
*   `Task`?- The label of the Task, from 1 to 13
    - 1-EC
    - 2-EO
    - 3-VEP
    - 4-AEP
    - 5-SEP
    - 6-SSVEP
    - 7-SSAEP
    - 8-SSSEP
    - 9-P300£¨Target and Nontarget£©
    - 10-FT
    - 11-RH
    - 12-LH
    - 13-SSVEP-SA
*   `Usage`?- The label for the usage of the Epoch, ranged from 1 to 4
    - 1-Enrollment
    - 2-Calibration
    - 3-Identification
    - 4-Verification


##  Electrode
