6138,nlp-competition-cuet-ete-day-2022,"# Description

Sarcasm detection is the process of identifying whether a piece of text is sarcastic or not. Sarcasm is one of the main challenges for sentiment analysis systems. This is because a sarcastic sentence usually carries a negative implicit sentiment, while it is expressed using positive expressions. This contradiction between the surface sentiment and the intended one creates a complex challenge for sentiment analysis systems. Sarcasm detection received attention in other languages, but Bengali still lags behind. There is a broader business and educational interest in developing a Bengali AI tool to identify sarcastic content.  In this regard, We, **[Department of Electronics and Telecommunication Engineering (ETE), CUET](https://www.cuet.ac.bd/dept/ete)**, is elated to present an **NLP competition**, as a part of  ETE DecaHertz 2022.

This competition is open to all, anyone from any institution can participate. However, Registration is required to participate in the competition.  
**Last Date of Registration:** 15th October 2022
Follow the Facebook event page for the updated information: [ETE DecaHertz 2022](https://facebook.com/events/s/ete-decahertz/2410254069113257/)


## Competition Information: 
The Competition will be held from 17th October to 21st October 2022.
Participants abide to follow the competition [Rules ](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1a5Zj7kgbafj74cHqsmgC3UYdjluO1JGI/view). Violation of any rules will lead to disqualifying the team from the competition.

**Competition Prize Money: 12K BDT**

**Important**: All the teams have to publish their notebooks after the end of the competition period.


----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for the competition is weighted F1-Score.

## Submission Format

The submission files should contain two columns: Id and category where the category will be your model predictions (0 or 1) for the respective Ids headlines. 

The file should contain a header and have the following format:
```
Id, category
11, 1
```","# Dataset Description

Electronic journalism powered by Social media has become one of the major sources of information consumption lately. Many media houses are using creative ways in order to tap into increasing views on posts. One of the ways is using sarcastic headlines as click baits. A model that is able to predict whether a piece of headline is sarcastic or not can be useful for media houses in order to analyze their quarterly earnings by strategy. Also, from a reader's perspective, search engines can utilize this information of sarcasm and depend on on the reader¡¯s preference, recommend similar articles to them.

Past studies are primarily focused on detecting sarcasm in news articles. **For this competition,  we curated a dataset comprised of around 50K news headlines labeled in two categories: Sarcastic (1) or Not-Sarcastic (0).** 

**The participants are strictly prohibited from manually labeling or using an external dataset for the competition.**

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format


## Columns

*   `Id` - Unique Number
*   `headlines`?- Bengali news headlines
*   `category`?- 1 if the text is sarcastic otherwise 0.",False,True
