6154,orca-call-recognition,"# Description

# **Binary Classification of Orca Calls**   ?? ??

## Your Mission

Given an audio clip from an underwater microphone (hydrophone), the challenge is to determine which seconds of the clip contain an orca call.

## The Real-World Application 

[Orcasound](https://www.orcasound.net/) maintains a hydrophone network in Puget Sound (near Seattle, WA, USA, Northeast Pacific). Killer whales (aka orcas) often swim by these hydrophones and vocalize with a wide range of calls.

A machine learning model is helpful to find these calls in live hydrophone audio. Automatically identifying these calls is at the core of the real time inference system we call OrcaHello. The goal of the detection/notification system is to help recover the endangered population of Southern Resident Killer Whale (SRKW) -- the iconic fish-eating orcas that seek salmon in Washington and British Columbia during the summer/fall and across their range from northern California to southeast Alaska during winter/spring. 

In the part of their critical habitat near Seattle, WA, the acoustic AI challenge is complicated by the fact that mammal-eating orcas and humpback whales also can be hear vocalizing on the Orcasound hydrophones. An ideal binary classifier for SRKW calls will not be confused by the similar signals from other types of orcas or the humpbacks.

## General Resources
Learn more about OrcaHello via:

- **[OrcaHello project summary page](https://ai4orcas.net/portfolio/orcahello/)** (for general public and context)
- **[Deployed public/moderator UI](https://aifororcas.azurewebsites.net/)** (public access to detections, plus moderation features upon authentication)
- **[Github repository for live inference system](https://github.com/orcasound/aifororcas-livesystem)** (for developers and data scientists) 
- **[OrcaHello wiki](https://github.com/orcasound/aifororcas-livesystem/wiki)** (for system administrators and moderators)

## Related Projects
- [Open data for orca bioacoustics](https://github.com/orcasound/orcadata/wiki)
- [aifororcas-podcast](https://github.com/orcasound/aifororcas-podcast) - A tool to crowdsource labeling of whale calls in Orcasound's hydrophone data.
- [aifororcas-orcaml](https://github.com/orcasound/aifororcas-orcaml) - Original baseline machine learning model and data preparation code.

----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is [AUC (Area under the ROC curve)](https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course/classification/roc-and-auc). 

## Submission Format

Submission files should be in .csv format and contain two columns: `Id` and `Predicted`. `Id` should be audiofile.wav + start of one second window. For example, `OS_7_05_2019_08_24_00_.wav0` refers to the window between seconds 0 and 1 of the audio file entitled `OS_7_05_2019_08_24_00_.wav`.  `Predicted` is 0 if no SRKW call is detected and 1 if a call is.","# Dataset Description

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training set of classified audio labels 
*   **train_audio_files** - the files that the audio labels in train.csv refer to
*   **test.csv** - the audio labels to classify in your submission
*   **test_audio_files** - the files that the audio labels in test.csv refer to
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

## Columns
*   **Id**: the audio label. Format is: audiofile.wav + second. For example, `OS_7_05_2019_08_24_00_.wav0` refers to the window between seconds 0 and 1 of the audio file entitled `OS_7_05_2019_08_24_00_.wav`. 
*   **Predicted**: the model prediction. 1 if orca call, 0 if not.",False,True
