6176,predict-viral-instagram-posts,"# Description

![](https://cdn-insights.statusbrew.com/images/2021/07/top_42_best_social_media_analytics_-_reporting_tools.png)

Do you have a passion for data science and find it intriguing to forecast the virality of Instagram posts? You will have two days to analyze the provided dataset and generate your predictions.

Social Media Analytics is one of the most explored topics in the world of data science. It covers multiple business  domains such as sentiment analysis, social media engagement and trend analysis.

In this competition, you are working as a Data Scientist for Instagram and your task is to predict the virality of the posts given other relevant factors such as type of posts, number of comments, Instagram user, etc. 

**NOTE:** : Virality is measured by the number of likes on any particular post.

## Acknowledgements

We thank Servian for sponsoring the event!
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation pages describes how submissions will be scored and how students should format their submissions. 

Root Mean Square Error (RMSE) is the standard deviation of the residuals (prediction errors). Residuals are a measure of how far from the regression line data points are; RMSE is a measure of how spread out these residuals are. In other words, it tells you how concentrated the data is around the line of best fit. Root mean square error is commonly used in climatology, forecasting, and regression analysis to verify experimental results.

![](https://www.statisticshowto.com/wp-content/uploads/2016/10/root-mean-square-error.png)

Where:

    ¦² = summation (¡°add up¡±)
    (zfi ¨C Zoi)2 = differences, squared
    N = sample size.


## Submission Format

**For every id column in the dataset**, submission files should contain two columns: `id` and `numbr_likes`. 


The file should contain a header and have the following format:
```
id,numbr_likes
1,10
```","# Dataset Description

We have sampled a chunk of data from a huge 42 million instagram posts. (Don't worry you don't need to wrangle the 16GB dataset, we got you :) ).

The sampled data consists of three different aspects of an instagram post:
1. Locations: 1,204,583  Locations across all over the world
2. Profiles: 4,509,586 Profiles of many different people and business
3. Posts: 42,710,197 Posts with location information, date, number of likes and comments, and many more.

## Files

There are a total of 4 files shared here to help you with the task!:

*   **train_beginner.csv** - You have to use this dataset to get an understanding of the data and build predictive models to estimate number of the likes for a given post.
*   **test_beginner.csv** - Using the best model which is trained on the train data, you have to estimate the number of likes for the posts in the test data.
*   **test_beginner_sample_solution.csv** - You have to submit your final solutions in the format of the sample solution file.
*  **metadata.csv** - Column information has been provided in the metadata.csv file. 
**NOTE:**  metadata may contain extra columns. Check the description for the relevant columns in train_beginner.csv for the purpose of the competition",False,True
