6206,image-geo-localisation-geoguessr-bot,"# Description

Geoguessr is a game whose goal is to find where you are on earth while being randomly placed in Google Street-View. 
Players have to guess the country, the city and even the street where they are by finding clues such as street names, flags, and even vegetation! 

This competition aims at building a model that plays Geoguessr and finds the correct location of a given image taken from Google Street-View.

Here is a handy link to Kaggle's [competition documentation](https://www.kaggle.com/docs/competitions), which includes, among other things, instructions on [submitting predictions](https://www.kaggle.com/docs/competitions#making-a-submission).

----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is the [mean Haversine distance](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Haversine_formula). The Haversine distance between two points on Earth given by their latitude and their longitude measures the distance on Earth between those two points. Check the wikipedia page for the exact formula. Here is an implementation in torch:

```python
class HaversineLoss(nn.Module):
    def forward(self, predictions, targets):
        """"""Compute the Haversine distance between two sets of coordinates on earth.

        :param predictions: Predicted coordinates (latitude, longitude) of size (batch_size, 2)
        :param targets: Ground truth coordinates (latitude, longitude) of size (batch_size, 2)
        """"""
        earth_radius = 6371e3
        phi_1 = predictions[:, 0] * torch.pi / 180.
        phi_2 = targets[:, 0] * torch.pi / 180.
        lambda_1 = predictions[:, 1] * torch.pi / 180.
        lambda_2 = targets[:, 1] * torch.pi / 180.
        return 2. * earth_radius * torch.arcsin(torch.sqrt(
            self._hav(phi_1, phi_2) +
            torch.cos(phi_1) *
            torch.cos(phi_2) *
            self._hav(lambda_1, lambda_2)
        ))

    @staticmethod
    def _hav(theta_1, theta_2):
        return torch.sin((theta_2 - theta_1) / 2.) ** 2
```

## Submission Format

Submission files should contain three columns: `image_id`, `latitude` and `longitude`, with the two last columns given in degrees. 

The file should contain a header and have the following format:
```
image_id,latitude,longitude
0,0,0
```","# Dataset Description

This is a Street-View Image Geolocalisation challenge, in which you have to guess the location where a particular street-view image has been taken. All images come from the Google Street View API and have been randomly taken in all street-viewed countries, following the density of street-viewed regions all over the world.
Each image is saved in the `image` folder and its name is an `id` used to retrieve the corresponding coordinate (latitude, longitude) in the CSV files (only for the train images).

For each image of the test set, you have to predict the **longitude** and the **latitude** where has been taken the image, such as in `sample_submission.csv`.

## Files

*   **images** folder containing all images
*   **train_coordinates.csv** - the training set containing the coordinates of the train images
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

**EDIT:** For now, only the test images are available in the archive `images.tar.gz`. The train images are coming soon ;)

## Columns

*   `image_id` - Image ID 
*   `path` - Image path from the root folder ( `image/{ID}.png` )
*   `longitude`?- longitude where the photo has been taken
*   `latitude`?- latitude where the photo has been taken
*   `country` - Name of the country in which the photo has been taken",False,True
