6211,etu-ai-club-competition-2,"# Description

In this competition, you need to make a regression on a noisy dataset. We recommend to follow three main steps for beginners:

-Feature Engineering
-Model Selection
-Hyperparameter Tuning
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is [Root mean squared error (RMSE)](https://www.kaggle.com/general/215997). Root mean squared error (RMSE) is the most popular metrics. It is simply the square root of MSE. MSE is the mean of all squared errors.

Squared Error = ( True Value ¨C Predicted Value )**2
Mean Squared Error = 1/N*(SE)

Root mean squared error = SQRT(MSE)

## Submission Format

**For every instance in the dataset**, submission files should contain two columns: `id` and `Target`.","# Dataset Description

""train.csv"", ""test.csv"" and ""sample_ submission.csv"" files can be downloaded here. You need to predict the Target which consists of float numbers.
 
## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

## Columns

*   `id` - unique identifier for each instance
*   `feature_0`?- feature_0 to be trained ...
*   `Target`?- the float number to be predicted",False,True
6212,waveform-inversion,"# Overview

In this competition, you¡¯ll estimate subsurface properties¡ªlike velocity maps¡ªfrom on seismic waveform data. Known as Full Waveform Inversion (FWI), this process could lead to more accurate and efficient seismic analysis, making it more useful in a variety of fields.
----------------------
# Description

[](url)Imagine a doctor analyzing an ultrasound scan¡ªnot just to see a blurry outline but to achieve a clearer, more detailed image crucial for an accurate diagnosis. That's the challenge geophysicists face when imaging the Earth's hidden structures. Beneath the surface lie vital resources, potential hazards, and clues to our planet's history¡ªall requiring sharper, more precise subsurface imaging to be fully understood and effectively utilized.

Full Waveform Inversion (FWI) is the key to unlocking these secrets. This powerful technique, crucial for energy exploration, carbon storage, medical ultrasound, and advanced material testing, aims to build a detailed picture of the subsurface by analyzing the entire shape of seismic waves. But current methods are hindered by a noisy reality.

Traditional physics-based approaches are accurate, but incredibly slow and prone to errors when the signal is weak from noisy data. Pure machine learning solutions are faster, but require vast amounts of labeled data and often fail to generalize to new, unfamiliar ""signal.""

This competition challenges you to bridge the gap by combining physics and machine learning to advance FWI. Success here could transform not only subsurface energy exploration but also a wide range of applications, from medical diagnostics to non-destructive material testing¡ªanywhere precise imaging matters.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated on the mean absolute error (MAE) across all columns and rows. Only the *odd* position values of `x_` (columns) should be submitted, as shown below and in the `sample_submission.csv` file. All `y_` positions (rows) must be predicted, with the predictions of each `oid` stacked onto each other.

## Submission File
For each `oid_ypos` in the test set, you must predict a value for each `x_{odd}` target. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    oid_ypos,x_1,x_3,...,x_69
    000039dca2_y_0,3000.0,3000.0,...,3000.0
    000039dca2_y_1,3000.0,3000.0,...,3000.0
    000039dca2_y_2,3000.0,3000.0,...,3000.0
    etc.

----------------------
# Timeline

* **April 8, 2025** - Start Date.

* **June 23, 2025** - Entry Deadline. You must accept the competition rules before this date in order to compete.

* **June 23, 2025** - Team Merger Deadline. This is the last day participants may join or merge teams.

* **June 30, 2025** - Final Submission Deadline.

All deadlines are at 11:59 PM UTC on the corresponding day unless otherwise noted. The competition organizers reserve the right to update the contest timeline if they deem it necessary.
----------------------
# Prizes

* 1st Place - $12,000
* 2nd Place - $10,000
* 3rd Place - $10,000
* 4th Place - $10,000
* 5th Place - $8,000
----------------------
# Acknowledgements

![](https://yaleidentity.yale.edu/sites/default/files/2023-11/yale%20in%20gray%20box_0.png) 
**[Department of Statistics and Data Science, Yale University](https://statistics.yale.edu/)**:  The competition further benefited from the advancements in physics-informed machine learning led by Prof. Lu Lu and [his research group](https://lugroup.yale.edu/).

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F1095143%2F1649ae2d2b905f4cc2ff088860b00a71%2FUNC-SDSS-Logo.png?generation=1744048661685884&alt=media)
**[School of Data Science and Society (SDSS), University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill (UNC)](https://datascience.unc.edu)**: This competition builds upon the foundational research of the [SMILE team](https://smileunc.github.io/) in Computational Wave Imaging and Machine Learning at SDSS, UNC. Prof. Youzuo Lin led the preparation of the competition dataset, with valuable contributions from Dr. Hanchen Wang, Yinan Feng,  Dr. Yinpeng Chen, and Dr. Shihang Feng.


![](https://secure-cdn.scdn6.secure.raxcdn.com/media/userfiles/subsite_54/images/Logos/Los%20Alamos%20National%20Laboratory.jpg)
