6227,patient-healthcare,"# Description

After rejoining the university, Hiro struggles to lead his dual life as a student and the leader of Big Hero 6. Although his friends are there helping him in every way possible, he still finds himself stuck in weird situations. Now, he is again faced with one such dilemma. 
Recently, he has been volunteering in various hospitals with his ¡°bae¡± Baymax. Baymax helps doctors with different tasks, whether it be diagnosing patients or prescribing medication in the clinics. Now, Hiro is in the middle of training & supervising Baymax when he gets the news that Callaghan has freed himself from prison and is hunting Hiro. Since Hiro cannot leave Baymax alone due to the development of Baymax being in its infant stages and thus needing continuous supervision, he has asked you to train the robot for specific hospital-related tasks. **Help him predict the survivability of the admitted patients** while the Big Hero 6 deals with Callaghan. And build the best model you can because, as Baymax says, and I quote, 
> *¡°I cannot deactivate until you say you are satisfied with your care¡±*
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is Macro F1 Score. The F1 score, commonly used in information retrieval, measures accuracy using the statistics precision (p) and recall (r).

Precision is the ratio of true positives (tp) to all predicted positives (tp + fp). Recall is the ratio of true positives (tp) to all actual positives (tp + fn). The F1 score is given by:


![Macro F-1 Image](https://forums.fast.ai/uploads/default/original/3X/c/c/cca1b3ad72fc927fbf3d3690f01d2e3b5a31dd2e.png)
The F1 metric weights recall and precision equally, and a good retrieval algorithm will maximize both precision and recall simultaneously. Thus, moderately good performance on both will be favored over extremely good performance on one and poor performance on the other.
Macro F1-score calculates the F1 score for each class in the dataset and takes the unweighted average.","# Dataset Description

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

## Columns
