6253,ada-image-recognition-fiber,"# Description

# Goal

The goal of this challenge is to provide a realistic computer vision problem to participants interested in getting hands-on experience in this field. This challenge is the perfect opportunity to face some of the typical steps of a computer vision problem. Working on this case will let you go from theory to practice, learn new things and have fun in the process.


# Description

As you might know, Proximus is actively rolling out fiber across Belgium. This requires the installation of many new devices. While this rollout is happening at a high speed, it is not at the cost of quality. Indeed, as manual quality assessment cannot keep up with the increasing amount of new fiber connections, Proximus turned to computer vision to make sure that every installation is up to our quality standards.

In this challenge, we focus on the installation of a hardware component called DTP-F. Technicians currently have to take several pictures each time a DTP-F is installed. The requirements listed below must be verified on the picture for a DTP-F installation to be considered as compliant:

1. Presence of 2 fixations screws
2. Presence of 2 tie-wraps around the cable
3. Presence of 2 rubbers
4. Presence of 2 plastic covers
5. Presence of 2 pieces of foam

You can find here below an example picture.

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F2322250%2F3675db2e2f4712866d660a4d80a9c63e%2Fexample.PNG?generation=1670401199640497&alt=media)

----------------------
# Evaluation

# Evaluation
In order to assess whether a certain image is compliant, your model/approach independent of its architecture should answer the following questions/sub-tasks:

- Are there 2 screws visible in the image?
- Are there 2 tie-wraps visible in the image?
- Are there 2 rubbers visible in the image?
- Are there 2 plastic covers visible in the image?
- Are there 2 foams visible in the image?

As [Kaggle ](https://www.kaggle.com/) doesn't support custom metrics, we couldn't choose several metrics to indicate the performance of your model/approach in each category. Eventually, all the predictions are treated in the same way regardless of their sub-task. [micro-f1-score](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/F-score) which takes class frequency into account, is used to evaluate your model/approach. At this point, class means an outcome of `0` or `1`. 

# Submission File

The submission file is required to be in a CSV format. The file should contain a header with `image_id_and_sub_task` and `prediction` columns and have the following format:
- `image_id_and_sub_task` column contains the image id and the sub-task information related to the image in `<image_id>_<sub-task>` format.
- `prediction` column contains `0` or `1` in case of the absence or presence of the related object, respectively.
-  **Pay attention, 5 rows related to 5 sub-tasks are required for each image.**
 
```
image_id_and_sub_task,prediction
image_id_0_two_screws,1
image_id_0_two_tie_wraps,1
image_id_0_two_rubbers,1
image_id_0_two_plastic_covers,1
image_id_0_two_foams,1
image_id_1_two_screws,0
image_id_1_two_tie_wraps,0
image_id_1_two_rubbers,0
image_id_1_two_plastic_covers,1
image_id_1_two_foams,1
...
``` 
----------------------
# About Ada

# About Proximus Ada

Proximus Ada is the first Belgian Center of excellence in Artificial Intelligence and Cybersecurity.  
Born in April 2022 by the willingness of Proximus SA to gather expertise and skills in AI & CS in one company.
One of the pillars of Proximus Ada is to promote education, knowledge exchange and learnings with schools, universities and promote sustainable initiative that brings value to society. 

Please have a look at https://www.proximus.be/en/id_Proximus-ADA/customs/proximus-ada.html","# Dataset Description

The dataset contains 1435 images split in train and test.
The annotations are provided in the YOLO format. All pictures having annotations have a corresponding *.txt* file containing the coordinates of these annotations. Note that pictures with no annotation don't have a corresponding *.txt* file. Note that no *.yaml* file is provided so that you can make your own train/val split.

```
<dataset>/
    images/
        train/
            <uuid1>.<ext>
            <uuid2>.<ext>
            ...
        test/
            <uuid3>.<ext>
            <uuid4>.<ext>
            ...
    labels/
        train/
            <uuid1>.txt
            <uuid2>.txt
            ...
```
The *.txt* files contain:
- one row per object: 
- format: class (starting from 0), x_center, y_center, width, height
- Box coordinates are in normalized xywh format (from 0 - 1).

Here is the number of available annotations:

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-forum-message-attachments/o/inbox%2F2322250%2Fecf7a7d7ae1ec992e36b5a15b0dfb120%2Fannotations.png?generation=1670401300674788&alt=media)

The annotations are given under the form of segmentation instances, as in the following example:

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-forum-message-attachments/o/inbox%2F2322250%2F2088543297fe92339f994c72020051fa%2Fexample_annotated.PNG?generation=1670401337711180&alt=media)

There are different types of views in the dataset. Note that it's not always possible to see all the components in the image. In some cases, no component is visible on the image, as illustrated below: 

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-forum-message-attachments/o/inbox%2F1673559%2F560d0742c4d5c2c657dc7eb6f91c742c%2Fvisibility_cases.PNG?generation=1670428750494133&alt=media)

Finally, there might be some inconsistencies in the dataset, such as the missing left rubber and tie-wrap in the following example. An iterative approach to dealing with labeling mistakes can maybe be considered.

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-forum-message-attachments/o/inbox%2F2322250%2Fdb2f5c762da0a7de0f8ee651d7f04520%2Fmistake.png?generation=1670401388800488&alt=media)",False,True
6254,ct5120suggestionmining,"# Description

This is an in-class competition for 2022-CT5120: Intro to NLP. We provide three training splits:
1. train.csv: contains training data
2. dev.csv: contains validation data
3. test.csv: contains test data

This is a binary classification problem whereby participants should develop a supervised classification model that could classify if the given test sample is suggestion or not suggestion. Suggestions could be implicit or explicit whereby an author could recommend, request or suggest directly or indirectly. 

The Competition leaderboard has two parts.

The public leaderboard provides publicly visible submission scores based on a representative sample of the test data. This leaderboard is visible throughout the competition.

The private leaderboard, by contrast, tracks model performance using the remainder of the test data. The private leaderboard thus has final say on whose models are best, and hence, who the winners and losers of the Competition will be. Which subset of data is calculated on the private leaderboard or a submission¡¯s performance on the private leaderboard is not released to users until the competition has been closed.

Many users watch the public leaderboard closely, as breakthroughs in the competition are announced by score gains in the leaderboard. These jumps in turn motivate other teams working on the competition in search of those advancements. But it¡¯s important to keep the public leaderboard in perspective. It¡¯s very easy to overfit a model, creating something that performs very well on the public leaderboard, but very badly on the private one. This is called overfitting.


## Reference
We used the dataset provided in the SemEval-2019 Task 9: Suggestion Mining from Online Reviews and Forums

Sapna Negi, Tobias Daudert, and Paul Buitelaar. 2019. SemEval-2019 Task 9: Suggestion Mining from Online Reviews and Forums. In Proceedings of the 13th International Workshop on Semantic Evaluation, pages 877¨C887, Minneapolis, Minnesota, USA. Association for Computational Linguistics
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation pages describes how submissions will be scored and how students should format their submissions. You don't need a sub-title at the top; the page title appears above. For an example of a high quality evaluation page, see the 2013 KDD Cup page, which we have included below:

---

The evaluation metric for this competition is [Mean F1-Score](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/F-score). The F1 score, commonly used in information retrieval, measures accuracy using the statistics precision \( \text{p} \) and recall \( \text{r} \).

Precision is the ratio of true positives \(\text{tp}\) to all predicted positives \(\text{tp} + \text{fp}\). Recall is the ratio of true positives \(\text{tp}\) to all actual positives \(\text{tp} + \text{fn}\). The F1 score is given by:

\[ \text{F1} = 2\frac{\text{p} \cdot \text{r}}{\text{p}+\text{r}}\ \ \mathrm{where}\ \ \text{p} = \frac{\text{tp}}{\text{tp}+\text{fp}},\ \ \text{r} = \frac{\text{tp}}{\text{tp}+\text{fn}} \]

The F1 metric weights recall and precision equally, and a good retrieval algorithm will maximize both precision and recall simultaneously. Thus, moderately good performance on both will be favored over extremely good performance on one and poor performance on the other.

## Submission Format

Submission files should contain two columns: `Id` and `Expected`. `Expected` should either 1 (if suggestion)or 0 (if not). `Id` is a unique identifier given in the `test.csv` ranges from *test_0 to test_1699*.  `Id` and `Expected` values should be separated by `,`

Use `StudentID_Lastname.csv` as filename while submitting

The file should contain a header and have the following format:
```
Id,Expected
test_0,1
test_1,0
```","# Dataset Description

You will work with the data from SemEval-2019 Task 9 subtask A to classify whether a piece of text contains a suggestion or not.

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training set
*   **dev.csv** - the test set
*   **test_no_label.csv** - the test set

*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format
## Columns

*   `id` - unique identifier for each sample
*   `text`?- text for each sample
*   `label`?- label for each sample (1 means suggestion and 0 is not)",False,True
6255,79dapt-sries-temporais-i,"# Description

O objetivo da competi??o ¨¦ praticarmos o que aprendemos durante a semana.

Esta competi??o foi estruturada a partir da competi??o [Store Sales - Time Series Forecasting](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/store-sales-time-series-forecasting/overview). Para conseguirmos desenvolver o projeto em apenas um dia, a competi??o inicial foi reduzida para apenas uma categoria e duas lojas (duas s¨¦ries temporais).

Discutiremos os detalhes da competi??o em aula! Segue abaixo o texto da competi??o original:

# Goal of the Competition

In this ¡°getting started¡± competition, you¡¯ll use time-series forecasting to forecast store sales on data from Corporaci¨®n Favorita, a large Ecuadorian-based grocery retailer.

Specifically, you'll build a model that more accurately predicts the **sales per transaction** for 2 stores from Corporation Favorita

# Get Started

Forecasts aren¡¯t just for meteorologists. Governments forecast economic growth. Scientists attempt to predict the future population. And businesses forecast product demand¡ªa common task of professional data scientists. Forecasts are especially relevant to brick-and-mortar grocery stores, which must dance delicately with how much inventory to buy. Predict a little over, and grocers are stuck with overstocked, perishable goods. Guess a little under, and popular items quickly sell out, leading to lost revenue and upset customers. More accurate forecasting, thanks to machine learning, could help ensure retailers please customers by having just enough of the right products at the right time.

Current subjective forecasting methods for retail have little data to back them up and are unlikely to be automated. The problem becomes even more complex as retailers add new locations with unique needs, new products, ever-transitioning seasonal tastes, and unpredictable product marketing.

# Potential Impact

If successful, you'll have flexed some new skills in a real world example. For grocery stores, more accurate forecasting can decrease food waste related to overstocking and improve customer satisfaction. The results of this ongoing competition, over time, might even ensure your local store has exactly what you need the next time you shop.

# Cr¨¦ditos

This dataset is a subset/transformation of the Favorita stores competition: https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/store-sales-time-series-forecasting/data
----------------------
# Evaluation

A avalia??o do erro sera sobre o per¨ªodo de 3 meses ap¨®s o fim do per¨ªodo de treinamento. A medida de erro utilizada ser¨¢ o RMSE.","# Dataset Description

A vari¨¢vel resposta ¨¦ a vari¨¢vel **sales_per_trans**.

## Files

### **train.csv** 

O conjunto de treinamento. Cont¨¦m as vari¨¢veis:
* **id** - o ID da observa??o
* **date** - data em format YYYY-MM-DD
* **store_nbr** - o c¨®digo de cada uma das duas lojas
* **onpromotion** - o n¨²mero de itens da categoria **GROCERIES I** promocionados na data/loja
* **sales_per_trans** - nossa **vari¨¢vel resposta**, cont¨¦m o n¨²mero de itens vendidos por transa??o da loja

### **test.csv** - o conjunto de teste

O conjunto de teste. Cont¨¦m as vari¨¢veis:
* **id** - o ID da observa??o
* **date** - data em format YYYY-MM-DD
* **store_nbr** - o c¨®digo de cada uma das duas lojas
* **onpromotion** - o n¨²mero de itens da categoria **GROCERIES I** promocionados na data/loja

### **stores.csv** - tabela com localiza??o e caracter¨ªsticas das lojas

Informa??es sobre as diferentes lojas da rede Favorita. Cont¨¦m as vari¨¢veis:
* **store_nbr** - o c¨®digo de cada loja
* **city** - a cidade da loja
* **state** - o estado da loja
* **type** - o tipo de loja (n?o precisa ser utilizado nesse problema)
* **cluster** - o cluster da loja (n?o precisa ser utilizado nesse problema)

### **holiday_events.csv** - tabela de feriados regionais do Equador
Holidays and Events, with metadata
NOTE: Pay special attention to the transferred column. A holiday that is transferred officially falls on that calendar day, but was moved to another date by the government. A transferred day is more like a normal day than a holiday. To find the day that it was actually celebrated, look for the corresponding row where type is Transfer. For example, the holiday Independencia de Guayaquil was transferred from 2012-10-09 to 2012-10-12, which means it was celebrated on 2012-10-12. Days that are type Bridge are extra days that are added to a holiday (e.g., to extend the break across a long weekend). These are frequently made up by the type Work Day which is a day not normally scheduled for work (e.g., Saturday) that is meant to payback the Bridge.
Additional holidays are days added a regular calendar holiday, for example, as typically happens around Christmas (making Christmas Eve a holiday).

### **oil.csv** - tabela de pre?os do Petr¨®leo para per¨ªodo de treinamento

Daily oil price. Ecuador is an oil-dependent country and it's economical health is highly vulnerable to shocks in oil prices.

### **sample_submission.csv** - um exemplo de arquivo de submiss?o.",False,True
