6269,tih-hackathon-digital-id-frameworks,"# Description

In an increasingly digital world where concerns around fraud and misuse of personal data are growing, there is a call for more efficient methods of identity verification that go beyond traditional Identity Documents (IDs) such as driver¡¯s licenses and passports. The Institute of Data Engineering, Analytics and Science (IDEAS) Foundation at ISI Kolkata is organizing a hackathon focused on digital identification frameworks. This hackathon is mainly aimed towards Indian Students, Indian Researchers and Indian Startups. Others can also participate to test their skills.
**Dataset**
The dataset consists of images of passports from different countries captured through a user's mobile phone. The passports have an machine-readable zone code. A machine-readable zone contains encoded machine-readable lines/ codes that include the document holder¡¯s data and forgery detection numbers known as ¡°checksum"". For travel documents such as passports, text recognition and conversion technology (OCR system) is used to read MRZ data and verify it. A machine-readable zone in a passport and other identity documents is a significant step to prevent unauthorized alteration in the IDs. Furthermore, it is an ideal way to speed up the verification process.

With MRZ, you get the personal information of a holder in a standardized format that naturally improves the recognition and verification process. Passports use Type 3 MRZ which contains two lines with 44 characters in each. It comprises information like nationality, passport number, date of birth, sex, passport expiration date, etc. The only characters used are A¨CZ, 0¨C9 and the filler character <.
First row
|Positions|	Length	| Characters|	Meaning|
|1	|1	|alpha	|P, indicating a passport|
|2	|1	|alpha+<	|Type (for countries that distinguish between different types of passports)|
|3¨C5	|3	|alpha+<	|Issuing country or organization (ISO 3166-1 alpha-3 code with modifications)|
|6¨C44|39	|alpha+<	|Surname, followed by two filler characters, followed by given names. Given names are separated by single filler characters. Note that some countries does not differentiate between surname and given name (i.e. no two filler character), such as the Malaysian Passport|
In the name field, spaces, hyphens and other punctuation are represented by <, except apostrophes, which are skipped. If the names are too long, names are abbreviated to their most significant parts. In that case, the last position must contain an alphabetic character to indicate possible truncation, and if there is a given name, the two fillers and at least one character of it must be included.

Second row
|Positions	|Length	|Characters	|Meaning|
|1¨C9	|9	|alpha+num+<	|Passport number|
|10	|1	|numeric	|Check digit over digits 1¨C9|
|11¨C13	|3	|alpha+<	|Nationality (ISO 3166-1 alpha-3 code with modifications)|
|14¨C19	|6	|numeric	|Date of birth (YYMMDD)|
|20	|1	|numeric	|Check digit over digits 14¨C19|
|21	|1	|alpha+<	|Sex (M, F or < for male, female or unspecified)|
|22¨C27	|6	|numeric	|Expiration date of passport (YYMMDD)|
|28	|1	|numeric	|Check digit over digits 22¨C27|
|29¨C42	|14	|alpha+num+<	|Personal number (may be used by the issuing country as it desires)|
|43	|1	|numeric+<	|Check digit over digits 29¨C42 (maybe < if all characters are <)|
|44	|1	|numeric	Check digit over digits 1¨C10, 14¨C20, and 22¨C43|

**Checksum calculation**
The check digit calculation is as follows: each position is assigned a value; for the digits 0 to 9 this is the value of the digits, for the letters A to Z this is 10 to 35, for the filler < this is 0. The value of each position is then multiplied by its weight; the weight of the first position is 7, of the second it is 3, and of the third it is 1, and after that the weights repeat 7, 3, 1, and so on. All values are added together and the remainder of the final value divided by 10 is the check digit.

**Task**
The task is to find the MRZ region in the passport image and bring out the ID pieces of information from it:
TYPE,ISSUING COUNTRY, SURNAME,	NAME, PASSPORT NUMBER, NATIONALITY, DOB, SEX, EXPIRY DATE

**Important Note**
The folder images consists of 99 images of passports from different countries. Submissions in kaggle would require only the passport number extracted from each image. The private leaderboard on kaggle would display the results on the exact matches with the passport number only. The sample file for kaggle submission is named as KaggleSample.csv. 

The final submission for grading to the hackathon needs to be submitted via google drive link at https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1R-QFXW8n3KxPx_KBbRoyx_AZcRANE1I-?usp=sharing

The SampleSubmission.csv file shows how the entries of the final grading output would be that needs to be submitted in the google drive. In case someone's sex isn't given, it should be marked as capital X in the output csv. Do not submit the kaggle submission directly to the drive as it will result in zero points. The kaggle submission has only a single column prediction but the final submission would have 9 columns.
----------------------
# Evaluation

The submissions will be scored using a perfect match with the extracted data in the columns. Any character mismatch in any field would be considered as a complete miss for that entire sample. The evaluations on kaggle are not representative of the actual score. 

The folder images consists of 99 images of passports from different countries. Submissions in kaggle would require only the passport number extracted from each image. The private leaderboard on kaggle would display the results on the exact matches with the passport number only. The sample file for kaggle submission is named as KaggleSample.csv. 

The final submission for grading to the hackathon needs to be submitted via google drive link at https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1R-QFXW8n3KxPx_KBbRoyx_AZcRANE1I-?usp=sharing

The SampleSubmission.csv file shows how the entries of the final grading output would be that needs to be submitted in the google drive. In case someone's sex isn't given, it should be marked as capital X in the output csv. Do not submit the kaggle submission directly to the drive as it will result in zero points. The kaggle submission has only a single column prediction but the final submission would have 9 columns.

The final scores will be disclosed on 4th and 5th November 2022.","# Dataset Description

