<h3>Metric</h3>
<p>Your score is the average squared difference between the estimated values and the actual value. This is known as <a href=""https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mean_squared_error"" target=""_blank"">MSE</a>.</p>
<h3>Submission File Format</h3>
<p>You should submit a csv file with exactly 25000 entries <b>plus</b> a header row. Your submission will show an error if you have extra columns (beyond CarId and Price) or rows.<br><br>The file should have exactly 2 columns:</p>
<ul>
<li>CarId (String)</li>
<li>Price</li>
</ul>
<pre><b>CarId,Price</b><br>x,y<br>x2,y2<br>Etc.</pre>","# Dataset Description

# Overview

The data has been split into two groups:

- training set (train.csv)
- test set (test.csv)

**The training set** should be used to train your model. For the training set, we provide the price for each car. Your model will be based on ¡°features¡± like cars¡¯ model or state. You can also use [feature engineering](https://towardsdatascience.com/feature-engineering-for-machine-learning-3a5e293a5114) to create new features.

**The test set** should be used to see how well your model performs on unseen data. For the test set, we do not provide the price for each car. It is your job to predict these outcomes. For each car in the test set, use the model you trained to predict its price.

# Data Dictionary

