6325,beginners-time-series-predictions,"# Description

Hi there,

As I am very interested in the field of data science, I thought to give something back to the Kaggle community.

A small and hopefully fun competition for you.

The data is described on the ""Data"" page, but if anything is not clear, please do write me so I can improve it.









*Acknowledgements*
Thanks go to Antonio Garcia Prats for the really nice header/listing image.","# Dataset Description

## Intro

I am monitoring some files 24/7 via an automated monitoring system. Of all the monitored files, I selected 2 with 2 datapoints each (size & modification time -- see more below).

The target is to predict the next values (see `sample_submission.csv`/`test.csv`).

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

## Columns

*   `Id` - combination of ""itemid"" and ""clock"" - please see below.
*   `value`?- the value

## `Id` interpretation

The `Id` column can be splitted into 2 columns like this:
```python
df[['itemid', 'clock']] = df.Id.str.split('_', expand=True).astype(int)
df = df[['itemid', 'clock', 'value']]
```

The `itemid` consists of these unique values:
* 36182: size of file1
* 36185: modification time of file1
* 36188: size of file2
* 36191: modification time of file2

The `clock` column is a UTC unix timestamp when the observation has `been made. fo

## Good to know

The data is for Belgium, so you might want to incorporate Belgium holidays in your logic as well.",False,True
