6344,competencia-clasificacin-sl,"# Description

<p>El proyecto consiste en predecir los diferentes perfiles de sus clientes de tarjetas de cr¨¦dito,
con el prop¨®sito de poder implementar las pol¨ªticas de mitigaci¨®n del riesgo apropiadas para el
producto. En este orden de ideas, el inter¨¦s principal del proyecto es poder modelar la probabilidad
de que un cliente entre en default (imposibilidad para pagar la deuda adquirida), teniendo en
cuenta su informaci¨®n financiera, comportamiento crediticio e informaci¨®n demogr¨¢fica. El banco
cuenta con la siguiente informaci¨®n para sus clientes del a?o 2017 La variable de respuesta es
default.payment.next.month, que es igual a 1 si existe default y 0 de lo contrario.</p>
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is AUC. The AUC is a commonly used evaluation metric for binary problems like this one. The interpretation is that given a random positive observation and negative observation, the AUC gives the proportion of the time you guess which is which correctly. It is less affected by sample balance than accuracy. A perfect model will score an AUC of 1, while random guessing will score an AUC of around of 0.5.</p>
<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p><strong>For every observation in the dataset, submission files should contain two columns: Id and Probability. The submission should be a csv file. The Id should just be the corresponding observation Id column from the dataset. The Probability column should be the predicted probability of the outcome 1 for that observation Id.

The file should contain a header and have the following format:.</strong></p><strong>
<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>Id,Probability<br>1,0.8<br></pre></strong>","# Dataset Description

<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>TrainClas.xlsx</strong> - the training set</li>
<li><strong>TestClas.xlsx</strong> - the test set</li>
<li><strong>DataDes.txt</strong> - supplemental information about the data</li>",False,True
6345,competencia-regresin-sl,"# Description

<p>What predicts an article popularity? In this competition, you'll be using data from Mashable (mashable.com), a digital media website, defined (by themselves) as a leading source for news, information &amp; resources for the Connected Generation.</p>
<p>Time magazin noted Mashable as one of the 25 best blogs in 2009, and has been described as ""one stop shop"" for social media. As of November 2015, it has over 6,000,000 Twitter followers and over 3,200,000 fans on Facebook. In this problem, you'll use data from thousands of articles published by Mashable in a period of two years to see which variables predict the popularity of an article.</p>
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>Submissions are evaluated on the Root Mean Square Error (RMSE). The RMSE is calculated as:</p>
<p>\[RMSE = \sqrt{\frac{1}{n}\sum^n_{i=1}(\hat{y}_{i}-y_{i})^{2}}\]</p>
<p>Where y_i denotes the shares of an article and yhat_i denotes the corresponding prediction.</p>
<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p><strong>For every observation in the dataset, submission files should contain two columns: Id and Shares. The submission should be a csv file. The Id should just be the corresponding observation Id column from the dataset. The shares column should be the predicted shares for that article Id.</strong></p><strong>
<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>Id,shares<br>1,300<br></pre></strong>","# Dataset Description

