6348,111-1-ntut-dl-app-hw3,"# Description

In homework, we are going to train models that can recommend hashtags based on the content of an image. We will use the dataset ""HARRISON"". Please refer to the paper (https://arxiv.org/abs/1605.05054) for more details about the dataset.

The original data of HARRISON can be downloaded here: https://drive.google.com/file/d/1B9NZf42J_GpslRNlTTAxvM5c9WRVl_1Z/view?usp=sharing. 

You can download the images and take a look, but this is not mandatory. We already extracted the image features, compressed them as ""harrison_features.npz"", and upload to Kaggle:
https://www.kaggle.com/kuantinglai/harrison-dataset


The training process is split into two stages: hashtag2vec training and image-to-word2vec training. Please refer to the notebook of this competition. The steps are
1. Run https://www.kaggle.com/kuantinglai/train-htag2vec-tf1
2. Download ""tag\_list\_word2vec\_64.npz"" (The number 64 may be changed, it depends on your vector dimension)
3. Run https://www.kaggle.com/kuantinglai/train-devise-w-ur-htag2vec, upload your  ""tag\_list\_word2vec\_64.npz""
4. Download ""harrison\_test\_predict.csv"" and submit

To evaluate the model performance, we use the mean Average Precision (mAP) @ 10. Enjoy the homework and Have fun!
----------------------
# Evaluation

We use mean Average Precision (mAP) to evaluate the performance. Please refer to 
https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Evaluation_measures_(information_retrieval)#Average_precision
Or 
https://darren1231.pixnet.net/blog/post/347752732-machine-learning-map%28mean-average-precision%29-%E8%A9%B3%E7%B4%B0%E8%A7%A3%E8%AA%AA

for detailed explanation of Average Precision.","# Dataset Description

Please search and add the HARRISON dataset directly in your Kaggle notebook.

https://www.kaggle.com/kuantinglai/harrison-dataset",False,True
6349,tfug-pune-natural-language-inference,"# Description

# TFUG Pune Natural Language Inference competition

## Task

In this competition, you are given an entailment identification task, but with a twist! The text is not bound to any one dataset or distribution. The text has various flavors and thus the models developed must be robust enough to mitigate the distribution shift. 

## Dataset

The dataset contains ""text_1"" which is the original statement and ""text_2"" which is a probable entailment of ""text_1"". The model needs to check if ""text_2"" is indeed an entailment of ""text_1"". Labels are 0 (entailment) 1 (contradiction).

## Evaluation

Evaluation is purely based on categorical accuracy. 

## Acknowledgements

We thank Google ML Ecosystem for the organizational help for this competition.","# Dataset Description

The dataset has three columns are mentioned below. Participants are expected to submit their solutions in the format mentioned in `sample_submission.csv`

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

## Columns

*   `text_1` - Premise of the sentence
*   `text_2`?- Hypothesis based on premise
*   `label` - Label whether `text_2` is entailment of `text_1` (0 is entailment, 1 otherwise)",False,True
