6381,tfug-prayagraj-rented-stay-quality-prediction,"# Description

<img src=""https://i.ibb.co/XDdtJWw/to-let.png"" alt=""Rental Property Image"" width=300 style=""display: block; margin: 0 auto"" />


One of the biggest problems landlords face is having vacant property. The most successful landlords or property management firms have low vacancy rates. Not only does a vacancy mean no rental income is coming in, but it is also actually costing money to advertise and prepare for occupancy. The best way to combat this is to have some reliable procedures in place such as good cleanliness measures, better check-in facilities, good communications, location of the property, etc. which can attract more customers organically and develop a positive image for the property.

This directly affects someone who is willing to rent a property and wishes to pay for good services. Understanding whether a say was worth its price is a big problem to solve! 

In this competition, your aim is to build solutions that can predict the parameter which best describes guest's perspective about what was the best service they encountered at the given rental property in terms of cleanliness, communication, location, ease in check-in, the value of the property, etc. 

Here is a handy link to Kaggle's [competition documentation](https://www.kaggle.com/docs/competitions), which includes, among other things, instructions on [submitting predictions](https://www.kaggle.com/docs/competitions#making-a-submission).


## About the data set

### 1) train.csv

- `id`: The unique ID for each property listing.
- `host_[n]`: 8 Features related to the host/owner of the listing. (where n -> [1, 8])
- `property_[n]`: 16 Features related to the amenities at the given listing. (where n -> [1, 16])
- `property_max_booking`: Maximum bookings for a given listing.
- `property_min_booking`: Minimum bookings for a given listing.
- `property_price`: The price provided for a given listing.
- `reviews_each_month`: Avg. reviews received each month by the given listing.
- `reviews_last_30_days`: Number of reviews received in the last 30 days by the given listing.
- `reviews_total`: Total number of reviews received by the given listing since its inception.
- `total_listings`: Total listings provided by the host/owner.
- `max_rating_class`: According to the guests, the factor which most excites them about the listing (like cleanliness, communication, location, check-in experience, etc.) [Target Feature]


### 2) test.csv

- `id`: The unique ID for each property listing.
- `host_[n]`: 8 Features related to the host/owner of the listing. (where n -> [1, 8])
- `property_[n]`: 16 Features related to the amenities at the given listing. (where n -> [1, 16])
- `property_max_booking`: Maximum bookings for a given listing.
- `property_min_booking`: Minimum bookings for a given listing.
- `property_price`: The price provided for a given listing.
- `reviews_each_month`: Avg. reviews received each month by the given listing.
- `reviews_last_30_days`: Number of reviews received in the last 30 days by the given listing.
- `reviews_total`: Total number of reviews received by the given listing since its inception.
- `total_listings`: Total listings provided by the host/owner.


### 3) reviews.csv (additional data)

- `id`: The unique id for each review.
- `listing_id`: The id corresponding to each listing from `train.csv`
- `month`: The month in which the review was provided.
- `year`: The year in which the review was provided.
- `renters_comments`: The comment that a renter has provided for a given listing.


### 4) sample_submission.csv

- `id`: The unique ID for each property listing.
- `max_rating_class`: According to the guests, the factor which most excites them about the listing (like cleanliness, communication, location, check-in experience, etc.) [Target Feature]

## Reading the data

The datasets provided could be read using the `read_csv()` function in the pandas module.
```
# code to read the dataset
import pandas
df = pandas.read_csv(""train.csv"")

```

## Acknowledgements

Thanks to Airbnb for providing the data.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Evaluation Metrics
---

The evaluation metric for this competition is [Mean F1-Score](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/F-score). The F1 score, commonly used in information retrieval, measures accuracy using the statistics precision ""p"" and recall ""r"". Submissions are evaluated on MeanFScore, which at Kaggle is actually a **micro-averaged F1-score**.


$$Precision{micro} = \frac{\sum{k \in C} TPk}{\sum{k \in C} TPk + FPk}$$

$$Recall{micro} = \frac{\sum{k \in C} TPk}{\sum{k \in C} TPk + FNk}$$

$$MeanFScore = F1{micro}= \frac{2 Precision{micro} Recall{micro}}{Precision{micro} + Recall_{micro}}$$

The F1 metric weights recall and precision equally, and a good retrieval algorithm will maximize both precision and recall simultaneously. Thus, moderately good performance on both will be favored over extremely good performance on one and poor performance on the other.

## Submission Format

For every `id` in the dataset, the submission file should contain two columns: `id` which is the unique id for each row from the testing data, and `max_rating_class` which is the class value. The second column should be a space-delimited string value.

The file should contain a header and have the following format:
```
id,max_rating_class
4a8f50a283b34795,location
```","# Dataset Description

### What files do I need?  
You would need the `train.csv` file to get the training data which consists of information about the property listings and your aim is to use the given data to develop your solution to predict the `max_rating_class` for the testing dataset present in `test.csv`

### What should I expect the data format to be? 
The dataset provided is tabular data with textual data in the additional dataset.

### What am I predicting? 
Your aim is to predict the value of the `max_rating_class` feature for the testing dataset and generate a submission file with `id` and `max_rating_class` as features. A sample submission file is provided in the dataset for reference.

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set
*   **reviews.csv** - the guest's reviews set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

## Columns

- `id`: The unique ID for each property listing.
- `host_[n]`: 8 Features related to the host/owner of the listing. (where n -> [1, 8])
- `property_[n]`: 16 Features related to the amenities at the given listing. (where n -> [1, 16])
- `property_max_booking`: Maximum bookings for a given listing.
- `property_min_booking`: Minimum bookings for a given listing.
- `property_price`: The price provided for a given listing.
- `reviews_each_month`: Avg. reviews received each month by the given listing.
- `reviews_last_30_days`: Number of reviews received in the last 30 days by the given listing.
- `reviews_total`: Total number of reviews received by the given listing since its inception.
- `total_listings`: Total listings provided by the host/owner.
- `max_rating_class`: According to the guests, the factor which most excites them about the listing (like cleanliness, communication, location, check-in experience, etc.) [Target Feature]",False,True
