ÒýÓÃÔª
https://www.kaggle.com/jessicali9530/stanford-dogs-dataset




Context
The Stanford Dogs dataset contains images of 120 breeds of dogs from around the world. This dataset has been built using images and annotation from ImageNet for the task of fine-grained image categorization. It was originally collected for fine-grain image categorization, a challenging problem as certain dog breeds have near identical features or differ in colour and age.

Content
Number of categories: 120
Number of images: 20,580
Annotations: Class labels, Bounding boxes
Acknowledgements
The original data source is found on http://vision.stanford.edu/aditya86/ImageNetDogs/ and contains additional information on the train/test splits and baseline results.

If you use this dataset in a publication, please cite the dataset on the following papers:

Aditya Khosla, Nityananda Jayadevaprakash, Bangpeng Yao and Li Fei-Fei. Novel dataset for Fine-Grained Image Categorization. First Workshop on Fine-Grained Visual Categorization (FGVC), IEEE Conference on Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (CVPR), 2011. [pdf] [poster] [BibTex]

Secondary:
J. Deng, W. Dong, R. Socher, L.-J. Li, K. Li and L. Fei-Fei, ImageNet: A Large-Scale Hierarchical Image Database. IEEE Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (CVPR), 2009. [pdf] [BibTex]

Banner Image from Hannah Lim on Unsplash",False,True
6411,imc2223la3,"# Description

# Source of data

The dataset is an altered part of another dataset and every pattern includes information of a person.

The database has 28 independent variables and one categorical dependent variable which is the one to be predicted and which takes value from a total of 2 labels or categories.

There are 1575, 525 and 1900 patterns in the training, validation and generalization sets, respectively. Training (*train.csv*) and validation (*val.csv*) sets are labelled, and they have the variable that must be predicted in order to create the best model. An unlabelled set called *test.csv* is provided which only contains the data of the 28 independent variables (inputs). The dependent variable is categorical and therefore it should be faced as a **classification problem**.

For all sets, the last input variable is the gender of the person (gender=1 woman, gender=0 man). The distribution of women and men is the following:
| Dataset | Women | Men |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Train | 157 | 1418 |
| Validation | 33 | 492 |
| Test | 1600 | 300 |

In this way, if nothing is done, the models can be biased to men, given that they are much more common in the training and validation sets. However, in the test set and in the different leaderboards, the performance obtained will be almost completely focused on women.

Your mission is to get the best possible model using the sets provided so that you can predict with the greatest certainty the dependent variable (output) from the *test* set. To do this, you will have to adjust the different parameters of the model and of the training and you can use the number of iterations that you consider appropriate. Given that the dataset is quite imbalanced, the performance metric selected is the [f1 score](https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/generated/sklearn.metrics.f1_score.html). Do not confuse the class label to be predicted (last column of *train.csv* and *val.csv*) with the gender (next-to-last column of *train.csv* and *val.csv*, and last column of *test.csv*). The Geometric Mean is calculated with the class labels, not the gender.

The data is not standarized!

#Participants

In this competition, only students of IMC subject can participate. To participate, students must use an identifier of the type ""`IMC2223{userUCO}`"". For those who already have a user account on kaggle they will have the options of: changing their Username and Display Name fields in their user profile, or creating a new account.

We recommend the option of creating a new account to avoid confusion with name changes.

#Rewards

Guided by the maxim ""*the important thing is to participate*"", we, the teachers and collaborators of the subject, would like the majority of students enrolled in the course to be involved.

Therefore, the qualification of the practice will be increased by 0.5 points for all those people (or teams, in this case the points would be distributed as indicated by the members) who are among the 75% of the best private scores of the *LeaderBoard* at the end of the competition.

Moreover, those who obtain the best scores in the private *LeaderBoard* of each competition will obtain:
- First place in the final ranking: 1.5 extra points.
- Second place in the final ranking: 1.0 extra point.
- Third place in the final ranking: 0.75 extra points.

# Acknowledgements

All thanks go to the members of the AYRNA research group for supporting and making this type of project possible.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Your mission is to get the best possible model using the sets provided so that you can predict with the greatest certainty the dependent variable (output) from the *test* set. To do this, you will have to adjust the different parameters of the model and of the training and you can use the number of iterations that you consider appropriate. Given that the dataset is quite imbalanced, the performance metric selected is the [f1 score](https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/generated/sklearn.metrics.f1_score.html). Do not confuse the class label to be predicted (last column of *train.csv* and *val.csv*) with the gender (next-to-last column of *train.csv* and *val.csv*, and last column of *test.csv*). The Geometric Mean is calculated with the class labels, not the gender.","# Dataset Description

# Source of data

The dataset is an altered part of another dataset and every pattern includes information of a person.

The database has 28 independent variables and one categorical dependent variable which is the one to be predicted and which takes value from a total of 2 labels or categories.

There are 1575, 525 and 1900 patterns in the training, validation and generalization sets, respectively. Training (*train.csv*) and validation (*val.csv*) sets are labelled, and they have the variable that must be predicted in order to create the best model. An unlabelled set called *test.csv* is provided which only contains the data of the 28 independent variables (inputs). The dependent variable is categorical and therefore it should be faced as a **classification problem**.

For all sets, the last input variable is the gender of the person (gender=1 woman, gender=0 man). The distribution of women and men is the following:
| Dataset | Women | Men |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Train | 157 | 1418 |
| Validation | 33 | 492 |
| Test | 1600 | 300 |

In this way, if nothing is done, the models can be biased to men, given that they are much more common in the training and validation sets. However, in the test set and in the different leaderboards, the performance obtained will be almost completely focused on women.

Your mission is to get the best possible model using the sets provided so that you can predict with the greatest certainty the dependent variable (output) from the *kaggle* set. To do this, you will have to adjust the different parameters of the model and of the training and you can use the number of iterations that you consider appropriate. Given that the dataset is quite imbalanced, the performance metric selected is the Geometric Mean of the sensitivities for both classes. Do not confuse the class label to be predicted (last column of *train.csv* and *val.csv*) with the gender (next-to-last column of *train.csv* and *val.csv*, and last column of *test.csv*). The Geometric Mean is calculated with the class labels, not the gender.

The data is not standarized!",False,True
