6427,machine-learning-if2,"# Description

## Cardiovascular  Dataset


Cardiovascular diseases (CVDs) are the number 1 cause of death globally, taking an estimated 17.9 million lives each year, which accounts for 31% of all deaths worldwide. Four out of 5CVD deaths are due to heart attacks and strokes, and one-third of these deaths occur prematurely in people under 70 years of age. Heart failure is a common event caused by CVDs and this dataset contains 11 features that can be used to predict a possible heart disease.

People with cardiovascular disease or who are at high cardiovascular risk (due to the presence of one or more risk factors such as hypertension, diabetes, hyperlipidaemia or already established disease) need early detection and management wherein a machine learning model can be of great help.

The dataset you have contains 12 columns. The column `HeartDisease`  is the target variable. You must construct models in order to predict it using the other columns.

Here the other 11 columns you will find in the dataset:

*  **Age** :  age of the patient [years] 
*  **Sex** :  sex of the patient [M: Male, F: Female]
*  **ChestPainType** : chest pain type [TA: Typical Angina, ATA: Atypical Angina, NAP: Non-Anginal Pain, ASY: Asymptomatic]
*  **RestingBP** :   resting blood pressure [mm Hg]
*  **Cholesterol** :   serum cholesterol [mm/dl]
*  **FastingBS** :  fasting blood sugar [1: if FastingBS > 120 mg/dl, 0: otherwise]
*  **RestingECG** :  esting electrocardiogram results [Normal: Normal, ST: having ST-T wave abnormality (T wave inversions and/or ST elevation or depression of > 0.05 mV), LVH: showing probable or definite left ventricular hypertrophy by Estes' criteria]
*  **MaxHR** :  maximum heart rate achieved [Numeric value between 60 and 202]
*  **ExerciseAngina** :  exercise-induced angina [Y: Yes, N: No]
*  **Oldpeak** :  oldpeak = ST [Numeric value measured in depression]
*  **ST_Slope** :  the slope of the peak exercise ST segment [Up: upsloping, Flat: flat, Down: downsloping]
*  **HeartDisease** :  output class [1: heart disease, 0: Normal]

## Source

This dataset was created by combining different datasets already available independently but not combined before. In this dataset, 5 heart datasets are combined over 11 common features which makes it the largest heart disease dataset available so far for research purposes.

https://archive.ics.uci.edu/ml/datasets/heart+disease
----------------------
# Evaluation

## Evaluation metric

The evaluation metric for this competition is the accuracy (proportion of the correct predictions)

## Submission Format

The test set contains 184 rows. You must submit a csv containing two columns. The first columns is named ""id"" and contains strings from ""1"" to ""184 "" (same order of the test set rows). The second column is named `HeartDisease` and is discrete (0 or 1).

Here is a sample of submitted csv :

```
""id"", ""HeartDisease""
""1"", ""1""
""2"", ""1""
...
""183 "", ""0""
""184 "", ""1""
```

Let's say your predictions are in a vector, you can put it in a dataframe, and add the id, and then write a csv file, for example if the predictions are in a vector called ""pred""

```
to_be_submitted = data.frame(id=rownames(test), HeartDisease=pred)
write.csv(to_be_submitted , file = ""to_be_submitted.csv"", row.names = F)
```","# Dataset Description

The dataset you have contains 12 columns. The column `HeartDisease`  is the target variable. You must construct models in order to predict it using the other columns.

Here the other 11 columns you will find in the dataset:

*  **Age** :  age of the patient [years]
*  **Sex** :  sex of the patient [M: Male, F: Female]
*  **Age** :  chest pain type [TA: Typical Angina, ATA: Atypical Angina, NAP: Non-Anginal Pain, ASY: Asymptomatic]
*  **ChestPainType** :  resting blood pressure [mm Hg]
*  **RestingBP** :  serum cholesterol [mm/dl]
*  **FastingBS** :  fasting blood sugar [1: if FastingBS > 120 mg/dl, 0: otherwise]
*  **RestingECG** :  esting electrocardiogram results [Normal: Normal, ST: having ST-T wave abnormality (T wave inversions and/or ST elevation or depression of > 0.05 mV), LVH: showing probable or definite left ventricular hypertrophy by Estes' criteria]
*  **MaxHR** :  maximum heart rate achieved [Numeric value between 60 and 202]
*  **ExerciseAngina** :  exercise-induced angina [Y: Yes, N: No]
*  **Oldpeak** :  oldpeak = ST [Numeric value measured in depression]
*  **ST_Slope** :  the slope of the peak exercise ST segment [Up: upsloping, Flat: flat, Down: downsloping]
*  **HeartDisease** :  output class [1: heart disease, 0: Normal]


## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set",False,True
6428,predict-shots-in-the-premier-league,"# Description

![](https://mediacloud.theweek.co.uk/image/private/s--X-WVjvBW--/f_auto,t_content-image-full-desktop@1/v1653551326/theweek/2022/May/man-city-premier-league-trophy-GettyImages-1398834855.jpg)

##Welcome to CGrow's Shot Prediction Competition!

Indonesia's CGrow Research is running a competition to find Indonesia's best data engineer for predicting shots in the Premier League. 

The English Premier League is the most watched sports league in the world and data is becoming an increasingly significant part of it. Clubs and companies connected with the Premier League are now using data to give them greater insights and understanding of the sport. As well as using predictions to inform their decision making. 

This Kaggle competition is designed to test an applicant's prediction and data manipulation skills, as well as allowing them to use their understanding of football to develop the smartest predictions possible. 

##Prizes

Whilst applicants are welcome worldwide, only Indonesian applicants are eligible for prizes. The Top 5 Indonesian submissions will receive an interview with [CGrow Research](https://www.cgrowresearch.com/). If you would like to be eligible for an interview please contact us via Kaggle or via our email cgrowresearch@gmail.com. Alternative you can leave your contact details in your notebook submission. 

##Good Luck!
----------------------
# Evaluation

##Goal
It is the applicant's job to predict how many shots each football player took in each row of the test.csv.

##Metric
The evaluation metric for this competition is Root Mean Logarithmic Squared Error. Whilst your leadeboard score will be based on this metric, other factors will be considered when determining the competition winner. Primarily:

- The quality, logic and clarity of the code in the applicant's notebook.
- The statistical robustness of their solution. e.g. appropriate distribution used, sensible variables used.


## Submission Format
The file should have exactly 2 columns and have the following format:

- Id (the id of each row sorted in any order)
- Predicted (contains your numeric predictions)

```
Id,Predicted
127_18631,1.048
127_18658,0.995
127_18686,1.176
```

You can download an example submission file (sample_submission.csv) on the [Data page](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/shots-in-the-premier-league/data).","# Dataset Description

## Files

The competition data is presented in the following 3 files:

*   **train.csv** - the training set, on which to build your model.
*   **test.csv** - the test set, on which to build your predictions using your trained model.
*   **sample_submission.csv** - an example submission file in the correct format.

## Guidance

- The goal of the competition is to predict the `Shots` variable for each row of the **test.csv** dataset. 
- You should use the **train.csv** data to identify and develop your predictive variables, and then construct your model. 
- Note that some of the variables are only known once the game is completed and therefore should not be used for prediction purposes. For example, ¡®Team.Goals¡¯ will only be known at the end of the match, so cannot be used for predicting how many shots the player will make in that match. If any of these post-match variables are used directly for prediction, the applicant submission will be invalidated. However, these variables may still serve some legitimate use, such as contextualising the player's shot performance in historic matches. Contextualising a player's historic shot performance may allow for more accurate (and legitimate) predictions in the **test.csv** dataset. 


## Columns

*   `Id` ¨C a unique ID specific to each row
*   `PlyID` ¨C a unique ID specific to each player
*   `Team.Possession` ¨C the percentage of the match for which the player¡¯s team had possession
*   `WinProb` ¨C the implied probability the team has of winning the game according to the betting markets prior to Kick Off.
*   `PlyPosition` ¨C the position on the pitch that player played. This is typically known an hour before kick off when lineups are announced. e.g. ""AML = Attacking Midfielder Left"", ""DR = Defender Right"", ""GK = Goalkeeper"".
*   `Minutes` ¨C how many minutes the player ended up playing.
*   `HmAw` ¨C was the player¡¯s team playing at home or away?
*   `Shots` ¨C how many shots the player made in the game, the metric we are trying to predict",False,True
