6473,datathon202,"# Description

These days it was very difficult to survive in cities due to the hazardous chemicals present in the atmosphere.
It is very difficult for us to survive in such a hazardous environment and also has adverse impacts on human health, the environment, and the economy. It will be very difficult for human survival. So, we use machine learning to predict the level of sustainability of that place by calculating the level of sustainability such that humans can survive in that environment with the help of chemical compounds present in the environment using regression.

----------------------
# Evaluation



The evaluation metric for this competition is  RMSE(Root Mean Squared Error).

## Submission Format

The file should contain two columns named Id, and SI.","# Dataset Description

The train.csv file has 17 columns , it is the training dataset , the dataset describes the presence of harmful chemical substance in the surroundings of some selected cities of USA . the target value is the ""SI"" column which stands for ""sustainability index"". the higher its value the less habitable is that city for a healthy living.
You need to predict the future value of ""SI"" using the other attributes.
## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training set ,it has 16000 rows , train your model on this datset.
*   **test.csv** - the test set ,it has 4000 rows , predict the target value for this dataset.
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format, your submission file should have the same column names as this file.


## Columns

*   `Id` - It is the series number of rows
*   `City`?- City names 
*   `Date` - Date of the values recorded
*   `WP1` - It stands for Weather_parameter-1  .
*   `WP2` - It stands for Weather_parameter-2
*    `N1 ,N2,N3,N4` - These are the compounds of Nitrogen like No2,No,etc.
*    `Carbon` - level of Carbon contaminant
*    `Sulphur` - level of Sulphur contaminant
*    `Ozone` - level of Ozone 
*    `IN1,IN2` - level of Inert gases.
*    `OC1,OC2` - level of Organic compounds .
*     `SI` - Sustainability Index the higher its value the higher is the damage to health.",False,True
6474,campus-recruitment-prediction,"# Description

# Hello AI Bootcampers ???? Welcome to the evaluation of the Machine Learning Track??

## Campus Recruitment:
A campus placement program is a program that provides jobs to students nearing the end of their studies at universities or other educational institutions. This type of program involves educational institutions partnering with corporations that want to recruit students.

## Goal of Competition:
The main objective is to build a Machine Learning model that predicts whether students will have jobs after graduation or not.


----------------------
# Evaluation

**This competition evaluates submissions based on the [Accuracy] evaluation metric. In addition, precision and recall should be considered. Precision is the ratio of true positives to all predicted positives. The recall is the ratio of true positives to all positives.**

## Submission Format
It is recommended that each group submit one Notebook in `.ipynb` format along with a CSV file that contains the `sl_no` and `status` of the best model you built **that achieved the highest accuracy**. You can resubmit it if necessary, but **please delete the old version.**","# Dataset Description

# Overview
This data set consists of placement data of students in an MR campus. It includes secondary and higher secondary school percentage and specialization. It also includes degree specialization, type and work experience, and salary offers to the placed students.


## Files
There are 4 files that will be included, which are:
* Competition Notebook Template: `Campus Recruitment Prediction Competition.ipynb`. **Use this template to build your models.** Also, you can find it under **Code** tap.
*  Train dataset file: `train.csv`
*  Test dataset file: `test.csv`
*  Submission sample file: `submission_sample.csv`, you have to submit along with your **Notebook** a **CSV** file that contains the `sl_no` and `status` of the best model you built with the highest accuracy.


## Data Dictionary
| Variable | Definition |
| --- | --- |
| sl_no | Serial Number |
| gender | Gender |
| ssc_p | Secondary Education percentage |
| ssc_b | Board of Education |
| hsc_p | Higher Secondary Education percentage |
| hsc_b | Board of Education |
| degree_p | Degree Percentage | 
| workex | Work Experience |
| etest_p | Employability test percentage | 
| specialisation | Post Graduation (MBA) |
| mba_p | MBA percentage | 
| hsc_s_freq | Specialization in Higher Secondary Education |
| degree_t_freq | Under Graduation (Degree type) |
| status | Status of placement |",False,True
6475,final-project-predictive-analytics,"# Description

This dataset contains information about used cars.
Based on training data, you are required to file build regression models to predict the price of cars.

## Acknowledgements

We thank Mr. Nguyen Thanh Tuan for providing this dataset.
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation pages describes how submissions will be scored and how students should format their submissions. You don't need a sub-title at the top; the page title appears above. For an example of a high quality evaluation page, see the 2013 KDD Cup page, which we have included below:

---

The evaluation metric for this competition is [Root mean square error]. The root mean square error, commonly used to measure the accuracy in regression problem:

\[ RMSE = \sqrt{ \cfrac{\sum\limits_{i=1}^n \ (y_i - \hat{y}_i)^2}{n} } \]

where \[n\] is the number of observations in the test data set; \[y_i\] is the \[i^{th}\] true value and \[\hat{y}_i\] is the \[i^{th}\] predicted value

Precision is the ratio of true positives \(\text{tp}\) to all predicted positives \(\text{tp} + \text{fp}\). Recall is the ratio of true positives \(\text{tp}\) to all actual positives \(\text{tp} + \text{fn}\). The F1 score is given by:

\[ \text{F1} = 2\frac{\text{p} \cdot \text{r}}{\text{p}+\text{r}}\ \ \mathrm{where}\ \ \text{p} = \frac{\text{tp}}{\text{tp}+\text{fp}},\ \ \text{r} = \frac{\text{tp}}{\text{tp}+\text{fn}} \]

The F1 metric weights recall and precision equally, and a good retrieval algorithm will maximize both precision and recall simultaneously. Thus, moderately good performance on both will be favored over extremely good performance on one and poor performance on the other.

## Submission Format

**For every author in the dataset**, submission files should contain two columns: `AuthorId` and `DuplicateAuthorIds`. `DuplicateAuthorIds` should be a space-delimited list. Every `AuthorId` counts as its own duplicate, and every duplicate should be listed under each of its respective ids. For example, if you suspect author A, B, and C are the same, you should list `A,A B C`, `B,B A C`, `C,C A B`.


The file should contain a header and have the following format:
```
AuthorId,DuplicateAuthorIds
1,1
```","# Dataset Description


This dataset contains information about used cars.
Based on training data, you are required to file build regression models to predict the price of cars.

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

## Columns

*   `example_id` - definition of example_id
*   `feature_1`?- definition of feature_1
*   etc.",False,True
6476,crypto-mid-freq-forecasting,"# Description

HKML is successful at trading cryptocurrencies, but we are always striving to improve our process.
We also have a passion to ***uncover and develop strong raw talents!***

In this competition, we provide you with trading features (10 of them), and your goal is to predict the daily 3-day future return for 65 coins (actually, the 3-day future return after some processing on our side).

----------------------
# Evaluation

This forecasting competition aims to predict 3-day future returns $R_i(t)$ for each coin $1 \leq i \leq 65$, at any time $t$. For each row in the dataset, we include the target for prediction `fwd_ret_3d`.
The target is derived from the 3-day horizon future return of the coin $i$.

The evaluation metric for this competition is the Spearman correlation between the vector of predicted $(\hat{R}\_1(t), \ldots, \hat{R}\_{65}(t))$ and the targets $(R\_1(t), \ldots, R\_{65}(t))$, that is the linear correlation between your predictions of the 3-day future returns and the actual 3-day future returns.
The higher the correlation, the better!

*But, do not expect very high correlations... systematic trading is not easy!*","# Dataset Description

The dataset essentially contains the features and the target to predict at date t, for all of the 65 cryptos.

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

## Columns

*   `date` - the date when the features were recorded
*   `coin_id` - identifier of the cryptocurrency (65 different cryptos)
*   `fwd_ret_3d`?- the target, 3-day horizon future return
*   `feat_1..10`?- 10 features available",False,True
