6489,tfugagadir-ml-olympiad-2023-argania-detection,"# Description

# ML Olympiad'23:  Argania Deforestation Detection with Satellite Imagery

The Argania tree is a unique and important species found primarily in Morocco, where it plays a crucial role in the ecosystem and economy. However, the tree and its surrounding environment are facing a number of dangers that threaten its survival.

One major threat to the Argania tree is over-harvesting. The tree produces a valuable nut from which oil is extracted, and this has led to the over-exploitation of the tree for commercial gain. This not only reduces the number of trees but also damages the remaining population by removing the fruit before it is ripe, which can prevent future growth and reproduction.

Another danger to the Argania tree is habitat destruction. The tree is native to a small region in Morocco, and as human populations have grown and expanded, much of its natural habitat has been destroyed or degraded. This not only reduces the number of trees but also makes it more difficult for the remaining population to survive and reproduce.

Climate change is also a significant threat to the Argania tree. The tree is adapted to a specific climatic range, and changes in temperature and precipitation patterns can have a significant impact on its survival. 

In addition, grazing by goats is another danger to the Argania tree, as goats can overgraze and cause damage to the tree.

To protect the Argania tree, it is important to implement sustainable harvesting practices, protect and restore its natural habitat, and address the impact of climate change. Additionally, managing the grazing of goats is also crucial.

## Goal of the Competition

This is a land cover competition that tries to predict whether the features of a single pixel are either an Argania tree or something else.

## Context 

The Argania region is known for its rich biodiversity and unique ecosystem, including the endangered argan tree. However, deforestation in the area is a growing concern, with illegal logging and land conversion for agriculture being the main drivers of forest loss.

In this competition, you will use Sentinel-2 satellite imagery to detect and map areas of deforestation in the Argania region. The Sentinel-2 satellite is equipped with multispectral imaging capabilities, providing high-resolution images with 13 spectral bands. These images will be used to extract pixel values that will be used to train models that can detect Argania cover and identify areas where trees have been cut down later on.

This competition aims to develop accurate and reliable algorithms for detecting deforestation in the Argania region using Sentinel-2 satellite imagery. The competition will be divided into two phases: a training phase, where participants will use labeled data to train their models, and a test phase, where the best-performing models will be evaluated on unseen data.

The competition will be hosted on Kaggle, and the winning solutions will be used to support conservation efforts in the Argania region.
The participants will have to create models to classify the input pixel as either Argania or not Argania based on the different spectral channels. 

## Data 

The dataset will include Sentinel-2 satellite images of the Argania region, with corresponding labels indicating whether a given pixel represents an argan tree or not. The dataset will be split into a training set and a test set, with the test set being used to evaluate the performance of the submitted models.

## Evaluation

The models will be evaluated on the test set using the F1-score metric. The F1-score is a measure of a model's accuracy that takes into account both precision and recall and is commonly used in imbalanced classification tasks such as this one. The team with the highest F1 score 

## Timeline

Competition start date: February 10th, 2023
Submission deadline: March 5th, 2023
Winner announcement: March 5th, 2023

## Acknowledgements

We would like to thank the IRF-SIC Laboratory for providing us with the Dataset and for Google Developers'ML Developer Programs Team for their support. 

Special Thanks to the team members, Youness Karmoud, Souad Saidi, Dr. Soufiane Idbraim, and Taha Bouhsine for the efforts made in creating this dataset. 

And to the host Taha Bouhsine, and co-host Souad Saidi for their efforts to formulate and maintain this competition. 

## Final Notes

Individuals or groups of up to three people are allowed to participate in the competition. 

You will be asked to provide the code and data you used to train your models. 

These models will be used to build an Argania Tree Monitoring system that will help protect the forests in the South of Morocco.

ML Olympiad is an associated Kaggle Community Competitions hosted by Google ML communities, supported by Google Developers

> This is a Code Competition. Refer to [Code Requirements](https://www.kaggle.com/c/tfugagadir-ml-olympiad-2023-argania-detection/overview/code-requirements) for details.


Thank you for participating #MLOlympiad #AppliedML #MLResearch #Satellite

