6502,mine-the-model-celesta-2023,"# Description

Artificial Intelligence is changing the landscape of society, disrupting many industries and unlocking various barriers. Underlying its power is the ability to predict crucial information given the data collected or mined by companies. ""Mine the Model"" is a competitive machine-learning event in which teams of up to 3 individuals will analyze and build machine-learning models to predict unknown attributes of datasets obtained from the industry. It gives a hands-on opportunity to tackle practical problems using AI/ML.

## Competition Description
The objective of this contest is to create a machine-learning model to **predict the retail price** of houses given tabular data like house sqft, # of toilets/rooms, and location along with **image of the house**. 

Duration: Jan 20th (6 PM) - Jan 28th (11:59 PM) 
Max team size: 3 people

## Prize
First: Rs.6000
Second: Rs.3500
Third: Rs.2500
The top 3 teams will get a chance to interview for an internship opportunity at ChiSquare X.

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F6016706%2F3002a4717779f2145832160d1f8524b7%2FMIND%20THE%20MODEL%20(5).png?generation=1673449554102107&alt=media)


----------------------
# Evaluation

## Evaluation Metric
Submissions are evaluated on Root-Mean-Squared-Error (RMSE) between the predicted value and the observed sales price. i.e,
![](https://media.geeksforgeeks.org/wp-content/uploads/20200622171741/RMSE1.jpg)

Note:
The top 2 submissions will be chosen for the final evaluation. 
To ensure that competitors cannot fool the system, the rows in the solution file are sampled into Public and Private rows. Public scores are shown on the public leaderboard, while only the private scores are used to determine the competition winner.

## Submission Format
Submission files should contain two columns: `index` and the predicted `retail price`. 
The file should contain a header and have the following format:
| index | retail_price |
| --- | --- |
| 3654 | 499900 |","# Dataset Description

The objective of this contest is to create a machine-learning model to predict the retail price of houses given tabular data like house sqft, # of toilets/rooms, and location, along with an image of the house. 

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set
*   **sample.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

### Images
The images of the house are given in the following [drive link](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1-2VG8WgzVNQrhyo3FCg8u9g3sf2CiMsA?usp=share_link) with the name of the image as `image_id.jpg`. 
*   **train_image** - folder containing the training images
*   **test_image** - folder containing the test images

## Columns
*   `retail_price` - the retail price of the house in dollars
*   `rooms`?- the number of rooms present in the house
*   `toilets`?- the number of toilets present in the house
*   `address`?- the address of the house 
*   `city`?- the name of the city where the house is present
*   `sqft`?- the area, in sqft, of the house

### Example

|index|rooms|retail price|address|name|sqft|toilets|image_id|
| ----- | ------- | ---------| ------------ | --- | --- | --- | --- |
|  981| 4| 349900|1903 Shamrock Avenue|Palmdale, CA | 1690|3|981|

981.jpg
![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F6016706%2F03a8993fc7759abb9a4be841e21b88e3%2F981.jpg?generation=1673000672536142&alt=media)",False,True
6503,copy-of-house-prices-prediction,"# Description

<h3>Start here if...</h3>
<p>You have some experience&nbsp;with R or Python and&nbsp;machine learning basics. This is a perfect competition for data science students&nbsp;who have completed an online course in machine learning and are looking to expand their skill set before trying a featured competition.&nbsp;</p>
<h3>Competition Description</h3>
<p><img src=""https://storage.googleapis.com/kaggle-media/competitions/kaggle/5407/media/housesbanner.png"" style=""display: block; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto"" height=""112"" width=""633""></p>
<p>Ask a home buyer to describe their dream house, and they probably won't begin with the height of the basement ceiling or the proximity to an east-west railroad. But this playground competition's dataset proves that much more influences price negotiations than the number of bedrooms or a white-picket fence.</p>
<p>With 79 explanatory variables describing (almost) every aspect of residential homes in Ames, Iowa, this competition challenges you to predict the final price of each home.</p>
<h3>Practice Skills</h3>
<ul>
<li>Creative feature engineering&nbsp;</li>
<li>Advanced regression techniques like random forest and gradient boosting</li>
</ul>
<h2>Acknowledgments</h2>
<p>The <a href=""http://www.amstat.org/publications/jse/v19n3/decock.pdf"" target=""_blank"">Ames Housing&nbsp;dataset</a>&nbsp;was compiled&nbsp;by Dean De Cock for use in data science education. It's an incredible alternative&nbsp;for data scientists looking for a modernized and expanded version of the often cited Boston Housing dataset.&nbsp;</p>

<p>Photo by <a href=""https://unsplash.com/@tthfilms"">Tom Thain</a> on Unsplash.</p>
----------------------
# Evaluation

<h3>Goal</h3>
<p>It is your job to predict the sales price for each house.&nbsp;For each Id in the test set, you must predict the value of the SalePrice variable.&nbsp;</p>
<h3>Metric</h3>
<p>Submissions are evaluated on <a href=""https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Root-mean-square_deviation"" target=""_blank"">Root-Mean-Squared-Error (RMSE)</a>&nbsp;between the logarithm of the predicted value&nbsp;and the logarithm of the observed sales price. (Taking logs means that errors in predicting expensive houses and cheap houses will affect the result equally.)</p>
<h3>Submission File Format</h3>
<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>Id,SalePrice<br>1461,169000.1<br>1462,187724.1233<br>1463,175221<br>etc.</pre>
<p>You can download an example submission file (sample_submission.csv) on the&nbsp;<a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/c/house-prices-advanced-regression-techniques/data"" target=""_blank"">Data page.</a></p>
