6505,data-engineering-lab-9b-fall-2022-section-3,"# Description

Analyze diamonds by their cut, color, clarity, price, and other attributes to predict the price of a diamond based on the characteristics of cut, color, clarity, and carat.

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F3532147%2F4cfa3401b21d61e4c66a12f447515c43%2Fe40_1.png?generation=1671447310335998&alt=media)


### Practice Skills

- Data Engineering.
- Creative feature engineering.
- Basic regression techniques such as Linear regression
`
### Overview of How Kaggle¡¯s Competitions Work

1. Join the Competition -> Read about the challenge description, accept the Competition Rules and gain access to the competition dataset.
2. Get to Work -> Download the data, build models on it locally or on Kaggle Kernels (our no-setup, customizable Jupyter Notebooks environment with free GPUs) and generate a prediction file.
3. Make a Submission -> Upload your prediction as a submission on Kaggle and receive an accuracy score.
4. Check the Leaderboard -> See how your model ranks against other Kagglers on our leaderboard.
Improve Your Score

### Acknowledgements

https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/shivam2503/diamonds

----------------------
# Evaluation

### Goal

It is your job to predict the price of diamonds.

### Evaluation Metric

Your score is the standard deviation of the residuals (prediction errors). This is known as Root Mean Square Error (RMSE).

### Submission File Format

You should submit a csv file with exactly 10788 entries plus a header row. Your submission will show an error if you have extra columns (beyond ID and price) or rows.

The file should contain a header and have the following format:

ID,price
10788,100","# Dataset Description

### Context

This classic dataset contains the prices and other attributes of almost 54,000 diamonds. It's a great dataset for beginners learning to work with data analysis and visualization.

### Content

price: price in US dollars (\$326--\$18,823)
carat: weight of the diamond (0.2--5.01)
cut: quality of the cut (Fair, Good, Very Good, Premium, Ideal)
color: diamond colour, from J (worst) to D (best)
clarity: a measurement of how clear the diamond is (I1 (worst), SI2, SI1, VS2, VS1, VVS2, VVS1, IF (best))
x: length in mm (0--10.74)
y: width in mm (0--58.9)
z: depth in mm (0--31.8)
depth: total depth percentage = z / mean(x, y) = 2 * z / (x + y) (43--79)
table: width of top of diamond relative to widest point (43--95)

### Files

**trainingD.csv** - the training set
**testingD.csv** - the test set
**sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format",False,True
6506,analytics-competition-havaianas-ec-data-2017,"# Description

## Goal of the Competition
The goal of this competition is to analyze Havaianas ECommerce data for several months of 2017. You will be given the real retail shopper data of platform [Shopee](https://shopee.sg/) and [Qoo10](https://www.qoo10.sg/).

Your work will help Havaianas and [Aroodai](https://www.aroodai.com/)  better understand their business. With better insights and visualization, they can apply these tactics into business growth. And you will also have the oppotunity to win the prizes sponsored by Aroodai.
<hr style=""height:1px;border:none;border-top:1px solid #555555;"" />
## Havaianas Introduction
- Havaianas is the largest sandal brand in the world, selling over 200 million pairs a year.
- We are sharing 10 years of actual data from our Havaianas Singapore business from 2009 to 2018, including our 24 retail shops, 65 distribution points, warehouses, mobile app, 25 virtual stores and 8 eCommerce stores, totalling over 10 million pairs of Havaianas sold, making Singapore the biggest Havaianas distributor per capita in the world.
- Actual Data includes: Sales & Cash-register data, transcribed Product data, product images, camera & IOT data, Marketing data, eCommerce data, CRM data, and much more.
- We have also built a Havaianas deep-learning color-and-pattern recognition model using product photos, transcribed catalog data & our sales data to predict future high-selling sandal designs.
<hr style=""height:1px;border:none;border-top:1px solid #555555;"" />
## Credential of Aroodai
- AROODAI PTE. LTD. (Singapore Company 201539281E) started as our own in-house data-analytics company to service our own retail & restaurant & partners businesses in Singapore. We also worked closely with the Singapore Government, the Singapore Agency for Science, Technology and Research (A*STAR) and Deloitte Digital for their own Retail & Restaurant clients.
- Our team built A.I. models to predict product sales, pricing & discounting, eCommerce traffic, customer behavior, marketing budgets & merchandise planning.
- Since the Pandemic¡¯s adverse effect on the retail & restaurant industry, we have closed our own retail businesses, and are now sharing our data & predictive models with outside retailers, restaurants and the data-science community at large by empowering a culture of sharing & knowledge.
- Here, you¡¯ll find our datasets on which much of our sample codes are built. We are encouraging the community to explore these datasets and, hopefully, make use of them towards your own learning journey in data science.
- If you are a retailer, restaurant or data-scientist interested in what we do, or need more free actual data and models from our stores, outlets, warehouses and ecommerce sites, please let us know at contact@aroodai.com.
<hr style=""height:1px;border:none;border-top:1px solid #555555;"" />
## What You Should Notice
- [Shopee](https://shopee.sg/) and [Qoo10](https://www.qoo10.sg/) are ECommerce platforms like Amazon. Havaianas runs online shops on Shopee and Qoo10. Customers buy Havaianas products via online payment, and Shopee and Qoo10 will deliver the package of products through express delivery.
- We released a lot of data or features, like purchase details, delivery information, inventory data and SKU styles, etc.
- In this competition, you are free to conduct any kinds of data analysis or visualization, which you think will add value to Havaianas or Aroodai. You should follow the [Rules of this competition](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/analytics-competition-havaianas-ec-data-2017/rules). Your solution will be evaluated based on the [metrics](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/analytics-competition-havaianas-ec-data-2017/overview/evaluation) here.
- **SPECIAL NOTICE:** This competition is an **Analytics Competition**. You **do not** need to submit any solution to Leaderboard. You **only** need to create **notebook** in [Code](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/analytics-competition-havaianas-ec-data-2017/code).
- We hold the rights to make some changes on the rules and arrangement of the competition.
----------------------
# Timeline

- January 10, 2023 - Start Date.

- July 31, 2023 - Final Submission Deadline.

All deadlines are at 11:59 PM UTC on the corresponding day unless otherwise noted. The competition organizers reserve the right to update the contest timeline if they deem it necessary.
----------------------
# Instructions

## Submission Instructions
You can make as many public notebooks as you like, but we will only consider your latest commit before the deadline of the copetition.

All team members must accept the competition rules before the submission deadline.

A valid submission or notebook will include a Notebook Analysis. All notebooks submitted must be made public on or before the submission deadline to be eligible.

**SPECIAL NOTICE:** This competition is an **Analytics Competition**. You **do not** need to submit any solution to Leaderboard. You **only** need to create **notebook** in [Code](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/analytics-competition-havaianas-ec-data-2017/code).
----------------------
# Prizes

## Prizes for Philippine Participants
For Philippine Participants, we are awarding Havaianas Online Gift Vouchers (Gift Vouchers valid only in the Philippines, delivered to winners via email)
* 1st Prize - P10,000
* 2nd Prize - P5,000
* 3rd Prize - P2,500

## Prizes for Participants that can accept Amazon Gift Cards in their country
Winning recipient must live in a country that Amazon allows them to receive Gift Cards in their country's Amazon website. Not all countries comply with this Amazon restriction, please check Amazon.com for more information. If the winning participant cannot receive an Amazon gift card in their country, we cannot issue prizes in that country, and the winner will not receive a prize.
* 1st Prize - USD$200 Amazon gift card
* 2nd Prize - USD$100 Amazon gift card
* 3rd Prize - USD$50 Amazon gift card
----------------------
# Evaluation

## Overview

Your challenge is to generate actionable, practical, and novel insights from Havaianas ECommerce data.

<hr>

## Scoring

An entry to the competition consists of a Notebook submission that is evaluated on the following five components, where 0 is the low score and 10 is the high score, and the Upvotes together as following formula:

total\_score = evaluation\_average\_score + Upvotes

Submissions will be judged based on how well they address:

Innovation:

- Are the proposed findings actionable?
- Is this a way of looking at tracking data that is novel?
- Is this project creative?

Accuracy:

- Is the work correct?
- Are claims backed up by data?
- Are the statistical models appropriate given the data?

Relevance:

- Does the analysis help for the business potential growth?

Clarity:

- Evaluate the writing with respect to how clear the writer(s) make findings.

Data visualization/tables:

- Are the charts and tables provided accessible, interesting, visually appealing, and accurate?

Judges will be Aroodai managers and Data Scientists. Scores will be averaged so that each of the components above are weighed equally.

The top eight scoring papers will be selected as finalists.

<hr>

## Notebook requirements

Notebooks should consist of no more than 2,000 words and no more than 10 tables/figures. Submissions will not be penalized for any number of words or figures under this limit. Participants are encouraged to show statistical code if it helps readers better understand their analyses; most, if not all code, however, should be hidden in the Appendix.

All notebooks submitted must be made public on or before the submission deadline to be eligible.

Any notebook that does not use the player tracking data will not be scored.","# Dataset Description

## File descriptions
*   **ECommerce_data_train.csv** - the released data for analytics
*   **Column Description.xlsx** - supplemental column information about the data",False,True
6507,ml-challenge-1,"# Description

eBird is the world's largest citizen science project. To date, more than 1 billion bird observations have been tallied across nearly 100 million checklists submitted by nearly 750,000 citizen scientists across the globe, documenting over 99% of the bird species known to exist today.

With big data comes big challenges. One of these challenges is ensuring the data is accurate and complete. Often, citizen scientists forget about some species they observed in the field when submitting their checklist, which leads to missing data. We don't want missing data in scientific datasets, especially if we expect it to be complete.  In this challenge, you will create a tool that recommends species that are most likely to be observed next given any checklist, so that citizen scientists in the field can double-check that their checklists not missing any species that they observed but forgot to record.
----------------------
# Evaluation

For each test checklist, you must submit 5 recommendations for species that were not reported but likely observed. The ground truth is 5 species that were observed but set to zero. The evaluation metric is Mean Absolute Error (MAE). What this means is that for a given checklist, if you get 3/5 recommendations correct, you got 2 wrong for a total of 4 incorrect entries (2 false positives, 2 false negatives). If there are 85 species, your error measurement is 4/85 = 0.047.

**What's an excellent score?**
Using methods we've talked about in class, an excellent score would be less than about 0.047 (3/5 correct recommendations). A score of 0.054 or less indicates a solid approach, but could possibly benefit from hyperparameter tuning or methodological optimization.  A score of 0.117 is an entirely incorrect solution, and a score of 0.10 or greater is to be expected from random chance.

**How can I improve my score?**
You get 10 submissions per day, so think about trying a number of different hyperparameters (e.g. *k* in k-means or kNN), trying different normalization strategies, or combining multiple approaches in a weighted algorithm.","# Dataset Description

You are given two files:  **test_set.csv** and **training_set.csv** and must submit something like **sample_submission.csv**.

**training_set.csv**
The training set is a matrix of bird species in rows, checklists in columns, and number of birds recorded on a checklist as values.  Zeros are true zeros because the species was not detected at the time of checklist submission.  

**test_set.csv**
The test set a matrix of additional checklists containing the same bird species in rows as the training set.  However, unlike the training set, each test checklist contains exactly 5 values that have been set to zero that were not zero in the submitted checklist.  Your task is to try and predict which of those values are actually non-zero based on information in the training set.  You will be evaluated against the ground truth using Mean Absolute Error (MAE).

**sample_submission.csv**
The sample submission is csv file containing a column called ""Id"" and a column called ""Expected"".  The **submission_ids.csv** file can help you understand how each ""Id"" lines up with species and checklists in the test set.  Briefly, you will create a matrix of 0s and 1s with the same number of rows and columns as the test set.  There will be exactly 5 1s in each column of this matrix, and none of the 1s will overlap with an index that is non-zero in the test set (because you are recommending 5 species not observed).  Then you will convert this matrix from ""wide"" format to ""tall"" format by stacking the columns on top of one another instead of next to one another.  See the notebook on ""Getting Started"" for a programmatic demonstration of how to do this.",False,True
6508,epita-nlp-medical,"# Description

Des m¨¦decins ont annot¨¦ pr¨¨s de **5000 morceaux (snippets) de documents m¨¦dicaux**. Dans chaque snippet, une mention de maladie a ¨¦t¨¦ extraite et class¨¦e dans une des 18 familles de maladies pr¨¦sent¨¦es en annexe. Par exemple:
> Motif
Consultation cardiologique avant chirurgie d'une r¨¦cidive de carcinome ¨¦pidermo?de vulvaire.
R¨¦sum¨¦ clinique - Histoire de la maladie

Chaque snippet contient
- Un **span** indiquant o¨´ se trouve l¡¯entit¨¦ extraite dans le snippet.
- Un **label** indiquant la maladie annot¨¦e
- Des qualificateurs d¡¯entit¨¦s, ¨¤ savoir:
   - La **n¨¦gation** (la maladie est-elle ni¨¦e ?)
   - La **parent¨¦** (la maladie est-elle associ¨¦e au patient ou ¨¤ un membre de sa famille ?)
   - **L¡¯hypoth¨¨se** (la maladie est-elle mentionn¨¦e comme hypoth¨¦tique ?)  

Le but est de cr¨¦er un algorithme de NLP permettant l¡¯extraction de ces maladies dans le texte, associ¨¦es ¨¤ leurs qualificateurs, afin d¡¯utiliser cet algorithme dans un but de recherche: celui-ci va permettre de classifier les patients pr¨¦sents dans l¡¯Entrep?t de Donn¨¦es de Sant¨¦ de l¡¯AP-HP, cette classification ¨¦tant ensuite utilis¨¦e au sein de projets de recherche.

> **Remarque**  
L'ensemble des donn¨¦es pr¨¦sent¨¦es ont ¨¦t¨¦ pr¨¦alablement pseudonymis¨¦es via un algorithme d¨¦di¨¦, puis une double v¨¦rification manuelle a ¨¦t¨¦ effectu¨¦e afin d'obtenir un dataset anonymis¨¦.

L'extraction des entit¨¦s se fera au travers d'algorithmes ¨¤ base de r¨¨gles. Pour la qualification, une approche ¨¤ base de r¨¨gles est ¨¦galement disponible, toutefois il s'agira ici d'impl¨¦menter une approche ¨¤ base de Deep Learning

# Le projet

Le projet se d¨¦coupe en 4 parties (la derni¨¨re ¨¦tape en bonus), chaque partie ¨¦tant d¨¦taill¨¦e dans son onglet d¨¦di¨¦. Par groupes de 3 personnes, suivez les indications pr¨¦sent¨¦es dans ces onglets, ainsi que dans les ¨¦ventuels notebooks associ¨¦es. L'onglet **3 - Visualisation** peut ¨ºtre appliqu¨¦ pour les parties 1 et 2. La partie 4 est volontairement tr¨¨s ouverte.

Chaque onglet contient un paragraphe **proposition de travail** mentionnant une approche possible. Il est conseill¨¦ de suivre cette approche mais vous pouvez vous en ¨¦carter si vous le jugez n¨¦cessaire et le justifiez.

## Rendu

- Un support (rapport, notebooks, HTML ...) pr¨¦sentant et argumentant le code et les d¨¦veloppements effectu¨¦s.
- Une soutenance orale avec PPT.

## D¨¦roulement

- **Lundi 16 janvier, 10h -> 12h** Pr¨¦sentation et lancement du projet
- **Mercredi 18 janvier, TBD** Session d'¨¦change et de r¨¦ponse aux ¨¦ventuelles questions
- **Vendredi 20 janvier apr¨¨s-midi** Soutenance orale (~ 15 minutes)

### Remarques sur Kaggle

- Apr¨¨s inscription sur kaggle puis v¨¦rification de votre t¨¦l¨¦phone, vous disposerez de 38h de GPU. Utilisez ces GPU lors de vos  entra?nements de mod¨¨les afin de r¨¦duire (drastiquement) les temps d'entra?nement. Suivant vos mod¨¨les, vous devriez pouvoir effectuer un fine-tuning en une dizaine de minutes en utilisant **GPU T4 x2**
- Sur vos notebooks kaggle , pensez ¨¤ activer internet (pour le t¨¦l¨¦chargement de modules / mod¨¨les) et la persistance des donn¨¦es.
- **?? bien que Kaggle serve traditionellement ¨¤ h¨¦berger des comp¨¦titions, aucune soumission n'est a effectuer dans le cadre de ce projet ! Kaggle ne sert qu'¨¤ partager les donn¨¦es et ressources**
- Si besoin, utilisez l'onglet **Discussion** pour discuter entre vous ou poser des questions


#### Bon courage !
----------------------
# 1 ¨C Algorithmes ¨¤ Base De R¨¨gles

## Pr¨¦sentation

La tache d'extraction passe par la construction de terminologies propres ¨¤ chaque maladie. Ces terminologies ont ¨¦t¨¦ g¨¦n¨¦r¨¦es ¨¤ l'aide de m¨¦decins et sont rendues disponibles dans une librairie Open-Source d¨¦velopp¨¦e ¨¤ l'AP-HP : [EDS-NLP](https://github.com/aphp/edsnlp)

### La librairie EDS-NLP

La librairie est construite autour de spaCy, un framework populaire de NLP. Vous trouverez un guide basique [ici](https://aphp.github.io/edsnlp/latest/tutorials/spacy101/).  
Dans le cadre de ce cours, une version d¨¦di¨¦e est disponible:
- Lien [GitHub](https://github.com/aphp/edsnlp/tree/epita)
- Lien vers la [documentation](https://aphp.github.io/edsnlp/epita/)

### Les algorithmes de NER

L'extraction d'entit¨¦ se fait ¨¤ l'aide des terminologies mentionn¨¦es ci-dessous ainsi qu'un certain nombre de r¨¨gles ad-hoc permettant d'afiner l'extraction et/ou d'exclurer des faux-positifs.

### Les algorithmes de qualification

De m¨ºme, des algorithmes de qualification (n¨¦gation, parent¨¦, hypoth¨¨se) se basent sur un ensemble de r¨¨gles pour qualifier une entit¨¦.

### Notebook
Le [notebook d'exemple](https://www.kaggle.com/thomzoy/1-ner-et-qualification-base-de-r-gles) vous montre comment instancier une pipeline spaCy contenant les diff¨¦rents algorithmes de NER et de qualification. Vous pouvez partir de ce notebook pour la suite:

>## **Proposition de travail**
>
>**- Se familiariser avec spaCy et EDS-NLP**
>**- Construire la pipeline compl¨¨te d'extraction d'entit¨¦ + qualification ¨¤ base de r¨¨gles**
>**- Calculer des m¨¦triques sur les donn¨¦es disponibles (NER seul, NER + Qualification / Qualification seule)**



## Liens utiles

- Classe de base utilis¨¦e pour cr¨¦er les pipelines de NER : https://aphp.github.io/edsnlp/epita/pipelines/core/contextual-matcher/
- Ex¨¦cuter une pipeline spaCy sur de nombreux textes: https://aphp.github.io/edsnlp/epita/tutorials/multiple-texts/
- Documentation sur les qualificateurs ¨¤ base de r¨¨gles: https://aphp.github.io/edsnlp/epita/pipelines/qualifiers/#available-pipeline

----------------------
# 2 ¨C Qualification D'entit¨¦ Par ML

## Pr¨¦sentation

La qualification ¨¤ base de r¨¦gles permet d'¨¦tablir une baseline. Toutefois, il  est possible d'obtenir de meilleurs performances en d¨¦veloppant un mod¨¨le de Deep Learning et en l'entra?nant sur cette tache. Vu la quantit¨¦ limit¨¦e de donn¨¦es d'entra?nement disponible, il est judicieux d'adopter une logique de **fine-tuning**: partir d'un mod¨¨le pr¨¦-entra?ner et le fine-tuner sur un tache annexe.

### BERT

**BERT** est un mod¨¨le de NLP bas¨¦ sur des *Transformers*. Tr¨¨s populaire, il a servi de base ¨¤ de nombreux mod¨¨les par la suite. Utilis¨¦ comme couche pr¨¦-entra?n¨¦e, il permet d¡¯obtenir de bien meilleurs r¨¦sultats tout en limitant drastiquement le temps d¡¯entra?nement. Un d¨¦riv¨¦ de BERT, CamemBERT, a ¨¦t¨¦ entra?n¨¦ sur un corpus fran?ais. Il est donc plus adapt¨¦ ¨¤ la tache pr¨¦sent¨¦e ici.  
CamemBERT va nous servir a g¨¦n¨¦rer des *embeddings* pour chaque token constituant la phrase d'entr¨¦e. Ensuite, une t¨ºte de classification compl¨¨tera le mod¨¨le

### Les approches possibles

Dans le cadre de ce projet, il y a 2 grandes approches possibles:

- **Classification globale**: on effectue une classification au niveau du texte complet, sans uiliser l'information de **position** de l'entit¨¦
- **Classification avec focus sur l'entit¨¦**: similaire ¨¤ la classification globale, mais avec une couche suppl¨¦mentaire entre la couche d'embeddings et la t¨ºte de classification afin d'utiliser l'information de position -> L'id¨¦e est de donner ¨¤ la t¨ºte de classification uniquement les embeddings des tokens de l'entit¨¦ initiale.

### Notebook
Le [notebook d'exemple](https://www.kaggle.com/code/thomzoy/2-qualification-par-ml) va vous servir de trame. Vous pouvez le suivre dans l'ordre, il contient des informations, du code complet ou a compl¨¦ter ainsi que des pistes de travail.

>## **Proposition de travail**
>
>**- Se familiariser avec les bibliot¨¨ques de DL (datasets, pytorch, transformers, pytorch-lightning)**
>**- Suivre les instructions du notebook et impl¨¦menter les fonctionnalit¨¦s manquantes**
>**- Rendre compte des performances**

## Workflow:

Le workflow d¨¦crit dans le notebook est le suivant

- Cr¨¦ation d'un [Dataset](https://huggingface.co/docs/datasets/installation) ¨¤ partir des donn¨¦es d'entr¨¦e
- Cr¨¦ation d'un [DataModule](https://pytorch-lightning.readthedocs.io/en/stable/data/datamodule.html)
- Cr¨¦ation du mod¨¨le [PyTorch](https://pytorch.org/docs/stable/generated/torch.nn.Module.html),  wrapp¨¦ autour d'un [Module PyTorch-Lightning](https://pytorch-lightning.readthedocs.io/en/latest/common/lightning_module.html)
- Entra?nement ¨¤ l'aide d'un [Trainer](https://pytorch-lightning.readthedocs.io/en/latest/common/trainer.html)

Dans ce workflow, diff¨¦rentes libraires sont utilis¨¦es, entre autres:
- `datasets` : permet de g¨¦rer le pr¨¦processing des donn¨¦es
- `pytorch` : librairie de base permettant la cr¨¦ation de mod¨¨les de ML/DL
- `transformers` : contient les mod¨¨les de Transformers (notamment BERT) ainsi que leur tokenizer associ¨¦.
- `pytorch-lightning` : wrapper autour de `pytorch` permettant un d¨¦veloppement plus simple et rapide

N'h¨¦sitez pas ¨¤ parcourir leur documentation pour vous familiariser avec les fonctionnalit¨¦s utilis¨¦es dans le notebook
