6541,ml-olympiad-waterqualityprediction,"# Description

What is ML Olympiad?

An associated Kaggle Community Competitions hosted by Google ML communities, supported by Google Developers. (ML GDEs, TFUGs, or 3rd party ML communities host ML community competitions on Kaggle )


## About the competition
Considering that one of the most effective ways to stop the spread of COVID-19 and other pathogens is proper hand hygiene, these numbers are alarming. This simple behavior of handwashing not only protects us from contracting the disease but also stops transmission to other people.<br>

But did you know that 2.2 billion people globally do not have access to safe water at home? And a further 1.37 billion people globally lack handwashing facilities at home? Almost two billion people around the world rely on health care facilities that do not have basic water services.<br>

The world¡¯s water resources are under increasing threat from the impacts of climate change, population growth, and pollution. As the global population grows, a persistent challenge is how to access enough water to meet humanity¡¯s needs while also preserving the integrity of aquatic ecosystems. The Pacific Institute works on water resource issues around the globe, collaborating with stakeholders to ensure communities and nature have the water they need to thrive now and in the future.<br>

This makes water cleanliness and understanding water sanitation is a must in both rural as well as urban areas. To do that, estimation of the quality of water we consume on a day-to-day basis could be the very first step towards water sanitation. <br>

In this competition, your aim is to train a machine learning model based on the water quality data provided to you in the training file and further predict the quality estimation `result` for the test dataset.<br>

Here is a handy link to Kaggle's [competition documentation](https://www.kaggle.com/docs/competitions), which includes, among other things, instructions on [submitting predictions](https://www.kaggle.com/docs/competitions#making-a-submission).


## About the data set
The dataset provided in the `train.csv` consists of the following features:
- `id`: The unique ID for each row.
- `categoryA` - `categoryF`: 6 category columns with suffix A to F.
- `featureA` - `featureI`: 9 feature columns with suffix A to I.
- `compositionA` - `compositionJ`: 10 composition columns with suffix A to J.
- `unit`: The unit of measurement for the result values.
- `result`: The measure for water quality (target variable).

The datasets provided could be read using the `read_csv()` function in the pandas module.
```
# code to read the dataset
import pandas
df = pandas.read_csv(""train.csv"")
```

## Acknowledgements

We thank European Environment Agency and The World Bank for providing this dataset.

#MLOlympiad 
#AppliedML
----------------------
# Evaluation

Evaluation Metric
---
The evaluation metric for this competition is **Root Mean Squared Logarithmic Error (RMSLE)**.

The RMSLE is calculated as

<img src=""https://miro.medium.com/max/875/0*AUzyQ1rc6mpQVYfn"" width=300 alt=""RMSLE formula"">

where:
\\(n\\) is the total number of observations in the (public/private) data set,
\\(xi\\) is your prediction of target, and \\(yi\\) is the actual target for \\(i\\).
\\(\log(x)\\) is the natural logarithm of \\(x\\)


## Submission Format

For every `id` in the dataset, the submission file should contain two columns: `id` which is the unique id for each data point from the testing dataset, and `result` which is the water quality measurement factor. The second column should be a space-delimited string value.

The file should contain a header and have the following format:
```
id,result
36c451925f31fbc7d248db938b9028711491a42c4e2524b3ca22a921169f06b8,0.0
```","# Dataset Description


## About the dataset

This dataset consists of a `train.csv` file which contains the unique id `id` for each row, 6 `category` columns with suffix A to F, 9 `feature` columns with suffix A to I, 10 `composition` columns with suffix from A to J, `unit` column which is the unit of measurement of the result value and finally a `result` columns which is the measure of the quality of water as a numerical value to be predicted.

1. The `category` columns are various categorical features for a data point such as country of data collection, the site from which the data is collected, media of sample, etc.

2. The `feature` columns are the various demographic features that affect the pollution of water in a particular region such as population density, GDP, droughts in a region, literacy rate of students in a region, etc.

3. The `composition` columns are the compositions of various elements like paper, plastic wastes, cardboard, etc. in water.

4. The `unit` value is the unit of measurement in which the result value is measured.

5. The `result` value is a floating number that expresses the quality of water based on the various factors provided in the dataset.


### FAQ

**What files do I need?** 

You are required to use the `train.csv` file to read the tabular dataset provided and train your algorithms to predict the numerical value in `result` column in `train.csv` file.
 
**What am I predicting?**

You would be training your model based on the `train.csv` (training dataset) and then would be predicting the `result` value for each row in `test.csv` to create a submission. 

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the provided training data set.
*   **test.csv** - the provided testing data set.
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format.

## Columns

- `id`: The unique ID for each row.
- `categoryA` - `categoryF`: 6 category columns with suffix A to F.
- `featureA` - `featureI`: 9 feature columns with suffix A to I.
- `compositionA` - `compositionJ`: 10 composition columns with suffix A to J.
- `unit`: The unit of measurement for the result values.
- `result`: The measure for water quality (target variable).

The training dataset provided could be read using the `read_csv()` function in the pandas module.",False,True
6542,ualberta-and-wandb-blood-mnist,"# Description

## Competition details

The details of this competition will be shared in a separate resource.

The BloodMNIST dataset is part of the wider MedMNIST dataset.

MedMNIST is a large-scale MNIST-like collection of standardized biomedical images, including twelve 2D datasets and six 3D datasets. All the images are pre-processed to the size of 28x28 pixels and working with the data requires no prior domain knowledge about medicine, genetics, biology, etc.

## BloodMNIST details

The BloodMNIST is based on a dataset of individual normal cells, captured from individuals without infection, hematologic or oncologic disease and free of any pharmacologic treatment at the moment of blood collection. It contains a total of 17,092 images and is organized into 8 classes (8 different classes of blood cell).


## Spoiler

If you'd like *a* solution to the proposed classification task we've included one that was authored by our colleagues Justin and Ayush here: [Integrating Keras with Weights and Biases](https://wandb.ai/ayush-thakur/medmnist-bloodmnist/reports/Integrating-Keras-with-Weights-Biases--VmlldzoxNDg1MDQy?utm_source=webinar&utm_medium=online&utm_campaign=windsor).

----------------------
# Evaluation

We'll be evaluating your prediction based on accuracy.","# Dataset Description

Please grab the data via the Colab Notebook: https://colab.research.google.com/drive/17EMgzwIwO1UmGJaW4SZgWGn9NTbCkviB?usp=sharing",False,True
6543,stable-diffusion-image-to-prompts,"# Description

### Goal of the Competition

The goal of this competition is to reverse the typical direction of a generative text-to-image model: instead of generating an image from a text prompt, can you create a model which can predict the text prompt given a generated image? You will make predictions on a dataset containing a wide variety of `(prompt, image)` pairs generated by Stable Diffusion 2.0, in order to understand how reversible the latent relationship is.

### Context

The popularity of text-to-image models has spurned an entire new field of prompt engineering. Part art and part unsettled science, ML practitioners and researchers are rapidly grappling with understanding the relationships between prompts and the images they generate. Is adding ""4k"" to a prompt the best way to make it more photographic? Do small perturbations in prompts lead to highly divergent images? How does the order of prompt keywords impact the resulting generated scene? This competition tasks you with creating a model that can reliably invert the diffusion process that generated to a given image.

In order to calculate prompt similarity in a robust way&mdash;meaning that ""epic cat"" is scored as similar to ""majestic kitten"" in spite of character-level differences&mdash;you will submit embeddings of your predicted prompts. Whether you model the embeddings directly or first predict prompts and then convert to embeddings is up to you! Good luck, and may you create ""highly quality, sharp focus, intricate, detailed, in the style of unreal robust cross validation"" models herein.

> **This is a Code Competition. Refer to [Code Requirements](https://www.kaggle.com/c/stable-diffusion-image-to-prompts/overview/code-requirements) for details.**
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated using the mean [cosine similarity](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cosine_similarity) score between the predicted and actual prompt embedding vectors. The precise details of how embeddings are calculated for the ground truth prompts are found in [this notebook](https://www.kaggle.com/code/inversion/calculating-stable-diffusion-prompt-embeddings).

## Submission File
For each image in the test set, you must predict the prompt that was used to generate the image and convert the prompt into a 384-length embedding vector. Predictions should be flattened to rows of image (`imgId`) and embedding (`eId`) pairs (`imgId_eId`). The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    imgId_eId,val
    20057f34d_0,0.018848453
    20057f34d_1,0.030189732
    ....
    20057f34d_383,-0.007934112
    227ef0887_0,0.017384542
    etc.

----------------------
# Timeline

* **February 13, 2023** - Start Date.

* **${competition.ProhibitNewEntrantsExplicitDeadline}** - Entry Deadline. You must accept the competition rules before this date in order to compete.

* **${competition.TeamMergerExplicitDeadline}** - Team Merger Deadline. This is the last day participants may join or merge teams.

* **${competition.Deadline}** - Final Submission Deadline.

All deadlines are at 11:59 PM UTC on the corresponding day unless otherwise noted. The competition organizers reserve the right to update the contest timeline if they deem it necessary.
----------------------
# Prizes

* 1st Place - $12,000
* 2nd Place - $10,000
* 3rd Place - $10,000
* 4th Place - $10,000
* 5th Place - $8,000

As a condition to being awarded a Prize, a Prize winner must provide a detailed write-up on their solution in the competition forums within 14 days of the conclusion of the competition.
----------------------
# Code Requirements

<img src=""https://storage.googleapis.com/kaggle-media/competitions/general/Kerneler-white-desc2_transparent.png"" style=""float: right; width: 70px; padding: 10px"">

### This is a Code Competition

Submissions to this competition must be made through Notebooks. In order for the ""Submit"" button to be active after a commit, the following conditions must be met:

- CPU Notebook <= 9 hours run-time
- GPU Notebook <= 9 hours run-time
- Internet access disabled
- Freely & publicly available external data is allowed, including pre-trained models
- Submission file must be named `submission.csv`

Please see the [Code Competition FAQ](https://www.kaggle.com/docs/competitions#notebooks-only-FAQ) for more information on how to submit. And review the [code debugging doc](https://www.kaggle.com/code-competition-debugging) if you are encountering submission errors.","# Dataset Description

Your task for this challenge is to predict the prompts that were used to generate target images. Prompts for this challenge were generated using a variety of (non disclosed) methods, and range from fairly simple to fairly complex with multiple objects and modifiers. Images were generated from the prompts using Stable Diffusion 2.0 ([768-v-ema.ckpt](https://huggingface.co/stabilityai/stable-diffusion-2/blob/main/768-v-ema.ckpt)) and were generated with 50 steps at 768x768 px and then downsized to 512x512 for the competition dataset. (This [script](https://github.com/Stability-AI/stablediffusion/blob/main/scripts/txt2img.py) was used, with the majority of default parameters unchanged.)

## Files

*   **images/** - images generated from prompts; your task it to predict the prompt that was used to generate each image in this folder. The hidden re-run test folder contains approximately 16,000 images.
*   **prompts.csv** - the prompts that were used to create the samples in the `images/` folder. These are provided as illustrative examples only. It is up to each competitor to develop their own strategy of creating a training set of images, using pre-trained models, etc. Note that this file is not contained in the re-run test set, and thus referencing it in a Notebook submission will result in a failure. 
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format. The values found in this file are embeddings of the prompts in the `prompts.csv` file and thus can be used validate your embedding pipeline. This [notebook](https://www.kaggle.com/code/inversion/calculating-stable-diffusion-prompt-embeddings) demonstrates how to calculate embeddings.",True,True
6544,im-hard-to-spot,"# Description

## ?? Introduction

?? Welcome to the bonus problem of [DataFest@Integration2023](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/datafestintegration2023).
?? **Submission on [Whale Sound Challenge](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/datafestintegration2023) is NOT a prerequisite for evaluation of solution to bonus problem** but it will improve the final score.

?? The **Sundarbans mangrove forest** is a place of dense forests, winding rivers, and an incredible diversity of plant and animal life. This UNESCO world heritage site, spanning over 140,000 hectares, is located on the delta of the Ganges, Brahmaputra, and Meghna rivers, on the Bay of Bengal. The forest is so vast and lush that it is often referred to as the ""green lungs"" of the Indian subcontinent.

 ?? But there is one creature that calls the Sundarbans home that is more majestic and fearsome than any other: the **Royal Bengal Tiger**. These powerful predators are known for their distinctive orange coats, black stripes, and piercing eyes. They are apex predators who rule the forest with an iron paw. They are both feared and revered by the people who live in the Sundarbans.

?? But despite their fearsome reputation, the number of Royal Bengal Tigers in the Sundarbans has been declining rapidly in recent years. There are fewer than a few hundred tigers left in the Sundarbans. This is due to many reasons, from habitat destruction to poaching. The situation is dire, and conservation efforts are underway to reverse this trend.

?? One of the most important ways to protect the tigers is by understanding their population numbers. And this is where you come in. In this competition, you will have the opportunity to contribute to conservation efforts by **analyzing the distinctive features of tiger pugmarks**, and **using that data to estimate the number of tigers living in the Sundarbans**. Your work will help conservationists to understand the population of tigers and aid in creating strategies to protect them.

?? **[REGISTER HERE](https://forms.gle/fyXABdTqATa1n52L6)** **(Deadline: 30th January 2023 - Extended)**
?? **Participants are requested to go through the [Rules](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/datafestintegration2023/rules) carefully.**
?? **Participants are requested to go through each of the informative sections below properly.**

## ?? Tiger Pugmarks

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F6668389%2Fb6f5aaa690a2759a7969c875bdc76d5e%2Ftiger-pugmark-strip.jpg?generation=1674402074451723&alt=media)

?? One of the most challenging aspects of studying and protecting tigers in the wild is that they are elusive creatures that are often difficult to spot. Even when a tiger is sighted, it can be difficult to determine whether it is the same animal that was seen before or a different one. This makes it difficult to accurately estimate the number of tigers living in an area and to track the population over time.

?? To tackle this problem, forest rangers use a different method to identify individual tigers: pugmarks. Tigers leave behind unique footprints, called pugmarks, as they move through the forest. These footprints can be used to identify individual tigers based on the size, shape, and other characteristics of the print.

## ?? Tasks
The two most important questions you will have to pursue using the data provided are:

**?? Find a subset of useful features, if any, for the discrimination of tigers from available features that characterize each pugmark.**

**?? Estimate the tiger population from the census.**

?? Go through the [Evaluation Page](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/im-hard-to-spot/overview/evaluation) carefully.

?? It is a low-data setting and calls for your statistical and analytical skills to play a key role in **identifying the key features that distinguish different tigers from their pugmarks. Use these features to correctly and reliably estimate the total number of distinct tigers from the 1000+ tiger pugmarks obtained from different or same trails over the years.**

?? It not being a prediction problem **requires participants to make notebook submissions with explanations and code for their analysis.**
?? It's okay if you are not able to solve both parts, **we appreciate your efforts and encourage you to submit whatever work you did along with explanations and codes in the notebook. We will evaluate it.**

## ?? Ideas

Though the problem is very open-ended and requires you to try out different ways to explore the data. We present some of our ideas,

???? **IDEA 0**: Clustering is quite helpful in identifying similar or different data points.

## ??????? Acknowledgement

- It wouldn't have been possible to make available the dataset associated with this problem without the help of the Indian Statistical Institute Kolkata's former **Dean Prof. Debasis Sengupta**.
- **Prof. Debasis Sengupta has agreed to accept the best-performing students based on the final score calculated as the weighted average of this bonus problem and the previous [Whale Sound Classification problem](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/datafestintegration2023) for a project internship under his guidance**. 
----------------------
# Evaluation

This is an open-ended problem, right from the archives of works conducted by the Indian Statistical Institute Kolkata. This problem is meant to measure the analytical, creative and data skills of participants to their extreme. 

- **The participants are required to brainstorm to find useful distinctive features for identifying different tigers. These features can then be used to estimate the total tiger population, which is the ultimate goal.**
- **Even if you can partially solve the problem just put it neatly in a notebook for us to evaluate.**
- **The evaluation will be made based on the notebook submitted to this competition by a team of professors and industry experts at the Indian Statistical Institute.** So, make sure to make your notebook informative and beautiful with visualizations and more!
- **Submission on the [Whale Sound Classification Challenge](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/datafestintegration2023) is NOT a prerequisite for evaluation of your submission to this bonus problem**, but it will help boost your final score.

## ?? Submission Format
**You are NOT required to submit any `.csv` file in this competition.**

There are two ways to submit:
- **Send a mail to `datafest.integration@gmail.com` with a link to your notebook (Colab, Drive, etc)**. 

OR
- **Share your notebook through Kaggle with the Competition host (Rishi Dey Chowdhury) in the [Code](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/im-hard-to-spot/code) section of this competition**. **Do NOT  make the notebook publicly visible** until competition results are declared.
- Make sure to **mention your team name in the mail as well as at the start of your notebook** for whichever way you choose to submit.

## ?? Scoring

- A weighted average of scores given by the cohort of professors and industry experts on both submissions independently will be used to arrive at the final score given to the teams.
- This problem is more challenging compared to the [Whale Sound Classification](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/datafestintegration2023) and hence the weightage for this problem's score is strictly greater than that of the other challenge.
- In case no submission is made to the bonus problem, the score for the bonus problem will be considered to be 0.
- In case no submission is made to the Whale Sound Classification problem, the score for that will be considered to be 0.","# Dataset Description

The **dataset is collected by the Forest Rangers at Sundarban** and some part of **it was collected by Indian Statistical Institute Kolkata Team**. The pugmarks images were captured and later using the computerized technique explained in `Data-Description.pdf` they were converted to features.

The number of pugmarks:
- **Train.csv**: 64
- **Census.csv (Unlabelled)**: 1059

![Tiger Paw Features](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-forum-message-attachments/o/inbox%2F6668389%2F7d54aa542f70e37260432c3acaa91bab%2FScreenshot%202023-01-22%20at%208.47.30%20PM.png?generation=1674400689403825&alt=media)

## ?? Files

*   **Train.csv** - The pugmark features that are labelled whether they are distinct or not.
*   **Census.csv** - The unlabelled pugmark features from which the number of distinct tigers is to be estimated.
*   **Data-Description.pdf** - The description of the data, collection procedure, visualization and definition of the columns.
*   **Column-Description.csv** - The column names and their descriptions.

## ?? Submission

**Kindly go through the [Overview/Evaluation](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/im-hard-to-spot/overview/evaluation) section** carefully for **submission and task details.**

- **Submission on the [Whale Sound Classification Challenge](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/datafestintegration2023) is NOT a prerequisite for evaluation of submission to this bonus problem**, but it will help boost your final score.
- **You are NOT required to submit any `.csv` file in this competition.**

There are two ways to submit:
- **Send a mail to `datafest.integration@gmail.com` with a link to your notebook (Colab, Drive, etc)**. 

OR
- **Share your notebook through Kaggle with the Competition host ([Rishi Dey Chowdhury](https://www.kaggle.com/rishideychowdhury)) in the [Code](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/im-hard-to-spot/code) section of this bonus problem**. **Do NOT make the notebook publicly visible** until competition results are declared.
- Make sure to **mention your team name in the mail as well as at the start of your notebook** in whichever way you decide to submit.",False,True
6545,nwds-xstrikes,"# Description

# Nick Wan's Expected Strikes Community Competition 
Using MLB Statcast data from Baseball Savant, use a selected columns of data to predict whether a pitch will be thrown for a strike not. 

The competition lasts until March 19th at 11:59pm US Eastern Time and winners will be announced on stream on March 20th. 

Check out the stream here: 
https://twitch.tv/nickwan_datasci  

