Join the discord to talk about data science and sports analytics here:
https://discord.gg/GR5p4ZY","# Dataset Description

This dataset includes only pitches that were contacted by the batter -- specifically foul balls and balls in play. This does not include any bunts, foul bunts, or foul tips. Simply speaking, if the ball was hit with what seems like a ""full swing"" (aka a non-bunt swing) then that pitch is in this dataset. 

This dataset is from the 2022 MLB season.  

The data comes from MLB Statcast via Baseball Savant. Please reference the Baseball Savant Statcast Search CSV documentation for a data dictionary: 
https://baseballsavant.mlb.com/csv-docs  

Some columns are unique to this competition. These are: 
- uid: a unique ID for each pitch in the dataset 
- is_hr: whether a pitch was a home run 

Note: It is possible to cheat fairly simply. Please do not cheat! There are no prizes, cheating is pointless! 

# The benchmark sample solution 
The benchmark is created by Nick Wan using out-of-the-box (aka no model tuning) modeling techniques and all the features in the dataset with no feature engineering. Submissions that can beat this benchmark may have better feature engineering and/or better modeling techniques. Perfect solutions may be suspicious of cheating (since it is very easy to cheat in this competition).",False,True
6547,water-quality-prediction-rk,"# Description

## About the competition

A growing number of factors, including pollution, population expansion, and climate change, are putting the world's water resources in danger. How to obtain enough water to satisfy human demands while also conserving the integrity of aquatic ecosystems is a recurring concern as the world's population rises. The Pacific Institute works on global water resource issues in partnership with stakeholders to make sure that people and nature have access to the water they require to survive today and in the future.<br>

In this competition, your aim is to train a machine learning model based on the water quality data provided to you in the training file and further predict the quality estimation `result` for the test dataset.<br>

Here is a handy link to Kaggle's [competition documentation](https://www.kaggle.com/docs/competitions), which includes, among other things, instructions on [submitting predictions](https://www.kaggle.com/docs/competitions#making-a-submission).

## Info about the dataset
The dataset provided in the `train.csv` consists of the following features:
- `id`: The unique ID for each row.
- `categoryA` to `categoryF`: 6 category columns with suffix A to F.
- `featureA` to `featureI`: 9 feature columns with suffix A to I.
- `compositionA` to `compositionJ`: 10 composition columns with suffix A to J.
- `unit`: The unit of measurement for the result values.
- `result`: The measure for water quality (target variable).

## Acknowledgements

We thank European Environment Agency and The World Bank for providing this dataset.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Evaluation Metric
---
The evaluation metric for this competition is **Root Mean Squared Logarithmic Error (RMSLE)**.

The RMSLE is calculated as

<img src=""https://miro.medium.com/max/875/0*AUzyQ1rc6mpQVYfn"" width=300 alt=""RMSLE formula"">

where:
\\(n\\) is the total number of observations in the (public/private) data set,
\\(xi\\) is your prediction of target, and \\(yi\\) is the actual target for \\(i\\).
\\(\log(x)\\) is the natural logarithm of \\(x\\)


## Submission Format

For every `id` in the dataset, the submission file should contain two columns: `id` which is the unique id for each data point from the testing dataset, and `result` which is the water quality measurement factor. The second column should be a space-delimited string value.

The file should contain a header and have the following format:
```
id,result
36c451925f31fbc7d248db938b9028711491a42c4e2524b3ca22a921169f06b8,0.0
```","# Dataset Description


## About the dataset

This dataset consists of a `train.csv` file which contains the unique id `id` for each row, 6 `category` columns with suffix A to F, 9 `feature` columns with suffix A to I, 10 `composition` columns with suffix from A to J, `unit` column which is the unit of measurement of the result value and finally a `result` columns which is the measure of the quality of water as a numerical value to be predicted.

1. The `category` columns are various categorical features for a data point such as country of data collection, the site from which the data is collected, media of sample, etc.

2. The `feature` columns are the various demographic features that affect the pollution of water in a particular region such as population density, GDP, droughts in a region, literacy rate of students in a region, etc.

3. The `composition` columns are the compositions of various elements like paper, plastic wastes, cardboard, etc. in water.

4. The `unit` value is the unit of measurement in which the result value is measured.

5. The `result` value is a floating number that expresses the quality of water based on the various factors provided in the dataset.


### FAQ

**What files do I need?** 

You are required to use the `train.csv` file to read the tabular dataset provided and train your algorithms to predict the numerical value in `result` column in `train.csv` file.
 
**What am I predicting?**

You would be training your model based on the `train.csv` (training dataset) and then would be predicting the `result` value for each row in `test.csv` to create a submission. 

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the provided training data set.
*   **test.csv** - the provided testing data set.
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format.

## Columns

- `id`: The unique ID for each row.
- `categoryA` - `categoryF`: 6 category columns with suffix A to F.
- `featureA` - `featureI`: 9 feature columns with suffix A to I.
- `compositionA` - `compositionJ`: 10 composition columns with suffix A to J.
- `unit`: The unit of measurement for the result values.
- `result`: The measure for water quality (target variable).

The training dataset provided could be read using the `read_csv()` function in the pandas module.",False,True
6548,indabaxsenegal2023-wolof-language-keyword-spotting,"# Description

> Bienvenue au hackathon IndabaX S¨¦n¨¦gal 2023 ??

# Qu'est-ce que les IndabaX ?

Les **IndabaX** sont des ¨¦v¨¦nements qui se d¨¦roulent chaque ann¨¦e dans diff¨¦rentes villes africaines. Il s'agit d'un moyen d'exp¨¦rimenter les fa?ons dont nous pouvons renforcer notre communaut¨¦ d'apprentissage automatique et de permettre ¨¤ un plus grand nombre de personnes de contribuer ¨¤ la conversation sur l'apprentissage automatique et l'intelligence artificielle sur notre continent. Elles ont pour but de renforcer la communaut¨¦ des praticiens, chercheurs, ¨¦tudiants, professionnels et passionn¨¦s du domaine de l¡¯apprentissage automatique (Machine Learning ¨C ML), de l¡¯apprentissage profond (Deep Learning ¨C DL) et de la Data Science. Elles cherchent en outre ¨¤ cr¨¦er un ¨¦cosyst¨¨me entre les acteurs (secteur priv¨¦, ¨¦coles, universit¨¦s et secteur public) afin d¡¯utiliser l¡¯intelligence artificielle (IA) comme un levier dans le d¨¦veloppement et la croissance de nos pays.

![Deep Learning Indaba logo](https://indabax.co.za/images/partners/deeplearningindaba_com.png)

Un Deep Learning IndabaX est un [Indaba](https://deeplearningindaba.com/2023/indabax/) organis¨¦ localement qui aide ¨¤ diffuser les connaissances et ¨¤ renforcer les capacit¨¦s en mati¨¨re d'apprentissage automatique et d'intelligence artificielle dans les diff¨¦rents pays d'Afrique. L'¨¦dition 2023 du IndabaX S¨¦n¨¦gal aura lieu les **25-26 Octobre 2023** ¨¤ [l'¨¦cole Sup¨¦rieure Polytechnique de Dakar](https://esp.sn/) qui est un des co-organisateurs aux c?t¨¦s de [WiMLDS Dakar](https://www.meetup.com/Dakar-Women-in-Machine-Learning-and-Data-Science/) et [GalsenAI](https://galsen.ai/).

# Objectif du challenge
L'objectif de cette comp¨¦tition est de **construire un mod¨¨le de classification multi-classes capable de pr¨¦dire avec pr¨¦cision l'¨¦tiquette (mots-cl¨¦s) correspondant ¨¤ l'audio d'entr¨¦e**.  
> Tous les audios utilis¨¦s dans ce concours sont en [langue wolof](https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wolof_(langue)), principalement parl¨¦e au S¨¦n¨¦gal en Afrique de l'Ouest.

Votre travail contribuera ainsi ¨¤ am¨¦liorer l'inclusion dans l'acc¨¨s aux services num¨¦riques et permettra aux startups de d¨¦velopper des services ¨¤ valeur ajout¨¦e.

## Contexte
[GalsenAI](https://galsen.ai/) est la premi¨¨re communaut¨¦ IA initi¨¦e par des s¨¦n¨¦galais passionn¨¦s de l'intelligence artificielle, la science des donn¨¦es et de l'IoT avec comme ambition de construire et de populariser un ¨¦cosyst¨¨me d'IA performant et productif pour l'Afrique de l'Ouest. Pour ce faire, GalsenAI s¡¯att¨¨le ¨¤ mettre en place une plateforme (hub) d'¨¦changes intellectuelles, de vulgarisation, d'apprentissage et de recherche & d¨¦veloppement sur tout sujet tournant autour de l'IA (Intelligence Artificielle), du Big Data ou encore de l'IoT (Internet of Things).

![GalsenAI logo](https://secure.meetupstatic.com/photos/event/9/9/e/d/600_481959405.jpeg)

GalsenAI a initi¨¦ le [projet Waxal](https://k4all.org/project/keyword-spotting-with-african-languages/) qui a permis d'¨¦tendre le jeu de donn¨¦es de keyword spotting *Speech commands* ([Warden 2018](https://arxiv.org/abs/1804.03209)) aux les langues africaines **et en particulier les langues s¨¦n¨¦galaises**. **Le Rep¨¦rage de Mots-cl¨¦s ou Keyword Spotting** se r¨¦f¨¨re ¨¤ la tache consistant ¨¤ apprendre ¨¤ **d¨¦tecter des mots-cl¨¦s parl¨¦s**. Il s'agit d'une technologie qui s'interface avec tous les assistants virtuels modernes actuellement sur le march¨¦ et bas¨¦s sur la voix tels que [Siri d'Apple](https://www.apple.com/fr/siri/), [Alexa d'Amazon](https://developer.amazon.com/fr-FR/alexa) ou encore [Google Assistant](https://assistant.google.com/).

Cette initiative favorisera l'inclusion et contribuera ¨¤ r¨¦duire la fracture num¨¦rique en permettant ¨¤ la majorit¨¦ des populations non alphab¨¦tis¨¦es de b¨¦n¨¦ficier des services num¨¦riques.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Les m¨¦triques standard utilis¨¦es pour le keyword spotting sont g¨¦n¨¦ralement [le recall, la precision et le F1 score](https://www.evidentlyai.com/classification-metrics/accuracy-precision-recall).

Pour ce challenge, les soumissions seront ¨¦valu¨¦es sur la base du [score F1 micro] (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/F-score) entre les r¨¦ponses pr¨¦dites et les r¨¦ponses attendues. Le F1 est calcul¨¦ comme suit : 

![Formule F1](https://inside-machinelearning.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/09/F1-Score.png) 

O¨´ : 
![formules de pr¨¦cision et de rappel](https://miro.medium.com/max/888/1*7J08ekAwupLBegeUI8muHA.png) 

La mesure F1 pond¨¨re ainsi le **recall** et le **precision** de mani¨¨re ¨¦gale, et **un bon algorithme de recherche maximisera simultan¨¦ment ces deux m¨¦triques**. Par cons¨¦quent, une performance mod¨¦r¨¦ment bonne dans les deux cas sera pr¨¦f¨¦r¨¦e ¨¤ une performance extr¨ºmement bonne dans l'un mais m¨¦diocre dans l'autre.


---


## Fichier de soumission
Le fichier de soumission se compose d'un **id** et d'un **keyword** (le mot-cl¨¦ ¨¤ faire pr¨¦dire par votre mod¨¨le). Veillez ¨¤ bien respecter **exactement** les noms de ces colonnes dans votre fichier de soumission.

NB: **Remarquez le _.mp3_ ¨¤ la fin de chaque id**.

    id, keyword
    9e43923d-6c6a-4b70-8171-93b9cb3c520b.mp3, N?aar
