6551,rit-business-analytics-competition-spring-2023,"# Description

The Saunders College of Business (SCB) at Rochester Institute of Technology (RIT) invites students to participate in its 3rd Annual Business Analytic Competition 2023. This Kaggle competition is part of this year challenge. The students can check out the official challenge website for details of completely submitting their solutions.

Competition website: https://www.rit.edu/business/business-analytics-competition

#Eligibility Criteria
- Students can form teams of up to five students to participate in the competition. Individual participation is welcome.
- The competition is open to both undergraduate and graduate students from any university. Ph.D. students are NOT eligible for this competition.
- Participating students must currently enroll in an academic program at a university/college.

#Forming Team
Students are encouraged to use the [Discussion board](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/rit-business-analytics-competition-spring-2023/discussion/382175) to form teams
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is [F1 score] (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/F-score). The F1 score measures accuracy between the predicted and the observed targets, and commonly used in classification for imbalanced data.

<h1>Submission Format</h1>
Submission files should contain two columns: `review_ID` and `fake_review`. The `fake_review` column contains the classification result for a given review (i.e., whether the review is fake or not)


The file should contain a header and have the following format:
```
review_ID,fake_review
427196,0
205467,0
202189,1
56726,1
```","# Dataset Description

In this competition you will be predicting whether a review will likely be fake or not.    

# File descriptions
- train_reviews.csv: contains the reviews posted on RIT Commerce (training dataset)
- test_reviews.csv: contains the reviews that need to be classified (testing dataset)
- price_data.csv: contains the price information of the products that appear in train_reviews.csv file
- rank_data.csv: contains the sales rank information of the products that appear in train_reviews.csv file
- kaggle-sample.csv - a sample submission in the correct format
- README.txt - a data dictionary of all columns",False,True
6552,towardsai-x-whitebox-startup-challenge,"# Description

Can we develop algorithms to play the game of financial markets that are determined by seemingly random factors? This question has been at the heart of quantitative trading, technical analysis, and has resulted in the two most successful hedge funds in the word, Bridgewater and Renaissance Technologies, and is the founding challenge for our new startup, Whitebox, to solve.

This competition will allow you to submit your best attempt at trading the equities markets using a quantitative strategy. The winner(s) of the competition will be selected using the final score on the private leaderboard and a review by the panel of anonymous judges.

# **What Is The Challenge?**
The goal of this competition is to develop a general stock trading strategy capable of performing well on over 212 stocks and ETFs. Note, we have chosen the words performing well carefully. This does not mean making the most profit or having the best win percentage, it means creating a strong, robust, and efficient strategy. You must generate an original quantitative strategy (which can be inspired by existing strategies) and measure your strategy's performance backtesting across each of our 212 stocks and ETF.

To submit your strategy, produce the Sharpe ratio using a backtesting engine (i.e. backtrader) or calculate the ratio yourself. Then record the Sharpe ratios of your strategy's performance on each symbol. We will then take the RMSE (Root Mean Squared Error) of your responses to generate your score. Afterwards, we will analyze the top performers with a set of anonymous judges.

# **Tutorial**
Here I have created an example submission explaining how to use backtesting libraries and how to develop a MACD strategy from scratch. The notebook is executable on the cloud via Google Colab. Please feel free to reach out to me, Arav Kumar, with any other questions!
[Stock Trading with Python Tutorial - Colab Notebook](https://colab.research.google.com/drive/1HZdEXSKqyvqDI8ALCf7wZBHTAUHrtFR_?usp=sharing)
If you need help learning how to submit, please refer to the guide linked below:
[Submission Guide](https://www.kaggle.com/general/166755)

# **Learning Resources**
**I'm a beginner to quantitative finance. Where should I start learning?**
*Videos:*
[What is algo-trading and how do I get started? (Playlist)](https://youtube.com/playlist?list=PLqpCwow11-Oq_ENXbaHso3WqUjRq_xhRG)
[Full course for algorithmic trading using Python (4.5hours)](https://youtu.be/xfzGZB4HhEE)
*Books:*
Machine Trading: Deploying Computer Algorithms to Conquer the Markets by Ernest P. Chan
Building Winning Algorithmic Trading Systems by Kevin J. Davey
The Man Who Solved the Market. How Jim Simons Launched the Quant Revolution
*MIT Opencourseware Online Courses:*
[Financial Theory](https://youtube.com/playlist?list=PLUl4u3cNGP63B2lDhyKOsImI7FjCf6eDW)
[Financial Mathematics: Stochastic and Ito Calculus, Portfolio Management, and more!](https://youtube.com/playlist?list=PLCRPN3Z81LCIZ7543AvRjWfzSC15K7l-X)

# **FAQs**
**What language should I write in?**
Preferably python, however C++, Rust, and other highly efficient languages are acceptable in place of the slower Python.
**Do I have to use machine learning/artificial intelligence?**
No. It is not required.
**How will the application of funding work?**
See Prize section below.
**Do we need to do any forms of risk management to match the risk profiles of any of Whitebox's clients?**
No. For the sake of this competition, you are given free reign to test your strategy with any risk profile. The judges will account for this when judging your strategy if you do decide to manage risk.
**Is there a limit for maximum drawdown?**
There is no limit for the max drawdown, however, our panel of judges will consider this as a factor in evaluating the submission.
**How should we calculate results?**
The backtester we recommend is known as backtrader. You will use this or any other backtesting library to calculate your sharpe ratio, sortino ratio, and profitability while trading. If utilizing an existing engine is too difficult or not compatible with your strategy, feel free to develop your own. Documentation for the recommended library, Backtrader, can be found at https://www.backtrader.com/
**Is there a minimum age for competing?**
While there is a minimum age to win the prize (dependent on location), there is no minimum age to compete! Anyone is welcome to compete and give it their all.
**What do I do if I have more questions?**
Show up to the live Q&A on Saturday, January 28th, 6PM EST! If you can't make it, feel free to message Arav on the server (Discord Tag @A Ravioli#7085) or leave a message on the discussion board.

# **Prize**
The winner(s) of this competition will receive an offer to join the Whitebox team, consult quantitatively for hedge funds, and thus have their strategies funded. The winners will be selected via the private leaderboard and a secondary panel of anonymous judges.

# **Mission**
At Whitebox, our mission is to use the world of finance and artificial intelligence to create a funded platform for AGI development. We hope to leverage financial markets to allow our machine learning engineers to research and explore all that which they dream of. Then, we support their projects completely and absolutely.

To do this, we have started a quantitative consulting agency that uses AI to improve modern hedge funds, Whitebox. By trading equities and derivatives markets, we hope to find value and gain a quantifiable understanding of the world around us.

# **What is a Code Competition?**
Participants in Code Competitions will submit their code via Kaggle Kernels. All kernels are private by default in Code Competitions. You can build your models in Kernels by running them on a training set and, once you're ready to submit your code, your model's performance will be evaluated against the test set and your score and public leaderboard position revealed. As with our traditional competitions, we still maintain a private leaderboard test set, which your code is also evaluated against for final scoring, but is not revealed until the competition closes.

Please report any issues you find in the forums and we'll do our best to respond.

# **Who owns my code?**
You do. Even though you are submitting code, the intellectual property exchange here works similarly to a standard prediction competition, whereby prize winners have the option to grant a non-exclusive license in exchange for a prize. There is a new addition to the terms for Code Competitions: Kaggle and the competition host reserve a right to review submissions ""for purposes related to evaluation and scoring in this Competition, including but not limited to the assessment of potential cheating behavior."" Please refer to the official competition rules for full details. If you accept the prize of a position on the Whitebox team, the conditions for intellectual property ownership will change moving forward.

# **Getting Started**
Review the data page for details about the data and the evaluation metric. You may download the train set for local training.
Take a look at the tutorial covering the new code submission process under the submission instructions tab. You'll find step-by-step instructions, some helpful pointers, plus details on environment constraints.
Get feedback on your benchmark code and share exploratory analyses with the community by making any of your kernels public.
Improve your score!
Note: there is no cost of entry for participation.

Here is a handy link to Kaggle's [competition documentation](https://www.kaggle.com/docs/competitions), which includes, among other things, instructions on [submitting predictions](https://www.kaggle.com/docs/competitions#making-a-submission).

# **Acknowledgements**
Thanks are given to Louis Bouchard and the Towards AI community for their support.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions will be evaluated on the RMSE (Root Mean Squared Error) between the recorded Sharpe ratio and an optimal preset ratio. The RMSE can be calculated as:

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F7490124%2F421a2b4a65eb02de14fc6195f2c502ca%2Frmse%20eq.png?generation=1675225333988505&alt=media)

where \\(y\\) is the target value, \\(\hat{y}\\) is the predicted value, and \\(n\\) is the number of predictions. Do not be discouraged by high RMSE values; in finance, given the high ratio of signal-to-noise, even a large RMSE can deliver meaningful value!

Negative RMSE values are clipped, i.e. the score you see will be \\(max(0,RMSE)\\). Additionally, if a submission errors for any reason, you will receive a simple ""Error"" status.

After viewing the calculated performance, the judges will review every submission anonymously for it's algorithmic merit. They will look at the creativity of the submission, the efficiency of the code written, the feature engineering completed, and the type of strategy used (pair trading, technical indicators, etc.). After scoring on many different metrics, winner(s) will be picked and notified!","# Dataset Description

This dataset contains the historical stock data of 15 minute bars on roughly 100 Stocks and 100 ETFS. Only the close prices of each stock and ETF. Time is represented by the 'timestamp' feature and the price is represented by the 'close' feature. Participants are welcome to use alternative sources of data, but they must leave comments at the beginning of their submission clarifying the type and source of alternative data.

Data is saved and accessed as a .csv file in the Kernels environment. The training set file is available for download and offline modeling outside of Kernels. The test set is not available for download.",False,True
6553,asl-signs,"# Description

## Goal of the Competition

The goal of this competition is to classify isolated American Sign Language (ASL) signs. You will create a [TensorFlow Lite](https://www.tensorflow.org/lite) model trained on labeled landmark data extracted using the [MediaPipe Holistic Solution](https://google.github.io/mediapipe/solutions/holistic.html).
 
Your work may improve the ability of PopSign* to help relatives of deaf children learn basic signs and communicate better with their loved ones.^

[<img src=""https://storage.googleapis.com/kaggle-media/competitions/Google-ASL/tf_yt.png"" alt=""video"" width=""720"" >](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jcyWo_1Q_jY)

## Context
#### Every day, 33 babies are born with permanent hearing loss in the U.S.
Around 90% of which are born to hearing parents many of which may not know American Sign Language. ([kdhe.ks.gov](https://www.kdhe.ks.gov/DocumentCenter/View/8482/Facts-about-Pediatric-Hearing-Loss-PDF), [deafchildren.org](https://deafchildren.org/2019/02/why-deaf-children-need-asl/)) Without sign language, deaf babies are at risk of Language Deprivation Syndrome. This syndrome is characterized by a lack of access to naturally occurring language acquisition during their critical language-learning years. It can cause serious impacts on different aspects of their lives, such as relationships, education, and employment.
#### Learning sign language is challenging.
Learning American Sign Language is as difficult for English speakers as learning Japanese. ([jstor.org](https://www.jstor.org/stable/44392557)) It takes time and resources, which many parents don't have. They want to learn sign language, but it's hard when they are working long hours just to make ends meet. And even if they find the time and money for classes, the classes are often far away. 
#### Games can help.
PopSign is a smartphone game app that makes learning American Sign Language fun, interactive, and accessible. Players match videos of ASL signs with bubbles containing written English words to pop them.
PopSign is designed to help parents with deaf children learn ASL, but it's open to anyone who wants to learn sign language vocabulary. By adding a sign language recognizer from this competition, PopSign players will be able to sign the type of bubble they want to shoot, providing the player with the opportunity to practice the sign themselves instead of just watching videos of other people signing.
#### You can help connect deaf children and their parents.
By training a sign language recognizer for PopSign, you can help make the game more interactive and improve the learning and confidence of players who want to learn sign language to communicate with their loved ones.
#### Why TensorFlow Lite
To allow the ML model to run on device in an attempt to limit latency inside the game, PopSign doesn¡¯t send user videos to the cloud. Therefore, all inference must be done on the phone itself. PopSign is building its recognition pipeline on top of TensorFlow Lite, which runs on both Android and iOS. In order for the competition models to integrate seamlessly with PopSign, we are asking our competitors to submit their entries in the form of TensorFlow Lite models.

#### Special thanks to our partners
We¡¯d like to thank the Georgia Institute of Technology, the National Technical Institute for the Deaf at Rochester Institute of Technology, and Deaf Professional Arts Network for their work to create the dataset, the PopSign game, and overall competition preparation.

> **This is a Code Competition. Refer to [Code Requirements](https://kaggle.com/competitions/asl-signs/overview/code-requirements) for details.**

**PopSign is an app developed by the Georgia Institute of Technology and the National Technical Institute for the Deaf at Rochester Institute of Technology. The app is available in beta on Android and iOS.*
^*We cannot guarantee the competition will benefit the competitors or the disabled community directly.*

----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this contest is simple classification accuracy.


## Submission Process

In this competition you will be submitting a TensorFlow Lite model file. The model must take one or more landmark frames as an input and return a float vector (the predicted probabilities of each sign class) as the output. Your model must be packaged into a `submission.zip` file and compatible with the [TensorFlow Lite Runtime v2.9.1](https://www.tensorflow.org/lite/guide/python#run_an_inference_using_tflite_runtime). You are welcome to train your model using the framework of your choice, as long as you convert the model checkpoint into the tflite format prior to submission.

Your model must also perform inference with less than 100 milliseconds of latency per video on average and use less than 40 MB of storage space. Expect to see approximately 40,000 videos in the test set. We allow an additional 10 minute buffer for loading the data and miscellaneous overhead.


Each video is loaded with the following function:

```
ROWS_PER_FRAME = 543  # number of landmarks per frame

def load_relevant_data_subset(pq_path):
    data_columns = ['x', 'y', 'z']
    data = pd.read_parquet(pq_path, columns=data_columns)
    n_frames = int(len(data) / ROWS_PER_FRAME)
    data = data.values.reshape(n_frames, ROWS_PER_FRAME, len(data_columns))
    return data.astype(np.float32)
```

Inference is performed (roughly) as follows, ignoring details like how we manage multiple videos:
```
import tflite_runtime.interpreter as tflite
interpreter = tflite.Interpreter(model_path)

found_signatures = list(interpreter.get_signature_list().keys())

if REQUIRED_SIGNATURE not in found_signatures:
    raise KernelEvalException('Required input signature not found.')

prediction_fn = interpreter.get_signature_runner(""serving_default"")
output = prediction_fn(inputs=frames)
sign = np.argmax(output[""outputs""])
```

