# Prizes

To be declared soon!",# Dataset Description,False,True
6573,forest-fire-prediction,"# Description

# Forest Fire Prediction Challenge
With rising global temperatures, forest fires are becoming more common and larger and present an increasing threat to the environment. If we can predict them in advance we will be able to prevent them and thus save lives and the environment.
## Problem Statement:
You will be given various parameters such as relative humidity, temperature, wind speed, etc. and your task will be to develop a program to predict whether or not there will be a forest fire.

## Dataset:
You are provided two splits of data - train, and test. Navigate to the Data Section for more details regarding these.

## Duration:
The coding challenge will be open to submissions from the 7th to the 19th of February (EOD).

## Submission:
You will have to submit the solution in the form of a .csv file, with the predicted values for each test case (using the ID column to denote the case). A sample submission file (sample_submission.csv) has been provided along with the dataset, you may simply fill the predictions column in the same before uploading it as the solution. But you are not done yet!

You also need to submit the link to your Colab Notebook (https://colab.research.google.com/) containing the code you used for generating the submission file. Kindly set view access to 'Everyone' while generating the shareable link.

These details must be furnished at: https://forms.gle/E6uHUaU8Q86XWVaR7
Kindly note that submitting the solution description and Colab Notebook on the aforementioned Google Form is mandatory, and the code submitted must be able to replicate your final submission file.

**Important:** In case you are using Kaggle notebooks, then strictly keep your notebook private. Sharing your Kaggle notebook publicly will make you liable for disqualification. Also, even if you are using Kaggle notebooks for writing your code, you need to first import it to Google Colaboratory and then share the link of the same, as mentioned above.

In case you are new to Kaggle, here is a handy link to Kaggle's [competition documentation](https://www.kaggle.com/docs/competitions), which includes, among other things, instructions on [submitting predictions](https://www.kaggle.com/docs/competitions#making-a-submission).

## Acknowledgements:
This challenge was created with the help of Yash Vashistha and Rupal Shah.
----------------------
# Evaluation

**The evaluation procedure for this challenge is as follows:**

1.  Submit your prediction file in the format as described in the Data section. 
2. Submit your solution approach and Colab notebook [here](https://forms.gle/E8jkusGWFKcvMpAM7) as mentioned in the Description section. 
3. Hang on tight until the results come out.

We will evaluate participants' performances based on the [Root Mean Squared Error or RMSE](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Root-mean-square_deviation) on the Test Set, as well as the solution description and Colab notebooks submitted.

An RMSE value of 0 denotes 100% correct predictions.","# Dataset Description

The data divides for training and testing are named train.csv and test.csv, respectively. There are **200** instances in the training split and **50** examples in the testing split. The complete descriptions for these splits are given below.

## train.csv (200 examples)
The training set consists of the following feature columns:

1. Day (Data type - INT)
2. Month (Data type - INT)
3. Year (Data type - INT)
4. Temperature (Data type - INT)
5. RH (Data type - INT)
6. Ws (Data type - INT)
7.  Rain (Data type - FLOAT)
8. FFMC (Data type - FLOAT)
9. DMC (Data type - FLOAT)
10. DC (Data type - FLOAT)
11. ISI (Data type - FLOAT)
12. BUI (Data type - FLOAT)
13. FWI (Data type - FLOAT)


The column - ""**Classes (Data type - string)**"" is the label column. 

## test.csv (50 examples)
The test split includes all of the training dataset's feature columns. Your task is to forecast the labels (**Classes** column) for each of these cases corresponding to their respective ID and submit the data in the same style as **sample_submission.csv**.

**Important** - Please keep in mind that the numbers in sample_submission.csv are only placeholders; you must build your own submission file for the whole test set.",False,True
6574,twitter-tokenization,"# Description

Welcome to the very first competition in the SYP23! ?? Your task is to come up with a tokeniser for provided data. ?? 

Next steps:
- Get familiar with the [data](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/twitter-tokenization/data)
- Get familiar with the [evaluation](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/twitter-tokenization/overview/evaluation) criteria
- Build a tokenizer, apply it to the provided `X_test.csv` and finally submit your solution!

If you have any problem/questions, please contact me (Ludek) on the Slack.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Your submission will be scored using [Levenshtein distance](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Levenshtein_distance) between each line. The final score is then the mean of all distances. Perfect score is 0, i.e., there are no differences. Consequently, the higher the final score, the worse.

??  Important to note:
- You can submit up to 20 solutions per day (maximum Kaggle limit)
- You will only see how well your tokeniser did on 20 % of the dataset (the public score). Once the competition is done, the team with the best score for the remaining 80 % will win. (this is to prevent you from overfitting on the test data) 

## Submission Format
You should submit a csv file with the header `Id,Predicted`. `Id` is already given in the `y_test.csv`. The `Predicted` column should be put in **double quotes** within which you should put your tokenized text split by **space**. You can look at `y_predicted_example.csv` which is example solution for the training dataset.

## Evaluation script
To make your life easier, I have provided an evaluation script (see in data section) that you can use to evaluate how well your tokeniser does on the training data.","# Dataset Description

Based on the provided `X_train.csv` and `y_train.csv` build a tokenizer. Then apply the tokeniser on the `X_test.csv`. See the evaluation section for details on the format of submission.",False,True
