6579,portland-housing-age-prediction-nwvs-s00e01,"# Description

## In this episode of Nick Wan Versus (NWVS),
Nick takes on all challengers in a live data science competition to predict the build year of houses based upon their features. This competition features a never-before-seen dataset and just two hours to code a solution to the prediction challenge. The evaluation metric for submissions is RMSE.

Episode 1: Live on Twitch Tuesday January 31 at 8PM ET: https://www.twitch.tv/nickwan_datasci. Competition begins at 9PM ET.

## How to participate

1. Log in or create a Kaggle account
2. Accept the competition's rules
3. Download the data (click on the ""Data"" tab) or create a notebook directly on Kaggle (click on the ""Code"" tab)
4. Train a model on `train.csv` predicting on `test.csv`
5. Generate a `submission.csv` file in the same format as the `sample_submission.csv`
6. Upload your `submission.csv` file on ""My Submissions""

You get 20 SUBMISSIONS during the episode. They must be submitted before the end of the two-hour live coding is up. You can hand select a submission from the ""My Submissions"" tab, otherwise the submission with the best public score (on 2% of the test data) will be taken and scored on the private leaderboard (on 98% of the test data).
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is [RMSE](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Root-mean-square_deviation).

The public leaderboard is calculated with only 2% of the test set (83 houses) and may be volatile.
The private leaderboard will rank submissions on the remaining 98% of the test set.

## Submission Format

For every house in the test.csv dataset, submission files should contain two columns: `houseID` and `yearBuilt`. 

The file should contain a header and have the following format:
```
houseID,yearBuilt
20001,1976
20002,2012
20003,1962
etc.
```","# Dataset Description

The datasets provided are generated from recent house sales around Portland, Oregon. The goal is to use features of houses to predict the years in which they were built. Rows represent individual houses, and columns represent house features.

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

## Columns

*   `houseID` - unique house identifier
*   `yearBuilt`?- year house was built
*   `lastSoldPrice`?- price of last sale
*   `latitude`?- latitude of house location
*   `longitude`?- longitude of house location
*   `homeType`?- home type classification
*   `architecturalStyle`?- architectural style classification
*   `bathrooms`?- number of bathrooms
*   `bedrooms`?- number of bedrooms
*   `livingArea`?- size of living area (sq. ft.)
*   `lotSize`?- size of the associated lot (sq. ft.)
*   `stories`?- number of stories (levels)
*   `constructionMaterials`?- materials used in construction
*   `hasAttachedGarage`?- 1 = attached garage, 0 = no attached garage
*   `hasFireplace`?- 1 = fireplace, 0 = no fireplace
*   `hasView`?- 1 = scenic view, 0 = no view",False,True
