*   **train.csv** - the training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format",False,True
6582,ml-olympiad-multilingual-spell-correction,"# Description

# ML Olympiad - Multilingual Spell Correction

[ML Olympiad](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions?hostSegmentIdFilter=10&searchQuery=ml+olympiad) is an associated Kaggle Community Competition hosted by Google ML communities, supported by Google Developers.

## Background

As NLP systems and conversational agents become more mainstream, developers and researchers are increasingly relying on texts from speech-to-text solutions, web crawlers, automated labelers, etc. However, these data sources usually contain spelling mistakes, wrong or missing punctuation placement, and missing words, tokens, or letters.

## Competition Goal

With this competition, we aim to create tools to help clean language datasets in the future. We have prepared a dataset with Wikipedia sentences in a diverse set of European languages. We have also augmented these sentences to make them noisy - with spelling mistakes, missing punctuation, missing letters, etc. Your goal is to use language models and/or other techniques to compare the sentence pairs and reconstruct the original sentences from the augmented ones.

## Languages

The main objective of the competition is to challenge you to work with unfamiliar languages. We have constructed our dataset to cover representatives from the language families used across Europe.
- Germanic - English, German;
- Romance - French;
- Slavic - Bulgarian;
- Turkic - Turkish;

## Solution Details

You can use a single multilingual solution to solve the challenge or employ multiple models/techniques for the separate languages. Per-word dictionary lookup is also an option, but we advise you to use techniques that take into account the sentence context as well. Preferable but not required framework for development is Tensorflow.
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is [Levenshtein Mean](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Levenshtein_distance).

## Submission Format

The file should contain a header and have the following format:

```
""Id"",""Predicted""
""bc996049-7f24-47ca-9754-9a596a9e7452"",""Sentence to be placed here""
```","# Dataset Description

Our dataset contains 550000 sentences in 5 languages (100000 train & 10000 test sentences for each language). Each sentence is augmented in multiple ways, so the spelling of the words and the punctuation placement are affected. Your task is to reverse the augmentation as much as you can.

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set

## Columns

*   `Id` - unique id for each sentence pair
*   `Language` - one of `en` (English), `bg` (Bulgarian), `tr` (Turkish), `fr` (French), `de` (German)
*   `Text`?- noisy text
*   `Expected` - original sentence before augmentation

Check out the [evaluation page](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/ml-olympiad-multilingual-spell-correction/overview/evaluation) for details on how the submission file should look like.

## License
The sentences in our dataset are sourced from [Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Main_Page). The contents of Wikipedia are licensed under the [Creative Commons 3.0 License](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Text_of_the_Creative_Commons_Attribution-ShareAlike_3.0_Unported_License).",False,True
