6596,sentiment-analysis-company-reviews,"# Description

## Goal of the Competition

The goal of this competition is to predict the sentiment of company reviews left by customers. The target is a star rating between 1 and 5, and because of the natural ordering of the categories we use a regression metric, namely Mean Absolute Error (MAE), to evaluate predictions. Your job is to build a model that minimizes the MAE on the test set.

The secondary goal is to provide a beginner friendly environment for people to learn and practice their Natural Language Processing (NLP) skills. Visit the 'Code' tab to see other people's solutions and visit the 'Discussion' tab to ask any questions you have. 

## About the dataset

The dataset consists of 100,000 reviews collected from Trustpilot and spans over 40 different companies. The samples are split according to: 60% train, 10% public leaderboard and 30% private leaderboard. 
----------------------
# Evaluation

## Goal

The task is to predict the sentiment of each customer as a number between 1 and 5. You can interpret the target as [1, 2, 3, 4, 5] = [Very unsatisfied, Unsatisfied, Neutral, Satisfied, Very satisfied]. 

## Metric

Submissions are evaluated using [Mean Absolute Error (MAE)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mean_absolute_error) between the predicted ratings and the true ratings. Note that your predictions do not have to be integers even though the true ratings are. 

## Submission Format

The file should contain a header and have the following format:

```
Id,Rating
60000,3.5
60001,4.7
60002,1.9
```","# Dataset Description

The dataset consists of 100,000 reviews across 45 different companies. The goal is to predict the sentiment of each customer as a star rating between 1 and 5. 

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

## Columns

*   `Id` - Unique id
*   `Review`?- Customer review
*   `Rating` - Number of stars awarded by customer, between 1 and 5.",False,True
6597,ml-olympiad-detect-chatgpt-answers,"# Description

??Hello, welcome to our competition! You¡¯re in the right place.

Welcome to the second edition of MLM, The machine learning Marathon, organized by **IEEE ESSTHS Student Branch, IEEE ESSTHS CIS SBC, GDSC ISETSo, and PyData Tunisia! **

Hashtags: #MLOlympiad #ResponsibleAI, #MLResearch #IEEE #IEEE_ESSTHS_SB #Google #GDSC  #PyData_Tunisia 

Thank you for participating in our competition, we wish you all good luck and let us all have fun!

##### Important Clause, please read carefully:
**By participating in this competition, you acknowledge to the following:**

- The theme ""Detect ChatGpt answers"" of this competition is not related to Google or any of its affiliates.
- The selection of this theme was made solely by the GDE and the competition team, without any influence from Google.
- This competition is an independent project and has no connection to Google in terms of theme or scope.

### The goal of the Competition
The goal of this competition is to classify ChatGpt answers vs real human answers for a variety 
of questions. You will work to develop a model that will train on our data to be able to classify whether the paragraph was AI-generated or human-written.  

Your work will help distinguish between human written paragraphs and answers vs state of the art ai language models, Chatgpt is our case study here. 

### Context 
Over the last few years since the introduction of transformers and self-attention,  there has been a significant rise in the development and deployment of large language models, such as OpenAI's GPT series. These models are trained on massive amounts of text data and can generate human-like text, answer questions, translate languages, and perform various other NLP tasks with high accuracy. The success of these models has led to widespread interest in them, with numerous research papers, startups, and large technology companies investing in the development of such models. As a result, there has been a rapid advancement in the field of NLP, and the use of these models is rapidly expanding into various industries such as customer service, marketing, and content creation.

With the popularity and widespread impact that Chatgpt caused since its release on November 30th,2022. Countless entities and notable names expressed their concern about how advanced AI has become nowadays and the concern is valid considering that Chatgpt already passed the final exam at UPenn's Wharton for the school's Master of Business Administration (MBA) program. 

Occurrences like these bring us closer and closer to the dystopian future where AI controls the world and surpasses human beings completely.  Nevertheless, it is still very much possible to distinguish the difference between AI work and human one, and here is your time to shine, Our ML Olympiad this year aims to detect AI using AI just like the old saying ""fight fire with fire"".  

Your work will help better tell the difference between an AI-generated code or response vs a human-written one 
this will have a real and substantial impact on the transparency and credibility of homework, letters of motivation, copyrighting campaigns, and professional emails. 

one of these paragraphs was AI-generated, a small challenge for you to know which one. 

### Overview of How Kaggle¡¯s Competitions Work
1. Join the Competition
Read about the challenge description, accept the Competition Rules and gain access to the competition dataset.

1. Get to Work
Download the data, build models on it locally or on Kaggle Kernels (our no-setup, customizable Jupyter Notebooks environment with free GPUs) and generate a prediction file.

1. Make a Submission
Upload your prediction as a submission on Kaggle and receive an accuracy score.

1. Check the Leaderboard
See how your model ranks against other Kagglers on our leaderboard.

1. Improve Your Score
Check out the discussion forum to find lots of tutorials and insights from other competitors.

##### What Data Will I Use in This Competition?
Train.csv will contain the details of a subset of prompts (questions) and  answers, and, will reveal which category they are a part of, also known as the ¡°ground truth¡±.
The test.csv dataset contains similar information but does not disclose the ¡°ground truth¡± for each answer. It¡¯s your job to classify these answers.
Using the patterns you find in the train.csv data, try to classify these answers as accurately as possible.
Check out the ¡°Data¡± tab to explore the datasets even further. Once you feel you¡¯ve created a competitive model, submit it to Kaggle to see where your model stands on our leaderboard against other Kagglers.

How to Submit your Prediction to Kaggle
Once you¡¯re ready to make a submission and get on the leaderboard:

1. Click on the ¡°Submit Predictions¡± button
2. Upload a CSV file in the submission file format. You¡¯re able to submit 10 submissions a day.

Submission File Format:
You should submit a CSV file with exactly 249 rows plus a header row. Your submission will show an error if you have extra columns (beyond Id and category) or rows. The file should have exactly 2 columns: Id (sorted in any order) category (contains 0 If the question was answered by a human being, 1 if It is a ChatGpt-generated answer .)

Got it! I¡¯m ready to get started. Where do I get help if I need it?
For Competition Help: ML Olympiad Discussion Forum
Technical Help: Kaggle Contact Us Page

Good luck to you all !","# Dataset Description

The training and test sets contain prompts, which are questions on various things and of multiple categories, and the replies to those prompts in the form of grammatically correct paragraphs.
The prompts include opinion-based questions, general knowledge questions, scientific facts, and special ones to spice things up.  each prompt contains an unannounced ratio of human to ChatGpt generated replies, per example ( 1 ChatGpt generated reply, 5 human generated replies).
The data has been approximately split 75%/25% for training/test.

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format


## Columns

*   `prompt` - The question or the prompt 
*   `answer`?- The answer given by the target 
*   `AI` - 0 If the question was answered by a human being, 1 if It is a ChatGpt-generated answer .",False,True
