](url)

## What is ML Olympiad?

An associated Kaggle Community Competitions hosted by Google ML communities, supported by Google Developers. (ML GDEs, TFUGs, or 3rd party ML communities host ML community competitions on Kaggleand ML Developer Programs team sends rewards to hosts of registered competitions.)

## ML Competition

This is a machine learning competition focused on classifying beer into 17 distinct styles based on key descriptors. Participants will be provided with a dataset containing information such as IBU (International Bitterness Units), ABV (Alcohol By Volume), as well as professional ratings and descriptions. The goal is to develop a model that can accurately categorize beer into one of the 17 styles, based on the provided descriptors. The competition is open to all machine learning enthusiasts, and offers a unique opportunity to put your skills to the test and showcase your ability to tackle a complex classification problem.


## Acknowledgements

The dataset used in this competition is based on the dataset from [Beer Profile And Rating Datsets](https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/ruthgn/beer-profile-and-ratings-data-set)

We would like to thank Google for the invite and the support provided while hosting and promoting this competition

 #MLOlympiad #AppliedML
----------------------
# Evaluation

## Goal
Predict beer style.

## Metric
F-Score (Macro)

## Submission File Format
You should submit a csv file with exactly 58 entries plus a header row. Your submission will show an error if you have extra columns or rows.

The file should have exactly 2 columns:

- ID (beer id)
- Style_id (beer style)","# Dataset Description

##**This competition was cancelled due to a bug in the dataset.**

Please register for this competition
[https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/ml-olympiad-can-you-guess-beer-style/
](url)

## Dataset Information

This dataset contains tasting profiles and consumer reviews for 403 unique beers from 281 different breweries. It was created by integrating information from two existing data sets on Kaggle: Beer Tasting Profiles Dataset and 1.5 Million Beer Reviews.

The training set should be used to build your machine learning models. For the training set, we provide the outcome (also known as the ¡°ground truth¡±) for each beer. Your model will be based on beer's features. You can also use feature engineering to create new features.

The test set should be used to see how well your model performs on unseen data. For the test set, we do not provide the ground truth for each beer. It is your job to predict these outcomes. For each beer in the test set, use the model you trained to predict its style.

We also include a sample submission file named sample_submission.csv, a set of predictions as an example of what a submission file should look like.

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the dataset used to train model.
*   **test.csv** - the dataset used to evaluate the model.
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format.

## Columns

* ID: A unique ID that identifies the beer
* Name:  Beer name
* Brewery:  Brewery name
* Beer Name (Full): Complete beer name (Brewery + Brew Name) 
* Description: Notes on the beer if available
* ABV: Alcohol content of beer (% by volume)
* Min IBU: The minimum IBU value each beer can possess
* Max IBU: The maximum IBU value each beer can possess

* The next eleven columns represent taste and aroma descriptors taken from up to 25 reviews of each beer.
* Astringency
* Body
* Alcohol
* Bitter
* Sweet
* Sour
* Salty
* Fruits
* Hoppy
* Spices
* Malty

The last six columns refer to information regarding beer reviews:

* review _ aroma: The average rating for aroma
* review _ appearance: The average rating for appearance
* review _ palate: The average rating for mouthfeel
* review _ taste: The average rating for taste
* review _ overall: The overall average rating of the beer
* number _ of _ reviews: The number of reviews taken into consideration",False,True
6599,ml-olympiad-tfugkol,"# Description

### Welcome to the 2023 edition of ML Olympiad!

[ML Olympiad](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions?hostSegmentIdFilter=10&searchQuery=ml+olympiad) is an associated Kaggle Community Competitions hosted by Google ML communities, supported by Google Developers.

[**Download assets for participants**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1pBzQdy49LSxaQ6HuBWn_k2ZjVGBb4ayf/view?usp=share_link)

The California Housing dataset is a well-known dataset in the field of applied machine learning. It contains information about housing prices and various features that can impact housing prices in the state of California. This dataset is important in applied machine learning because it can be used to train machine learning models to predict housing prices based on various features, such as the number of rooms, the median income of the area, etc. This can be used to understand the factors that affect housing prices and make informed decisions about buying or investing in real estate.

In addition to its practical use in the field of real estate, the California Housing dataset is also socially helpful because it provides insight into the factors that impact housing prices and affordability. This can be used to address social and economic issues related to housing, such as gentrification, unequal access to affordable housing, and the impact of zoning laws on housing prices. By using machine learning models to understand these factors, policymakers and communities can make data-driven decisions to improve access to affordable housing and create more equitable communities.


### Synthetically-Generated Datasets

Using synthetic data for ML Olympiad allows us to strike a balance between having real-world data (with named features) and ensuring test labels are not publicly available. This allows us to host competitions with more interesting datasets than in the past. While there are still challenges with synthetic data generation, the state-of-the-art is much better now, and that goal is to produce datasets that have far fewer artifacts. Please feel free to give us feedback on the datasets for the different competitions so that we can continue to improve!


Share on Social Media with **#MLOlympiad #AppliedML**
----------------------
# Evaluation

## Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE)
Submissions are scored on the root mean squared error. RMSE is defined as:

$$
\textrm{RMSE} =  \sqrt{ \frac{1}{N} \sum\_{i=1}^{N} (y\_i - \hat{y}\_i)^2 }
$$

where \\( \hat{y}\_i \\) is the predicted value and \\( y_i \\) is the original value for each instance \\(i\\).

## Submission File
For each `id` in the test set, you must predict the value for the target `MedHouseVal`. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    id,MedHouseVal
    37137,2.01
    37138,0.92
    37139,1.11
    etc.

----------------------
# Timeline

* **Start Date** - February 7, 2023
* **Entry Deadline** - Same as the Final Submission Deadline
* **Team Merger Deadline** - Same as the Final Submission Deadline
* **Final Submission Deadline** -  March 7, 2023

All deadlines are at 11:59 PM UTC on the corresponding day unless otherwise noted. The competition organizers reserve the right to update the contest timeline if they deem it necessary.
----------------------
# Prizes

To be declared soon!","# Dataset Description

The dataset for this competition (both train and test) was generated from a deep learning model trained on the [California Housing Dataset](https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/generated/sklearn.datasets.fetch_california_housing.html). Feature distributions are close to, but not exactly the same, as the original. Feel free to use the original dataset as part of this competition, both to explore differences as well as to see whether incorporating the original in training improves model performance.

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training dataset; `MedHouseVal` is the target
*   **test.csv** - the test dataset; your objective is to predict `MedHouseVal`
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format",False,True
6600,machine-learning-challenge-2-prediction,"# Description

CITE-seq is a single-cell sequencing assay in which both gene and cell-surface protein expression is measured in single-cells.  This ""multi-modal"" analysis allows for biological knowledge discovery about the relationships between protein markers of cell type and the underlying transcriptional (gene expression) state.  

There is no biology that you need to understand, other than that there are strong relationships between expression of particular proteins and corresponding gene expression.

Protein expression is measured using Antibody-Derived Tags (ADT), while single-cell RNA-sequencing is used to quantify the abundance of RNA transcripts in each cell.

The goal of this competition is to predict the protein expression profile of 25 proteins in 1000 test cells given the gene expression of 639 genes in those cells.  In order to predict this information, you are given 4000 cells from the same experiment with information on all 25 proteins and 639 genes.  Use the training data (possibly including the test RNA data) to most accurately predict the protein expression.

**About this Competition**
This is a team project for a Machine Learning class.  Anyone is welcome to participate, but the challenge is geared towards class content and learning.  Students are challenged to write algorithms at a very low level (e.g. BLAS Level 3) not using ML libraries.

**Normalization**
Both RNA and ADT data has been standard log-normalized.  Highly variable genes were selected for analysis, and non-variable ADT proteins were removed.

**Simple Approaches**
Parametric methods such as K-means and KNN will not perform very well because you cannot fully appreciate signal substructure across both samples and features.  Thus, you might try multivariate regression or dimension reduction (NMF or SVD) as a simple way to learn tunable and generalizable models.  
- Multivariate regression would involve learning a model on the training set that predicts each ADT feature in terms of all RNA features, then plugging in for the ADT features given the learned coefficients on the test set.
- NMF could be approached several ways:
   - Learn a model on the training dataset, project onto the test dataset using RNA features, and then project onto the ADT features.
   - Learn a model on the entire training + test dataset, masking missing values in the ADT/test sample/feature block. Then multiply out the model.
   - Learn a model on the RNA features in the training+test dataset, then run a multivariate regression between the model embedding and ADT features in the training set.  Use those coefficients to solve for the test ADT features.

Of course, there are many other approaches that are not as easy to code from scratch that will outperform the above methods.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Entries are evaluated using **Pearson Correlation Coefficient**.  This means that your predictions must correlate well with the ground truth, although they may be scaled differently.  This objective avoids issues with normalizations and focuses rather on your ability to recover meaningful information about the signals.

Consider a good score &gt;0.78.  If you achieve this score, your method appreciates signal substructure appropriately.","# Dataset Description

This experiment consists of two modalities -- ADT (25 features) and RNA (600-some features).  The training set has 4000 samples, the test set has 1000 samples.  Given the training ADT assay (training_set_adt.csv), the training RNA assay (training_set_rna.csv), and the test RNA assay (test_set_rna.csv), predict the test ADT.

| training samples | test samples |
| --- | --- |
|  training_set_adt.csv | PREDICT THIS |
|  training_set_rna.csv | test_set_rna.csv |",False,True
6601,ucalgary-and-weights-biases-blood-mnist,"# Description

## Competition details

The details of this competition will be shared in a separate resource. You can find the Notebook for this contest here: [W&B and UCalgary BloodMNIST Colab Notebook](https://colab.research.google.com/drive/1SPx7_vS-IOoG5lXuu9ZSIhRIDOMyPz3V?usp=sharing)

The BloodMNIST dataset is part of the wider MedMNIST dataset.

MedMNIST is a large-scale MNIST-like collection of standardized biomedical images, including twelve 2D datasets and six 3D datasets. All the images are pre-processed to the size of 28x28 pixels and working with the data requires no prior domain knowledge about medicine, genetics, biology, etc.

## BloodMNIST details

The BloodMNIST is based on a dataset of individual normal cells, captured from individuals without infection, hematologic or oncologic disease and free of any pharmacologic treatment at the moment of blood collection. It contains a total of 17,092 images and is organized into 8 classes (8 different classes of blood cell).


## Spoiler

If you'd like *a* solution to the proposed classification task we've included one that was authored by our colleagues Justin and Ayush here: [Integrating Keras with Weights and Biases](https://wandb.ai/ayush-thakur/medmnist-bloodmnist/reports/Integrating-Keras-with-Weights-Biases--VmlldzoxNDg1MDQy?utm_source=webinar&utm_medium=online&utm_campaign=windsor).

----------------------
# Evaluation

We'll be evaluating your prediction based on accuracy.","# Dataset Description

Please grab the data via the Colab Notebook: https://colab.research.google.com/drive/17EMgzwIwO1UmGJaW4SZgWGn9NTbCkviB?usp=sharing",False,True
