6624,bengali-ged,"# Description

On the occasion of International language month, what better way to automate unsolved Bangla Grammatical Error Detection through Artificial Intelligence?
EEE Day 2023 and Bengali.Ai are organizing ""Apurba presents Bhashabhrom"": Bangla Grammar error detection Datathon. The competition will be launched on a virtual platform followed by multiple hybrid and online workshops.
Event Timeline: 10 - 28 February
Participant requirement: 1-4 in one group. Students or young professionals (everyone must be current student/alumni of a Bangladeshi University) can form a team
Event platform: Kaggle
Organizer: EEE Day 2023, Bengali.Ai
Technical support: Banan Andolon
Sponsored by: Apurba Technologies
Register at shorturl.at/ezBLZ 
More details [here](https://www.facebook.com/events/1283706012488022).

----------------------
# Bengali Grammatical Error Detection

The task is to detect substrings that contain grammatical or spelling error.

In the sentence ""??? ??? ????"", the first word is erroneous. So we flag the word only and return ""$???$ ??? ????"" The participants have to detect all the substrings and provide the predicted string with '$' marks.

The major error types are:
1. Single word error ???? $?????$ ??????
2. Multi word error $????? ???$ ?????? ?????????
3. Wrong Punctuation ???? ?? ????? ???????$?$
4. Punctuation Omission ???? ?? ??? ??????? ????? ???????$$ (there will be a punctuation that is not present. So we mark the position of the error with two $ signs)
5. Merge Error $????????$ ????? ?????
6. Form/Inflection Error
7. Unwanted space error ???? ??? ???$ $?
8. Hybrid

----------------------
# Timeline

- Team Merger deadline: **February 25, 2023.**
- Release of the remaining train data: **February 22, 2023.**
- Phase 1 Submission Deadline: **February 28, 2023.**
- Notebook Submission Deadline: **March 3, 2023.**
- Phase 2 (Offline Presentation): **First Week of March (tentative).**
----------------------
# Evaluation

For the first round, the Kaggle leaderboard will be based on [Levenshtein Distance](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Levenshtein_distance) of the predicted string and ground truth. After the end of the first round (28th February), all the submissions will be evaluated using a separate held-out test set. The participants will have to submit their Training and inference Notebook as well as a detailed paper on their approach after the first phase ends.

The final Leaderboard will be decided based on the first-phase score on the partial test set (20%), the second-phase score on the held-out test set (50%), Runtime (10%), and the Paper + Final Presentation (20%).

##### Demonstration:


Suppose a given sentence is ""???? ?? ?????? ????? ???????""
There is an error at the beginning of the sentence : ""???? ??"" &gt; ""??????""

Also, there should also be punctuation at the end of the sentence.

So, the ground truth should be &gt;""$???? ??$ ?????? ????? ???????$$""

Now, if the prediction is :  ""???? ?? ?????? $?????$ ???????"",
 the Levenshtein Distance between the ground truth and the prediction is &gt;lev(""$???? ??$ ?????? ????? ???????$$"", ""???? ?? ?????? $?????$ ???????"").","# Dataset Description

## FIles
DataSetFold1.csv - training data
test.csv - test data
SampleSubmission.csv - a sample submission file in the correct format
## Columns
* sentence - the erroneous sentence 
* gt - '$' signs are the markers for each error",False,True
