6626,iiitdm-hacks-track-2,"# Description

**Goal of the Competition**

The goal of this competition is to give a continuous prediction for the values column i.e. what a current player might be worth.

**Context**

A new Football Club has come into existence in India and got hold of a pretty large database of around 20000 values. The table contains what are the specifics of the various players and what are the age, position, weight, and height of various players. They have hired you as a Data Analyst intern and given you a task to train a model on this particular dataset so that they can use the same on various other Indian Players to get to know their true worth so that they can get a tactical advantage while bidding on them for the next auction.
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation will be done based on the Root Mean Squared score of the submitted file and the true value of the Player Value.","# Dataset Description

The dataset that is received contains two parts. First, the 18000 entries have various columns and the new entries have around 500+ entries. You need to give a prediction for these 500 entries. 

**Files**

    train.csv - the training set with 18000 entries
    test.csv - the test set with 539 entries
    sample_submission.csv - a sample submission file in the correct format

**Columns**

*  Overall - Overall Rating of the player determining his ability out of 99
*  Value - transfer valuation of the player as seen by his club/the market which depends on various factors
*  Positions Played - The positions that the player can usually play in
*  Best position - the optimal position of the player
*  BaseStats - Sum of all the base stats pace, passing, shooting, defending, dribbling and physical
*  Wage - salary paid to the player by his club
*  Release clause - The amount of money a club has to pay to his parent club to buy the player outright without any negotiation	
*  Club position - the position the player plays for his club
*  Contract until -  the year until which the players contract with his current club is valid
*  Joined on - The year in which the player joined his current club
*  On loan- true if the player is on loan to another club from his parent club
*  Preferred foot - the players favoured foot
*  Weak foot rating - rating of the players weak foot on a scale of 1-5
*  Skill moves - the ability to perform skills rated on a scale of 1-5
*  International reputation - the reputation of a football player across the world / his marketability, fame etc
*  Attacking workrate - the work of a player to contribute in the attacking aspect of the game
*  Defensive workrate - the work of a player to contribute in the defensive aspect of the game
*  (Pac,sho,pas,dri,def,phy) total - the rating of the player given out of 99 for these different aspects of the game(the ratings of these for a goalkeeper are given from his diving, reflexes etc to maintain homogeneity?in?the?dataset)",False,True
6627,beer2023,"# Description

<p>The International Bittering Units, or IBU, scale is used to quantify the bitterness of a beer. In this competition, you will build a machine learning model to predict the IBU of a beer, given other attributes about the beer, including its color, alcohol content, and other features.</p>
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>Submissions will be scored based on root mean-squared error (RMSE) on the test set. If \(y\) represents the actual IBU and \(\hat y\) is the predicted IBU, then RMSE is defined as:</p>
<p>\[ RMSE = \sqrt{\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n (y_i - \hat y_i)^2}. \]</p>
<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p>Each beer in the training and test data?is given a unique ID. You should submit your predictions for the test data in the following format. (A sample file, <strong>beer_test_sample_submission.csv</strong>, has been provided for you.)</p>
<pre><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">id,ibu<br />6000,38.2<br />6001,13.1<br />6002,40.4<br />6003,22.0<br /></span><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">etc.<br /></span></pre>","# Dataset Description

<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>beer_train.csv</strong> - the training set</li>
<li><strong>beer_test.csv</strong> - the test set</li>
<li><strong>beer_test_sample_submission.csv</strong> - a sample submission file in the correct format</li>
</ul>
<h2>Data fields</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>id</strong> - a unique ID for each beer</li>
<li><strong>name</strong> - the name of the beer</li>
<li><strong>description</strong>?- a description of the beer</li>
<li><strong>available</strong> - when the beer is available</li>
<li><strong>isOrganic</strong> - ""Y"" if the beer is organic, ""N"" otherwise</li>
<li><strong>glass</strong> - the type of glass that the beer is intended to be served in</li>
<li><strong>abv</strong> - <a href=""https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alcohol_by_volume"">alcohol by volume</a>?of the beer</li>
<li><strong>originalGravity</strong> - <a href=""https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gravity_(alcoholic_beverage)"">original gravity</a>?of the beer</li>
<li><strong>srm</strong> - <a href=""https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Standard_Reference_Method"">SRM</a>?of the beer</li>
<li><strong>ibu</strong> - <a href=""https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beer_measurement"">IBU</a>?of the beer</li>
</ul>",False,True
6628,ml-olympiad-tfug-mumbai,"# Description

This repo is archived. please head to the https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/tfug-mumbai-ml-olympiad
----------------------
# Evaluation

## Goal
It is your job to predict for each transaction, **whether it will result in TX_FRAUD (1) or not (0). Your predictions should be a probability between 0 and 1.0**.

## Metric
Submissions are evaluated on [AUC](https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course/classification/roc-and-auc), which is a number between 0 and 1, where 0.0 means 100% of the predictions are wrong, and 1.0 means 100% of the predictions are correct. Randomly guessing should give a score of 0.5.

## Submission File Format
The file should contain a header and have the following format:
```
TX_ID,TX_FRAUD
121212121212,0.30
3434343434,1.0
... 
```
You can download an example submission file (csv) on the Data page.

Please see the Starter Notebook to get started with some code in Python:
- <a href=""https://console.cloud.google.com/ai-platform/notebooks/deploy-notebook?name=Fraud%20Detection&amp;download_url=https://storage.googleapis.com/kaggleworkshops-fraud-detection-public/payments_tx_fraud_detection.ipynb&amp;url=https://storage.googleapis.com/kaggleworkshops-fraud-detection-public/payments_tx_fraud_detection.ipynb"" target=""_blank""><img src=""https://cloud.google.com/images/products/ai/ai-solutions-icon.svg"" alt=""AI Platform Notebooks"" target=""_blank"">Starter Notebook [Python]</a>  

----------------------
# BI Challenge

# Business Intelligence Challenge

## Goal
The goal of this challenge is to explore the available dataset from a payments business perspective to draw conclusions and gather insights that could be of interest for a client from the payments industry.

Share your dashboard/notebook/visualizations at the end competition deadline.

## How you will be evaluated
Your team will be given 3 minutes to present your dashboard/insights at the end of the hackathon, and you will be evaluated by judges based on:
1. **Business Impact**: what is the potential business impact of your dashboard or insights?
2. **Storytelling**: the effectiveness of a story or theme acrosss your dashboard/visualizations/insights
3. **Creativity**: how creative were you in the use of data, visualizations, tooling?


## Getting Started
As a starting point, you can explore the data using Looker Studio or a Jupyter notebook using the templates below:

### Sample dashboard with Looker Studio ([documentation](https://developers.google.com/looker-studio))
Here's a template Looker Studio dashboard that you can copy from:
- <a href=""https://datastudio.google.com/u/0/reporting/c8441d43-d4af-403c-8422-4d726d56df4b/page/cVK6C/edit""><b>Starter Dashboard Business Intelligence</b></a>



### Sample visualization Jupyter notebook in Python
Here's a template notebook that you can use:

- <a href=""https://console.cloud.google.com/ai-platform/notebooks/deploy-notebook?name=Fraud%20Detection&download_url=https://storage.googleapis.com/kaggleworkshops-fraud-detection-public/Starter%20Notebook%20Business%20Intelligence.ipynb&url=https://storage.googleapis.com/kaggleworkshops-fraud-detection-public/Starter%20Notebook%20Business%20Intelligence.ipynb"" target=""_blank""><img src=""https://cloud.google.com/images/products/ai/ai-solutions-icon.svg"" alt=""AI Platform Notebooks"" target=""_blank""><b>Starter Notebook Business Intelligence [Python]</b></a>   
----------------------
# Tips On Improving ML Models

# How can I improve my predictions? My rank is not going up.
### Feature selection
- try selecting the features that you think are most important to the model

### Model selection and hyperparameter tuning
- have you tried choosing different classification models? have you tried tuning some of its hyperparameters? Check out the docs on each of the models below:
 - [`logistic_reg`](https://cloud.google.com/bigquery-ml/docs/reference/standard-sql/bigqueryml-syntax-create)
 - [`boosted_tree_classifier`](https://cloud.google.com/bigquery-ml/docs/reference/standard-sql/bigqueryml-syntax-create-boosted-tree)
 - [`dnn_classifier`](https://cloud.google.com/bigquery-ml/docs/reference/standard-sql/bigqueryml-syntax-create-dnn-models)
 - [`automl_classifier`](https://cloud.google.com/bigquery-ml/docs/reference/standard-sql/bigqueryml-syntax-create-automl)

You can also learn how to use [BigQuery ML's automated HP tuning capabilities in this blogpost](https://cloud.google.com/blog/topics/developers-practitioners/access-free-training-and-learn-how-automate-hyperparameter-tuning-find-best-model).

### Feature engineering
- could the day of week have an impact on purchasing patterns? use the [EXTRACT](https://cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/reference/standard-sql/timestamp_functions#extract) function to retrieve the year/month/day/hour/minute/etc. from a TIMESTAMP column
- can you multiple, divide, add, or subtract numerical features to create new features?
- can you augment the weight of outliers by squaring numerical features, or on the other hand, taking the square root?
- could combinations of features impact purchasing patterns more than individual columns? You can use [ML.FEATURE_CROSS](https://cloud.google.com/bigquery-ml/docs/reference/standard-sql/bigqueryml-preprocessing-functions#mlfeature_cross) to create interactions on category variables 
- would it help to scale quantitative variables using [ML.STANDARD_SCALER](https://cloud.google.com/bigquery-ml/docs/reference/standard-sql/bigqueryml-preprocessing-functions#mlstandard_scaler)?
- are numerical features too granular? Could you bucketize them using [ML.QUANTILE_BUCKETIZE](https://cloud.google.com/bigquery-ml/docs/reference/standard-sql/bigqueryml-preprocessing-functions#quantile_bucketize)?
- check out all of the [pre-processing features built into BigQuery ML](https://cloud.google.com/bigquery-ml/docs/reference/standard-sql/bigqueryml-preprocessing-functions)


### Ensembling models
- the combination of multiple model outputs may be better than any single model alone. As a simple example, you can try to take the mean/median of the probability outputs of ML.PREDICT across multiple models. For example, for a specific row, if model_1 and model_2 predict 0.5 and 1.0 respectively, then the averaged prediction would be 0.75.",# Dataset Description,False,True
6629,cmo-me-siento,"# Description

##Facial Expression Recognition
Guess whether these people are feeling good or not regarding their faces. ?? ??

It is believed that a human has at least 8 basics emotions, where sadness and happiness are the ones the people usually talk more about. For the human being is kind of an easy job to find out how a person is feeling by the expression on their face. But this time you should train a Neural Network, using the layers and params you may find necessaries in order to get the highest accuracy, differentiating between sad and happy people.

You're right, people can feel more than one emotion at a time but, let's forget about it for a sec and try to simplify the problem.

The pictures are already in black and white, have a 48x48 size and have the faces cropped, what should makes everything a little easier. However, feel free to make any changes to the pictures, but remember to make the same kind of treatment to the train set of images and the test set of images, just before you make the predictions.

In case you don't already know, this is a problem of classification, with two classes.

Are you happy? Are you sad?
