----------------------
# Evaluation

***[En]***

### **Earthquake Road Crack Detection Computer Vision Competition Evaluation Criteria**

**Accuracy**: The most important evaluation criterion is the accuracy of the crack detection algorithm. Participants' algorithms will be evaluated based on their ability to correctly identify cracks in road images.

**Speed**: The second most important evaluation criterion is the speed of the algorithm. Participants' algorithms will be evaluated based on the amount of time it takes for the algorithm to complete its processing.

**Robustness**: The third most important evaluation criterion is the robustness of the algorithm. Participants' algorithms will be evaluated based on their ability to perform well on different types of road images, including images with varying levels of damage and different lighting conditions.

**Technical Report**: Participants are required to submit a technical report along with their algorithm. The report should explain the methodology used to develop the algorithm and present the results obtained. The report will be evaluated based on the clarity, conciseness, and relevance of the information presented.

**Innovation**: Participants' algorithms will also be evaluated based on their level of innovation. Innovative algorithms that use novel techniques to address the challenge of road crack detection will be given extra consideration.

**Voluntary Participation**: This competition is based on voluntary participation, and participants are encouraged to participate for the love of learning and solving the problem. The evaluation panel will take into account the spirit of voluntary participation when evaluating participants' algorithms.

***[TR]***

### **Deprem Yol Hasar Tespiti Bilgisayarla G?r¨¹ Yar??mas? De?erlendirme Kriterleri**

**Do?ruluk**: En ?nemli de?erlendirme kriteri, ?atla??n do?ru olarak tespit edilmesidir. Kat?l?mc?lar?n algoritmalar?, yol g?r¨¹nt¨¹lerindeki ?atlamalar? do?ru bir ?ekilde tespit etme becerilerine g?re de?erlendirilecektir.

**H?z**: ?kinci en ?nemli de?erlendirme kriteri, algoritman?n h?z?d?r. Kat?l?mc?lar?n algoritmalar?, algoritman?n i?lemini tamamlamas? gereken zaman ¨¹zerinden de?erlendirilecektir.

**Dayan?kl?l?k**: ¨¹?¨¹nc¨¹ en ?nemli de?erlendirme kriteri, algoritman?n dayan?kl?l???d?r. Kat?l?mc?lar?n algoritmalar?, farkl? hasar seviyelerine ve farkl? ???k ko?ullar?na sahip farkl? yol g?r¨¹nt¨¹lerinde iyi performans g?sterme becerilerine g?re de?erlendirilecektir.

**Teknik Rapor**: Kat?l?mc?lar, algoritmalar?n?n yan? s?ra bir teknik rapor sunmal?d?r. Rapor, algoritman?n geli?tirilmesi i?in kullan?lan metodolojiyi a??klamal? ve elde edilen sonu?lar? sunmal?d?r. Raporun a??kl?k, ?zetlilik ve sunulan bilginin ?nemi ¨¹zerinden de?erlendirilecektir.

**Yenilik**: Kat?l?mc?lar?n algoritmalar? ayn? zamanda yenilik d¨¹zeylerine g?re de?erlendirilecektir. Yol ?atla?? alg?lama zorlu?unu ele almak i?in yenilik?i teknikler kullanan algoritmalara ekstra de?er verilecektir.

**G?nl¨¹l¨¹ Kat?l?m**: Bu yar??ma g?n¨¹ll¨¹ kat?l?ma dayal?d?r ve kat?l?mc?lar ??renme ve problem ??zme a?k?yla kat?lmaya te?vik edilir. De?erlendirme paneli, kat?l?mc?lar?n algoritmalar?n? de?erlendirirken g?n¨¹ll¨¹ kat?l?m ruhunu dikkate alacakt?r.","# Dataset Description

On February 6, 2023, a major earthquake occurred in Turkey. One of the biggest problems after the earthquake was the cracks and deterioration in the roads. Therefore, there were many problems in the delivery of aid. 

The ""Earthquake Road Crack Detection"" competition is a computer vision challenge that aimed at developing algorithms to immediately and automatically detect roads damaged by aerial imagery after affected by earthquakes. The goal of the competition is to build a model that can accurately identify and locate cracks in the images, which can help in the assessment of the damage caused by earthquakes and the planning of repair and maintenance activities.

We are starting this project based on volunteerism to do everything in our power after the earthquake. We will present the project to the authorities and create a text with the names of everyone who participated.T

Not only for the earthquake in Turkey, but we will also share the model that emerged for use in many countries as open source. We are also working on this project ourselves. While doing this, we thought of the idea of why not work together with everyone the rest of the world and that's why we thought of opening this competition.The winning solutions will not only demonstrate the cutting-edge technology in computer vision, but also have the potential to impact real-world applications in the field of road assessment and maintenance.

We already thank everyone who voluntarily participates in this competition. And we share the problems related to our topic.

Step 1: Data
The data is very dirty and we collected it ourselves. We tried to filter it as much as we could, but there is still a lot of work to do. But this can also be considered as a challenge in the competition. Collecting more data/images, filtering and labeling is also a big challenge and we take this as part of the competition. Also, if we can improve this data, it can be turned into a great data set that everyone can use.

There are 2 folders in the data, ""filtered"" and ""unfiltered"". In these two folders, there are two folders named ""neg""(negative) and ""pos""(positive). The ""unfiltered"" folder contains the unfiltered images that we downloaded from the internet. In the ""filtered"" folder, there are filtered, resized, jpg formatted images. There are craked road images in the ""neg"" file, and regular road images with in the ""pos"" file.

If the images are not enough, you can use google/yahoo/bing/yandex images for data collection. There are browser extension for batch downloading. You can also use different keywords such as earthquake ground crack, road crack while searching. Also, searching in different languages can help you find different images.

Resize: Everyone may want to implement the resize themselves, that why we share images with original sizes. You can do it with photo editing applications has batch option or can with Python.

File format/type has been converted to JPG format (only for the files within the filtered folder).

Size = pos: 2556 images neg: 4000  images  / pos(unfiltered): 0000 , neg(unfiltered): 0000


Step 2: Model
We aim to create a model with as high accuracy as possible. We want to use it to automatically detect cracks in images and videos taken with drones and similar vehicles.

In addition, we have more post-earthquake and projects in the future.

The first one is to detect damaged buildings (cracked).

The second is to detect damaged structures such as bridges, viaducts, dams, etc.

We will collect data as much as possible and open a separate competition for these as well.

If you know any data for these 3 projects, we would be very happy if you shared it with us. We will also share everything with you such as researches, articles etc. Please share with us as well. For example, in wall crack detection ... the data set can be used. When we start that project, we will touch on that subject. Earthquake is a world problem not just Turkey's. Let's make the world a better place together!",False,True
6636,pos-tagging-en,"# Description

### About
Welcome to the second NLP23 course competition ?? Last time, your task was to build a tokeniser. This time, you have to build POS tagger, i.e., given tokenised text, assign a correct part of speech tag to each of the tokens.

### Get started
To get started, you should:
- Get familiar with the [data](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/pos-tagging-en/data)
- Get familiar with the [evaluation](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/pos-tagging-en/overview/evaluation) criteria
- Build a POS tagger, apply it to the provided `lexnorm-en-test.conll-masked` and finally submit your solution! (in the required form)

### Questions/issues
If you have any problem/questions, please contact me (Ludek) on the Slack.
----------------------
# Evaluation

### Metric
For evaluation, `accuracy` is used. 

### Submission format (important)
In the data tab, see the `y_predicted_en_example.csv` file. You essentially need to do the following:
- your `csv` file should have header `Id,Predicted`
- `ID` is based on the order of the token in the input text, note that you need to skip the spaces that denote split between sentences
- `Predicted` column includes IDs of the predicted labels - use `mapping_en.json` that you can find in the data tab to map the original labels to the corresponding numbers","# Dataset Description

You are essentially provided three files: `train`, `dev`, `test` (masked). In addition, you are provided with an example submission and mapping to map the predicted labels to corresponding ints.",False,True
