6638,feelings,"# Description

###  Facial Expression Recognition

Guess whether these people are feeling good or not regarding their faces. ?? ??

It is believed that a human has at least 8 basics emotions, where sadness and happiness are the ones the people usually talk more about. For the human being is kind of an easy job to find out how a person is feeling by the expression on their face. But this time you should train a Neural Network, using the layers and params you may find necessaries in order to get the highest accuracy, differentiating between sad and happy people.

You're right, people can feel more than one emotion at a time but, let's forget about it for a sec and try to simplify the problem.

The pictures are already in black and white, have a 48x48 size and have the faces cropped, what should makes everything a little easier. However, feel free to make any changes to the pictures, but remember to make the same kind of treatment to the train set of images and the test set of images, just before you make the predictions.

In case you don't already know, this is a problem of classification, with two classes.

Are you happy? Are you sad?
----------------------
# Evaluation

Your submissions will be scored as the percentage of correct predictions. 
**Categorization accuracy**??  Submit results 
The test set is divided into a public part (with which the public leaderboard is calculated, accessible during the competition) and another private part, with which the final positions are calculated, after the end of the competition. 
Predictions will be sent in the format indicated in the sample_submission.csv file in the data section.","# Dataset Description

## Data
In this section you will find data needed to train your NN and make the best prediction.

## Files
**plantilla_howyoufeel_images.ipynb** jupyter notebook to guide in your first Images Competition .??
**sample_submission.csv:** your submission should be something like this, same columns, same shape, same id, different emotions ??
**train:** this directory contains two other directories happy?? and sadness?? which contains the images.
**test:** this directory contains the pictures which you have to predict the emotions of.

**train_set.csv:** on this file you'll find the labels related to the path where the pictures are.
**test_set.csv:** this file should help you to make the predictions and prepare your submission file.",False,True
6639,phys591000-2023-week01,"# Description

We will learn Kaggle platform and basic data visualization.

Identify your teammate.  
Student A should Copy and Edit notebooks in Kaggle.   
Rename it and share to Student B, Student C, and **NTHU-AIPHYS2022**.

> **Please rename notebooks:**

> **lab_01 _ [StudentA ID] _ [StudentB ID] _ [StudentC ID]**","# Dataset Description

# Top jets and QCD jets.

There are 5000 Monte Carlo events in this tutorial file.   
It contains several features for top jets and QCD jets.   
You can get high-level features such as transverse momentum, pseudo-rapidity, azimuth angle, mass and four-momentum of jets.   
Also, you can convert low-level features from jet images.     

This is a starting point to learn how to deep into the data.   
Please have fun !  **: )**


>Supplement: [For Week01 Lab](https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1kunUxgkrxsuNyYOllxM7fuQpZnG2K-hz0mwDvnUqZGk/edit?usp=sharing)",False,True
6640,planttraits2023,"# Description

## Background

This competition aims to predict plant properties - so called plant traits - from citizen science plant photographs. Why are plant traits currently so relevant? **Plant traits are plant properties that are used to describe how plants function how they interact with the environment**. For instance, the trait plant canopy height indicates how good a plant is at overshadowing its neightbors the competition for sun light. Robust leaves (indicated by the leaf mass pear leaf area) indicate that plants optimize towards extreme conditions, such as heavy winds or droughts. Yet, environmental conditions are not static. Due to global change, the **biosphere is being transformed at accelerating pace**. Especially climate change is assumed to drastically impact the functioning of the ecosystems. This includes several processes, e.g. adaptions of plants and their traits to new conditions or even a altered plant species distribution with a resulting modification of the distribution of plant traits. However, **we can hardly project on a global scale how plant traits and as such entire ecosystems will react to climate change because we do not have sufficient data on plant traits**.

A **data treasure** in this regard may be the very growing availability of **citizen science photographs**. Thousands of citizens around the globale photograph plants with species identification apps (exmaples are iNaturalist or Pl@ntNet). The species are identified using AI algorithms and the prediction, the photograph and the geolocation are being curated in open databases. There are already more than **20 million plant photographs available covering all ecosystem types and continents**.

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F3319247%2Fec99f4290e7c7ff3cdc5fe57ed9c1509%2Fsample_images.png?generation=1676369779907551&alt=media)

In its original form, this data at first only informs on the species name of a plant and not its traits. However, a pioneering study, showed that **we can use artificial intelligence to predict plant traits from such photographs using Convolutional Neural Networks** (Schiller et al. 2021). For doing this, we paired sample images from the iNaturalist database with plant trait data. Such plant trait data is being curated from scientists for decades for for different species. The problem: The images and plant trait observations were not acquired for the same plant individuals or at the same time. Yet, using a weakly supervised learning approach we were able to train models that indicated the potential of this approach for a few plant traits. Yet, this data only showed this potential for a few plant traits and for a couple of thousand images. **This competition therefore aims to further unleash the potential of predicting plant traits from plant photographs**. Therefore, we gather more training data (more than 30.000 images with labels) and expanded the plant traits to be predicting from 6 to 33 plant traits.

Find here the original article:
* *Schiller, C., Schmidtlein, S., Boonman, C., Moreno-Mart¨ªnez, A., & Kattenborn, T. (2021). Deep learning and citizen science enable automated plant trait predictions from photographs. Scientific Reports, 11(1), 16395.*
[https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-021-95616-0](url)

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F3319247%2F4df028d3fc43ea0fade8833e14671360%2Fsample_preds.png?generation=1676369823384723&alt=media)

The interested reader may also see these references for some background and the general idea:
* *Wolf, S., Mahecha, M. D., Sabatini, F. M., Wirth, C., Bruelheide, H., Kattge, J., ... & Kattenborn, T. (2022). Citizen science plant observations encode global trait patterns. Nature Ecology & Evolution, 1-10.*
[https://www.nature.com/articles/s41559-022-01904-x](url)
* *Moles, A.T., Xirocostas, Z.A. Statistical power from the people. Nat Ecol Evol 6, 1802¨C1803 (2022).*
[https://www.nature.com/articles/s41559-022-01902-z](url)

## The competition

The aim of the competition is to predict the plant traits from the plant photographs using deep learning-based regression models (e.g. CNNs). The plant traits are available for each image. Yet, we do not expect exceptionally high accuracies (see above mentioned publication). The citizen science data is very heterogeneous and various plant traits describe chemical tissue properties that are somewhat loosely related to the *visible* appearance of plants in images. Thus, we do not expect that such models will result in very high accuracies, but we aim to understand how far we can generally go with this approach. Any prediction that helps us to better understand the global change on ecosystems is helpful; so please **help us exploring data treasure and the distribution of plant traits around the world!**

Here are some strategies that can be used to enhance model performance:
* We highly recommend to implement a **multi-task learning scheme**, where a model predicts multiple traits from photographs at once. This way the model can learn relationships between plant traits that may boost the overall model performance. We, therefore, assume that its most promising to predict all traits at once.
* The citizen science data comes with geolocations. This enables us to integrate ancillary geodata to the prediction process. In the pioneering study (Schiller et al. 2021), we incorporated climate information at a photographs location (plants very much depend on temperature, precipitation and related seasonality). Therefore, the dataset of this competition also comes with associated climate data for each image that can be easily integrated as additional predictor to train a **multi-input model**. Thus the preditors (x) of the modelling task may thus include 1) a plant image together and 2) a vector with climate information at the specified location. The climate information corresponding to each image is included in `data_train_mean.csv` (with the prefix `bio`) and was derived from the WorldClim database: [https://www.worldclim.org/data/bioclim.html](url)
* It is important to consider that plant traits are not static (we all have seen big and small trees of the same species). The labels on plant traits are mean values calculated from the TRY database. However, we know that for some species the plant traits have a quite wide range (since some plants can adopt to very different conditions). It is hence promising to inform the model on the expected variation of the plant traits for a given species. In the previous study we did that by **augmenting the labels** within the standard deviation. For this, we also added a .csv file that includes the expected standard deviation for the plant traits of each image (`data_train_sd.csv`). Thus, instead of providing the model with a fixed value for each observation you may vary the labels according to the standard deviation which is assumed to enhance the generalization (see Schiller et al. 2021).
* It may also be promising to test model ensembles (different CNN backbones in combination), given that four *eyes* see more than one.

Questions can be asked in the discussion or directly to Teja Kattenborn ([https://rsc4earth.de/authors/tkattenborn/](url)).

## Acknowledgements

* The TRY initiative: [https://www.try-db.org/de/de.php](url): Kattge, J., B?nisch, G., D¨ªaz, S., Lavorel, S., Prentice, I. C., Leadley, P., Tautenhahn, S., Werner, G., et al. (2020). TRY plant trait database - enhanced coverage and open access. Global Change Biology, 26(1), 119-188. doi:10.1111/gcb.14904.
* The iNaturalist project: [https://www.inaturalist.org/](url)

----------------------
# Evaluation

The models will be evaluated against the independent test data. The evaluation metric for this competition is the mean [R2](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coefficient_of_determination) over all 33 traits. The R2 is commonly used for evaluating regression models and is the ratio of the sum of squares the residuals (SSres) to the total sum of squares (SStot).

The R2 can result in large negative values. To prevent that the , we will only consider R2 values &gt; 0.

The submission should include a `.csv` file with a prediction for each trait and the following columns: `uniqID` (see labels), `pic_name `(see labels) and a prediction for each trait (`trait_6`, `trait_11`, ...). An example is given with `sample_submission.csv`


Note that the Kaggle-internal evaluation currently only supports column-wise spearman  rho and not R2. Thus, the leaderboard will for the time being report Spearman's rho, while the eventual results will be based on R2 calculated from the submission.","# Dataset Description

## Files

*   **01_data_train** - The folder with the training images (.jpg)
*   **data_train_mean.csv** - The labels and the ancillary data (climate data) for each training image (see folder **01_data_train**)
*   **01_data_test** - The folder with the test images (.jpg) that shall be used to create the predictions for the submission.
*   **data_test.csv** - The ancillary data (climate data) for each test image (see folder *01_data_test**)
*   **metadata_varnames_traits.csv** - full names of the traits obtained from the TRY database (see description)
*   **metadata_varnames_bio_clim.csv** - full names of the bioclimatic variables obtained from the WorldClim database (see description)
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

## Columns


*   `uniqID` - definition of example_id
*   `pic_name`?- definition of feature_1
*   `trait_6,` - These are the targets to be predicted. There are multiple traits (trait_6, trait_11, ...). The full names of the traits can be found in **metadata_varnames_traits.csv**
*   `bio_2`?- These are ancillary climate variables that can be used to faciliate the trait prediction. The full names of the traits can be found in **metadata_varnames_bio_clim.csv** 
*   `species` - The plant species as found in the training data. This is just some metadata for the people interested in botany. The test data does not contain this column to prevent that people will use the species to predict the traits. We are really aiming for models that *see* traits and not species.",False,True
6641,test-competition-sharif,"# Description

### Welcome to the 2023 edition of Kaggle's Playground Series!

Thank you to everyone who participated in and contributed to last year's Tabular Playground Series. And many thanks to all those who took the time to provide constructive feedback! We're thrilled that there continues to be interest in these types of challenges, and we're continuing the series this year but with a few changes.

First, the series is getting upgraded branding. We've dropped ""Tabular"" from the name because, while we anticipate this series will still have plenty of tabular competitions, we'll also be having some other formats as well. You'll also notice freshly-upgraded (better looking and more fun!) banner and thumbnail images. 

Second, rather than naming the challenges by month and year, we're moving to a Season-Edition format. This year is Season 3, and each challenge will be a new Edition. We're doing this to have more flexibility. Competitions going forward won't necessarily align with each month like they did in previous years (although some might!), we'll have competitions with different time durations, and we may have multiple competitions running at the same time on occasion.

Regardless of these changes, the goals of the Playground Series remain the same&mdash;to give the Kaggle community a variety of fairly light-weight challenges that can be used to learn and sharpen skills in different aspects of machine learning and data science. We hope we continue to meet this objective!

To start the year with some fun, January will be the month of Tabular Tuesday. We're launching four week-long tabular competitions, with each starting Tuesday 00:00 UTC. These will be fairly light-weight datasets that are synthetically generated from real-world data, and will provide an opportunity to quickly iterate through various model and feature engineering ideas, create visualizations, etc.


### Synthetically-Generated Datasets

Using synthetic data for Playground competitions allows us to strike a balance between having real-world data (with named features) and ensuring test labels are not publicly available. This allows us to host competitions with more interesting datasets than in the past. While there are still challenges with synthetic data generation, the state-of-the-art is much better now than when we started the Tabular Playground Series two years ago, and that goal is to produce datasets that have far fewer artifacts. Please feel free to give us feedback on the datasets for the different competitions so that we can continue to improve!

----------------------
# Evaluation

## Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE)
Submissions are scored on the root mean squared error. RMSE is defined as:

$$
\textrm{RMSE} =  \sqrt{ \frac{1}{N} \sum\_{i=1}^{N} (y\_i - \hat{y}\_i)^2 }
$$

where \\( \hat{y}\_i \\) is the predicted value and \\( y_i \\) is the original value for each instance \\(i\\).

## Submission File
For each `id` in the test set, you must predict the value for the target `MedHouseVal`. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    id,MedHouseVal
    37137,2.01
    37138,0.92
    37139,1.11
    etc.

----------------------
# Timeline

* **Start Date** - January 3, 2023
* **Entry Deadline** - Same as the Final Submission Deadline
* **Team Merger Deadline** - Same as the Final Submission Deadline
* **Final Submission Deadline** -  January 9, 2023

All deadlines are at 11:59 PM UTC on the corresponding day unless otherwise noted. The competition organizers reserve the right to update the contest timeline if they deem it necessary.
----------------------
# Prizes

* 1st Place - Choice of Kaggle merchandise
* 2nd Place - Choice of Kaggle merchandise
* 3rd Place - Choice of Kaggle merchandise

**Please note:** In order to encourage more participation from beginners, Kaggle merchandise will only be awarded once per person in this series. If a person has previously won, we'll skip to the next team.","# Dataset Description

The dataset for this competition (both train and test) was generated from a deep learning model trained on the [California Housing Dataset](https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/generated/sklearn.datasets.fetch_california_housing.html). Feature distributions are close to, but not exactly the same, as the original. Feel free to use the original dataset as part of this competition, both to explore differences as well as to see whether incorporating the original in training improves model performance.

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training dataset; `MedHouseVal` is the target
*   **test.csv** - the test dataset; your objective is to predict `MedHouseVal`
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format",False,True
6642,psychosis-classification-with-rsfmri,"# Description

Schizophrenia and bipolar disorder are among the top 20 leading causes of disability, demanding timely, accurate diagnosis to select the optimal treatment and reduce the overall burden of the disease. However, the misdiagnosis rate of current diagnostic procedures is high, which is rooted in the descriptive, symptom-based nature of current diagnostics, which do not use objective biomarkers. 
It is increasingly recognized that biomarkers that reflect underlying neurobiological mechanisms can improve early, accurate diagnosis. These biomarkers can facilitate the optimal treatment to reduce suicide risk, significant side effects of medication, and the overall burden of the disease. Such biomarkers can be achieved by leveraging resting-state functional magnetic resonance imaging (rsfMRI), which provides noninvasive and indirect measurements of neuronal activities. This competition aims to evaluate the ability of rsfMRI to develop valid neuroimaging diagnostic tools to differentiate schizophrenia (SZ) and bipolar psychosis (BPP) using a large dataset of individuals with fully developed symptoms that allowed near to perfect classification using DSM criteria.

**IMPORTANT:** This competition is associated with the IEEE Signal Processing Society's signal processing cup. In order to be eligible to compete in SP Cup, you MUST register using the link below and submit a PDF file explaining your work (e.g., processing steps and model) and a zip file containing your codes and the CSV file of your soft classification score. This code and soft classification file **MUST** be the same code and file used for your final Kaggle submission. Please also hand-select your final submission and avoid Kaggle's auto-select.

SP Cup registration: [https://signalprocessingsociety.org/community-involvement/signal-processing-cup](url)

***Please read the criteria for forming teams under the ""rules"" page.***

----------------------
# Evaluation

The problem proposed is a classification task of SZ versus BPP. The dataset is split into training and test sets. The entire dataset with the label of the public train set will be available to the teams. Each team is allowed up to 5 public submissions per day, and 1 private submission. For each submission, the team has to submit soft classification scores for the test set and will receive feedback about AUC on the public test set only. Teams can select 1 submission that will count toward their final leaderboard score. Please hand-select 1 final submission, as opposed to allowing Kaggle to auto-select. This hand selected submission should be what you submit to the SPS submission page.

*Please read ""Description"" page for important requirements?to be eligible?for the SP Cup competition*

**Submission File**
Your submission should be a CSV file with 315 rows and a header. The ID column should contain subject folder names in the test set (e.g., sub001) and the Predicted column should contain **soft classification scores (0~1)**, 0 for schizophrenia (SZ) and 1 for Bipolar (BP). 

*Example)*
![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F13647203%2Fd4a4e60f0d50acb7fd48b0b690f8fdae%2Fexample_prediction.png?generation=1676476195275754&alt=media)
----------------------
# Additional Information About Data

The data for this competition consists of features extracted from the rsfMRI data of individuals with Schizophrenia and Bipolar with psychosis. We used one hundred fifteen intrinsic connectivity network (ICN) timecourses derived from a multi-spatial-scale spatially constrained ICA approach and their functional network connectivity (FNC). The analysis steps to extract these features are as follows. (1) Quality control was applied to identify high-quality data. (2) Each subject's rsfMRI data were preprocessed using a common procedure, including rigid body motion correction, slice timing correction, and distortion correction. (3) Preprocessed subject data were registered into a common space, resampled to 3 mm3 isotropic voxels, and spatially smoothed using a Gaussian kernel with a 6 mm full width at half-maximum (FWHM). (4) We used a multi-spatial-scale template of 115 ICNs obtained from 100k+ subjects and a constraint ICA approach to obtain subject-specific ICNs time course.?To calculate FNC, ICN time courses were cleaned using a common standard and FNC is estimated by calculating the Pearson correlation between each pair of ICN time courses resulting in one FNC matrix for each individual.
----------------------
# Prizes

Kaggle will not pay any prizes. See the BSI website (https://brainspaceinitiative.github.io/competition/) for additional details.
----------------------
# Important Dates

Submission deadline: March 17, 2023 (03/17/2023 11:59 PM UTC.)
Finalists Announcement: March 31, 2023
Final @ICASSP: June 4-10, 2023

----------------------
# Acknowledgement

We acknowledge and thank the [IEEE-SPS](https://signalprocessingsociety.org/), [TReNDS Center](https://trendscenter.org/), and the [Computer Science department](https://csds.gsu.edu/) of Georgia State University for their support!


![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F13647203%2Ff71ecc38a2aca831a0756da364733aa9%2FPicture1.png?generation=1676740435106629&alt=media)","# Dataset Description

The dataset for this competition consists of rsfMRI from individuals with schizophrenia and bipolar psychosis. The data for each subject is stored in one folder (e.g., sub001). Each folder consists of one hundred and five intrinsic connectivity network (ICN) timecourses (icn_tc.mat and icn_tc.npy) and their functional network connectivity (fnc.mat and fnc.npy). The shape of time course files (icn_tc.mat and icn_tc.npy) is (T,105), where T is the number of time points which is different between subject. The functional network connectivity files are vectors of 5460 values. The shape of fnc.mat file is (5460, 105), and the shape of fnc.npy file is (5460, )
Additional information about the data: We used one hundred five intrinsic connectivity network (ICN) timecourses derived from a multi-spatial-scale spatially constrained ICA approach and their functional network connectivity (FNC). The analysis steps to extract these features are as follows. (1) Quality control was applied to identify high-quality data. (2) Each subject's rsfMRI data were preprocessed using a common procedure, including rigid body motion correction, slice timing correction, and distortion correction. (3) Preprocessed subject data were registered into a common space, resampled to 3 mm3 isotropic voxels, and spatially smoothed using a Gaussian kernel with a 6 mm full width at half-maximum (FWHM). (4) We used a multi-spatial-scale template of 105 ICNs obtained from 100k+ subjects and a constraint ICA approach to obtain subject-specific ICNs time course. To calculate FNC, ICN time courses were cleaned using a common standard and FNC is estimated by calculating the Pearson correlation between each pair of ICN time courses resulting in one FNC matrix for each individual.  

For the binarized labeling, use 0 for schizophrenia (SZ) and 1 for Bipolar (BP).

<h2> <b> Files </b> </h2>

<h3> <b> train </b>  </h3>
The train directory consists of 183 BP and 288 SZ folders

<h3> <b> test </b>  </h3>
The test directory consists of 315 folders which we need to classify into either BP or SZ",False,True
6643,imlclassification,"# Description

<p>El proyecto consiste en predecir los diferentes perfiles de sus clientes de tarjetas de cr¨¦dito,
con el prop¨®sito de poder implementar las pol¨ªticas de mitigaci¨®n del riesgo apropiadas para el
producto. En este orden de ideas, el inter¨¦s principal del proyecto es poder modelar la probabilidad
de que un cliente entre en default (imposibilidad para pagar la deuda adquirida), teniendo en
cuenta su informaci¨®n financiera, comportamiento crediticio e informaci¨®n demogr¨¢fica. El banco
cuenta con la siguiente informaci¨®n para sus clientes del a?o 2017 La variable de respuesta es
default.payment.next.month, que es igual a 1 si existe default y 0 de lo contrario.</p>
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is AUC. The AUC is a commonly used evaluation metric for binary problems like this one. The interpretation is that given a random positive observation and negative observation, the AUC gives the proportion of the time you guess which is which correctly. It is less affected by sample balance than accuracy. A perfect model will score an AUC of 1, while random guessing will score an AUC of around of 0.5.</p>
<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p><strong>For every observation in the dataset, submission files should contain two columns: Id and Probability. The submission should be a csv file. The Id should just be the corresponding observation Id column from the dataset. The Probability column should be the predicted probability of the outcome 1 for that observation Id.

The file should contain a header and have the following format:.</strong></p><strong>
<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>Id,Probability<br>1,0.8<br></pre></strong>","# Dataset Description

<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Train.xlsx</strong> - the training set</li>
<li><strong>Test.xlsx</strong> - the test set</li>
<li><strong>DataDes(1).txt</strong> - supplemental information about the data</li>",False,True
