6660,neymark-signal-processing,"# Description

Welcome to the Neymark-2023 Signal Processing challenge!

You will be finding the heartbeats in the PPG (photoplethysmographic) signals from the smartwatches. The PPG device emits light into the human wrist and detects the pulse wave by measuring the reflected light intensity.

A PPG signal is a sequence of data points, each associated with a timestamp. Each data point represents the intensity of the light detected by a light sensor inside the PPG device.

The amount of reflected light heavily depends on the amount of oxyhemoglobin in the human wrist. Correspondingly, the PPG signal has peaks at each heartbeat, as the pulse wave carries a lot of oxyhemoglobin.

However, that signal is quite noisy. The accuracy of peak detection might be majorly increased by providing different signal processing techniques to denoise that signal, proposing a good peaks search solution and so on. 

Any approach and method you find suitable will do!

Good luck!
----------------------
# Evaluation

We aim to provide the correct interbeat intervals to represent the heartbeat dynamics as precisely as possible. An interbeat interval is a distance between the neighbouring heartbeats in seconds.

To measure the PPG interbeat intervals' correctness, we compare it with ground truth ECG heartbeats in the following manner. First, we compute the PPG peaks that represent the heartbeats. Secondly, we compute the distance between them by taking a derivative. At last, we interpolate these interbeat interval values on some evenly discrete timeline to ensure proper mapping between the heartbeat sets.

Please see the **Sample_Submission1** notebook to check the exact procedure.

The scheme of this technique is presented below.
![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F1659314%2F1247fb6ca67261a616181474145f1a89%2Fphoto_2023-02-21_02-30-35.jpg?generation=1676932260691561&amp;alt=media)

The competition metric is the RMSE between the lines *""Interp interbeat intervals (PPG)""* and *""Interp interbeat intervals (ECG)""* on the picture above.","# Dataset Description

The data is the signal from the PPG device.

The ground truth is the heartbeats detected by the ECG device.

The data is split by the experiments. An experiment is a continuous sequence of the ECG and PPG data. Typically, the experiment participant performs some routine task (e.g. playing a video game). Each experiment participant is unique and the participants within the train and the test sets do not intersect.

## Directories

**watch_data_train/** - the train set with both PPG and ECG available. Split it on train\val however you want!

**watch_data_test/** - the test set, only PPG is available.

## Files

*   **PPG_EXP_*.csv** - the files with the PPG data for each experiment.
*   **ECG_EXP_*.csv** - the ECG data for each experiment.
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

## Columns

*   `PPG_signal` - raw signal from the PPG device
*   `Timestamp`?- a timestamp, corresponding to each sample of the PPG signal
*   `ECG_peaks` - a timestamp of the ECG heartbeat",False,True
