6664,how-you-feel-fellas,"# Description

**Facial Expression Recognition**

It is believed that a human has at least 8 basics emotions, where sadness and happiness are the ones the people usually talk more about. For the human being is kind of an easy job to find out how a person is feeling by the expression on their face. But this time you should train a Neural Network, using the layers and params you may find necessaries in order to get the highest accuracy, differentiating between sad and happy people.

You're right, people can feel more than one emotion at a time but, let's forget about it for a sec and try to simplify the problem.

The pictures are already in black and white, have a 48x48 size and have the faces cropped, what should makes everything a little easier. However, feel free to make any changes to the pictures, but remember to make the same kind of treatment to the train set of images and the test set of images, just before you make the predictions.

Now you have tools as OpenCV, NN and CNN to improve your last competition.

Are you happy? Are you sad? Are you mad or wanna kill someone?
----------------------
# Evaluation

### Evaluation Your submissions will be scored as the percentage of correct predictions. 

**Categorization accuracy??** 
Submit results The test set is divided into a public part (with which the public leaderboard is calculated, accessible during the competition) and another private part, with which the final positions are calculated, after the end of the competition. 

Predictions will be sent in the format indicated in the sample_submission.csv file in the data section.","# Dataset Description

**Data**
In this section you will find data needed to train your NN and make the best prediction.

**Files**
*sample_submission.csv:* your submission should be something like this, same columns, same shape, same id, different emotions ??
*train:* this directory contains the images to feed your model.
*train_set.csv:* on this file you'll find the labels related to the path where the pictures are.
*test:* this directory contains the pictures which you have to predict the emotions of.
*test_set.csv:* this file should help you to make the predictions and prepare your submission file.",False,True
6665,se-masterclass,"# Description

<h3>????? Ahoy, welcome to Kaggle! You¡¯re in the right place. </h3>
<p>This is the legendary SE Masterclass Titanic ML competition ¨C the best, first challenge for you to dive into ML competitions.</p>
<p>The competition is simple: use machine learning to create a model that predicts which passengers survived the Titanic shipwreck.
</p>
<p>Read on to explore more details. Once you¡¯re ready to start competing, click on the <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/account/login?returnUrl=%2Fc%2Ftitanic"" target=""_blank"">""Join Competition button</a> to create an account and gain access to the <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/c/titanic/data"" target=""_blank"">competition data</a>. 
<h3>The Challenge</h3>
<p>The sinking of the Titanic is one of the most infamous shipwrecks in history.</p>
<p>On April 15, 1912, during her maiden voyage, the widely considered ¡°unsinkable¡± RMS Titanic sank after colliding with an iceberg. Unfortunately, there weren¡¯t enough lifeboats for everyone onboard, resulting in the death of 1502 out of 2224 passengers and crew.</p>
<p>While there was some element of luck involved in surviving, it seems some groups of people were more likely to survive than others.</p>
<p>In this challenge, we ask you to build a predictive model that answers the question: ¡°what sorts of people were more likely to survive?¡± using passenger data (ie name, age, gender, socio-economic class, etc).
</p>

<h3>Overview of How Kaggle¡¯s Competitions Work</h3>
<p><b>1. Join the Competition</b></p>
Read about the challenge description, accept the Competition Rules and gain access to the competition dataset.<p></p>
<p><b>2. Get to Work</b></p>
Download the data, build models on it locally and generate a prediction file.<p></p>
<p><b>3. Make a Submission</b></p>
Upload your prediction as a submission on Kaggle and receive an accuracy score.<p></p>
<p><b>4. Check the Leaderboard</b></p>
See how your model ranks against other Kagglers on our leaderboard. <p></p>
<p><b>5. Improve Your Score</b></p>

<h3>What Data Will I Use in This Competition?</h3>
<p>In this competition, you¡¯ll gain access to two similar datasets that include passenger information like name, age, gender, socio-economic class, etc. One dataset is titled `train.csv` and the other is titled `test.csv`.</p>
<p>Train.csv will contain the details of a subset of the passengers on board (891 to be exact) and importantly, will reveal whether they survived or not, also known as the ¡°ground truth¡±.</p>
<p>The `test.csv` dataset contains similar information but does not disclose the ¡°ground truth¡± for each passenger. It¡¯s your job to predict these outcomes.</p>
<p>Using the patterns you find in the train.csv data, predict whether the other 418 passengers on board (found in test.csv) survived. </p>
<p>Check out the <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/c/titanic/data"">¡°Data¡± tab</a> to explore the datasets even further. Once you feel you¡¯ve created a competitive model, submit it to Kaggle to see where your model stands on our leaderboard against other Kagglers.</p>
<h3>How to Submit your Prediction to Kaggle</h3>
<p>Once you¡¯re ready to make a submission and get on the leaderboard:</p>
<p>1. Click on the ¡°Submit Predictions¡± button</p>
<img width=""699"" align=""center"" src=""https://storage.googleapis.com/kaggle-media/welcome/screen1.png"">
<p>2. Upload a CSV file in the submission file format. You¡¯re able to submit 10 submissions a day.</p>
<img width=""699"" align=""center"" src=""https://storage.googleapis.com/kaggle-media/welcome/screen2.png"">
<p><b>Submission File Format:</b></p>
<p>You should submit a csv file with exactly 418 entries plus a header row. Your submission will show an error if you have extra columns (beyond PassengerId and Survived) or rows.</p>
<p>The file should have exactly 2 columns:</p>
<ul>
<li>PassengerId (sorted in any order)</li>
<li>Survived (contains your binary predictions: 1 for survived, 0 for deceased)</li>
</ul>


<h3>???¡âReady to Compete? <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/account/login?returnUrl=%2Fc%2Ftitanic"">Join the Competition Here!</a>
</h3>
----------------------
# Evaluation

<h3>Goal</h3>
<p>It is your job to predict if a passenger survived the sinking of the Titanic or not. <br>For each  in the test set, you must predict a 0 or 1 value for the  variable.</p>
<h3>Metric</h3>
<p>Your score is the percentage of passengers you correctly predict. This is known as <a href=""https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Accuracy_and_precision#In_binary_classification"" target=""_blank"">accuracy</a>.</p>
<h3>Submission File Format</h3>
<p>You should submit a csv file with exactly 418 entries <b>plus</b> a header row. Your submission will show an error if you have extra columns (beyond PassengerId and Survived) or rows.<br><br>The file should have exactly 2 columns:</p>
<ul>
<li>PassengerId (sorted in any order)</li>
<li>Survived (contains your binary predictions: 1 for survived, 0 for deceased)</li>
</ul>
<pre><b>PassengerId,Survived</b><br>892,0<br>893,1<br>894,0<br>Etc.</pre>
<p>You can download an example submission file (gender_submission.csv) on the <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/c/titanic/data"">Data page</a>.</p>","# Dataset Description

<h3>Overview</h3>
<p>The data has been split into two groups:</p>
<ul>
<li>training set (train.csv)</li>
<li>test set (test.csv)</li>
</ul>
<p><b> The training set </b>should be used to build your machine learning models. For the training set, we provide the outcome (also known as the ¡°ground truth¡±) for each passenger. Your model will be based on ¡°features¡± like passengers¡¯ gender and class. You can also use <a href=""https://triangleinequality.wordpress.com/2013/09/08/basic-feature-engineering-with-the-titanic-data/"" target=""_blank""> feature engineering </a>to create new features.</p>
<p><b>The test set </b>should be used to see how well your model performs on unseen data. For the test set, we do not provide the ground truth for each passenger. It is your job to predict these outcomes. For each passenger in the test set, use the model you trained to predict whether or not they survived the sinking of the Titanic.</p>
<p>We also include <b>gender_submission.csv</b>, a set of predictions that assume all and only female passengers survive, as an example of what a submission file should look like.</p>
<h3>Data Dictionary</h3>
<table style=""width: 100%;"">
<tbody>
<tr><th><b>Variable</b></th><th><b>Definition</b></th><th><b>Key</b></th></tr>
<tr>
<td>survival</td>
<td>Survival</td>
<td>0 = No, 1 = Yes</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>pclass</td>
<td>Ticket class</td>
<td>1 = 1st, 2 = 2nd, 3 = 3rd</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>sex</td>
<td>Sex</td>
<td></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Age</td>
<td>Age in years</td>
<td></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>sibsp</td>
<td># of siblings / spouses aboard the Titanic</td>
<td></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>parch</td>
<td># of parents / children aboard the Titanic</td>
<td></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>ticket</td>
<td>Ticket number</td>
<td></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>fare</td>
<td>Passenger fare</td>
<td></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>cabin</td>
<td>Cabin number</td>
<td></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>embarked</td>
<td>Port of Embarkation</td>
<td>C = Cherbourg, Q = Queenstown, S = Southampton</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3>Variable Notes</h3>
<p><b>pclass</b>: A proxy for socio-economic status (SES)<br /> 1st = Upper<br /> 2nd = Middle<br /> 3rd = Lower<br /><br /> <b>age</b>: Age is fractional if less than 1. If the age is estimated, is it in the form of xx.5<br /><br /> <b>sibsp</b>: The dataset defines family relations in this way...<br /> Sibling = brother, sister, stepbrother, stepsister<br /> Spouse = husband, wife (mistresses and fianc¨¦s were ignored)<br /><br /> <b>parch</b>: The dataset defines family relations in this way...<br /> Parent = mother, father<br /> Child = daughter, son, stepdaughter, stepson<br /> Some children travelled only with a nanny, therefore parch=0 for them.</p>",False,True
6666,runi-nlp-2023,"# Description

# Title:  RUN NLP 2023 course competition

**Overview:**
Welcome to the RUN NLP 2023 course competition!
This competition is designed to help you learn how to apply natural language processing (NLP) techniques and language models (LM) to validate how similar a pair of texts are. We invite you to compete and challenge yourself.

**Goal**
Text Similarity is the process of comparing a piece of text with another and finding the similarity between them. It¡¯s basically about determining the degree of closeness of the text. Text Similarity can be used for a variety of purposes, including search engines, summarization, essay scoring, plagiarism detection, machine translation, and more. Text similarity algorithms can vary greatly depending on the type of text and the purpose it is being used for.  
Generally, text similarity algorithms will compare two texts by looking at the words and phrases used in each of the texts, looking at the phrase or sentence structure, or examining the differences between the two documents in terms of context. The algorithms may also consider other factors, such as the length of the texts, the number of words, or the number of sentences. Additionally, the NLP algorithms use semantic information to determine the degree of similarity between two texts. By using a text similarity algorithm, users can quickly and accurately determine the degree of similarity between two texts.  
And this is what you'll try to achieve in this competition :) 

**Instructions:**
1. Register for the competition by clicking the Register button at the top of the page.
2. Download the data and read the task description.
3. Develop your NLP LM model.
4. Submit your answer.
4. Track the progress of your submission on the Leaderboard.
5. After the competition ends, participants with the top scores will be rewarded with certificates of recognition. 

We wish you the best of luck in this competition!

~~~~

## Acknowledgements
Special thanks to Dr. Kfir Bar, Mr. Amir Cohen, and Mr. Sahar Millis
----------------------
# Evaluation

Your final submission will include a classification of each pair as similar or dissimilar.  

```
- Label 0: dissimilar  
- Label 1: similar  
```

You will be scored based on **accuracy**.  
----------------------
# Tips

#### Here are some tips to get started:
1. Kaggle notebook and competitions are a great way to show off your talent.
2. Start with an explanatory analysis of your dataset. Look at other Kaggle users for inspiration and ideas.
3. Start modeling with a nice and easy baseline - majority class, TF-IDF, etc. - only then move forward to DL models and LM.

Good luck.","# Dataset Description

Please review this information regarding your data.

### Your dataset includes 3 files:
*   **train.csv** - the training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

Please use the training file as you see fit (pun intended).
#### Columns
*   `id` - the data sample ID
*   `text1`?- the 1st sentence 
*   `text2`?- the 2nd sentence
*   `similarity`?- a similarity score (between 0-1)that answers the question ""how similar are the pair"". A low similarity score, means it's dissimilar. A high similarity score means it's similar.

~~~~

Please use the test file only for inference and submission. Also, ensure that the indices remain unchanged.
You have an example of a submission file - you need only to submit the ID of the pair, with a similarity score of 0 or 1.
####  Columns
*   `Id` - the pair ID.
*   `Category`?- the similarity score 0 or 1.",False,True
6667,ml-olympiad-can-you-guess-beer-style,"# Description

## What is ML Olympiad?

An associated Kaggle Community Competitions hosted by Google ML communities, supported by Google Developers. (ML GDEs, TFUGs, or 3rd party ML communities host ML community competitions on Kaggleand ML Developer Programs team sends rewards to hosts of registered competitions.)

## ML Competition

This is a machine learning competition focused on classifying beer into 17 distinct styles based on key descriptors. Participants will be provided with a dataset containing information such as IBU (International Bitterness Units), ABV (Alcohol By Volume), as well as professional ratings and descriptions. The goal is to develop a model that can accurately categorize beer into one of the 17 styles, based on the provided descriptors. The competition is open to all machine learning enthusiasts, and offers a unique opportunity to put your skills to the test and showcase your ability to tackle a complex classification problem.


## Acknowledgements

The dataset used in this competition is based on the dataset from [Beer Profile And Rating Datsets](https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/ruthgn/beer-profile-and-ratings-data-set)

We would like to thank Google for the invite and the support provided while hosting and promoting this competition

 #MLOlympiad #AppliedML
----------------------
# Evaluation

## Goal
Predict beer style.

## Metric
F-Score (Macro)

## Submission File Format
You should submit a csv file with exactly 58 entries plus a header row. Your submission will show an error if you have extra columns or rows.

The file should have exactly 2 columns:

- ID (beer id)
- Style_id (beer style)","# Dataset Description

## Dataset Information

This dataset contains tasting profiles and consumer reviews for 403 unique beers from 281 different breweries. It was created by integrating information from two existing data sets on Kaggle: Beer Tasting Profiles Dataset and 1.5 Million Beer Reviews.

The training set should be used to build your machine learning models. For the training set, we provide the outcome (also known as the ¡°ground truth¡±) for each beer. Your model will be based on beer's features. You can also use feature engineering to create new features.

The test set should be used to see how well your model performs on unseen data. For the test set, we do not provide the ground truth for each beer. It is your job to predict these outcomes. For each beer in the test set, use the model you trained to predict its style.

We also include a sample submission file named sample_submission.csv, a set of predictions as an example of what a submission file should look like.

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the dataset used to train model.
*   **test.csv** - the dataset used to evaluate the model.
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format.

## Columns

* ID: A unique ID that identifies the beer
* Name:  Beer name
* Brewery:  Brewery name
* Beer Name (Full): Complete beer name (Brewery + Brew Name) 
* Description: Notes on the beer if available
* ABV: Alcohol content of beer (% by volume)
* Min IBU: The minimum IBU value each beer can possess
* Max IBU: The maximum IBU value each beer can possess

* The next eleven columns represent taste and aroma descriptors taken from up to 25 reviews of each beer.
* Astringency
* Body
* Alcohol
* Bitter
* Sweet
* Sour
* Salty
* Fruits
* Hoppy
* Spices
* Malty

The last six columns refer to information regarding beer reviews:

* review _ aroma: The average rating for aroma
* review _ appearance: The average rating for appearance
* review _ palate: The average rating for mouthfeel
* review _ taste: The average rating for taste
* review _ overall: The overall average rating of the beer
* number _ of _ reviews: The number of reviews taken into consideration",False,True
6668,iuss-mlh-2023,"# Description

This academic competition has the aim to accopmany students through the *Machine Learning in Healthcare* course at the University School for Advanced Studies IUSS Pavia.

The target of the copmetition is to implement a model able to automatically discriminate *malignant* from *benign* lesions in breast ultrasound (US) studies.

The dataset and rules of submission are briefly described in the corresponding sections of this hosting page.
Here is a handy link to Kaggle's [competition documentation](https://www.kaggle.com/docs/competitions), which includes, among other things, instructions on [submitting predictions](https://www.kaggle.com/docs/competitions#making-a-submission).

Good luck and have fun!

------

**DEADLINE**: 05/10/2023 11:59 PM

**PRIVATE LEADERBOARD RELEASE DATE**: 05/11/2023 10:00 AM
----------------------
# Evaluation

User submissions are evaluated by comparing their Submission CSV to the ground truth Solution CSV with respect to the <a href=""https://it.wikipedia.org/wiki/Area_Under_the_Curve"" target=""blank"">Area Under the ROC Curve</a> (AUC) metric.
<br><br>
The Submission CSV file must contain a prediction score or probability for each classified study, corresponding to the probability of that lesion(s) to belong to class ""Malignant"" or ""Benign"".
<br><br>
It is expected that the probability to belong to class ""Benign"" ranges from 0 to 0.5, and that the probability to belong to class ""Malignant"" ranges from 0.5 to 1 (p &lt; 0.5 -&gt; ""Benign"", p &gt; 0.5 -&gt; ""Malignant"").","# Dataset Description

The training set contains two folders, corresponding to the ""benign"" and ""malignant"" classes of interest. This folder separation provides information regarding the label of each study (which is also the target of our predictions).

Each breast ultrasound study is referred to with a numerical *Id* (e.g. ""benign (25)"") and has a corresponding lesion segmentation (e.g. ""benign (25)_mask""). Each study has at least one corresponding segmentation file, containing the mask of a lesion.
However, there are studies with more than one segmented lesion, and each segmentation mask is reported in a different file (e.g. ""benign (25)_mask"" and ""benign (25)_mask_1""). In these cases, all the lesions belonging to the same study share the same class (e.g. both lesions of study ""benign (25)"" are benign). 
Studies and masks are provided in *png* format.",False,True
6669,ai-at-mit-and-weights-biases-blood-mnist-2023,"# Description

## Competition details

The details of this competition will be shared in a separate resource.

The BloodMNIST dataset is part of the wider MedMNIST dataset.

MedMNIST is a large-scale MNIST-like collection of standardized biomedical images, including twelve 2D datasets and six 3D datasets. All the images are pre-processed to the size of 28x28 pixels and working with the data requires no prior domain knowledge about medicine, genetics, biology, etc.

## BloodMNIST details

The BloodMNIST is based on a dataset of individual normal cells, captured from individuals without infection, hematologic or oncologic disease and free of any pharmacologic treatment at the moment of blood collection. It contains a total of 17,092 images and is organized into 8 classes (8 different classes of blood cell).


## Spoiler

If you'd like *a* solution to the proposed classification task we've included one that was authored by our colleagues Justin and Ayush here: [Integrating Keras with Weights and Biases](https://wandb.ai/ayush-thakur/medmnist-bloodmnist/reports/Integrating-Keras-with-Weights-Biases--VmlldzoxNDg1MDQy?utm_source=webinar&utm_medium=online&utm_campaign=windsor).

----------------------
# Evaluation

We'll be evaluating your prediction based on accuracy.","# Dataset Description

The dataset is in the Colab Notebook. You should use the Colab Notebook to generate a `submission.csv` file which you submit over here in Kaggle.",False,True
6670,ml-olympiad-bahrain-uob,"# Description

# Who is GDG Manama?

GDG Manama is a community-led group that is part of the global Google Developer Groups (GDG) network. The group is based in Manama, Bahrain, and is dedicated to promoting and supporting the local developer community.

GDG Manama hosts regular events, workshops, and meetups that bring together developers, entrepreneurs, and technology enthusiasts to share knowledge and expertise on a wide range of topics related to software development, cloud computing, machine learning, and more.

So if you are a developer, entrepreneur, or technology enthusiast based in Bahrain or the surrounding region, be sure to check out GDG Manama and join the community of like-minded individuals who are passionate about using technology to drive positive change in the world.

# Introduction

Welcome to the ML Olympiad competition hosted by GDG Manama on Kaggle! This competition is designed to challenge participants to build powerful machine-learning models that can accurately predict the outcomes of a given task.

The task for this competition will be announced at the start and will require participants to work with a large dataset to train and validate their models. Participants will need to leverage advanced machine learning techniques to build models that can accurately predict this task's outcomes while ensuring that their models are robust and can generalize to new data.

To ensure that the competition is fair and unbiased, participants will be given access to a dataset and will be required to submit their models for evaluation using a standardized evaluation metric. The top performers will be awarded prizes and recognition, and their models may even be used in real-world applications.

So if you are passionate about machine learning and are looking for a challenge, join the ML Olympiad competition hosted by GDG Manama and compete with other talented data scientists from around Bahrain!


# Participant Confirmation:


By participating in this competition, you agree to the following:

Abide by the competition rules and guidelines set forth by GDG Manama and Kaggle.
Only use the provided dataset and refrain from using external data sources or pre-trained models.
Use ethical and fair practices when building and training models, and avoid any form of data manipulation or bias.
Agree to have your models and submissions evaluated and potentially used in real-world applications.
Agree to have your name and affiliation published in the competition leaderboard and any related publicity materials.
Refrain from sharing your submission code with others or attempting to hack or compromise the competition platform in any way.
Accept that the decision of the competition judges is final and cannot be challenged or disputed.
Agree to indemnify and hold harmless GDG Manama, Kaggle, and any related sponsors from any claims, damages, or liabilities arising from your participation in the competition.


----------------------
# Evaluation

# Metric

The performance of the models in the ML Olympiad competition hosted by GDG Manama will be evaluated based on the Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE) metric.

The RMSE metric measures the average difference between the predicted and actual values, with lower values indicating better performance. In this competition, the RMSE will be calculated on a holdout set of data that is not used for training the models.

To ensure that the competition is fair and unbiased, participants will not have access to the holdout set during the competition. Instead, they will be required to submit their predictions for the holdout set to Kaggle, which will calculate the RMSE metric and generate a public leaderboard that ranks the models based on performance.

The top performers on the leaderboard will be awarded prizes and recognition, and their models may even be used in real-world applications to optimize solar energy generation. So if you are interested in competing in the ML Olympiad competition hosted by GDG Manama, start building and training your machine learning models now and see how they perform on the holdout set!","# Dataset Description

# Dataset Description
The dataset includes meteorological data collected by the HI-SEAS weather station for four months, specifically from September to December 2016, during Mission IV and Mission V. The data contains information related to solar radiation, temperature, humidity, barometric pressure, wind direction, wind speed, and sunrise/sunset timings, measured at regular intervals throughout the day.

## Files

*   **train_data.csv** - the training set
*   **val_data.csv** - the validation set
*   **test_data.csv** - the test set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

> **Note**: Make sure that your submission is in the same format to the sample submission

# Columns:

Row Number - This column represents a unique identifier assigned to each row of data. It is useful in sorting the results of this export with other exports.

UNIX Time_t Date - This column represents the date and time of the data point in Unix time format, which is the number of seconds that have elapsed since January 1, 1970. It is also useful in sorting the results of this export with other exports.

Numeric Data - This column contains the numeric measurement recorded for each variable. It may be an empty string if no data was recorded at that time.

Text Data - This column contains any text data associated with the recorded measurement. It may also be an empty string if no text data was recorded at that time.

Solar Radiation - This column represents the intensity of solar radiation measured in watts per meter squared.

Temperature - This column represents the temperature measured in degrees Fahrenheit.

Humidity - This column represents the percentage of relative humidity measured.

Barometric Pressure - This column represents the barometric pressure measured in Hg (mercury).

Wind Direction - This column represents the direction from which the wind is blowing, measured in degrees.

Wind Speed - This column represents the speed of the wind measured in miles per hour.

Sunrise/Sunset - This column represents the time of sunrise and sunset in Hawaii time.




> Disclaimer:
>The data provided for the competition is for research and educational purposes only. Any commercial use or redistribution of the data is prohibited without explicit permission from the data owners. The organizers of the competition are not responsible for the accuracy or completeness of the data provided, and participants are responsible for ensuring that their models are built and trained using ethical and fair practices.",False,True
6671,hackapucsp2023-enem,"# Description

## Objetivo da Competi??o


O desafio nesse Hackathon ¨¦ responder a pergunta a seguir: 

