6708,kcl-artificial-intelligence-competition-22-23,"# Description

Hello KCL! ??

Welcome to the first yearly King's AI Competition, hosted by the KCL AI Society.

## The Objective

Your task is to build an AI model that performs **music emotion recognition**, tagging songs into one of four emotions: 

Happy, sad, angry or chill.

We have made this challenging on purpose, to model how real-world engineering and start-up problems rarely have full and complete data available. 

You may enter in teams of up to four. 

And the **winning team** will each receive a prize and the honour of building the best AI model. 

Good luck! ??

Any questions, please message @kclaisociety on Instagram.

## The Rationale
Music emotion recognition presents an opportunity to improve personalised music recommendations while you listen and allowing for targeted adverts to play to user's at appropriate times. Furthermore, it could also be used for creating mood-based playlists and enhancing music discovery on platforms like Spotify.

Here is a handy link to Kaggle's [competition documentation](https://www.kaggle.com/docs/competitions), which includes, among other things, instructions on [submitting predictions](https://www.kaggle.com/docs/competitions#making-a-submission).

## Acknowledgements

We thank Professor Plum, Ph.D. for providing this dataset and the Spotify API.


----------------------
# Submission

## Submission Format
Our test_set.csv is provided for you WITHOUT the emotion labels. Use your model to predict these songs. Then, you must submit a csv with only 2 columns: the track 'id' and the corresponding emotion 'emotion_tag' (Happy, Angry, Sad, Chill). 

Download ""submission_example.csv"" to see what your submission file should be like

In the description of the submission, please include the names and K Numbers of every team member. Without this, your submission will not be counted!
----------------------
# FAQs

##Who can participate?
The competition is open to all KCL students who have an interest in AI and machine learning. You don't need to have any prior experience in the field to participate.
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is category accuracy.

Category accuracy is a performance metric for multi-class classification models that measures the percentage of correctly classified examples within each category. 

It is calculated by dividing the number of correctly classified examples in a category by the total number of examples in that category, and then averaging these values across all categories.","# Dataset Description

You are given a set of 2800 Spotify tracks, with 20% of them given an emotion tag. Your task is when given an unknown song, to design an algorithm to be able to guess what emotion out of the four: **happy, sad, angry and chill** best corresponds to the track.

We will be using categorisation accuracy (the % of correct tags on new music) to judge.

******What files do I need and why?******
There are 4 files.

-train_set.csv - to train your model. 
-val_set.csv - help with optimising your model.
-test_set_no_labels.csv - for submission of your emotion tags
-example_submission.csv - to see how to upload a successful submission csv to Kaggle.

******What should I expect the data format to be?******
CSV

******What am I predicting?******
The emotion tag of a given song. 

## Files

*   **train_set.csv** - the training set - semi-labelled training data 20% of the data is labelled.
*   **val_set.csv** - the validation set - 100% labelled data that is not in train_set.csv.
*   **test_set_no_labels.csv** - the private set that we will use to judge your AI model, but 100% unlabelled. You will give this to your AI to find the accompanying music emotion.
*   **example_submission.csv** - An example submission file. 

## Columns

*   `acousticness` - A measure of the degree to which a song is acoustic (as opposed to electronic). 
*   `danceability` - A measure of how suitable a song is for dancing, based on a combination of factors such as tempo, rhythm, and beat strength.
*   `energy` - A measure of the intensity and activity level of a song, based on factors such as loudness, speed, and timbre.
*   `instrumentalness` - A measure of the degree to which a song contains vocals (as opposed to purely instrumental music).
*   `key` - The musical key of the song, represented as an integer value from 0 to 11 (with 0 representing the key of C, and increasing integers representing other keys in a clockwise fashion).
*   `loudness` - A measure of the overall volume of a song, in decibels (dB).
*   `mode` - Indicates whether the song is in a major (mode = 1) or minor (mode = 0) key.
*   `speechiness` - A measure of the degree to which a song contains spoken words (as opposed to singing or purely instrumental music).
*   `tempo` - The tempo (or speed) of the song, in beats per minute (BPM).
*   `time_signature` - The time signature of the song, represented as an integer value indicating the number of beats per measure (e.g. 3, 4, 5, etc.).",False,True
