6713,111-2-ntut-dl-app-hw1,"# Description

<h2>Objective</h2>
<p>In this homework, we will train a model for classifying handwritten letters and digits in Extended-MNIST (EMNIST) dataset (<a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/crawford/emnist"">link</a>). 
The EMNIST include 52 letters (capital and small letters) and 10 digits. We will use the default test dataset (emnist-byclass-test) to evaluate your model.   </p>
<p>
You are free to use any neural network architecture, any DL library (PyTorch, TensorFlow, ...), or even any language. This is just a warm-up homework so don't worry about the accuracy too much. As long as your accuracy is better than random guess, you will get the credit.
</p>
<p>
<b>Please register a Kaggle account starting with your student ID, so we can recognize you. </b>
(Í¬ŒW‚ƒµÄKaggleŽ¤Ì–ÕˆÓÃŒWÌ–®”Ž¤Ì–Ãû·Qé_î^, ÒÔ±ãµÇÓ›·Ö”µ•r±æ×R.)
</p>
<h2>Input Data</h2>
<p>We've converted the EMNIST binary data to NumPy npz format. The code below shows how to load training data:</p>
<pre>import numpy as np
# Load training data
data = np.load('emnist-byclass-train.npz')
train_labels = data['training_labels']
train_data = data['training_images']

# Load testing data
test_data = np.load('emnist-byclass-test.npz')['testing_images']
</pre>
<p>You are free to download the original EMNIST from its official website. However <b>DON'T USE ByClass Testing Images for Training!</b>
The statistics of the EMNIST is shown in the table below: </p>
![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F1978103%2F22d9375e2ccf3684eb2d2732314f57ec%2Femnist_stats.png?generation=1569073009753035&amp;alt=media)

<p>The <b>By_Class</b> split includes 62 classes comprising [0-9], [a-z] and [A-Z], while the <b>By_Merge</b> split has merged the uppercase and lowercase letters of C, I, J, K, L, M, O, P, S, U,  V, W, X, Y and Z, and include only 47 classes. </p>
<br>

<h2>Output Format</h2>
<p>A submission file of prediction results should be a CSV file containing two columns: Id and Category, separated by ','. </p>
<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>Id,Category
0,15
1,7
2,30
3,22
...
</pre>

We will use Category Accuracy as the metric. There are 62 classes (0 ~ 9 &amp; A ~ Z &amp; a ~z) in ByClass split of EMNIST dataset, which are labeled sequentially as 0, 1, 2, ..., 61.

<h2>Kaggel Notebook Example</h2>
You can refer to the notebook to see how to train a model on Kaggle: (Code -&gt; Notebook)
<a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/kuantinglai/110-2-dl-app-hw1"">https://www.kaggle.com/kuantinglai/110-2-dl-app-hw1</a>

<h2>References</h2>
<ol>
<li><a href=""https://www.nist.gov/itl/iad/image-group/emnist-dataset"">EMNIST Official Website</a></li>
<li><a href=""https://arxiv.org/abs/1702.05373v1"">EMNIST paper</a></li>
</ol>","# Dataset Description

<h2> Data Format </h2>
The training and testing images are 28x28 gray images.  There are 62 classes (0 ~ 9 & A ~ Z & a ~z) in EMNIST dataset, which are labeled sequentially as 0, 1, 2, ..., 61. The original data are saved in both binary and MATLAB format. We've converted it into NumPy format so you can easily load the data.

<h3>?? <b><i>Note! the data have been uploaded to Kaggle, which is called ""NTUTEMNIST"". </i></b></h3>
[www.kaggle.com/darkfanxing/ntutemnist](https://www.kaggle.com/darkfanxing/ntutemnist)
<h3><b><i>You can add the data directly in Python notebook. Our example code already included the data.</i></b></h3>
 [how to add dataset to your notebook.](https://ibb.co/HdNsLjq)

<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>emnist-byclass-train.npz</strong> - the training images and labels</li>
<li><strong>emnist-byclass-test.npz</strong> - the test images</li>
<li><strong>simple_nn_train.ipynb</strong> - A Jupyter notebook example of training a simple DNN model </li>
</ul>",False,True
6714,ml-olympiad-bahrain-rcsi,"# Description

# About RCSI Bahrain 

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F2106784%2F53434c537316389db04eac001bda0ba1%2FRCSI%20Logo.png?generation=1678463567465907&alt=media)

Established 20 years ago, The Royal College of Surgeons in Ireland- Bahrain is a constituent university of the 250-year-old RCSI. Like its Dublin based counterpart, RCSI Bahrain is a not-for-profit health sciences university incorporating schools of medicine, nursing, postgraduate studies, and research. Our primary aims are the enhancement of health in Bahrain, the GCC and beyond, and the development of healthcare practitioners who lead and contribute to the enhancement of healthcare systems worldwide.


## What is the competition about?
At RCSI Bahrain, our admissions process is underpinned by our values of equality and excellence. Prospective applicants to the various schools at the university undergo a rigorous, data-driven admissions process that aims to guarantee that the candidates admitted to the university demonstrate attributes conducive of them becoming successful future healthcare providers and leaders, and that they represent the best in their cohort. 
To support this process, the challenge we pose to the competition¡¯s participants is to develop a predictive model that utilizes the data collected from prospective applicants to predict their likelihood of graduating with Honors

## Participant Confirmation
By participating in this competition, you agree to the following:
1.	The data provided by RCSI Bahrain is to be used for the purposes of the competition only.
2.	RCSI Bahrain shall retain ownership of any data submitted by the participant.
3.	RCSI Bahrain has the right to cancel, terminate, modify, or suspend this competition at any time at its sole discretion. Additionally, RCSI Bahrain reserves the right to disqualify any participant who tampers with the submission process or any other part of the competition.
4.	Participants may not approach RCSI Bahrain to request more data beyond what is provided as part of the competition.
5.	Participants agree that the submission and any information gathered as a part of this competition may be used by RCSI Bahrain. 
6.	Participants agree that RCSI Bahrain, Kaggle and its affiliates may use the participants¡¯ names and likeness for advertising and promotional purposes without additional compensation.

----------------------
# Evaluation

# Metric

The performance of the models in the ML Olympiad competition hosted by GDG Manama will be evaluated based on the Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE) metric.

The RMSE metric measures the average difference between the predicted and actual values, with lower values indicating better performance. In this competition, the RMSE will be calculated on a holdout set of data that is not used for training the models.

To ensure that the competition is fair and unbiased, participants will not have access to the holdout set during the competition. Instead, they will be required to submit their predictions for the holdout set to Kaggle, which will calculate the RMSE metric and generate a public leaderboard that ranks the models based on performance.

The top performers on the leaderboard will be awarded prizes and recognition, and their models may even be used in real-world applications to optimize solar energy generation. So if you are interested in competing in the ML Olympiad competition hosted by GDG Manama, start building and training your machine learning models now and see how they perform on the holdout set!","# Dataset Description

Dataset Description
This synthetic, structured dataset contains numerical and categorical data columns that summarize some of the academic and personal attributes of prospective applicants and whether they graduated with Honors or not. The data is divided into Training, Testing and Cross Validation sets representing 75%, 15% and 10% of the available data respectively. 
Disclaimer: The synthetic dataset does not represent RCSI Bahrain data in any shape or form and is to be used solely for this competition



## Rows
Each row represents an individual candidate

## Columns
| Column                                | Description                                                                               |
|---------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| Age at admission                  | The candidate¡¯s age at the time of admission to the university                                    |
| Most recent qualification at admission| highest attained level of education the candidate holds at the time of their application                                  |
| High School Qualification             |  The secondary education system from which the candidate graduated                                  |
| Studied Biology in High School/University?| This column describes whether a candidate has studied biology in secondary school                    |
| Studied Chemistry in High School/University| This column describes whether a candidate has studied chemistry in secondary school                    |
| High School GPA                        | This column specifies the Grade Point Average (GPA) attained by the candidate upon completion of their secondary education (Range: 0.0- 4.0)                                      |
| Admissions Interview Score             | This  column describes the score received by a candidate based on their admissions interview. Scores range from 0 to 9; with a minimum of 5 being required for consideration of admission. The score is awarded based on the candidate¡¯s responses to a set of standardized interview questions.        |
| English Proficiency                    | This categorical column awards candidates a score ranging from 1 to 3 based on their English language abilities. This score is calculated based on several factors. A score of 3 describes a candidate whose proficiency in English is at the level of a native speaker |
| Arabic Proficiency                     | This categorical column awards candidates a score ranging from 1 to 3 based on their Arabic language abilities. This score is calculated based on several factors. A score of 3 describes a candidate whose proficiency in Arabic is at the level of a native speaker. |
| Graduated with Honors 2.1 or 1?        | This binary categorical column is the target column, describing whether a candidate completed their degree with honours                            |",False,True
