# Who is GDG Manama?

GDG Manama is a community-led group that is part of the global Google Developer Groups (GDG) network. The group is based in Manama, Bahrain, and is dedicated to promoting and supporting the local developer community.

GDG Manama hosts regular events, workshops, and meetups that bring together developers, entrepreneurs, and technology enthusiasts to share knowledge and expertise on a wide range of topics related to software development, cloud computing, machine learning, and more.

So if you are a developer, entrepreneur, or technology enthusiast based in Bahrain or the surrounding region, be sure to check out GDG Manama and join the community of like-minded individuals who are passionate about using technology to drive positive change in the world.

# Introduction

Welcome to the ML Olympiad competition hosted by GDG Manama on Kaggle! This competition is designed to challenge participants to build powerful machine-learning models that can accurately predict the outcomes of a given task.

The task for this competition will be announced at the start and will require participants to work with a large dataset to train and validate their models. Participants will need to leverage advanced machine learning techniques to build models that can accurately predict this task's outcomes while ensuring that their models are robust and can generalize to new data.

To ensure that the competition is fair and unbiased, participants will be given access to a dataset and will be required to submit their models for evaluation using a standardized evaluation metric. The top performers will be awarded prizes and recognition, and their models may even be used in real-world applications.

So if you are passionate about machine learning and are looking for a challenge, join the ML Olympiad competition hosted by GDG Manama and compete with other talented data scientists from around Bahrain!


# Participant Confirmation:

By participating in this competition, you agree to the following:

Abide by the competition rules and guidelines set forth by GDG Manama and Kaggle.
Only use the provided dataset and refrain from using external data sources or pre-trained models.
Use ethical and fair practices when building and training models, and avoid any form of data manipulation or bias.
Agree to have your models and submissions evaluated and potentially used in real-world applications.
Agree to have your name and affiliation published in the competition leaderboard and any related publicity materials.
Refrain from sharing your submission code with others or attempting to hack or compromise the competition platform in any way.
Accept that the decision of the competition judges is final and cannot be challenged or disputed.
Agree to indemnify and hold harmless GDG Manama, Kaggle, and any related sponsors from any claims, damages, or liabilities arising from your participation in the competition.
----------------------
# Evaluation

* Metric: The performance of the models in the ML Olympiad competition hosted by GDG Manama will be evaluated based on the Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE) metric.

* RMSE Metric: The RMSE metric measures the average difference between the predicted and actual values, with lower values indicating better performance.

* The lower the RMSE, the better the model performance. A RMSE of 0 would indicate a perfect fit, but this is rare in practice. In most cases, there will be some level of error or variance between the predicted and actual values, and the goal is to minimize this error as much as possible.

* On the other hand, a high RMSE value would indicate poor model performance and a large deviation between the predicted and actual values. In summary, the RMSE is a useful metric for evaluating the accuracy of regression models, where lower values indicate better performance and higher values indicate poorer performance.

* Awards: The top performers on the leaderboard will be awarded prizes and recognition.

* At the end of the competition, the top performers will be awarded prizes and recognition, and their models may even be used in real-world applications.

* Competition invitation: So if you are interested in competing in the ML Olympiad competition hosted by GDG Manama, start building and training your machine learning models now and see how they perform on the holdout set!","# Dataset Description

# Dataset Description
The dataset contains information about customer churn (i.e., customers who have left the company) for a telecom company. The dataset includes 21 columns and multiple rows, with each row representing a different customer.

This dataset can be used for predicting customer churn based on their demographic and service usage information, with the goal of identifying customers who are likely to leave the company and taking proactive measures to retain them.

## Files
*   **train_data.csv** - the training sets
*   **test_data.csv** - the test set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

> **Note**: Make sure that your submission is in the same format to the sample submission

# Columns:
The dataset contains the following columns:

- **customerID**: a unique identifier for each customer
- **gender**: the gender of the customer (male or female)
- **SeniorCitizen**: whether the customer is a senior citizen (1) or not (0)
- **Partner**: whether the customer has a partner (yes or no)
- **Dependents**: whether the customer has dependents (yes or no)
- **tenure**: the length of time the customer has been with the company (in months)
- **PhoneService**: whether the customer has phone service (yes or no)
- **MultipleLines**: whether the customer has multiple lines (yes, no, or no phone service)
- **InternetService**: the type of internet service the customer has (DSL, fiber optic, or no)
- **OnlineSecurity**: whether the customer has online security (yes, no, or no internet service)
- **OnlineBackup**: whether the customer has online backup (yes, no, or no internet service)
- **DeviceProtection**: whether the customer has device protection (yes, no, or no internet service)
- **TechSupport**: whether the customer has tech support (yes, no, or no internet service)
- **StreamingTV**: whether the customer has streaming TV (yes, no, or no internet service)
- **StreamingMovies**: whether the customer has streaming movies (yes, no, or no internet service)
- **Contract**: the type of contract the customer has (month-to-month, one year, or two years)
- **PaperlessBilling**: whether the customer has paperless billing (yes or no)
- **PaymentMethod**: the payment method the customer uses (electronic check, mailed check, bank transfer (automatic), or credit card (automatic))
- **MonthlyCharges**: the amount the customer pays each month
- **TotalCharges**: the total amount the customer has paid over their tenure with the company
- **Churn**: whether the customer has churned (left the company) or not (yes or no)




Disclaimer:
The data provided for the competition is for research and educational purposes only. Any commercial use or redistribution of the data is prohibited without explicit permission from the data owners. The organizers of the competition are not responsible for the accuracy or completeness of the data provided, and participants are responsible for ensuring that their models are built and trained using ethical and fair practices.",False,True
