## raw_health_export.xml
Raw data with over 8 years of health data for you to use to aid your submission. You've got to parse it yourself.",False,True
6767,early-detection-of-3d-printing-issues,"# Description

# Goal of this competition

3D printing is an innovative way to fabricate parts from any 3D geometry model. However, 3D printing is also known to be prone errors. Most of these errors are visible via a close-up camera mounted right near the printer nozzle.

The goal of this competition is to predict 1 specific kind of error - **under extrusion**.

# Examples of **under extrusion**.

If you haven't done 3D printing before and you don't understand what **under extrusion** is, it's ok. You don't need intimate knowledge about 3D printing to participate in this competition. This section will give you everything you need to participate in this competition effectively.

### A little background on 3D printing

In a nutshell, 3D printing is a process of pushing molten plastic through a small nozzle onto a print bed. This is illustrated in the picture below. Here is [a video that shows this process](https://youtu.be/_NN0MtbnrII).

<img src=""https://storage.googleapis.com/obico-public/kaggle-contest-files/1672779975.667825-1.jpg""  width=640 style=""display: block; margin: 0 auto"" />

### What does a good 3D print looks like?

When 3D printing goes well, the plastic will form smooth, continuous, and neat geometrical shapes. An example is below:

<img src=""https://storage.googleapis.com/obico-public/kaggle-contest-files/1672776329.535205.jpg""  width=640 style=""display: block; margin: 0 auto"" />


### What does **under extrusion** looks like?

When **under extrusion** happens, the plastic will have all kinds of artifacts, such as wrinkles, bubbles, curls, etc. The artifacts are marked by the red arrows in the example below:

<img src=""https://storage.googleapis.com/obico-public/kaggle-contest-files/1672795786.41987.jpg""  width=640 style=""display: block; margin: 0 auto"" />

### More **under extrusion** examples

<div style=""flex-direction: row; display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap;"">
<img src=""https://storage.googleapis.com/obico-public/kaggle-contest-files/1679010017.102943.jpg""  width=300 /><img src=""https://storage.googleapis.com/obico-public/kaggle-contest-files/1672869233.10414.jpg""  width=300 /><img src=""https://storage.googleapis.com/obico-public/kaggle-contest-files/1672793332.725275.jpg""  width=300 />
<img src=""https://storage.googleapis.com/obico-public/kaggle-contest-files/1672794243.584641.jpg""  width=300 />
<img src=""https://storage.googleapis.com/obico-public/kaggle-contest-files/1678594775.145677.jpg""  width=300 />
</div>

### **Under extrusion** may not always be obvious

Please be aware that the examples above were some of the most obvious. Most of the time, the **under extrusion** is less obvious. Some examples below:

<div style=""flex-direction: row; display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap;"">
<img src=""https://storage.googleapis.com/obico-public/kaggle-contest-files/1672859299.907916.jpg""  width=300 /><img src=""https://storage.googleapis.com/obico-public/kaggle-contest-files/1672795105.25779.jpg""  width=300 /><img src=""https://storage.googleapis.com/obico-public/kaggle-contest-files/1672869136.330449.jpg""  width=300 />
<img src=""https://storage.googleapis.com/obico-public/kaggle-contest-files/1678593814.141769.jpg""  width=300 />
<img src=""https://storage.googleapis.com/obico-public/kaggle-contest-files/1678593588.901188.jpg""  width=300 />
</div>

Some times they will even look very like the good prints.

<div style=""flex-direction: row; display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap;"">
<img src=""https://storage.googleapis.com/obico-public/kaggle-contest-files/1672858029.370999.jpg""  width=300 /><img src=""https://storage.googleapis.com/obico-public/kaggle-contest-files/1678590815.334203.jpg""  width=300 />
</div>

**It's important to keep in mind that there are plenty of pictures in the training and test dataset that are labelled as ""under extrusion"" but look very much like good prints.** Welcome to the messy real world. ;)

For this very reason, you are highly encourage to randomly sample data pictures from the training set and plot them so that you can build up some intuition between good prints and prints of under extrusion. This is a good practice for data scientists anyway.

# Again, the goal of this competition

Now you have some more background about the 3D printing problem we are trying to predict for this competition, let's make it more clear: the goal is to predict if a 3D printing picture shows any sign of under extrusion. This is binary classification problem. More specifically, this is an anomaly detection problem.

# Prior researches

This competition is inspired by this paper: https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-022-31985-y

The source code for this paper is: https://github.com/cam-cambridge/caxton

However, a word of caution: this research is based on a very pure lab environment. It uses data from the printers that share the same setup and hence very close to each other. Our dataset is more diverse (from 7 printers that look distinct from each other).
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>Since this competition is a binary classification problem, we will use <b>F1 score</b> for evaluation.</p>

<pre>F1 = 2 * (precision * recall) / (precision + recall)</pre>

<p>
<b>Positive:</b> has_under_extrusion=1
</p>
<p>
<b>Negative:</b> has_under_extrusion=0
</p>

----------------------
# Timeline

We will keep this competition open for at least 4 weeks from March 23rd.

We will continuously evaluate all the submissions from all the candidates during this period. It is possible, although not likely, that we will identify a good candidate before the end of this 4-week period. If this is the case, we will notify all participants of this decision.

Even if we make a hiring decision early, we will keep the competition open for 4 weeks. The prize will be given to the winner who score the highest at the end.

We may decide to keep this competition open for longer than 4 weeks if we see fit.","# Dataset Description

The dataset contains pictures from 7 different 3D printers.

Each 3D printer has 6-20 prints. A print contains a series of snapshots that are taken from the nozzle camera at a quick succession (about 0.5s apart).

Each print is either a good print, or purposefully set to produce under extrusion. So **all labels for snapshots in the same print will be the same.**

## Risk of overfitting

Because there are very limited number of 3D printers in the data set, it's very easy to build a ML model that overfits: it performs really well on the snapshots produced by one these printers, but fails miserably when we put it in production to make predictions on other printers that were not part of the dataset.

In order to prove the ML model doesn't overfit to the printers in the training set, we withheld 2 printers for test only:

```
021
023
```

Even within the same printer, there is still the risk of overfitting: the model performs well for all the prints in the existing data set, but when a printer does a new print, the model will perform a lot worse. For this reason, we withheld several prints for test only:

```
101/1678343246
101/1678578332
102/1678744931
102/1678764144
103/1678831256
103/1679011090
104/1678452362
104/1679222113
```

## A few ideas to counter overfitting

*Note: This is by no means a definite guide to overcome the overfitting problem that we mentioned earlier. The ideas we listed here may not help at all. Instead of counting on them, you should use them as your inspiration.*

https://papers.nips.cc/paper/2018/file/717d8b3d60d9eea997b35b02b6a4e867-Paper.pdf
https://arxiv.org/abs/2208.07422
https://towardsdatascience.com/deep-domain-adaptation-in-computer-vision-8da398d3167f

## Files

*   **train.csv** - The training data set
*   **test.csv** - The test data set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - A sample submission file in the correct format
*   **images** - Folders for the images. The names of the folders are the printer ids.

## Columns

*   `img_path` - the path of the snapshot
*   `printer_id`?- the id of the 3D printer. The data with the same printer_id are guaranteed to be produced by the same printer. You may use this info **in your training** if the ML model you create tend to overfit one or some particular printers. However, since `printer_id` is highly correlated to the ground truth, **you are not allowed to use this info in testing**.
*   `print_id` - the id of the print (see above for the definition of a ""print""). The data with the same print_id are guaranteed to be from the same print. You may use this info **in your training** if the ML model you create tend to overfit one or some particular prints. However, since `print_id` is highly correlated to the ground truth, **you are not allowed to use this info in testing**.
*    `has_under_extrusion`
    * 1 - Under extrusion is present in this image.
    * 0 - Under extrusion is **NOT** present in this image. This means this image shows a good print.",False,True
6768,happiness-index,"# Description

## Competition Description

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F3745768%2F79c9fd224f62de9038aec8e65932b115%2FHapp.png?generation=1679395578109060&alt=media)

[Source of Image](https://www.visualcapitalist.com/visualizing-the-happiest-country-on-every-continent/)

The happiness index of a country plays a crucial role in the well-being and prosperity of its residents. Generally, a country's happiness level depends on different social, economic, and cultural factors, significantly impacting the happiness score.

With 22 explanatory variables describing (almost) every aspect of social, economic, and cultural features (Xs) in different countries (masked as Id), this competition challenges you to predict their happiness score (Happiness, Y).

## Acknowledgements

Dr. Tanujit Chakraboty would like to thank his team for building this dataset.
----------------------
# Evaluation

## Goal

Your job is to predict the happiness score for each instance for the test dataset (30 data points). For each Id in the test set, you must predict the value of the ''Happiness"" variable.  


## Metric

Submissions are evaluated on Root-Mean-Squared-Error (RMSE) between the predicted value and the observed happiness score. 


**Submission File Format**
The file should contain a header and have the following format:

```
Id, Happiness 
1, 5.122 
2, 5.123 
3, 5124 
etc.
```

You can download an example submission file (Sample_Submission.csv) on the Data page.","# Dataset Description

## Files

*   **Features_Train.csv** - features of the training set
*   **Features_Test.csv** - features of the test set
*   **Output_Train.csv** - output of the training set
*   **Sample_Submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

## Columns

*   `Id` - Unique identifier of countries
*   `X1` - `X22`?- relevant features for predicting the happiness score
*   `Happiness` - the response variable",False,True
6769,adams-sose23,"# Description


##**Overview**

The learning goal of ADAMS is to familiarize you with recent developments in deep learning and to enable you to use the corresponding methodology for solving real-world problems. In accordance with this objective, the evaluation of the module is based on an assignment.

Your submission will consist of a Jupyter notebook that includes all steps that we typically encounter in a machine learning pipeline (explanatory data analysis, data preparation, parameter tuning, etc.). Grading of the notebook will be based on several criteria including:
a) the quality of the exposition (e.g., a healthy mix of prose, code, comments, math. formalism)
b) the organization of the notebook
c) the scope of your analysis
d) whether you have solved the task in a sound and rigorous manner

The final assignment is hosted as a Kaggle in-class competition. The main purpose of this setting is to give you the possibility to interactively query the quality of your modeling pipeline by submitting the model predictions to the online leaderboard. You may submit up to 5 entries daily to track the quality of your solution on unseen test data. Please remember that the actual accuracy of your solution is not the main factor that will determine your grade.

##**Modeling task**
Airbnb is an online marketplace for accommodation rental founded in 2008. Properties hosted on the Airbnb platform are owned by private companies and individuals. The hosts determine the rental price themselves and pay a service fee to Airbnb.

In this assignment, you will predict property prices for Airbnb listings in London. You will be provided with data on more than 55.000 properties containing the listing attributes (e.g., location, number of rooms, property type), texts with the listing description, and rental price per night.

Using a broad range of NLP techniques, you will need to build an NN-based model that predicts the price of listings in the test set based on both text and tabular data. If you wish to further refine the accuracy of your model, you will have access to additional data containing the listing reviews.

Please refer to other sections of the competition page to download the data and read more about competition rules, evaluation and data description.

##**Deadline**
The submission deadline is the 2th of September 2023
----------------------
# Evaluation

1. Evaluation Metric:<br>
Your submissions will be evaluated by computing RMSE (Root Mean Squared Error) between the actual property prices and submitted price predictions. <br><br>

2. Submission File:<br>

You should submit your predictions in `.csv` format. For every `listing_id` in the test set, you must predict the price of the corresponding listing per night. The file should contain a header and have the following format: ``` listing_id, price 0FEMC4VA5U, 100.00 PQ2BYEO0QR, 100.00 3FP6WFHUBN, 100.00 ... ``` <br><br>

3. Sample Submission:<br>

A demo notebook that illustrates how to create a submission is available.","# Dataset Description

##**Data partitioning**
The data has been randomly partitioned into a training set and a test set. You should use the labeled training set for developing your models and validating their quality. Next, you should use the developed models to predict the property prices for the listings in the unlabeled test set.

To prevent overfitting, the test set is divided into a public and a private subset. The leaderboard score of your submission is computed on a private part of the test set. Once the competition is finished, you will see a private score of your submissions. The private score determines the winner of the competition. While the competition is running, you will be able to see the public score of your submissions. In this way, you can track your progress over time, when testing different models.

##**File descriptions**
The data consists of three .csv files containing different information:

- **train.csv**: training set with listing features, description and prices
- **test.csv**: test set with listing features and description
- **reviews.csv**: [optional for master students, mandatory for PhD students] reviews for listings in both training and test set

##**Data fields**
Files train.csv and test.csv have the same columns except for the price variable:

- listing_id: unique property identifier
- price: [target variable] property price per night in US dollars
- summary: [text] listing summary
- description: [text] listing description
- space: [text] space description
- neighborhood_overview: [text] neighborhood description

As indicated above, the text data is provided in several columns devoted to different topics. You will need to think whether to merge them into a single text or consider separately in order to improve your solution.

The remaining columns contain numeric/categorical features with the listing attributes such as location, host information, reviewer scores and more. You would need to perform some data preprocessing and think how to incorporate the tabular data into your modeling pipeline. The column containing the urls to the property images should be dropped.

##**Additional data**
The data on property reviews in reviews.csv is optional for master students. It can be used to further refine the quality of the solution, but master students can also choose to ignore it. By contrast, PhD students are required to include the review data in their submissions.

##**Reviews**
Data dictionary for reviews.csv:

- listing_id: unique property identifier
- review_id: unique review identifier
- reviewer_id: unique reviewer identifier
- comments: [text] review

Reviews can be matched with train.csv by listing_id. You would need to think how to deal with the following challenges:

- reviews may come in different languages
- listings have a different number of reviews
- aggregating signal from multiple reviews",False,True
6770,diamonds-datamad1022,"# Description


![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F6574539%2F0c19cfb9eede3bba62a781f47351f663%2Fdiamantes.png?generation=1679676764746704&alt=media)

The goal of this competition is the prediction of the price of diamonds based on their characteristics (weight, color, quality of cut, etc.), putting into practice all the machine learning techniques you know.
----------------------
# Evaluation

### Evaluation 

The evaluation metric chosen for this competition is the MSE (Mean Squared Error): https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mean_squared_error 

### Submit results 

- Submissions are limited to 5 times a day. 

- The test set is divided into a public part (with which the public leaderboard is calculated, accessible during the competition) and another private part, with which the final positions are calculated, after the end of the competition. 

- Predictions will be sent in the format indicated in the sample_submission.csv file in the data section. 

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F6574539%2F0317c1e547ea36d20b1280bb62bbe434%2Finbox_3824396_f6235a3402992f0fc94af9e0ca3465c2_inbox_3824396_4554b5824665256889dafdd5c4c59890_Bbf0GWk.jpg?generation=1679676873831818&amp;alt=media)","# Dataset Description


**Data**

In this section you will find data needed to train your model and a detailed description of it.

**Files**

- `train.csv`: training set
- `test.csv`: test set
- `sample_submission.csv`: sample submission
**Features**

- `id`: only for test & sample submission files, id for prediction sample identification
- `price`: price in USD
- `carat`: weight of the diamond
- `cut`: quality of the cut (Fair, Good, Very Good, Premium, Ideal)
- `color`: diamond colour
- `clarity`: a measurement of how clear the diamond is
- `x`: length in mm
- `y`: width in mm
- `z`: depth in mm
- `depth`: total depth percentage = z / mean(x, y) = 2 * z / (x + y) (43--79)
- `table`: width of top of diamond relative to widest point (43--95)",False,True
