6772,ee5180-course-contest,"# Description

**Problem Statement**
Insurance firms have been running direct marketing efforts through telemarketing to encourage clients to apply for health insurance. So far, we have called all of our target clients, but to save money, we would like to limit the number of calls we make to those most likely to place purchases.

**Task** 
As a data scientist in the digital department of the insurance company, you are asked to output an attack list that predicts whether or not you will receive an order. Create the most accurate model possible. You need to analyse the data and match it with the quantitative result you get. We will see the analysis which you would perform on the data more than the accuracy of the model. Make all the analysis in a jupyter notebook using pandas and numpy library. 

**Rules**
* You must use your team name for submissions
* You are allowed to take inspiration from other sources. Copying any existing implementation directly without understanding will lead to disqualification from the contest. 
* You are allowed to use numpy, pandas and sklearn libraries. 
* Submit the code along with the application through the google form provided below. https://forms.gle/xhToyQ3QTtbUgefo7

**Contact**
If you have any doubts, you can contact:
Vinayak - 9500180979


If you are new to Kaggle Competitions, you can check the below links, which will give you a brief overview of how kaggle competition works.
Here is a handy link to Kaggle's [competition documentation](https://www.kaggle.com/docs/competitions), which includes, among other things, instructions on [submitting predictions](https://www.kaggle.com/docs/competitions#making-a-submission).
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation pages describes how submissions will be scored and how students should format their submissions. You don't need a sub-title at the top; the page title appears above. For an example of a high quality evaluation page, see the 2013 KDD Cup page, which we have included below:

---  d

The evaluation metric for this competition is [Mean F1-Score](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/F-score). The F1 score, commonly used in information retrieval, measures accuracy using the statistics precision \( \text{p} \) and recall \( \text{r} \).

Precision is the ratio of true positives \(\text{tp}\) to all predicted positives \(\text{tp} + \text{fp}\). Recall is the ratio of true positives \(\text{tp}\) to all actual positives \(\text{tp} + \text{fn}\). The F1 score is given by:

\[ \text{F1} = 2\frac{\text{p} \cdot \text{r}}{\text{p}+\text{r}}\ \ \mathrm{where}\ \ \text{p} = \frac{\text{tp}}{\text{tp}+\text{fp}},\ \ \text{r} = \frac{\text{tp}}{\text{tp}+\text{fn}} \]

The F1 metric weights recall and precision equally, and a good retrieval algorithm will maximize both precision and recall simultaneously. Thus, moderately good performance on both will be favored over extremely good performance on one and poor performance on the other.

## Submission Format

**For every author in the dataset**, submission files should contain two columns: `AuthorId` and `DuplicateAuthorIds`. `DuplicateAuthorIds` should be a space-delimited list. Every `AuthorId` counts as its own duplicate, and every duplicate should be listed under each of its respective ids. For example, if you suspect author A, B, and C are the same, you should list `A,A B C`, `B,B A C`, `C,C A B`.


The file should contain a header and have the following format:
```
AuthorId,DuplicateAuthorIds
1,1
```","# Dataset Description

# DataSet Description 

The data is related with direct marketing campaigns of a Health Insurance Company. The marketing campaigns were based on phone calls. Often, more than one contact to the same client was required, in order to access if the insurance would be ('yes' or 1) or not ('no', or 0) subscribed.


## Files

*   **Insurance_Train.csv** - the training set
*   **Insurance_Test.csv** - the test set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

## Columns

**Client data:**

* `Age` (numeric)
* `Job`: type of job (categorical: 'admin.', 'blue-collar', 'entrepreneur', 'housemaid', 'management', 'retired', 'self-employed', 'services', 'student', 'technician', 'unemployed', 'unknown')
* `Marital`: marital status (categorical: 'divorced', 'married', 'single', 'unknown' ; note: 'divorced' means divorced or widowed)
* `Education`: (categorical: 'basic.4y', 'basic.6y', 'basic.9y', 'high.school', 'illiterate', 'professional.course', 'university.degree', 'unknown')
* `Balance`: average yearly balance, in euros (numeric)
* `Housing`: has housing loan? (categorical: 'no', 'yes', 'unknown')
* `Loan`: has personal loan? (categorical: 'no', 'yes', 'unknown')

**Related with the last contact of the current campaign:**

* `Contact`: contact communication type (categorical:
'cellular','telephone')
* `Month`: last contact month of year (categorical: 'jan', 'feb', 'mar',
¡­, 'nov', 'dec')
* `Duration`: last contact duration, in seconds (numeric). Important
note: this attribute highly affects the output target (e.g., if
duration=0 then y='no'). Yet, the duration is not known before a call
is performed. Also, after the end of the call y is obviously known.
Thus, this input should only be included for benchmark purposes and
should be discarded if the intention is to have a realistic
predictive model.

**Other attributes:**

* `Campaign`: number of contacts performed during this campaign and for
this client (numeric, includes last contact)
* `Pdays`: number of days that passed by after the client was last
contacted from a previous campaign (numeric; 999 means client was not
previously contacted)
* `Previous`: number of contacts performed before this campaign and for
this client (numeric)
* `Poutcome`: outcome of the previous marketing campaign (categorical:
'failure','nonexistent','success')

**Output variable (desired target):**

y - has the client subscribed a term deposit? (binary: 'yes', 'no')",False,True
6773,gait-data-processing,"# Description

# Goal of the Competition:
Develop a machine learning model to decoding the gait phase by using electroencephalogram (EEG) data collected from a person with spinal cord injury using the Lokomat system. As a reference, the signal from a inertial unit motion (IMU) sensor is used.

# Motivation
Wearable devices, such as robotic exoskeletons, have been used to assist spinal cord injury (SCI) individuals in regaining some of their lost mobility. These devices can be further enhanced by incorporating the individual's intended movement via motor imagery (MI) detected through EEG. To promote the development of more accurate and efficient machine learning models for decoding joint values from EEG data, we propose here a competition that utilizes data collected from SCI individuals who walked on a treadmill with the Lokomat's assistance.

# Methodology:
To record EEG signals, 16 wet electrodes were placed on the scalp according to the international 10-20 system (FP1, FP2, F3, F4, FC1, FC2, C4, C3, Cz, CP2, CP1, P4, Pz, P3, O2, and O1). The Fpz and Fz electrodes were used as ground and reference, respectively. The electrode's impedance was kept below 10 k¦¸. Signals were acquired with the V-Amp 16 system at 512 Hz sampling rate. An in-house inertial sensor, termed  JAMA, was placed between the calf and ankle braces to acquire the gait phases by using sampling rate at 20 Hz. Both raw EEG and IMU signal were synchronized for analysis. The participant was positioned on Lokomat, and received 100% assistance walking at 1 km/h with body weight support. 

The Lokomat robotic system is designed to assist and control lower-limb movements during gait rehabilitation. It is equipped with a sophisticated software system that allows the therapist to program various walking patterns and levels of assistance, which can be tailored to the individual needs of each patient. The Lokomat also provides body weight support and an adjustable speed control.

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F10622352%2F4d7955623ff1e61153cbce9403e9a029%2FLogos%20assinatura.png?generation=1681933899167807&alt=media)

----------------------
# Evaluation

This competition aims to develop a machine learning model to predict inertial signal values from EEG data collected from a person with spinal cord injury using a Lokomat exoskeleton. The predictions will be evaluated based on their **root mean squared error (RMSE)** between the predicted and actual values.

## Submission Format
Participants are required to submit their predictions in a CSV file with the following format:

The file should contain a header row with two columns: ""id"" and ""prediction"".
The ""id"" column should contain unique identifiers for each test sample.
The ""prediction"" column should contain the predicted inertial signal values for each test sample.

## Evaluation
Submissions will be evaluated using the mean squared error (MSE) between the predicted and actual inertial signal values. The formula for the MSE is as follows:

$$RMSE = \sqrt{1/N * sum((y_{pred} - y_{true})^2)}$$

where N is the total number of test samples, $$y_{pred}$$ is the predicted value, and $$y_{true}$$ is the actual value.

The score for each submission will be calculated based on the RMSE, and the submission with the lowest MSE will be ranked at the top of the leaderboard. The final ranking will be based on the scores of the top five submissions.

Note: In the event of a tie, the submission with the lower run time will be ranked higher.
----------------------
# Timeline

- **June 1, 2023** - Start Date.

- **September 1 , 2023** - Entry Deadline. You must accept the competition rules before this date in order to compete.

- **September 1 , 2023** - Final Submission Deadline.
----------------------
# Paper Publishing Opportunity

The top-performing **code submissions** will have the opportunity to publish a methodological paper.

Selected teams will be contacted and asked to submit a manuscript within 30 days of the competition's completion. The manuscript will be evaluated by the Scientific Committee and, if accepted, sent for publication.

Please note that although the competition will be accepting submissions with and without code, **only submissions with accompanying code will be considered for publication**. This is to ensure that the methods presented in the manuscript are transparent and reproducible, as well as to encourage open science practices in the field of neural engineering. We encourage all participants to carefully review the competition rules and guidelines and to submit their best work for consideration.","# Dataset Description

The data for this competition consist of EEG and inertial signals, which were recorded with sampling rate at 512 Hz and 20 Hz, respectively, while patients with complete spinal cord injury (SCI) walked under the Lokomat's assistance. The inertial signals were resampled at 512 Hz, and together to the EEG data are provided in CSV files, which later were split into a training set and a testing set. 

## Training Data
The file ""train_set.csv"" contains the EEG recordings that were collected in several sessions from two SCI individuals. The file is composed of columns, indicating the following information:
1)  ID: Each row contains a unique identifier;
2) Subject: The SCIX indicates the subject;
3) Session: The number of each session takes values from 0001 to 00XX;
4) Time:512Hz: The time sequence.
5) 16 EEG locations, according to the international 10-20 system:  FP1, FP2, F3, F4, FC1, FC2, C4, C3, Cz, CP2, CP1, P4, Pz, P3, O2, and O1. 

## Labels of training
1) ID: Each row contains the same unique identifier that corresponds to the training set;
2) Angle: The inertial signal recorded simultaneously with the EEG data.

## Testing Data
The file ""test_set.npy"" contains EEG epochs of 1 s (16 channels x 512 samples) extracted each 0.1 s from unseen EEG recording of two SCI individuals. This file has the following structure:
To submit the results, the competitors of this challenge are required to predict the inertial signal values from the testing set, and follow the instruction below.

## Example for submission
The provided file ""example_submission.csv"" contains the required format to submit the predictions from the testing set. Notice that this file contains two columns with ID and prediction, where ID contains the same unique identifier corresponding to the testing set, and prediction contains the predicted inertial signal values.

It is worth noting that the training and testing sets were randomly separated from the full dataset, taking into account the session number. The competitors are allowed to use external data sources for their models, as long as they are publicly available and properly cited.",False,True
6774,stock-arbitrage-profitability-prediction,"# Description

Hello, dear participants of the competition! 

You are facing a **very financially important** task: to learn how to distinguish a profitable arbitration transaction from a non-profitable one.

There are a large number of brokers in the world that allow you to trade shares of companies. Sometimes the price of the same shares differs from one broker to another. If you catch the moment, noticing such a difference, and buying from one broker cheaper and selling from another more expensive, you will make a profit. 

The problem with this approach is that during the transfer of shares from one broker to another, the price may change, and it turns out that we bought more expensive and sell cheaper, which is not good

Therefore, here is a dataset containing historical data on arbitrage trading in the world of stocks. Your task is to learn how to correctly distinguish the status of a transaction - will it be profitable or unprofitable according to its data.

The main metric of the project is **accuracy**. It is necessary to learn how to distinguish well between profitable and non-profitable transactions. **The classical binary classification problem**.

I wish good luck and success to all participants of the competition!
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is Accuracy. Also Precision is important, but not the main.","# Dataset Description

### What files do I need?  
## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format *(values in Category column in this file are random)*

### What am I predicting?  
You are predicting the status of the arbitration deal, presented by the **Category column**

## Columns

*   `Type` - this is already a coded translation from one exchange to another, in fact represents their names, only in such an impersonal form, to which the codes are assigned;
*   `A_Depth, B_Depth`?-  reflect the depth of the market for this stock at the broker;
*   `A_Volativity...`?- reflects the volatility of the market for this stock at the broker;
*   `RSI, schol`?-   these are the RSI and scholastic oscillator technical analysis indicators;
- Others - clear from the name.

## Some columns values ratio and their meaning:
*   `A_schol_K_5m, A_schol_K_1m, B_schol_K_5m, B_schol_K_1m`?-  0-100; 
it is the Stochastic Oscillator, a technical analysis tool used to measure the momentum of an asset's price.
if number is less than 20, it means asset is oversold, 
if number is between 20 and 80, it means asset is trading in a normal range;
if number is more than 80, it means asset is overbought.
*   `A_RSI_5m, A_RSI_1m, B_RSI_5m, B_RSI_1m`?- 0-100; 
RSI stands for Relative Strength Index, which is another popular technical analysis indicator used to measure the strength of an asset's price action. 
If number is less than 30, it means asset is oversold;
If number is more than 70, it means asset is overbought .
When the RSI is overbought, it suggests that the asset may be due for a price correction or pullback, while oversold readings suggest that the asset may be undervalued and could be due for a price increase
*   `A_Depth, B_Depth`?-  calculated as the total bid volume divided by the total ask volume. This can be interpreted as the level of market liquidity, with higher values indicating that there is more liquidity available;
*   `A_Spread_Total, B_Spread_Total`?-  measures the difference between the highest bid and lowest ask prices, as a percentage of the ask price. This can be interpreted as the cost of trading, with higher values indicating that there is a higher cost to buy or sell an asset;
*   `A_Spread_Peaks, B_Spread_Peaks`?-  measures the spread between the highest bid and lowest ask prices in terms of the lowest ask price. This can be interpreted as the impact of trading, with higher values indicating that the market is more volatile and that there is a higher cost to trade due to market fluctuations.;
*   `A_Ask_Volume_Ratio, B_Ask_Volume_Ratio`?-  the ratio of total ask volume to the total volume for a given financial asset or instrument at a particular point in time. It represents the proportion of the total trading activity that is driven by buyers who are willing to buy at the ask price. A high A_Ask_Volume_Ratio indicates that there is a relatively large amount of trading activity being driven by buyers who are willing to buy at the ask price, while a low A_Ask_Volume_Ratio indicates that there is more trading activity being driven by buyers who are willing to buy at lower prices (i.e., at the bid price or below).",False,True
6775,home-credit-credit-risk-model-stability,"# Overview

The goal of this competition is to predict which clients are more likely to default on their loans. The evaluation will favor solutions that are stable over time.

Your participation may offer consumer finance providers a more reliable and longer-lasting way to assess a potential client¡¯s default risk.
----------------------
# Description


The absence of a credit history might mean a lot of things, including young age or a preference for cash. Without traditional data, someone with little to no credit history is likely to be denied. Consumer finance providers must accurately determine which clients can repay a loan and which cannot and data is key. If data science could help better predict one¡¯s repayment capabilities, loans might become more accessible to those who may benefit from them the most.

Currently, consumer finance providers use various statistical and machine learning methods to predict loan risk. These models are generally called scorecards. In the real world, clients' behaviors change constantly, so every scorecard must be updated regularly, which takes time. The scorecard's stability in the future is critical, as a sudden drop in performance means that loans will be issued to worse clients on average. The core of the issue is that loan providers aren't able to spot potential problems any sooner than the first due dates of those loans are observable. Given the time it takes to redevelop, validate, and implement the scorecard, stability is highly desirable. There is a trade-off between the stability of the model and its performance, and a balance must be reached before deployment.

Founded in 1997, competition host Home Credit is an international consumer finance provider focusing on responsible lending primarily to people with little or no credit history. Home Credit broadens financial inclusion for the unbanked population by creating a positive and safe borrowing experience. We previously ran a competition with Kaggle that you can see [here](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/home-credit-default-risk). 

Your work in helping to assess potential clients' default risks will enable consumer finance providers to accept more loan applications. This may improve the lives of people who have historically been denied due to lack of credit history.


----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated using a gini stability metric. A gini score is calculated for predictions corresponding to each `WEEK_NUM`.

$$
\text{gini} = 2 * \text{AUC} - 1
$$

A linear regression, \\(a \cdot x + b \\), is fit through the weekly gini scores, and a `falling_rate` is calculated as \\( \min(0, a) \\). This is used to penalize models that drop off in predictive ability.

Finally, the variability of the predictions are calculated by taking the standard deviation of the residuals from the above linear regression, applying a penalty to model variablity.

The final metric is calculated as

$$
\begin{aligned}
\text{stability metric} = mean(gini) +88.0 \cdot min(0, a) -0.5 \cdot std(\text{residuals})
\end{aligned}
$$ 

## Submission File
For each `case_id` in the test set, you must predict a probability for the target `score`. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    case_id,score
    57543,0.1
    57544,0.9
    57545,0.5
    etc.

----------------------
# Timeline

* **February 5, 2024** - Start Date.

* **May 20, 2024** - Entry Deadline. You must accept the competition rules before this date in order to compete.

* **May 20, 2024** - Team Merger Deadline. This is the last day participants may join or merge teams.

* **May 27, 2024** - Final Submission Deadline.

All deadlines are at 11:59 PM UTC on the corresponding day unless otherwise noted. The competition organizers reserve the right to update the contest timeline if they deem it necessary.
----------------------
# Prizes

- 1st Prize: $ 25,000

- 2nd Prize: $ 20,000

- 3rd Prize: $ 15,000

- 4th Prize: $ 10,000

- 5th ¨C 9th Prize: $ 5,000

- Special Prize: $ 10,000 - Special prize for consistent model stability over time

----------------------
# Stability Prize

We are hosting a second track of this competition that focuses on stability. Credit scoring models typically have a lifecycle of a year or more, and their consistent performance over time is key. In this competition, you will work with a new metric that encounters both AUC and stability over weeks on the future sample (test set).

For the Stability Prize, we will evaluate submissions based on how well they included this custom stability metric directly into model training. We can't wait to see the innovative approaches you come up with to tackle stability in your models.

####Requirements for qualification for special Stability Prize:

- Your solution is among top 20% on the private leaderboard
- Your approach to stability should be: 
 - 1) general, not dependent explicitly on used dataset, and 
 - 2) incorporate stability directly into your training model / loss function (not on the level of feature preparation or feature selection)
- Your notebook is public (can be made public at the end of the competition)

####Scoring
1. Innovation (40%): Analyzes credit stability over time, specifically focusing on the competition evaluation metric beyond just feature selection. e.g. High average gini, non-decreasing performance over time, small oscillations...
2. Quality (20%): Any tables, figures, and/or code samples are high quality (e.g. information-dense, easy-to-understand, and informative)
3. Clarity (20%): The overall composition is articulate, concise, accurate, and easy-to-understand.
4. Generalization (20%): The implementation of stability as a metric can be applied to non-competition datasets.

####Click here to go to the [Submission form](https://www.kaggle.com/home-credit-stability-prize)
----------------------
# Code Requirements

<img src=""https://storage.googleapis.com/kaggle-media/competitions/general/Kerneler-white-desc2_transparent.png"" style=""float: right; width: 70px; padding: 10px"">

### This is a Code Competition

Submissions to this competition must be made through Notebooks. In order for the ""Submit"" button to be active after a commit, the following conditions must be met:

- CPU Notebook <= 12 hours run-time
- GPU Notebook <= 12 hours run-time
- Internet access disabled
- Freely & publicly available external data is allowed, including pre-trained models
- Submission file must be named `submission.csv`

Please see the [Code Competition FAQ](https://www.kaggle.com/docs/competitions#notebooks-only-FAQ) for more information on how to submit. And review the [code debugging doc](https://www.kaggle.com/code-competition-debugging) if you are encountering submission errors.",,True,True
6776,classifying-customers-into-segments,"# Description

### Overview about the Competition

Customer segmentation classification is a marketing strategy that involves dividing a large and heterogeneous market into smaller and more manageable groups of customers with similar needs and characteristics. The process involves analyzing customer data to identify commonalities such as demographics, behavior, purchasing patterns, and preferences.

Once these groups have been identified, businesses can tailor their marketing efforts and product offerings to better meet the specific needs and preferences of each segment. This can help to increase customer satisfaction and loyalty, as well as improve overall business performance.

Customer segmentation classification can be carried out using various methods, such as geographic, demographic, psychographic, and behavioral segmentation. The choice of method will depend on the nature of the business and the goals of the marketing strategy.

Overall, customer segmentation classification is an essential marketing tool that can help businesses to effectively target and engage their customers, increase sales, and build stronger relationships with their customer base.
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation will be done by comparing the submitted predictions on the testing dataset and the observed target for the testing data.","# Dataset Description

The following are the Data for the Competition:
**Training Dataset**

**Testing Dataset**

**Make sure you drop the Unnamed column in the Testing dataset before you start working on it.**

Following are the columns in the Datasets:

ID:	                          Unique ID
Gender:	                  Gender of the customer
Ever_Married:	          Marital status of the customer
Age:	                  Age of the customer
Graduated:	          Is the customer a graduate?
Profession:	          Profession of the customer
Work_Experience:	  Work Experience in years
Spending_Score:	  Spending score of the customer
Family_Size:             Number of family members for the customer (including the customer)
Var_1:	                  Anonymised Category for the customer
Segmentation:	  Target -> Customer Segment of the customer

**Acknowledgements:**
This dataset was acquired from the **Analytics Vidhya Hackathon.**",False,True
