6813,ucsd-cse-251b-class-competition,"# Description

<h2>Introduction</h2>
This is the home page of the deep learning class competition (CSE 251b Spring 2023). 

Here is a handy link to Kaggle's [competition documentation](https://www.kaggle.com/docs/competitions), which includes, among other things, instructions on [submitting predictions](https://www.kaggle.com/docs/competitions#making-a-submission).


<h2>Background</h2>

<p>Autonomous vehicles (AV)  are expected to dramatically redefine the future of transportation. However, there are still significant engineering challenges to be solved before one can fully realize the benefits of self-driving cars. One such challenge is building models that reliably predict the movement of traffic agents around the AV, such as cars, cyclists, and pedestrians.

</p><p>
In this competition, you¡¯ll apply your data science skills to build motion forecasting models for self-driving vehicles.  You'll have access to a large AV motion dataset to train and test your models. The goal of the motion forecasting task is to predict the positions of a tracked object 3 seconds into the future, given an initial 2-second observation. Your knowledge of deep learning will then be required to predict how cars  move in the AV's environment.
</p>

<p>
The training, validation, and test sequences are taken from different areas of Pittsburgh and Miami so that there is no geographical overlap. Each sequence follows the trajectory of the main agent for 5 seconds, while keeping track of all other agents including cars and pedestrians.   Agents follow very complex trajectories, such as changing lanes, navigating intersections, and turning.  We also provide the HD map information that is associated with the trajectory.
</p>

<h2>Team Guideline</h2>
This is a group project for the class.  You can request the computational resources from UCSD Data Science/Machine Learning platform allocated for this course. See more details on [Course Website](https://sites.google.com/view/cse251b). 

- Maximum submission per day: 10
- Submission deadline: 11:59 pm UTC (16:00 pm PST) June 9, 2023





<h2>Acknowledgements</h2>
<p>We thank Argoverse for sharing this dataset.</p>
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation pages describes how submissions will be scored and how you should format their submissions. </p>
<hr>

<p>We will use the following evaluation metrics for this competition, see more details at <a href=""https://eval.ai/web/challenges/challenge-page/454/evaluation""> Argoverse Evaluation Criteria </a>. 

-  Root Mean Squared Error  (RMSE): The average Root Mean Squared Error distance between the best-forecasted trajectory and the ground truth.  


<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p><strong>For every scene in the dataset</strong>, submission files should contain 61 columns: scene ID and predicted x, y positions over 30 time steps. scene ID should be a string. Every x, y positions should be listed in a time-ordered fashion. That means x_1, y_1, x_2, y_2...</p>
<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>ID, v1<br>1,1<br></pre>

See the sample_submission.csv file in the data folder for an example.","# Dataset Description

<p>This page describes the details of the dataset. We provide the training set and the input of the test set. You are requested to submit the output (predictions) for the test set. The test set is further split into 80% for the public leaderboard and 20% for the private leaderboard.  </p>
<p> We also provide a sample submission file with zero predictions. You are expected to replace the zeros with the predictions generated by your deep learning model. </p>

<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>train</strong> - the training set including the output labels</li>
<li><strong>val_in</strong> - the test set with the inputs only</li>
<li><strong>Load_Argoverse_Data_Public.ipynb</strong> - a sample Jupyter notebook to load and visualize the data </li>
<li><strong>sampleSubmission.csv</strong> - a sample submission file in the correct format</li>
</ul>
<h2>Data fields</h2>
<p>Every pickle is a scene with trajectory tracking sequences for a maxium of 60 vehicles recorded at 10HZ (10 records per second). The target vehicle is called agent with a corresponding agent_id. Track_id is a sequence of IDs that track the ID of all vehicles including the agent itself.  The pickle file contains a dictionary with the following keys/values 
<ul>
<li><strong>city</strong>- a string for the city the data was taken from. Can either be 'PIT' or 'MIA' </li>
<li><strong>scene_idx</strong>- a unique non-zero integer identifying the scene</li>
<li><strong>track_id</strong>- tracked maximum 60 agents with 32 digits ID in the form of 
""00000000-0000-0000-0000-0000XXXXXXXX"" </li>
<li><strong>agent_id</strong>- The track id for the agent to be predicted in this scene</li>
<li><strong>p_in</strong>- the (x,y) position input in the first two seconds (19 time steps) of max 60 tracked agents </li>
<li><strong>v_in</strong>-the (x,y) velocity input in the first two seconds (19 time steps)  of max 60 tracked agents 
 </li>
<li><strong>p_out</strong>- the (x,y) position output in the next three seconds (30 time steps) of max 60 tracked agents </li>
<li><strong>v_out </strong>- the (x,y) velocity output in the next three seconds (30 time steps) of max 60 tracked agents </li>
<li><strong>car_mask</strong>- boolean index for the real car, we need to align the car numbers. We track maximum 60 agents. Mask equals 1 means the agent appears in the scene, and 0 otherwise </li>
<li><strong>lane</strong>- a (k, 2) numpy array of floats where 'k' is the number of lanes in the scene, representing (x,y) position for centerline nodes in this scene</li>
<li><strong>lane_norm</strong>- a (k, 2) numpy array of floats where 'k' is the number of lanes in the scene, representing the direction of each lane node</li>
</ul>

<h2>Submission Format</h2>
You will submit a csv file with 3,201 rows and 61 columns. The first row is the header indicating ID, v1, v2...
The first column corresponds to scene_idx. The rest of the 60 columns are p_out (x,y) over 30 time steps.",False,True
6814,ucsd-cse-151b-class-competition,"# Description

<h2>Project Description</h2>
<p>To improve transportation,  it is important to be able to predict how long a taxi driver will have his or her taxi occupied. If a central dispatch system knows approximately when a taxi driver would be ending their current ride, it would be able to assign drivers more efficiently to each pickup request. </p>

<p> In this group project, we ask you to build a deep learning model that can predict the trip time of taxi rides in Porto, Portugal based on their (initial) partial trajectories. The output of the model is the travel time of a given taxi trip.</p>

<h2>Submission Guideline</h2>
<p> This is a group project for the class. </p>


 - Maximum submission per day: 10
 - Submission deadline: 11:59 pm UTC (16:00 pm PST) June 9, 2023

<h2>Acknowledgements</h2>
<p>We acknowledge the ECML/PKDD community for sharing this dataset.</p>
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is <a href=""https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Root-mean-square_deviation""> Root Mean Square Error (RMSE) </a>. 
<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p><strong>For every trip in the dataset</strong>, submission files should contain two columns: TRIP_ID and TRAVEL_TIME.</p>
<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>TRIP_ID, TRAVEL_TIME<br />T1,150<br /><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">T9,80<br />T9,100<br /></span><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">T10,500<br /></span><span style=""font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.4em;"">etc.</span></pre>","# Dataset Description

<p>We provide a dataset for all the 442 taxis running in the city of Porto, in Portugal in one year. These taxis operate through a dispatch central, using mobile terminals installed in the vehicles. We classify each ride into three categories: A) taxi central based, B) stand-based or C) non-taxi central based. For A), we provide an anonymized id, when such information is available from the telephone call. The last two categories refer to services that were requested directly to the taxi drivers on a B) taxi stand or on a C) random street.</p>

<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>train.csv</strong> - the training set</li>
<li><strong>test_public.csv</strong> - the testing set without the targets</li>
<li><strong>sampleSubmission.csv</strong> - a sample submission file in the correct format</li>
<li><strong>metaData_taxistandsID_name_GPSlocation.csv</strong> -meta data of the taxi stands  including id and location.</li>

</ul>
<h2>Data fields</h2>
<ul>
<p>Each sample contains 9 fields.  </p>
<li><strong>TRIP_ID: (String)</strong> - An unique identifier for each trip;
<li><strong>CALL_TYPE: (char)</strong> - Category of the ride. It may contain one of three possible values:
  <ul>
   <li> ¡®A¡¯ if this trip was dispatched from the central </li>
   <li>¡®B¡¯ if this trip was demanded directly to a taxi driver on a specific stand </li>
   <li>¡®C¡¯ otherwise (i.e. a trip demanded on a random street </li>
</ul>

<li><strong>ORIGIN_CALL: (integer)</strong> - An unique identifier for the phone number to call the taxi. It identifies the trip¡¯s customer if CALL_TYPE=¡¯A¡¯. Otherwise, it assumes a NULL value;
<li><strong>ORIGIN_STAND: (integer) </strong> - An unique identifier for the taxi stand. It identifies the starting point of the trip if CALL_TYPE=¡¯B¡¯. Otherwise, it assumes a NULL value;
<li><strong>TAXI_ID: (integer) </strong> - An unique identifier for the taxi that performed each trip;
<li><strong>TIMESTAMP: (integer) </strong> - Unix Timestamp (in seconds). It identifies the trip¡¯s start; 
<li><strong>DAYTYPE: (char)</strong> - It identifies the daytype of the trip¡¯s start. It assumes one of three possible values:
<ul>
 <li> ¡®A¡¯ if this trip started on a normal day or weekend </li>
 <li> ¡®B¡¯ if this trip started on a holiday or other special day </li>
 <li>¡®C¡¯ if the trip started on a day before a type-B day </li>
</ul>
<li><strong>MISSING_DATA: (Boolean)</strong> - It is FALSE when the GPS data stream is complete and TRUE whenever one (or more) locations are missing
<li><strong>POLYLINE: (String):</strong> - A list of GPS coordinates (i.e. WGS84 format) mapped as a string. The beginning and the end of the string are identified with brackets (i.e. [ and ]). Each pair of coordinates is also identified by the same brackets as [LONGITUDE, LATITUDE]. The coordinates were recorded every 15 seconds during the trip. The first item represents the starting point and the last item corresponds to the destination.
</ul>

<strong>The travel time of the trip (the prediction target of this project) is defined as the (number of points-1) x 15 seconds. For example, a trip with 101 data points in POLYLINE has a length of (101-1) * 15 = 1500 seconds. Some trips have missing data points in POLYLINE, indicated by MISSING_DATA column, and it is part of the challenge how you utilize this knowledge. </strong>",False,True
6815,project-2023-mpc-m1,"# Description

This dataset contains 13397 house prices composed of 20 different features:

*    id - unique identified for a house
*   pricePrice - is prediction target
*  bedroomsNumber - of Bedrooms/House
*  bathroomsNumber - of bathrooms/bedrooms
* sqft_livingsquare - footage of the home
* sqft_lotsquare - footage of the lot
*  floorsTotal - floors (levels) in house
*    waterfront - House which has a view to a waterfront
*   view - An index from 0 to 4 of how good the view of the property was
*  condition - How good the condition is ( Overall )
*    grade - overall grade given to the housing unit, based on King County grading system
*    sqft_above - square footage of house apart from basement
*    sqft_basement - square footage of the basement
*    yr_built - Built Year
*    yr_renovated - Year when house was renovated
*    zipcode - zip
*    lat - Latitude coordinate
*    long - Longitude coordinate
*    sqft_living15 - The square footage of interior housing living space for the nearest 15 neighbors
*    sqft_lot15 - The square footage of the land lots of the nearest 15 neighbors

The purpose of this project is to predict the price of unknown houses.
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is [Mean Squarred Error](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mean_squared_error). 
 $$MSE = \frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^n (Yi-\hat{Y}i)^2$$

## Submission Format
For every house in the dataset</strong>, submission files should contain two columns: ID and Price.","# Dataset Description

The file 'houses.csv' contains 15762 houses represented by multiple information as explained in the section 'Overview'. The file 'house_competition.csv' contains 2365 new houses for which we do not have the price. The purpose is to predict the price of each new house using regression algorithms that we have seen in this course.",False,True
6816,comment-crush,"# Description

Are you trying to begin with NLP? 
This may be the proper competition for you, or maybe not ??.
The competition aims to classify whether or not the sentiment associated with a particular comment is positive. It is of instrumental importance to the service industry. With the advent of emojis, it has become even more challenging to identify the correct meaning of a comment via ML models.
If you want to test your robustness to classify the comments irrespective of sarcasm and build a model to decipher the meaning of what a review intends to say, dive straight into the competition.
So, no time to dilly dally (iykyk). Get Started.
## Acknowledgements
The dataset is an amalgamation of sentiment-labeled sentences datasets from UCI and additions by Jha Rohan and PV Sekhar.
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation criterion is categorization accuracy. Upload the submission file in the format as described in the Data section.","# Dataset Description

The train.csv contains the comment id, comment and a column positive, which highlights whether the statement is positive (1) or not (0).
test.csv follows the same structure.

## Files

*   **Dataset_Kaggle.csv** - the training set
*   **Test_upload.csv** - the test set
*   **Sample Submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

## Columns

*   `Id` - comment id.
*   `Comment`?- The comment to be analysed.
*   `Positive` - The comment is positive or not.",False,True
