6824,cs-475-2023-generative-models-sentiment-analysis,"# Description

<p>In this short project, we will develop a sentiment analysis tool. We have provided a data set containing short customer reviews, or snippets of reviews, for products. Each has been labeled as a positive or negative review. For instance, below is an example of a positive review.</p>
<pre>&lt;p&gt;i downloaded a trial version of computer associates ez firewall and antivirus and fell in love with a computer security system all over again&lt;/p&gt;</pre>
and a negative one <pre>&lt;p&gt;i dont especially like how music files are unstructured ; basically they are just dumped into one folder with no organization , like you might have in windows explorer folders and subfolders&lt;/p&gt;</pre>
<p>from the training set. We will use generative models to learn to classify such review sentences into positive and negative classes.

We will use the word occurrence features: if a particular word (or more generally, a token, which may include punctuation, numbers, etc.) w occurs in an example, the corresponding feature is set to 1, otherwise to 0.</p>
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is classification accuracy.</p>",# Dataset Description,False,True
6825,cs-475-2023-generative-models-for-mnist,"# Description

<p>This is a handwritten digit recognition competition.</p>
Data
<pre>&lt;p&gt;Each sample is a 24x24 binary image. We vectorize each image as a 1x576 vector and stack N samples to represent the dataset as a matrix X of size Nx576. Each label takes value y in the set {0,1,2,...,9}.&lt;/p&gt;</pre>
Data
<pre>&lt;p&gt;Sometimes it will be convenient to represent each label as a one-hot-vector, with one element set to one and the rest zero. The dataset labels can then be represented as a matrix, Y \in {0,1}^{Nx10} such that the sum of each column adds up to 1.&lt;/p&gt;</pre>
File format
<pre>&lt;p&gt;
mnist/train/data
mnist/train/labels 
mnist/val/data 
mnist/val/labels
mnist/kaggle/data
&lt;/p&gt;</pre>
<p>
There are three datasets:<br>
large_train: training data (7000 samples)<br>
val: validation data (2000 samples)<br>
kaggle: kaggle competition test data (1000 samples, no labels provided)
</p>
<p>
You should train on the training data and validate your models using the validation data. Once you are happy with your performance, you should predict the samples in kaggle and submit the results (see Evaluation).
</p>

----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is classification accuracy. <br><br>

Submission Format<br><br>

For every image in the dataset, submission files should contain two columns: Id and predicted label. Id is the index of each image in the gaggle set and predicted label is one of {0,1,2,...,9}.<br><br>

The file should contain a header and have the following format:<br><br>

Id,Prediction<br>
0,2<br>
1,5<br>
2,6<br>
3,0<br>
4,2<br>
</p>",# Dataset Description,False,True
6826,predict-the-building-safety-under-the-earthquake,"# Description

## Goal of the Competition

The goal of this competition is to predict the structural response of steel frames subjected to seismic loads. You will develop a machine learning model to estimate the maximum interstorey displacement given its structural properties, acting gravity, and seismic loads.

## Context

Earthquakes are natural phenomena that occur when the Earth's crust shakes due to the movement of tectonic plates. These movements can cause a range of consequences, from mild tremors to devastating earthquakes that can have severe consequences for the affected regions.

Earthquakes can also have devastating consequences. They can cause significant damage to buildings, roads, and other infrastructure, resulting in loss of life and economic disruption. In some cases, earthquakes can trigger other natural disasters, such as landslides, tsunamis, and volcanic eruptions. One of the most devastating earthquakes in recent history happened in Turkey and Syria in 2023 and caused the death of more than 50,000 people.

Structural engineers use various types of seismic analysis to evaluate the behavior of buildings and other structures subjected to earthquake forces. The most precise one is Nonlinear Time History (NLTH) analysis. NLTH analysis is particularly important for structures that are critical to public safety, such as hospitals, nuclear power plants, and high-rise buildings. It is also used in the design of structures with complex or irregular configurations, where the response to seismic forces cannot be accurately predicted using simpler analysis methods. However, this type of analysis is also the most time-consuming one, and given the iterative nature of structural design, structural engineers are usually discouraged to use it opting for more simple and less accurate linear analysis. Having the machine learning model to rapidly estimate the global building's behavior could help engineers to accelerate NLTH analysis and consequently use it more frequently in their practice. This can lead to more efficient and cost-effective designs that provide greater safety and resilience in earthquake-prone regions.

## Acknowledgements

The competition data was synthetically created via Finite Element Model (FEM) software by Politecnico di Milano, Department of Architecture, Built Environment and Construction Engineering (DABC).

The data was created by Ph.D. candidate Aleksei Kondratenko with a help of Master's student Hassan Haidar and Assistant Professor Alper Kanyilmaz.

----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is Mean Absolute Percentage Error ([MAPE](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mean_absolute_percentage_error)). Therefore, the interpretation of this error in the case of this competition will be ""How many percentages my prediction deviates from real interstorey displacements on average"".
![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F6706536%2Facd3ff55d823e2119299339c014f74f2%2FMAPE.JPG?generation=1680786840230678&alt=media)

## Submission Format

Submission files should contain two columns: `Index` and `Max drift mm` where index goes in ascending order from 0 to 324 as it is done in **sample_submission.csv**.","# Dataset Description

The data consists of the structural properties, and responses of 2D steel moment-resisting frames under gravity and seismic loads. The data has been generated synthetically via Finite Element Model (FEM) software using nonlinear time history analysis as a type of seismic analysis. The frames were generated randomly in terms of geometry and elements' cross-sections while 11 real acceleration time histories have been used as seismic input. The dataset consists of a similar number of data points for each accelerogram. 2D frames were used to approximate 3D buildings' behavior, therefore the nodal masses were applied respected to the seismic tributary area of the moment resisting frame.

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F6706536%2F71e20ee630bd2144e852ade56ed191bf%2Ftributary.JPG?generation=1680787125104890&alt=media)

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format. Simple linear regression was used as an ML algorithm to get the results there.

## Columns

*   `Column fy Mpa ` - the yielding limit of columns' steel material
*   `Beam fy Mpa `?- the yielding limit of beams' steel material
*   `Columns 1-3 I mm4*10^6 `?- a moment of inertia of the bottom columns' (1-3 floors) cross-section 
*   `Columns 4-6 I mm4*10^6 `?- a moment of inertia of the upper columns' (4-6 floors) cross-section 
*   `Columns 1-3 A mm2  `?- an area of the bottom (1-3 floors) columns' cross-section 
*   `BColumns 4-6 A mm2  `?- an area of the upper (1-3 floors) columns' cross-section
*   `Beam I mm4*10^6 `?- a moment of inertia of the beams' columns' cross-section
*   `Spans  `?- frame's number of spans
*   `Number of floors  `?- frame's number of floors
*   `Floor height m   `?- the height of each floor
*   `Span width m `?- the width of each span
*   `LLRS tributary width m `?- seismic tributary width of lateral load resisting system under consideration
*   `Floor mass kg  `?- the mass of one floor (excluding the frame's self-weight)
*   `Facade Load kN/m `?- the gravity load from facade acting on beams
*   `PGA g  `?- peak ground acceleration of the acting ground motion
*   `Magnitude `?- the magnitude of the acting ground motion
*   `Distance to fault km `?- distance to fault of the acting ground motion
*   `Period s `?- structural period (fundamental)
*   `Max drift mm `?- maximum interstorey displacement (this is a value you need to predict)
*   `Final Dead Load `?- acting dead load on beams (excluding self-weight) considering gravity loads' tributary width
*   `Final Live Load  `?- acting live load on beams considering gravity loads' tributary width
*   `soil_class__B `?- 1 is soil class of acting ground motion is B (according to Eurocode 8), 0 otherwise
*   `soil_class__C `?- 1 is soil class of acting ground motion is C (according to Eurocode 8), 0 otherwise",False,True
6827,dev-o-thon-2k23,"# References 

#### Reference & Inspiration for Participants
There are various prebuilt models based on **lead-scoring algorithms**. When developing a **lead-scoring model**, it can be helpful to keep in mind prebuilt lead-scoring companies that offer similar services. These companies can provide inspiration for your algorithm development and help you to understand the types of features and techniques that are commonly used in the industry. Some examples of prebuilt lead-scoring companies include:


    **Infer**: Infer offers a lead-scoring platform that uses predictive analytics and machine learning to score leads based on their likelihood to convert. 


    **MadKudu**: Their platform provides a simple dashboard that allows sales and marketing teams to easily prioritize their leads based on their likelihood to convert.


----------------------
# Description

**Introduction**
In **Dev-a-thon,** participants are challenged to develop a **lead-scoring algorithm** that can accurately predict the likelihood of a customer making a purchase on an e-commerce platform based on** accuracy and recall value**.

**Recall value** is an essential metric in lead scoring, as it measures the algorithm's ability to correctly identify all positive cases (i.e., customers who are likely to make a purchase) out of all actual positive cases in the dataset. In other words, it measures how well the algorithm is able to capture all potential customers who are likely to make a purchase.

The competition judges the participants' algorithms based on both accuracy and recall value. The winning entry is the one that provides the **most accurate predictions while also achieving the highest recall value**. This ensures that the algorithm is accurate and effective at identifying potential customers who are likely to make a purchase. The competition is designed to provide valuable insights and solutions to** e-commerce** businesses looking to increase their sales and revenue by accurately identifying potential customers who are likely to make a purchase.

**Email : [ylp@growthjockey.com](mailto:ylp@growthjockey.com) , [diviyansh.modi@growthjockey.com](mailto:diviyansh.modi@growthjockey.com)**

----------------------
# Judging & Prizing

 
**Judging & Prizes:** The evaluation of the model will be based strictly on its **recall value and accuracy**, as previously mentioned. To conduct the evaluation, the provided **.pynb** file will be tested using the same parameters as the original Excel file, but with modified values.

**Growth Jockey's YLP** has successfully organized several events, including **Cas-a-thon 2k23 and Dat-a-thon 2k23**, where participants have received over **INR 50000** in **E-Commerce Cash Vouchers**. As an acknowledgment of their efforts, participants were awarded a certificate of merit and given the opportunity to attend an exclusive founder's talk. Moreover, participants had a valuable chance to network with Growth Jockey's leadership team. The prizes of the **Dev-a-thon** Include prizes worth upto **1 Lakh Rupees**.

----------------------
# Getting Started


**Getting Started**
Here are few Suggestions for the participants so to smoothen their journey through Make-o-thon 



1. **Understand the problem:** The problem is to develop an algorithm that can accurately predict the likelihood of a customer making a purchase on an e-commerce platform based on both accuracy and recall value.
2. **Gather and preprocess the data**: Growth Jockey will provide you with a dataset containing information about various customers. The dataset may contain various features such as sale date , subscribed to newsletter etc.  Preprocessing the data involves cleaning the data, removing any irrelevant features, and normalizing the data to make it more suitable for analysis.
3. **Develop and test your model**: Once you have preprocessed the data, you can start developing your lead-scoring algorithm. You can use various machine learning techniques such as logistic regression, decision trees, or random forests to develop your model. Once you have developed your model, you should test it using cross-validation techniques to evaluate its accuracy and recall value.
4. **Submit your entry** After testing your model, you can submit your entry on the platform. The entry will be a .pynb file which further will be tested on different dataset values with the same parameters. They will evaluate your entry based on both accuracy and recall value, and the entry with the best overall performance will be declared the winner.


----------------------
# Score


The goal of the Dev-o-thon competition is to develop a lead scoring algorithm that can predict the probability of a customer converting into a buyer based on various features such as demographics, behavior, preferences, etc. The Benchmark Values for Submission in the Competition include following : 

| Score Matrix | Expected Score |
| --- | --- |
| F1 Score | 50% |
| Precision %'age | 66% |
| Recall %'age | 70% |


## Submission Format

The submission file should have a header and contain two columns:

- `id`: the unique identifier of each customer
- `score`: the predicted label of conversion (0 or 1)

For example:
| ID | Status |
| --- | --- |
| 985asdf5 | 0.0 |


## Evaluation Metric

The submissions will be evaluated on the **F1 score with micro averaging**. The F1 score is the harmonic mean of precision and recall, and it ranges from 0 (worst) to 1 (best). Precision is the ratio of true positives to all predicted positives, and recall is the ratio of true positives to all actual positives.

The F1 score with micro averaging can be calculated as follows:

```
F1_micro = 2 * (TP / (TP + FP)) * (TP / (TP + FN)) / ((TP / (TP + FP)) + (TP / (TP + FN)))
```

Where TP is the total number of true positives across all classes, FP is the total number of false positives across all classes, and FN is the total number of false negatives across all classes.

The F1 score with micro averaging gives equal weight to each class and each sample, and it is suitable for imbalanced datasets.

The higher the F1 score, the better the performance of the lead scoring algorithm.

## Leaderboard

The leaderboard will show the public F1 score of each submission based on a subset of the test data. The final ranking will be determined by the private F1 score based on the remaining test data, which will be revealed after the competition ends.","# Dataset Description

**Title:** Lead Scoring Algorithm Development Dataset

**Test Description** : The ""EST.csv"" is a csv file specifically designed to test your model . The Answer should be in csv format containing ID,Status Columns.

**Train Description:**
This dataset contains information about leads that a company has acquired. The purpose of this dataset is to develop a lead scoring algorithm, which will predict the likelihood of a lead becoming a customer based on their demographic and behavioral data.

The dataset is provided as a CSV file named 'Rain.csv'. It contains **28 columns** and more than **100,000 rows** of data. Each row represents a lead, and each column contains a different attribute of that lead. The columns are as follows:

**Email:** The email address of the lead
**Leads_Source**: The source through which the lead was acquired
**Work email**: The work email address of the sales person
**Job title**: The job title of the sales person
**Sales owner:** The name of the branch owner assigned to the lead
**Owner Email**: The email address of the salesperson assigned to the lead
**Territory**: The geographical territory of the lead
**Team**: The team assigned to the lead
**Product_interested**: The product(s) that the lead has shown interest in
**Lead_Zipcode**: The zipcode of the lead's location
**Lead_State**: The state of the lead's location
**Lead_city**: The city of the lead's location
**url-campaign**: The URL campaign through which the lead was acquired
**page-url**: The webpage from which the lead was acquired
**Lead_hour**: The time of day when the lead was acquired
**Fulfillment Status:** The status of the lead's order fulfillment
**Fulfilled at**: The date and time when the lead's order was fulfilled
**Accepts Marketing**: Whether the lead has given permission to receive marketing communications
**Shipping**: The shipping method used for the lead's order
**Total**: The total value of the lead's order
**Discount Amount:** The amount of discount applied to the lead's order
****Payment Method:**** The payment method used for the lead's order
**Refunded Amount**: The amount refunded for the lead's order
**Vendor**: The vendor associated with the lead's order
**Outstanding Balance**: The outstanding balance on the lead's order
**Source**: The original source of the lead
**Status**: The Target Value of The Lead
**ID**: The unique identifier for the lead

The target variable is explicitly defined in the dataset and it is implied that the goal is to predict which leads are most likely to become customers. The algorithm will be evaluated on the basis of its recall and precision metrics.

This dataset is suitable for data scientists and machine learning engineers who are interested in developing lead scoring algorithms. It can also be used to study customer behavior and to identify trends in lead acquisition and conversion.",False,True
6828,2023-kaggle-ai-report,"# Description

## Goal of the Competition

More than a hundred AI papers are published every day, making it exceedingly hard to keep up with current innovations. The goal of this competition is to tap into the diverse expertise of the Kaggle community to centralize and summarize the rapid advancements in AI from the past two years. The Kaggle community has a breadth and depth of AI experience which extends beyond the reach of any single individual or research group. We aim to share your collective perspective with the broader research community.

## Context

In this analytics competition, participants will write an essay on one of the following seven topics, with a prompt to describe what the community has learned over the past 2 years of working and experimenting with:

  - **Text data**
  - **Image and/or video data**
  - **Tabular and/or time series data**
  - **Kaggle Competitions**
  - **Generative AI**
  - **AI ethics**
  - **Other** (anything that does not fall into any other category)

Kaggle has provided a collection of [writeups](https://www.kaggle.com/code/paultimothymooney/every-kaggle-competition-solution-writeup-to-csv/output) and a collection of [scholarly articles](https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/Cornell-University/arxiv) in the official competition dataset, but participants are free to use any resources. Participants will also take part in a peer feedback process that involves reviewing three peer essays, and your  submission will be assessed by an expert grading panel of seven Kaggle Grandmasters.  Winning essays will be aggregated in a post-competition publication and given authorship credit.

In response to demand and feedback from prior analytics competitions, this competition also pilots a format in which all eligible participants will be ranked on the leaderboard and awarded points based on their performance. Results will show on your profile, just as in a standard competition. 

See the [submission instructions](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/2023-kaggle-ai-report/overview/submission-instructions) and [evaluation](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/2023-kaggle-ai-report/overview/evaluation) pages for more detail.

<iframe width=""699"" height=""630"" src=""https://www.youtube.com/embed/UskiEJaqLCw"" title=""YouTube video | Kaggle AI Report 2023 | Kaggle"" frameborder=""0"" allow=""encrypted-media"" allowfullscreen></iframe>
----------------------
# Timeline

- **May 11, 2023** - Start Date
- **July 5, 2023** - Team merger deadline
  - This is the last day participants may join or merge teams
- **July 5, 2023** -  Start of the peer-feedback period
  - You are required to make the notebook containing your essay public prior to this deadline
  - You have from the start of the peer-feedback period until the final notebook submission deadline to provide feedback to three of your peers
- **July 16, 2023** -  Final notebook submission deadline
  - All peer-feedback is due by the final notebook submission deadline
  - Authors are encouraged to make updates in response to peer feedback received prior to the final submission deadline

All submissions are evaluated after the deadline and all deadlines are at 11:59 PM UTC on the corresponding day unless otherwise noted. The competition organizers reserve the right to update the contest timeline if they deem it necessary. 

----------------------
# Prizes

- $10,000 for each of the top essays in each of the seven categories (based on the [evaluation criteria](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/2023-kaggle-ai-report/overview/evaluation))
- Winning essays will be aggregated in a post-competition publication and given authorship credit. 
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions to the 2023 Kaggle AI Report competition will be assessed by an expert grading panel of seven Kaggle Grandmasters. Each of the seven topic areas has a dedicated grader who will be responsible for applying the evaluation rubric to essays submitted to their section. The Kaggle Team will also participate in the evaluation process in order to ensure a fair and standardized evaluation process. In the event of tied scores, the judging panel will stack rank submissions within the tied group.

To learn more about how your notebook will be assessed, see the resources below:

- The eligibility checklist - this checklist provides guidance on how to structure your essays. If you follow these guidelines, your submission will be considered for the prize.
- The essay-grading rubric - this rubric will be used to score and rank eligible essays.

