----------------------
# Submission Instructions

We will be using the Kaggle competition infrastructure in order to rank participants and award points/rankings. **This entails creating and submitting a `submission.csv` file in addition to writing your essay.** The purpose of this extra step is to assign teams, allow teams to merge, and create a leaderboard of participants.

The submission file should contain a header and have the following format:

    type,value
     essay_category,'copy/paste the exact category that you are submitting to'
     essay_url,'http://www.kaggle.com/your_username/your_public_notebook'
     feedback1_url,'http://www.kaggle.com/.../your_1st_peer_feedback'
     feedback2_url,'http://www.kaggle.com/.../your_2nd_peer_feedback'
     feedback3_url,'http://www.kaggle.com/.../your_3rd_peer_feedback'

You will modify the four placeholder URLs to contain links to your 1x essay + 3x peer feedbacks.

Some important notes about the submission process:

- All teams will have a score of 1 until grading is completed.
- Submitting this file signals your intent to participate. It is not an obligation to participate and you will not be ranked if you decide not to participate.
- You may submit multiple times and there is no harm in leaving placeholder values until you complete them. We encourage submitting this file as soon as you decide you may wish to enter. This will allow you to join teams and give us a rough indication of how many essays to expect for the grading process.
- Each team should write a single essay focusing on a single topic. 
- Your most recent submission will be the one we use to pull your essay and peer feedback. Please ensure you submit a final, updated file once you have peer feedback completed. 

----------------------
# Peer Feedback Instructions

Peer-feedback must take the form of a public comment on the bottom of a Kaggle Notebook that is attached to the AI Report Competition. Your feedback should provide constructive criticism to the original author and qualitatively assess how well they met the essay grading rubric expectations.

- Requirement: Provide comments with peer-feedback for a total of 3 different essays.
- Prioritize peer-feedback for notebooks on the same topic you wrote about.
- Try to not provide feedback on notebooks that already have three constructive comments. If every notebook on your topic already has 3 or more comments providing peer-feedback, you can instead provide peer-feedback on a notebook from a different topic that does not yet have 3 comments on it.
- Teams with one member and teams with multiple members have the same requirement to submit 3 reviews in total per team.

Guidance for providing high quality peer-feedback:
 - Provide an accurate summary of the notebook being reviewed
 - Assess whether appropriate context was set, and that all necessary
references were cited
 - Evaluate the overall quality of the writing and the composition of the
work
 - Evaluate the significance of the work and interest to the ML community
 - Verify the correctness of the claims made within the essay
 - Provide feedback concerning potential flaws for any unsupported claims 
 - Assess whether there exists content that might be considered inappropriate (e.g.
plagiarism, academic misconduct, or incorrect statements)","# Dataset Description

Participants are encouraged to reference resources such as **winning solution writeups** and **scholarly articles**.  We have included a collection of writeups and scholarly articles for your convenience, but participants are free to use additional resources as well. There is no requirement to refer to any of these files using code.
 - **kaggle_writeups.csv** - metadata from recent Kaggle Competition Writeups
  - Source: https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/kaggle/meta-kaggle
 - **arXiv_metadata.json** - metadata for recent arXiv publications
  - Source: https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/Cornell-University/arxiv
 - **sample_submission.csv** -a template file that you can modify for making your submission",True,True
6829,summer-internship-cistup-iisc-2023,"# Description

##Test 3 (Machine Learning, Python) - Round 1

Fill in the missing code in the provided `Template` file and submit the modified code using the `Submit to Competition` button.


**! IMPORTANT**:
- You must use the provided `Template`, and only add your code where indicated (using `#write your code here#`).
- Only submitted notebooks that have been shared with *vpattanaik* (before the competition deadline) will be evaluated.

**For more details please see [User Guide](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/summer-internship-cistup-iisc-2023/overview/user-guide).**
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions will be evaluated using F1-score, based on the entire dataset. The leaderboard score will be based on 70% of the dataset. Candidates with the highest score will be selected for the next round. 
----------------------
# User Guide

Please follow these steps to make your submissions:

1. Goto the `Code` tab.

2. There under `Notebooks` search for `Template`. Click on the notebook titled `Template`.

3. Once the notebook is open, click on `Copy & Edit` on the top-right corner of the page.

4. Rename the notebook using the textbox at the top-left of the page. Look for `Fork of Template`. Using your Kaggle username as the notebook name will make it easier for the host to recognize and evaluate your code.

5. Now search the notebook for `#write your code here#`.

6. Read through the notebook text (i.e., written in Markdown) and replace only the text `#write your code here#` with the appropriate code. Note that the notebook text provides equations that need to be translated into code.

7. Once you have filled in the missing code, click on `Save Version` on the top-right of the page.

8. Then click on `Save` (on the bottom-right side of the page). Before clicking on `Save` make sure, `VERSION TYPE` is set to `Save & Run All (Commit)`.

9. As the notebook is saved, you will see a notification in the `Active Events` section on the bottom-left of the screen. If the status says `Successful`, then you are ready to ready to submit the code. Else, check the code for bugs/errors.

10. If you are unable to find the issue, you can re-fork the `Template` and start again.

11. If the status is `Successful`, look for the `Submit to competition` button on the right side of the screen. Click on `Submit to competition` and then on the `Submit` button.

12. Then click on `Submit` again. This will take a few minutes.
If you followed the steps correctly, you should see a notification stating that the submission was successful.

13. Once the submission is complete, `Share` the notebook, by adding *vpattanaik* as a collaborator. Set `PERMISSION` to `Can View`, and click `Save`.

***

**If while submitting the notebook you see a message like this:**
>Summer Internship 2023 @ CiSTUP, IISc
Competition submission
Submission file not found: submission.csv`

**Please follow these steps:**
14. Go to the competition home page.

15. Towards the right side of the screen you will find the `Submit Predictions` button, click it.

16. Here you will see two options: `File Upload` and `Notebook`. Select `Notebook`.

17. Select the notebook version (from the drop-down) you would like to submit, and click `Submit`.

18. Here you will see the `Public Scores` of your submission. Select the checkbox, with the highest `Public Score`.

19. That's it. Your submission is complete. The `Public Scores` that is selected by will be visible on the `Leaderboard` used for the evaluation. 

20. Note that `test accuracy` printed in the submitted notebook will also be used for evaluation. And that you are only supposed to modify the code, where it is explicitly stated.","# Dataset Description

## Files

*   **Full_Data.csv** - The input file can be found below.
*   **Output_Data.csv** - Output file. The available code (see *Code* -> *Template*) exports the file that will be used for evaluation.

## Columns

*   `RowIdx` - Row index (** *please ignore this column*)
*   `Attribute1`?- numerical input data
*   `Attribute2`?- numerical input data
*   `Attribute3`?- numerical input data
*   `Attribute4`?- numerical input data
*   `OutputClass`?- Output class (0 or 1)",False,True
