6839,budgeted-model-training-iccv-2023-rcv-workshop,"# Description

## Motivation

Despite the success of CNNs and, more recently, transformers, the scale of deep learning models has grown exponentially due to the rapid development of computational resources. Going forward, it is critical to focus on the practical training and inference efficiency of these models, as well as ensuring that even the largest models have viable real-world applications. Moreover, compute efficiency of computer vision models is becoming increasingly crucial for downstream tasks such as segmentation, object tracking, and action recognition, among others.

There exist several different directions towards making deep learning efficient for computer vision, thereby leading to a reduction in the required computational memory or the associated training and inference time. This competition will aim at bringing research efforts into practice for one such direction: *budget-aware model training*. Most research that exists focuses on making deep learning methods efficient, however, the resource associated with real-world AI devices can vary drastically, and a method termed efficient for a certain choice of resource budgets might be completely inefficient for a different one. This competition will also focus on methods that aim at performing budget-aware model training in best possible ways under limited GPU memory memory as well as limited training time.

**This challenge is part of the ICCV 2023 Resource-Efficient Deep Learning (RCV) Workshop to be held between Oct 2-5, 2023 in Paris, France.**

## Problem description
As part of this budgeted model training challenge, we pose the problem of the classification of ImageNet100. The goal is to build the best possible classifier for classifying the samples of ImageNet100 into one of the pre-defined 100 classes. However, there are certain rules to be respected to qualify as a winning solution. 
1. For final evaluation, each submitted solution will be used to train ImageNet100 with a different set of 100 classes than those revealed here. The score on this corresponding test set will be used to decide the performance of the model. 
2. All the models will be trained on a P100 GPU card along with 4 cpu cores for a maximum of 9 hours.
3. The GPU memory consumption should not exceed 6 GB at any point of the training process of the final evaluation.
4. Note that your code should not use any pretrained models and we will be training your models in the absence of internet, so make sure your code does not depend on any external pretrained models. 
5. Together with your code (only python scripts), you will be required to submit the run environment as a .yaml that will be used to run the code.
6. The main script file should be named main.py and should take only one command line argument --data_dir to produce a submission.csv in the current working directory.

This competition will be running in two phases: **Phase I** and **Phase II**. 

**Phase I**: This phase is a standard classification problem where the goal is to maximize the accuracy on the test set and improve the ranking on the leaderboard. This phase of the competition will be hosted here on Kaggle and the leaderboard of Kaggle will be used to rank the solutions. Note that this phase will only use accuracy as the evaluation measure. Although this phase does not take the GPU memory and time constraint into consideration, participants are recommended to adhere to an approximate estimate and use the leaderboard to assess the performance of their efficient models that could pass on to the next Phase. **Using very large networks and pretrained models, that cannot later qualify the time and memory constraint criterion, is highly discouraged, since this will improve your ranking in Phase I, but will not be able to qualify you for Phase II.** See the rules stated above for more details.

**Phase II**: We will post a baseline solution and all solutions that obtain an accuracy higher than baseline accuracy will be eligible to submit their solutions for Phase II. For details on the evaluation of the solutions for this phase, see the final evaluation details describe for the final evaluation above. This phase will be in an offline mode and results can be viewed at [Phase II Leaderboard](https://sites.google.com/view/rcv2023/leaderboard-workshop-challenges). We will keep updating the leaderboard on a rolling basis, however, every solution can be expected to get reflected on the leaderboard in 5 days from the day of the submission. Every team will be able to submit their two best solutions in one instance. For the start date of this phase, check the timeline page.

## Challenge Organizer team
For details related to the organizing team, see https://sites.google.com/view/rcv2023/organizing-committee

----------------------
# Timeline

## Timeline (in UTC)
- Phase I starts: April 12, 2023
- Phase II starts: June 12, 2023
- Phase II ends: July 30, 2023
- Final leaderboard update: Aug 05, 2023
- Notification of results: Aug 05, 2023
- Submission of report: Aug 09, 2023
----------------------
# Evaluation

All submissions will be evaluated with different metrics at the two phases of the competition.

- **Phase I**: This phase is hosted on Kaggle and will be using accuracy as the evaluation measure. 

- **Phase II**: This phase will run in an offline manner in which the qualifying participants from Phase I will submit their code scripts to be re-run on another set of private data under 6GB of GPU (RTX8000) memory limit and 9 hours of time constraints and the accuracy on the test set of this private data will be used for the final rankings.
The following machine configuration will be used for training your models/frameworks -
GPU : Quadro RTX 8000 
GPU Mem. Limit : 6GB
Total Time Limit : 9 Hours 
CPU Cores : 4
RAM : 32GB

## Submission File for Phase I

For each `image_id`, you should predict the corresponding image label in the `label` column

The file should be named submission.csv and contain the columns `image_id` and `label` in the following format:
~~~~
image_id,label
asdfghjklo,0
zxcvbnmlkj,1
~~~~

## Submission File for Phase II
Each qualifying team is required to submit scripts that will be run and evaluated on a private dataset. The details of qualification to Phase II and format of code scripts is specified in the **Description** tab.
----------------------
# Prizes

## Prizes

Note that Kaggle platform is only used to run Phase I of the challenge. The final prizes will be based on Phase II results and the details can be found here: https://sites.google.com/view/rcv2023/workshop-challenges","# Dataset Description

**Imagenet-100 Set1**
This dataset is a subset of ImageNet-1k created by choosing 100 classes of imagenet and splitting them into train, val and test sets.

## Files

*   **train.csv** - a csv file containing training image names and their corresponding labels.
*   **val.csv** - a csv file containing validation image names and their corresponding labels.
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

## Folders
* **train** - contains training images
* **val** - contains validation images
* **test** - contains test images",False,True
6840,2023-deeplearning-hw2-transformer,"# Description

# Introduce
This is the Kaggle competition for HW2 in NYCU Deep Learning Course.
Given the headline and the content of the news, you need to train a model to correctly classify the news into 4 different category:

1. Sports
2. Business
3. Tech
4. Media

# Upload
You need to upload the result file for the submission
Details of the format, please check the Evaluation and Data
You need to upload related file to NewE3 (Check the announcement on NewE3 for details)

