6885,kidney-stone-detection,"# Description

The ""Kidney Stone Dataset"" comprises information on 200 patients diagnosed with kidney stones, encompassing various attributes such as age, gender, stone type, and laboratory test results. Additionally, the dataset incorporates a continuous target variable, ""Risk of Stone,"" which reflects the likelihood of developing kidney stones. This dataset is intended for employment in tasks such as predicting the probability of kidney stones based on patients' characteristics and diagnostic test outcomes.

The aforementioned dataset can serve as the foundation for a competition aimed at developing models that can accurately predict the risk of kidney stones. The competition participants will be required to utilize the dataset to develop predictive models that can determine the risk of kidney stones for new patients. The task will involve developing and training machine learning models that can analyze the relevant attributes and generate precise predictions.

The competition offers an opportunity for researchers, data scientists, and machine learning enthusiasts to apply their skills and knowledge in the field of predictive modeling. The competition outcome will contribute towards the advancement of knowledge in the field of medical diagnosis and pave the way for the development of accurate and reliable prediction models.

credits of dataset to :
https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/harshghadiya/kidneystone
----------------------
# Evaluation

Root Mean Square (RMS) is a popular evaluation method used in many fields such as signal processing, statistics, and data analysis. It is used to measure the difference between two sets of values by computing the square root of the average of the squared differences. The RMS equation can be expressed as follows:

RMS = sqrt((1/n) * Sum(i=1 to n) of (xi - x)^2)

where n is the number of values being compared, xi represents each value in the dataset, and x is the mean of the dataset. The RMS is a useful metric because it penalizes large deviations more than smaller ones, giving a more accurate representation of the overall difference between two sets of values.","# Dataset Description

The kidney-stone-dataset.csv is a comma-separated values file containing data on patients with kidney stones. The file has 255 rows and 21 columns, with each row representing a patient and each column providing information on various characteristics and laboratory test results. The dataset also includes a target variable, ""Risk of Stone"", which is a continuous variable representing the risk of developing kidney stones. This dataset can be used for tasks such as predicting the risk of kidney stones based on patient characteristics and test results.

This kidney stone dataset contains 12 features related to the risk of developing kidney stones, including age, gender, weight, height, family history, and various blood and urine measurements. The dataset includes 180 records, and the target variable is a continuous value representing the risk of developing kidney stones. The dataset is useful for building predictive models to identify individuals at high risk for kidney stones, which can aid in the development of prevention and treatment strategies.

The kidney-stone-dataset.csv is a dataset containing information on 255 patients with kidney stones, including characteristics such as age, gender, and stone type, as well as laboratory test results. The dataset also includes a target variable, ""Risk of Stone"", which is a continuous variable representing the risk of developing kidney stones. This dataset can be used for tasks such as predicting the risk of kidney stones based on patient characteristics and test results.

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format",False,True
