6900,desafio-do-titanic-ai-league-brain,"# Description

## ????? Ahoy, welcome to Kaggle! You¡¯re in the right place. 
This is the legendary Titanic ML competition ¨C the best, first challenge for you to dive into ML competitions and familiarize yourself with how the Kaggle platform works.

The competition is simple: use machine learning to create a model that predicts which passengers survived the Titanic shipwreck.

Read on or watch the video below to explore more details. Once you¡¯re ready to start competing, click on the <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/account/login?returnUrl=%2Fc%2Ftitanic"" target=""_blank"">""Join Competition button</a> to create an account and gain access to the <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/c/titanic/data"" target=""_blank"">competition data</a>. Then check out <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/alexisbcook/titanic-tutorial"">Alexis Cook¡¯s Titanic Tutorial</a> that walks you through step by step how to make your first submission!

<a href=""https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8yZMXCaFshs&amp;feature=youtu.be"" target=""_blank"">
  <img width=""699"" align=""center"" src=""https://storage.googleapis.com/kaggle-media/welcome/video_thumbnail.jpg"">
</a>

## The Challenge
The sinking of the Titanic is one of the most infamous shipwrecks in history.

On April 15, 1912, during her maiden voyage, the widely considered ¡°unsinkable¡± RMS Titanic sank after colliding with an iceberg. Unfortunately, there weren¡¯t enough lifeboats for everyone onboard, resulting in the death of 1502 out of 2224 passengers and crew.

While there was some element of luck involved in surviving, it seems some groups of people were more likely to survive than others.

In this challenge, we ask you to build a predictive model that answers the question: ¡°what sorts of people were more likely to survive?¡± using passenger data (ie name, age, gender, socio-economic class, etc).

> #### Recommended Tutorial
> We highly recommend [Alexis Cook¡¯s Titanic Tutorial](https://www.kaggle.com/alexisbcook/titanic-tutorial) that walks you through making your very first submission step by step and [this starter notebook](https://www.kaggle.com/code/gusthema/titanic-competition-w-tensorflow-decision-forests) to get started.

## Overview of How Kaggle¡¯s Competitions Work
1. **Join the Competition**
Read about the challenge description, accept the Competition Rules and gain access to the competition dataset.
1.  **Get to Work**
Download the data, build models on it locally or on Kaggle Notebooks (our no-setup, customizable Jupyter Notebooks environment with free GPUs) and generate a prediction file.
1.  **Make a Submission**
Upload your prediction as a submission on Kaggle and receive an accuracy score.
1.  **Check the Leaderboard**
See how your model ranks against other Kagglers on our leaderboard. 
1.  **Improve Your Score**
Check out the <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/c/titanic/discussion"" target=""_blank"">discussion forum</a> to find lots of tutorials and insights from other competitors.

> #### Kaggle Lingo Video
> You may run into unfamiliar lingo as you dig into the Kaggle discussion forums and public notebooks. Check out  Dr. Rachael Tatman¡¯s [video on Kaggle Lingo](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sEJHyuWKd-s) to get up to speed!

## What Data Will I Use in This Competition?
In this competition, you¡¯ll gain access to two similar datasets that include passenger information like name, age, gender, socio-economic class, etc. One dataset is titled `train.csv` and the other is titled `test.csv`.

`Train.csv` will contain the details of a subset of the passengers on board (891 to be exact) and importantly, will reveal whether they survived or not, also known as the ¡°ground truth¡±.

The `test.csv` dataset contains similar information but does not disclose the ¡°ground truth¡± for each passenger. It¡¯s your job to predict these outcomes.

Using the patterns you find in the `train.csv` data, predict whether the other 418 passengers on board (found in `test.csv`) survived.

Check out the <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/c/titanic/data"">¡°Data¡± tab</a> to explore the datasets even further. Once you feel you¡¯ve created a competitive model, submit it to Kaggle to see where your model stands on our leaderboard against other Kagglers.

## How to Submit your Prediction to Kaggle
Once you¡¯re ready to make a submission and get on the leaderboard:

1. Click on the ¡°Submit Predictions¡± button
<img width=""699"" align=""center"" src=""https://storage.googleapis.com/kaggle-media/welcome/screen1.png"">
1. Upload a CSV file in the submission file format. You¡¯re able to submit 10 submissions a day.
<img width=""699"" align=""center"" src=""https://storage.googleapis.com/kaggle-media/welcome/screen2.png"">

## Submission File Format:
You should submit a csv file with exactly 418 entries plus a header row. Your submission will show an error if you have extra columns (beyond `PassengerId` and `Survived`) or rows.

The file should have exactly 2 columns:
- `PassengerId` (sorted in any order)
- `Survived` (contains your binary predictions: 1 for survived, 0 for deceased)

## Got it! I¡¯m ready to get started. Where do I get help if I need it?
- For Competition Help: <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/c/titanic/discussion"">Titanic Discussion Forum</a>
- Technical Help: <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/contact"">Kaggle Contact Us Page</a>

Kaggle doesn¡¯t have a dedicated support team so you¡¯ll typically find that you receive a response more quickly by asking your question in the appropriate forum. The forums are full of useful information on the data, metric, and different approaches. We encourage you to use the forums often. If you share your knowledge, you'll find that others will share a lot in turn!

## A Last Word on Kaggle Notebooks
As we mentioned before, Kaggle Notebooks is our no-setup, customizable, Jupyter Notebooks environment with free GPUs and a huge repository of community published data &amp; code.

In every competition, you¡¯ll find many Notebooks shared with incredible insights. It¡¯s an invaluable resource worth becoming familiar with. Check out this competition¡¯s Notebooks <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/c/titanic/notebooks"">here</a>.

## ???¡âReady to Compete? <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/account/login?returnUrl=%2Fc%2Ftitanic"">Join the Competition Here!</a>
----------------------
# Evaluation

<h1>Goal</h1>
<p>It is your job to predict if a passenger survived the sinking of the Titanic or not. <br>For each  in the test set, you must predict a 0 or 1 value for the  variable.</p>
<h1>Metric</h1>
<p>Your score is the percentage of passengers you correctly predict. This is known as <a target=""_blank"" href=""https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Accuracy_and_precision#In_binary_classification"">accuracy</a>.</p>
<h1>Submission File Format</h1>
<p>You should submit a csv file with exactly 418 entries <b>plus</b> a header row. Your submission will show an error if you have extra columns (beyond PassengerId and Survived) or rows.<br><br>The file should have exactly 2 columns:</p>
<ul>
<li>PassengerId (sorted in any order)</li>
<li>Survived (contains your binary predictions: 1 for survived, 0 for deceased)</li>
</ul>
<pre><b>PassengerId,Survived</b><br>892,0<br>893,1<br>894,0<br>Etc.</pre>
<p>You can download an example submission file (gender_submission.csv) on the <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/c/titanic/data"">Data page</a>.</p>
----------------------
# Frequently Asked Questions

<h1>What is a Getting Started competition?</h1>
<p>Getting Started competitions were created by Kaggle data scientists for people who have little to no machine learning background. They are a great place to begin if you are new to data science or just finished a MOOC and want to get involved in Kaggle.</p>
<p>Getting Started competitions are a non-competitive way to get familiar with Kaggle¡¯s platform, learn basic machine learning concepts, and start meeting people in the community. They have no cash prize and are on a rolling timeline.</p>
<h1>How do I create and manage a team?</h1>
<p>When you accept the competition rules, a team will be created for you. You can invite others to your team, accept a merger with another team, and update basic information like team name by going to the More &gt; <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/c/titanic/team"" target=""_blank"">Team</a> page.</p>
<p>We've heard from many Kagglers that teaming up is the best way to learn new skills AND have fun. If you don't have a teammate already, consider asking if anyone wants to team up in the <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/c/titanic/discussion"" target=""_blank"">discussion forum</a>.</p>
<h1>What are Notebooks?</h1>
<p>Kaggle Notebooks is a cloud computational environment that enables reproducible and collaborative analysis. Notebooks support scripts in Python and R, Jupyter Notebooks, and RMarkdown reports. You can visit the <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/c/titanic/notebooks"" target=""_blank"">Notebooks</a> tab to view all of the publicly shared code for the Titanic competition. For more on how to use Notebooks to learn data science, check out our <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/learn/overview"" target=""&quot;_blank"">Courses</a>!</p>
<h1>Why did my team disappear from the leaderboard?</h1>
<p>To keep with the spirit of getting-started competitions, we have implemented a two month rolling window on submissions. Once a submission is more than two months old, it will be invalidated and no longer count towards the leaderboard.</p>
<p>If your team has no submissions in the previous two months, the team will also drop from the leaderboard. This will keep the leaderboard at a manageable size, freshen it up, and prevent newcomers from getting lost in a sea of abandoned scores.</p>
<p><i>""I worked so hard to get that score! Give it back!""</i> Read more about our decision to implement a rolling leaderboard <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/c/titanic/discussion/6240"" target=""_blank"">here</a>.</p>
<h1>How do I contact Support?</h1>
<p>Kaggle does not have a dedicated support team so you¡¯ll typically find that you receive a response more quickly by asking your question in the appropriate forum. (For this competition, you¡¯ll want to use the <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/c/titanic/discussion"" target=""_blank"">Titanic discussion forum)</a>.</p>
<p>Support is only able to help with issues that are being experienced by all participants. Before contacting support, please check the discussion forum for information on your problem. If you can¡¯t find it, you can post your problem in the forum so a fellow participant or a Kaggle team member can provide help. The forums are full of useful information on the data, metric, and different approaches. We encourage you to use the forums often. If you share your knowledge, you'll find that others will share a lot in turn!</p>
<p>If your problem persists or it seems to be effective all participants then please <a href=""https://www.kaggle.com/contact"" target=""_blank"">contact us</a>.</p>","# Dataset Description

<h3>Overview</h3>
<p>The data has been split into two groups:</p>
<ul>
<li>training set (train.csv)</li>
<li>test set (test.csv)</li>
</ul>
<p><b> The training set </b>should be used to build your machine learning models. For the training set, we provide the outcome (also known as the ¡°ground truth¡±) for each passenger. Your model will be based on ¡°features¡± like passengers¡¯ gender and class. You can also use <a href=""https://triangleinequality.wordpress.com/2013/09/08/basic-feature-engineering-with-the-titanic-data/"" target=""_blank""> feature engineering </a>to create new features.</p>
<p><b>The test set </b>should be used to see how well your model performs on unseen data. For the test set, we do not provide the ground truth for each passenger. It is your job to predict these outcomes. For each passenger in the test set, use the model you trained to predict whether or not they survived the sinking of the Titanic.</p>
<p>We also include <b>gender_submission.csv</b>, a set of predictions that assume all and only female passengers survive, as an example of what a submission file should look like.</p>
<h3>Data Dictionary</h3>
<table style=""width: 100%;"">
<tbody>
<tr><th><b>Variable</b></th><th><b>Definition</b></th><th><b>Key</b></th></tr>
<tr>
<td>survival</td>
<td>Survival</td>
<td>0 = No, 1 = Yes</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>pclass</td>
<td>Ticket class</td>
<td>1 = 1st, 2 = 2nd, 3 = 3rd</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>sex</td>
<td>Sex</td>
<td></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Age</td>
<td>Age in years</td>
<td></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>sibsp</td>
<td># of siblings / spouses aboard the Titanic</td>
<td></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>parch</td>
<td># of parents / children aboard the Titanic</td>
<td></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>ticket</td>
<td>Ticket number</td>
<td></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>fare</td>
<td>Passenger fare</td>
<td></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>cabin</td>
<td>Cabin number</td>
<td></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>embarked</td>
<td>Port of Embarkation</td>
<td>C = Cherbourg, Q = Queenstown, S = Southampton</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3>Variable Notes</h3>
<p><b>pclass</b>: A proxy for socio-economic status (SES)<br /> 1st = Upper<br /> 2nd = Middle<br /> 3rd = Lower<br /><br /> <b>age</b>: Age is fractional if less than 1. If the age is estimated, is it in the form of xx.5<br /><br /> <b>sibsp</b>: The dataset defines family relations in this way...<br /> Sibling = brother, sister, stepbrother, stepsister<br /> Spouse = husband, wife (mistresses and fianc¨¦s were ignored)<br /><br /> <b>parch</b>: The dataset defines family relations in this way...<br /> Parent = mother, father<br /> Child = daughter, son, stepdaughter, stepson<br /> Some children travelled only with a nanny, therefore parch=0 for them.</p>",False,True
6901,increment-testosterone,"# Description

Welcome to the **Increment Testosterone** Kaggle competition! Testosterone is a hormone that plays an important role in the development and maintenance of male reproductive and sexual health, as well as overall health and well-being in both men and women. However, many lifestyle factors, such as diet, exercise, stress, and environmental factors, can affect testosterone levels, and predicting these levels accurately can be challenging.

This competition aims to address this challenge by providing a platform for data scientists and researchers to develop predictive models that estimate testosterone levels based on lifestyle factors. By doing so, we hope to gain insights into the complex relationships between lifestyle factors and testosterone levels, and to identify potential interventions that can help improve testosterone levels and promote better health outcomes.

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F14778197%2Fb196b553084bbd59d65d83d8440b70db%2FDALLE%202023-01-13%2019.35.04%20-%20Leonardo%20Da%20Vinci%20style%20painting%20of%20the%20second%20world%20war.png?generation=1682514959023882&alt=media)

To participate in this competition, you are invited to use your own dataset related to hormone levels and lifestyle factors, including data from clinical studies, surveys, or other sources. We encourage participants to use a wide range of machine learning techniques and feature engineering methods to develop their models, and we will consider additional evaluation metrics such as model interpretability and generalizability to new data.

We recognize that testosterone is a sensitive topic and that there may be legal or ethical considerations related to the use of hormone level data. Therefore, we ask that all participants comply with the highest ethical standards when collecting, handling, and using hormone level data, and that they ensure that any data they use is obtained with the appropriate legal and ethical approvals.

We also want to ensure that this competition is accessible and inclusive to all participants, regardless of their background or experience level. We encourage participants to collaborate and share their insights and expertise on the forum, and to provide constructive feedback and support to their fellow competitors.

We believe that this competition has the potential to make a significant contribution to the field of health and well-being, and we look forward to seeing the innovative solutions that you develop for this important problem. If you have any questions or concerns, please don't hesitate to contact us on the forum, and we wish you the best of luck in the competition!
----------------------
# Evaluation

Welcome to the evaluation page for the **Increment Testosterone** Kaggle competition! In this section, we will provide details on how submissions will be scored and how participants should format their submissions.

1. Scoring:
The primary evaluation metric for this competition is the mean absolute error (MAE) between the predicted and actual testosterone levels. Lower MAE scores indicate better model performance. However, we also encourage participants to consider additional evaluation metrics such as model interpretability, generalizability to new data, and computational efficiency when developing their models.

2. Formatting:
To submit your results, you will need to upload a CSV file with two columns: the first column should contain the ID of each individual in your test dataset, and the second column should contain your predicted testosterone levels for each individual. The ID column should match the corresponding ID in the original test dataset provided.

To ensure consistency across submissions, we ask that participants use the following naming convention for their submission files: ""submission.csv"". We also ask that you include a brief description of your modeling approach and any notable features or techniques used in your submission.

To be eligible for evaluation, your submission must adhere to the following guidelines:

-Your submission file should contain exactly two columns: ""ID"" and ""testosterone"".
-Your ""ID"" column should contain the same IDs as the original test dataset.
-Your ""testosterone"" column should contain only numeric values.
-Your submission file should be in CSV format.
-Your submission file should have a header row with column names.

We strongly encourage participants to thoroughly test their submissions before uploading them to ensure that they meet these requirements and are able to be evaluated successfully.

We also remind participants that any code or scripts used to generate their submissions must be made available upon request for verification purposes.

We hope that this information provides clarity on how submissions will be evaluated and how to format your submissions. If you have any questions or concerns, please don't hesitate to contact us on the forum, and we wish you the best of luck in the competition!","# Dataset Description

In this competition, we are allowing participants to use their own datasets related to hormone levels and lifestyle factors, which can include data from clinical studies, surveys, or other sources. To ensure a level playing field for all participants, we ask that you provide a clear dataset description using the template provided, including details on the data format, variable descriptions, and any known data quality issues or ethical considerations.

The evaluation metric for this competition will be based on the mean absolute error (MAE) between the predicted and actual testosterone levels. We encourage participants to use a range of machine learning techniques and feature engineering methods to develop their models, and we will also consider additional evaluation metrics such as model interpretability and generalizability to new data.

We look forward to seeing the innovative solutions that you develop for this important problem, and we wish you the best of luck in the competition! If you have any questions or concerns, please don't hesitate to reach out to us on the forum.",False,True
6902,book-prices-prediction,"# Description

<p>This competition is an in-class competition for the course, ""Artificial Intelligence and Machine Learning (Spring 2023)"", in Renmin University of China.</p>

<p>The competition is to predict the prices of books collected from the Best Books Ever list on Goodreads.com.</p>
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is <a href=""https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Root-mean-square_deviation""> Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE)</a>.</p>
<h2>Submission Format</h2>
<p>For <strong>each book</strong> in the test dataset, submission files should contain two columns: <strong><i>id</i></strong> and <strong><i>price</i></strong>, i.e. the id and the predicted price for the book.</p>
<p>The file should contain a header and have the following format:</p>
<pre>id,price<br>25884,11.29<br>46382,9.72<br>2062,4.81<br>......</pre>","# Dataset Description

The dataset consists of over 38000 books collected from the Best Books Ever list on Goodreads.com in 2020.

## Files

*   **books_train.csv** - the training set
*   **books_test.csv** - the test set
*   **books_sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

## Columns

*   `id` - id of the book
*   `title` - the title of the book
*   `series` - the series the book belongs to, if any
*   `author` - the author of the book
*   `rating` - the average rating of the book on GoodReads
*   `description` - a brief description of the book
*   `language` - the language the book is written in
*   `isbn` - the ISBN number of the book
*   `genres` - the genres the book falls into
*   `characters` - the characters featured in the book
*   `bookFormat` - the format of the book (e.g paperback or e-book)
*   `edition` - the edition of the book
*   `pages` - the number of pages in the book
*   `publisher` - the publisher of the book
*   `publishDate` - the date the book was published
*   `firstPublishDate` - the date the book was first published
*   `awards` - any awards the book has received
*   `numRatings` - the number of ratings the book has received
*   `ratingsByStars` -  the ratings the book has received broken down by star ratings
*   `likedPercent` - the percentage of readers who liked the book
*   `setting` - the setting of the book
*   `bbeScore` - the score of the book on GoodReads' Best Books Ever list
*   `bbeVotes` - the number of votes cast for the book on GoodReads' Best Books Ever list
*   `price` -  the price of the book, Target variable",False,True
6903,counting-cells-in-microscopy-images-2023,"# Description

How many cells appear in a given microscopy image?
Your goal is to develop an algorithm which is capable of answering that question.

## Acknowledgements

The data for this competition was collected in the Schroeder lab at the University of San Francisco. The creation of this lab was funded by a donation from Kirk Schroeder: https://www.usfca.edu/news/gift-expands-biotech
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is mean absolute error.


## Submission Format

Your submission file should be in .csv format with two columns: ""index"" and ""count"". Each index is an integer between 0 and 1999 which specifies a particular test image.

The ""count"" column contains your predictions for the number of cells which appear in each test image.","# Dataset Description

The training data consists of 2000 microscopy images of cells, stored in a numpy array X, and corresponding segmentation masks stored in a numpy array y. Each image (and each corresponding mask) has shape (128, 128). The segmentation mask y[i] for image X[i] contains ""soft labels"" (that is, numbers between 0 and 1) which tell you which pixels in the image X[i] belong to a cell nucleus.

The test data consists of 2000 more images of cells. No masks are provided for the test images. Your goal is to count how many cells appear in each test image.

The training data and test images are stored in numpy's .npz file format. You can load the training data using the following Python commands:

`import numpy as np`

`npzfile = np.load('train_data.npz')`

`X = npzfile['X']`

`y = npzfile['y']`

Then, you can display an image with superimposed labels using the following Python commands:

`import matplotlib.pyplot as plt`

`i = 0 # change this value to view different images`

`plt.imshow(X[i]/255 + .5*y[i], cmap='gray')`

## Files

*   **test_images.npz** - Contains the test images, described above
*   **train_data.npz** - Contains the training data, described above
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

## Columns
Your submission file should be in .csv format with the following two columns:

*   `index` - An integer between 0 and 1999. Index i corresponds to the test image X[i].
*   `count`?- Your predicted cell counts for each test image.",False,True
6904,retencion-en-telefonia-movil-2023-10,"# Description

Las comunicaciones por tel¨¦fono celular siempre han sido objeto de una feroz competencia, en la que, usualmente, hay un n¨²mero reducido de competidores (operadores) en el mercado que oscila entre cuatro y seis. Los operadores deben hacer fuertes inversiones de entrada para poder operar y prestar un servicio adecuado. El mercado de telefon¨ªa celular est¨¢ usualmente saturado (poca posibilidad de ampliaci¨®n del mercado) y, por tanto, el conjunto de consumidores disponibles es reducido, por lo que los operadores usualmente cambian su estrategia de adquisici¨®n de clientes a una de retenci¨®n, pues en el mundo se calcula que el costo de adquirir un nuevo cliente es cinco veces superior al de retenerlo, sin contar con el posible CLTV (customer lifetimevalue) del cliente, esto es, el retorno a largo plazo que se genera por un cliente que contin¨²a consumiendo los servicios de un operador. Otra cifra clave es que la tasa de deserci¨®n (churn) suele rondar el 2% mensual promedio para un operador de telecomunicaciones. Desde el punto de vista de la inteligencia de negocios, bajo la perspectiva de CRM hay dos esfuerzos fundamentales de Anal¨ªtica: a) predecir la deserci¨®n (churn) de manera adecuada y b) evaluar la manera m¨¢s costo/efectiva en la que un operador puede hacer para la retenci¨®n (incluyendo no hacer nada).

Un operador de telecomunicaciones les ha contratado para resolver las dos inquietudes anal¨ªticas presentadas anteriormente. Para la primera desea que, a partir de una base de datos de entrenamiento (que es una muestra aleatoria simple de la base de datos total de la compa?¨ªa) que contiene el resultado de retenci¨®n en los ¨²ltimos seis meses, se realice la mejor predicci¨®n posible (basada en el ¨¢rea bajo la curva COR) para una base de datos de prueba en un concurso de Kaggle. Para la segunda parte, adicional a un reporte con metodolog¨ªa CRISP-DM (de m¨¢ximo 12 p¨¢ginas) de los modelos realizados, desea que se le presente una estrategia (incluyendo puntos de corte, costos y posibles ganancias) para atacar la retenci¨®n.
----------------------
# Evaluation

La m¨¦trica de evaluaci¨®n es el ¨¢rea bajo la curva caracter¨ªstica de operaci¨®n (AUC bajo la curva COR) en la base de datos de test.

Los sometimientos deben ser archivos separados por comas, con un encabezado que contenga las variables ""id"" y ""resultado"".","# Dataset Description

La base de datos de entrenamiento tiene una muestra aleatoria simple de los clientes de la compa?¨ªa. Las variables de consumo representan los ¨²ltimos seis meses del cliente con el operador. Si el cliente no ha entrado en churn, corresponden al segundo semestre de 2018 (el corte es primero de enero de 2019). Si el cliente ha entrado en churn, corresponden a los ¨²ltimos seis meses con el operador, y el churn pudo haber ocurrido en cualquier momento dentro de la ventana de tiempo del segundo semestre de 2018. Todos los clientes tienen contratos de suscripci¨®n (son clientes pospago).

La base de datos de prueba contiene una muestra no aleatoria adicional de clientes de la compa?¨ªa. Las variables de consumo representan los ¨²ltimos seis meses del cliente con el operador. Si el cliente no ha entrado en churn, corresponden al segundo semestre de 2018 (el corte es primero de enero de 2019). Si el cliente ha entrado en churn, corresponden a los ¨²ltimos seis meses con el operador, y el churn pudo haber ocurrido en cualquier momento dentro de la ventana de tiempo del segundo semestre de 2018. Todos los clientes tienen contratos de suscripci¨®n (son clientes pospago). La variable de posible churn (resultado) no se incluye, pues es la variable a predecir.

## Archivos

*   **traintelco.xlsx** - La base de datos de entrenamiento
*   **testelco.xlsx** - La base de datos de prueba
*   **samplesubtelco.csv** - Archivo muestra de submission

## Campos de la base de datos

*   `id` - id an¨®nimo que identifica al cliente.
*   `Fecha de nacimiento`?- Fecha de nacimiento del cliente, dd/mm/yyyy.
*   `tipo cliente`?- Tipo de cliente: 1 - Hombre, 2 - Mujer, 3 - Cliente empresarial.
*   `Factura online`?- Binario que indica si el cliente recibe ¨²nicamente su factura online.
*   `Antiguedad equipo`?- Meses de antig¨¹edad del equipo.
*   `Plan de datos`?- Binario. 1 = el cliente tiene plan premium de datos.
*   `Facturaci¨®n`?- Total facturaci¨®n de los ¨²ltimos seis meses (suma).
*   `Mora`?- D¨ªas de mora acumulados.
*   `Fecha inicio contrato`?- Fecha de inicio de su primer contrato de plan pospago en la compa?¨ªa.
*   `minutos`?- total minutos consumidos en los ¨²ltimos seis meses.
*   `resultado`?- 0 = No hubo deserci¨®n; 1 = Hubo deserci¨®n (churn)",False,True
6905,rsna-2023-abdominal-trauma-detection,"# Description

## Goal of the Competition

Traumatic injury is the most common cause of death in the first four decades of life and a major public health problem around the world. There are estimated to be more than 5 million annual deaths worldwide from traumatic injury. Prompt and accurate diagnosis of traumatic injuries is crucial for initiating appropriate and timely interventions, which can significantly improve patient outcomes and survival rates. Computed tomography (CT) has become an indispensable tool in evaluating patients with suspected abdominal injuries due to its ability to provide detailed cross-sectional images of the abdomen.

  

Interpreting CT scans for abdominal trauma, however, can be a complex and time-consuming task, especially when multiple injuries or areas of subtle active bleeding are present. This challenge seeks to harness the power of artificial intelligence and machine learning to assist medical professionals in rapidly and precisely detecting injuries and grading their severity. The development of advanced algorithms for this purpose has the potential to improve trauma care and patient outcomes worldwide.

## Context

Blunt force abdominal trauma is among the most common types of traumatic injury, with the most frequent cause being motor vehicle accidents. Abdominal trauma may result in damage and internal bleeding of the internal organs, including the liver, spleen, kidneys, and bowel. Detection and classification of injuries are key to effective treatment and favorable outcomes. A large proportion of patients with abdominal trauma require urgent surgery. Abdominal trauma often cannot be diagnosed clinically by physical exam, patient symptoms, or laboratory tests.

  

Prompt diagnosis of abdominal trauma using medical imaging is thus critical to patient care. AI tools that assist and expedite diagnosis of abdominal trauma have the potential to substantially improve patient care and health outcomes in the emergency setting.

  

The RSNA Abdominal Trauma Detection AI Challenge, organized by the RSNA in collaboration with the American Society of Emergency Radiology (ASER) and the Society for Abdominal Radiology (SAR), gives researchers the task of building models that detect severe injury to the internal abdominal organs, including the liver, kidneys, spleen, and bowel, as well as any active internal bleeding.

> **This is a Code Competition. Refer to [Code Requirements](https://kaggle.com/competitions/rsna-2023-abdominal-trauma-detection/overview/code-requirements) for details.**

----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated using the average of the sample weighted log losses from each injury type and an `any_injury` prediction generated by the metric. The [metric implementation notebook can be found here](https://www.kaggle.com/code/metric/rsna-trauma-metric/notebook).

The sample weights are as follows:
- 1 for all healthy labels.
- 2 for low grade solid organ injuries (liver, spleen, kidney).
- 4 for high grade solid organ injuries.
- 2 for bowel injuries.
- 6 for extravasation.
- 6 for the auto-generated `any_injury` label.

For each patient ID in the test set, you must predict a probability for each of the different possible injury types and degrees. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

```
patient_id,bowel_healthy,bowel_injury,extravasation_healthy,extravasation_injury,kidney_healthy,kidney_low,kidney_high,liver_healthy,liver_low,liver_high,spleen_healthy,spleen_low,spleen_high
10102,0.5,0.5,0.5,0.5,0.5,0.5,0.5,0.5,0.5,0.5,0.5,0.5,0.5
10107,0.5,0.5,0.5,0.5,0.5,0.5,0.5,0.5,0.5,0.5,0.5,0.5,0.5
etc.
```

