- Freely & publicly available external data is allowed, including pre-trained models
- Submission file must be named `submission.csv`

Please see the [Code Competition FAQ](https://www.kaggle.com/docs/competitions#notebooks-only-FAQ) for more information on how to submit. And review the [code debugging doc](https://www.kaggle.com/code-competition-debugging) if you are encountering submission errors.
----------------------
# Biological Overview

# **Overview of Kidney Regions**

The dataset for this competition is obtained from Periodic acid-Schiff (PAS) stained tissue slides from human kidneys. PAS stains carbohydrate-rich structures, such as the basement membranes of capillaries and tubular epithelium in the nephron, the large functional unit of the kidney.

The kidney¡¯s role in the body is the regulation and filtration of blood. The kidneys are responsible for removing waste and extra fluid from the body through the urine, which is excreted via the ureter. 

In this dataset, there are tissues samples from the **renal cortex**, **renal medulla** and **renal papilla**. As tissue slices are 2D sections of 3D structures, the orientation of the cut may depict horizontal or longitudinal sections of nephron and vasculature structures. 

?	**Renal cortex:** the renal cortex is the outer portion of the kidney that contains round renal corpuscles enclosing glomerular tufts, balls of capillary loops. The renal corpuscle is the start of the nephron, through which the filtration of blood occurs. The renal cortex also contains proximal and distal convoluted tubules, PCTs and DCTs, which are also regions of the nephron. Between these tubular structures is a complex network of capillaries called the **peritubular capillaries**. 
**Figure 1.** Example tile from renal cortex (includes portion of glomerular tuft)
![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F1536542%2F797f6ca7153e45b2fd22b97fea965142%2FRenal%20Cortex%20Example%20for%20K3%20Website.png?generation=1683831343568293&alt=media =250x250)

?	**Renal medulla:** the renal medulla is the inner portion of the kidney and is arranged in 8-15 renal pyramids containing linearly arranged tubules comprising the loops of Henle and ducts that gather products for excretion. The capillary network in the renal medulla consists of capillaries called vasa recta. The renal pyramids (medullary tissue) are divided by extensions of the renal cortex called renal columns. There are also projections of the renal medulla into the outer cortex called medullary rays.
**Figure 2.** Example tile from renal medulla
![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F1536542%2F500cb9b45029cc024d995746212dccb1%2FRenal%20Medulla%20Example%20for%20K3%20Website.png?generation=1683831378675978&alt=media =250x250)

?	**Renal papilla (subsection of medulla):** the broad bases of the pyramids connect to the renal cortex at the corticomedullary junctions while the tips form structures called the renal papilla, which project in the minor renal calyces where urine is collected. 
**Figure 3.** Example tile from renal papilla
![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F1536542%2F19767bdafb6b9c88a0f6c9924d96771b%2FRenal%20Papilla%20Example%20for%20K3%20Website.png?generation=1683831388535372&alt=media =250x250)

Please refer to [this image](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK459158/figure/article-28333.image.f1/?report=objectonly) as a reference  for kidney anatomy.

# Overview of Blood Vasculature

Large arteries bringing oxygenated blood into organs or tissues branch into smaller and smaller structures, until they are considered arterioles. The arterioles continue to branch into capillary networks. Capillaries, ~8-10 ¦Ìm in diameter, are tiny tube-shaped structures comprised of a thin wall of endothelial cells. This wall is thin enough that oxygen, from red blood cells, and nutrients are able to cross into the surrounding tissues. Capillaries convalesce into venules which carry oxygen and nutrient deficient blood out of a system. The venules continue to join into larger and larger structures called veins. 

This competition focuses on microvasculature in the kidney, including **arterioles**, **capillaries** and **venules**. Larger vessels structures, i.e. arteries and veins, were not segmented in this dataset.


**Reference:** Mescher, A. L. (2018). Junqueira's basic histology: Text and Atlas (15th ed.). McGraw-Hill Education. Pg.383-411

----------------------
# Organizers & Sponsors

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F1536542%2Fa0693c615d0887bcddae91dcce98a81d%2FK3_orgs_and_sponsors_v2.png?generation=1683833607777074&alt=media)

# Our Sponsors
![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F1536542%2Fa57741045aa4f064bb05313b71971322%2Fgoogle.png?generation=1683815664198235&alt=media =300x100)
![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F1536542%2F70794de16191b4be529705f328278015%2FKaggle_logo.png?generation=1683815681973634&alt=media =300x100)
![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F1536542%2Fb1ecad0557d0f7caaadebe8fbf9ce69b%2Findiana-university.png?generation=1683815695047794&alt=media =300x150)","# Dataset Description

# Data Description #

Your goal in this competition is to locate microvasculature structures (blood vessels) within human kidney histology slides.

The competition data comprises tiles extracted from five Whole Slide Images (WSI) split into two datasets. Tiles from Dataset 1 have annotations that have been expert reviewed. Dataset 2 comprises the remaining tiles from these same WSIs and contain sparse annotations that have not been expert reviewed.
- All of the test set tiles are from Dataset 1.
- Two of the WSIs make up the training set, two WSIs make up the public test set, and one WSI makes up the private test set.
- The training data includes Dataset 2 tiles from the *public* test WSI, but *not* from the *private* test WSI.

We also include, as Dataset 3, tiles extracted from an additional nine WSIs. These tiles have not been annotated. You may wish to apply semi- or self-supervised learning techniques on this data to support your predictions.

Note that this is a [**Code Competition**](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/hubmap-hacking-the-human-vasculature/overview/code-requirements), in which the actual test set is hidden. In this version, we give some sample data drawn from the public test set to help you author your solutions. When your submission is scored, this example test data will be replaced with the full test set. There are about 650 tiles in the full test set.

You may find resources from the previous HuBMAP competitions useful as well:
- [HuBMAP: Hacking the Kidney](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/hubmap-kidney-segmentation/)
- [HuBMAP + HPA: Hacking the Human Body](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/hubmap-organ-segmentation/)

## Files and Field Descriptions ##

- **{train|test}/** Folders containing TIFF images of the tiles. Each tile is 512x512 in size.
- **polygons.jsonl** Polygonal segmentation masks in JSONL format, available for Dataset 1 and Dataset 2. Each line gives JSON annotations for a single image with:
  - `id` Identifies the corresponding image in **train/**
  - `annotations`  A list of mask annotations with:
    - `type` Identifies the type of structure annotated:
      - `blood_vessel` The target structure. Your goal in this competition is to predict these kinds of masks on the test set.
      - `glomerulus` A capillary ball structure in the kidney. These parts of the images were excluded from blood vessel annotation. You should ensure none of your test set predictions occur within glomerulus structures as they will be counted as false positives. Annotations are provided for test set tiles in the hidden version of the dataset.
      - `unsure` A structure the expert annotators cannot confidently distinguish as a blood vessel.
    - `coordinates` A list of polygon coordinates defining the segmentation mask.
- **tile_meta.csv** Metadata for each image. The hidden version of this file also contains metadata for the test set tiles.
  - `source_wsi` Identifies the WSI this tile was extracted from.
  - `{i|j}` The location of the upper-left corner within the WSI where the tile was extracted.
  - `dataset` The dataset this tile belongs to, as described above.
- **wsi_meta.csv** Metadata for the Whole Slide Images the tiles were extracted from.
  - `source_wsi` Identifies the WSI.
  - `age`, `sex`, `race`, `height`, `weight`, and `bmi` demographic information about the tissue donor.
- **sample_submission.csv** A sample submission file in the correct format. See the [**Evaluation**](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/hubmap-hacking-the-human-vasculature/overview/evaluation) page for more details.",True,True
6907,mdm-competition-2023,"# Description

Welcome to the [24th IEEE International Conference on Mobile Data Management (MDM)](https://mdmconferences.org/mdm2023/) challenge! In this competition, your task is to build predictive models that can accurately forecast the missing locations of the trajectories, based on historical locations of trajectories from two cities: Singapore and Jakarta. 

Note that the missing locations in these trajectories could either be the next location in the trajectory or the final endpoint (or end location) of the trajectory.

This challenge has two tracks:

**Grab-Posisi Missing Location Prediction**. Your task is to develop a model that accurately predicts the missing location of the trajectories in two cities: Singapore and Jakarta.

**Transfer Learning for Missing Location Prediction.** The objective of this task is to enhance the performance of the predictive models developed in the previous track by leveraging the data from both cities to predict the missing locations of trajectories.

**Phase 1: Model Development (May 17th ¨C Jun 15th):** 
During this phase, participants will be required to sign a [non-disclosure agreement (NDA)](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1VS9--yPAtcxVeLoyeqUXB6FRCRNtMLfT/edit?usp=sharing&ouid=105009820734707434784&rtpof=true&sd=true) and will gain access to the dataset to develop and refine their models. Participants are encouraged to make submissions to the public leaderboard, and their scores will be evaluated for the Phase 1 leaderboard. It's important to note that scores obtained in this phase will not determine the final leaderboard and ranking for prizes.

**Phase 2: Final Scoring (June 15th - June 17th):**
In this phase, participants will be given the opportunity to make up to five submissions against a new, unseen test set. The performance of the models against this test set will be used to determine the final leaderboard and ranking for prizes.

The top teams will be invited to present their methodologies at the prestigious MDM Conference in Singapore, held from **July 3rd to July 6th, 2023**!

This is a fantastic opportunity for the highest-ranking teams to showcase their innovative and effective approaches to solving the challenge. Presenting at the MDM Conference will not only provide valuable exposure to industry experts and potential collaborators but also serve as a significant accomplishment in your professional portfolio. 

**About Grab**
Grab Holdings Inc., a Singaporean multinational technology company, is best known for developing the Grab super-app which provides a range of services to users such as transportation, food delivery, and digital payments, all through a single mobile application. Grab currently operates in eight countries including Singapore, Malaysia, Cambodia, Indonesia, Myanmar, the Philippines, Thailand and Vietnam. As Southeast Asia's first decacorn and the largest technology startup in the region, Grab has made significant strides in revolutionizing the way people live and work in Southeast Asia.


**Reference**
Xiaocheng Huang, Yifang Yin, Simon Lim, Guanfeng Wang, Bo Hu, Jagannadan Varadarajan, Shaolin Zheng, Ajay Bulusu, Roger Zimmermann. Grab-Posisi: An Extensive Real-Life GPS Trajectory Dataset in Southeast Asia. 2019.

----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated based on the averaged Haversine distance (AHD) which measures the angular distance between two points on the surface of a sphere. Given X=(x1, y1) and Y= (x2, y2), where the first coordinates x1 and y1 are the latitudes and the second ones x2 and y2 are the longitudes.","# Dataset Description

The whole data were collected in Southeast Asia from both developed and developing countries, including Singapore and Indonesia, as shown in Table 1.

Table 1. Grab-Posisi Dataset Statistics
| City | Mode | Device |	Total Trajectories |
| ---- | ---- | ---- | ---- |
| Singapore (SIN) |	Car | iOS | 14K |
| Singapore (SIN) | Car | Android | 14K |
| Jakarta (JKT)	Car	iOS	14K
| Jakarta (JKT)	Car	Android	14K
| Jakarta (JKT)	Motorcycle	iOS	14K
| Jakarta (JKT)	Motorcycle	Android	14K

Grab-Posisi dataset has total 7 attributes including Trajectory identifier (Trajectory_ID), Latitude, Longitude, Timestamp, Accuracy Level, Bearing, and Speed, as shown in Table 2.
Table 2. Grab-Posisi Data Attribute
| Attribute | Data Type	| Remark/Format |
| ---- | ---- | ---- |
| Trajectory_ID | String | Identifier for the trajectory |
| Latitude | Float | WGS84 |
| Longitude | Float | WGS84 |
| Timestamp | Bigint | UTC |
| Accuracy Level |	Float | Circle radius, in meter |
| Bearing	Float | Degrees relative to true north |
| Speed | Float | In meters/second |


## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set

Please sign and send us [non-disclosure agreement (NDA)](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1VS9--yPAtcxVeLoyeqUXB6FRCRNtMLfT/edit?usp=sharing&ouid=105009820734707434784&rtpof=true&sd=true) to the following email address: **xlli@i2r.a-star.edu.sg**, with email subject title **NDA for MDM Competition 2023**. After that, we will send you a link to download the data.

*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format",False,True
