*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

## Columns

*   `date` - seconds
*   `Usage_kWh`?- Electricity usage in Kilo Watt Hour (KWH)
*   `Lagging_Current_Reactive.Power_kVarh`?- Lagging power in 1000 reactive volt-amps per hour (KVARH)
*   `Leading_Current_Reactive_Power_kVarh`?- Leading power in 1000 reactive volt-amps per hour (KVARH)
*   `CO2(tCO2)`?- Carbon dioxide level in Parts per million (PPM)
*   `Lagging_Current_Power_Factor`?- Lagging_Current_Power_Factor	Lagging power factor in %
*   `Leading_Current_Power_Factor`?- Leading_Current_Power_Factor	Leading power factor in %
*   `NSM`?- Number of seconds from midnight
*   `WeekStatus`?- Weekday or weekend  
*   `Day_of_week`?- Sunday, Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday, Thursday, Friday, Saturday
*   `Load_Type`?- Light_Load, Medium_Load, Maximum_Load (**Target**)",False,True
6917,mit-ml4moleng-predict-drug-effects-by-cell-lines,"# Description

Predicting Cell Line Viability from Chemical Perturbations

In this problem, we will model the survival of 578 human cell lines under 4684 small molecule perturbations. The cell lines are selected across all tissue types and represent primary, cancer, and immortalized lineages. Training a predictive model of the impact of small molecules on cell state is an important question in drug development, as one might imagine, where in many diseases (e.g., cancer) the goal is often to optimize a selective chemical target to treat specific diseased cell phenotypes. The data for this exercise is taken from the Broad Institute's [PRISM repurposed drug screening dataset](https://depmap.org/repurposing/) - one of the largest of its kind. 

We've preprocessed and prepared the test data for you to provide predictions. Please download primary-screen-logfold-change-by-smiles-test.csv and used the perturbation smiles strings as features to predict each cell line's log fold viability score. We prepared an example submission file as an example. After you train your model on the training data, use the model to compute the probabilities for the unseen perturbation smiles strings and store your predictions in the submission file. Upload your solution and you will be able to see your ranking on the leaderboard computed with 50% of the test data. The final ranking will be calculated using the other 50% of the data points.

 
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is the root mean squared error (RMSE) score. Importantly, NaN values in the test set are not considered when calculating RMSE.","# Dataset Description

## Files

*   **prism_train.csv** - contains the training set which you can use to train your model. Each row has a SMILES string and 578 cell line viability scores.
*   **prism_test.csv** - the test set SMILES strings
*   **prism_cell_line_metadata.csv** - supplemental information about the cell lines 
*   **prism_perturbation_metadata.csv** - supplemental information about the perturbations",False,True
6918,skku-ml2023-hw4,"# Description

Imbalanced classification refers to a type of data where the quantity of data points in each class is different. This is a common occurrence in real-world datasets. For this assignment, the number of data points for each class is as follows:
![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F5279496%2Fbff8a03dfb3f6bcfad733062cbacc204%2F1.png?generation=1682656051609816&alt=media)
Imbalanced classification can be addressed in various ways. we introduce some of the methods below.

- Data-level approaches:
1.  Down sampling - This method involves reducing the number of data points for each class to the same quantity as the class with the smallest number of data points. For example, if the dataset consists of [5000 dogs, 100 cats], down-sampling would involve randomly selecting a subset of the dog data points to match the number of cat data points.
2. Up sampling - This method involves increasing the number of data points for each class to the same quantity as the class with the largest number of data points. For example, if the dataset consists of [5000 dogs, 100 cats], up-sampling would involve duplicating the cat data points until there are 5000 data points for each class.
3. Synthetic Sampling- Replicating the same data multiple times during training may not be very effective in some cases. To address this, we can add random noise to the duplicated data points to create more diverse training data.
- Loss-level approaches:
1. Weighted loss function - In this approach, we assign higher weights to the data points in the minority class and lower weights to the data points in the majority class during training. This way, the model gives more attention to the minority class during training, improving its ability to correctly classify those data points.



There are many ways to address imbalanced classification problems.

The following URL provides solutions for class imbalance: 
	1. https://www.tensorflow.org/tutorials/structured_data/imbalanced_data?hl=ko
	2. https://imbalanced-learn.org/stable/user_guide.html#user-guide

----------------------
# Evaluation

**Evaluation**
Submissions are evaluated based on their classification accuracy, the percentage of predicted labels that are correct.
You can see the scores on the public leaderboard. This is based on 60% of the total data. The final ranking calculation will only be based on remaining 40% of the data.","# Dataset Description

# Files
`train_x.npy` - the training set
`train_y.npy` - the training set
`valid_x.npy` - the validation set
`valid_y.npy` - the validation set
`test_x.npy` - predict labels for this test set
`sample_submission.csv` - a sample submission file in the correct format
For information on fashion MNIST dataset, please refer to https://github.com/zalandoresearch/fashion-mnist

# Instruction
Download files above respectively. Refer to baseline files in the given folder to train your model and make submission files. You should predict the class for each data and write the prediction on the submission file.
Check the submission file to follow the data format.",False,True
