6924,data-science-capstone-project-2023,"# Description

#About Dataset
##Context
Since 2008, guests and hosts have used Airbnb to expand on traveling possibilities and present a more unique, personalized way of experiencing the world. This dataset describes the listing activity and metrics in NYC, NY for 2019.

##Content
This data file includes all needed information to find out more about hosts, geographical availability, and necessary metrics to make predictions and draw conclusions.


##Inspiration
What can we learn about different hosts and areas?
What can we learn from predictions? (ex: locations, prices, reviews, etc)
Which hosts are the busiest and why?
Is there any noticeable difference in traffic among different areas and what could be the reason for it?
How does proximity to different points of interest impact the prices?

## Task
The task is to predict the prices of Airbnb listings.

## Acknowledgments
This public dataset is part of Airbnb, and the original source can be found on this [website](http://insideairbnb.com/)
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is Root Mean Squared Error.  RMSE measures the square root of the average of the squares of the errors¡ªthat is, the square root of the average squared difference between the estimated values and the actual value.

## Submission Format

The submission files should contain two columns: `Id` and `Price`. 

The file should contain a header and have the following format:
```
Id,Price
1,1
```","# Dataset Description

## Columns
1. id -> Airbnb Listing Id
2. name -> Airbnb Listing Name
3. host_id -> Host Name who is hosting the Airbnb Listing
4. host_name -> Name of the host
5. neighbourhood_group -> Name of Borough in New York
6. neighbourhood -> Name of Neighbourhood in New York
7. latitude -> Latitude of Airbnb Listing
8. longitude -> Longitude of Airbnb Listing
9. room_type -> Type of Room
10. minimum_nights -> Minimum number of nights to stay for that particular Airbnb Listing
11. number_of_reviews -> Number of Reviews of that particular Airbnb Listing
12. last_review -> Date of the last review of that particular listing
13. reviews_per_month -> Average Reviews each month
14. floor ¡ú The floor of the airbnb listing
15. noise (dB) ¡ú Average sound levels recorded in dB
16. Location -> Address of the airbnb listing
17. price -> Price of that particular Airbnb Listing",False,True
6925,ventas-de-portatiles,"# Description

Manuel, ha decidido cambiar de aires por eso ha comprado una tienda de port¨¢tiles, pero en lo ¨²nico que se destaca es en el data science, por eso ha decido crear un modelo de ML para poder colocarle los mejores precios

Podr¨ªas ayudar a Manuel y su futura socia Carla a crear ese modelo...


----------------------
# Evaluation

Error de ra¨ªz cuadrada media (RMSE) es la desviaci¨®n est¨¢ndar de los valores residuales (errores de predicci¨®n). Los valores residuales son una medida de la distancia de los puntos de datos de la l¨ªnea de regresi¨®n; RMSE es una medida de cu¨¢l es el nivel de dispersi¨®n de estos valores residuales. En otras palabras, le indica el nivel de concentraci¨®n de los datos en la l¨ªnea de mejor ajuste.

 $$ RMSE = \sqrt{\frac{1}{n}\Sigma_{i=1}^{n}{\Big(\frac{d_i -f_i}{\sigma_i}\Big)^2}} $$","# Dataset Description

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format",False,True
6926,developers-day-2023-data-science-competition,"# Description

Hello  participants! Welcome to the data science competition of **Developer¡¯s Day¡¯23**, It's great to have you here. Get ready to showcase your skills. We hope you enjoy the challenge.
**Best of luck for the competition!**


----------------------
# Evaluation

In this task, you are given two files: a train dataset and a test dataset. Your goal is to train your model on the train dataset and use it to generate predictions for the **Credit_Score** column in the test dataset. You are expected to submit a notebook with your code and the results of your predictions.
To accomplish this, you will need to perform **exploratory data analysis (EDA)** on the train dataset to gain insights into the underlying patterns and relationships in the data. This will help you select appropriate features and preprocessing techniques for your model. Once you have trained your model on the train dataset, you can use it to predict the **Credit_Score** for the test dataset.
Each submission will be scored on the basis of **EDA** and **prediction**.
Remember to evaluate your model's performance on the test dataset and report your results accurately. 

**Good luck with the task!**","# Dataset Description

You should download the train.csv file and perform EDA on it. You are required to predict Credit_Score column in the data.

The metric kept for ML score will be F1 accuracy.


## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training set
