*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format",False,True
6927,train-an-ai-to-play-chess,"# Description

This week at `Global Hack Week: Games` we're teaching an AI to play chess!

While there are plenty of great chess AI engines out there already, it can still be a fun project to help us learn about chess and machine learning!

Besides tuning into the GHW Livestreams at [twitch.tv/mlh](https://twitch.tv/mlh), we'll be posting notebooks with code to help you get started. Check out:

[Part 1: Understanding python-chess and FEN](https://www.kaggle.com/code/wlifferth/part-1-understanding-python-chess-and-fen)
[Part 2: Building a simple chess playing model](https://www.kaggle.com/code/wlifferth/part-2-building-a-simple-chess-playing-model)
[Part 3: Making Predictions and Submitting](https://www.kaggle.com/code/wlifferth/part-3-making-predictions-and-submitting)

----------------------
# Evaluation

In this competition, you'll be trying to predict the best move given a chess board in [Forsyth¨CEdwards Notation (FEN)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Forsyth%E2%80%93Edwards_Notation). For our purposes, the ""best move"" is the move stockfish gives when it runs at a depth of 10.

Your submission should look like the sample_submission.csv file, with one column called `id` (the same id as the test.csv file) and one column called `best_move`, the move your model suggests in [Long Algebraic Notation (AN)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Algebraic_notation_(chess))

```
id,best_move
31233,a1a2
41573,a1a2
75095,a1a2
```


Your submission will be evaluated based on accuracy: the percentage of moves you select that match the ""best"" move as defined above.","# Dataset Description

In this competition, your input is a chess game in [Forsyth¨CEdwards Notation (FEN)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Forsyth%E2%80%93Edwards_Notation) and your predictions are the next best move to make in [Long Algebraic Notation](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Algebraic_notation_(chess)). If either of these are new concepts, check out [this notebook](https://www.kaggle.com/code/wlifferth/part-1-understanding-python-chess-and-fen) for an explainer!

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training set with board states, scores, and best moves
*   **test.csv** - the test set with just board states
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

## Columns

*   `board` - a chess game in FEN
*   `black_score`?- the value of the board in centipawn loss for the player paying black (a high value means a board that looks really good for black, a low value is a board that looks really bad for black)
*   `best_move` - a move in long algebraic notation",False,True
6928,ufaz-navigate-robots,"# Description

The Competition

Robots are smart¡­ by design. To fully understand and properly navigate a task, however, they need input about their environment.

In this competition, you¡¯ll help robots recognize the floor surface they¡¯re standing on using data collected from Inertial Measurement Units (IMU sensors).

The task is to predict which one of the nine floor types (carpet, tiles, concrete) the robot is on using sensor data such as acceleration and velocity. Succeed and you'll help improve the navigation of robots without assistance across many different surfaces, so they won¡¯t fall down on the job.


----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated on Multiclass Accuracy, which is simply the average number of observations with the correct label.


**Submission File**
For each series_id in the test set, you must predict a value for the surface variable. The file should have the following format:

```python
series_id,surface 
0,fine_concrete
1,concrete
2,concrete
etc
```","# Dataset Description

The dataset for this competition contains measurements collected from robots as they traverse a path. The goal is to predict the type of floor surface that the robot is currently on, based on sensor readings collected during its movement.
The dataset consists of the following columns:

## Columns

*   `surface` - the target for this competition

*  `series_id` - ID number for the measurement series.

* `measurement_number` - Measurement number within the series.

In addition, the dataset includes 10 sensor channels that provide information about the robot's orientation, angular velocity, and linear acceleration at different points in time:

- orientation_X
- orientation_Y
- orientation_Z
- orientation_W 
- angular_velocity_X
- angular_velocity_Y
- angular_velocity_Z
- linear_acceleration_X
- linear_acceleration_Y
- linear_acceleration_Z
- group_id: ID number for all of the measurements taken in a recording session. Provided for the training set only, to enable more cross validation strategies.

The orientation channels encode the current angles how the robot is oriented as a quaternion, which is a mathematical notation for representing rotations in three-dimensional space. For more information about quaternions, please see the following : 
[see Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Conversion_between_quaternions_and_Euler_angles)
[Visualizing quaternions (4d numbers)](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=d4EgbgTm0Bg&t=227s&ab_channel=3Blue1Brown) ??",False,True
