6933,beyond-visible-spectrum-ai-for-agriculture-P1,"# Description

# Overview

Early accurate diagnosis and quantification of crop diseases are crucial for precision management by allowing targeted interventions at the field level. Effective diagnosis and control measures are dependent on symptom recognition and severity assessment (in the vast majority of cases, diseases exhibit a range of visual symptoms, e.g. coloured spots or streaks). At present, these are mainly achieved by visual observation and estimation.  

Recent advancements in digital imaging have facilitated the development of a variety of image-based methods for plant management that are capable of performing automated image acquisition and analysis. These methods have shown great potential for automated crop disease diagnosis based on images from RGB (red, green and blue), thermal, multispectral, and hyperspectral sensors.

Particularly, hyperspectral imagery (HSI), which contains more narrow spectral bands over a contiguous spectral range, provides detailed spectral¨Cspatial information of the disease infestation and offers the potential to provide better diagnostic accuracy.

In this challenge, we will mainly focus on the accurate diagnosis of crop diseases from hyperspectral imagery (in this case, yellow rust disease). The benchmark model for this competition is based on our published paper on remote sensing [1]. 

[1. A Deep Learning-Based Approach for Automated Yellow Rust Disease Detection from High-Resolution Hyperspectral UAV Images](https://www.mdpi.com/2072-4292/11/13/1554)

![](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/robeson1010/Agvision/main/imgs/hy.png)

# Challenge keywords
Remote sensing; Hyperspectral; Crop disease; Deep learning 


# Workshop/conference

This challenge is part of the conference on ¡°[The Fourth conference of Monitoring, Early Warning, and Scientific Management of Vegetation Pests and Diseases]()¡± in Aug 2023.

# Publication and Future Plans 

We will write a summary paper of top 5 winning methods to be published in Remote Sensing Special issue of ¡°Monitoring, Early Warning, and Scientific Management of Vegetation Pests and Diseases¡±.  
Our goal is to update the dataset with different types of diseases and continue to make it available for research use.


----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is the categorization accuracy.

Three types are set as follows: Rust, Health and Other

## Submission Format

Submission files should contain two columns: `Id` and `Category`. 

The file should contain a header and have the following format:
```
Id	Category
101.tif	Rust
102.tif	Health
103.tif	Other
----------------------
# People

# Organisers
Prof. Liangxiu Han, Manchester Metropolitan University
Prof. Wenjiang Huang, Aerospace Information Research Institute, Chinese Academy of Sciences

# Technical Chairs
Dr. Xin Zhang, Manchester Metropolitan University
Dr. Yingying, Dong Aerospace Information Research Institute, Chinese Academy of Sciences
Tam Sobieh, Manchester Metropolitan University

# Primary contact
Liangxiu Han - Email: l.han@mmu.ac.uk
Wenjiang Huang - Email: huangwj@radi.ac.cn","# Dataset Description

The hyperspectral imagery retrieval was conducted five times from the 25th, April to the 18th, May in 2018 to cover the entire growing season of winter wheat. A DJI S1000 UAV system (SZ DJI Technology Co Ltd., Gungdong, China) with a snapshot hyperspectral sensor was used for data acquisition. The model of the hyperspectral sensor was UHD 185 Firefly (UHD 185) which can obtain reflect radiation from the visible to near-infrared band between 450-950nm wavelength. The spectral resolution was 4nm. The raw data was recorded as a 1000*1000 panchromatic image and a 50*50 hyperspectral image with 125 bands. With a data fusion processed by the Cubert software, the output was a 1000*1000 image with 125bands, and the image was also mosaiced and orthorectified. In this work, all the images were obtained at an altitude of 30 meters, with a spatial resolution close to 2cm per pixel. 

# Dataset Overview Paper 
[A Deep Learning-Based Approach for Automated Yellow Rust Disease Detection from High-Resolution Hyperspectral UAV Images](https://www.mdpi.com/2072-4292/11/13/1554)
![](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/robeson1010/Hyperspectral-competition/main/20220428113850.png)


## Files

The training dataset<br/>
The validation dataset

##Submission Columns

*   `Id` - file name
*   `Category`?- Rust, Health, Other",False,True
6934,beyond-visible-spectrum-ai-for-agriculture-2023-p2,"# Description

# Overview

The Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations (FAO) estimates that current agricultural production needs to grow by up to 70 percent by 2050 in order to meet the expected population growth on Earth. This poses a significant challenge for food production, which requires solutions that not only enhance agricultural methods but also adapt them radically to environmental pressures. Big data, machine learning and artificial intelligence (AI) have the potential to play a vital role in this process.

The crop type classification is an important part of agriculture as an industry. Remote sensing data offers large scale, long term, continuous monitoring on the ground and has long been used as an extremely useful tool for crop classification. Identifying crops using traditional supervised learning methods can be a challenging task, as obtaining a large number of ground-truthing training samples is often a time-consuming and laborious work.


In recent years, there has been a new research trend to train models without using labels, named Self-supervised learning (SSL). It has been effectively applied in many fields such as computer vision, natural language processing, and object detection. In fact, by extracting representative features from unlabelled data, the SSL algorithm has outperformed supervised pre-training on many problems. In this respect, self-supervised learning is a promising avenue for remote sensing research.


In this challenge, we will release a massive remote sensing dataset in cropland area, including multispectral and SAR data for participants to train their self-supervised learning models. A crop classification task will be set up to evaluate the effectiveness of their SSL model.

![](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/robeson1010/Agvision/main/imgs/KAGGLE.png)



# Challenge keywords

Remote sensing; Hyperspectral; Crop disease; Self-supervised learning; Crop classification; Deep learning


# Participation instructions

You can participate in the competition via this link! [https://www.kaggle.com/t/beb5449750ca4c85bf7c166698f2833b](https://www.kaggle.com/t/beb5449750ca4c85bf7c166698f2833b)

# Workshop/conference

This challenge is part of the conference on ¡°[The Fourth conference of Monitoring, Early Warning, and Scientific Management of Vegetation Pests and Diseases]()¡± in Aug 2023.

# Publication and Future Plans 

We will write a summary paper of top 5 winning methods to be published in Remote Sensing Special issue of ¡°Monitoring, Early Warning, and Scientific Management of Vegetation Pests and Diseases¡±.  
Our goal is to update the dataset with different types of diseases and continue to make it available for research use.


----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is the categorization accuracy.

Three types are set as follows: Rust, Health and Other

## Submission Format

Submission files should contain two columns: `Id` and `Category`. 

The file should contain a header and have the following format:
```
Id	Category
101.tif	Rust
102.tif	Health
103.tif	Other
