6975,shotquality-rebounding,"# Description

**[ShotQuality](https://shotquality.com/)** is a basketball data company using AI and computer vision to extract precise location data from video feeds for any shot. Our data is being used by top NCAA teams to optimize efficiency.  We want to give you a look under the hood at our NCAA player location data that is used to analyze player and team performance.


Traditionally, the prediction of offensive rebound percentages for NCAA basketball (Off Reb Pct) has been binary, hinging on whether or not a rebound is secured. However, at ShotQuality, we believe in a process-oriented approach, valuing the context behind each rebound far more than the mere outcome. Our data is first of it's kind unlocking a deeper analysis of NCAA basketball.

In this year's Kaggle competition, we are challenging data scientists, machine learning enthusiasts, and statisticians to delve into the rich tapestry of in-game contexts that contribute to securing an offensive rebound. We're interested in understanding the probability of offensive rebounding based on the exact locations of NCAA players at the moment a shot is released.

Participants will be supplied with a rich dataset encompassing variables such as player positions, shot release points, shot types, and other relevant factors. The objective is to create a predictive model that encapsulates this context to calculate the probability of an offensive rebound occurring in NCAA basketball games.

This competition aims to advance our comprehension of in-game dynamics and enhance the analytical framework of ShotQuality. The insights gathered from your models could significantly transform our understanding of offensive rebounds and reshape strategic decision-making in basketball.

We invite participants to think outside the box, embrace innovative methodologies, and join us on this journey to redefine the analysis of rebound probabilities. May the most insightful model win!

**This is a code competition all submissions must be made via notebook.**

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F15113839%2Fe8c946cef83df7440fd0b734cb0b5a37%2FUConnShotQuality.gif?generation=1685744326348778&amp;alt=media)


----------------------
# Evaluation

This competition will be judged using logarithmic loss (log loss), in-depth details are available [here](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cross_entropy). 
Importantly, this loss metric measures the performance of a model where the prediction is a probability. A perfect model has a log loss of 0. Log loss increases as the prediction diverges from the actual result. A predicted probability of .15 when the event actually occurred (an actual probability of 1) results in a high log loss.



Your submission will be a single CSV file with two columns:

- `id` - the `id` from the test set
- `pred` - your prediction for the probability that this shot is an offensive rebound


----------------------
# Prizes

### Leaderboard Prizes
?? **1st Place**  :  Access to the  [ShotQuality](https://shotquality.com) API for the 2023-24 NCAA Season

                   
### ShotQuality MVP Prize

This prestigious prize is awarded to the most knowledgeable and helpful competitor in the competition.

- **1st Place** :  Access to the [ShotQuality](https://shotquality.com) API for the 2023-24 NCAA Season

The evaluation for the ShotQuality MVP prize will be carried out by experts from the SQ team, based on the following criteria:

   1. Forum and notebook discussion/comment contributions
   2. Quality of code samples in public notebooks
   3. Thoughtful analysis and clear explanations of the code content.

### BONUS:

In addition to fantastic rewards for the winners, there's an exclusive bonus prize: **the opportunity to work closely with us!** A select few may have the chance to join our team and be a part of our mission. So, gear up and put forth your best efforts for the chance to seize this extraordinary opportunity! Best of luck to everyone!

**Note:** To claim their prizes, the winners must provide their code by August 15, 2023, which is three days after the conclusion of the competition. ShotQuality experts will meticulously review the notebooks and compare the results between the notebook output and the submitted files.","# Dataset Description

Your challenge is to use the location of basketball players to predict the probability of an offensive rebound after a miss.

This competition uses two different types of datasets. Play-by-play data, often abbreviated ""pbp"", contains play-level details. This includes teams names and, for the training set, whether the shot is an offensive or defensive rebound. We also use location data, often abbreviated ""locs"", as the focus on this challenge. The location data includes the on-court location of each player, along with an indicator to show whether the player is the shooter, an offensive player, or a defensive player.

Note that all shots in the train or test set are misses that are rebounded by the offensive or defensive team. A shot is  from the coordinates where annotation_code == ""s"" in train_locs.csv  and test_locs.csv.

Coordinates for key locations on the court are (position | x,y): 
Top Left |  0,0
Left hoop |  4,25
Bottom left |  0,50
Three Point Arc |  17.4, 25 
Center | 47,25
Three Point Arc |  66.6, 25
Top Right | 94,0
Right Hoop|  90,25
Bottom Right |  94,50

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F15113839%2Fe800fcf6fefa6f66409cfeaf838465d1%2FScreenshot%202023-06-21%20at%207.38.39%20AM.png?generation=1687358416977539&alt=media)

## Files

*   **train_pbp.csv** - the training of play-by-play data
*   **test_pbp.csv** - the test of play-by-play data
*   **train_locs.csv** - the training of location data
*   **test_locs.csv** - the test of location data
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file

## Columns
### Play-by-Play
* `id` - Unique play identifier
* `team` - Offensive team name
* `opponent` - Defensive team name
* `conference` - Offensive team conference name
* `opp_conference` - Defensive team conference name
* `is_oreb` - 1 if an offensive rebound, 0 if a defensive rebound

### Location
* `id` - Unique play identifier, key into play-by-play
* `court_x` - Court x location at the moment of the shot, measured in feet from the top-left corner
* `court_y` - Court y location at the moment of the shot, measured in feet from the top-left corner
* `annotation_code` - Unique player identifier for a play. `""s""` is the shooter, `(""t1"", ""t2"", ""t3"", ""t4"")` are the other offensive players, and `(""d1"", ""d2"", ""d3"", ""d4"", ""d5"")` are the defensive players. Note that the numbers in `annotation_code` after `""d""` and `""t""` do not have any meaning.

### Submission
* `id` - Unique game identifier
* `pred` - Predicted offensive rebound probability",False,True
6976,execmba-2023-daii,"# Description

In this competition you will be using the data from the case Sarah Gets a Diamond. You will be predicting diamond prices based on attributes of the diamond.

The training set contains information on 6000 diamonds. You will be predicting the prices of 3142 other diamonds. We will use mean absolute percentage error (MAPE), to evaluate your submissions.
----------------------
# Evaluation

<p>The evaluation metric for this competition is MAPE</a>. The MAPE is a measure of prediction accuracy, where the forecast error is measured relative to the magnitude of the quantity of interest.  MAPE can be expressed as:  </p>
<p>\[ MAPE = \frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^n (\frac{\left|A_{t}-F_{t}\right|}{A_{t}})  \]</p>

<p>where At is the actual value and Ft is the forecast value.</p>","# Dataset Description

<p>Data used here came from the Darden Case Study: ""Sarah Gets a Diamond"" (UVA-QA-0702). <\p>

<p>Diamonds are primarily based on four criteria known as the ¡°four Cs¡±: cut, color, clarity, and carat weight. In addition to these factors, other less-known qualifiers (e.g., fluorescence, polish, and symmetry) marginally influence the value of the stone. </p>

<h2>File descriptions</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>train.csv</strong> - the training set of 6000 rows and 9 columns</li>
<li><strong>test.csv</strong> - the test set of 3142 rows and 9 columns. The final column of 'price' has all NA</li>
<li><strong>Submission.csv</strong> - a sample submission file in the correct format</li>
</ul>
<h2>Data fields</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>ID</strong> - a unique id given to each diamond</li>
<li><strong>Carat Weight</strong> - size of the diamond. </li>
<li><strong>Cut</strong> - diamond cut ratings, in ascending order, are: poor, fair, good, very good, ideal, and signature ideal</li>
<li><strong>Color</strong> - diamonds are graded on a letter scale from Z (noticeable color) to D (absolutely colorless)</li>
<li><strong>Clarity</strong> Gems free of blemishes are known to be FL (flawless), followed by IF (internally flawless), VVS1 and VVS2 (very, very slight inclusions), VS1 and VS2 (very slight inclusions), SI1 and SI2 (slight inclusions), and I1, I2, and I3</li>
<li><strong>Polish</strong> - graded using G (good), VG (very good), and EX (excellent) categories.</li>
<li><strong>Symmetry</strong> - graded using G (good), VG (very good), and EX (excellent) categories. </li>	
<li><strong>Report</strong> - graded using G (good), VG (very good),  EX (excellent) and ID (ideal) categories. </li>	
<li><strong>Price</strong> the price of the diamonds in USD. </li>
</ul>",False,True
6977,language-macroarea-classification,"# Description

Human beings have always been fascinated by the variety of languages that exist in the world, wondering on the reasons behind their differences, similarities and sometimes oddities. It is known that one of the main origins for shared characteristics between two languages is geographical proximity. In other words, if two languages are spoken close to each other, then they are likely to be similar.

It is possible to neatly divide the world into six independent macroareas. These are North America, South America, Africa, Eurasia, Australia and Papunesia. Papunesia refers to a geographic area that includes the isles of South-East Asia and Ocenia, save for Australia. The image below shows this competition's languages plotted on the world map and colored based on the macroarea they belong to. ![Macroareas](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F9926799%2F806fc949e1b60a73b0114cd61299d82b%2FMacroareas.png?generation=1685116852909089&alt=media)

Your objective is to correctly predict the **macroarea** where each language is spoken based on its characteristics. The features that were considered for this challenge are **structural** and **phonological**.

The structural features are taken from the World Atlas of Language Structures ([WALS](https://wals.info)). WALS is a large database of structural (phonological, grammatical, lexical) properties of languages gathered from descriptive materials (such as reference grammars) by a team of 55 authors. The atlas provides information on basic typological features of a great number of the world's languages.

WALS Online is a publication of the [Max Planck Institute for Evolutionary Anthropology](https://www.eva.mpg.de/index/). It is a separate publication, edited by Dryer, Matthew S. & Haspelmath, Martin (Leipzig: Max Planck Institute for Evolutionary Anthropology, 2013). The main programmer is Robert Forkel.

The phonological features are taken from [PHOIBLE](http://phoible.org). PHOIBLE is a repository of cross-linguistic phonological inventory data containing 3020 phonological inventories from 2186 distinct languages. A phonological inventory consists of the sounds (phonemes) possessed by a language.  A [phoneme](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phoneme) is a unit of sound that can distinguish one word from another in a particular language. Phonemes are usually transcribed using the [International Phonetic Alphabet](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/International_Phonetic_Alphabet).

Good luck and have fun!

## Acknowledgements

- The World Atlas of Language Structures was edited by Matthew Dryer and Martin Haspelmath. If you use this data in your work, please include the following citation:

    Dryer, Matthew S. & Haspelmath, Martin (eds.) 2013. WALS Online (v2020.3) [Data set]. Zenodo. https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.7385533 (Available online at https://wals.info, Accessed on 2023-05-19.)

    WALS is licensed under a [Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International License](https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/) .

- PHOIBLE was edited by Steven Moran and Daniel McCloy. If you use this data in your work, please include the following citation:

    Moran, Steven & McCloy, Daniel (eds.) 2019. PHOIBLE 2.0. Jena: Max Planck Institute for the Science of Human History. (Available online at http://phoible.org, Accessed on 2023-05-19.)

    PHOIBLE is licensed under a [Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 3.0 Unported License](https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/).

- I'd also like to thank Rachael Tatman's [dataset](https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/rtatman/world-atlas-of-language-structures) as inspiration for this competition.
----------------------
# Evaluation

## Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated based on their macro [F1 score](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/F-score).
The macro F1 score consists in:

1. Finding the marginal F1 score for each class.
2. Calculating their unweighted mean.


## Submission Format

The submission format for the competition is a csv file with the following format:

```
Language_ID,Macroarea
1001,1
1002,1
1003,1
1004,1
etc.
```","# Dataset Description

In this competition your task is to predict the macroarea where a language is spoken. To help you make these predictions, you're given a set of features for every language.

# File and Data Field Descriptions #

- **train.csv** - Features for about three-fourths (1000) of the languages, to be used as training data.
 - `Language_ID` - A unique ID for each language.
 - `Macroarea` - The macroarea the language belongs to. This is the target, the column you are trying to predict.
 - Columns 3 to 192 - Structural features. Each feature is a categorical variable taking two or more values. They contain many missing values. For their interpretation, see [here](https://wals.info/feature).
 - Columns 193 to 392 - Phonological features. Each feature is a binary variable representing a phoneme. It takes value 1 if the phoneme is present in the language, and 0 otherwise. They have no missing values. A feature's name is the phoneme's representation using the International Phonetic Alphabet. For their interpretation, see [here](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/International_Phonetic_Alphabet#Letters).

- **test.csv** - Features for the remaining one-fourth (346) of the languages, to be used as test data. Your task is to predict the value of `Macroarea` for the languages in this set.

- **sample_submission.csv** - A submission file in the correct format.
    - `Language_ID` - Id for each language in the test set.
    - `Macroarea` - The target. For each language, predict either `1`, `2`, `3`, `4`, `5` or `6`. Each number corresponds to a macroarea in the following way:

    | Number     | Macroarea     |
    | ----------- | ----------- |
    | 1    | Africa    |
    | 2    | Australia    |
    | 3    | Eurasia    |
    | 4    | North America    |
    | 5    | Papunesia    |
    | 6    | South America    |

    For **sample_submission** all predictions have been set to `1` (Africa), that is, the most common class in the training data.",False,True
6978,dsaa5013-course-project-2023-spring-f1-score,"# Description

# Project description

Credit default is critical to the continuous operation of commercial banks. A retail bank is expecting a credit default model to identify the consumers who are likely to default on their credit card payments over the next 12 months. This model can be used for future actions taken to manage the bank's risk and reduce future losses.

Some sample data extracted from their systems are available for you to build a credit default model and predict likely default consumers. Based on the bank¡¯s experience, the number of derogatory reports is a strong indicator of default. This is all the information you are able to get from the bank at the moment.

You can choose any model you want to do this task, and you are suggested to learn some basic knowledge of banking and get familiar with its terminology. Data preprocessing is needed, and you may want to do some feature engineering (like normalization or discretization). All kinds of information related to the bank model are welcome to refer to.
----------------------
# Evaluation

# Evaluation Metric 

The evaluation metric for this Kaggle competition is [Mean F1-Score](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/F-score). The F1 score, commonly used in information retrieval, measures accuracy using the precision of the statistics \( \text{p} \) and recall \( \text{r} \). Precision is the ratio of true positives \( \text{tp} \) to all predicted positives \( \text{tp+fp} \). The recall is the ratio of true positives \( \text{tp} \) to all actual positives \( \text{tp+fp} \). The F1 score is given by:

\[ \text{F1} = 2\frac{\text{p} \cdot \text{r}}{\text{p}+\text{r}}\ \ \mathrm{where}\ \ \text{p} = \frac{\text{tp}}{\text{tp}+\text{fp}},\ \ \text{r} = \frac{\text{tp}}{\text{tp}+\text{fn}} \]

The F1 metric weights recall and precision equally, and a good retrieval algorithm will maximize both precision and recall simultaneously. Thus, moderately good performance on both will be favored over extremely good performance on one and poor performance on the other. In your report, you can use various metrics to demonstrate your prediction results and you need to briefly describe the metrics you choose. Matrices and diagrams are also welcomed with your analysis.","# Dataset Description

# Dataset Description

## File descriptions

* **train.csv** - the training set (2,000 samples)
* **test.csv** - the test set (1,000 samples)
* **samplesubmission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

## Columns of training data

* **DEFAULT** - 1 if defaulted 0 if not
* **AGE - Age** in years plus twelfths of a year
* **ACADMOS** - months living at current address
* **ADEPCNT** - 1 + number of dependents
* **MAJORDRG** - Number of major derogatory reports
* **MINORDRG** - Number of minor derogatory reports
* **OWNRENT** - 1 if owns their home, 0 if rent
* **NCOME** - Monthly income (divided by 10,000)
* **SELFEMPL** - 1 if self employed, 0 if not
* **INCPER** - Income divided by number of dependents
* **EXP_INC** - Ratio of monthly credit card expenditure to yearly income
* **SPENDING** - Average monthly credit card expenditure
* **LOGSPEND** - Log of spending",False,True
