6998,colac-test-evaluation,"# Description

Public evaluation of Corpus of Linguistic Acceptability in Chinese (CoLAC) test set. 

There are two sets of labels in the training and development data: label0 (linguist label) and label1 (crowd label). Label1 is preferred, but you may be creative and make use of label0. Refer to the [paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.14091) for more details.

Please cite us as:
```bibtex
@article{hu2023revisiting,
      title={Revisiting Acceptability Judgements},
      author={Hai Hu and Ziyin Zhang and Weifang Huang and Jackie Yan-Ki Lai and Aini Li and Yina Ma and Jiahui Huang and Peng Zhang and Rui Wang},
      year={2023},
      eprint={2305.14091},
      journal={CoRR},
      volume={abs/2305.14091},
      url={http://arxiv.org/abs/2305.14091},
      eprinttype={arXiv},
}
```
----------------------
# Evaluation

CoLAC is a binary classification task and uses Matthew's Correlation Coefficient as the evaluation metric. A random prediction will receive a score around 0, and negative scores are possible if your performance is worse than random.

Training, development, and unlabeled test data can be downloaded in **Data**. To receive your test result, submit a CSV file containing two columns: 'id' (in the same order as `test.csv`, and 'label' (either 0 or 1)). We provide a random sample submission file.",# Dataset Description,False,True
6999,airbus-finance-ml-workshop,"# Description

## Competition

Welcome to the **Airbus Finance NLP Kaggle Competition** on classifying Twitter comments regarding airlines. 

* Participants will be provided with a dataset of tweets related to various airlines, along with their sentiment labels (positive or negative)(airline_sentiment_train.csv)
* We will process the text using common **Natural Language Processing (NLP)** techniques
* We will train a **ML Classification Model**
* Participants will also be provided with a dataset of tweets without a sentiment label (airline_sentiment_test.csv)
* Each team will submit their predictions and see their ranking in the **Leaderboard** section


## Python Code:
* A working code will be provided, only minor adjustments required
* The aim of this session is not to completely understand the code, but what the code is performing
* Don't worry if some parts of the code are unclear

Good luck, and we look forward to seeing your sentiment analysis!

## Prize
To format pages, stick to the following conventions:
- Inline `code` should be encapsulated by back-ticks \``. 
- Block code should have  \``` as the first and last line of the block to produce

----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric used will be Accuracy: The number of instances correctly classified divided by the total number of instances.

In this case our dataset is very balanced (same number of positives and negatives) so this metric is an appropriate one.","# Dataset Description

The Twitter airline dataset is a collection of comments posted by Twitter users about their travel experiences with different airlines. The dataset is composed of a total of 5,313 tweets that were gathered over a period of six months.

Each tweet has been labelled as either positive or negative based on the sentiment of the tweet towards the airline. 

## The data has been split into training (3,963 rows) and testing (1,350 rows) datasets

*   **airline_tweets_train.csv** - The training set, we will use this dataset to train our models
*   **airline_tweets_test.csv** - The test set, we will evaluate and rank teams with this dataset

## Columns

*   `Id` - Numeric Id of the tweet
*   `airline_sentiment`?- Sentiment of the tweet, 'positive' 'negative' or 'neutral'
*   `airline`?- Name of the airline referenced in the tweet
*   `text`?- Content of the tweet
*   `retweet_count`?- Number of retweets performed on tweet
*   `user_timezone`?- Timezone of the user posting tweet",False,True
7000,1871-ai-tech-challenge-2023,"# Description

## 1871 ¨C AI Tech Challenge
## Context

The company receives millions of calls from its customers every month and the calls are serviced by thousands of agents. The call channel is one of the primary channels through which customers inquire, provide feedback about the product and services, and raise any concerns. So, the call channel and the subsequent interactions with customers are key to delivering an exceptional customer experience and gaining insights into the needs of the customers.

The customer interaction data obtained through these calls provides valuable data to the operations and other groups within the company. The voice data, when transcribed and mined appropriately, can act as a secondary data source, and can enable the firm to identify new opportunities. 

The following are some of the use cases of transcribed data with near-term business benefits:
- Assessment of the level of customer satisfaction with a call interaction and the reasons for satisfaction or dissatisfaction.
- Identification of complaints associated with products or services so that complaints can be addressed efficiently and expeditiously.
- Understanding the context of the call so that the feedback and opportunities can be summarized and shared with the product development teams.
- Understanding the topic(s) associated with the calls so that customer service agents can be trained to address questions associated with multiple topics with minimal call transfers.

## Goal of the Competition
With the proliferation of Transformer-based-models aiding Natural Language Processing and Understanding, the company is interested in exploring opportunities to enhance current voice to text transcription capability to aid several downstream analytical, reporting, and modeling tasks. Taking advantage of Large Language Model, *******the challenge is to build a reliable voice-to-text transcription model. *******

## Challenge Overview
The training data for this challenge consists of audio files and their respective transcribed text. The test data only includes the audio files, that you will need to transcribe and submit. While your submission for the leaderboard will be scored on the accuracy of your transcribed text, we encourage you to take this one step further and perform a topic modeling step on the transcribed text to identify the main topic of the audio, saved as a list of strings of ""topics"" in an additional column. This should not be included in your competition submission, but may be reviewed by the host and company.
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is [Levenshtein Mean](https://medium.com/@ethannam/understanding-the-levenshtein-distance-equation-for-beginners-c4285a5604f0#:~:text=The%20Levenshtein%20distance%20is%20a,change%20one%20into%20the%20other.). The Levenshtein distance is a number that tells you how different two strings are. The higher the number, the more different the two strings are. You are seeking to minimize this metric to rise on the leaderboard.

**Submission Format**

Submission files should contain two columns: `Id` and `Predicted`. These should both be saved as string, and the `Predicted` column should contain the transcribed in all lowercase with no punctuation, except in the event of a contraction (i.e. that's).","# Dataset Description

# About Dataset

## General Information

The data for this challenge has been sourced from a Kaggle public dataset (https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/mozillaorg/common-voice?resource=download). Common Voice is a corpus of speech data read by users on the Common Voice website (http://voice.mozilla.org/), and based upon text from a number of public domain sources like user submitted blog posts, old books, movies, and other public speech corpora. Its primary purpose is to enable the training and testing of automatic speech recognition (ASR) systems.

## Structure

There is an audio zip folder that holds all of the audio clips. The path to these files is included as a value in the train data that accompanies the audio.

Each row of a csv file represents a single audio clip, and contains the following information:
id - unique identifier for that clip
filename - relative path of the audio file within the audio zip folder
text - supposed transcription of the audio
up_votes - number of people who said audio matches the text
down_votes - number of people who said audio does not match text

These final two columns indicate how accurate the training transcription text is. Because some callers may have loud background noise or may be otherwise hard to understand, we have included data that has uncertain transcriptions. This may be used to guide how you train on the data.

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format",False,True
