7022,nawfal-inductions-trial,"# Description

# PART - 1

The task is to implement a neural network for the below-given architecture, preferably dynamically, by extending the [micrograd course](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VMj-3S1tku0&ab_channel=AndrejKarpathy). You must implement both forward and backward passes and train it on the MNIST dataset given in the dataset here. You must also pickle your model weight, and your notebook should generate a submission.csv consisting of predictions for the test set.

During training, you must print your train and validation losses and accuracy (prefer a line plot using matplotlib or any other library too).

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F10121391%2F31abb2c54fc1644b3d8794e3d9f7a7f7%2FScreenshot%20from%202023-05-30%2013-01-04.png?generation=1685437287010186&alt=media)

Allowed Library: Numpy, Pickle, Matplotlib, CV2
Any other ML framework, such as PyTorch, TensorFlow, Keras, Theano, JAX and other libraries with autograd functionality, are NOT allowed. 

Submission:
 Make a private Kaggle notebook for the following (mentioned in resources) dataset and save using ¡°Commit and run all¡±. Make sure the notebook is successfully run without errors before the given deadline. ONLY After the deadline you will be asked to set your notebook to public. The latest version that was run before the deadline will only be considered.

The dataset contains trainingSet which you can use to train the model, and the test_set, which contains the images for which you have to predict the labels. The predicted labels of the corresponding images need to be saved in submission.csv format as shown in sample.csv.

During training, you must print your train (validation if you have split a portion from the train set) losses and accuracy (prefer a line plot using matplotlib or any other library too).


# PART - 2

Using Pytorch for this competition, train any model to train the given MNIST dataset in a different notebook.  You must save your model weight, and your notebook should generate a submission.csv consisting of predictions for the test set. Your main goal is to find and train an efficient (high accuracy, low model size) neural network model and are free to explore any architecture.

Your goal is to stay at the top of the leaderboards in Kaggle Competition.

During training, you must print your train and validation losses and accuracy (prefer a line plot using matplotlib or any other library too).

Allowed Library: Pytorch, Numpy, Pickle, Matplotlib, CV2

Brownie Points:
Explore Data Augmentation
Using Kaggle multiple GPU


Dataset Description:
The dataset contains trainingSet, which you must split into train and validation to train and validate the model, and the test_set, which includes the images for which you must predict the labels. The predicted labels of the corresponding images need to be saved in submission.csv format as shown in submission.csv (replace ¡°?¡± with your prediction for the corresponding image).

Submission:

 Make a PRIVATE Kaggle notebook for the following (mentioned in resources) competition and save using ¡°Commit and run all¡±. Make sure the notebook is successfully run without errors before the given deadline. 

You can then go to the OUTPUT section of your notebook and ¡°SUBMIT¡± the submission.csv file generated. ONLY After the deadline you will be asked to set your notebook to public. You can do multiple submissions while choosing which to consider for the final submission. Your aim is to get the highest leaderboard score while having a small model size. 

Check this for a more detailed explanation of how to make a Kaggle submission in competition. 

(Tip: First try to hit the highest accuracy and then start decreasing your model size)

Once you have submitted your notebook and submission.csv (any number of attempts) files in Kaggle to get your best score BEFORE the deadline, you must submit the final 2 URLs for BOTH part-1 and part-2 in the portal. AFTER the deadline, set all of your notebooks to the PUBLIC.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Evaluation Criteria:
Part -1: Your code implementation, idea/Solution
Part-2: How much you have experimented, Accuracy/Competition Scores, Code Implementation,  Efficient experimentation","# Dataset Description

