7059,drug-design-challenge,"# Description

## Drug design challenge 
The Physical Chemistry and Biology of Materials laboratory (LCBM) was created in 2020 with the aim of:
1.	Contribute to the development of industry (pharmaceutical, energy, agri-food, etc.) in Algeria through the development of new drugs, nano-based smart cards, and food supplements.
2.	Develop and optimize industrial processes in the various sectors (energy, pharmaceuticals and agriculture and foods).
3.	Develop and exploit patents in the field of drug design technologies and nanotechnology.
4.	Create a communication space bringing together the various actors in the fields of Chemistry, Physics, Biology, Computer sciences, mathematics, and other disciplines.

The laboratory includes four research teams. The research team that created this challenge is the following: Simulation and Modeling of Materials and Biomaterials
Among the activities of this team is the study of the structure of molecules and biomolecules by molecular modeling, in particular structure/ligand-based drug design methods which will make it possible to determine structures with determined biological activity (cancer, diabetes, HIV-1).
 
## Context
Cancer is one of the leading causes of global mortality according to the World Health Organization. The traditional methods of combating this health problem are chemotherapy, radiation therapy and surgery. Currently, the development of anticancer drugs is undergoing a methodological change thanks to artificial intelligence and data science. 
The main objective of this challenge is the identification of the molecules responsible for the inhibition of the ErbB1. The ErbB1 protein is involved in cell signaling pathways that control cell division and survival. Sometimes, mutations (changes) in genes cause ErbB1 proteins to be produced higher than the normal, which causes cancer cells to divide more rapidly. Drugs that block ErbB1 proteins are used in the treatment of some types of cancer. 

----------------------
# Evaluation

Since the latter problem is a Regression problem, the evaluation metric used is R squared (R^2)","# Dataset Description

## Dataset 
To achieve the objective explained in the previous section, a machine learning model should be trained to be used for virtual screening of chemical libraries such as ZINC, CHEMBLE. 
The present dataset includes the human ErbB1 target and an IC50 activity (enzyme inhibition parameter). The dataset contains 6953 molecules divided into 5215 molecules for training and 1738 for testing. Each molecule is represented using 1416 molecular descriptors. 
In this challenge, the following column is targeted:

1.	Regression target (pIC50_nM): continuous target which should be predicted using regression modeling. Here, the R^2 should be used as a performance measure. 

It is preferable that the work be done in an ipython notebook (Google collab could be considered as runtime). Moreover, all data science phases should be considered (Exploratory analysis, preprocessing, visualizations, modeling, model evaluation).

## Useful links
¡ñ	[The half maximal inhibitory concentration (IC50)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IC50#:~:text=IC50%20is%20a%20quantitative,cell%2C%20cell%20receptor%20or%20microorganism.)
¡ñ	[Quantitative structure¨Cactivity relationship models (QSAR)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quantitative_structure%E2%80%93activity_relationship)
¡ñ	[ErbB1](https://www.cancer.gov/publications/dictionaries/cancer-terms/def/erbb1)",False,True
7060,neurips-2023-machine-unlearning,"# Overview

Deep learning has recently driven tremendous progress in a wide array of applications, ranging from [realistic image generation](https://imagen.research.google/) and [impressive retrieval systems](https://ai.googleblog.com/2023/06/retrieval-augmented-visual-language-pre.html) to [language models that can hold human-like conversations](https://blog.google/technology/ai/bard-google-ai-search-updates/). While this progress is very exciting, the widespread use of deep neural network models requires caution: researchers should seek to develop AI technologies responsibly by understanding and mitigating potential risks, such as the propagation and amplification of unfair biases and protecting user privacy.

This competition, which is has been selected for the NeurIPS'23 competition track, considers a realistic scenario in which an age predictor has been trained on face images, and, after training, a certain subset of the training images must be forgotten to protect the privacy or rights of the individuals concerned.
----------------------
# Description

## Motivation

Fully erasing the influence of the data requested to be deleted is challenging since, aside from simply deleting it from databases where it¡¯s stored, it also requires erasing the influence of that data on other artifacts such as trained machine learning models. Moreover, recent research [[1](https://arxiv.org/abs/1610.05820), [2](https://arxiv.org/abs/2112.03570)] has shown that in some cases it may be possible to infer with high accuracy whether an example was used to train a machine learning model using [membership inference attacks](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adversarial_machine_learning#Model_extraction) (MIAs). This can raise privacy concerns, as it implies that even if an individual's data is deleted from a database, it may still be possible to infer whether that individual's data was used to train a model.

Given the above, machine unlearning is an emergent subfield of machine learning that aims to remove the influence of a specific subset of training examples ¡ª the ""forget set"" ¡ª from a trained model. Furthermore, an ideal unlearning algorithm would remove the influence of certain examples while maintaining other beneficial properties, such as the accuracy on the rest of the train set and generalization to held-out examples. A straightforward way to produce this unlearned model is to retrain the model on an adjusted training set that excludes the samples from the forget set. However, this is not always a viable option, as retraining deep models can be computationally expensive. An ideal unlearning algorithm would instead use the already-trained model as a starting point and efficiently make adjustments to remove the influence of the requested data.

## Competition

The competition considers a realistic scenario in which an age predictor has been trained on face images, and, after training, a certain subset of the training images must be forgotten to protect the privacy or rights of the individuals concerned. 

Your goal is to submit code that takes as input the trained predictor, the forget and retain sets, and outputs the weights of a predictor that has unlearned the designated forget set.  We will evaluate submissions based on both the strength of the forgetting algorithm and model utility. We also enforce a hard cut-off that rejects unlearning algorithms that run slower than a fraction of the time it takes to retrain. A valuable outcome of this competition will be to characterize the trade-offs of different unlearning algorithms.

![](https://blogger.googleusercontent.com/img/b/R29vZ2xl/AVvXsEiRnut8P03hlk5tKJPEEsqUl1DSlqN2ScdJeiaRfC3mWbQ_PBBwf7wBU9xgxuzr1GoqgkB6MwCa6Zrdo6LQxSOIPXIUrl1Yug73k2Q2zFI61VDAi9K21JOPox0Hc1CIh6ShKxW9Tgy45TYV3p3r5IiI7yxzzzOpzvbJ-5o3QVtjZn6vhDZLntnCcUSi1mb_/s720/image1.png)


The [starting kit](https://github.com/unlearning-challenge/starting-kit) provides an example of unlearning algorithms for participants to build their unlearning models upon. Note that the starting kit uses the CIFAR10 dataset, while submissions will be scored using a different (secret) dataset. You may also want to review this  [example submission notebook](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/neurips-2023-machine-unlearning/code).


> **This is a Code Competition. Refer to [Code Requirements](https://www.kaggle.com/c/neurips-2023-machine-unlearning/overview/code-requirements) for details.**


----------------------
# Evaluation

### Metric
Submissions are evaluated using [a metric outlined in this paper](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/neurips-2023-machine-unlearning/data?select=Machine_Unlearning_Notion_Metric.pdf) that takes into account both forgetting quality as well as model utility. For forgetting quality, our metric is an estimate of the the indistinguishability of the distribution of unlearned models and the distribution of retrained-from-scratch models. We then penalize unlearning algorithms that reduce the accuracy of the model. Full details of the metric will be released once the competition is over.

Important note: To measure forgetting quality we require an estimation of two distributions: the one obtained from unlearning and that obtained from retraining-from-scratch. In the case of retraining-from-scratch, two important sources of randomness are the initialization and the order with which data appears in mini-batches. In the case of unlearning, we expect that the provided original model is used as the initialization, but it is up to the participants to ensure different ordering of data in each epoch (e.g. by shuffling the dataset). If no shuffling is done, for example, it might be that running unlearning many times may not accurately capture the desired distribution and may consequently lead to a lower score. _You are responsible for introducing sufficient randomness into your forgetting runs_.

The metric's estimation of forgetting quality is unavoidably not perfect. The evaluation protocol of the challenge has been implemented with a high level of care, given our available human and computational resources and using state-of-the-art knowledge. However, no guarantees are provided by the organizers that our metric is flawless, nor that the results of the challenge will be indicative of the fitness of the algorithms to perform unlearning in other settings. Competitors should understand these factors as among the risks of the competition. 

### Submission Mechanics
You will need to submit a notebook that generates a file named `submission.zip` containing 512 Pytorch model checkpoints derived by causing **original_model.pth** to unlearn the image IDs in **forget.csv**. Failing to introduce random variation will most likely degrade your score. We recommend, at a minimum, shuffling the data each iteration. At the moment you must create your checkpoint files outside of the working directory to avoid exceeding each notebook's exported file disk size cap. We expect zipping to take approximately one hour.

_For most of the competition your submission notebooks will only have access to the public set._ At the end of the competition we will rescore your selected submissions on the private set. Unlike most competitions, it's possible to get a public leaderboard score from a submission that will not ultimately generate a private score. You are strongly encouraged to add error handling to your code and to test it against a variety of inputs. Competitors should understand this as among the risks of the competition.

----------------------
# Timeline

* **September 11, 2023** - Start Date.

* **November 20, 2023** - Entry Deadline. You must accept the competition rules before this date in order to compete.

* **November 20, 2023** - Team Merger Deadline. This is the last day participants may join or merge teams.

* **November 27, 2023** - Final Submission Deadline.

All deadlines are at 11:59 PM UTC on the corresponding day unless otherwise noted. The competition organizers reserve the right to update the contest timeline if they deem it necessary.
----------------------
# Prizes

* 1st Place - $ 10,000
* 2nd Place - $ 9,000
* 3rd Place - $ 8,000
* 4th Place - $ 7,000
* 5th Place - $ 6,000
* 6th Place - $ 5,000
* 7th Place - $ 5,000

Given the highly exploratory nature of this research, and the risks involved with participating, this competition will _not_ award points and medals.
----------------------
# Code Requirements

<img src=""https://storage.googleapis.com/kaggle-media/competitions/general/Kerneler-white-desc2_transparent.png"" style=""float: right; width: 70px; padding: 10px"">

### This is a Code Competition

Submissions to this competition must be made through Notebooks. In order for the ""Submit"" button to be active after a commit, the following conditions must be met:

- CPU Notebook <= 8 hours run-time
- GPU Notebook <= 8 hours run-time
- Internet access disabled
- Freely & publicly available external data is allowed, including pre-trained models
- Submission file must be named `submission.zip`. We timed zip creation as taking roughly one hour.

Please see the [Code Competition FAQ](https://www.kaggle.com/docs/competitions#notebooks-only-FAQ) for more information on how to submit. And review the [code debugging doc](https://www.kaggle.com/code-competition-debugging) if you are encountering submission errors.","# Dataset Description

Removing examples from a trained machine learning model is a major unsolved problem of ML privacy research. This competition challenges you to cause a simple model to forget images from several individuals.

## Hidden Dataset Files
This is a code competition with a hidden dataset which is completely unavailable for download. We highly recommend reviewing [this example notebook](https://www.kaggle.com/code/eleni30fillou/run-unlearn-finetune) for a demonstration of how to generate a valid submission.

**[retain/forget/validation].csv** The data used to train and validate the original model checkpoint, with the original train set split into ids that need to be retained and images from 15 subjects that must be unlearned. Unlike most competitions, the public and private scores are generated using the same dataset with different splits and evaluated across separate notebook runs.
*   `person_id` - A unique ID code for each subject.
*   `age_group` - Binned ages. The target label for the models.
*   `age` - The age of the subject.
*   `image_id` - A unique ID for the image. Includes the `person_id` to ensure each value is unique, like 'abc-1'.

**images/[person_id]/[image_id].png** Images of people's faces. If the image ID is 'abc-1' the path is `images/abc/1.png`. All of the images have been resized to 32x32 pixels. Expect approximately 30,000 images in the hidden dataset. Note that roughly 2% of the images in the dataset have identical perceptual hashes; this is a known limitation of the problem setup.

**age_class_weights.json** The `age_group` weights used to train the original model. Loading this file yields a dict of the form `{age_group: n_occurrences}`.

**original_model.pth** The original Resnet-18 Pytorch model checkpoint that you will need to cause to forget certain images. The model was trained to predict the age group associated with each image of a person¡¯s face. It was trained with class weights to mitigate class imbalance and no data augmentation. The original model obtains 99% accuracy on the training set and 96% on the test set and was trained with torch 1.13.1.

However, these performance figures come with a substantial caveat: the train/test split was done using the image ID rather than the subject ID. All subjects in the test set also exist in the train set. For a _classification_ task this would be considered leakage but _may_ have different implications in an unlearning task. As the retain/forget splits were done by subject ID, it's possible this approach may exaggerate the impact of unlearning any single example on the underlying model. This is a known limitation of the problem setup and competitors should understand this as one of the risks of the competition.",True,True
7061,dlsprint2,"# Description

Bengali is the fifth most spoken of all native languages worldwide. After completing the month-long signature Deep Learning competition on Bengali Automatic Speech Recognition (ASR) last year, we at the **Department of Computer Science and Engineering, BUET** now focus on another computational challenge. This year again, in partnership with **Bengali.AI**, we welcome participants to attend the **DL Sprint 2.0** and play around with the first multi-domain large **Bengali Document Layout Analysis Dataset: BaDLAD**, as a part of **BUET CSE Fest 2023.**

For training the model, we will use a subset of the BaDLAD dataset contributed by our co-host, Bengali.AI. The details of the dataset can be found [in this paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.05325)

***This competition is open to all, not only for undergraduates. Anyone from any institution can participate.***
***However, in the spirit of fairness, all students from BUET CSE18 (the organizing batch) are disallowed from participating.***

## How to Participate

Registration link: https://forms.gle/FffwUxn3huKYBjhZ8. **Anyone can join this competition. However, registration is mandatory to be eligible for prizes.**

Facebook event link: [DL Sprint 2.0](https://www.facebook.com/events/1882607365439555)

## Competition Schedule
Phase 1: Will be held in Kaggle from 18th June to 5th August (11:59 PM GMT+6). Participants are expected to build and train their models during this phase. The top 15 teams from this round will be selected for Phase 2.
Phase 2: Will be held onsite at the Department of CSE, BUET, on 11th August (tentative). Only invited participants can participate. Participants will present their methods live in front of the judges¡¯ panel.

**Important**
All teams will submit working Kaggle notebooks for Train and Test with appropriate instructions and documentation for reproducibility after Phase 1. **Test(/Inference) notebook must run on Kaggle Notebooks (with any accelerator).**

## Scoring
**50% Weight on Phase-1**
<ul>
<li>15% public DICE</li>
<li>35% private DICE</li>
</ul>

**50% Weight on Phase-2**
<ul>
<li>25% mAP </li>
<li>20% Judge and Presentation (Explainability, Reproducibility)</li>
<li>5% Inference Speed</li>
</ul>


## Acknowledgements
We thank BJIT for sponsoring BUET CSE Fest 2023 and Bengali.AI for co-hosting the DL Sprint 2.0 event with technical support.
----------------------
# Evaluation

# Prediction

Your model should predict the masks for **segmentation** bounding polygons in each image. 

A mask is a 2D matrix corresponding to each pixel of the input image. The contents of each pixel position can be `0` or `1`. If the pixel position is marked `0`, then that position corresponds to the background category, no paragraph, text box, image, or table exists at that pixel. This mask is called a **binary mask** because of its content. 

Each prediction category (paragraph, text box, image, table) has a binary mask for each image. For example, image `846df66a-610e-4356-b369-6788885a0dc5.png` has 4 binary masks for each existing category - that predict where that specific category occurs in the image. 

*Bounding boxes play no role in the evaluation for this competition.*

# Run Length Encoding (RLE) of Masks

The mask matrices become too big, considering the number of images you must predict. To compress the masks, we use RLE encoding. The function used for RLE conversion is the following:

```python
def rle_encode(mask: np.ndarray) -> str:
    # flatten the mask column wise
    pixels = mask.T.flatten()

    # pad the list of pixels to ensure they have leading and trailing 0
    use_padding = False
    if pixels[0] or pixels[-1]:
        use_padding = True
        pixel_padded = np.zeros([len(pixels) + 2], dtype=pixels.dtype)
        pixel_padded[1:-1] = pixels
        pixels = pixel_padded

    # get the pixel indices where consecutive pixels don't match => binary masks begin or end
    # +1 to convert to inclusive start indices, exclusive end indices of masks
    # +1 to convert to 1-indexed pixel ordering
    rle = np.where(pixels[1:] != pixels[:-1])[0] + 2
    if use_padding:
        rle = rle - 1  # remove the extra offset introduced by padding

    # end pixel indices of masks - start pixel indices of masks = length of RLE runs
    # store the RLE run lengths replacing the end pixel indices 
    rle[1::2] = rle[1::2] - rle[:-1:2]
    
    return ' '.join(str(x) for x in rle)
```

You can read about the RLE encoding format [here](https://www.kaggle.com/code/leahscherschel/run-length-encoding). *(NB: The explanation in the link uses RLE encoding in a 0-indexed grid, but the function above uses a 1-indexed grid. You are advised to use the 1-indexed implementation above to match the ground truth.)*

# Submission Format

You will submit a `CSV` file for each image, and each category. Your `submission.csv` will contain 2 columns: `Id` and `Predicted`. 
1. `Id`: This column will be composed of each `image_id`, and each `category_id` for that image. They will be concatenated in the form `<image_id>_<category_id>`. 
2. `Predicted`: This will contain the RLE encoded mask for each image category. 

You have a sample submission (`sample_subimssion.csv`) available in the dataset. 

**All teams will submit working Kaggle notebooks for Train and Test with appropriate instructions and documentation for reproducibility to verify the authenticity of the solution.**

# Evaluation Metric: Dice Score
 ![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F10747121%2F93d164345ff828b16e609c96f64d6791%2Fdice.png?generation=1687102960736961&alt=media)","# Dataset Description

# BaDLAD
The Dataset paper is available at: https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.05325.

# About the Dataset
The dataset for this competition comprises about 34,000 Bengali documents annotated using polygon boundaries and rectangular bounding boxes. These documents range from newspaper articles, official documents, notices, and government gadgets to novels, comics, magazines, and even liberation war records.


<img src=""https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F10747121%2F5174eee762e639cc95d3947559af144f%2FIMG_0052.png?generation=1687142796077441&alt=media""  width=""90%"" height=""30%"">

<img src=""https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F10747121%2F326a661f994df5d01e0a697a1eeded42%2FIMG_0053.png?generation=1687142841349263&alt=media""  width=""90%"" height=""30%"">


The annotations specify the regions in these documents that fall into 4 classes - 
- Paragraph
- Text Box
- Image
- Table

Your task is to design a deep learning pipeline that takes in a document image (`.png`) and predicts bounding polygon segments (masks) for each occurrence of the classes mentioned above in the input image. 

# Data Details
There are 20,365 training and 13,000 test images. The `train` and `test` images can be found in in `badlad/images/` directory, whereas the labels for the `train` dataset can be found in `badlad/labels/` directory. 

## Train

Training set images are in `badlad/images/train`. 

The `train` images have `labels` corresponding to them. For your convenience, we provide 2 forms of labels:
1. **COCO Format**: `badlad/labels/coco_format/train` 
    The COCO format labels are in `badlad-train-coco.json` file. This `JSON` file has some notable fields:
   1. `categories`: Maps instance categories to their assigned IDs. 
   2. `images`: Maps image IDs (`id`) to their hashed name, height, and width. 
    For example,

        | ImageId | Width | Height | ImageFile | FilePath |
        | ------- | ----- | ------ | --------- | -------- |
        | 0 | 720 | 995 | `846df66a-610e-4356-b369-6788885a0dc5.png` | `badlad/images/train/846df66a-610e-4356-b369-6788885a0dc5.png` |
    
   3. `annotations`: This is a list of the polygon bounding segments for each instance of the categories occurring in each image. The contents of this category are the crux of the problem:
      - `segmentation`: Polygon `(x, y)` coordinate pairs of it's vertices. 
For example, the first annotation has its first vertex at `(86.328, 179.007)`. The following pairs complete the polygon region of the annotation. 
      - `image_id`: The image this annotation is for. 
      - `category_id`: The category this annotation is bounding. It may be a paragraph, text box, image, or table. 
      - `id`: Annotation ID, identifies each annotation. 
      - `bbox`: The rectangular bounding box that best estimates the annotated region. 
    
    This list holds all the annotations for each instance of the categories for all images. 

2. **YOLOv8 Format**: `badlad/labels/yolov8_format/train`
    Each training image has its label as a text file of the same name in the mentioned directory. 
    For example,

    | Image | Label |
    | ----- | ----- |
    | `badlad/images/train/846df66a-610e-4356-b369-6788885a0dc5.png` | `badlad/labels/yolov8_format/train/846df66a-610e-4356-b369-6788885a0dc5.txt` |

    You can read more about the expected YOLOv8 data format [here](https://docs.ultralytics.com/datasets/segment/).

## Test

Test set images are in `badlad/images/test`. 

The test set has no provided annotations. Your task is to learn the bounding polygon from the train set and predict it for the test set images. 

However, the test set does have metadata associated with each image. That metadata can be found in `badlad/test-metadata.json`. This `JSON` file consists of all the fields of the training labels *except the annotations*. 

# Predictions

Your model should predict the masks for bounding polygons in each image. A mask is a 2D matrix corresponding to each input image pixel. The contents of each pixel position can be `0` or `1`. If the pixel position is marked `0`, then that position corresponds to the background category, no paragraph, text box, image, or table exists at that pixel. This mask is called a **binary mask** because of its content. Each prediction category (paragraph, text box, image, table) has a binary mask for each image. For example, image `846df66a-610e-4356-b369-6788885a0dc5.png` has 4 binary masks, for each existing category - that predict where in the image, that specific category occurs. 

<img src=""https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F10747121%2F54232aef9b0ceab41a33f8113cd0600e%2F__results___71_0.png?generation=1687185171344197&alt=media"" height=""80%"" width=""80%""/>

# Cite the Paper

```
@article{shihab2023badlad,
  title={BaDLAD: A Large Multi-Domain Bengali Document Layout Analysis Dataset},
  author={Shihab, Md Istiak Hossain and Hasan, Md Rakibul and Emon, Mahfuzur Rahman and Hossen, Syed Mobassir and Ansary, Md Nazmuddoha and Ahmed, Intesur and Rakib, Fazle Rabbi and Dhruvo, Shahriar Elahi and Dip, Souhardya Saha and Pavel, Akib Hasan and others},
  journal={arXiv preprint arXiv:2303.05325},
  year={2023}
}
```",False,True
7062,predict-acquire-winner,"# Description

## The game
[Acquire](https://boardgamegeek.com/boardgame/5/acquire) has been [in and out](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Acquire#Publication_history) of physical print with a few publishers over the last 6 decades since its invention. The [online version](https://acquire.tlstyer.com/) has been around for about 1 of those decades and the site's owner was gracious enough to share the history of almost every game ever recorded on the site.

You can join the site and play, but be ye warned: these are people that take this game _very_ seriously. Many have played over 10,000 times. It's free to join (FYI: passwords are NOT resettable) and free to watch any and all games on the site. And if you dare to join a game, whatever you do, don't quit in the middle (_especially_ if you see you're about to lose). If you do, you'll be instantly branded a pariah and no one will play with you. Some players even create dummy users simply to mock users who quit (see `Silverado21=quitter+liar` and `Silverado21=quitter-liar`).

## A quick tour of the game's rules
Players place tiles to grow and merge companies, shares of which they can buy and sell to build wealth (I would have named this game ""pump-and-dump"", but whatever). Whoever has the most net worth at the end of the game wins. (there's obviously LOADS more to the details, but that's for later competitions since I'm planning on making this somewhat of a series) This dataset is limited to only and all the fully-completed (the cardinal sin of the online community is quitting mid-game), 4-player games, totaling about XXXXXX records.

## Inspiration
I was taking a graduate level course in data science and decided to see if I could predict the winner of a proposed matchup. Based solely on the players who sat down to play, and their past record, I didn't get much better accuracy than pure chance (0.33). The game has a strong element of chance (which is part of what makes it fun), so a 100% accurate model is effectively impossible. But there are definitely ""good"" players (roughly winning 40% of their matches) and ""not-so-good"" players (barely winning 10% of their matches).

## The data
The data is 8 columns of records of games straight from the server logs (with a timestamp) and one calculated column to indicate the winning player (calculated based on their score). This `winning_seat` (`1, 2, 3 or 4`) is the target variable, so random chance gets you 0.25% accuracy for free.

## Acknowledgements
I am deeply grateful to Tim Styer, both for sharing the data with me and allowing me to share it with this community.
----------------------
# Evaluation

## Metric
Simple accuracy. No frills.

## Submission Format

For every game in the dataset, submission files should contain a `winning_seat` column containing the predicted winning seat for that game.


The file should contain a header and have the following format:
```
gameID,winning_seat
1,1
```","# Dataset Description

The online version of Acquire has been running for almost 10 years and Tim Styer was kind enough to share his server logs with me (shared by permission). The data here have 9 columns created from server logs (4 player IDs, 4 final scores, timestamp of game-start), and 1 calculated column: `winning_seat` (the seat (1, 2, 3, or 4) of the player with the highest final score). This is the target value for this dataset. The `train.csv` file has all values and all columns for the ""first"" 70% of the data. The split for this data is chronological in nature to simulate predicting the most-likely winner of a new game. The test data is split 50/50 public/private and has only the 4 player IDs for each of the 4 seats (the final scores are known, but would not be known at the start of the game).

All timestamps are unique, as are all player IDs. While it is technically feasible for a player to finish the game with $0, one would have to work extra hard in order to achieve that. I have not seen a conclusive proof of the maximum possible score, but it is effectively impossible in a real-world game, let alone a competitive one.

The game Acquire is fun specifically _because_ it has such a strong balance between skill and luck. The luck part is getting dealt the right tiles at the right times, and the skill is knowing what tiles to play when, and what stock to buy when. Even with the perfect tiles, you can screw it up and lose. But even a poor player can beat a master if the tiles are just perfect. A poor player might only win 1/10 games consistently, but a truly excellent player can win 5/10 games on a good day. So predicting the winner with >90% accuracy will be effectively impossible, but the results I was getting were so abysmal, I'm kind of curious what this community is capable of.

I can upload my notebook (as a benchmark?) if needed, but I've just done a stupid random benchmark to show that randomly choosing a player does in fact get non-zero accuracy.

The purpose of my original analysis was to see if players who played more were better or if there was some other factor involved in player skill level. I haven't unearthed the 'secret sauce' yet, but I will absolutely do more competitions from the same server logs if there's interest. The logs have every move by every player in every game, so who's sitting at the table is the most `a priori` sort of knowledge-state for predictions. I'm open to other suggestions for competition schema.


## Columns

*   `playerX` - player1, player2, player3, and player4. These are the unique IDs for the players in each of the respective positions.
*   `scoreX`?- score1, score2, score3, and score4 are the final scores for each respective player position (NOT by ranking).
*   `gameID` - This is used as the ID, but it's a server timestamp of when the game started (there were half a dozen or so 'collisions', but I just added 1000 msecs to those).
*   `winning_player` - This is a calculated column that identifies the seat # of the player with the highest final score.",False,True
7063,k-means-clustering-for-heart-disease-analysis,"# Description

#Introduction

Clustering algorithms play a crucial role in grouping similar items together. The application of unsupervised learning algorithms can provide valuable insights in various industries. For instance, retailers can effectively target customers by clustering them based on similarities, biologists can identify plants with shared characteristics, clustering can also be used to group similar documents together based on their content, allowing for effective document organization, etc. In this particular challenge, we will investigate the suitability of employing clustering algorithms to group medical patients.

#Description

Doctors often analyze past cases to enhance their understanding of optimal treatment approaches for their patients. When a patient exhibits similar health history or symptoms to a previous case, they may benefit from undergoing a similar treatment regimen. 
This challenge aims to explore the possibility of grouping patients based on common characteristics using unsupervised learning techniques. Specifically, we will investigate the use of k-means algorithm. By employing this method, we can identify clusters of patients with similar attributes, facilitating targeted treatment strategies.

#Objectives
In this challenge, our focus is on examining anonymized patients who have received a diagnosis of heart disease. By identifying patients who share similar characteristics, we can gain insights into the effectiveness of specific treatments. This information would be valuable for doctors, as they could learn from the outcomes of patients similar to those they are treating. The dataset originates from the V.A. Medical Center in Long Beach, California.

----------------------
# Evaluation

#How to submit ?
After creating and fitting a K-Means model to the heart_disease dataset. Perform a clustering on the patients that have their IDs present on the sample_submission file. Format your submission as a table of (id, cluster) just like the sample and submit to kaggle.

#Evaluation
# Log Loss

Log Loss, also known as Logistic Loss or Cross-Entropy Loss, is commonly used in machine learning as a performance metric for classification problems. It quantifies the accuracy of a classifier by penalising false classifications.

Mathematically, it is defined as:

$$
-log P(y_t|y_p) = -(y_t log(y_p) + (1 - y_t) log(1 - y_p))
$$

Where:
- `y_t` is the true label (0 or 1 in case of binary classification)
- `y_p` is the predicted probability of the instance being in class 1

Log loss penalises both types of errors (false positives and false negatives) but especially those predictions that are confident and wrong!

Log loss has the following properties:

1. Log Loss is a *strictly convex* function, making it a good choice for optimization algorithms.
2. It penalises hugely confident wrong predictions infinitely.
3. The best possible log loss score is 0, indicating perfect predictions. The score gets worse as the predicted probabilities diverge from the true labels.
4. It's sensitive to the confidence of the prediction, not just the prediction outcome. It rewards the model if the predicted probability matches the actual class.

Note that, when using log loss, correct predictions must have predicted probabilities that reflect the underlying confidence in the prediction. It's not enough to predict the correct class, the probabilities need to be accurate as well.","# Dataset Description

| N¡ã | Feature  | Type        | Description                                      | Units  |
|----|----------|-------------|--------------------------------------------------|--------|
| 1  | id       | Discrete    | id of the patient                                |        |
| 2  | age      | Discrete    | age                                              | years  |
| 3  | sex      | Categorical | sex of the patient (1=male, 0=female)            |        |
| 4  | dataset  | Categorical | origin of instance                               |        |
| 5  | cp       | Categorical | chest pain type                                  |        |
| 6  | trestbps | Discrete    | resting blood pressure                           | mm Hg  |
| 7  | chol     | Discrete    | cholesterol measurement                          | mg/dl  |
| 8  | fbs      | Categorical | fasting blood sugar > 120 mg/dl                  |        |
| 9  | restecg  | Categorical | resting electrocardiographic results             |        |
| 10 | thalach  | Discrete    | maximum heart rate achieved                      |        |
| 11 | exang    | Categorical | exercise induced angina (1 = yes, 0 = no)        |        |
| 12 | oldpeak  | Discrete    | ST depression induced by exercise relative to rest |        |
| 13 | slope    | Categorical | slope of the peak exercise ST segment            |        |
| 14 | ca       | Discrete    | number of major vessels (0-3)                    |        |
| 15 | thal     | Categorical | a blood disorder called thalassemia              |        |",False,True
7064,smurfs-mushotels-reservation,"# Description

## Introduction:
     Welcome to the Smurfs Data Challenge: Unveiling the Secrets of Smurf Village! In this competition, we explore the enchanting world of the Smurfs and analyze a rich dataset containing information about their characters and activities. Before we begin, it is crucial to understand the importance of data cleaning. Data cleaning ensures the quality and reliability of the dataset by addressing errors, inconsistencies, and anomalies. By conducting thorough data cleaning, we can unlock meaningful insights and draw accurate conclusions from the Smurfs dataset. So, let's dive in and discover the hidden secrets of the Smurf village through the power of clean data!

## Back-story:
     In the enchanting realm of the Smurfs, Papa Smurf, the wise and caring leader of the Smurf village, owns a company dedicated to unraveling the mysteries of their magical world. Recently, Papa Smurf has identified a pressing challenge faced by the hospitality sector in the Smurf village ¨C predicting hotel reservation cancellations. To address this, he has gathered a team of his most trusted and skilled Smurfs: Smurfette, known for her keen intuition and understanding of emotions; Handy Smurf, the master craftsman and problem-solver; Brainy Smurf, the resident intellectual with a wealth of knowledge; and Jokey Smurf, the mischievous prankster who adds a touch of humor to the group. As a participant in this competition, you step into the role of a data scientist entrusted with the task of merging the diverse datasets provided by each team member and creating a powerful machine learning model that can accurately predict hotel reservation cancellations. Papa Smurf and the entire Smurf village eagerly await the insights and solutions you will uncover on this exhilarating data-driven journey. Together, let us harness the power of data to enhance the Smurfs' understanding of their world and improve their hospitality endeavors.

## Objectives:
     The primary objective of the Smurfs Data Challenge is to leverage the collective knowledge and skills of the Smurf team and the participant, serving as the data scientist, to develop an effective machine learning model that accurately predicts hotel reservation cancellations in the Smurf village. By merging the datasets contributed by Papa Smurf, Smurfette, Handy Smurf, Brainy Smurf, and Jokey Smurf, we aim to create a comprehensive and cohesive dataset that captures the diverse aspects influencing reservation cancellations. Through meticulous data cleaning, feature engineering, and model development, our goal is to unlock valuable insights that will empower hotel owners in the Smurf village to proactively manage cancellations, optimize resource allocation, and enhance the overall guest experience. We strive to deliver a robust and reliable predictive model that can contribute to the sustainable growth and success of the Smurf village's hospitality industry. As we embark on this exciting data-driven journey, our shared objectives are to showcase the power of data science, foster collaboration among team members, and unveil actionable solutions that will shape the future of hotel reservations in the magical realm of the Smurfs.
 

----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is [Mean F1-Score](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/F-score). The F1 score, commonly used in information retrieval, measures accuracy using the statistics precision \( \text{p} \) and recall \( \text{r} \).

Precision is the ratio of true positives \(\text{tp}\) to all predicted positives \(\text{tp} + \text{fp}\). Recall is the ratio of true positives \(\text{tp}\) to all actual positives \(\text{tp} + \text{fn}\). The F1 score is given by:

\[ \text{F1} = 2\frac{\text{p} \cdot \text{r}}{\text{p}+\text{r}}\ \ \mathrm{where}\ \ \text{p} = \frac{\text{tp}}{\text{tp}+\text{fp}},\ \ \text{r} = \frac{\text{tp}}{\text{tp}+\text{fn}} \]

The F1 metric weights recall and precision equally, and a good retrieval algorithm will maximize both precision and recall simultaneously. Thus, moderately good performance on both will be favored over extremely good performance on one and poor performance on the other.","# Dataset Description

Each smurf collected his own data. For that reason, there are multiple datasets. The datasets may have different  

