# About this Task??
Recent years have seen a rise in online crowdsourcing platforms that facilitate volunteering efforts, allowing individuals to contribute to collective goals and address community needs. To optimize these efforts, it is crucial to understand volunteer behavior and collaboration patterns. In this challenge, we present a volunteer participation dataset with two tasks:

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F9176866%2F03e5406fa4ebf49cc9ad51542896e7dc%2Fslide8(1).gif?generation=1687098246971184&alt=media)

Volunteer Dataset: The dataset is collected from a mobile crowdsourcing app called ¡°Anti-Pandemic Pioneer¡± used for organizing volunteers during the COVID-19 pandemics in Shenzhen, China. It contains records of each volunteer participating in a task, including features like volunteer ID, task ID, timestamp, task location, etc. Task names and descriptions will also be provided.

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F9176866%2F95e49f9816dd5fd63af1987ab3297717%2Fmap.png?generation=1687256611473169&alt=media)

**Task 1: Retention Prediction:** As indicated by the orange highlighted area in the above figure, the dataset used in the retention prediction task encompasses all districts in Shenzhen. Participants are given a dataset consisting of volunteers and their participation histories from February 2020 to May 2021. The goal is to predict the retention of each volunteer, which is defined as the number of future participations during the prediction time span from May 2021 to February 2022. The ground truths for evaluation are determined by counting the number of tasks in which each user participated during the prediction time span.

**Key Points & Competition Details:**
- Participants can choose to participate in either one or both tasks.
- The datasets for both tasks have identical structure but sampled from distinct districts, ensuring no overlap.
- The evaluation metrics are Root Mean Squared Error(RMSE) for Task 1 and Area Under Receiver Operating Characteristic curve (AUC-ROC) for Task 2.
- Both datasets are split into a train set and a test set. The test sets are further split into private test set (2/3 for final evaluation) and public test set (1/3 for getting feedbacks during the competition).
- Participants can access all datasets but only labels for the train sets.
- Submissions can be made for the public test set twice a day, and immediate scores (RMSE and AUC-ROC) will be provided.
- Submission deadline will be one months after the start of the competition.

**If you have any questions or inquiries regarding this competition, please do not hesitate to contact us. You can reach out to us through [our Slack channel](https://join.slack.com/t/volunteer-challenge/shared_invite/zt-1xq09saep-50EqZDlw7ds5EHN3Lbp1_g).**

**To access further details about the competition, please visit our [competition website](https://volunteerchallenge.github.io/).**

**Please NOTE.** that the intellectual property (IP) is not transferred to the challenge organizers, i.e., if code is shared/submitted, the participants remain the owners of their code (when the code is made publicly available, an appropriate license should be added).

**Privacy Notice.** The volunteer activity data used in this challenge were passively collected from the mobile platform with personal identification information removed. No investigations on humans were involved. During the preparation of this challenge, the organizers did not have access to any identification information associated with the collected data. We are committed to adhering to strict privacy and ethical guidelines during the entire competition process.

**Acknowledgement.** We thank the Shenzhen Pioneers APP operation team for providing the volunteer data.
