Hello colleagues, we made some important updates regarding the **Task 2** dataset. The affected files, **`colab_train.csv`** and **`colab_train_edge_list.csv`**, have been modified to adjust the training data time span to a more realistic setting. The new time range is **""2020.2-2021.8""** instead of ""2020.2-2022.2"".  
 
Please make sure to use the training dataset with the **corrected time span** when training your model. Furthermore, when submitting your paper, please **include your code**. 

Please note that these changes **may affect the leaderboard**. We sincerely apologize for the inconvenience this may cause and appreciate your cooperation.  

# About this Task??
Recent years have seen a rise in online crowdsourcing platforms that facilitate volunteering efforts, allowing individuals to contribute to collective goals and address community needs. To optimize these efforts, it is crucial to understand volunteer behavior and collaboration patterns. In this challenge, we present a volunteer participation dataset with two tasks:

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F9176866%2Fd902a44e3d8e4097d23f39211feb7e24%2Fslide8(1).gif?generation=1687097913843193&alt=media)

**Volunteer Dataset**: The dataset is collected from a mobile crowdsourcing app called ¡°Anti-Pandemic Pioneer¡± used for organizing volunteers during the COVID-19 pandemics in Shenzhen, China. It contains records of each volunteer participating in a task, including features like volunteer ID, task ID, timestamp, task location, etc. Task names and descriptions will also be provided.

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F9176866%2F95e49f9816dd5fd63af1987ab3297717%2Fmap.png?generation=1687256611473169&alt=media)

**Task 2: Future Collaboration Prediction:** Our objective is to predict potential collaborations between pairs of volunteers who will continue their volunteering efforts. As shown in the purple highlighted areas in the above figure, we have selected two districts, Futian District and Longhua District, from the data as the dataset for this task. The available resources consist of volunteer activities spanning from February 2020 to July 2021, along with an edge list serving as the ground truth. Our goal is to forecast the collaboration between pairs of volunteers on a monthly basis over a 6-month period, ranging from August 2021 to February 2022. Predicting future collaborations holds diverse applications, including gaining insights into group dynamics within volunteer communities and developing social recommendations for volunteers.

**Key Points & Competition Details:**
- Participants can choose to participate in either one or both tasks.
- The datasets for both tasks have identical structure but sampled from distinct districts, ensuring no overlap.
- The evaluation metrics are Root Mean Squared Error(RMSE) for Task 1 and Area Under Receiver Operating Characteristic curve (AUC-ROC) for Task 2.
- Both datasets are split into a train set and a test set. The test sets are further split into private test set (2/3 for final evaluation) and public test set (1/3 for getting feedbacks during the competition).
- Participants can access all datasets but only labels for the train sets.
- Submissions can be made for the public test set twice a day, and immediate scores (RMSE and AUC-ROC) will be provided.
- Submission deadline will be one months after the start of the competition.

**If you have any questions or inquiries regarding this competition, please do not hesitate to contact us. You can reach out to us through [our Slack channel] (https://join.slack.com/t/volunteer-challenge/shared_invite/zt-1xq09saep-50EqZDlw7ds5EHN3Lbp1_g).**

**To access further details about the competition, please visit our [competition website](https://volunteerchallenge.github.io/).**

**Please NOTE.** that the intellectual property (IP) is not transferred to the challenge organizers, i.e., if code is shared/submitted, the participants remain the owners of their code (when the code is made publicly available, an appropriate license should be added).

**Privacy Notice.** The volunteer activity data used in this challenge were passively collected from the mobile platform with personal identification information removed. No investigations on humans were involved. During the preparation of this challenge, the organizers did not have access to any identification information associated with the collected data. We are committed to adhering to strict privacy and ethical guidelines during the entire competition process.

**Acknowledgement.** We thank the Shenzhen Pioneers APP operation team for providing the volunteer data.
----------------------
# Evaluation

The goal of this task is to forecast the collaboration between pairs of volunteers on a monthly basis over a 6-month period, ranging from August 2021 to February 2022.

## Submission Format

- The file should contain two columns named `Id` and `Predicted`.
- The `Id` column should consist of three hyphen-connected elements. These elements represent pairwise volunteers and a timestep `i` ranging from 0 to 5, indicating the prediction for the next `i-th` month.
- Each `Predicted` value in the file represents the probability of collaboration between the corresponding two participants.

The file should contain a header and have the following format. Also, a sample submission form is given at [Data](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/MLSP2023-volunteers-02/data?select=sample_submission_collaboration_multistep.csv) Page. 
```
Id,Predicted
122028-204227-0,0.7
185807-155353-3,0.3
185807-155353-5,0.4
```

## Area Under Receiver Operating Characteristic Curve (AUC-ROC Curve)

The evaluation metric for this competition is the [Area Under Receiver Operating Characteristic Curve (AUC-ROC Curve)](https://towardsdatascience.com/understanding-auc-roc-curve-68b2303cc9c5). The AUC-ROC curve is a graphical representation of the performance of a binary classification model. It plots the True Positive Rate (TPR) against the False Positive Rate (FPR) for different classification thresholds. The area under this curve, known as AUC-ROC, is commonly used as a metric to evaluate the model's performance.

### Formula

The TPR and FPR are calculated using the following formulas:

TPR = TP / (TP + FN)

FPR = FP / (FP + TN)

Where:
- TP (True Positives): The number of positive instances correctly classified as positive.
- FN (False Negatives): The number of positive instances incorrectly classified as negative.
- FP (False Positives): The number of negative instances incorrectly classified as positive.
- TN (True Negatives): The number of negative instances correctly classified as negative.

### Properties

- The AUC-ROC value ranges between 0 and 1, where a higher value indicates better performance. An AUC-ROC of 0.5 suggests a random classifier, while an AUC-ROC of 1 indicates a perfect classifier.
- The curve is generated by varying the classification threshold and calculating the corresponding TPR and FPR values.
- The AUC-ROC curve is commonly used in machine learning to compare different models or to optimize classification thresholds.
- AUC-ROC is insensitive to class imbalance, making it a suitable metric when dealing with imbalanced datasets.
- The AUC-ROC curve is a useful tool for visualizing the trade-off between sensitivity and specificity in a classification model.

### Interpretation

By examining the AUC-ROC curve, you can determine how well your model is performing across different classification thresholds. The curve's shape can provide insights into the model's behavior and its ability to discriminate between positive and negative instances.

A model with a curve closer to the top-left corner indicates higher overall accuracy, while a curve closer to the diagonal line suggests poor performance.

Remember that the AUC-ROC curve is just one tool for evaluating model performance, and it should be used in conjunction with other metrics and domain knowledge to make informed decisions.
