7083,pdfvqa,"# Description

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F3738436%2Fab92f7df4ace06b3c716dc70df257f2e%2F9ab639d482ed4ea88462b1cfe5f94b62.png?generation=1691206412476907&alt=media)
Welcome to the Document-based Visual Question Answering Kaggle competition! This competition is the sub-activity of **The first Workshop on Document Intelligence and Understanding (DocIU)** in conjunction with the **32nd ACM International Conference on Information and Knowledge Management (CIKM 2023)** in **Birmingham, UK**.  Please go to the DocIU workshop website [workshop website](https://doc-iu.github.io/) for detailed workshop schedule and important dates. The objective of this exciting competition is to develop a cutting-edge model that takes as inputs a natural language question and a collection of Regions of Interest (RoIs) representing document layout components of the entire document. The challenge lies in training your model to accurately predict the index(es) of the RoI(s) that can provide the correct answers to the given questions. 

<img src=""https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F3738436%2F600acecdfc60ba5ef8ea05e6aea7dc7b%2Ftask_samples.png?generation=1690768397248148&alt=media"" width = ""400"" style=""display: block; margin: 0 auto"" />

The dataset used in this competition, derived from Task C of PDF-VQA Dataset (accepted by ECML-PKDD), sets a new challenge of document visual question answering by focusing on the entire document of multiple consecutive document pages rather than just single pages. To succeed, participants are encouraged to propose innovative models that leverage state-of-the-art techniques in computer vision and natural language processing. The winning models should demonstrate a deep understanding of the document's layout, logical structure, and content. Well-designed multi-modality feature representation approaches are crucial for comprehending the document's information effectively. Participants are also required to submit a short **two pages** paper using the ACM sigconf template (Please refer [Call for Short Papers for CIKM 2023](https://uobevents.eventsair.com/cikm2023/call-for-short-papers) to get more formatting details). 

We invite participants to think beyond the conventional and push the boundaries of what's possible in document understanding. By doing so, you can contribute to developing innovative solutions with practical applications, such as automated document retrieval, question-answering systems, and intelligent information extraction. Before conducting this competition, please ensure you read and understand the **Rules** of this competition first. Please get in touch with us anytime if you get confused about this competition.  **!!Note a [strong baseline model](https://colab.research.google.com/drive/1C4HgX_Yl3vc6u5Wq6duZe0YQBU34ooh2?usp=sharing) is provided for starting this competition!**

So, gather your expertise in computer vision, natural language processing, and deep learning, and join us in this competition. Engage with a vibrant community of like-minded individuals, exchange ideas, and strive for the top spot on the leaderboard. Together, let's push the boundaries of document-based visual question answering and shape the future of intelligent document analysis. 

<img src=""https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F3738436%2F6550e10d9f16fad9a619d78498d9c541%2Fsponsors.png?generation=1692758191667395&alt=media"" width = ""800"" style=""display: block; margin: 0 auto"" />
----------------------
# Evaluation

**Evaluation Metric: Exact Matching Accuracy (EMA)**

In this competition, the evaluation metric used is Exact Matching Accuracy. The proposed model's prediction will be considered accurate only if it correctly predicts all the answers to the corresponding questions. This means that for each question, the model must precisely match all the ground truth answers (there would be multiple answers for one question).
Let:
- *N* be the total number of questions in the dataset.
- *Q_i* be the *i*-th question in the dataset.
- *GT(Q_i)* be the set of ground truth answers for question *Q_i*.
- *Pred(Q_i)* be the set of answers predicted by the model for question *Q_i*.

The Exact Matching Accuracy (*EMA*) is calculated as:

*EMA = (1 / N) ¡Æ (I(GT(Q_i) = Pred(Q_i)))*

Where *I(condition)* is an indicator function that returns 1 if the condition is true and 0 otherwise.

In simple terms, *EMA* is the proportion of questions for which the model's predicted answers exactly match the ground truth answers.

For example, if there are 100 questions in the dataset, and the model accurately predicts all the answers for 85 questions, the Exact Matching Accuracy would be 0.85 (85/100). The goal for participants is to achieve the highest Exact Matching Accuracy possible by developing models that make precise predictions for all questions in the dataset.
----------------------
# Submission Requirement


# **Kaggle Competition Submission Guidelines**

Thank you for participating in our exciting competition! We are delighted to see the enthusiasm and dedication from all of you. To ensure that the competition runs smoothly and that everyone has a fair chance, we have outlined the submission guidelines below. Please follow these guidelines carefully to submit your entries successfully.

## **Submission Components:**
1. **Leaderboard(submission.csv):**
When submitting your predictions for the Kaggle competition, please make sure to adhere to the following guidelines:

   - Submission File Name: The file containing your prediction results **must be named** **""submission.csv.""**

   - Format: Your submission file **MUST use the ""global_id""** of the prediction layout components (RoIs). Please avoid using the ""id"" (local id) found in specific document pages, as it is not unique.

   - Evaluation: Ensure that the format of your submission file matches the sample submission file provided, ""submission.csv."" This will allow us to evaluate your performance correctly.

By following these guidelines, you can successfully submit your predictions for the Kaggle competition. Good luck!

2. **Code (Notebook):**
   - The code should be well-documented, organized, and self-explanatory.
   - Use Jupyter Notebook/Kaggle Notebook/Colab Notebook as the preferred format for code submission.
   - Include all necessary libraries and dependencies required to run the code.
   - Ensure your code can be executed end-to-end without any errors.
   - Make sure to handle any external data dependencies and include instructions on how to download and use them, if applicable.
   - The code should be structured in a way that allows reproducibility of your results.
   - Submissions to this competition must be made through Notebooks. For the ""Submit"" button to be active after a commit, the following conditions must be met:
      - CPU Notebook <= 9 hours run-time
      - GPU Notebook <= 9 hours run-time


3. **Short Paper (CIKM Format):**
   - Prepare a short paper in the [CIKM (Conference on Information and Knowledge Management) format] (https://uobevents.eventsair.com/cikm2023/call-for-short-papers)  to accompany your code submission.
   - The paper should follow the CIKM guidelines for formatting, including the title, author names, affiliations, abstract, keywords, introduction, main sections (background, related work, methods, dataset, results), conclusion, and references.
   - The paper should be concise yet comprehensive, providing sufficient details about your approach.
   - The background section should clearly state the problem's significance in the domain.
   - The related work section should present an overview of the existing literature related to the problem you are tackling.
   - The methods section should describe the approach and algorithms employed in your solution.
   - The dataset section should provide information about the dataset and external data, including their sources, size, and any preprocessing steps you performed. 
   - Sections can be merged based on your demands.
   - The results section should present your findings, experiments, and any evaluation metrics used.
   - Make sure to highlight the strengths and limitations of your approach.
   - **Aim for a paper length of 2 pages**, adhering to the CIKM format guidelines.

##**Submission Process:**
   - Compress your **code (Notebook)** and the **short paper (PDF)** into **a single zip file**. The zip file should be named appropriately, e.g., ""YourTeamName_Submission.zip.""
   - Upload your submission to the Kaggle competition platform under the ""Submit"" tab.
   - Provide a brief description of your submission and the key highlights in the ""Description"" field.
   - Double-check your submission to ensure that all components are included and that the code runs without errors.
   - Submit your entry before the competition deadline. Late submissions will not be considered.

## **Evaluation:**
- Submissions will be evaluated based on the performance on a hidden test dataset using relevant evaluation metrics, as defined in the competition guidelines.
- **Additionally**, **the clarity, innovation, and quality of the short paper** will also be **taken into account** during the evaluation process.
- **IMPORTANCE**: the accepted papers (winners of the competition) will be required to submit to [Arxiv] (https://arxiv.org/). 

## **Note:**
- Plagiarism and copying from other participants or external sources will not be tolerated. Any such action will lead to disqualification.
- Participate in the competition with honesty and integrity, following the competition rules and guidelines.

Good luck with your submissions! We are eager to see the remarkable work you all have done. If you have any questions or face any issues during the submission process, feel free to contact our support team.
