# Prize

<img src=""https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F3738436%2F6550e10d9f16fad9a619d78498d9c541%2Fsponsors.png?generation=1692758191667395&alt=media"" width = ""800"" style=""display: block; margin: 0 auto"" />
We are excited to announce the generous prize pool for this Kaggle competition, made possible by our esteemed sponsors from Microsoft Research, Google Research, and the Centre for Data-driven Geoscience. The sponsors' support has enabled us to offer the following attractive prizes:

1st place: $1000 USD prize + official certificate 
2nd place: $500 USD prize + official certificate 
3rd-5th place: official certificate
The prize and certificate are sponsored by Google Research, Microsoft Research, and the Centre for Data-driven Geoscience.","# Dataset Description

Our PDF-VQA dataset collected the PDF version of visually-rich documents from the PubMed Central (PMC) Open Access Subset. The detailed annotation procedure is clearly described in the PDFVQA paper. The challenge lies in training your model to accurately predict the index(es) of the RoI(s) that can provide the correct answers to the given questions.
<img src=""https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F3738436%2Fb2e3cfc548480b53b4ac174d00183524%2Fdataset_statistics.png?generation=1690700510714073&alt=media"" width = ""400"" style=""display: block; margin: 0 auto"" />
**Note**: we provide an [official tutorial](https://colab.research.google.com/drive/1RvjvXd4gVXaTIn31Ht6Sqk2uTMlscvRl?usp=sharing) for assisting participants to understand the data structure and start this competition easily. 
## Files
This dataset consists of three main splits: training, validation, and testing. For each split, we provide two types of files:

CSV File: This file contains questions and their corresponding ground truth answers. We only provide the answer for training and validation set to support you in developing a model to predict answers based on the given questions accurately.

PKL File: The pkl file is a valuable resource as it contains essential document information, including the bounding box coordinates of each document layout component, textual contents inside each bounding box, and parent-child relationships (structural relationships between document components). This information is crucial for understanding the structure of the visually-rich documents and identifying relevant Regions of Interest (RoIs) that might contain the answers to the questions.
In this competition, participants can use the training dataset for developing their model, and the original validation and testing dataset will be used for leaderboard evaluation.  We offer an [official tutorial](https://colab.research.google.com/drive/1RvjvXd4gVXaTIn31Ht6Sqk2uTMlscvRl?usp=sharing) to help users quickly understand the dataset format and structure, making it easier for participants to get started with the competition. Here is the link to the tutorial notebook. 

### CSV files
The dataset contains three CSV files containing different splits: training, validation, and testing. Each file contains document IDs (PMCID) and corresponding questions for each item. Note the ground truth answers (answer text list and global-ID list) are only provided for the training dataset during the competition period. The ground truth for the validation dataset will be made available after the competition has finished.

   *   `train_dataframe.csv`- the training set which provides the questions and corresponding answers. 
   *   `val_dataframe` - the validation dataset which provides the questions and corresponding answers.
   *   `test_dataframe_without_answer.csv` - the leaderboard testing set 40% for public and 60% for private.

   *   `submission.csv` - a **sample** submission file in the correct format
  #### Columns

       *   `pmcid` - id of question-answer pairs
       *   `question`?- text of natural language question
       *   `question_type`?- question types including parent-relationship understanding and child-relationship understanding
       *   `answer` (training and validation only)?- a list store answer text contents
       *   `global_id` (training and validation only)?- global id of corresponding answers. Additionally, -1 in this column represents ""No subsection!"" or ""No section!"" can be found for answering this question. 
### PKL files
We've provided detailed pkl files containing essential information about document splits. Follow our [official tutorial]((https://colab.research.google.com/drive/1RvjvXd4gVXaTIn31Ht6Sqk2uTMlscvRl?usp=sharing) to harness this data effectively and gain insights for building winning models. 

### Image Files
We provide the document images for each data split. Participants can use the PKL file provided information to locate the document images of each target document. Image files can provide additional layouts and visual cues to make the feature representation more comprehensive. 
 
The pkl files provided detailed information about each document used in the Google doc. 
With this dataset, you can explore the challenging task of training your model to predict the correct index or indices of the RoI(s) that hold the answers within the PDF documents. The provided files are meant to assist you in building a robust and accurate model for the PDF-VQA task. We hope this dataset will inspire creative solutions and foster progress in Visual Question Answering for PDF documents on the full-document level. Happy modelling!",False,True
7084,dynamic-modeling-of-wastewater-treatment-process,"# Overview


----------------------
# Description

**Context**

Water resources recovery facilities (WRRFs) are integral components of urban water management, responsible for complex and partially observable processes that require monitoring and control to ensure stable, safe, and efficient operation. Modelling these facilities' biological, chemical, and physical processes is valuable for gaining process understanding, simulating and testing control strategies, and predicting future behaviour under changing conditions. While engineers have traditionally favoured mechanistic modelling approaches, data-driven methods are gaining increasing interest due to advancements in sensor technologies, enabling widespread data collection and real-time monitoring. Hybrid modelling (HM) offers a solution that combines the strengths of both mechanical and data-driven models. By integrating mechanical models, which retain process knowledge and extrapolation capabilities, with data-driven models, which enhance predictive capabilities by uncovering unknown relationships. HM aims to optimize the modelling process and overcome the limitations of individual approaches.

We are the international water association's ([IWA](https://iwa-network.org/)) working group on hybrid modelling in water and wastewater treatment. If you are an IWA member you find us on [IWAconnectplus](https://www.iwaconnectplus.org/group/feeds?CommunityKey=a0M4K000004QCWGUA4) or otherwise on [LinkedIn](https://www.linkedin.com/company/hybrid-modelling-working-group/). We are dedicated to explore the effectiveness of different modelling approaches in the field of water and wastewater treatment. Mechanistic models, pure data-driven models, and hybrid models are all utilized in this domain. We are intrigued to determine which model type is most suitable for practical wastewater treatment applications. To gather exceptional modellers and foster innovation, we are hosting this competition. We encourage you to employ your diverse knowledge and skills to compete for the prize!

**Acknowledgements**
[Darrow Project](https://www.wastewater.ai/)
[International Water Association (IWA)](https://iwa-network.org/)
----------------------
# Evaluation

## Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE)
Submissions are scored on the root mean squared error. 
The root mean square error (**RMSE**) is defined as:

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F7018210%2F68ae095ff5e1b7e55c762063ecf92bbd%2FRMSE.png?generation=1696640481023160&alt=media)

where, `EQ` means effluent quality, `i` stands for the data points, `N` is the total number of data points, `B` are the weighting factors for the different types of pollution, `NH` stands for ammonia, `NO` means nitrate.

## Submission File 
For each `DATETIME` row in the `evaluation_online_dataset.csv`, the `EQ_predicted` should be calculated and listed in the submission file. The files should contain a header and have the following format:

```
DATETIME, EQ_measured
2022/3/13 22:57, Val1
2022/3/13 23:12, Val2
2022/3/13 23:27, Val3
```
## One Page of Summary (Linked to the second award)
Stating your methods and why you used the method.
Please submit your Summary to kaggle_summary(at)meiru.ch or mariane.schneider(at)alumni.ethz.ch (both will reach the same place. We will confirm the reception within 24 hours)
----------------------
# Timeline

- **Start Date** - September 27, 2023
- **Entry Deadline** - Same as the Final Submission Deadline
- **Team Merger Deadline** - Same as the Final Submission Deadline
- **Final Submission Deadline** - March 8, 2024 (we keep the competition open a bit longer so that no matter where in the world you will be able to submit comfortably)
----------------------
# Prize

**1000 Euro** to win:
500 Euros go into the metrics and 500 Euros into a jury award for best modelling practice. The second award is linked to a facultative, maximum 1 page summary that you submit, stating your methods and why you used the method.
Additionally, we have 5 individual one year memberships for the [international water association (IWA) ](https://iwa-network.org/join/) as a prize. All the participants can apply for 15% discount of IWA membership.","# Dataset Description

**General description of the WWTP**
Tilburg WWTP (The Netherlands) is located in the south of The Netherlands and is the second largest wastewater treatment plant of Waterschap De Dommel. The plant has a design capacity of 350000 population-equivalent (P.E.), with its current organic load being approximately 360000 P.E. and the wastewater flow rates during dry and wet weather conditions, respectively, 2000 m3/h and 12585 m3/h. The plant has both water and sludge treatment lines and is considered as an ""energy factory"" converting the produced biogas to energy through combined heat and power (CHP) generation system. The water line is made up of three parallel trains, including primary clarifiers, biological treatment, chemical P-removal, and secondary clarifiers.
 

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F7018210%2F38c87c4d63956bc9d22a3156947bd698%2FWWTP.png?generation=1695203304903681&alt=media)

**Layout of the WWTP**
The influent goes through the preliminary treatment (including screening and grid chamber) and primary sedimentation tanks (PST) before being introduced to the biological activated sludge treatment process (preliminary and primary treatments are not shown in the figure). After the PST, the influent flows into three parallel biological treatment lanes (AT1, AT2 and AT3). The AT line consists of an aerobic selector (SEL), a denitrification tank (DET), a facultative tank (DNT) and three consecutive aerobic nitrification tanks (NIT). The internal recycle flows from the end of the aerobic zone to the beginning of the denitrification tank where the return activated sludge is also being pumped to. The aeration is controlled through an ammonium-dissolved-oxygen (DO) cascade controller. The MLSS in the aeration tank is kept at around 4.5-5 g/L.

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F16650794%2F1502c7c97ed9ed81d53fca2f05c7ee38%2Flayout_updated.png?generation=1695755882134194&alt=media)


**Volumes and Flows**
Volume of the anoxic tank: 10920 [m3]
Volume of the facultative tank: 10100 [m3]
Volume of each of the three aertion tanks: 11179 [m3]
Average internal recycle from aerobic to anoxic zone (nitrate recycle): 9800 [m3/h]
Average return activated sludge: 1600 [m3/h]
Average wastage flow: 30.5 [m3/h]


**Dataset Description**
There are 2 periods of time for which online/offline datasets are provided:

- Training/validation/testing period complete datasets (9 months from May 2021 to January 2022)
- Evaluation period without output variables (3 months from March 2022 to June 2022). This data is used for the prediction of the output variables to submit here for the competition. 

Training/validation/testing period dataset files include:
1) train_val_test_online_dataset.csv
Online sensor measurements for:
 __INPUT__
	- Q_inf: total influent flow rate coming to the plant [m3/h]
	- Q_air_1: Air flow rate supplied to the aerobic selector tank [m3/h]
	- Q_air_2: Air flow rate supplied to the facultative tank [m3/h]
	- Q_air_3: Air flow rate supplied to the first aerobic tank [m3/h]
	- Q_air_4: Air flow rate supplied to the second aerobic tank [m3/h]
	- Q_air_5: Air flow rate supplied to the third aerobic tank [m3/h]
	- Temp: Temperature at the end of the aerobic zone [degC]
__OUTPUT__
	- DO_1: Dissolved oxygen concentration in the first aerobic tank [mg/L]
	- DO_2: Dissolved oxygen concentration in the second aerobic tank [mg/L]
	- DO_3: Dissolved oxygen concentration in the third aerobic tank [mg/L]
	- NO3: Nitrate concentration at the end of the aerobic zone [mg/L]
	- NH4: Ammonium concentration at the end of the aerobic zone [mg/L]

2) train_val_test_lab_dataset.xlsx
Offline lab measurements at:
__INPUT__
	- Influent:
		- COD: Total chemical oxygen demand [mg/L]
		- BOD5: 5-day biological oxygen demand [mg/L]
		- TKN: Total Kjeldahl nitrogen [mg/L]
		- Ntot: Total nitrogen [mg/L]
		- TSS: Total suspended solids [mg/L]
		- Q: Daily influent flow rate [m3/d]
 __OUTPUT__
	- Effluent:
		- COD: Total chemical oxygen demand [mg/L]
		- BOD5: 5-day biological oxygen demand [mg/L]
		- TKN: Total Kjeldahl nitrogen [mg/L]
		- NH4: Ammonium [mg/L]
		- NOx: Nitrogen oxides [mg/L]
		- Ntot: Total nitrogen [mg/L]
		- TSS: Total suspended solids [mg/L]
		- Q: Daily influent flow rate [m3/d]

3) train_val_test_dynamic_influent.csv
A dynamic influent data generated by an influent generator based on average flow, COD, TKN, and TSS concentrations (from lab analysis). The dynamic influent dataset is already fractionated into ASM1 state variables. The influent fractionation was calibrated specifically for the Tilburg wastewater characteristics. The purpose of this dynamic influent dataset is to be used as input for the provided mechanistic model. 


Evaluation period dataset files include:
1) evaluation_online_dataset.csv
Online sensor measurements for:
 __INPUT__
	- Q_inf: total influent flow rate coming to the plant [m3/h]
	- Q_air_1: Air flow rate supplied to the aerobic selector tank [m3/h]
	- Q_air_2: Air flow rate supplied to the facultative tank [m3/h]
	- Q_air_3: Air flow rate supplied to the first aerobic tank [m3/h]
	- Q_air_4: Air flow rate supplied to the second aerobic tank [m3/h]
	- Q_air_5: Air flow rate supplied to the third aerobic tank [m3/h]
	- Temp: Temperature at the end of the aerobic zone [degC]
__OUTPUT__
	Not provided

2) evaluation_lab_dataset.xlsx
Offline lab measurements at:
__INPUT__
	- Influent:
		- COD: Total chemical oxygen demand [mg/L]
		- BOD5: 5-day biological oxygen demand [mg/L]
		- TKN: Total Kjeldahl nitrogen [mg/L]
		- Ntot: Total nitrogen [mg/L]
		- TSS: Total suspended solids [mg/L]
		- Q: Daily influent flow rate [m3/d]
 __OUTPUT__
	Not provided

3) evaluation_dynamic_influent.csv
A dynamic influent data generated by an influent generator based on average flow, COD, TKN, and TSS concentrations (from lab analysis). The dynamic influent dataset is already fractionated into ASM1 state variables. The influent fractionation was calibrated specifically for the Tilburg wastewater characteristics. The purpose of this dynamic influent dataset is to be used as input for the provided mechanistic model. 

**NOTE 1**: All lab data are 24-h flow-proportioned composite samples.

**NOTE 2**: on the influent flow rate data:
The influent flow rate data is provided in all 3 datasets. While the ones reported in ""train_val_test_online_dataset.csv"" and ""train_val_test_dynamic_influent.csv"" files are from the online sensor measurement and they are practically the same, the one reported in ""train_val_test_lab_dataset.xlsx"" file is calculated daily flow rates based on the operating hours of the influent pumps. Looking at both online and offline together, it is observed that the calculated daily values start to shift from ~October 2021 onward compared to the average online values while for the rest of the data they follow each other in a reasonable way. After discussion with the plant's operators it is now verified that the reason for this pattern is basically unreliable data for the calculated daily flows in that period. Therefore, it is recommended to take only the online influent flow rate data as the reliable one for the mentioned period.


**Mechanistic model**
We recommend [PeePyPoo ](https://gitlab.com/datinfo/PeePyPoo) as a referenced mechanical model. We provide you with a [script](https://gitlab.com/sbrml/kagglecompetition/) of an uncalibrated mechanistic model in python with the activated sludge model (ASM) number 1. You are free to use this one, make your own implementation or not use a mechanistic part at all. We however would like to warn you that the dataset, which you get for training and testing does not contain all seasonalities and rain events of the evaluation dataset used for scoring the competition, therefore, you might want to consider including a mechanistic part.",False,True
