7100,authorship-comparison,"# Description

### Welcome to the Summer Challenge
Whether you are entering the field of AI and want to get familiarised with language models or simply test your skills and have fun, this challenge is for you. You're about to encounter a unique task even those who've dabbled in ml will find intriguing

In this challenge, you will create a model to detect whether the same author has written two texts. The data we provide you is collected from articles from our [blog](https://tantusdata.com/insights/) and two publicly available datasets of [news](https://archive.ics.uci.edu/dataset/217/reuter+50+50) and [blog posts](https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/rtatman/blog-authorship-corpus). The total data set is small, enabling you to iterate quickly by only using resources available on Kaggle or your computer. But also to test how you can manage with limited data, which is a situation that you will encounter in many cases.
The key links and resources for you to keep track of:
- [data](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/authorship-comparison/data) - here, you will find the relevant files and a description of available data.
- [code](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/authorship-comparison/code) - here, you can create and share notebooks with code.
If you are new to Kaggle, [here](https://www.kaggle.com/docs/notebooks) is notebooks documentation. Notebooks created through this page will have competition data automatically attached.
- [discussion](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/authorship-comparison/discussion) - here is a place for discussion. We have created [a thread](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/authorship-comparison/discussion/425956) to ask questions.
- [leaderboard](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/authorship-comparison/leaderboard) - here, you can see the ongoing results. Note that the public leaderboard is calculated using ~30% of test data, and the remaining 70% will be visible after the competition ends
- [evaluation](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/authorship-comparison/overview/evaluation) - here is the information about the evaluation metric and submission file format.
- [submissions](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/authorship-comparison/submissions) - here you can see your submissions (all attempts) and choose up to three for the final scoring that will take place after the deadline
- [rules](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/authorship-comparison/rules) - here you can see all the competition rules.
- [team](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/authorship-comparison/team) - here you can add a link to your solution¡ªmore on that is explained in your task section.

### Your task
Participants are challenged to create a model to determine if two texts come from the same author. We provide a training dataset (linked above), but you can also use an external dataset. But please note that the goal of this competition is not to train a huge model on all data available on the internet but to come up with a smart solution which can take advantage of even a small dataset. 
That is why you should also write a description and make your notebook public after the submission deadline so we can understand your approach and check whether it complies with the rules (to check them out, use the link above). [Here](https://www.kaggle.com/discussions/product-feedback/373153), you can find a description about creating a write-up attached to your best submission, which will be visible after the competition ends.
You don't need to access external software, and the code can be run using Kaggle Notebooks.
Now, a bit about the dataset: It was created from three sources [Blog Authorship Corpus](https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/rtatman/blog-authorship-corpus), [Reuter 50 50](https://archive.ics.uci.edu/dataset/217/reuter+50+50), and articles from our [blog](bloghttps://tantusdata.com/insights/).
The training data consists of content text and author. For the test set, participants are provided with texts and pairs of text IDs for which they have to predict whether it comes from the same author. To access the data and for detailed files description head to the [data page](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/authorship-comparison/data).

> ### ?? Tips and advice
> To make things easier for those looking for more guidance, we have created [an example of a solution](https://www.kaggle.com/code/tantusdata/example-solution/notebook). You don't need to follow it, and it is only an idea to get you started. 
Those looking to get more familiar with ML before starting this task are welcome to use the following resources/we recommend:
> - [this blog post](https://jalammar.github.io/illustrated-transformer/) is an excellent description of what the architecture looks like
> - [NLP course on Huggingface](https://huggingface.co/learn/nlp-course/chapter1/1?fw=pt), which goes through all key aspects of training and using language models.

You can jump ahead and start if you're not interested in extra tips or learning resources. But there is no need to hurry, and we do not evaluate by the solution date, but only the score and description. We will start the evaluation after the deadline. 
What we are looking for, aka how to score high upon submission: obviously, you want your score to be good, but remember that it should be coupled with a description of a scaled and optimal solution that best fulfils the task without needlessly overusing resources. It is part of the challenge to figure out what is too complicated and what is just right.
The task should be solved independently; that is why we disabled teaming. To see all the rules, head to the tab linked above.

## Keep in mind
There is an extra side contest that will run during this competition, so make sure to sign up to our [LinkedIn](https://www.linkedin.com/company/tantusdata/mycompany/) to be notified when it starts and how to win.
If you have any questions, use the enquiries thread in the discussions. You are also encouraged to respond to your fellow challenge participants. If you want to share tips, you are welcome to.
The deadline to submit is the 18th of August, 2023, 23:59 CEST. No late entries will be considered, so plan ahead!
Well, that's the basics covered, make sure to have a look through the listed tabs and good luck.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated on [accuracy score](https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/generated/sklearn.metrics.accuracy_score.html) between the predicted label and the true target.

You should also write a description and make your notebook public after the submission deadline so we can understand your approach and check whether it complies with the rules (to check them out, use the link above). [Here](https://www.kaggle.com/discussions/product-feedback/373153), you can find a description about creating a write-up attached to your best submission, which will be visible after the competition ends.

## Submission File
For each prediction_id in the test set, you must predict whether two content samples come from the same author. 1 corresponds to samples from the same author and 0 otherwise. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    prediction_id,label
    0,0
    1,1
    2,1
    3,0
    etc.","# Dataset Description

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set of pairs 
*   **test_texts.csv** - the test text contents with corresponding ids
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

## Columns

**train.csv**
*   `author ` - author id
*   `content `?- corresponding text content

**test.csv**
*   `prediction_id` - id of prediction to use in submission file
*   `content_id_1` - id of first content from the pair corresponding to **test_texts.csv**
*   `content_id_2` - id of second content from the pair corresponding to **test_texts.csv**

**test_texts.csv**
*   `content_id` - id
*   `content` - corresponding text content

**sample_submission.csv**
*   `prediction_id` - predicted pair id
*   `label` - target column to predict. 1 for matching author and 0 otherwise.

There are no additional authors in test set other than those from the training set.",False,True
7101,hatenorm23,"# Description

**Fire HASOC Task 3: Identification of Tokens Contributing to Explicit Hate in Text by Span Detection**

**IMPORTANT NOTES [DO NOT MISS]:**
1. To participate in the task, the teams must first officially register at the Fire HASOC Google Form.  [HASOC FORM](https://hasocfire.github.io/hasoc/2023/) is available under the registration tab, and you need to agree to the terms of the participation.
2. The samples in this task contain explicitly hateful statements that can be harmful to vulnerable groups. The organizers neither supported nor authored these statements. They are picked verbatim from publicly available hate speech datasets.
3. **DO NOT PERFORM ANY FURTHER PREPROCESSING ON THE TEXT** AS IT WILL LEAD TO A MISMATCH OF BIO TAGS PER TOKEN ON THE TEST SET.
5. **DO NOT SHUFFLE ROWS ON THE TEST SET**.
6. In case of any issues, contact the organizers here [HateNorm23](https://lcs2.in/hatenorm-2023/). 

**Task Outline**
This task aims to detect the various hateful spans within a sentence already considered
hateful. A hate span is a set of continuous tokens that, in tandem, communicate the explicit hatefulness in a sentence. For instance, in the statement, ""Women... Can't live with them... **Can't shoot them**"", the portion highlighted in bold will be considered a hateful span. This shared task aims to extract all such spans from a hateful text.

The input is preprocessed sentences from social media in English, and the detection of hateful spans is achieved by mapping this into a sequence labeling problem. For every token of the sequences, we have manually annotated the start and end of a hateful span. This is achieved by the BIO notation tagging, where B represents the beginning of the hate span, I forms the continuation of a hate span, and O represents all other tokens not part of the hate span. The task is then to learn the correct sequence of the BIO tags for a given sentence. 

For example, in the above sentence, the tag sequence for the previous sentence after preprocessing will be ""women can't live with them, can't shoot them"" ¡ú ""O O O O O B I I"".

For more details, participants can refer to 
* Hate Normalization via Span Detection: https://arxiv.org/abs/2206.04007
* Toxic Span Detection: https://aclanthology.org/2021.semeval-1.6/
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated on [SeqEval macro-F1](https://github.com/chakki-works/seqeval) between the predicted and ground spans.


## Submission File
For each ID in the validation or test set, you must predict the target BIO for each token in the sentence. The result file should be a ""**csv**"" contain headers ""Id"" and ""bio"" and have the following format:

    Id, bio
    2, ""B I O""
    5, ""O O B""
    6, ""B I I O O B""
    etc....

**IMPORTANT:** The bio tags will be one per token in the input sentence and should be a string separated by a single space to capture the BIO information. 
----------------------
# Paper Citation:

[Paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.09834)
@misc{masud2023overview,
      title={Overview of the HASOC Subtrack at FIRE 2023: Identification of Tokens Contributing to Explicit Hate in English by Span Detection}, 
      author={Sarah Masud and Mohammad Aflah Khan and Md. Shad Akhtar and Tanmoy Chakraborty},
      year={2023},
      eprint={2311.09834},
      archivePrefix={arXiv},
      primaryClass={cs.CL}
}","# Dataset Description

## Files
* **train.csv**: the training set and labels (BIO tags) are released when the competition starts. 
* **test.csv**: the test set will be released without labels. 70% will be used for the private leaderboard until the competition ends, while 30% will be used for the public leaderboard. **A 70:30 combination of private and public leaderboards will be used for the final rank assessment on the test set.**
*   **submission.csv**: The submission file in the correct format with headers in the format as provided under overview-->evaluation. The submission must have an `Id` column and a `bio` column with the correct naming convention of the columns. The separator for the submission file is `,`
* **IMP NOTE:** The train has a `span` column, but we do not need it for the test set. Test set submissions should only have columns as provided in the **sample_submission.csv**

## Separator
We use `|` as a separator in the `.csv` files in the `train.csv` and the `test.csv`.
The separator for the submission file is `,`.

## Sizes
* **train.csv**: 2421 rows and 4 columns
* **test.csv**: 606 rows and 2 columns

## Columns
*   `Id`: index of the row sample
*   `sentence`: preprocessed explicitly hateful sentence where a single space separates each token (alphanumeric or punctuation).
* `span`: the start and end index of the hateful tokens in the span in the form start[s1,s2,s3...sNth] and end[e1,e2,e3...eN], where pairs (sKth,eKth) indicate the beginning and end of a Kth span in the sentence. 
* `bio`: the BIO notations for each token in the sentence, i.e., len(sentence.split()) ==len(bio.split()).",False,True
