7107,telecom-churn-case-study-hackathon-cc50,"# Description

# Problem Statement 

In the telecom industry, customers are able to choose from multiple service providers and actively switch from one operator to another. In this highly competitive market, the telecommunications industry experiences an average of 15-25% annual churn rate. Given the fact that it costs 5-10 times more to acquire a new customer than to retain an existing one, customer retention has now become even more important than customer acquisition.

For many incumbent operators, retaining high profitable customers is the number one business goal. To reduce customer churn, telecom companies need to **predict which customers are at high risk of churn**. In this project, you will analyze customer-level data of a leading telecom firm, and build predictive models to identify customers at high risk of churn.

In this competition, your goal is to build a machine learning model that is able to predict churning customers based on the features provided for their usage.

----------------------
# Evaluation

### Goal

It is your job to predict if a customer will churn, given the ~170 columns containing customer behavior, usage patterns, payment patterns, and other features that might be relevant. Your target variable is ""churn_probability""

Note: Make sure your accuracy is greater than the sample submission that is present in the leaderboard

## Metric

Submissions are evaluated on [Classification Accuracy](https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/model_evaluation.html#accuracy-score) between the value of the predicted value and the actual value of churn for each of the customers. 

![Accuracy score formula](https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/7bfe40cea126a04004b82f729cf7df1ec435fbf6)

The public leaderboard is going to rank your submission against other users while the competition is active, however, once the competition is ended, the final ranks will be calculated on the private leaderboard.

## Submission file format

The file should contain a header and have the following format (CSV):

```
id,churn_probability
70005,0.0
70006,1.0
70007,0.0
etc.
```
A sample file can be found attached in the Data section of this competition.
----------------------
# FAQs

### How do I contact support?

For this competition, any questions, concerns, or technical difficulties will be addressed at the Discussion forum on UpGrad Learn platform. Any discussion posted regarding support on this competition page will have to be redirected to the same portal.

### What¡¯s the difference between a private and public leaderboard?

The Kaggle leaderboard has a public and private component to prevent participants from ¡°overfitting¡± to the leaderboard. If your model is ¡°overfit¡± to a dataset then it is not generalizable outside of the dataset you trained it on. This means that your model would have low accuracy on another sample of data taken from a similar dataset.

**Public Leaderboard:** For all participants, the same 70% of predictions from the test set are assigned to the public leaderboard. The score you see on the public leaderboard reflects your model¡¯s accuracy on this portion of the test set.

**Private Leaderboard:** The other 30% of predictions from the test set are assigned to the private leaderboard. The private leaderboard is not visible to participants until the competition has concluded. At the end of a competition, we will reveal the private leaderboard so you can see your score on the other 30% of the test data. The scores on the private leaderboard are used to determine the competition winners.

### What are kernels?

Kaggle Kernels is a cloud computational environment that enables reproducible and collaborative analysis. Kernels support scripts in R and Python, Jupyter Notebooks, and RMarkdown reports. Go to the Kernels tab to view all of the publicly shared code on this competition.","# Dataset Description

This page appears alongside the data files. It describes?what files have been provided and?the format of each. There is no single format for this page that is appropriate for all competitions, but you should strive to describe as much as you can here. A little time spent describing the data here can save a lot of time answering questions later.

Participants should be able to answer these types of questions after reading the data description:

What files do I need?  
What should I expect the data format to be?  
What am I predicting?  
What acronyms will I encounter?

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format
*   **metaData.csv** - supplemental information about the data

## Columns

*   `example_id` - definition of example_id
*   `feature_1`?- definition of feature_1
*   etc",False,True
7108,ai-village-capture-the-flag-defcon31,"# Overview

The popular and competitive game, Capture the Flag (CTF), isn't only played outdoors. A digital CTF refers to a set of challenges or puzzles that require participants to exploit vulnerabilities, solve problems, or uncover hidden information within computer systems, software, or networks.

In this competition, you'll interact with 27 hand-crafted machine learning security challenges to find flags, solve puzzles, and gain hands-on experience with concepts of AI security and safety. Every challenge is worth 1 point, so don't worry about starting late, just dive in!
----------------------
# Description

CTFs are puzzles meant to challenge you and help you learn new things. Sometimes they may be a little ambiguous or misleading. That's part of the challenge! Once you successfully solve a challenge, you¡¯ll capture a ""digital flag"" (a unique-to-you string). Save that flag, then upload it to receive credit and move up the Kaggle leaderboard. 

Feel free to drop questions in the Kaggle Discussion forums, but don¡¯t share code or spoilers! Please do not try and hack any infrastructure or share flags. Any teams found sharing flags will be disqualified. For help, contact us on [Discord](http://www.discord.gg/kaggle) or use the Kaggle discussion board.

Competition host [AI Village](https://aivillage.org/) is a community of hackers and data scientists working to educate the world on the use and abuse of artificial intelligence in security and privacy. They aim to bring more diverse viewpoints to this field and grow the community of hackers, engineers, researchers, and policymakers working on making AI safer. They believe that we need more people with a hacker mindset assessing and analyzing machine learning systems. They have a presence at DEFCON, the world¡¯s longest-running and largest hacking conference.

Winners will be asked to share their notebooks. Checkout the [starter notebook](https://www.kaggle.com/code/moohax/ctf-starter-aiv)!
----------------------
# Evaluation

The evaluation metric for this competition is simple cumulative scoring. Each solve of a challenge is worth 1 point. Some challenges are materially harder, so expect variability in how long each challenge takes! 

Each challenge will provide a unique flag, please respect the spirit of the competition and do not share flags. **Teams found sharing flags or using shared flags will be disqualified!**

## Submission Format

Participants will submit a `.csv` containing the flags captured at that point in time. All teams start at 0 and work their way towards a perfect score of 27. You do not need to have all the flags in order to submit. In fact, you should submit as soon as possible after getting a flag, since ties in the leaderboard are sorted by the first teams to have achieved a score.

Submission files should contain two columns: `Name` and `Flag`. The file should contain a header and have the following format: There should be 27 rows (not counting the header).

```
Name,Flag test,gAAAAABlHIBqmt9Z8LMWZ_YO9fvWvJAGSqz1KY5Tf45rZ1VTtH24KvzX8MEfll8rdXRyxgGk4_DQIhJEhT2S9ePe4bT-YgKzjCKyVgWiaWVIcFG2_kP8hkixOlLYI8wmC0a3ZQhE8CAdE2nwvrGFI5Din...
etc.
```
----------------------
# Timeline

October 9, 2023 - Start Date.

November 9, 2023 - Final Submission Deadline.

All deadlines are at 11:59 PM UTC on the corresponding day unless otherwise noted. The competition organizers reserve the right to update the contest timeline if they deem it necessary.
----------------------
# Prizes

* 1st Place - $12,000
* 2nd Place - $10,000
* 3rd Place - $10,000
* 4th Place - $10,000 
* 5th Place - $8,000
----------------------
# Sponsors 

We extend our gratitude to Kaggle for their partnership and sponsorship of prize money. If you're looking to run a similar challenge, we can only recommend Kaggle - especially with their new custom python scoring metric infrastructure.

Additionally, there are several organizations and individuals that provided challenges and other material help in getting this set up. In no particular order:

1. Isaiah Pressman (a winner from last year)
2. Ed Prior [The Missing Link](https://themissinglink.com.au)
1. [NVIDIA](https://www.nvidia.com/)
2. [NetSPI](https://www.netspi.com/) 
3. [Giskard](https://www.giskard.ai/)
4. [ProtectAI](https://protectai.com/)","# Dataset Description

This CTF follows a different flow than most Kaggle competitions. Competitors will be interacting with an externally hosted API endpoint during each of the challenges, using the data provided below. Information about how to interact with this endpoint is provided in the template notebook.

### Template Notebook
You may interact with the challenges from your local host or any other machine that can access the internet. The template notebook is just one method for submitting flags to the scoreboard - don't feel constrained to that single operating environment. 

Once you¡¯ve finished, add your flags to the notebook to update the scoreboard - find our template [here](https://www.kaggle.com/moohax/ctf-starter-aiv). This notebook is simply to get you started with the challenges. For each challenge, there is a prompt to help guide your thinking toward solving the challenge, and a web request to help interact with the the model or flag server. Each challenge is unique, but you will be able to use the same code within levels - Granny 2 is an evolution of Granny and so on. Challenges generate a unique flag - so don't share them, flags will be obvious and will begin with the following pattern `gAAAAABl`.

Use the starting `query` function provided to interact with the challenge. This is the only endpoint and scanning for other paths or services is not necessary. You should build your solutions and strategies around these web requests. Errors try to be helpful and nudge you in the right direction.

To start, run the sample request manually, read the error or prompt, and start modifying your inputs until a flag falls out. Here is a handy link to Kaggle's [competition documentation](https://www.kaggle.com/docs/competitions), which includes instructions on [submitting predictions](https://www.kaggle.com/docs/competitions#making-a-submission).",True,True
7109,optiver-trading-at-the-close,"# Overview


In this competition, you are challenged to develop a model capable of predicting the closing price movements for hundreds of Nasdaq listed stocks using data from the order book and the closing auction of the stock. Information from the auction can be used to adjust prices, assess supply and demand dynamics, and identify trading opportunities.


----------------------
# Description

Stock exchanges are fast-paced, high-stakes environments where every second counts. The intensity escalates as the trading day approaches its end, peaking in the critical final ten minutes. These moments, often characterised by heightened volatility and rapid price fluctuations, play a pivotal role in shaping the global economic narrative for the day.

Each trading day on the Nasdaq Stock Exchange concludes with the Nasdaq Closing Cross auction. This process establishes the official closing prices for securities listed on the exchange. These closing prices serve as key indicators for investors, analysts and other market participants in evaluating the performance of individual securities and the market as a whole.

Within this complex financial landscape operates Optiver, a leading global electronic market maker. Fueled by technological innovation, Optiver trades a vast array of financial instruments, such as derivatives, cash equities, ETFs, bonds, and foreign currencies, offering competitive, two-sided prices for thousands of these instruments on major exchanges worldwide.

In the last ten minutes of the Nasdaq exchange trading session, market makers like Optiver merge traditional order book data with auction book data. This ability to consolidate information from both sources is critical for providing the best prices to all market participants.

In this competition, you are challenged to develop a model capable of predicting the closing price movements for hundreds of Nasdaq listed stocks using data from the order book and the closing auction of the stock. Information from the auction can be used to adjust prices, assess supply and demand dynamics, and identify trading opportunities.

Your model can contribute to the consolidation of signals from the auction and order book, leading to improved market efficiency and accessibility, particularly during the intense final ten minutes of trading. You'll also get firsthand experience in handling real-world data science problems, similar to those faced by traders, quantitative researchers and engineers at Optiver.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated on the Mean Absolute Error (MAE) between the predicted return and the observed target. The formula is given by:

$$MAE = \frac{1}{n} \sum\limits_{i=1}^{n} {|y_i - x_i|} $$

Where:
-  n  is the total number of data points.
-  y_i is the predicted value for data point i.
-  x_i is the observed value for data point i. 


## Submitting
You must submit to this competition using the provided python time-series API, which ensures that models do not peek forward in time. To use the API, follow [the template in this notebook](https://www.kaggle.com/code/sohier/optiver-2023-basic-submission-demo).


----------------------
# Timeline

**This is a forecasting competition with an active training phase and a second period where models will be run against real market data.**

**Training Timeline:**
* **September 20, 2023** - Start Date.

* **December 13, 2023** - Entry Deadline. You must accept the competition rules before this date in order to compete.

* **December 13, 2023** - Team Merger Deadline. This is the last day participants may join or merge teams.

* **December 20, 2023** - Final Submission Deadline.

All deadlines are at 11:59 PM UTC on the corresponding day unless otherwise noted. The competition organizers reserve the right to update the contest timeline if they deem it necessary.

**Forecasting Timeline:**
Starting after the final submission deadline there will be periodic updates to the leaderboard to reflect market data updates that will be run against selected notebooks. Updates will take place roughly every two weeks.

* **March 20, 2024** - Competition End Date
----------------------
# Prizes

* 1st Place - $25,000
* 2nd Place - $20,000
* 3rd Place - $15,000
* 4th Place - $10,000
* 5th - 10th Place - $5,000
----------------------
# Code Requirements

<img src=""https://storage.googleapis.com/kaggle-media/competitions/general/Kerneler-white-desc2_transparent.png"" style=""float: right; width: 70px; padding: 10px"">

Submissions to this competition must be made through Notebooks. In order for the ""Submit"" button to be active after a commit, the following conditions must be met:

- CPU Notebook <= 9 hours run-time
- GPU Notebook <= 9 hours run-time
- Internet access disabled
- Freely & publicly available external data is allowed, including pre-trained models
- Submission file must be named `submission.csv` and be generated by the API.

Please see the [Code Competition FAQ](https://www.kaggle.com/docs/competitions#notebooks-only-FAQ) for more information on how to submit. And review the [code debugging doc](https://www.kaggle.com/code-competition-debugging) if you are encountering submission errors.","# Dataset Description

This dataset contains historic data for the daily ten minute closing auction on the NASDAQ stock exchange. Your challenge is to predict the future price movements of stocks relative to the price future price movement of a synthetic index composed of NASDAQ-listed stocks.

This is a forecasting competition using the time series API. The private leaderboard will be determined using real market data gathered after the submission period closes.


##Files

**[train/test].csv** The auction data. The test data will be delivered by the API.

*   `stock_id` - A unique identifier for the stock. Not all stock IDs exist in every time bucket.
*   `date_id` - A unique identifier for the date. Date IDs are sequential & consistent across all stocks.
*   `imbalance_size` - The amount unmatched at the current reference price (in USD).
*   `imbalance_buy_sell_flag` - An indicator reflecting the direction of auction imbalance.
    * buy-side imbalance; 1
    * sell-side imbalance; -1
    * no imbalance; 0
*   `reference_price` - The price at which paired shares are maximized, the imbalance is minimized and the distance from the bid-ask midpoint is minimized, in that order. Can also be thought of as being equal to the near price bounded between the best bid and ask price.
*   `matched_size` - The amount that can be matched at the current reference price (in USD).
*   `far_price` - The crossing price that will maximize the number of shares matched based on auction interest only. This calculation excludes continuous market orders.
*   `near_price` - The crossing price that will maximize the number of shares matched based auction and continuous market orders.
*   `[bid/ask]_price` - Price of the most competitive buy/sell level in the non-auction book.
*   `[bid/ask]_size` - The dollar notional amount on the most competitive buy/sell level  in the non-auction book.
*   `wap` - The weighted average price in the non-auction book. $$\frac{ {BidPrice * AskSize + AskPrice * BidSize}}{BidSize + AskSize} $$
*   `seconds_in_bucket` - The number of seconds elapsed since the beginning of the day's closing auction, always starting from 0.
*   `target` - The 60 second future move in the wap of the stock, less the 60 second future move of the synthetic index. Only provided for the train set.
    * The synthetic index is a custom weighted index of Nasdaq-listed stocks constructed by Optiver for this competition.
    * The unit of the target is basis points, which is a common unit of measurement in financial markets. A 1 basis point price move is equivalent to a 0.01% price move.
    * Where t is the time at the current observation, we can define the target:
$$ Target = (\frac{StockWAP_{t+60}}{StockWAP_{t}} - \frac{IndexWAP_{t+60}}{IndexWAP_{t}}) * 10000 $$

**All size related columns are in USD terms.**

**All price related columns are converted to a price move relative to the stock wap (weighted average price) at the beginning of the auction period.**


**sample_submission** A valid sample submission, delivered by the API. [See this notebook](https://www.kaggle.com/code/sohier/optiver-2023-basic-submission-demo) for a very simple example of how to use the sample submission.


**revealed_targets** When the first `time_id` for each date  (i.e. when `seconds_in_bucket` equals zero) the API will serve a dataframe providing the true `target` values for the entire previous date. All other rows contain null values for the columns of interest.


**public_timeseries_testing_util.py** An optional file intended to make it easier to run custom offline API tests. See the script's docstring for details. You will need to edit this file before using it.


**example_test_files/** Data intended to illustrate how the API functions. Includes the same files and columns delivered by the API. The first three date ids are repeats of the last three date ids in the train set, to enable an illustration of how the API functions.


**optiver2023/** Files that enable the API. Expect the API to deliver all rows in under five minutes and to reserve less than 0.5 GB of memory. The first three date ids delivered by the API are repeats of the last three date ids in the train set, to better illustrate how the API functions. You must make predictions for those dates in order to advance the API but those predictions are not scored.",True,True
7110,habla-imaginada-clasificacion-senales-EEG,"# Descripci¨®n

Esta competencia es organizada por el sinc(i) - UNL/CONICET, en marco de las jornadas [IA@Litoral 2023](https://sinc.unl.edu.ar/ia-litoral/).

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F16048619%2F2b39943a45a10bc03b48ca0e6892b38a%2FPara_Redes_Color.png?generation=1693313341865657&alt=media)

Esta apasionante competencia desaf¨ªa a los participantes a desarrollar algoritmos de inteligencia artificial capaces de clasificar con precisi¨®n diferentes patrones de se?ales cerebrales registradas mediante electroencefalograf¨ªa (EEG) para determinar qu¨¦ palabras imagina una persona que est¨¢ pronunciando.

En esta competencia, los participantes tendr¨¢n acceso a un conjunto de datos de EEG registrados en diferentes individuos a quienes se les pidi¨® que pensaran en ciertos comandos, que cubren diversas condiciones experimentales. El objetivo es utilizar t¨¦cnicas de aprendizaje autom¨¢tico y procesamiento de se?ales para desarrollar modelos que puedan discernir correctamente en se?ales de EEG si una persona est¨¢ pensando en una vocal (/a/, /e/, /i/, /o/, /u/) o en una direcci¨®n. Es posible encontrar m¨¢s detalles sobre estos experimentos en [1], debido a que es una de las bases de datos abiertas m¨¢s citadas sobre habla imaginada y fue desarrollada por cient¨ªficos de Argentina. Esto convierte la competencia en una alternativa muy desafiante ya que diferentes autores han probado distintos enfoques, pero todav¨ªa hay mucho margen de mejora. Para este trabajo, los datos fueron ofuscados con el fin de generar una competencia leal.

Los participantes pueden utilizar cualquier lenguaje de programaci¨®n y bibliotecas de aprendizaje autom¨¢tico. Adem¨¢s, se proporcionar¨¢n herramientas y recursos para ayudar a los participantes a familiarizarse con los datos y comprender los desaf¨ªos espec¨ªficos asociados con la clasificaci¨®n de se?ales EEG. M¨¢s info en el siguiente Google Colab: [enlace al Colab](https://colab.research.google.com/drive/1Wwu-QmpFtGjVx7It-qdtZJfXbx5WXxwH?usp=sharing).

?Pon¨¦ a prueba tus habilidades en el apasionante campo de la clasificaci¨®n de se?ales de electroencefalograf¨ªa relacionado con la comunicaci¨®n oral! Esta competencia ofrece una oportunidad ¨²nica para explorar nuevas t¨¦cnicas de inteligencia artificial y demostrar tu capacidad para descifrar los secretos del cerebro humano. Los mejores participantes tendr¨¢n la oportunidad de presentar sus enfoques y resultados en una sesi¨®n especial durante la conferencia.

![](https://sinc.unl.edu.ar/wp-content/themes/sinci/img/sinc-logo.png)

https://sinc.unl.edu.ar/
----------------------
# Evaluaci¨®n

Todas las presentaciones ser¨¢n evaluadas seg¨²n la m¨¦trica accuracy.

**La reproducibilidad es importante**. Al finalizar la competencia se pedir¨¢ a los primeros puestos un Jupyter Notebook que genere las salidas predichas a partir de los datos proporcionados. Se pueden utilizar otras fuentes de datos, soluciones o modelos pre-entrenados mientras sean p¨²blicos. Se penalizar¨¢ las entregas con soluciones tramposas.

---

**Archivo de Presentaci¨®n**
El archivo debe contener un encabezado y tener el siguiente formato, donde True indica que esa se?al corresponde a una vocal:

id,prediccion
1,True
2,False
3,True","# Dataset Description

Estos datos contienen registros de EEG de diversas personas, registrado en condiciones experimentales. Se puede encontrar una descripci¨®n detallada de los datos en: *G. Pressel-Coreto, I. E. Gareis, H. L. Rufiner*. (2016) **Base de datos de acceso abierto de se?ales de EEG registradas durante el habla imaginada**. XII Simposio Internacional sobre Procesamiento y An¨¢lisis de Informaci¨®n M¨¦dica (SIPAIM). https://sinc.unl.edu.ar/sinc-publications/2016/PGR16/

Este conjunto de datos ha sido registrado a partir de 15 personas, con diferente n¨²mero de ensayos entre ellos.

Los datos de entrada se basan en registros temporales de los distintos canales (F3/F4, C3/C4, P3/P4), como se describe en la siguiente imagen:

![](https://www.googleapis.com/download/storage/v1/b/kaggle-user-content/o/inbox%2F16048619%2F1fdda36e3390a9d258b940fdfd4d0a1f%2FScreenshot%202023-07-21%20at%2011.53.08.png?generation=1689952699264255&alt=media)

## Archivos

*   **train.csv** - conjunto de datos de entrenamiento
*   **test.csv** - conjunto de datos de test
*   **random_sample_submission.csv** - ejemplo de predicciones para ser subido a esta plataforma

## Columnas

*   `subject_id` - c¨®digo identificador de la persona
*   `F3_1`?- marca temporal en el instante 1 del canal F3.
*   `F3_2`?- marca temporal en el instante 2 del canal F3.
* ...
*   `P4_1`?- marca temporal en el instante 1 del canal P4.
*   `P4_2`?- marca temporal en el instante 2 del canal P4.
*   etc.",False,True
7111,annihilate-hates,"# Description

# HASOC-2023 Task 4 (FIRE 2023): Annihilate Hates

## Task objective:
The objective of the task is to detect hate speech in Bengali, Bodo, and Assamese languages. It is a binary classification task. Each dataset (for the three languages) consists of a list of sentences with their corresponding class (hate or offensive (HOF) or not hate (NOT)). Data is primarily collected from Twitter, Facebook, or youtube comments. The Macro F1 score will be the yardstick of the task. Team rank will be determined based on the Macro F1 score.

## Run submission guidelines:
- Submission should be in a CSV file. 
- CSV file must contain S. No. and task_1 column.
- DO NOT SHUFFLE ROWS ON THE TEST SET.

### For more details, participants can refer to

Hate Speech Detection in Hindi, Marathi and Bengali: https://aclanthology.org/2022.paclic-1.94.pdf
Assamese Hate Speech Detection: https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/abstract/document/10183497/

##### Please visit our website: https://sites.google.com/view/hasoc-2023-annihilate-hates/home and join the WhatsApp group for further queries.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Team rank will be determined based on the Macro F1 score. 


## Submission File
The submission file should be in CSV format.  S.No column contains text numbers, and task_1 contains the predicted class, either HOF or NOT.


    S.No,task_1
    1,NOT
    2,HOF
    3,NOT","# Dataset Description

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training set is a CSV file containing S. No., text and task_1 columns. 
*   **test.csv** - the test set is a CSV file containing S. No. and text columns.
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file with the S. No. and task_1 columns in the correct format.

## Columns

*   `S. No.` - row numbers
*   `text `?- sentences in native scripts
*   `task_1 ` - classes (HOF or NOT)",False,True
7112,air-toxicity-and-chronic-respiratory-diseases-us,"# Description

Welcome to the Air Toxicity and Chronic Respiratory Diseases Kaggle competition! This competition is designed to challenge and inspire you to apply data science techniques in uncovering the underlying connections between air toxicity, specifically lead concentration, and the incidence of chronic respiratory diseases in the elderly population across the US. The dataset we provide for this competition combines data from the Global Burden of Disease (GBD) Results tool and the United States Environmental Protection Agency (EPA), giving you a unique opportunity to explore the multifaceted dynamics of health and environment. 

## About The Task

Your task is to develop a predictive model that can accurately predict the incidence of chronic respiratory diseases based on the given air toxicity parameters and other relevant variables. The 'Incidence' rate is the number of new cases divided by the population at the middle of the year for that age group and state.

Use the `train.csv` file to train your models, and make predictions on the `test.csv` dataset. The data has been split into training and testing sets to ensure fair evaluation of your model. Please make sure you understand the dataset structure, variables, and supplementary data provided.

## How to Contribute to the Scientific Knowledge

The way to contribute to the scientific knowledge is to share the notebooks with the EDA and prediction models. Thus, epidemiologists can find your notebooks and use your data analysis and model in their research. Participants are encouraged to use external data on air pollution. However, external public health data should be avoided so that the model has environmental data as input and public health data as output.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated on [Spearman's rank correlation coefficient](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spearman%27s_rank_correlation_coefficient) between the predicted and the observed incidences of chronic respiratory diseases. 

Spearman's rank correlation coefficient measures the strength and direction of association between two ranked variables. It is a nonparametric measure of rank correlation. Its values range from -1 to 1, where 1 denotes perfect positive association, -1 denotes perfect negative association, and 0 denotes no association.

The team with the highest Spearman's rank correlation coefficient will be declared the winner.

## Submission File
For each ID in the test set, you must predict the incidence of the chronic respiratory disease. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

```python
    ID,Incidence
    f8312a4,4827.05
    3effa36,4827.05
    1e8044b,4377.95
    etc.
```

Please ensure that your submission file has predictions for all the records in the test dataset and follows the correct format. Incomplete submissions or those not adhering to the correct format will be scored as zero.
----------------------
# References

**References**
Below are references to the data sets we used for this competition. Our use of these datasets is non-commercial and motivated by educational and scientific purposes only.

**GBD Results tool:**
*Global Burden of Disease Collaborative Network.*
*Global Burden of Disease Study 2019 (GBD 2019) Results.*
Seattle, United States: Institute for Health Metrics and Evaluation (IHME), 2020.
Available from: [https://vizhub.healthdata.org/gbd-results?params=gbd-api-2019-permalink/4236f14fa0a1ca16f5588ff87364c591](https://vizhub.healthdata.org/gbd-results?params=gbd-api-2019-permalink/4236f14fa0a1ca16f5588ff87364c591)

**AQS Data:**
*United States Environmental Protection Agency (EPA).* 
*Toxics, Precursors, and Lead Data.* 
United States Environmental Protection Agency, 2023. 
Available from: [https://aqs.epa.gov/aqsweb/airdata/download_files.html](https://aqs.epa.gov/aqsweb/airdata/download_files.html)","# Dataset Description

# Dataset Description for ""Air Toxicity and Chronic Respiratory Diseases (US)""

Welcome to the dataset description page for the Kaggle competition ""Air Toxicity and Chronic Respiratory Diseases (US)"". Here we provide detailed information about the data files, format, the prediction task and definitions of acronyms used.

## Files

- **train.csv** - This is the training set, which you will use to build your models. It contains various features along with the target variable that represents the prevalence of chronic respiratory diseases.

- **test.csv** - The test set contains the same features as the training set but without the target variable. You will use this to generate your predictions.

- **sample_submission.csv** - This is a sample submission file in the correct format. It includes all the IDs from the test set and assumes a constant prediction for the target variable.

- **State.Names.csv** - This file provides a list of all U.S states referenced in the data.

- **supplementary_*.parquet** - These are supplementary files containing additional features that can be merged with the main training and test sets based on the 'example_id'.

## Columns

### train.csv

- `ID` - The ID representing each record in the dataset. This ID is consistent across `train.csv`, `test.csv`, and the supplementary files.

- `State.Name` - The name of the U.S. state where the data was recorded.

- `Year` - The year when the data was recorded.

- `Age` - The age group of the elderly population for which the data is recorded. Age is grouped into ranges, for example, ""65-69"", ""70-74"", etc.

- `Incidence` - This is the target variable representing the incidence rate of chronic respiratory diseases in the given age group. This means how many new cases there are in a year divided by how many people there are in the middle of the year.

### test.csv

- `ID` - The ID representing each record in the dataset. This ID is consistent across `train.csv`, `test.csv`, and the supplementary files.

- `State.Name` - The name of the U.S. state where the data was recorded.

- `Year` - The year when the data was recorded.

- `Age` - The age group of the elderly population for which the data is recorded. Age is grouped into ranges, for example, ""65-69"", ""70-74"", etc.

### supplementary_*.parquet

- `State.Name` - The name of the U.S. state where the data was recorded.

- `Date.Local` - The local date when the measurements were taken.

- `Parameter.Name` - The name or description assigned in AQS to the parameter measured by the monitor. Parameters may be pollutants or non-pollutants.

- `Arithmetic.Mean` - The average (arithmetic mean) value for the year for the given parameter.

- `X1st.Max.Value` - The highest value for the year for the given parameter.

## Data Cleaning
Data cleaning was performed using the following GitHub repository: [Data Cleaning](https://github.com/josephcmac/chronic-respiratory-diseases)",False,True
7113,underwater-acoustic-signal-modulation-recognition,"# Description

## Acknowledgements
The underwater acoustic signal data for this competition was simulated based on the publicly available underwater acoustic channel dataset Watermark[1]. 
[1] P. A. van Walree, F. Socheleau, R. Otnes, and T. Jenserud, ¡°The watermark benchmark for underwater acoustic modulation schemes,¡± IEEE J. Ocean. Eng., vol. 42, no. 4, pp. 1007¨C1018, Oct. 2017.","# Dataset Description

The dataset is a underwater acoustic communication signal based on the simulation of the underwater acoustic channel environment, including a total of nine modulation types at eight signal-to-noise ratios.

Modulation Type: OFDM, 2FSK, 4FSK, 8FSK, BPSK, QPSK, 8PSK, 16QAM, 64QAM.
SNR: -10dB, -6dB, -2dB, 2dB, 6dB, 10dB, 14dB, 18dB.

## Files

*   **trainDataset.npy** - the training set
        The training set data is stored as a dictionary, including four keys: 'ID', 'SNR', 'labels', 'features'.
               - 'ID': A unique identifier for each signal.
               - 'SNR': The SNR of each signal.
               - 'labels': The modulation type of each signal, indicated by 0-8.
               - features: I (In-phase) /Q (Quadrature) features of each signal. Each signal consists of 2048 features, the first 
                                 1024 being the I-component of the signal and the last 1024 being the Q-component.

*   **testDataset.npy** - the test set
        The test set data is stored as a dictionary, including four keys: 'ID', 'features'.
               - 'ID': A unique identifier for each signal.
               - features: I (In-phase) /Q (Quadrature) features of each signal. Each signal consists of 2048 features, the first 
                                 1024 being the I-component of the signal and the last 1024 being the Q-component.

*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format
       An example of submitting test set results.  Submit a two-column "".csv"" file, the first column is the ID number and the second column is the classification result, i.e. 0-8.",False,True
7114,prediciton-of-ocean-temperature-profile,"# description

# You get 3 files about competetion.
The file named ""train_data_target.npz"" include data of training and corresponding target. You can use the program `np.load(filename)[part needed]` to get the data you need.
Data represents the temperature profiles of a ocean region about 138¡ã-141¡ãE and 1¡ã-4¡ãN. The max depth of the data is 2000m.
The shape of every data is  (625, 37, 12) which represents number of horizontal vertices, number of vertical profiles and number of time strides used to predict future region temperature respectively. **You can reshape it in (25, 25, 37, 12) datasize to observe or utilize the spatial feature.**
*Attention:*
(25, 25, 37, 12) is just the shape of one piece of data. There is an dimension of num of data at position 0. Just use the `data_train.reshape(-1, 25, 25, 37, 12)`
The corresponding target is (625, 37, 1).
If you decide to use torch structure to make your deeplearning net model, we advice you to use the program to transform the numpydata to torch tensor:

```
train_x_tensor = torch.from_numpy(data_train).type(torch.FloatTensor)  # (B, N, F, T)

train_target_tensor = torch.from_numpy(target_train).type(torch.FloatTensor)  # (B, N,F T)
```


Then, you can use the program to make your data set by your needs

```
train_dataset = torch.utils.data.TensorDataset(train_x_tensor, train_target_tensor)

train_loader = torch.utils.data.DataLoader(train_dataset, batch_size=batch_size, shuffle=True/Flase)
```

# test data
The shape of test data is (304, 625, 37, 12), the target is (304, 625, 37, 1)(*Attention: The first location represents the number of all test data, and the last one is the prediction time. The others are as same as the training data.*)  **which is the data you need to submit but you need to reshape it as the (304, -1) using the program.**
```
reshaped_data = target.reshape(304, -1)

df = pd.DataFrame(reshaped_data)

#add ID
df.insert(0, 'ID', range(1, 304+1))

df.to_csv('output.csv', index=False)
```


