7115,mrm2023,"# Description

La generaci¨®n de precios automatizada en tiempo real se centra en los minoristas en l¨ªnea. Cada d¨ªa, la gesti¨®n de categor¨ªas se enfrenta al desaf¨ªo de adaptar miles de productos a los factores cambiantes del mercado. Esto sucede de forma completamente autom¨¢tica cuando se utiliza una herramienta de precios inteligente. Los precios reflejan la apreciaci¨®n del cliente y conducen a un aumento de las ventas.

El tema de esta competencia es desarrollar un modelo que predice los ingresos de una farmacia de pedidos por correo con la mayor precisi¨®n posible. Se proporcionan datos hist¨®ricos de transacciones ficticios de una farmacia de pedidos por correo. La caracter¨ªstica especial es que los precios de los productos se generaron de forma din¨¢mica y autom¨¢tica.
----------------------
# Evaluation

La evaluaci¨®n de esta competencia ser¨¢ con RMSE.
La ra¨ªz del error cuadr¨¢tico medio (RECM) o ra¨ªz de la desviaci¨®n cuadr¨¢tica media (RDCM) (en ingl¨¦s: root-mean-square deviation, RMSD, o root-mean-square error, RMSE) es una medida de uso frecuente de las diferencias entre los valores (valores de muestra o de poblaci¨®n) predichos por un modelo o un estimador y los valores observados.
La RECM representa la ra¨ªz cuadrada del segundo momento de la muestra de las diferencias entre los valores previstos y los valores observados o la media cuadr¨¢tica de estas diferencias.
Estas desviaciones se denominan residuos cuando los c¨¢lculos se realizan sobre la muestra de datos que se utiliz¨® para la estimaci¨®n y se denominan errores (o errores de predicci¨®n) cuando se calculan fuera de la muestra.
La RECM sirve para agregar las magnitudes de los errores en las predicciones para varias veces en una sola medida de poder predictivo.
La RECM es una medida de precisi¨®n, para comparar errores de predicci¨®n de diferentes modelos para un conjunto de datos en particular y no entre conjuntos de datos, ya que depende de la escala.

La RECM es siempre no negativa, y un valor de 0 (casi nunca alcanzado en la pr¨¢ctica) indicar¨ªa un ajuste perfecto a los datos. En general, una RECM m¨¢s baja es mejor que una m¨¢s alta. Sin embargo, las comparaciones entre diferentes tipos de datos no ser¨ªan v¨¢lidas porque la medida depende de la escala de los n¨²meros utilizados.

La RECM es la ra¨ªz cuadrada del promedio de errores cuadrados. El efecto de cada error en la RECM es proporcional al tama?o del error cuadrado; por lo tanto, los errores mayores tienen un efecto desproporcionadamente grande en la RECM. Por lo tanto, la RECM es sensible a los valores at¨ªpicos.?","# Dataset Description

A continuaci¨®n encontrar¨¢ los archivos para resolver el problema.


## **Descripci¨®n de archivos:**

?	data_train.csv : el conjunto de entrenamiento.

?	data_test.csv : el conjunto de datos a predecir.

?	submit_ejemplo.csv : un archivo de env¨ªo de muestra en el formato correcto que deben subirse

?	Items_especificacion.csv : informaci¨®n relacionada a los productos.





## **Campos de informaci¨®n:**


### Archivo Items_especificacion:

?	pid = numero de producto, formato entero, sin valores faltantes.

?	manufacturer = fabricante, numero natural.

?	group = grupo de producto, formato texto.

?	content = contenido del paquete, contiene flotantes y strings.

?	unit = Unidad, formato texto.

?	pharmForm = forma de dosificaci¨®n, formato de texto.

?	genericProduct = flag de si es g¨¦nerico o no, toma valores 0 y 1.

?	salesIndex = ¨ªndice de ventas, formato entero.

?	category = categor¨ªa principal, formato entero.

?	campaignIndex = indice de campa?a de acci¨®n, toma valores A, B o C.

?	rrp = precio de referencia, formato de valor flotante.





### Archivo data_train:

?	id= clave unica de identificacion, numero natural.

?	day = dia en que se observa la accion, numero natural.

?	pid = numero de producto, numero natural.

?	adFlag = flag que indica si el producto es parte de una campa?a, toma valores 0 y 1.

?	availabiliy = estado de disponibilidad, toma valores 1, 2, 3, 4.

?	competitorPrice = precio del competidor mas barato, valor de tipo flotante.

?	click = flag de si fue clickeado, toma valores 0 y 1.

?	basket = flag de canasta de comra, toma valores 0 y 1.

?	order = flag de si fue comprado, toma valores 0 y 1.

?	price = precio de nuestro producto, toma valores flotantes.

?	revenue = ingresos, toma valores flotantes.





### Archivo data_test:

?	id= clave unica de identificacion, numero natural.

?	day = dia en que se observa la accion, numero natural.

?	pid = numero de producto, numero natural.

?	adFlag = flag que indica si el producto es parte de una campa?a, toma valores 0 y 1.

?	availabiliy = estado de disponibilidad, toma valores 1, 2, 3, 4.

?	competitorPrice = precio del competidor mas barato, valor de tipo flotante.

?	price = precio de nuestro producto, toma valores flotantes.",False,True
7116,datathon-alicorp-2023-b,"# Description

**Requisitos para participar**

Para poder participar del evento debi¨® registrarse previamente en la p¨¢gina oficial: http://datathonalicorp.com.pe que estuvo abierto hasta el mi¨¦rcoles 19 de julio 2023 a las 23:59 hrs.
S¨®lo quienes se inscribieron en ese sitio ser¨¢n elegibles para ser premiados.

**Overview:**

El reto consiste en crear un algoritmo de recomendaci¨®n de skus para nuestros clientes que cuentan con un canal de atenci¨®n digital, estos clientes pueden ser atendidos de manera an¨¢loga como digital (Bodegas, Panader¨ªas y Restaurantes). El objetivo es que los participantes puedan enviarnos un listado de clientes con skus priorizados (m¨¢ximo 30) de mayor a menor prioridad, es decir el primer sku de un cliente es el de mayor probabilidad de compra en la siguiente semana de venta.


**Dataset de entrenamiento:**

Data Entrenamiento: Informaci¨®n de ventas extra¨ªda de los sistemas de informaci¨®n digital y an¨¢loga, desde Febrero 2022 hasta Enero 2023.

Data Producto : Maestro de productos con informaci¨®n de las caracter¨ªsticas propias de cada sku.

Submisison : Dataset que contiene el formato de entregable, cada cliente debe tener como m¨¢ximo 50 skus.


----------------------
# Evaluation

Sea el Recall por cliente obtenido:

$$ Recall_{Cliente} = \frac{\lvert Recomendados \bigcap  Comprados \rvert}{\lvert Comprados \rvert} $$

La evaluaci¨®n se har¨¢ mediante la siguiente m¨¦trica:

$$ Recall_{Promedio} = \frac{\sum \limits_{i=1} ^{n} Recall_{Cliente-i}}{N} $$

Donde:
- Recomendados: Cantidad de productos de recomendados (m¨¢ximo 50).
- Comprados: Cantidad de productos relevantes; es decir, que fueron recomendados y comprados.
- N: Cantidad de Clientes

*Observaci¨®n:* solamente se permitir¨¢ la evaluaci¨®n de SKUs distintos para cada cliente.

La forma de evaluar ser¨¢ mediante un Recall Promedio el cual consiste en contrastar estos 50 SKUs con la venta y ver cu¨¢nto de los SKUs vendidos forman parte de la lista de SKUs recomendados, para luego obtener el promedio de todos los Recall obtenidos por cada cliente.

## Submission File
Por cada customer_id, tendran que recomendar una lista de 50 productos. El archivo deber¨¢ contener las columnas y el formato de la siguiente forma:

    customer_id,product_id
    1,120 987 203 ... (etc.)
    2,80 2 10 ... (etc.)
    3,40 25 11 ... (etc.)","# Dataset Description

## Descripci¨®n de los datos

## Data Entrenamiento [train.csv]
*  `fecha_compra`	Dia donde un cliente hizo una compra.
*  `customer_id`	Codigo de cliente
*  `product_id`	Codigo de producto
*  `type_id`	Tipo de negocio (GA: Gastronomia, PA: Panificacion, BO: Bodegas)
*  `business_id`	Codigo tipo de producto (a que negocio pertenece el producto)
*  `channel_id`	Canal de venta (A: Diadia, B: Insuma, V: Tradicional)
*  `cantidad_venta`	Cantidad de producto comprado
*  `monto_venta_transf`	Monto de compra
*  `peso_venta_transf`	Volumen de compra

## Data Producto [RentabilidadProduct.csv]
*  `product_id`	Codigo de producto
*  `family_id`	Codigo de familia (Marca a la que pertenece)
*  `negocio_id`	Codigo tipo de producto (a que negocio pertenece el producto)
*  `category_id`	Codigo de categoria de producto
*  `tier_id`	Codigo de tier, indica jerarquia de un producto ( 1 = premiun, 2 = mainstream, 3=economy, 4=super economy)
*  `utilidad_bruta_transf`	Utilidad bruta del producto

## Submission
*  `customer_id`	Codigo de cliente
*  `product_id`	Codigo de los 50 productos recomendados (Debe estar ordenado de acuerdo al resultado de su rank)",False,True
7117,ai-ninja-warrior-july-2023,"# Description

Hey Ninjas!
The primary purpose of the AI Ninja Warrior Hackathon is to provide a platform for participants to expand their AI knowledge, learn new techniques, and put their skills to the test. The event is designed to foster an environment of creativity, innovation, and camaraderie, where individuals compete against each other while also learning from one another's expertise.
----------------------
# Evaluation

Evaluation metric will be F1 score:

    ""The F1 score can be interpreted as a harmonic mean of the precision and
    recall, where an F1 score reaches its best value at 1 and worst score at 0.
    The relative contribution of precision and recall to the F1 score are
    equal. The formula for the F1 score is::    
    F1 = 2 * (precision * recall) / (precision + recall)""","# Dataset Description

The dataset consists of feature vectors belonging to multiple sessions of an e-commerce website

Each session would belong to a different user in a 1-year period to avoid any tendency to a specific campaign, special day, user profile, or period. 

Given a session the model has to predict if it will end up in a purchase.

The '**Purchased**' attribute can be used as the target.

**'Internal', 'Internal_Duration', 'Research', 'Research_Duration', 'Goods_Related', 'Goods_Related_time'** represent the number of different types of pages visited by the visitor in that session and total time spent in each of these page categories. 

The values of these features are derived from the URL information of the pages visited by the user and updated in real time when a user takes an action, e.g. moving from one page to another. The ""Bounce_Rates"", ""Exit_Rates"" and ""Page_Values"" features represent the metrics measured by ""Google Analytics"" for each page in the e-commerce site. 

The value of ""**Bounce_Rates**"" feature for a web page refers to the percentage of visitors who enter the site from that page and then leave (""bounce"") without triggering any other requests to the analytics server during that session. 

The value of ""**Exit_Rates**"" feature for a specific web page is calculated as for all page views to the page, the percentage that were the last in the session. 

The ""**Page_Values**"" feature represents the average value for a web page that a user visited before completing an e-commerce transaction. 

The ""**SpecialOccasion**"" feature indicates the closeness of the site visiting time to a specific special day (e.g. Mother¡¯s Day, Valentine's Day) in which the sessions are more likely to be finalized with transaction. The value of this attribute is determined by considering the dynamics of e-commerce such as the duration between the order date and delivery date. For example, for Valentina¡¯s day, this value takes a nonzero value between February 2 and February 12, zero before and after this date unless it is close to another special day, and its maximum value of 1 on February 8. 

The dataset also includes operating system, browser, region, traffic type, visitor type as returning or new visitor, a Boolean value indicating whether the date of the visit is weekend, and month of the year.",False,True
7118,used-car-price-prediction-competition2,"# Description

**Competition Description**

The demand for used cars in the market has significantly increased, particularly as the sales of new cars have slowed down in recent times. Consequently, the pre-owned car market has experienced continuous growth, surpassing the size of the new car market. As a result, establishing an effective pricing scheme for these used cars becomes crucial for success in this expanding market. To address this challenge, we are organizing a used car price prediction competition, where you provide a dataset containing information about used cars, including their corresponding prices. 
The objective is to develop accurate machine learning models that can predict the prices of the used cars in the test set based on the given input features. By devising such models, participants can assist businesses in formulating profitable strategies using differential pricing and gaining a competitive edge in the market.


**Practice Skills**
Creative feature engineering: analyzing and transforming the provided features to enhance the performance of the models
Advanced regression techniques to build predictive models like random forest and gradient boosting


----------------------
# Evaluation

**Goal**
It is your job to predict the price for each car. For each id in the test set, you must predict the value of the Price (Target) variable.

**Metric**
Submissions are evaluated on **R-Squared (R2)**, is a statistical measure in a regression model that determines the proportion of variance in the dependent variable that can be explained by the independent variable.

**Submission File Format**
The file should contain a header and have the following format:
| id | Target |
| --- | --- |
| 1 | 581851 |
| 2 | 4552.2 |
| 3 |548122 |","# Dataset Description

The used car dataset includes a variety of information fields related to pre-owned vehicles available for sale. Here's a description of each field:

## Columns 

- **ID:** An identification number or code assigned to each car listing in the dataset. It is a unique identifier that distinguishes one listing from another.

- **Price:**The price of the used car listed for sale. It represents the asking price set by the seller.

- **Year:**The year of manufacture or production of the vehicle. It indicates the age of the car.

- **Manufacturer:** The brand or manufacturer of the car, such as Toyota, Honda, Ford, etc.

-  **Model:** The specific model or variant of the car, such as Camry, Civic, Mustang, etc.

- **Condition:**Describes the condition of the car, which can range from new to excellent, good, fair, or salvage. It provides an indication of the overall state of the vehicle.

- **Cylinders:** The number of cylinders in the car's engine. It indicates the engine's configuration and affects the performance of the vehicle.

- **Fuel:**The type of fuel used by the car, such as gasoline, diesel, electric, hybrid, or other alternative fuels.

- **Odometer:** The recorded mileage or distance traveled by the vehicle. It gives an indication of the car's usage and can influence its value.

- **Title Status:** Refers to the status of the car's title or ownership documents, such as clean, salvage, rebuilt, or lien. It provides information about the legal status of the vehicle.

- **Transmission:**Specifies the type of transmission system in the car, such as manual, automatic, or continuously variable transmission (CVT).

- **Drive:** Indicates the drivetrain configuration of the vehicle, such as front-wheel drive (FWD), rear-wheel drive (RWD), all-wheel drive (AWD), or four-wheel drive (4WD).

- **Size:** Describes the size or class of the car, such as compact, mid-size, full-size, SUV, truck, etc.

- **Type:** Refers to the body type or style of the car, such as sedan, coupe, hatchback, SUV, truck, convertible, etc.

- **Paint Color:** Specifies the exterior color of the car, such as black, white, red, blue, etc.

- **State:**Indicates the state or location where the car is listed for sale.

- **Latitude (Lat) and Longitude (Long):** The geographical coordinates of the location where the car is listed. It allows for mapping and geospatial analysis.

- - **Posting Date:** The date and time when the car listing was posted or made available for sale.

These fields provide a comprehensive set of information about used cars, enabling various analyses and insights into the market dynamics, pricing trends, vehicle characteristics, and buyer preferences.

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format",False,True
7119,eso-battery-forecasting,"# Description

## Goal of the Competition

The goal of this competition is to predict the charging and discharging patterns of batteries on the electricity network, and the key drivers of this behaviour. 

This exciting challenge will give you the opportunity to learn more about the Electricity System Operator (ESO) of Great Britain, and how we're trying to improve our understanding of battery behaviour on the network  to ensure we can balance supply and demand of electricity reliably and economically.

## Context 

The ESO sits at the heart of the electricity system in Great Britain. Our job is to move electricity around the country's high voltage network to balance supply and demand in real-time, using a mix of power sources (including batteries), to make sure electricity is available wherever and whenever it's needed. 

One of our core ambitions is to facilitate the decarbonisation of Great Britain's energy network and ensure the delivery of reliable, affordable , and green electricity for consumers. 

Batteries enable us to store excess energy from intermittent renewable  sources such as sun and wind, so that when the sun isn't shining or the wind isn't blowing, we can deploy batteries to help us balance the network.  Currently, the capacity of batteries in GB is growing steadily, and you will have the opportunity to explore various datasets and understand how batteries support demand on the network, forecast their impact and see how they can be used to make real-time decisions to balance the system.

Improving our forecasts of electricity demand across the network, and ultimately optimising the use of batteries will help us make better decisions in real-time. Your solutions may help shape the future of GB's energy system to help us facilitate net zero and a fair transition to a decarbonised system for all of GB's consumers. 

Here is a link to the ESO webpage to find out more about electricity 
https://www.nationalgrideso.com/electricity-explained

----------------------
# Evaluation

Submissions are evaluated on Mean Absolute Error (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mean_absolute_error) between the predicted battery output and the observed battery output.


## Submission File
For each ID in the test set, you must predict the value for the battery_output variable. The file should contain a header and have the following format:

    ID,battery_output
    0,12.413
    1,10.021
    2,-5.123
    etc.","# Dataset Description

Your challenge in this competition  is to predict battery behaviour on Great Britain's electricity network. Through this challenge you will identify trends,  detect anomalies, and develop predictive models to balance the system. 

This is a forecasting competition. The data we have provided is open sourced, available to the public but we highly encourage you to explore additional data sets we've not considered.

## Files

*   **train.csv** - the training set
*   **test.csv** - the test set
*   **sample_submission.csv** - a sample submission file in the correct format

All files will have the same column types.

## Columns

*   `Settlement` - A settlement period is a half-hourly period used in trading and balancing of electricity. Each day has 48 settlement periods where generators and suppliers (including batteries) trade electricity in half-hourly blocks https://www.elexon.co.uk/data/open-settlement-data/
*   `Battery Output`- The battery output is the amount of Mega Watt (MW) that  flow's  through a battery at a determined time. 
*   `Location and Price`?- This field describes the price for electricity in a given location. This is data generated for the Octopus Agile Tariff and collected here: https://energy-stats.uk/download-historical-pricing-data/ 
*   `Location and Weather`- This field describes the hourly weather conditions in a given location https://open-meteo.com and https://open-meteo.com/en/license. You can find detailed metadata and units of measurement here: https://open-meteo.com/en/docs

## Useful Info about this specific battery

*   `Notice to deviate from zero` - 1 minutes
*   `Notice to deliver bids` - 1 minutes
*   `Notice to deliver offers` - 1 minutes
*   `Maximum delivery Capacity` - 35 MW
*   `Maximum delivery period` - 60 minutes
*   `Minimum zero time` - 0 minutes
*   `Minimum non-zero time` - 1 minutes
*   `Location` - North East England

## Other useful data sources

**BMReports:** https://bmrs.elexon.co.uk/ `(System Data, including demand and generation actuals.)`
**Carbon Intensity:** https://carbonintensity.org.uk/ `(Current and future carbon intensity of the GB electricity grid)`
**ESO Data Portal:** https://www.nationalgrideso.com/data-portal `(System Data, including Demand forecast and actuals, constraints, balancing costs and lots more)`
**ENTSO-E Transparency Platform:** https://transparency.entsoe.eu/ `(EU electricity data including GB)`
**Sheffield Solar PV Live:** https://www.solar.sheffield.ac.uk/pvlive/ `(GB Solar Generation actuals)`
**Price Data:** `There are various websites providing wholesale GB price data like NordPool (SEM-GB half hourly intraday) and Epexspot. However price data can be sensitive and restrictive, so please adhere to any terms and conditions on the respective sites when attempting to use and download data`",False,True
